          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ca3c1e3561f2856045116fc4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI have to go now, and I don\u2019t know what will happen to me. But I will come back here, or join your group, as soon as I can. One word, one look, will be enough to decide whether I come into your father\u2019s house this evening\u2014or never.\u201d\\n\\nSuch a letter would not be soon forgotten. A half hour alone with her thoughts might have calmed her, but the ten minutes that passed before she was interrupted\u2014while she still had to follow all the rules of her situation\u2014did nothing to make her feel calm. Every moment only brought new agitation. It was overwhelming happiness. And before she had even reached the first full wave of feeling, Charles, Mary, and Henrietta came in.\\n\\nShe had to keep up the act of being herself, and that caused an immediate struggle. After a while, she could not follow a word they said. She had to pretend she was unwell and excuse herself. Then they could see she looked very sick. They were shocked and worried, and they would not leave her for anything. This was awful. If they had only gone away and left her alone in that room, it might have helped her recover. But having them all standing around her was too distracting. In desperation, she said she would go home.\\n\\n\u201cBy all means, my dear,\u201d Mrs Musgrove cried, \u201cgo home at once and take care of yourself, so you\u2019ll be well enough for the evening. I wish Sarah were here to treat you, but I\u2019m not a doctor. Charles, ring for a chair. She must not walk.\u201d\\n\\nBut a chair would not do. Worse than everything! She could not bear the thought of losing the chance to say two words to Captain Wentworth while she made her quiet, solitary way up the town\u2014especially since she felt almost certain she would meet him. She protested strongly against the chair. Mrs Musgrove, who was only thinking about one kind of illness, reassured herself with some anxiety that there had been no fall. She was sure Anne had not slipped down at any time lately, and hit her head. She was perfectly convinced there had been no fall. She could let Anne go cheerfully and trust that she would be better by night.\\n\\nStill, anxious not to miss any precaution, Anne struggled to speak and said\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid, ma\u2019am, that it isn\u2019t quite understood. Please be so kind as to tell the other gentlemen that we hope to see your whole party this evening. I\u2019m afraid there was some mistake. And I want you especially to assure Captain Harville and Captain Wentworth that we hope to see them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! my dear, it is quite understood. I give you my word.\u201d\"}"
