{"request_id":"bcef59b1ab0f5cbdb67d27b1","rewritten_text":"My father had been everything to me, and his memory was the very life of my life. I might feel gratitude toward another, but I could never again love or hope as I had once done; it was all suffering— even my pleasures were endured, not enjoyed. I was like a lone place among mountains, shut in on every side by steep, black precipices, where no breath of warmth could reach and from which there was no passage out to sunnier fields. So it was that, though the spirit of friendship soothed me for a time, it could not restore me. It came like a gentle visitation; it left, and I scarcely noticed the loss. The spirit of existence was dead within me; therefore do not be surprised that when it returned I did not welcome it more gladly, or when it departed I did not lament more bitterly the best gift of heaven—a friend.

The name of my friend was Woodville. I will briefly set down his history, so you may judge how cold my heart must have been not to be warmed by his eloquent words and tender sympathy; and how, too, since he was himself most unhappy, we were well matched to be a mutual consolation to one another—if I had not been hardened into stone by the Medusa head of Misery. Woodville’s misfortunes were not of the same inward core as mine; his grief was natural, not meant to ruin but to purify the heart, and from which, when its shadow had passed, he might shine forth again brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman, and he had received a classical education. He was among those very few whom fortune favors from their birth—those on whom she lavishes, without stint, every gift of intellect and person, and whom, under her special protection, no small imperfection, however slight, and no disappointment, however brief, is allowed to touch. It seemed she had shaped his mind into an excellence that no dross can stain, and his understanding was such that no mistake could twist it. His genius was transcendent; when it rose like a bright star in the east, all eyes turned toward it in admiration. He was a Poet. That title has been so often cheapened that it cannot convey what he truly was. He was like a poet of old, whom the muses seemed to crown even in his cradle, and on whose lips bees had fed. When he moved among other men, he appeared surrounded by a heavenly halo that set him apart from them and lifted him above them. It was his surpassing beauty, the dazzling fire of his eyes, and the richness of his speech—accents so full that they wrap the listener in silent, rapt wonder—that made him rise above all others, so that, in his presence, the rest seemed only formed to serve his superior excellence."}

