          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c2f5bb761d6d8667a4a656d4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But it is of little importance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI might as well ask,\u201d she replied, \u201cwhy, with such an obvious plan to offend and insult me, you chose to tell me that you liked me against your will, against your judgment, and even against your character. Was that not an excuse for rudeness, if I was rude? But I have other reasons to be provoked. You know I do. If my own feelings had not already turned against you\u2014if they had been indifferent, or even favorable\u2014do you think any consideration would have tempted me to accept the man who has been the means of ruining, perhaps forever, the happiness of a sister I love most dearly?\u201d\\n\\nAs she said this, Mr. Darcy changed color; but the emotion passed quickly. He listened without trying to interrupt her while she went on\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI have every reason in the world to think badly of you. No motive can excuse the unjust and ungenerous part you played there. You can\u2019t deny it\u2014you dare not, you cannot deny that you were the principal, if not the only, means of driving them apart: exposing one to the world\u2019s censure for caprice and instability, the other to its ridicule for disappointed hopes, and dragging them both into the most acute misery.\u201d\\n\\nShe paused, and with no small indignation saw that he was listening with an expression that showed he felt no remorse at all. He even looked at her with a smile that seemed to be made for disbelief.\\n\\n\u201cCan you deny that you did it?\u201d she repeated.\\n\\nWith an air of practiced calm, he answered, \u201cI have no wish to deny that I did everything in my power to separate my friend from your sister, or that I am pleased with the result. Toward him I have been kinder than toward myself.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth refused to pretend she had noticed the polite remark, but its meaning did not escape her, nor was it likely to soften her.\\n\\n\u201cBut it is not only this matter,\u201d she continued. \u201cMy dislike is based on more than what happened then. Long before it took place, my opinion of you was already formed. Your character was laid out in the account I received many months ago from Mr. Wickham. On that subject, what do you have to say? What imaginary act of friendship could you use here to defend yourself? Or what misrepresentation could you use here to mislead others?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou take an eager interest in that gentleman\u2019s affairs,\u201d Darcy said, in a less steady tone, with his color heightened.\\n\\n\u201cWho wouldn\u2019t feel concerned for him, knowing what his misfortunes have been?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis misfortunes!\u201d Darcy repeated contemptuously. \u201cYes, his misfortunes have been very great indeed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd the harm you\u2019ve done him,\u201d Elizabeth cried with force. \u201cYou\u2019ve reduced him to his present condition\u2014comparative poverty.\u201d\"}"
