{"request_id":"dbd0d5374d048f46d0365a26","rewritten_text":"Do not misunderstand me; I was never truly furious. I remained aware of my condition whenever my ungoverned thoughts seemed to drive me toward madness, and I never let them show themselves to anyone—only silence and solitude betrayed nothing. Those about me perceived none of it. They saw only a poor girl whose spirit was crushed, speaking in a low, gentle tone, and beneath whose lowered lids tears would sometimes slip away as she tried to conceal them. She was one who loved to be alone, and shrank from being noticed; who never smiled—no, I never smiled, and that was all.

At last, I escaped. I left my guardian’s house, and I was never heard of again; from the letters I left and from other circumstances, it was believed that I had resolved upon my own destruction. Therefore I was sought for with less diligence than might otherwise have been the case, and soon every trace and recollection of me was lost. I quitted London in a small vessel bound for a port in the north of England. And now that my design had succeeded, and I was entirely alone, peace returned to me. The sea lay calm, and the vessel went on gently; I sat upon deck beneath the open canopy of heaven, and it seemed to me I had become another creature. Not the wild, raving, and most wretched Mathilda, but a youthful hermitess devoted to seclusion, whose very bosom she must strive to keep clear of all tumult and unholy despair. There was the fanciful nunlike dress I had taken up; there was the knowledge that my existence was a secret known only to myself; and the solitude to which I was henceforth destined nursed gentle thoughts in my wounded heart. The breeze that stirred my hair revived me, and I watched with quiet eyes the sunbeams flashing on the waves, and the birds that darted over the water, barely touching it with their plumes. I slept without being troubled by dreams, and awoke refreshed, ready again to enjoy my tranquil freedom.

In four days we reached the harbour for which we were bound. I would not stay on the sea coast, but went inland at once. I had already arranged where I would live. It was to be a solitary house on a wide plain, far from other dwellings—so that I might command the whole horizon, and wander far without being molested by the sight of my fellow creatures. I was not a misanthrope, yet I felt that the mild current of my feelings depended upon my being alone. I fixed myself in a broad solitude. On a dreary heath strewn with stones, where short grass grew; and here and there a few rushes beside a small pool."}

