          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"059dfba1f6500818a8fa2fac\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Other chances to observe them would not fail to come. Anne soon found herself often in the company of all four together, enough to form an opinion, though she was too wise to admit it at home, where it would have satisfied neither husband nor wife. She thought Louisa was the favorite, but she also believed\u2014at least as far as she dared judge from what she remembered and had seen\u2014that Captain Wentworth was not truly in love with either of them. They were more in love with him, but it was not love. It was a kind of fever of admiration, and it might, and probably must, turn into love.\\n\\nCharles Hayter seemed to feel that he was being slighted, and yet Henrietta sometimes looked as if she were pulled in two directions between him and the others. Anne wished she had the power to tell them all what they were doing, and to point out the dangers they were bringing on themselves. She did not think anyone was being dishonest. The greatest comfort to her was to believe that Captain Wentworth was not in the least aware of the pain he was causing. There was no triumph in his manner, and no cruel kind of triumph either. He had probably never heard, and certainly had never considered, any claims that Charles Hayter might have. He was only wrong in accepting\u2014accepting, that must be the word\u2014the attention of two young women at once.\\n\\nAfter a short struggle, however, Charles Hayter seemed to give up. Three days passed without his coming once to Uppercross, which was a clear change. He even refused one regular invitation to dinner. And when Mr Musgrove found him on that occasion with several large books in front of him, Mr and Mrs Musgrove were sure that something could not be right. They spoke gravely about his studying himself to death. Mary hoped and believed that Henrietta had given him a clear dismissal, and her husband lived with the constant expectation of seeing him the next day. Anne could only think that Charles Hayter was wise.\\n\\nOne morning, at about this time, when Charles Musgrove and Captain Wentworth had gone shooting together, the sisters in the Cottage were sitting quietly at their work when the sisters from the Mansion-house came to the window.\\n\\nIt was a fine November day. The Miss Musgroves walked through the small grounds and stopped only to say that they were going on a long walk, so they decided Mary would not want to go with them. When Mary answered at once, with a touch of jealousy that they did not think her a good walker\u2014\u201cOh, yes, I should like to join you very much. I am very fond of a long walk\u201d\u2014Anne felt, from the look on the two girls\u2019 faces, that this was exactly what they did not want. She admired again the kind of necessity that seemed to come from the family habits: that everything had to be shared, and everything had to be done together, even when it was unwanted and inconvenient.\"}"
