          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1c9a67a99a2674e083d49baf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I summon you, spirits of the dead, and you, wandering ministers of vengeance, to help and lead me in what I must do. Let the accursed, infernal monster drink deep of agony; let him taste the despair that now torments me.\u201d\\n\\nI began my appeal with solemnity and a reverence so strong that I almost believed the shades of my murdered friends were listening and approving my devotion, but the furies seized me as I finished, and fury choked my words.\\n\\nThrough the stillness of night I was answered by a loud, devilish laugh. It struck my ears and lingered, heavy and long; the mountains rang it back, and it seemed to me that all of hell pressed around me with mockery and laughter. I might, in that instant, have been driven mad and ended my wretched life, were it not that my vow was heard and that I was kept for vengeance. The laughter faded, and then, with a voice well known and loathed\u2014seemingly right beside my ear\u2014came an audible whisper: \u201cI am content, miserable wretch! You have chosen to go on living, and I am content.\u201d\\n\\nI sprang toward the place from which the sound came, but the devil slipped beyond my reach. At once the broad face of the moon rose and shone full upon his ghastly, twisted form as he fled with speed beyond anything mortal.\\n\\nI followed him, and for many months this has been my work. With only a slight hint to guide me, I traced the windings of the Rhone, but in vain. The blue Mediterranean opened before me, and by some strange turn I saw the fiend enter by night and conceal himself in a vessel bound for the Black Sea. I took passage on the same ship, yet he escaped\u2014how, I cannot say.\\n\\nEven through the wilds of Tartary and Russia, though he still slips away from me, I have never ceased to pursue his trail. At times the peasants, frightened by this dreadful apparition, told me where he had gone; at times it was he himself