{"request_id":"96bd546fb300f90b6860aa6b","rewritten_text":"The next day he didn’t leave the house. In fact, he spent most of his time in his own room, sick with a wild terror of dying, and yet strangely indifferent to life itself. The awareness of being hunted, trapped, tracked down had begun to take over his mind. If the tapestry even trembled in the wind, he flinched. The dead leaves blown against the leaded panes seemed to him like his own wasted resolutions and wild regrets. When he closed his eyes, he saw again the sailor’s face peering through the mist-stained glass, and horror seemed to lay its hand on his heart once more.

But maybe it had only been his imagination that had summoned vengeance out of the night and set those hideous shapes of punishment before him. Real life was chaos, but there was something terribly logical in the imagination. It was the imagination that made remorse dog the steps of sin. It was the imagination that gave each crime its misshapen offspring. In the ordinary world of facts, the wicked weren’t punished, and the good weren’t rewarded. Success went to the strong; failure was pushed onto the weak. That was all. Besides, if any stranger had been prowling around the house, the servants or the keepers would have seen him. If any footprints had been found in the flowerbeds, the gardeners would have reported it. Yes—it had been only fancy. Sibyl Vane’s brother hadn’t come back to kill him. He had sailed away in his ship to founder in some winter sea. From him, at any rate, he was safe. The man didn’t even know who he was—he couldn’t know who he was. The mask of youth had saved him.

And yet if it had been only an illusion, how terrible it was to think that conscience could conjure up such fearful phantoms, give them visible form, and make them move before him! What kind of life would his be if, day and night, shadows of his crime kept peering at him from silent corners, mocking him from secret places, whispering in his ear as he sat down to eat, and waking him with icy fingers as he lay asleep? As the thought crept through his mind, he went pale with terror, and the air seemed to grow suddenly colder. Oh, in what wild hour of madness he had killed his friend! How ghastly the mere memory of the scene was. He saw it all again. Every hideous detail returned, with even greater horror. Out of the black cave of time, wrapped in scarlet, rose the image of his sin."}

