          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"09c8ccadb112fbab8f3d8631\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStarve you to death? Oh, Mr. Ghost\u2014no, Sir Simon\u2014are you hungry? I\u2019ve got a sandwich in my case. Would you like it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, thank you. I don\u2019t eat anything anymore; but it\u2019s very thoughtful of you all the same, and you\u2019re far nicer than the rest of your dreadful, impolite, common, dishonest family.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cStop!\u201d Virginia snapped, stamping her foot. \u201cYou\u2019re the rude one\u2014horrid and vulgar besides. And as for dishonesty, you know you stole the paints from my box to spruce up that ridiculous blood-stain in the library. First you took all my reds, even the vermilion, so I couldn\u2019t paint any more sunsets; then you grabbed the emerald-green and the chrome-yellow, and in the end I was left with nothing but indigo and Chinese white. That meant I could only manage moonlight pictures, which are always miserable to look at and quite hard to paint. I never reported you, though I was very annoyed, and the whole business was absurd\u2014who ever heard of emerald-green blood?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, really,\u201d said the Ghost, sounding rather subdued, \u201cwhat was I supposed to do? Getting real blood is a very difficult business these days, and since your brother started it all with his Paragon Detergent, I saw no reason why I shouldn\u2019t use your paints. And as for colour, that\u2019s always a question of taste. The Cantervilles, for instance, have blue blood\u2014the bluest in England; but I know you Americans don\u2019t care much for such things.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou know nothing about it. The best you can do is emigrate and improve your mind. My father would be only too glad to give you a free passage, and although there\u2019s a heavy duty on spirits of every sort, there won\u2019t be any trouble at the Custom House, since the officers are all Democrats. Once you\u2019re in New York, you\u2019re bound to be a great success. I know plenty of people there who would pay a hundred thousand dollars just to have a grandfather\u2014and far more than that for a family Ghost.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think I\u2019d care for America.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI suppose it\u2019s because we have no ruins and no curiosities,\u201d Virginia said with a touch of satire.\\n\\n\u201cNo ruins! No curiosities!\u201d the Ghost replied. \u201cYou\u2019ve got your navy and your manners.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood evening. I\u2019ll go and ask papa to give the twins an extra week\u2019s holiday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPlease don\u2019t go, Miss Virginia,\u201d he cried. \u201cI\u2019m so lonely and so miserable, and I truly don\u2019t know what to do. I want to go to sleep, and I can\u2019t.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s nonsense! You only have to go to bed and blow out the candle.\u201d\"}"
