          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"567d205c2123be10745e911b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 19th. This morning, to my great joy, at about nine o\u2019clock, the moon\u2019s surface was frightfully close, and my fears were at their highest point. At last, my condenser pump showed clear signs that the air around me had changed. By ten, I had good reason to think the air had become much denser. By eleven, the apparatus needed very little work. At twelve o\u2019clock, with some hesitation, I unscrewed the tourniquet. Finding no trouble from doing so, I finally opened the gum-elastic chamber and took it off the car.\\n\\nAs you might expect, spasms and a violent headache were the immediate results of such a sudden, dangerous experiment. But although breathing was difficult, it was not so bad that I was in danger of losing my life. I decided to endure it as best I could, because I would leave these problems behind me as I moved into the denser air near the moon. Still, my descent was extremely fast. Soon it became painfully clear that, even if I had not been wrong to expect an atmosphere denser in proportion to the moon\u2019s mass, I had been wrong about how dense it was\u2014at even the surface. It was not enough to hold up the great weight in the car of my balloon.\\n\\nThis should have been true, just as it is on Earth: the actual gravity of bodies on either planet should match the ratio of atmospheric condensation. But since it was not, my sudden fall proved it. The only explanation I can give is that there must be geological disturbances of the kind I mentioned earlier. In any case, I was now very close to the planet and coming down with terrifying force.\\n\\nI wasted no time. I threw overboard my ballast first, then my water-kegs, then my condensing apparatus and the gum-elastic chamber, and finally everything else in the car. It made no difference. I kept falling with horrible speed, and I was now less than half a mile from the surface. So, as a last resort, I got rid of my coat, hat, and boots. Then I cut the car loose from the balloon itself, even though it was heavy. Holding on with both hands to the netting, I barely had time to see that the land, as far as I could see, was covered with tiny houses. Before I could take in more, I tumbled headlong into the middle of a strange-looking city, right into a vast crowd of ugly little people. None of them said a word or even tried to help me. They just stood there like a bunch of idiots, grinning in a ridiculous way, and staring at me and my balloon from the side, with their arms stuck akimbo.\u201d\"}"
