          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eaf99e2faba4082ad26fe811\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Poor Jack was later shot in a duel by Lord Canterville on Wandsworth Common, and Lady Barbara died of a broken heart at Tunbridge Wells before the year had run out; so, in every respect, it had been a great success. Still, it was an exceedingly trying \u201cmake-up,\u201d if I may borrow a theatrical phrase for one of the greatest mysteries of the supernatural, or, in a more scientific vein, of the higher-natural world, and it kept him busy for a full three hours while he made his preparations. At last everything was in readiness, and he was pleased enough with how he looked. The big leather riding-boots that belonged with the costume were just a touch too large, and he could only turn up one of the two horse-pistols; yet, on the whole, he felt fairly satisfied, and at a quarter past one he slipped out of the wainscoting and stole down the corridor. Reaching the room occupied by the twins\u2014which, I should add, was called the Blue Bed Chamber because of the colour of its hangings\u2014he found the door left slightly ajar. Hoping to make a striking entrance, he threw it wide, only for a heavy jug of water to tumble straight down on him, soaking him to the skin and missing his left shoulder by a couple of inches. At the same instant he heard muffled shrieks of laughter coming from the four-post bed. The shock to his nerves was so severe that he bolted back to his room as fast as he could, and the following day he was laid up with a bad cold. The only thing that in any way comforted him afterward was that he had not brought his head along; had he done so, the results might well have been far more serious.\\n\\nHe now abandoned any real expectation of frightening this impudent American family, and for the most part contented himself with skulking through the passages in list slippers, a thick red muffler about his throat to guard against draughts, and a small arquebuse in case the twins should come at him. The last blow fell on the 19th of September. He had gone down to the great entrance-hall, confident that there, at least, he would not be bothered, and he amused himself by tossing off satirical remarks at the large Saroni photographs of the United States Minister and his wife, which had now replaced the Canterville family portraits. He was dressed plainly but neatly in a long shroud, flecked with churchyard mould, had bound his jaw with a strip of yellow linen, and carried a small lantern and a sexton\u2019s spade.\"}"
