          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bca0b86cf039fa70efdf348c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dear me, she was a master at talking. It would not do for me to remark that it was getting very late, that I was worn out, and that I felt heavy with sleep. I only wished I had gone to bed when I still might. Now I had to endure it; there was no other course. So she went on, tinkling along and along, through the otherwise deep and ghostly hush of the sleeping castle, until presently there came\u2014seemingly from far below us\u2014a distant sound, like a muffled shriek, and with an expression of agony in it that made my skin creep. The queen halted, and her eyes brightened with delight; she bent her graceful head the way a bird does when it listens. The noise worked its way back up through the stillness.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is it?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cIt is truly a stubborn soul, and endureth long. It is many hours now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEndureth what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe rack. Come\u2014ye shall see a blithe sight. If he yield not his secret now, ye shall see him torn asunder.\u201d\\n\\nWhat a sleek, smooth little hellion she was; and yet how steady and calm she seemed, while the cords all down my legs were aching in sympathy with that man\u2019s suffering. With mailed guards, torches flaring, we marched through echoing corridors and down stone stairways that were dank and dripping, all the while smelling of mould and of ages of imprisoned night\u2014a cold, uncanny journey, and a long one, made no shorter or pleasanter by the sorceress\u2019s talk, which concerned this sufferer and his crime. He had been accused by an anonymous informer of killing a stag in the royal preserves. I said:\\n\\n\u201cAnonymous testimony isn\u2019t the right thing, your Highness. It would be fairer to set the accused face-to-face with the accuser.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI had not considered it, as though it were of little consequence. But if I would, I could not; for the accuser came masked by night, told the forester, and straightway got him taken away again, so that the forester knoweth him not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen is this Unknown the only person who saw the stag killed?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMarry, no man saw the killing, but this Unknown saw this hardy wretch near to the spot where the stag lay, and came with right loyal zeal and betrayed him to the forester.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo the Unknown was near the dead stag, too? Isn\u2019t it possible he did the killing himself? His loyal zeal\u2014behind a mask\u2014looks just a shade suspicious. But what is your highness\u2019s notion for racking the prisoner? Where is the gain?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe will not confess, else; and then were his soul lost. For his crime his life is forfeited by the law\u2014and of a surety will I see that he payeth it!\u2014but it were peril to my own soul to let him die unconfessed and unabsolved.\u201d\"}"
