          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"37b03a2fe67a2b507932ade1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBasil, this is astonishing! I simply have to see Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\nHallward rose from his seat and paced the garden. After a while he returned. \u201cHarry,\u201d he said, \u201cto me Dorian Gray is nothing more than a spur to art. You may see nothing in him; I see everything. He is never more alive in my work than when there is no picture of him there. As I\u2019ve said, he is a hint of a new manner. I catch him in the sweep of certain lines, in the grace and fine distinctions of certain colours. That\u2019s all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen why not show his portrait?\u201d Lord Henry asked.\\n\\n\u201cBecause, without meaning to, I\u2019ve slipped into it some trace of all this odd artistic worship\u2014of which, naturally, I\u2019ve never cared to tell him. He knows nothing of it. He shall never know. Still, the world might suspect it, and I won\u2019t lay my soul bare to their shallow curiosity. My heart will never be set beneath their microscope. There\u2019s too much of me in it, Harry\u2014too much of me!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPoets aren\u2019t nearly as delicate as you,\u201d said Henry. \u201cThey know how useful passion is for getting printed. These days a broken heart will find its way into countless editions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI despise them for it,\u201d Hallward burst out. \u201cAn artist ought to make beautiful things, but put nothing of his own life into them. We live in an age where men treat art as though it were meant to be a kind of autobiography. We\u2019ve lost the pure sense of beauty. Someday I\u2019ll show the world what it is; and for that reason the world shall never see my portrait of Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you\u2019re mistaken, Basil, though I won\u2019t argue with you. Only the intellectually stranded ever argue. Tell me\u2014does Dorian Gray care much for you?\u201d\\n\\nThe painter paused, weighing his words. \u201cHe likes me,\u201d he answered at last, \u201cI\u2019m sure he likes me. Of course I flatter him shamelessly. I take a peculiar satisfaction in telling him things I know I\u2019ll regret saying. Most of the time he is charming to me, and we sit in the studio and talk of a thousand matters. But now and then, he is dreadfully thoughtless, and it seems he truly enjoys hurting me. Then I feel, Harry, that I\u2019ve given away my entire soul to someone who treats it as if it were a flower to pin on his coat\u2014just decoration to feed his vanity, an ornament for a summer day.\u201d\"}"
