          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"36ba7e4ad186fe4844d74638\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But then, how was I\u2014this poor, dazed village boy\u2014to steer clear of that splendid contradiction into which even the best and wisest men slip day after day?\\n\\n\u201cIt may be all true enough,\u201d I told Biddy, \u201cbut I can\u2019t help admiring her dreadfully.\u201d\\n\\nIn any case, when that thought came over me, I turned right over on my face, seized a handful of hair on either side of my head, and yanked it hard. All the while I knew the folly of my own heart\u2014so frantic, so misplaced\u2014that I was fully aware it would have been justice if I\u2019d lifted my face by the hair and knocked it against the pebbles as punishment for being such an idiot.\\n\\nBiddy was the wisest of girls, and she made no further attempt to argue with me. She laid her hand on mine\u2014steady and comfortable, though roughened by work\u2014and drew them gently out of my hair, one after another. Then, with my face still pressed against my sleeve, she gave my shoulder a soft pat meant to soothe, and I cried a little, just as I had done in the brewery yard, feeling\u2014somehow, dimly\u2014that I\u2019d been ill-used by someone, or by everyone; I couldn\u2019t say which.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m glad of one thing,\u201d said Biddy. \u201cThat you\u2019ve felt you could trust me, Pip. And I\u2019m glad of another: that you know you can rely on my keeping it safe, and on my always deserving that trust so far as I can. If your first teacher\u2014dear me, such a poor one, and so much in need of being taught herself\u2014had been your teacher at this very time, she thinks she\u2019d know what lesson she\u2019d set. But it would be a hard one to learn, and you\u2019ve already gone past her, so it\u2019s no use now.\u201d With a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank. Then, with a fresh and pleasant shift in her voice, she asked, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I cried, as I got up, slipped my arm around her neck, and kissed her, \u201cI shall always tell you everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly till you\u2019re a gentleman,\u201d said Biddy.\\n\\n\u201cYou know I never shall be\u2014so that\u2019s settled. And besides, I don\u2019t have to tell you anything, because you already know everything I know, as I told you the other night at home.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Biddy, in a whisper, as she looked off toward the ships. Then, with that same former pleasant change, she asked again, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\nI told Biddy we would walk a little farther, and we did. The summer afternoon faded into summer evening, and it was truly beautiful.\"}"
