{"request_id":"d2cd8214e1fe069a8d0da01d","rewritten_text":"The next morning, when the Otis family gathered for breakfast, they talked at length about the ghost. The United States Minister was naturally a little annoyed to learn that his gift had not been accepted. “I have no wish,” he said, “to hurt the ghost in any personal way. And I must say that, after how long he has been in this house, it doesn’t seem polite to throw pillows at him.” This was a fair point, and I’m sorry to say the twins burst out laughing. “On the other hand,” he went on, “if he really refuses to use the Rising Sun Lubricator, then we will have to take his chains away from him. It would be impossible to sleep, with that noise going on outside the bedrooms.”

For the rest of the week, though, they were left alone. The only thing that drew attention was the constant return of the blood-stain on the library floor. That was strange, because Mr. Otis always locked the door at night, and the windows were kept tightly barred. People also commented on how the stain seemed to change color like a chameleon. Some mornings it was a dull, almost Indian, red. Then it would turn vermilion. After that, it might become a rich purple. Once, when they came down for family prayers, held in the simple rites of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, they found it bright emerald-green. These shifting colors amused everyone, and each evening they made bets about what it would be next. The only one who didn’t join in was little Virginia. For some reason she was always upset by the blood-stain, and she nearly cried the morning it was emerald-green.

The ghost appeared again on Sunday night. Not long after they had gone to bed, they were suddenly startled by a terrible crash in the hall. They rushed downstairs and found that a large suit of old armor had come loose from its stand and fallen onto the stone floor. In a high-backed chair sat the Canterville ghost, rubbing his knees with an expression of intense pain. The twins, who had brought their pea-shooters with them, immediately fired two pellets at him, with the kind of accurate aim that only comes from long practice under a writing-master. The United States Minister then covered him with his revolver and, following Californian etiquette, told him to hold up his hands. The ghost sprang up with a wild shriek of rage, swept through them like a mist, and as he passed he put out Washington Otis’s candle, leaving them all in complete darkness."}

