content.text:
{"request_id":"ef1185aa186cfce9f5050233","rewritten_text":"FOUR days after those odd happenings, a funeral set out from Canterville Chase at about eleven o’clock in the night. The hearse was drawn by eight black horses, each wearing on its head a great tuft of nodding ostrich-plumes, while the leaden coffin lay beneath a rich purple pall, embroidered in gold with the Canterville coat-of-arms. Alongside the hearse and the coaches walked the servants, carrying lighted torches, and the entire procession was striking in the extreme. Lord Canterville was the chief mourner; he had come up specially from Wales to be present, and sat in the first carriage with little Virginia. After them came the United States Minister and his wife, then Washington with the three boys, and in the last carriage rode Mrs. Umney. It was commonly thought that, since she had been terrified by the ghost for more than fifty years, she had every right to see the end of him. In the corner of the churchyard, just beneath the old yew-tree, a deep grave had been dug, and the service was read with great solemnity by the Rev. Augustus Dampier. When the ceremony ended, the servants, following an old custom of the Canterville family, put out their torches; and as the coffin was being lowered into the grave, Virginia stepped forward and laid upon it a large cross of white and pink almond-blossoms. At that moment the moon slipped out from behind a cloud, pouring its silent silver over the little churchyard, and from a distant copse a nightingale began to sing. She recalled the ghost’s account of the Garden of Death; her eyes filled with tears, and during the drive home she could scarcely bring herself to say much at all.

The following morning, before Lord Canterville went up to town, Mr. Otis had an interview with him regarding the jewels the ghost had given to Virginia. They were quite magnificent—above all, a particular ruby necklace with an old Venetian setting—which was indeed a splendid example of sixteenth-century workmanship; and their value was so great that Mr. Otis felt real scruples about letting his daughter accept them.

“My lord,” he said, “I understand that in this country mortmain is taken to cover trinkets as well as land, and it seems plain to me that these jewels are, or ought to be, heirlooms in your family. I must therefore ask you to take them with you to London, and to treat them simply as part of your property, restored to you under certain strange conditions. As for my daughter, she is only a child, and, I am happy to say, has as yet but little taste for such ornaments of idle luxury.”"}

