          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"76655b0749de6b1b475c57d4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the days of my youth, discontent never once took hold of my thoughts; and if _ennui_ ever did assail me, the sight of whatever is beautiful in nature, or the contemplation of what is excellent and sublime in the works of mankind, would still draw my heart toward it and give a kind of spring to my spirits. But I am now a blasted tree: the bolt has entered my soul. Then I understood that I should live only to show what I shall soon no longer be\u2014a wretched spectacle of ruined humanity, pitiful to others and unbearable to myself.\\n\\nWe spent a considerable time at Oxford, wandering through its neighbourhood and trying to mark out every place that could be connected with the most stirring period of English history. These small excursions of discovery were often prolonged by the successive objects that kept presenting themselves. We went to the tomb of the illustrious Hampden, and to the field where that patriot fell. For a moment my soul rose above its degrading, miserable fears, to dwell on the divine notions of liberty and self-sacrifice which those scenes served as monuments and reminders. For an instant I dared to cast off my chains and survey my surroundings with a freer, higher spirit; yet the iron had already eaten into my flesh, and I sank again, trembling and hopeless, back into my wretched self.\\n\\nWe quitted Oxford with regret, and made our next resting-place Matlock. The countryside about this village bore, in a still greater degree, the look of Switzerland; only everything there is on a smaller scale, and the green hills lack the crown of far-off white Alps that always attend the pine-clad mountains of my native land. We visited the remarkable cave and the little cabinets of natural history, where the curiosities are arranged in the same fashion as in the collections at Servox and Chamounix. The very last name made me shudder when Henry pronounced it, and I was eager to leave Matlock, since that dreadful scene was thus bound up with it.\\n\\nFrom Derby, continuing northward, we remained two months in Cumberland and Westmorland. I could now almost persuade myself that I was among the Swiss mountains. The small remnants of snow that still lingered on the northern slopes, the lakes, and the dash of the rocky streams were sights at once familiar and dear to me. Here, too, we formed some acquaintances, who nearly managed to lure me into happiness. Clerval\u2019s pleasure was, in proportion, greater than mine; his mind widened in the company of men of talent, and he discovered in his own nature larger powers and resources than he had ever supposed he possessed while he kept to those beneath him.\"}"
