          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"618a5985204ed9822a342326\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The next day the ghost was very weak and exhausted. The terrible excitement of the last four weeks was starting to show. His nerves were completely shattered, and he startled at the smallest sound. For five days he kept to his room, and at last he decided to drop the whole point of the bloodstain on the library floor. If the Otis family didn\u2019t want it, then clearly they didn\u2019t deserve it. They were evidently people living on a low, material level of existence, and quite incapable of appreciating the symbolic value of sensuous phenomena. The question of phantasmal apparitions, and the development of astral bodies, was, of course, a different matter\u2014something really not under his control. It was his solemn duty to appear in the corridor once a week, and to gibber from the large oriel window on the first and third Wednesday of every month, and he couldn\u2019t see how he could honorably escape those obligations.\\n\\nIt was true that his life had been very evil, but on the other hand he was most conscientious in everything connected with the supernatural. For the next three Saturdays, accordingly, he walked the corridor as usual between midnight and three o\u2019clock, taking every possible precaution to make sure he was neither heard nor seen. He removed his boots, stepped as lightly as he could on the old worm-eaten boards, wore a large black velvet cloak, and made sure to use the Rising Sun Lubricator for oiling his chains. I have to admit it took him quite a bit of effort to get himself to adopt this last method of protection. Still, one night, while the family were at dinner, he slipped into Mr. Otis\u2019s bedroom and stole the bottle. At first he felt a little humiliated, but later he was sensible enough to see that there was a lot to be said for the invention, and that\u2014at least to a certain degree\u2014it served his purpose.\\n\\nEven so, he wasn\u2019t left