          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"70ca4c81ed22ca5237a70d2b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He slipped out of the wall paneling, wearing an evil smile on his cruel, wrinkled mouth. The moon hid behind a cloud as he crept past the big oriel window, where his own arms and those of his murdered wife were shown in blue and gold. He kept gliding on, like an evil shadow, and even the darkness seemed to hate him as he went by. Once he thought he heard someone call, so he stopped. But it was only a dog barking from the Red Farm, and he went on, muttering strange curses from the sixteenth century. Now and then he waved a rusty dagger through the night air.\\n\\nAt last he reached the corner of the passage that led to luckless Washington\u2019s room. He paused there for a moment. The wind stirred his long gray hair, and it twisted around his head, making the nameless horror of the dead man\u2019s shroud look even more twisted and unreal. Then the clock struck the quarter, and he felt the time had come. He chuckled to himself and turned the corner. But the moment he did, he let out a terrified wail, stumbled backward, and hid his pale face in his long, bony hands.\\n\\nRight in front of him stood a horrible spectre, perfectly still, like something carved in stone, and monstrous, like a madman\u2019s dream. Its head was bald and shining. Its face was round, fat, and white. Its hideous laughter seemed to twist its features into a grin that would never end. From its eyes, red rays streamed out. Its mouth was a wide pit of fire. A dreadful garment, like his own, wrapped its Titan-sized body in silent snow. On its chest was a placard with strange writing in old characters\u2014something like a scroll of shame, a record of wild sins, or an awful calendar of crime. With its right hand, it held up a gleaming falchion.\\n\\nHe had never seen a ghost before, so of course he was terrified. After a quick look at the phantom, he ran back to his room. He tripped over his long winding-sheet as he hurried down the corridor, and finally he dropped the rusty dagger into the Minister\u2019s jack-boots. The butler found it the next morning. Once he was alone in his own apartment, he threw himself onto a small pallet-bed and covered his face with the clothes.\\n\\nAfter a time, though, the brave old Canterville spirit took over again. He decided he would go and speak to the other ghost as soon as it was daylight. So, just as dawn began to touch the hills with silver, he returned toward the spot where he had first seen the grisly phantom. After all, he thought, two ghosts were better than one, and with the help of his new friend, he might be able to deal with the twins safely.\"}"
