{"request_id":"ef0096521b4a9743d78a9291","rewritten_text":"He said that he was wasting his time where he was, and that letters from the friends he had made in London urged him to return so they could finish the negotiation they had started for his Indian business. He could not delay his departure any longer. But since his trip to London might be followed, even sooner than he had expected, by his longer voyage, he asked me to give him as much of my time and company as I could spare. He therefore begged me to leave my lonely island and meet him at Perth, so we could travel south together. This letter brought me back to life in a way, and I decided to leave my island after two days.

But before I left, I had to do something that I dreaded. I had to pack up my chemical instruments, and to do that I had to go into the room where my hateful work had taken place. I had to handle the tools whose sight made me sick. The next morning, at daybreak, I gathered enough courage to unlock the door of my laboratory. The remains of the half-finished creature I had destroyed were scattered across the floor, and I almost felt as if I had torn apart a living human body. I stopped for a moment to steady myself, then went into the room. With a trembling hand, I carried the instruments out. Still, I thought I should not leave the remains of my work where the peasants might find them and become horrified or suspicious. So I put everything into a basket, along with a large number of stones, and I decided to throw it into the sea that same night. In the meantime, I sat on the beach, cleaning and arranging my chemical equipment.

Nothing could show a greater change in my feelings than what had happened since the night the dæmon appeared. Before, I had seen my promise as something I had to carry out, no matter what it would cost me, and I felt only dark despair. Now it was as if a film had been lifted from my eyes, and I could finally see clearly. The thought of starting my labors again never even crossed my mind. The threat I had heard stayed in my thoughts, but I did not let myself believe that my own choice could prevent it. I had already decided in my mind that creating another being like the fiend I had first made would be the most selfish and terrible act. I pushed away every thought that could lead to any other conclusion."}

