          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2fbeefd4a8ca4df1346b345a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGood-bye, Uncle George. I\u2019ll be late for lunch if I stay any longer. Thanks for giving me the information I wanted. I always like to know everything about my new friends, and nothing about my old ones.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere are you having lunch, Harry?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt Aunt Agatha\u2019s. I\u2019ve asked myself and Mr. Gray. He is her latest _prot\u00e9g\u00e9_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHumph! Tell your Aunt Agatha, Harry, not to bother me any more with her charity appeals. I\u2019m sick of them. The good woman seems to think I have nothing to do but write cheques for her silly causes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right, Uncle George, I\u2019ll tell her, but it won\u2019t make any difference. Philanthropic people lose all sense of humanity. It\u2019s their defining trait.\u201d\\n\\nThe old gentleman growled approvingly and rang the bell for his servant. Lord Henry walked past the low arcade into Burlington Street and turned his steps toward Berkeley Square.\\n\\nSo that was the story of Dorian Gray\u2019s parentage. Even though it had been told to him in such a crude way, it still stirred him with the suggestion of a strange, almost modern romance. A beautiful woman risking everything for a mad passion. A few wild weeks of happiness cut short by a hideous, treacherous crime. Months of voiceless agony, and then a child born in pain. The mother snatched away by death, the boy left to solitude and the tyranny of an old, loveless man. Yes; it was an interesting background. It shaped the boy, made him, in a sense, even more perfect. Behind every exquisite thing that exists, there is something tragic. Worlds have to be in turmoil, so that even the meanest flower can bloom.... And how charming he had been at dinner the night before\u2014how, with startled eyes and lips parted in frightened pleasure, he had sat opposite him at the club, the red candle shades staining the awakening wonder of his face to a richer rose. Talking to him was like playing on an exquisite violin. He responded to every touch and thrill of the bow.... There was something terribly enthralling in the act of exerting influence. No other activity was like it. To project one\u2019s soul into some gracious form, and let it linger there for a moment; to hear one\u2019s own intellectual views echoed back with all the added music of passion and youth; to pour one\u2019s temperament into another as though it were a subtle fluid or a strange perfume\u2014there was real joy in that, perhaps the most satisfying joy left to us in an age so limited and vulgar as our own, an age grossly given to the flesh in its pleasures, and grossly common in its aims.... He was a marvelous type, too\u2014this lad whom, by such a curious chance, he had met in Basil\u2019s studio, or could, at any rate, be fashioned into a marvelous type.\"}"
