{"request_id":"3dbd2ce462812a9cbf5dc484","rewritten_text":"I uncovered it, and a gentle breeze quickly fanned it into a flame. I noticed this too, and I made a fan out of branches, which helped the embers catch again when they were almost out. When night came again, I was pleased to find that the fire gave light as well as heat. I also learned that this discovery could help me with food. Some of the scraps the travelers had left behind had been roasted, and they tasted far better than the berries I gathered from the trees. So I decided to cook my food the same way, putting it on the live embers. The berries spoiled when I did this, but the nuts and roots turned out much better.

“Food, however, soon became scarce. I often spent the whole day searching in vain for a few acorns to ease the pain of hunger. When I finally found some, I decided to leave the place I had been living in and look for somewhere where my few needs could be met more easily. During this move, I was very upset about losing the fire I had gotten by accident, and I did not know how to make it again. I spent several hours thinking seriously about the problem, but I had to give up trying to solve it. Then, wrapped in my cloak, I walked across the wood toward the setting sun. I wandered for three days, and at last I reached open country. A heavy snowfall had happened the night before, and the fields were covered in one unbroken white. The sight was bleak, and the cold, wet ground chilled my feet.

“It was about seven in the morning, and I was eager to find food and shelter. At last I saw a small hut on higher ground. It must have been built for a shepherd. To me, it was a new and strange sight, so I studied the building with great curiosity. I found the door open and went inside. An old man was sitting there near a fire, cooking his breakfast. When he heard a noise, he turned, saw me, shrieked loudly, and then ran out of the hut across the fields with a speed his weak body hardly seemed able to manage. His appearance was unlike anything I had ever seen, and his sudden escape surprised me. Still, I was delighted by the hut itself. Snow and rain could not get in, the ground was dry, and it seemed to me then as perfect and heavenly a place to hide as Pandæmonium looked to the dæmons of hell after their suffering in the lake of fire.”"}

