          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4cfaec41ec11d8084bf7b081\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI\u2019m with you. It\u2019s the best thing. Just hold hands and swear that we\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh no, not for this. That\u2019s fine for little, trashy common things\u2014especially with girls, because they always turn on you anyway, and they blab if they get mad\u2014but there ought to be writing about a big thing like this. And blood.\u201d\\n\\nTom\u2019s whole self seemed to applaud the idea. It was deep, and dark, and awful; the hour, the circumstances, the surroundings, all matched it. He picked up a clean pine shingle lying in the moonlight, took a little fragment of \u201cred keel\u201d from his pocket, set the moon on his work, and painfully scratched out these lines, pressing each slow down-stroke by clamping his tongue between his teeth, then easing up on the up-strokes.\\n\\n\u201cHuck Finn and Tom Sawyer swear they will keep mum about This and They wish They may Drop down dead in Their Tracks if They ever Tell and Rot.\u201d\\n\\nHuckleberry was filled with admiration for Tom\u2019s skill in writing, and for the grandeur of his language. He immediately took a pin from his lapel and was about to prick his flesh, but Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cWait! Don\u2019t do that. A pin\u2019s brass. It might have verdigris on it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s verdigris?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s poison. That\u2019s what it is. You swallow some of it once\u2014you\u2019ll see.\u201d\\n\\nSo Tom unwound the thread from one of his needles, and each boy pricked the ball of his thumb and squeezed out a drop of blood. After many squeezes, Tom finally managed to sign his initials, using the ball of his little finger as a pen. Then he showed Huckleberry how to make an H and an F, and the oath was complete. They buried the shingle close to the wall, with grim ceremonies and chants, and the fetters that bound their tongues were considered locked, with the key thrown away.\\n\\nA figure