          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b2acf08472680fb38f333c7e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was at that instant that my eyes\u2014and his as well\u2014rested on a bit of parchment, which I at the time took for paper. It lay half covered by the sand, with one corner jutting up. Close to where we came upon it, I noticed the remains of the hull of what seemed to have been a ship\u2019s long boat. The wreck must have been there a very great while; the likeness to boat timbers could scarcely be made out.\\n\\n\u201cWell, Jupiter took up the parchment, wrapped the beetle in it, and handed it to me. Not long after, we set out for home, and on the way we met Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014. I showed him the insect, and he begged me to let him carry it to the fort. When I agreed, he at once put it into his waistcoat pocket\u2014without the parchment that had wrapped it, which I kept holding in my hand while he examined it. Perhaps he feared I might change my mind, and thought it safer to secure the prize at once; you know how eager he is about everything connected with Natural History. Yet, without my being aware of it, I must have put the parchment into my own pocket at the same time.\\n\\n\u201cYou recall that when I went to the table, intending to make a sketch of the beetle, I found no paper where it was usually kept. I looked in the drawer, and there was none. I searched my pockets, hoping to come across an old letter, when my hand struck the parchment. I set down the exact way it came into my possession, because the circumstances impressed me with unusual force.\\n\\n\u201cNo doubt you\u2019ll call me fanciful\u2014but I had already formed a sort of connexion. I had joined together two links of a great chain. There was a boat lying on a sea-coast, and not far from it a parchment\u2014not paper\u2014with a skull drawn upon it. You will, of course, ask, \u2018where is the connexion?\u2019 I answer that the skull, or death\u2019s-head, is the well-known emblem of the pirate. The death\u2019s-head flag is raised in all their engagements.\\n\\n\u201cI have said the scrap was parchment, not paper. Parchment is lasting\u2014almost imperishable. Things of little consequence are seldom committed to parchment; for ordinary drawing or writing, it is not nearly so suitable as paper. This thought led me to suspect some meaning\u2014some relevancy\u2014in the death\u2019s-head. I did not overlook, either, the shape of the parchment. Though one of its corners had been destroyed by some accident, it could be seen that the original form was oblong.\u201d\"}"
