          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"992e83964d71bb5ffca7657d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But it is very remarkable that she should always hear Jane better than she hears me. Jane speaks so clearly! Still, she will not find her grandmama any less deaf than she was two years ago. That is saying a great deal at my mother\u2019s age, and it really has been a full two years since she was here. We have never gone so long without seeing her, and as I was telling Mrs. Cole, we shall hardly know how to make enough of her now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you expecting Miss Fairfax here soon?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes; next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u2014that must be a very great pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you. You are very kind. Yes, next week. Everyone is so surprised, and everyone says the same nice things. I am sure she will be as happy to see her friends at Highbury as they can be to see her. Yes, Friday or Saturday; she cannot say which, because Colonel Campbell will want the carriage for himself on one of those days. It is so good of them to send her the whole way! But they always do, you know. Oh yes, Friday or Saturday next. That is what she writes about. That is why she writes out of her usual schedule, as we call it; because, in the normal way of things, we would not have heard from her before next Tuesday or Wednesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, so I imagined. I was afraid there might be little chance of my hearing anything about Miss Fairfax today.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo kind of you! No, we would not have heard, if it had not been for this particular reason\u2014her being on her way here so soon. My mother is delighted\u2014for she will be with us for at least three months. Three months, she says, positively, and I am going to have the pleasure of reading to you. The situation is this: the Campbells are going to Ireland. Mrs. Dixon has persuaded her father and mother to come over and see her right away. They had not planned to go until the summer, but she is so eager to see them again. Since she married last October, she has never been away from them for more than a week, which must make it very strange to be in different kingdoms\u2014though I mean different countries. So she wrote a very urgent letter to her mother\u2014or her father, I truly do not know which it was, but we will see when Jane\u2019s letter arrives. She wrote in Mr. Dixon\u2019s name as well as her own, to urge their coming over directly. They will meet them in Dublin and then take them back to their country home, Baly-craig, a beautiful place, I imagine.\u201d\"}"
