          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d318603836ff7fad9aa69dd4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Not a single word, however, passed his lips in reference to it, until their visitor had taken his leave for the night; but the moment he was gone, he turned to his daughter and said,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cJane, I congratulate you. You are going to be a very happy woman.\u201d\\n\\nJane went to him at once, kissed him, and thanked him for his kindness.\\n\\n\u201cYou are a good girl,\u201d he answered, \u201cand I take real satisfaction in believing you will be settled so happily. I have not the least doubt that you will do very well together. Your tempers are by no means unlike. You are both so yielding, that nothing will ever be decided; so easy, that every servant will manage to cheat you; and so liberal, that you will always run beyond your income.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI hope not. Any imprudence or thoughtlessness in money matters would be unforgivable in _me_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cRun beyond their income! My dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d cried his wife, \u201cwhat are you saying? Why, he has four or five thousand a year, and very likely more.\u201d Then, turning to her daughter, \u201cOh, my dear, dear Jane, I am so delighted! I know I shan\u2019t sleep a wink all night. I always knew it would come to this. I told you it must be so at last. I was certain you could not be so beautiful for nothing! I remember, the very first time I saw him, when he came into Hertfordshire last year, I thought it was very likely you would be brought together. Oh, he is the handsomest young man that ever was seen!\u201d\\n\\nWickham and Lydia were soon forgotten. Jane was her favourite child, beyond all comparison. At that moment she cared for no one else. Her younger sisters soon began to press her with hopes of other sources of happiness they might, in time, be able to enjoy.\\n\\nMary asked for the use of the library at Netherfield; and Kitty pleaded, with all her might, for a few balls there every winter.\\n\\nFrom this time, Bingley was, of course, a daily visitor at Longbourn; he came often before breakfast, and always stayed until after supper; except when some barbarous neighbour\u2014one whom he could never be made to detest enough\u2014had given him an invitation to dinner, which he felt himself bound to accept.\\n\\nElizabeth now had little opportunity to talk with her sister; for while he was there, Jane had no attention to spare for anyone else: yet in those intervals of separation, which must sometimes occur, she found herself of considerable use to both. When Jane was absent, he always attached himself to Elizabeth, for the pleasure of talking about her; and when Bingley was gone, Jane constantly sought the same relief.\\n\\n\u201cHe has made me so happy,\u201d said she, one evening, \u201cby telling me that he was quite unaware of my being in town last spring!\u201d\"}"
