          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d2d86cfb8f38266ac612b08b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGit up! What you \u2019bout?\u201d\\n\\nI opened my eyes and looked around, trying to figure out where I was. It was after sunrise, and I\u2019d been asleep hard. Pap was standing over me, looking sour and sick. He said:\\n\\n\u201cWhat you doin\u2019 with this gun?\u201d\\n\\nI figured he didn\u2019t know anything about what he\u2019d been doing, so I told him, \u201cSomebody tried to get in, so I was waiting for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy didn\u2019t you roust me out?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI tried, but I couldn\u2019t. I couldn\u2019t move you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, all right. Don\u2019t stand there talking all day. Get out and see if there\u2019s a fish on the lines for breakfast. I\u2019ll be along in a minute.\u201d\\n\\nHe unlocked the door, and I went out along the riverbank. I saw bits of limbs and other stuff floating down, and some bark too. That meant the river had started to rise. I figured I\u2019d have a good time now if I was over in town. The June rise always brought me luck. As soon as it starts here, cordwood comes floating down, along with pieces of log rafts\u2014sometimes a dozen logs together. All you have to do is catch them and sell them to the wood yards and the sawmill.\\n\\nI walked up the bank, keeping one eye out for Pap and the other on what the rise might bring. Then, all at once, a canoe came drifting in. It was a fine one, about thirteen or fourteen feet long, riding high like a duck. I dove headfirst off the bank like a frog, clothes and all, and swam for the canoe. I expected someone might be in it, because people sometimes did that to trick you. When a man pulled a skiff out, they\u2019d pop up and laugh at him. But that didn\u2019t happen this time.\\n\\nIt was really a drift canoe. I climbed in and paddled it to shore. I thought, the old man will be glad when he sees this. It\u2019s worth ten dollars. But when I got to shore, Pap wasn\u2019t there yet. As I pushed the canoe into a small creek, like a narrow gully, covered over with vines and willows, I got another idea. I decided to hide it well. Instead of running off into the woods, I\u2019d take the canoe down the river about fifty miles, find a place to camp, and stay there for good. Then I wouldn\u2019t have to keep dealing with the hard walking.\\n\\nIt was pretty close to the shanty. I thought I could hear the old man coming the whole time, but I hid the canoe first. Then I stepped out and looked around through the willows. The old man was down the path a ways, aiming his gun at a bird.\"}"
