{"request_id":"8d1351919924cda441ff5834","rewritten_text":"“By eight o’clock, I had actually reached an altitude of seventeen miles above the earth’s surface. It seemed clear to me, then, that my rate of ascent was not only increasing, but that the change would have been noticeable even if I hadn’t released the ballast, which I did. The pains in my head and ears returned at intervals with real force, and I still bled occasionally from my nose; but, overall, I suffered much less than I might have expected. Still, I breathed with increasing difficulty every moment, and each inhalation came with a troublesome, spasmodic tightening in my chest. I now unpacked the condensing apparatus and got it ready for immediate use.

“The view of the earth at this point in my ascent was truly beautiful. To the west, north, and south, as far as I could see, lay a boundless sheet of ocean, apparently unruffled. With every moment it deepened into a richer blue and began already to show a slight curve. Far to the east, though still clearly visible, the islands of Great Britain stretched out, along with the entire Atlantic coasts of France and Spain, and a small portion of the northern continent of Africa. Not a single building could be seen, and even the proudest cities of mankind had completely vanished from the face of the earth. From the rock of Gibraltar—now reduced to a dim speck—the dark Mediterranean Sea, scattered with shining islands like stars in the sky, spread out to the east as far as my vision reached, until its whole mass of water seemed at last to tumble headlong over the abyss of the horizon. I found myself listening on tiptoe for the echoes of the mighty cataract. Above me, the sky was a deep, jet-black, and the stars were brilliantly visible.

“Since the pigeons at this time seemed to be suffering greatly, I decided to set them free. First, I untied one of them—a beautiful gray-mottled pigeon—and placed him on the rim of the wicker basket. He looked extremely uneasy, anxiously scanning his surroundings, fluttering his wings, and making a loud cooing sound, but I couldn’t persuade him to trust himself away from the car. I finally picked him up and threw him about half a dozen yards from the balloon. He made no attempt to descend, as I had expected; instead, he struggled violently to get back, crying out with very shrill, piercing calls. At last he managed to regain his former position on the rim, but hardly had he done so when his head dropped to his breast, and he fell dead inside the car.”"}

