          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dd56820045dca2a84df73d9a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Darcy spoke of his sister\u2019s skill with warm approval.\\n\\n\u201cI am very glad to receive so favorable an account of her,\u201d said Lady Catherine; \u201cand do tell her for me, that she cannot expect to excel unless she practises a great deal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you, madam,\u201d he answered, \u201cthat she has no need of such counsel. She practises with great constancy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen so much the better. It cannot be urged too strongly; and when I next write to her, I shall insist that she neglect it on no account. I often tell young ladies that no excellence in music can be gained without constant practice. I have told Miss Bennet more than once that she will never play really well unless she practises more; and though Mrs. Collins has no instrument, she is still welcome, as I have often said to her, to come to Rosings every day and play on the pianoforte in Mrs. Jenkinson\u2019s room. She would not be in anyone\u2019s way there, you know.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy looked a little embarrassed by his aunt\u2019s rudeness, and made no reply.\\n\\nWhen coffee was finished, Colonel Fitzwilliam reminded Elizabeth that she had promised to play for him, and she went at once to the instrument. He drew a chair close by. Lady Catherine listened to half a song, and then, as before, spoke to her other nephew; until the latter left her. With his usual deliberation he then walked toward the pianoforte and took his place so that he could command a full view of the fair performer\u2019s face. Elizabeth understood what he was doing, and at the first convenient pause turned toward him with an arch smile and said,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou mean to frighten me, Mr. Darcy, by coming in all this state to hear me. But I will not be alarmed, even if your sister _does_ play so well. There is a stubbornness in me that can never endure being frightened at the bidding of others. My courage always grows stronger with every attempt to intimidate me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI shall not pretend that you are mistaken,\u201d he replied, \u201cfor you could not truly suppose I meant to alarm you; and I have had the pleasure of knowing you long enough to be sure that you take great satisfaction in sometimes stating opinions which, in truth, are not your own.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth laughed heartily at the likeness of herself, and said to Colonel Fitzwilliam, \u201cYour cousin will give you a very charming idea of me, and teach you not to believe a word I say. I am particularly unfortunate in meeting with someone so well placed to uncover my real character, in a part of the world where I had hoped to pass myself off with at least some credit.\u201d\"}"
