          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0000f552fd6802b22fef1e12\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Could our lofty dreams have been meant to vanish so? Our name had been set \u201ca little lower than the angels,\u201d and, see now, we were no better than fleeting breath. We had styled ourselves the \u201cparagon of animals,\u201d and lo! we turned out to be a \u201cquint-essence of dust.\u201d We grieved that the pyramids endured longer than the embalmed body of their maker. And yet, on the road, the simple shepherd\u2019s hut of straw we passed held within its very frame a rule of greater staying-power than the entire race of man. How can one square this bitter shift with our former hopes, with the seeming strength we once possessed?\\n\\nThen, suddenly, an inward voice\u2014plain and distinct\u2014seemed to declare:\u2014So from eternity it was decreed; the steeds that carry Time onward have this hour and its fulfilment bound upon them, since the void brought forth its burden. Would you try to read backward the unalterable laws of Necessity?\\n\\nMother of the world! Servant of the Omnipotent! eternal, unchanging Necessity! You, with busy fingers, sit ever weaving the unbreakable chain of events! I will not complain of thy doings. If my human mind cannot admit that whatever is, is right; still, since what is must be, I will take my place among the ruins and smile. Truly, we were not fashioned for enjoyment, but for submission\u2014and for hope.\\n\\nWill the reader grow weary, if I set down our long, drawn-out passage from Paris to Geneva? If, day by day, I should write\u2014like a journal\u2014the crowding miseries of our lot, could my hand find words, or language grant terms, to show the many shapes of our woe; the pressing and jostling of one dreadful occurrence upon another? Patience, O reader, whoever thou art, wherever thou dwellest\u2014whether of a spiritual kind, or sprung from some pair that survived\u2014your nature will still be human, your dwelling the earth. Here you will read the deeds of the extinct race, and you will wonder, as you look on what you find recorded, whether those who endured it were of frail flesh and tender organization like your own. Most certainly they were\u2014so weep; for surely, being alone, you will be of a gentle temper: shed compassionate tears; yet, while you do, lend your attention to the tale, and learn the acts and sufferings of your forebears.\\n\\nStill, the last events that marked our progress through France were so steeped in strange terror and dark misery that I cannot linger long over the telling. If I were to break down each incident, every small fragment of a second would carry an appalling story, and even the least word would chill the blood in your young veins. It is fitting that I raise for your instruction this monument to the race that has gone before; but not that I should drag you through the wards of an hospital, nor into the hidden rooms of the charnel-house.\"}"
