          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f81e0427c635ecc0933ef608\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cListen, then! You!\u201d returned Monks. \u201cHis father fell ill in Rome, and was joined there by his wife\u2014my mother\u2014who had been separated from him for a long time. She left Paris and brought me with her, to look after his property, as far as I know; for she had no real affection for him, and he had none for her. He knew nothing of us, because his senses were gone, and he slept on until the next day, when he died. Among the papers in his desk were two, dated the night his illness first began, addressed to yourself\u201d\u2014he turned to Mr. Brownlow\u2014\u201cand enclosed in a few short lines to you, with a note on the outside of the package saying it was not to be forwarded until after he was dead. One of those papers was a letter to this girl, Agnes; the other was a will.\\n\\n\u201cWhat about the letter?\u201d asked Mr. Brownlow.\\n\\n\u201cThe letter?\u2014A sheet of paper crossed and crossed again, with a confession written over and over, and prayers to God to help her. He had told the girl a story\u2014that some secret mystery, to be explained one day, prevented him from marrying her just then\u2014and so she went on trusting him patiently, until she trusted too far and lost what no one could ever give back. At that time she was only a few months from giving birth. He told her everything he had meant to do, to hide her shame if he had lived, and prayed her, if he died, not to curse his memory or think that the consequences of their sin would be visited on her or their young child; for all the guilt was his. He reminded her of the day he had given her the little locket and the ring with her Christian name engraved upon it, and a blank left for that which he hoped one day to bestow upon her\u2014prayed her yet to keep it and wear it next her heart, as she had done before\u2014and then he went on, wildly, in the same words, over and over again, as if he had lost his mind. I believe he had.\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow, as Oliver\u2019s tears fell fast.\\n\\nMonks was silent.\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow, speaking for him, \u201cwas in the same spirit as the letter. He talked about the miseries his wife had brought on him; about the rebellious disposition, vice, malice, and your premature bad passions\u2014his only son\u2014who had been trained to hate him; and he left you, and your mother, each an annuity of eight hundred pounds. He divided the bulk of his property into two equal portions\u2014one for Agnes Fleming, and the other for their child, if it should be born alive, and ever come of age.\u201d}\"}"
