{"request_id":"1e2e6cacce52e3a758e6745e","rewritten_text":"And Sir Arnold, and Sir Gauter, knights of the castle, encountered Sir Brandiles and Sir Kay, and these four knights met mightily and broke their spears in their hands. Then Sir Pertolope came from the castle, and there he met Sir Lionel; and there Sir Pertolope, the green knight, struck down Sir Lionel, brother to Sir Launcelot. All of this was marked by noble heralds, who bore witness to it and named them. Then Sir Bleobaris broke his spear upon Sir Gareth, but in that stroke Sir Bleobaris fell to the earth. When Sir Galihodin saw that, he told Sir Gareth to keep him, and Sir Gareth smote him down to the earth. Then Sir Galihud took up a spear to avenge his brother, and in the same way Sir Gareth served him. And so did Sir Dinadan and his brother La Cote Male Taile, and Sir Sagramore le Disirous, and Sir Dodinas le Savage; all of these he drove down with one spear.

When King Aswisance of Ireland saw how Sir Gareth fared, he marveled at what he might be: one time he seemed green, and another time, when he came again, he seemed blue. And thus, at every course that he rode to and fro, he changed his color, so that neither king nor knight could have ready recognition of him. Then Sir Agwisance, King of Ireland, encountered with Sir Gareth, and there Sir Gareth struck him from his horse, saddle and all. And then came King Carados of Scotland, and Sir Gareth smote him down, horse and man. And in the same way he served King Uriens of the land of Gore. And then Sir Bagdemagus came in, and Sir Gareth smote him down, horse and man, to the earth. And Bagdemagus’s son Meliganus broke a spear upon Sir Gareth mightily and knightly.

Then Sir Galahault, the noble prince, cried out from on high: “Knight with the many colors, well hast thou justed; now make thee ready, that I may just with thee.” Sir Gareth heard him, and he took up a great spear; and so they encountered together. There the prince broke his spear, but Sir Gareth struck him on the left side of the helm, so that he reeled here and there, and he would have fallen had not his men recovered him. “Truly,” said King Arthur, “that knight with the many colors is a good knight.” Wherefore the king called to him Sir Launcelot and prayed him to encounter with that knight. “Sir,” said Launcelot, “I may as well find in my heart to forbear him at this time, for he has had travail enough this day. And when a good knight does so well on some day, it is no good knight’s part to let him have his worship—especially when he sees a knight has done so great labor. For, peradventure,” said Sir Launcelot, “his quarrel is here this day, and peradventure he is best beloved with this lady of all that be here. And therefore, said Sir Launcelot, as for me, this day he shall have the honor; though it lay in my power to put him from it, I would not.”"}

