          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9713a7878553a21ea8b588f4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Attorney-General had to tell the jury that the prisoner in front of them, though young, was already old in the treasonous practices that should cost him his life. He said this correspondence with the public enemy was not something that had started recently\u2014today, yesterday, last year, or even the year before. It was certain the prisoner had been traveling back and forth between France and England for longer than that, on secret business he could not explain honestly. If treasonous behavior had been the kind that could thrive, then the real wrongdoing behind his actions might have stayed hidden. But Providence had put it in the heart of a person who was fearless and above reproach to uncover what the prisoner was planning. Horrified by it, this person reported everything to his Majesty\u2019s Chief Secretary of State and to the most honorable Privy Council. That patriot would now be brought before the jury.\\n\\nMr. Attorney-General said the man\u2019s position and attitude were, in general, sublime. He had been the prisoner\u2019s friend, but at a time that was both promising and dangerous, he had recognized the prisoner\u2019s dishonesty. He decided to destroy the traitor he could no longer protect in his own home, offering him up on the sacred altar of his country. If Britain made statues for public benefactors the way ancient Greece and Rome did, this shining citizen would surely have one. Since Britain did not do that, he probably would not.\\n\\nHe added that virtue, as the poets had said in many lines the jury knew by heart, was contagious. The jury\u2019s faces showed a guilty awareness that they did not actually know those lines. He said especially that patriotism\u2014the love of one\u2019s country\u2014spreads from person to person. The high example of this spotless, unimpeachable witness for the Crown, whom it was an honor to mention, though he did so in a way that was not worthy, had passed to the prisoner\u2019s servant. It led the servant to decide, with a holy determination, to search the prisoner\u2019s table drawers and pockets, and to hide away his papers.\\n\\nMr. Attorney-General said he was ready to hear that this admirable servant had been criticized, but that, in general, he preferred him to his own brothers and sisters. He honored him more than his own father and mother. He urged the jury, with confidence, to do the same. He said the testimony of these two witnesses, along with the documents showing what they discovered, would prove that the prisoner had been given lists of his Majesty\u2019s forces and how they were arranged and prepared, both by sea and by land. He said this would leave no doubt that the prisoner had regularly passed such information to a hostile power.\"}"
