          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f4375a54ee96c4733b5c15e4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"One day, while I was occupied with my books and Mr. Pocket, a letter came by post, and the very outside of it set me in a great flutter; for although I had never before seen the handwriting in which it was addressed, I could tell whose hand it was. It did not open in any regular way\u2014no \u201cDear Mr. Pip,\u201d or \u201cDear Pip,\u201d or \u201cDear Sir,\u201d or \u201cDear Anything\u201d\u2014but went on thus:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am to come to London the day after to-morrow by the midday coach. I believe it was settled you should meet me? At all events Miss Havisham has that impression, and I write in obedience to it. She sends you her regard.\\n\\n\u201cYours, ESTELLA.\u201d\\n\\nHad there been time, I might have ordered several suits of clothes for the occasion; but since there was none, I had to make do with what I already had. My appetite disappeared at once, and I could find no ease or rest until the day came. Yet even when it arrived, I was no better; I grew worse, and began prowling about the coach-office in Wood Street, Cheapside, before the coach had even left the Blue Boar in our town. I knew, of course, that I was being perfectly unreasonable, but still I felt as though it would not be safe to let the coach-office out of my sight for more than five minutes at a time. In that state of folly I had already done the first half-hour of a watch that was to last four or five hours, when Wemmick ran into me.\\n\\n\u201cHalloa, Mr. Pip,\u201d said he; \u201chow do you do? I shouldn\u2019t have thought this was _your_ beat.\u201d\\n\\nI told him I was waiting to meet someone who was coming up by coach, and I asked after the Castle and the Aged.\\n\\n\u201cBoth flourishing, thankye,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cand particularly the Aged. He\u2019s in wonderful feather. He\u2019ll be eighty-two next birthday. I have a notion of firing eighty-two times, if the neighbourhood shouldn\u2019t complain, and that cannon of mine should stand up to the strain. But this isn\u2019t London talk. Where do you suppose I\u2019m going?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo the office?\u201d I said, for he was making straight for it.\\n\\n\u201cNext thing to it,\u201d returned Wemmick. \u201cI\u2019m going to Newgate. We\u2019re in a banker\u2019s-parcel scrape just now, and I\u2019ve been down the road taking a look at the scene of action, and so I must have a word or two with our client.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid your client commit the robbery?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cBless your soul and body, no,\u201d answered Wemmick, very quietly. \u201cBut he\u2019s accused of it. So might you or I be. Either of us might be accused of it, you know.\u201d\"}"
