          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9de5036cb2c6ddbb093ce599\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At that moment, his eyes\u2014and mine as well\u2014fell on a scrap of parchment, which I first took to be paper. It lay half buried in the sand, with one corner sticking up. Near where we found it, I noticed the remains of the hull of what looked like a ship\u2019s longboat. The wreck seemed to have been there for a very long time, because you could hardly make out the shape of the boat timbers anymore.\\n\\n\u201cWell, Jupiter picked up the parchment, wrapped the beetle in it, and handed it to me. Not long after that, we started back home and, along the way, met Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014. I showed him the insect, and he begged me to let him take it to the fort. When I agreed, he immediately put it into his waistcoat pocket, but without the parchment that had been wrapped around it. I kept holding that parchment in my hand while he looked at the beetle. Maybe he feared I would change my mind, and thought it was safer to secure the prize right away. You know how excited he gets about anything connected to Natural History. At the same time, without realizing it, I must have put the parchment into my own pocket.\\n\\n\u201cYou remember that when I went to the table to make a sketch of the beetle, I found no paper where it was usually kept. I checked the drawer, and there was none there either. I searched my pockets, hoping to find an old letter, and then my hand touched the parchment. That is why I\u2019m explaining the exact way I came to have it; the circumstances made a strong impression on me.\\n\\n\u201cNo doubt you\u2019ll think I\u2019m being fanciful, but I had already made a kind of connection. I had put together two links in a chain. There was a boat on a seacoast, and not far from the boat was a parchment\u2014not paper\u2014with a skull drawn on it. You\u2019ll probably ask, \u2018Where is the connection?\u2019 I answer that the skull, or death\u2019s-head, is the well-known symbol of pirates. The death\u2019s-head flag is raised in all their battles.\\n\\n\u201cI said the scrap was parchment, not paper. Parchment lasts a long time\u2014almost forever. Things of little importance are rarely written on parchment, because for ordinary drawing or writing it isn\u2019t nearly as suitable as paper. This made me think there was some meaning, some relevance, in the death\u2019s-head. I also noticed the shape of the parchment. Even though one corner had been damaged by some accident, you could still tell it was originally oblong.\u201d}\"}"
