          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6d7d455d582e8d8459c6c6d3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Darcy spoke with warm praise of his sister\u2019s skill.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m very glad to hear such a good report of her,\u201d said Lady Catherine. \u201cAnd please tell her for me that she can\u2019t expect to excel unless she practises a great deal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you, madam,\u201d he replied, \u201cthat she doesn\u2019t need advice like that. She practises very consistently.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEven better. It can never be done too much; and when I next write to her, I shall tell her not to neglect it for any reason. I often tell young ladies that no excellence in music can be achieved without constant practice. I\u2019ve told Miss Bennet several times that she will never play really well unless she practises more; and although Mrs. Collins has no instrument, she\u2019s very welcome, as I\u2019ve often told her, to come to Rosings every day and play on the pianoforte in Mrs. Jenkinson\u2019s room. She wouldn\u2019t be in anyone\u2019s way in that part of the house.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy looked a little ashamed of his aunt\u2019s bad manners and said nothing.\\n\\nWhen the coffee was finished, Colonel Fitzwilliam reminded Elizabeth of her promise to play for him, and she sat down at once at the instrument. He pulled a chair close to her. Lady Catherine listened to half a song, and then, as before, spoke to her other nephew, until the latter walked away from her. With his usual deliberate care, he moved toward the pianoforte and positioned himself so that he could get a full view of the fair performer\u2019s face. Elizabeth noticed what he was doing, and at the first convenient pause turned to him with an arch smile and said,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou mean to frighten me, Mr. Darcy, by coming in all this formality just to hear me. But I won\u2019t be intimidated, even if your sister does play so well. There\u2019s a stubbornness in me that can never stand being frightened at the will of other people. My courage always grows with every attempt to intimidate me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI won\u2019t say you\u2019re wrong,\u201d he replied, \u201cbecause you couldn\u2019t really believe I would have any intention of alarming you; and I\u2019ve had the pleasure of knowing you long enough to understand that you take great enjoyment in sometimes stating opinions that, in fact, aren\u2019t your own.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth laughed heartily at this picture of herself, and said to Colonel Fitzwilliam, \u201cYour cousin will give you a very flattering idea of me and teach you not to believe a word I say. I\u2019m especially unlucky in meeting someone so capable of exposing my true character, in a part of the world where I\u2019d hoped to pass myself off with at least some credibility.\u201d\"}"
