          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ca2562b94289016f2f95ae52\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After we saw our friend properly settled in his new office, we turned our attention to Windsor. Windsor was so close to London that leaving Raymond and Perdita did not feel like a painful separation. We said goodbye to them at the Protectoral Palace. It was very moving to watch my sister step into her role as if she truly belonged in the drama, and try to carry herself with the dignity her position required. Her pride and her humility were now in conflict more than ever. Her shyness was not something she pretended; it came from her fear of not being valued, and from the way she seemed to think the world did not truly notice her. Raymond had the same kind of attitude. But Perdita thought about other people more often than he did, and part of her bashfulness came from a wish to keep those around her from feeling inferior. That feeling never once crossed her own mind.\\n\\nBecause of how she was born and educated, Idris would probably have been better suited to formal ceremonies. Still, the ease with which she could do those things, because she was used to them, made them feel dull. Even with all the drawbacks, Perdita clearly enjoyed her situation. She was too full of new ideas to feel much pain when we left. She said goodbye to us warmly, promised to visit soon, and yet she did not regret the circumstances that caused our separation.\\n\\nRaymond\u2019s spirits were high. He did not know what to do with his new power. His head was full of plans, and he had not chosen any yet, but he promised himself, his friends, and the world that the start of his protectorship would be marked by an act of extraordinary glory. So we talked about them and tried to make sense of it all, as we returned to Windsor Castle with fewer people than before. We felt great relief at escaping political trouble, and we threw ourselves into our private life with even more energy. We did not lack for things to do. My own eagerness was now directed only toward intellectual work. I found that hard study was an excellent cure for the restless fever of the mind that, in idleness, would surely have taken hold of me. Perdita had let us take Clara back with us to Windsor, and she, along with Perdita and my two lovely infants, was a constant source of interest and amusement.\\n\\nThe only thing that disturbed our peace was Adrian\u2019s health. It clearly was getting worse, and there were no signs that would have made us suspect his illness\u2014unless, perhaps, his brighter eyes, lively look, and flushed cheeks made us fear consumption. Still, he had no pain and no fear. He threw himself into books with great energy, and when he rested from study, he did so with the company he loved most: his sister and me.\"}"
