          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"36f7a5f87c94aa7d8a707470\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Wake up later on and look around to see what caused it. You might even spot a steamboat coughing along upstream, so far off toward the other side that you couldn\u2019t tell anything about her\u2014only whether she was a stern-wheel or a side-wheel. Then, for about an hour, there would be nothing to hear and nothing to see, just solid loneliness. After that, you\u2019d see a raft sliding by, way out there, and maybe a man on it chopping, because they\u2019re always doing it on a raft. You\u2019d see the axe flash and come down. You wouldn\u2019t hear anything at first. You\u2019d watch the axe rise again, and by the time it was above the man\u2019s head you\u2019d finally hear the k\u2019chunk!\u2014because it took all that time for the sound to travel over the water.\\n\\nSo we would spend the day drifting and lounging around, listening to the quiet. Once there was a thick fog, and the rafts and other things passing by were beating tin pans so the steamboats wouldn\u2019t run over them. A scow or a raft would come by so close we could hear the people talking, cussing, and laughing\u2014we could hear them clearly\u2014but we couldn\u2019t see any sign of them. It made you feel uneasy, like spirits carrying on out in the air. Jim said he believed it was spirits, but I said:\\n\\n\u201cNo. Spirits wouldn\u2019t say, \u2018Dern the dern fog.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nThe moment night came, we shoved off. When we got the raft out to about the middle, we left it alone and let it float wherever the current wanted. Then we lit the pipes, dangled our legs in the water, and talked about all kinds of things. We were always naked, day and night, whenever the mosquitoes would let us. The new clothes Buck\u2019s folks made for me were too nice to be comfortable, and besides, I didn\u2019t care much for clothes anyway.\\n\\nSometimes we\u2019d have the whole river to ourselves for the longest time. Over there were the banks and the islands, across the water. Maybe you\u2019d see a spark\u2014like a candle in a cabin window. And sometimes, out on the water, you could see one or two sparks on a raft or a scow. You might even hear a fiddle or a song coming from one of those boats. It\u2019s lovely to live on a raft. Up in the sky there were stars scattered all over, and we used to lie on our backs and look up at them, talking about whether they were made or if they just happened. Jim said they were made, but I said they happened. I figured it would have taken too long to make that many.\"}"
