          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a27c5b9a7136b728a8d33c91\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His account was that, after leaving home and passing through Datchet, he was surprised to see smoke coming from the inn\u2019s chimney. He thought he might find other people traveling with him, so he knocked and was let in. There was no one in the house except Lucy and her mother. Her mother had been left unable to use her limbs by rheumatism, and because of that, one by one, the rest of the people in the country had gone away, leaving Lucy and her mother alone.\\n\\nLucy begged the man to stay with her. In a week or two her mother would be better, and then they could set out together. But, she said, they would surely die if they were left helpless and alone. The man replied that his wife and children were already among the emigrants, and that, in his view, he could not stay.\\n\\nAs a last resort, Lucy gave him a letter for Idris, to be delivered to her wherever he might meet us. He carried out this task at least, and Idris received the following letter with deep feeling:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHONOURED LADY,\\n\\n\u201cI am sure you will remember me and feel sorry for me, and I dare hope you will help me. What other hope do I have? Please forgive the way I write. I am so confused. A month ago my dear mother was left unable to use her limbs. She is already better, and in another month I am sure she will be able to travel, in the way you were kind enough to say you would arrange for us. But now everyone has gone\u2014everyone. As they left, each person said that perhaps my mother would be better before we were completely deserted.\\n\\n\u201cThree days ago I went to Samuel Woods. He stayed until the last because of his newborn child, and since they have a large family, I thought I could persuade them to wait a little longer for us. But I found the house empty. I have not seen a single person since this good man came. What will become of us? My mother does not know our situation. She is so ill that I have hidden it from her.\\n\\n\u201cWill you not send someone to us? I am sure we must perish miserably as we are. If I tried to move my mother now, she would die on the road. And if, when she gets better, I were able to travel, I cannot even imagine how I would find the roads and make so many miles to the sea. You would all be in France by then, and the great ocean would be between us, which is so frightening even for sailors.\u201d\"}"
