{"request_id":"44d5c4495323bc3807d40fc0","rewritten_text":"“Oh, confound my hat! I want Virginia!” the little Duke exclaimed, laughing as they rode hard on toward the railway station. There Mr. Otis asked the station-master whether anyone matching Virginia’s description had been seen on the platform, but he could learn nothing. Still, the station-master sent messages along the line in both directions and promised that a close watch would be kept for her. After that, and after buying a hat for the little Duke from a linen-draper who was just closing up his shutters, Mr. Otis set off to Bexley, a village some four miles off, where he had been told the gypsies were wont to gather, since there was a large common beside it. They woke the rural policeman, but he could offer no information; and after riding over the common in every direction, they turned their horses back and made for the Chase, arriving at about eleven o’clock, utterly worn out and nearly broken-hearted. 

At the gate-house they found Washington and the twins waiting with lanterns, for the avenue was very dark. No trace of Virginia had been found. The gypsies had been taken up on Brockley meadows, yet she was not with them. They said they had left in a hurry because they had misread the date of Chorton Fair and feared they would be late. In truth, they seemed genuinely distressed at hearing of Virginia’s disappearance; they were grateful to Mr. Otis for letting them camp in his park, and four of their number had stayed behind to assist in the search. The carp-pond had been dragged, and the whole Chase searched thoroughly, but to no effect. It was plain that, at least for that night, Virginia was beyond their reach. With the deepest gloom upon him, Mr. Otis and the boys went up to the house, the groom trailing behind with the two horses and the pony. 

In the hall they met a knot of frightened servants, and in the library they found poor Mrs. Otis lying on a sofa, almost beside herself with terror and anxiety, while the old housekeeper bathed her forehead with eau-de-cologne. Mr. Otis immediately insisted she should have something to eat, and he ordered supper brought up for the whole party. It was a dismal meal—so little was said that even the twins were subdued and silent, for they were very fond of their sister. When they had finished, Mr. Otis, despite the little Duke’s pleading, sent them all to bed, declaring that nothing more could be done that night and that he would telegraph in the morning to Scotland Yard to have some detectives sent down at once."}

