{"request_id":"5d8473e6065b3cb868277d23","rewritten_text":"Among the other public buildings of a certain town—about which, for several reasons, I think it best not to speak, and to which I shall give no invented title—there is one, as old and familiar as any that most towns, whether large or small, can boast: namely, a workhouse; and in that workhouse he was born. It happened on a day and date which I need not repeat, since it can matter nothing to the reader at this point in the business; for it is the mortal event whose name stands at the opening of this chapter.

For a long time after he was brought into this world of sorrow and hardship, by the parish surgeon, there was real uncertainty whether the child would live long enough to have any name at all; and, had that been the case, it is more than likely that these memoirs would never have been written. Or, if they had, they would have been compressed into two or three pages, and would have had the priceless advantage of being the briefest and truest example of biography ever produced in any age or country.

I am not, indeed, arguing that to be born in a workhouse is, in itself, the happiest and most enviable lot that can befall a human being; but I do say that, in Oliver Twist’s particular case, it was the best thing that could possibly have occurred. The plain truth is that there was some difficulty in getting Oliver to do the work of breathing—an awkward business, though custom has made it indispensable to our comfortable existence. For a time he lay gasping on a small flock mattress, unsteadily balanced between this world and the next, with the scales decidedly turned toward the latter. Had, during that short interval, Oliver been attended by careful grandmothers, anxious aunts, skilful nurses, and doctors of the deepest learning, he would almost certainly have been killed at once. But as it was, there was no one there except a pauper old woman, made rather hazy by an unusual allowance of beer, and a parish surgeon who undertook such matters by contract; and between them Oliver and Nature fought out the question. The outcome was that, after a few struggles, Oliver breathed, sneezed, and then—by raising as loud a cry as could reasonably be expected from a male infant who lacked that very useful appendage—set about informing the workhouse inmates that a new burden had been laid upon the parish, and kept up the sound for longer than three minutes and a quarter."}

