{"request_id":"e0dc5504f118648fca71491d","rewritten_text":"We finally lightened the box by removing two thirds of its contents, and, with some trouble, we were able to lift it out of the hole. The items we took out were deposited among the brambles, and the dog was left to guard them, with strict orders from Jupiter that, under no circumstances, he was to move from the spot or open his mouth until our return. We then hurried home with the chest, reaching the hut safely, but only after exhausting effort, at one o’clock in the morning. Even so worn out, it wasn’t in human nature to do more right away. We rested until two, ate supper, and then set out for the hills immediately afterward, armed with three stout sacks, which—by good luck—were on the premises. A little before four we arrived at the pit, divided the remaining booty as evenly as we could among us, and, leaving the holes unfilled, set out again for the hut. For the second time, we deposited our golden burdens there, just as the first faint streaks of dawn appeared over the treetops in the East.

We were thoroughly worn down now, but the intense excitement of the moment wouldn’t let us rest. After an uneasy sleep of about three or four hours, we got up, as if we’d planned it together, to examine our treasure.

The chest had been full to the brim, and we spent the whole day, and most of the next night, going through its contents. There was nothing like order or arrangement. Everything had been piled in a jumble. Once we sorted it carefully, we realized we had even vaster wealth than we’d first thought. In coin there was a little more than four hundred and fifty thousand dollars—estimating the value of the pieces as accurately as we could using the tables of the time. There wasn’t a trace of silver. All of it was antique gold of great variety—French, Spanish, and German money, with a few English guineas, and some counters we’d never seen before. There were several very large and heavy coins, so worn that we couldn’t make out their inscriptions. There was no American money. We had even more trouble estimating the value of the jewels. There were diamonds—some of them extremely large and fine—one hundred and ten in all, and not one small; eighteen rubies of remarkable brilliance; three hundred and ten emeralds, all very beautiful; and twenty-one sapphires, with an opal. These stones had all been broken out of their settings and thrown loose into the chest. The settings themselves, which we picked out from among the other gold, looked as though they’d been smashed with hammers, as if to prevent identification."}

