          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"692942cfff02b66543abacbc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat\u2019s the matter?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTen o\u2019clock, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean? Ten o\u2019clock at night?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, sir. Your honor told me to call you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I remember. Very well, very well.\u201d\\n\\nAfter a few dull attempts to get to sleep again\u2014which the man skillfully fought by stirring the fire nonstop for five minutes\u2014he got up, tossed his hat on, and walked out. He turned into the Temple, and, having refreshed himself by pacing King\u2019s Bench-walk and Paper-buildings twice, he went into the Stryver chambers.\\n\\nThe Stryver clerk, who never helped with these conferences, had gone home, and the Stryver principal opened the door. He had his slippers on, and a loose bed-gown, and his throat was bare for his comfort. He had that rather wild, strained, scorched look around the eyes, which you can see in all free drinkers of his class, from the portrait of Jeffries downward, and which can be traced, under various disguises of Art, through the portraits of every Drinking Age.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re a little late, Memory,\u201d said Stryver.\\n\\n\u201cAbout the usual time; it might be a quarter of an hour later.\u201d\\n\\nThey went into a dingy room lined with books and scattered with papers, where a fire was blazing. A kettle steamed on the hob, and amid the wreck of papers a table shone, with plenty of wine on it, and brandy, and rum, and sugar, and lemons.\\n\\n\u201cI see you\u2019ve had your bottle, Sydney.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTwo tonight, I think. I\u2019ve been dining with the day\u2019s client; or watching him dine\u2014it\u2019s all the same!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat was a rare point, Sydney, that you brought to bear on the identification. How did you come by it? When did it hit you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI thought he was rather a handsome fellow, and I thought I\u2019d be much the same kind of fellow if I\u2019d had any luck.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Stryver laughed until he shook his precocious paunch.\\n\\n\u201cYou and your luck, Sydney! Get to work, get to work.\u201d\\n\\nSullenly enough, the jackal loosened his dress, went into the adjoining room, and came back with a large jug of cold water, a basin, and a couple of towels. He soaked the towels in the water, wrung them out only partly, folded them on his head in a way hideous to behold, sat down at the table, and said, \u201cNow I\u2019m ready!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot much boiling down to be done tonight, Memory,\u201d said Mr. Stryver cheerfully, as he looked through his papers.\\n\\n\u201cHow much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly two sets of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGive me the worst first.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere they are, Sydney. Fire away!\u201d\\n\\nThe lion then settled himself on his back on a sofa to one side of the drinking-table, while the jackal sat at his own paper-strewn table on the other side, with the bottles and glasses ready to his hand.\"}"
