          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2428ec65ec0e75c6b6bf1441\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell, monsieur le mauvais sujet,\u201d the old lady called out when he came into the room, \u201cwhy haven\u2019t you come to see me at all this time?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lady Clem, I never seem to have a moment to myself,\u201d said Lord Arthur, smiling.\\n\\n\u201cI suppose you mean you spend all day with Miss Sybil Merton\u2014picking up chiffons and talking nonsense. I can\u2019t see why people make such a fuss about being married. In my day, we never dreamed of billing and cooing in public, or even in private.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you I haven\u2019t seen Sybil for twenty-four hours, Lady Clem. As far as I can tell, she belongs entirely to her milliners.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly\u2014that\u2019s the only reason you come to visit an ugly old woman like me. I wonder you men don\u2019t take warning. On a fait des folies pour moi, and here I am, a poor rheumatic creature, with a false front and a bad temper. Why, if it weren\u2019t for dear Lady Jansen, who sends me all the worst French novels she can get hold of, I don\u2019t think I could get through the day. Doctors are no good at all, except to take fees. They can\u2019t even cure my heartburn.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve brought you something to ease that, Lady Clem,\u201d said Lord Arthur gravely. \u201cIt\u2019s a wonderful thing\u2014an invention from America.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t care for American inventions, Arthur. I\u2019m quite sure I don\u2019t. I\u2019ve read a few American novels lately, and they were nothing but nonsense.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, but there\u2019s no nonsense in this, Lady Clem! I assure you it\u2019s a perfect cure. You must promise to try it.\u201d And Lord Arthur drew the little box from his pocket and handed it to her.\\n\\n\u201cWell, the box is charming, Arthur. Is it truly a present? That\u2019s very kind of you. And is this the wonderful medicine? It looks just like a bonbon. I\u2019ll take it at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood heavens, Lady Clem!\u201d Lord Arthur cried, catching her hand. \u201cYou mustn\u2019t do that. It\u2019s homoeopathic medicine, and if you take it when you aren\u2019t suffering heartburn, it may do you no end of harm. Wait until you have an attack, and then take it. You\u2019ll be astonished by the result.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019d rather take it now,\u201d said Lady Clementina, holding the little transparent capsule up to the light, watching the floating bubble of liquid aconitine. \u201cI\u2019m sure it\u2019s delicious. The truth is, though I dislike doctors, I do love medicines. Still, I\u2019ll keep it for my next attack.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd when will that be?\u201d Lord Arthur asked eagerly.\"}"
