{"request_id":"d7d6479454d11687ea89e2e7","rewritten_text":"Perdita’s name was never mentioned, but I couldn’t doubt that his wavering purpose came from his love for her. And who was more worthy of love than my noble-minded sister? Who deserved the hand of this self-exalted king more than she, whose gaze belonged to a queen of nations? Who loved him as he loved her—despite the fact that disappointment had crushed her passion, while ambition fought hard against it.

We went together to the House in the evening. Raymond, even though he knew their plans and prospects would be discussed and decided in the expected debate, was cheerful and careless. The moment we entered the coffee-room, a hum rose up—like the sound of ten thousand hives of swarming bees—that stunned us. Knots of politicians stood gathered, with anxious brows and loud or deep voices. The aristocratic party—the richest and most influential men in England—seemed less agitated than the others, because the question was to be discussed without their interference. Near the fire were Ryland and his supporters. Ryland was a man of obscure birth and immense wealth, inherited from his father, who had been a manufacturer. As a young man, he had witnessed the king’s abdication and the merging of the two houses of Lords and Commons; he had sympathized with these popular advances, and it had been the work of his life to consolidate and increase them. Since then, the influence of the landed proprietors had grown; and at first Ryland was not sorry to see Lord Raymond’s schemes, which drew off many of his opponents’ partisans. But now the whole thing was going too far. The poorer nobility welcomed the return of sovereignty as an event that would restore them to the power and rights they had lost. The half-dead spirit of royalty stirred again in men’s minds; and they, willing slaves—self-constituted subjects—were ready to bend their necks to the yoke. Some upright and manly spirits still remained, pillars of state; but the word republic had grown stale to the common ear, and many—whether it was a majority, the event would soon prove—craved the glitter and show of royalty. Ryland was stirred to resistance. He claimed that only his tolerance had allowed this party to grow; but the time for indulgence was over, and with one motion of his arm he would sweep away the cobwebs that blinded his countrymen.

When Raymond entered the coffee-room, his friends greeted him with almost a shout. They gathered around him, counted their numbers, and laid out the reasons they were now going to gain an addition of so many members—people who had not yet declared themselves."}

