          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"29c269004643835dc46456b8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But now she would rather it had not happened. She believed he had had too much of Mr. Weston\u2019s good wine, and she was sure he would want to talk nonsense.\\n\\nTo keep him as restrained as she could, using her own manners, she was immediately preparing to speak with exquisite calmness and gravity about the weather and the night; but scarcely had she begun, scarcely had they passed the sweep-gate and joined the other carriage, than she found her topic cut off\u2014her hand seized\u2014her attention demanded\u2014and Mr. Elton actually making violent love to her. Taking advantage of the precious opening, he declared sentiments that must already be well known, hoping\u2014fearing\u2014adoring\u2014ready to die if she refused him; yet flattering himself that his ardent attachment, unequalled love, and unprecedented passion could not fail to have some effect. In short, he was very much resolved to be seriously accepted as soon as possible. It really was so. Without scruple\u2014without apology\u2014without much visible diffidence, Mr. Elton, the lover of Harriet, was presenting himself as her lover. She tried to stop him, but in vain; he kept going and said it all.\\n\\nAngry as she was, the thought of the moment made her decide to restrain herself when she did speak. She felt that half of this folly must be drunkenness, and therefore could hope it would belong only to the passing hour. So, with a mixture of seriousness and playfulness\u2014something she hoped would best fit his half-and-half state\u2014she replied,\\n\\n\u201cI am very much astonished, Mr. Elton. This to me! You forget yourself\u2014you take me for my friend. Any message to Miss Smith I shall be happy to deliver; but no more of this to me, if you please.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Smith!\u2014a message to Miss Smith!\u2014What could she possibly mean!\u201d\u2014and he repeated her words with such confidence of accent, such boastful show of amazement, that she couldn\u2019t help answering quickly,\\n\\n\u201cMr. Elton, this is the most extraordinary behavior! I can account for it only in one way: you are not yourself, or you could not speak either to me, or of Harriet, in such a manner. Command yourself enough to say no more, and I will try to forget it.\u201d\\n\\nBut Mr. Elton had drunk wine enough only to lift his spirits, not at all to confuse his intellect. He knew perfectly well what he meant. After warmly protesting against her suspicion as most injurious, and lightly touching on his respect for Miss Smith as her friend\u2014while admitting his surprise that Miss Smith should even be mentioned\u2014he returned to the subject of his own passion, and was very urgent for a favorable answer.\"}"
