          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8bf6004d954302973830a7a4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Their arrival lit a kind of fire in Saint Antoine\u2019s chest. It spread quickly as they moved along, stirring and flickering in the faces of people at nearly every door and window. Still, no one followed them, and when they entered the wine-shop, no man spoke. Every man\u2019s eyes turned to them.\\n\\n\u201cGood day, gentlemen!\u201d said Monsieur Defarge.\\n\\nIt may have been the cue for everyone to start talking. The room answered with a chorus of \u201cGood day!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is bad weather, gentlemen,\u201d said Defarge, shaking his head.\\n\\nThen every man looked at his neighbor, and after that they all lowered their eyes and sat in silence. Only one man stood up and went out.\\n\\n\u201cMy wife,\u201d said Defarge, speaking to Madame Defarge, \u201cI have traveled certain leagues with this good road-mender, called Jacques. I met him\u2014by accident\u2014about a day and a half\u2019s journey from Paris. He is a good child, this road-mender, called Jacques. Give him something to drink, my wife!\u201d\\n\\nA second man stood up and went out. Madame Defarge poured wine for the road-mender named Jacques. He took off his blue cap to the others, then drank. In the breast of his blouse he carried some coarse, dark bread. He ate it between sips, then sat munching and drinking near Madame Defarge\u2019s counter. A third man stood up and went out.\\n\\nDefarge took a drink of wine, but less than he gave the stranger, since it was not unusual for him to drink. He waited until the countryman had finished eating. He did not look at anyone, and no one looked at him now\u2014not even Madame Defarge, who had picked up her knitting and kept working.\\n\\n\u201cHave you finished your meal, friend?\u201d Defarge asked at the right moment.\\n\\n\u201cYes, thank you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen come! You will see the room I told you you could use. It will suit you perfectly.\u201d\\n\\nThey went out of the wine-shop into the street, from the street into a courtyard, from the courtyard up a steep staircase, and from the staircase into a garret. It was the same garret where a white-haired man used to sit on a low bench, leaning forward and working hard, making shoes.\\n\\nNo white-haired man was there now. But the three men who had left the wine-shop one by one were there. And between them and the white-haired man far away was the one small link they had once seen him through, looking in through the cracks in the wall.\\n\\nDefarge closed the door carefully and spoke in a low voice:\\n\\n\u201cJacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three! This is the witness encountered by appointment, by me, Jacques Four.\u201d\"}"
