          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f9f7d3e32d5114ec031217b4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Many American ladies, when they leave their native country, fall into the habit of wearing an air of long-standing ill-health, believing it to be a kind of European polish; but Mrs. Otis had never made that mistake. She possessed a magnificent constitution, and an astonishing store of animal spirits. In fact, she was in many ways quite English, and served as a fine illustration of how we have everything in common with America these days\u2014language, of course, excepted. Her eldest son, christened Washington by his parents in a burst of patriotism that he never stopped regretting, was fair-haired and rather good-looking. He had prepared himself for American diplomacy by taking the German at the Newport Casino for three seasons running, and even in London was known as an excellent dancer. Gardenias and the peerage were his only weak points. Otherwise, he was extremely sensible.\\n\\nMiss Virginia E. Otis was a girl of fifteen\u2014lithe and lovely as a fawn\u2014with a frankness in her large blue eyes. She was a splendid amazon, and once raced old Lord Bilton on her pony twice round the park, winning by a length and a half, just ahead of the Achilles statue, to the immense delight of the young Duke of Cheshire, who proposed for her on the spot and was sent back to Eton that very night by his guardians, in floods of tears. After Virginia came the twins, usually dubbed \u201cThe Stars and Stripes,\u201d because they were forever getting swished. They were delightful boys, and, with the exception of the worthy Minister, the only true republicans in the family.\\n\\nSince Canterville Chase lay seven miles from Ascot, the nearest railway station, Mr. Otis had telegraphed for a waggonette to meet them, and they set off for their drive in high spirits. It was a beautiful July evening, and the air was delicately scented with pine-woods. Now and then they heard a wood pigeon brooding over its own sweet note, or caught sight, deep among the rustling fern, of the pheasant\u2019s burnished breast. Little squirrels watched them from the beech-trees, and the rabbits darted away through the brushwood and over the mossy knolls, their white tails flashing in the air. Yet as they entered the avenue of Canterville Chase, the sky suddenly darkened with clouds; an odd stillness seemed to settle on the atmosphere, a great company of rooks swept silently over their heads, and before they reached the house, some heavy drops of rain had already fallen.\\n\\nOn the steps, ready to receive them, stood an old woman, neatly dressed in black silk, with a white cap and apron.\"}"
