          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8a23fbe7bc7a428d10c460c6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And if he pressed me on why I wanted it, and on what grounds I believed I had any claim to it, I would have told him\u2014though he cared little for such thin hopes\u2014that I had loved Estella dearly and for a long time; and that even though I had lost her, and must go on living in grief, whatever touched her was still closer and dearer to me than anything else in the world. Seeing, too, that Mr. Jaggers remained quite motionless and silent, and seemed stubbornly unmoved by my appeal, I turned to Wemmick and said, \u201cWemmick, I take you for a man with a kind heart. I\u2019ve seen your comfortable home, and your old father, and all the innocent, cheerful, playful ways that brighten your business life. I beg you to speak a word for me to Mr. Jaggers, and to make it plain to him that, taking all things into account, he ought to be more open with me!\u201d\\n\\nI had never witnessed two men regard one another so strangely as Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick did after that outburst. At first, a fear crossed my mind that Wemmick would be dismissed on the spot; but it faded when I saw Mr. Jaggers ease into something like a smile, and Wemmick grow more daring.\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s all this?\u201d asked Mr. Jaggers. \u201cYou, with an old father\u2014and you, with pleasant and playful ways?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d said Wemmick. \u201cIf I don\u2019t bring \u2019em here, what\u2019s the difference?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, putting his hand on my arm and smiling openly, \u201cthis fellow must be the craftiest impostor in all London.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d answered Wemmick, becoming bolder and bolder. \u201cI rather think you\u2019re the other.\u201d\\n\\nOnce more they exchanged those same odd looks, each still seeming to distrust the other, as if neither could quite believe he was being taken in.\\n\\n\u201c_You_ with a pleasant home?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers