          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8a9239f7ea3a35266eedaaa6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Why, this hint might lead to a truly exciting bit of superstition. I suppose you will call the bug _scarab\u00e6us caput hominis_, or something like that\u2014there are many similar names in the Natural Histories. But where are the _antenn\u00e6_ you mentioned?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe _antenn\u00e6_!\u201d Legrand said, and he seemed to be getting unaccountably hot under the collar. \u201cI\u2019m sure you can see the _antenn\u00e6_. I made them as clear as they are in the original insect, and I think that should be enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well,\u201d I said, \u201cmaybe you have\u2014but I still don\u2019t see them.\u201d Then I handed him the paper without any more comment. I didn\u2019t want to upset him further. Still, I was very surprised by how things had turned. His bad temper confused me. And as for the drawing of the beetle, there were plainly _no antenn\u00e6_ at all. The picture looked very much like the usual drawings of a death\u2019s-head.\\n\\nHe took the paper back in a very irritated way, and he was about to crumple it, as if he meant to throw it in the fire. Then, with a casual glance, he suddenly stopped and stared at the design. In an instant his face turned violently red, and then, a moment later, it became extremely pale. For several minutes he kept studying the drawing closely, sitting right where he was. At last he stood up, took a candle from the table, and went to sit on a sea-chest in the farthest corner of the room. He examined the paper again, turning it over and looking at it from every direction.\\n\\nHe said nothing, but his behavior greatly surprised me. Still, I thought it was safer not to make any remarks and worsen his growing mood. After a while he took a wallet from his coat pocket, put the paper carefully inside it, and then placed both in a writing-desk that he locked. He seemed calmer afterward, but his earlier excitement was gone. He didn\u2019t seem so much angry as lost in thought. As the evening went on, he became more and more absorbed in his reverie, and nothing I said could pull him out of it.\\n\\nI had planned to spend the night at the hut, as I often had before. But seeing my host like this, I decided it was best to leave. He didn\u2019t try to persuade me to stay. When I went, he shook my hand with even more than his usual warmth.\\n\\nAbout a month later\u2014during the time when I hadn\u2019t seen Legrand at all\u2014I received a visit in Charleston from his man, Jupiter. I had never seen the old negro look so downhearted, and I feared that something serious had happened to my friend.\"}"
