          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8cbe9f80a9adea71e6c56194\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Knights and their horses, clad in heavy armor, couldn\u2019t make much speed. Still, they\u2019d do as well as they could; now that the ground was firm and there was neither snow nor mud, they might manage a seven-mile pace. They\u2019d have to swap horses a time or two, and they ought to reach us around six o\u2019clock, or just after. That would leave plenty of daylight, and they\u2019d spot the white cloth I\u2019d tie around my right arm, and then I\u2019d take command. We\u2019d close in on that prison and have the king out before long. It would look striking and picturesque enough, given the circumstances, though I would have preferred midday, if only because the whole business would have had a more theatrical air to it.\\n\\nNow, to build up the strings for my bow, I figured I\u2019d go and find some of the people I\u2019d recognized earlier, and let them know me. That should help us out of our scrape, even without the knights. But I had to be careful\u2014this was a touchy undertaking. I needed to dress in rich finery, and it wouldn\u2019t do to dash and leap into it. No, I\u2019d have to work up to it gradually: buying one outfit after another, in shops set well apart, and getting a finer piece each time, until I finally reached silk and velvet and was set for my plan. So I began.\\n\\nBut the whole scheme collapsed like a puff of smoke. The first corner I turned, I ran straight into one of our slaves, hanging about with a watchman. I cleared my throat at that instant, and he gave me a look so sharp it seemed to go straight through my bones. I take it he thought he\u2019d heard that cough before. I immediately ducked into a shop and moved along the counter, checking prices and keeping an eye on things from the corner of my vision. Those fellows had paused; they were talking among themselves and peering in at the door. I decided I\u2019d slip out the back way, if there was one, and I asked the shopwoman whether I might step out there and look for the escaped slave\u2014supposed to be hiding somewhere back behind. I told her I was an officer in disguise, and that my partner was waiting at the door with one of the murderers under his charge. Would she be kind enough to go tell him he needn\u2019t wait, but should head at once to the far end of the back alley, so he could be met and stopped when I went to roust him out.\\n\\nShe was practically bursting with eagerness to see one of those already famous murderers, and she set off on the errand right away.\"}"
