{"request_id":"c958e04374599cc61633e0e5","rewritten_text":"The young people were all eager to see Lyme. Captain Wentworth talked about going there again himself; it was only seventeen miles from Uppercross. And although it was November, the weather was by no means bad. In short, Louisa—more eager than the most eager—had made up her mind to go, and besides the pleasure of doing what she liked, she was now encouraged by the idea of earning merit by sticking to her own plan. So she pushed aside all her father and mother’s wishes that they should wait until summer. To Lyme they were to go—Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth.

The first careless plan had been to leave in the morning and return at night, but Mr Musgrove would not agree, for the sake of his horses. When it was considered more sensibly, a day in the middle of November would not leave much time for seeing a new place, after deducting seven hours—since the nature of the country required it—for going and coming back. They were therefore to stay the night there, and should not expect to be back until the next day’s dinner. This was felt to be a considerable improvement. And although they all gathered at the Great House for breakfast at a rather early hour and set off very punctually, it was so far past noon before the two carriages—Mr Musgrove’s coach, with the four ladies inside, and Charles’s curricle, in which he drove Captain Wentworth—were descending the long hill into Lyme, and then entering the even steeper street of the town itself, that it was clear they would have no more than time to look around before the light and warmth of the day were gone.

Once they had secured accommodations and ordered dinner at one of the inns, the next thing to do was unquestionably to walk straight down to the sea. They had arrived too late in the year for any amusement or variety that Lyme, as a public place, might offer. The rooms were shut up, the lodgers almost all gone, and scarcely any family remained except those of the residents. And since there is nothing much to admire in the buildings themselves, what will catch the stranger’s eye is the town’s remarkable setting: the principal street almost hurrying into the water; the walk to the Cobb, circling around the pleasant little bay, which in season is lively with bathing machines and people; the Cobb itself, with its old wonders and new improvements; and the very beautiful line of cliffs stretching out to the east of the town. A very strange stranger indeed must be the one who does not find charms in the immediate surroundings of Lyme, and who does not want to know it better."}

