          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7ff77c55047cf4e14b03dfa4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201c\u2018We\u2019ve been to your residence,\u2019 said the first. \u2018We weren\u2019t fortunate enough to find you there, and we were told you were probably walking in this direction, so we followed in the hope of catching up. Will you please get into the carriage?\u2019\\n\\n\u201cThe way both of them spoke was commanding, and as they said it they moved so that I was positioned between them and the carriage door. They were armed. I was not.\\n\\n\u201c\u2018Gentlemen,\u2019 said I, \u2018pardon me; but I usually ask who it is that has the honour to seek my assistance, and what the matter is that I\u2019m being summoned to address.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cThe second man answered. \u2018Doctor, your clients are people of standing. As for the nature of the case, our confidence in your skill assures us that you\u2019ll understand it for yourself better than we can describe it. That\u2019s enough. Will you please get into the carriage?\u2019\\n\\n\u201cI could do nothing but comply, and I got in in silence. They both got in after me\u2014the last one springing in after putting up the steps. The carriage turned around and drove on at its former speed.\\n\\n\u201cI repeat this conversation exactly as it happened. I have no doubt it is, word for word, the same. I set down everything exactly as it took place, forcing my mind not to drift from the task. Where I make the broken marks that follow here, I stop for the time being, and put my paper back in its hiding-place.\\n\\n*****\\n\\n\u201cThe carriage left the streets behind, passed the North Barrier, and came out onto the country road. At two-thirds of a league from the Barrier\u2014I didn\u2019t measure the distance then, but later, when I traversed it\u2014it turned off from the main avenue and soon stopped at a solitary house. We all three got out and walked, along a damp, soft footpath in a garden where a neglected fountain had overflowed, to the door of the house. It wasn\u2019t opened right away in response to the ringing of the bell, and one of my two conductors struck the man who opened it across the face with his heavy riding glove.\\n\\n\u201cThere was nothing in this action that particularly caught my attention, because I had seen common people struck more often than dogs. But the other of the two, also angry, struck the man in the same way with his arm; and the look and bearing of the brothers were so exactly alike that it was then I first realized they were twin brothers.\\n\\n\u201cFrom the moment we got down at the outer gate\u2014which we found locked, and which one of the brothers had opened to let us in and then relocked\u2014I had heard cries coming from an upper room.\u201d\"}"
