{"request_id":"afdc11a4f1beaf244db91290","rewritten_text":"“By eight o’clock I had actually reached an altitude of seventeen miles above the earth’s surface. It therefore struck me as plain that my ascent was not merely continuing, but that the speed of it was increasing; and even if I had not let go of the ballast, the change would still have shown itself, though only a little. The pains in my head and ears came back in violent fits at intervals, and I still bled now and then from the nose; yet, taking everything together, I endured far less than I might have expected. Still, I found myself breathing more and more laboriously every moment, and each inhalation brought with it a troublesome spasmodic action in the chest. I now unpacked the condensing apparatus and set it in readiness for immediate use.

“The earth’s appearance at this stage of my ascent was truly beautiful. To the west, the north, and the south—so far as my sight could carry—stretched an unbounded expanse of ocean, apparently smooth and unbroken, which every moment deepened into a richer blue and began already to show a slight convex curve. Far to the east, though still clearly visible, lay the islands of Great Britain, the whole Atlantic coasts of France and Spain, and a small portion of the northern continent of Africa. Not a single building could be distinguished, and even the proudest cities of mankind had vanished entirely from the face of the earth. From the rock of Gibraltar, now reduced to a faint speck, the dark Mediterranean spread eastward, strewn with shining islands like stars scattered across the heavens, until the whole body of its waters seemed at last to pour headlong over the brink of the horizon; and I caught myself listening, on tiptoe, for the echoes of the great cataract. Above me the sky was a deep, jet-black, and the stars shone with great brilliance.

“The pigeons, which at this time appeared to be suffering greatly, led me to decide to give them their liberty. I first untied one of them—a handsome gray-mottled pigeon—and set him upon the rim of the wicker-work. He looked exceedingly distressed, casting anxious glances about, fluttering his wings, and making a loud cooing, yet he could not be persuaded to trust himself away from the car. I took him up at last and threw him to a distance of about half a dozen yards from the balloon. He made, however, no effort to come down as I had expected; instead, he struggled with great vehemence to get back, uttering at the same time very shrill and piercing cries. At length he managed to regain his former place on the rim, but scarcely had he done so when his head sank upon his breast, and he fell dead within the car.”"}

