ering greatly, led me to decide to give them their liberty. I first untied one of them\u2014a handsome gray-mottled pigeon\u2014and set him upon the rim of the wicker-work. He looked exceedingly distressed, casting anxious glances about, fluttering his wings, and making a loud cooing, yet he could not be persuaded to trust himself away from the car. I took him up at last and threw him to a distance of about half a dozen yards from the balloon. He made, however, no effort to come down as I had expected; instead, he struggled with great vehemence to get back, uttering at the same time very shrill and piercing cries. At length he managed to regain his former place on the rim, but scarcely had he done so when his head sank upon his breast, and he fell dead within the car.\u201d\"}"
