          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0f261e036c2e52cfd72988f2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGood-bye, Uncle George. I\u2019ll be late for lunch if I linger any longer. Thank you for the information you gave me. I\u2019ve always liked knowing everything about my new friends, and nothing at all about my old ones.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere are you taking lunch, Harry?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt Aunt Agatha\u2019s. I\u2019ve asked myself and Mr. Gray. He\u2019s her newest _prot\u00e9g\u00e9_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHumph! Tell your Aunt Agatha, Harry, that she needn\u2019t trouble me with her charity appeals any more. I\u2019m tired of them. The woman actually believes I have nothing better to do than write cheques for her foolish little fancies.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cVery well, Uncle George. I\u2019ll pass it on, though it won\u2019t change a thing. People who are philanthropic lose all feeling for humanity. That\u2019s their distinguishing trait.\u201d\\n\\nThe old gentleman growled his approval and rang for his servant. Lord Henry went on past the low arcade into Burlington Street, then turned his steps toward Berkeley Square.\\n\\nSo that was the tale of Dorian Gray\u2019s parentage. Even as crudely as it had been laid before him, it still stirred him with the hint of a strange, almost modern romance. A beautiful woman risking everything for a wild passion. A few reckless weeks of happiness cut short by a hideous, treacherous crime. Months of wordless agony, and then a child born in pain. The mother snatched away by death, the boy left to solitude and the rule of an old, loveless man. Yes; it was an absorbing background. It shaped the boy, made him, as it were, more perfect. For behind every exquisite thing that exists there is something tragic. Worlds must be in travail, so that the meanest flower may bloom.... And how delightful he had been at dinner the night before\u2014how, with startled eyes and lips parted in frightened pleasure, he had sat opposite to him at the club, the red candle-shades turning the waking wonder of his face to a deeper rose. Speaking with him was like drawing music from an exquisite violin. He responded to every touch and every thrill of the bow.... There was something dreadfully captivating in the act of exerting influence. Nothing else was like it. To send one\u2019s soul into some gracious form, and let it remain there for a moment; to have one\u2019s own intellectual thoughts returned with all the added music of passion and youth; to pour one\u2019s temperament into another, as though it were a subtle fluid or a strange perfume\u2014there was a genuine joy in that, perhaps the most satisfying joy left to us in an age so narrow and vulgar as our own, an age grossly given over to the flesh in its pleasures, and grossly common in its aims.... This lad was a marvellous type as well\u2014one whom, by such a curious turn, he had met in Basil\u2019s studio, or at least could be made into a marvellous type.\"}"
