          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0a4d8f61e04d3c2d4096a652\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Moreover, this knot is one that only a few besides sailors can tie, and it\u2019s peculiar to the Maltese. I picked up the ribbon at the foot of the lightning rod. It could not have belonged to either of the deceased. Now, if I\u2019m wrong in my conclusion from this ribbon\u2014that the Frenchman was a sailor belonging to a Maltese vessel\u2014then I\u2019ve still done no harm by saying what I did in the advertisement. If I\u2019m mistaken, he will simply assume that I\u2019ve been misled by some circumstance he won\u2019t bother to look into. But if I\u2019m right, then a great advantage is gained. Though he\u2019s aware of the situation and innocent of the murder, the Frenchman will naturally hesitate before replying to the advertisement\u2014before demanding the Ourang-Outang. He will reason like this:\u2014\u2018I\u2019m innocent; I\u2019m poor; my Ourang-Outang is of great value\u2014to someone in my circumstances it\u2019s practically a fortune. Why should I give it up because of idle fears of danger? It\u2019s right there within my reach. It was found in the Bois de Boulogne\u2014at a vast distance from the scene of that slaughter. How could it ever be suspected that a brute animal did the deed? The police are to blame; they\u2019ve failed to obtain the slightest clue. Even if they traced the animal, it would be impossible to prove that I knew about the murder, or to implicate me in guilt because of that knowledge. Above all, I am known. The advertiser identifies me as the owner of the beast. I don\u2019t know how far his knowledge goes. If I avoid claiming property of such great value\u2014property that it\u2019s known I possess\u2014I\u2019ll at least make the animal liable to suspicion. It\u2019s not my plan to draw attention either to myself or to the beast. I\u2019ll answer the advertisement, get the Ourang-Outang, and keep it close until this matter has blown over.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAt that moment, we heard a step on the stairs.\\n\\n\u201cBe ready,\u201d said Dupin, \u201cwith your pistols, but don\u2019t use them and don\u2019t show them until you get a signal from me.\u201d\\n\\nThe front door of the house had been left open, and the visitor had entered without ringing and gone several steps up the staircase. Now, however, he seemed to pause. Soon we heard him going back down. Dupin was moving quickly toward the door when we heard him coming up again. He didn\u2019t turn back a second time; instead, he stepped up with determination and knocked on the door of our room.\\n\\n\u201cCome in,\u201d said Dupin, in a cheerful, hearty tone.\\n\\nA man entered. He was a sailor, clearly\u2014a tall, thickset, muscular-looking person, with a certain daredevil expression on his face, not entirely unappealing.\"}"
