          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"41581a931bce3767ce63e864\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On Saturday at noon I went to the well and stood there for a while, watching. Merlin was still at it with his smoke-powders\u2014scratching at the air and muttering nonsense as fiercely as ever\u2014yet he looked distinctly cast down, because, naturally, he hadn\u2019t even managed to get a sweat going in that well yet. At last I said:\\n\\n\u201cPartner, what does the business look like by now?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSee for yourself: I am even now trying the mightiest enchantment ever known to the princes of the occult arts in the Eastern lands. If it fails me, nothing else can help. Wait until I\u2019m done.\u201d\\n\\nThis time he sent up a smoke that darkened the whole region, and it must have made things awkward for the hermits, since the wind was their way; it rolled down over their dens in a thick, rolling fog. He poured out a torrent of words to go with it, and twisted his body and sawed the air with his hands in the strangest fashion. After twenty minutes he dropped down, panting and nearly spent. Then the abbot arrived with several hundred monks and nuns, and behind them came a crowd of pilgrims and a couple of acres of foundlings, all drawn by the astonishing smoke and all in a fine state of excitement. The abbot asked, with anxious urgency, whether there were any results. Merlin answered:\\n\\n\u201cIf any work of mortal hands could break the spell that holds these waters, the attempt I\u2019ve just made would have done it. It has failed; and so I now know that what I feared is indeed true. The sign of this failure is that the most powerful spirit known to the magicians of the East\u2014whose name no one may speak and live\u2014has laid his spell upon this well. No mortal breathes, nor ever will, who can uncover the secret of that spell; and without the secret, none can break it. The water will not flow again, Father. I\u2019ve done what a man can. Let me go.\u201d\\n\\nNaturally, this set the abbot into a fair amount of consternation. He faced me, and you could see it plainly in his expression, saying:\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ve heard him. Is it true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSome of it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot all, then\u2014not all! Which part is true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat the spirit with the Russian name has put his spell upon the well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood heavens\u2014then we\u2019re ruined!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPossibly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not certainly? You mean, not certainly?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s the point.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd so you also mean that when he says none can break the spell\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. When he says that, he\u2019s saying what isn\u2019t necessarily true. There are circumstances under which an attempt to break it might have some chance\u2014only a small one, a trifling one\u2014of succeeding.\u201d\"}"
