          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dc7b6f317fe238daef0d371b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He shivered, then went back and closed the window behind him.\\n\\nWhen he reached the door, he turned the key and opened it. He did not even look at the dead man. He felt the secret to everything was not to understand what was happening. The friend who had painted the portrait that had led to all his misery was no longer in his life. That was enough.\\n\\nThen he remembered the lamp. It was a strange one, made in Moorish style. It was dull silver, decorated with patterns of burnished steel, and set with rough turquoise stones. Maybe his servant would not notice it, and no one would ask questions. He hesitated, then turned back and took it from the table. He could not stop himself from looking at the dead thing. How still it was. How terrible the long hands looked, so white. It was like a horrible wax figure.\\n\\nAfter he locked the door behind him, he moved quietly downstairs. The woodwork creaked, and it seemed to cry out as if it hurt. He stopped several times and waited. No\u2014everything was quiet. It was only the sound of his own footsteps.\\n\\nWhen he got to the library, he saw the bag and coat in the corner. They had to be hidden somewhere. He unlocked a secret press built into the wall paneling, a place where he kept his own unusual disguises, and put the bag and coat inside. He could burn them later. Then he took out his watch. It was twenty minutes to two.\\n\\nHe sat down and started to think. Every year\u2014almost every month\u2014men were strangled in England for what he had done. It felt like murder was in the air. As if some red star had come too close to the earth. Still, what proof was there against him? Basil Hallward had left the house at eleven. No one had seen him come back in. Most of the servants were at Selby Royal. His valet had gone to bed.\\n\\nParis. Yes. Basil had gone to Paris, and he would take the midnight train, just as he planned. Because of his quiet, careful habits, it would be months before anyone became suspicious. Months. By then, everything could be destroyed.\\n\\nA sudden thought came to him. He put on his fur coat and hat and went into the hall. He paused there, listening to the slow, heavy steps of the policeman outside on the pavement, and watching the flash of the bull\u2019s-eye reflected in the window. He waited and held his breath.\\n\\nAfter a few moments, he pulled back the latch and slipped out, closing the door very softly behind him. Then he began ringing the bell.\"}"
