{"request_id":"20741e0f66e623ff8b9f2bf6","rewritten_text":"I cannot properly set down the rapture with which I turned away from the political quarrels at home, and from the bodily miseries of far-off lands, toward my own dear home, toward the chosen dwelling of goodness and love; toward peace, and the exchange of every hallowed sympathy. If I had never left Windsor, these feelings would not have burned so brightly; yet in Greece I had been the victim of fear and of wretched change. In Greece, after a stretch of anxiety and sorrow, I had watched two depart—two whose very names stood as the sign of greatness and virtue. Still, no such calamities could intrude on the domestic circle that remained to me, while, sheltered in our beloved forest, we spent our days in quiet. Some small alteration, to be sure, came with the passing years; and time, as it always does, set the marks of mortality upon our pleasures and hopes. Idris—the most affectionate wife, sister, and friend—was a tender, devoted mother. Her love was not like that of many, a mere diversion; it was a true passion. We had three children: one, the second in age, died while I was in Greece. That blow shattered the proud, exultant joy of maternity into grief and terror. Before it, the little ones—born of her, the young heirs of her brief life—seemed to her to have a secure lease of existence; afterward she feared that the pitiless destroyer might seize her remaining darlings, as it had taken their brother. The smallest illness brought on convulsions of dread; she was wretched whenever she was in the least separated from them. She had gathered her store of happiness in their frail being, and kept it always under watch, lest the sly thief, as before, steal those precious gems. Yet she had, in truth, little reason for fear. Alfred, now nine years old, was an upright, manly little fellow, with a radiant brow, gentle eyes, and a kind disposition that was still independent. Our youngest was still an infant; but his soft cheek carried the roses of health, and his tireless liveliness filled our rooms with innocent laughter.

Clara had already passed the age, when her silent ignorance had been the source of Idris’s anxieties. Clara was dear to her, and dear to all. There was so much understanding joined to innocence, so much feeling tempered by restraint, and seriousness joined to perfect good-humor; a beauty so transcendent, joined to such winning simplicity, that she seemed like a pearl set in the shrine of our possessions—a treasure of wonder and excellence.

At the start of winter, our Alfred, now nine, went first to school at Eton. It struck him as the first real step toward manhood, and he was accordingly pleased. Sharing study and amusement brought out the best in him: his steady perseverance, his generosity, and his firmness kept in good order."}

