          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"af5936dc40714b2e0cfdd686\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Watching him in these moods, I often found myself drifting into the old philosophy of the Bi-Part Soul, and I amused myself with the fancy of a double Dupin\u2014the creative one and the resolvent.\\n\\nLet it not be supposed, from what I have just said, that I am laying out any mystery or writing a romance. What I described in the Frenchman was simply the result of an excited, or perhaps a diseased, intelligence. Still, an example of the character of his remarks at those moments will best convey the idea.\\n\\nWe were strolling one night down a long, dirty street near the Palais Royal. Both of us, apparently lost in thought, had not spoken a single word for at least fifteen minutes. All at once Dupin broke in with these words:\\n\\n\u201cHe\u2019s a very small fellow, that\u2019s true, and he\u2019d do better at the _Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere can be no doubt of that,\u201d I replied, without meaning to, and only afterward\u2014so absorbed had I been in my reflections\u2014did I notice the extraordinary way the speaker had fallen into step with my thoughts. In an instant I snapped back to myself, and my astonishment was profound.\\n\\n\u201cDupin,\u201d I said gravely, \u201cthis is beyond my comprehension. I don\u2019t hesitate to say that I\u2019m amazed, and I can barely believe what I\u2019m hearing. How could you possibly know I was thinking of \u2014\u2014?\u201d I paused to make sure, beyond any doubt, that he really knew whom I was thinking about.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014 of Chantilly,\u201d he said. \u201cWhy do you pause? You were telling yourself that his diminutive figure didn\u2019t suit tragedy.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly what had been the subject of my reflections. Chantilly had once been a cobbler from the Rue St. Denis; after he became obsessed with the stage, he had tried to play Xerxes in Cr\u00e9billon\u2019s so-called tragedy, and he\u2019d been notoriously mocked for it.\\n\\n\u201cTell me, for Heaven\u2019s sake,\u201d I exclaimed, \u201cthe method\u2014if there is a method\u2014by which you\u2019ve been able to read my soul in this matter.\u201d In truth, I was even more startled than I was willing to admit.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the fruiterer,\u201d my friend replied, \u201cwho led you to the conclusion that the mender of soles wasn\u2019t tall enough for Xerxes _et id genus omne_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fruiterer!\u2014you astonish me\u2014I don\u2019t know any fruiterer at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe man who bumped into you as we entered the street\u2014it might have been fifteen minutes ago.\u201d\\n\\nI now remembered that, as we passed from the Rue C\u2014\u2014 into the thoroughfare where we were standing, a fruiterer carrying a large basket of apples on his head had nearly knocked me down by accident; but I still couldn\u2019t understand what that had to do with Chantilly.\"}"
