          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e81eae044f177b1a136bcff9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The little man\u2019s body was far broader than it should have been, making his whole figure look oddly round. His feet could not be seen at all. Still, a hard, suspicious-looking substance sometimes pushed out through a tear in the bottom of the car, or, more accurately, through the top of the hat. His hands were extremely large. His hair was very gray, gathered into a queue behind. His nose was very long, bent, and inflamed-looking. His eyes were full, bright, and sharp. Even though his chin and cheeks were wrinkled with age, they were broad, puffy, and doubled. Yet there was no sign of any ears anywhere on his head.\\n\\nThis strange little gentleman wore a loose surtout made of sky-blue satin, with tight breeches to match, held in place by silver buckles at the knees. His vest was made of bright yellow material. A white taffety cap sat tilted to one side of his head. To finish his outfit, a blood-red silk handkerchief wrapped around his throat and fell neatly onto his chest, tied into an elaborate bow-knot that was far too large.\\n\\nAs I said before, once he had come down to about one hundred feet above the ground, the little old man suddenly became frightened and seemed unwilling to get any closer to the earth. So he stopped at once. He did this by throwing out a quantity of sand from a canvas bag, which he lifted with great difficulty.\\n\\nThen he hurried and fidgeted as he pulled a large morocco pocket-book from a side pocket in his surtout. He held it up suspiciously, stared at it with extreme surprise, and clearly seemed shocked by how heavy it was. At last he opened it. From inside, he drew a huge letter, sealed with red sealing wax and tied carefully with red tape, and dropped it right at the feet of the burgomaster, Superbus Von Underduk.\\n\\nHis Excellency bent down to pick it up. But the aeronaut, still very upset and apparently having no more reason to stay in Rotterdam, began preparing to leave. Since he needed to throw out some ballast to rise again, he tossed out half a dozen bags one after another. He did this without bothering to empty them first. Unfortunately, every bag tumbled onto the burgomaster\u2019s back, and rolled him over and over\u2014no fewer than twenty-one times\u2014in full view of every man in Rotterdam.\\n\\nIt should not be assumed, however, that the great Underduk let this disrespect pass without consequence.\"}"
