          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b431f7bfbc66a22598cb5938\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She and the children could stay quite comfortably.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, Papa\u2014that\u2019s something you\u2019ve never been able to do, and I don\u2019t think you ever will. Isabella can\u2019t bear to stay behind her husband.\u201d\\n\\nThat was too true to argue with. Even though it was unwelcome, Mr. Woodhouse could only give a quiet, resigned sigh. And when Emma saw that his mood was affected by the idea of his daughter being so attached to her husband, she quickly changed the subject in a way that would lift his spirits.\\n\\n\u201cHarriet should spend as much time with us as she can while my brother and sister are here. I\u2019m sure she\u2019ll enjoy the children. We\u2019re very proud of them, aren\u2019t we, Papa? I wonder which one she\u2019ll think is the handsomest\u2014Henry or John?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAye, I wonder which she\u2019ll think. Poor little dears\u2014how happy they\u2019ll be to come. They really love being at Hartfield, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say they do, sir. I\u2019m sure I don\u2019t know anyone who wouldn\u2019t.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHenry is a fine boy, but John is very much like his mother. Henry is the eldest. He was named after me, not after his father. John, the second, is named after his father. Some people are surprised, I believe, that the eldest wasn\u2019t named after him, but Isabella would have him called Henry, and I thought that was very sweet. And he\u2019s a very clever boy, indeed. They\u2019re all remarkably clever, and they have so many charming little habits. They\u2019ll come and stand by my chair and say, \u2018Grandpapa, can you give me a bit of string?\u2019 Once Henry even asked me for a knife, but I told him knives are only made for grandpapas. I think their father is too rough with them far too often.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe seems rough to you,\u201d Emma said, \u201conly because you are so gentle yourself. But if you compared him with other fathers, you wouldn\u2019t think him rough. He wants his boys to be active and strong. If they misbehave, he can give them a sharp word now and then, but he is an affectionate father\u2014certainly Mr. John Knightley is an affectionate father. The children all love him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd then their uncle comes in and throws them up to the ceiling in a very frightening way!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut they like it, Papa. There\u2019s nothing they enjoy more. It\u2019s such fun for them that, if their uncle didn\u2019t set a rule about taking turns, whoever started would never let the other have a go.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I can\u2019t understand it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s the way it is with all of us, Papa. One half of the world can\u2019t understand the pleasures of the other.\u201d\"}"
