          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"709f84e2a74e343cd5efd9ef\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Shall I be borne aloft to the skies, on flow\u2019ry beds of ease,\\n\\nWhile others strive to seize the prize, and cut their way through blood-y seas?\\n\\nHe was looked upon as a remarkable reader. At church \u201csociables\u201d he was always asked to recite poetry; and once he had finished, the ladies would raise their hands and let them drop helplessly into their laps, \u201cwall\u201d their eyes, and shake their heads, as though to say, \u201cWords cannot express it; it is too beautiful, TOO beautiful for this mortal earth.\u201d\\n\\nAfter the hymn had been sung, the Rev. Mr. Sprague turned himself into a bulletin-board, and read off \u201cnotices\u201d of meetings and societies and all such matters until it seemed the list might run on to the crack of doom\u2014a peculiar habit still observed in America, even in cities, in spite of this present age of plentiful newspapers. The less there is to justify a time-honored custom, the more stubbornly it clings.\\n\\nAnd now the minister prayed. It was a kind, hearty prayer, and it went into particulars: it pleaded for the church, and for the church\u2019s little children; for the other churches in the village; for the village itself; for the county; for the State; for the State officers; for the United States; for the churches of the United States; for Congress; for the President; for the officers of the Government; for poor sailors, tossed by stormy seas; for the oppressed millions groaning beneath the heel of European monarchies and Oriental despotisms; for those who have the light and the good tidings, yet have not eyes to see nor ears to hear; for the heathen in the far islands of the sea; and it ended with a request that the words he was about to speak might find grace and favor, and be as seed sown in fertile ground, bringing forth in time a grateful harvest of good. Amen.\\n\\nThere was a rustle of dresses, and the congregation, standing, sat down. The boy whose story this book tells did not care for the prayer; he only endured it\u2014if he did that much. He was restless from start to finish; he kept count of the prayer\u2019s particulars, without meaning to\u2014for he was not listening, yet he knew the old ground and the clergyman\u2019s usual path through it\u2014and when a small bit of new matter was slipped in, his ear caught it and his whole nature bristled at the intrusion; he judged additions to be unfair, and downright villainous. While the prayer was still going on, a fly settled on the back of the pew ahead of him and worried his spirit by calmly rubbing its hands together, embracing its head with its arms, and polishing it so vigorously that it seemed to almost separate from the body, and a slender thread of a neck showed itself; it scraped its wings with its hind legs and smoothed them back to its body as if they were coat-tails; it went through its entire toilet with the same quiet confidence as though it knew it was perfectly safe.\"}"
