          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8e57e63961071f0fb423a615\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIs that all? It isn\u2019t much at all! Many are under the Republic\u2019s displeasure, and have to stare out through a little window. Jarvis Lorry. Banker. English. Which one is he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am he. Necessarily, being the last.\u201d\\n\\nIt is Jarvis Lorry who has answered all the questions so far. It is Jarvis Lorry who has stepped down and stands with his hand on the coach door, replying to a group of officials. They walk around the carriage at their leisure and climb up onto the box at their leisure, to look at the little luggage it carries on the roof; the country people linger nearby, press closer to the coach doors, and stare in greedily; a small child, carried by its mother, has its short arm held out so it can touch the wife of an aristocrat who has gone to the Guillotine.\\n\\n\u201cBehold your papers, Jarvis Lorry\u2014countersigned.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne can depart, citizen?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne can depart. Forward, my postilions! A good journey!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI salute you, citizens\u2014and the first danger is passed!\u201d\\n\\nThese are again Jarvis Lorry\u2019s words, as he clasps his hands and looks upward. Terror is in the carriage; there is weeping; there is the heavy breathing of the insensible traveler.\\n\\n\u201cAre we not going too slowly? Can\u2019t they be made to go faster?\u201d asks Lucie, clinging to the old man.\\n\\n\u201cIt would seem like flight, my darling. I must not urge them too much; it would raise suspicion.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLook back, look back\u2014see if we\u2019re being followed!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe road is clear, my dearest. So far, we are not being followed.\u201d\\n\\nHouses in twos and threes pass by us, solitary farms, ruined buildings, dye-works, tanneries, and the like; open country, avenues of leafless trees. The hard, uneven pavement is beneath us; the soft, deep mud lies on either side. Sometimes we veer into the edge mud to avoid the stones that clatter against us and shake the coach; sometimes we get stuck in ruts and sloughs there. The agony of our impatience becomes so intense that, in our wild alarm and hurry, we\u2019re ready to get out and run\u2014hide\u2014do anything but stop.\\n\\nOut of the open country, back among ruined buildings, solitary farms, dye-works, tanneries, and the like; cottages in twos and threes, avenues of leafless trees. Have these men tricked us and taken us back by another road? Isn\u2019t this the same place twice over? Thank Heaven, no. A village. Look back, look back, and see if we\u2019re being followed! Hush\u2014the posting-house.\\n\\nLeisurely, our four horses are taken out; leisurely, the coach stands in the little street, without horses and with no chance of ever moving again; leisurely, the new horses come into view, one by one; leisurely, the new postilions follow, sucking and braiding the lashes of their whips; leisurely, the old postilions count their money, make wrong additions, and end up with results that leave them dissatisfied.\"}"
