          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6905b16f7f02c0cc80c369af\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThese thoughts soothed me, and in the afternoon I sank into a deep sleep; yet the fever in my blood would not let me have any gentle dreams. The dreadful spectacle of the day before kept returning, always before my eyes: the females were flying, and the furious Felix was wrenching me from my father\u2019s feet. I woke worn out, and, finding it was already night, I slipped out from my hiding-place and went looking for food.\\n\\n\u201cWhen my hunger was satisfied, I took the well-known path that led to the cottage. Everything there seemed calm. I crawled into my hovel and waited in silent expectation for the usual hour when the family rose. That time came and went; the sun climbed high in the heavens, but the cottagers did not show themselves. I shook with violent fear, convinced that some dreadful misfortune had occurred. The cottage inside was dark, and I heard no movement; I cannot put into words the torment of that suspense.\\n\\n\u201cSoon two countrymen went by, but when they reached the cottage they stopped and began to talk, with violent gestures; I could not make out what they said, for they spoke the language of the country, which was different from that of my protectors. Not long after, Felix arrived with another man. I was startled, because I knew he had not left the cottage that morning, and I waited anxiously to learn from their conversation why these strange appearances had begun.\\n\\n\u201c\u2018Do you think,\u2019 said his companion to him, \u2018that you will be forced to pay three months\u2019 rent and to give up the produce of your garden? I don\u2019t want to gain any unfair advantage, so I ask you to take a few days to consider your decision.\u2019\\n\\n\u201c\u2018It is quite useless,\u2019 Felix answered; \u2018we can never live in your cottage again. My father\u2019s life is in the gravest danger because of the terrible thing I have told you. My wife and my sister will never recover from the horror they have suffered. I beg you, do not argue with me any longer. Take your tenement and let me flee from this place.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cFelix trembled violently as he spoke. He and his companion went into the cottage, stayed there for a few minutes, and then left. I never saw any member of the De Lacey family again.\\n\\n\u201cI spent the rest of the day in my hovel, sunk in a dull, utter despair. My protectors were gone, and they had snapped the only thread that kept me connected to the world. For the first time, the feelings of revenge and hatred filled my breast; I made no effort to restrain them, but let myself be carried along by the current, turning my thoughts toward injury and death.\u201d\"}"
