          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c07ab15521ac19103b94ffd8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, your father, naturally, may spare you\u2014if your mother can. A daughter is never of such consequence to a father. And if you can manage to remain another _month_ complete, I shall have it in my power to take one of you as far as London; for I mean to be there early in June, for a week. Since Dawson does not object to the barouche-box, there will be very comfortable room for one of you\u2014and, if the weather should chance to be cool, I would not object to taking you both, for neither of you is large.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are all kindness, madam; yet I think we must keep to our first plan.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine looked as though she had resigned herself. \u201cMrs. Collins, you must send a servant with them. You know I always say what I think, and I cannot bear the notion of two young women travelling post by themselves. It is quite improper. You must arrange for someone to go. I have the strongest dislike to that sort of thing. Young women ought always to be properly guarded and attended, according to their station. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate last summer, I made a point of having two men-servants accompany her. Miss Darcy, the daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley, and Lady Anne, could not possibly have appeared in any other way with propriety. I am exceedingly particular about such matters. You must send John with the young ladies, Mrs. Collins. I am glad it occurred to me to mention it; for it would truly be a discredit to _you_ to let them go alone.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy uncle is to send a servant for us.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Your uncle\u2014does he keep a man-servant? Then I am very pleased that you have someone who considers these things. Where will you change horses? Oh, Bromley, of course. If you give my name at the Bell, you will be taken care of.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine had still many other questions to put to them about their journey; and as she did not answer them all herself, some attention was required\u2014which Elizabeth thought fortunate for her; for, with her thoughts so taken up, she might otherwise have forgotten where she was. Reflection, she felt, must be kept for private hours: whenever she was alone, she indulged in it as her greatest relief; and not a day passed without a solitary walk, in which she could give way to all the pleasure of those disagreeable recollections.\\n\\nMr. Darcy\u2019s letter, she was soon likely to know by heart. She examined every sentence; and her feelings toward its writer were at times very different. When she recalled the manner of his address, she was still full of indignation; but when she considered how unjustly she had condemned and reproached him, her anger turned upon herself, and his disappointed feelings became the subject of her compassion.\u201d}\"}"
