          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"381b017880cf427abaf6d874\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The loud voice\u2014something we might call inorganic, because we could not connect it to any pipe or key\u2014fell silent. The girl turned to help her elderly companion, and only then did she finally notice us.\\n\\nIt was her father. Since she was a child, she had guided his steps in the dark. They were Germans from Saxony. They had moved there only a few years earlier, and in that time they had formed new ties with the villagers around them. Around the time the pestilence began, a young German student joined them. Their story was easy to understand. He was noble, and he loved the beautiful daughter of the poor musician. He followed them when they fled persecution by his friends. But soon the great leveler came with an unblunted scythe, mowing down the youth along with the grass and the tall flowers of the field. She kept herself alive for her father\u2019s sake. Because he was blind, she could keep up her lie. At first it was only an accident, but now they were alone\u2014only survivors in that land. He did not know anything had changed. When he listened to her music, he believed the mute mountains, the silent lake, and the unconscious trees were still there. In truth, he was the only one who could hear her, and she was the only one who could see.\\n\\nThe very day we arrived, she had been struck by a sickness that showed itself in the usual way. She was filled with horror at the thought of leaving her aged, sightless father alone on the empty earth. But she did not have the courage to tell him the truth. Her desperation only made her push herself harder. At the usual hour for evening prayers, she led him to the chapel. Even though she trembled and cried for him, she played the hymn perfectly\u2014on time, with no wrong notes\u2014one that celebrated the creation of the decorated earth, soon to become her grave.\\n\\nWe came to her as if we were visitors from heaven. Her courage, which had been strained to the breaking point, and her firmness, barely held together, collapsed the moment she felt relief. With a shriek she ran toward us, threw her arms around Adrian\u2019s knees, and, sobbing and crying in hysterical bursts, she managed only, \u201cO save my father!\u201d Then her grief poured out without restraint.\\n\\nPoor girl. She and her father now lie side by side beneath the tall walnut tree where her lover rests, and which, in her last moments, she had pointed out to us. Her father, at last realizing she was in danger, could not see the changes in her face. Still, he stubbornly held her hand until death made it cold and stiff. He did not move or speak even then. Only twelve hours later, gentle death carried him to his endless rest. They lie under the sod, and the tree is their monument. The sacred place is still clear in my memory, though the craggy Jura and the far, immeasurable Alps pale it. The church spire they used to attend still rises above the surrounding trees. And even though her hand is cold, I still imagine the divine music they loved drifting around, comforting their gentle ghosts.\"}"
