          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"64ed50fbc48f98dcc03919a6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Weeks and weeks passed, and I became more and more absorbed in you. Then something new happened. I had drawn you as Paris in delicate armor, and as Adonis with a huntsman\u2019s cloak and a gleaming boar-spear. Crowned with heavy lotus blossoms, you had sat on the prow of Adrian\u2019s barge, looking out across the green, muddy Nile. You had leaned over the quiet pool of some Greek woodland and watched, in the water\u2019s silent silver, the wonder of your own face. And it had all been exactly what art should be\u2014unconscious, ideal, and distant.\\n\\nOne day\u2014a day I sometimes think was fatal\u2014I decided to paint a portrait of you exactly as you are, not in the costume of dead ages, but in your own clothes and in your own time. I can\u2019t say whether it was the realism of the method, or simply the force of your own personality, presented to me so directly, without any haze or veil. But I know that as I worked, every flake and film of color seemed to show me my secret. I grew afraid that others would recognize my idolatry. I felt, Dorian, that I had said too much, that I had put too much of myself into it.\\n\\nThen I resolved never to allow the picture to be exhibited. You were a little annoyed, but you didn\u2019t understand what it meant to me. Harry, whom I told about it, laughed at me. I didn\u2019t mind that. When the picture was finished, and I sat alone with it, I felt that I was right.... Well, after a few days the thing left my studio. And once I had gotten rid of the unbearable fascination of its presence, it seemed to me that I had been foolish to think I had seen anything in it beyond the fact that you were extremely good-looking and that I could paint. Even now I can\u2019t help feeling that it\u2019s a mistake to believe that the passion we feel in creating is ever truly shown in the work we create. Art is always more abstract than we imagine. Form and color tell us only about form and color\u2014that\u2019s all. It often seems to me that art conceals the artist far more completely than it ever reveals him.\\n\\nSo when I received this offer from Paris, I decided to make your portrait the main piece in my exhibition. It never occurred to me that you would refuse. I see now that you were right. The picture cannot be shown. You must not be angry with me, Dorian, for what I have told you.\"}"
