          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f472213a2a12dfbb237dd6a1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The nephew glanced at him as he passed through, but it was no use, and he went on to the door.\\n\\n\u201cGood night!\u201d said the uncle. \u201cI look forward to seeing you again in the morning. Sleep well. Light Monsieur my nephew to his room there\u2014and burn Monsieur my nephew in his bed, if you like,\u201d he added to himself, before he rang his small bell again and called his valet to his own bedroom.\\n\\nWhen the valet came and went, Monsieur the Marquis paced back and forth in his loose robe, getting himself ready for sleep on that hot, still night. He rustled around the room, his soft slippers making no sound on the floor. He moved like a refined tiger\u2014like some enchanted marquis in a story of shameless wickedness, whose change into a tiger was either just starting or just ending.\\n\\nHe crossed the room to the other end and back again, letting the scraps of the day\u2019s journey come into his mind without being invited. He thought of the slow climb up the hill at sunset, the sun setting, the descent, the mill, the prison on the crag, the small village in the hollow, the peasants at the fountain, and the road mender with his blue cap pointing out the chain under the carriage. The fountain brought to mind the one in Paris, the small bundle on the step, the women bending over it, and the tall man with his arms raised, shouting, \u201cDead!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m calm now,\u201d said Monsieur the Marquis. \u201cI can go to bed.\u201d\\n\\nHe left only one light burning on the big hearth. Then he let his thin gauze curtains fall around him, and he listened as the night broke its silence with a long sigh while he settled down to sleep.\\n\\nFor three heavy hours, the stone faces on the outer walls stared blindly into the black night. For three heavy hours, the horses in the stables rattled their racks, the dogs barked, and the owl made a sound that had very little to do with the one men-poets usually give it. But these creatures are stubbornly unlikely to do what people expect.\\n\\nFor three heavy hours, the chateau\u2019s stone faces\u2014lion and human\u2014stared blindly at the night. Dead darkness covered the whole landscape, and the darkness added its own quiet to the quiet of the dust on all the roads. The burial place had reached the point where its small mounds of poor grass looked like they were all the same. The figure on the Cross might as well have come down, for all anyone could see of it. In the village, the taxers and the taxed were asleep. They were probably dreaming of banquets, as the starving often do, and of comfort and rest, as the driven slave and the yoked ox may. The lean people slept deeply, and were fed and freed.\"}"
