          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8b5bea3c412843516a4ad3c4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We both agreed on Adrian, and we loved her; but since she had not yet escaped childhood, she could not understand, the way I could, how great his gifts were. She also could not feel the same sympathy for his interests and opinions. I was always with him. There was a kind of sensitivity and sweetness in his nature that made our conversations feel gentle and almost unreal. Then he was cheerful, singing in his thoughts like a lark from a high, sky-blue tower. He would soar in his mind like an eagle, and he was as innocent as a dove with mild eyes. He could calm Perdita\u2019s seriousness and take the pain out of the tormenting restlessness of my own nature. I began to look back on my restless desires and my painful struggles with other people as if they were only a troubled dream. I felt as changed as if I had been turned into someone else, with new senses and a different way of nerves working, so that the world I saw in the mirror of my mind looked different. But that was not true. I was still the same in strength, still hungry for sympathy, still longing for real, active effort. My manly virtues did not leave me. The witch Urania spared the locks of Sampson while he lay at her feet; yet everything in me was softened and made more human.\\n\\nAdrian did not teach me only the cold truths of history and philosophy. While he used those lessons to help me control my own reckless and untrained spirit, he also showed me the living story of his own heart. He made me feel and understand its wonderful character.\\n\\nThe former queen of England had tried, even when her son was still a child, to plant bold and ambitious plans in his mind. She saw that he had genius and unusual talent, and she worked to develop those gifts so she could use them later to support her own goals. She encouraged his desire to learn and his impulsive courage. She even tolerated his wild love of freedom, hoping\u2014because this often happens\u2014that it would turn into a passion for command. She tried to raise him with resentment toward, and a wish to get revenge on, the people who had helped bring about his father\u2019s abdication. She did not succeed in that.\\n\\nStill, the stories she gave him\u2014no matter how distorted\u2014about a great and wise nation asserting its right to govern itself made him admire it. In his early years, he became a republican from principle. Even so, his mother did not give up. To her love of power and her proud sense of noble birth, she added firm ambition, patience, and self-control. She studied her son\u2019s character closely. Through praise, blame, and urging, she tried to find the right way to reach him. Even if the \u201cmusic\u201d that followed her touch seemed wrong to her, she kept her hopes on his talents and believed that, in the end, she would win him over.\"}"
