          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"92f965894cef257b882697b0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But it is of little consequence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI may as well ask,\u201d she answered, \u201cwhy, with such plain intent to offend and insult me, you chose to tell me that you liked me against your will, against your judgment, and even against your nature. Was that not a ready pretext for rudeness, if I _was_ rude? Yet I have other grounds for complaint. You know I do. Had my own feelings not turned against you\u2014had they been indifferent, or even inclined\u2014do you suppose any consideration would persuade me to accept the man who has been the instrument of ruining, perhaps forever, the happiness of a sister I love most dearly?\u201d\\n\\nAs she spoke, Mr. Darcy flushed; but the feeling passed quickly, and he listened without trying to interrupt her while she went on, \u201cI have every reason to think ill of you. No motive can make excusable the unjust and ungenerous part you played _there_. You cannot, you dare not deny that you were the chief\u2014if not the only\u2014means of setting them at odds, of exposing one to the world\u2019s censure for caprice and instability, the other to its mockery for hopes disappointed, and of bringing them both into the keenest misery.\u201d\\n\\nShe paused, and with no small indignation observed that he listened with an air that showed him wholly untouched by any remorse. He even regarded her with a smile that seemed meant to disbelieve her.\\n\\n\u201cCan you deny that you have done it?\u201d she repeated.\\n\\nWith an air of studied calm he replied, \u201cI have no wish to deny that I did everything in my power to separate my friend from your sister, or that I am glad of the result. Towards _him_ I have been kinder than towards myself.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth affected not to notice the politeness of the remark, but its meaning did not escape her, nor was it likely to soften her.\\n\\n\u201cBut it is not only this matter,\u201d she continued. \u201cMy dislike rests on more than what has just occurred. Long before it ever happened, my opinion of you was already formed. Your character was laid open in the account I received many months ago from Mr. Wickham. On that point, what have you to say? In what imagined act of friendship can you justify yourself here? Or by what misrepresentation can you expect to impose on others?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou show a lively concern for that gentleman\u2019s affairs,\u201d said Darcy, in a less steady voice, and with a deeper flush.\\n\\n\u201cWho, knowing what his misfortunes have been, could help feeling interest in him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis misfortunes!\u201d Darcy repeated, with contempt. \u201cYes, his misfortunes have been very great indeed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd those misfortunes, and your doing,\u201d cried Elizabeth, with spirit; \u201cYou have brought him down to his present condition\u2014comparative poverty.\u201d\"}"
