          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"14b40d9b8311b25057d2d16c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Why, we might well be in for a rather stirring piece of superstition from this hint. I suppose you\u2019ll name the bug _scarab\u00e6us caput hominis_, or something along those lines\u2014there are plenty of similar headings in the Natural Histories. Still, where are the _antenn\u00e6_ you mentioned?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe _antenn\u00e6!_\u201d Legrand said, and he appeared to be getting unaccountably hot under the collar at the very thought. \u201cI\u2019m sure you can see the _antenn\u00e6_. I made them as clear and separate as they are in the original insect, and I take that to be enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well,\u201d I replied, \u201cmaybe you have\u2014only I can\u2019t see them;\u201d and I handed him the paper without any further comment, not wanting to set his temper off. Yet I was genuinely astonished at how things had turned. His ill humor left me at a loss; and as for the beetle\u2019s drawing, there were plainly _no antenn\u00e6_ to be seen at all, and the whole _did_ resemble the usual death\u2019s-head engravings.\\n\\nHe took the paper back in a very peevish manner, and seemed on the point of crumpling it\u2014apparently to throw it into the fire\u2014when a casual look at the design suddenly held him fast. In the next moment his face flushed violently red; and then, almost at once, it became excessively pale. For several minutes he kept studying the drawing closely, as he sat. At last he stood up, took a candle from the table, and went to sit himself upon a sea-chest in the farthest corner of the room. There too he examined the paper with anxious care, turning it this way and that. He said nothing, but his behavior greatly surprised me; still, I thought it safer not to inflame the growing gloom of his temper with any remark. Presently he drew a wallet from his coat pocket, put the paper carefully into it, and laid both into a writing-desk, which he locked. He then regained a steadier composure, though the first air of excitement had entirely vanished. Yet he was less sulky than absorbed. As the evening dragged on, he sank deeper and deeper into reverie, from which none of my attempts could draw him out.\\n\\nI had meant to spend the night at the hut, as I often had before, but seeing my host in that state, I judged it right to take my leave. He did not urge me to stay; but when I went, he shook my hand with even more than his usual warmth.\\n\\nIt was about a month later\u2014during which time I had seen nothing of Legrand\u2014when I received a visit, in Charleston, from his man, Jupiter. I had never seen the good old negro look so cast down, and I feared that some serious misfortune had befallen my friend.\"}"
