          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b3df23a23c2447580b56cb58\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In my younger days, I never felt discontent. And if I was ever overcome by _ennui_, I could always be pulled back by the beauty of nature or by studying what is great and sublime in the works of human beings. But I am a blasted tree. The bolt has entered my soul, and I realized then that I would live long enough to show what I will soon stop being: a miserable sight of broken humanity, pitiful to others and unbearable to myself.\\n\\nWe spent a long time in Oxford. We wandered through the surrounding area, trying to find every place that could be connected to the most inspiring period of English history. Our little trips of discovery were often delayed by the things we kept coming across. We visited the tomb of the famous Hampden and the field where that patriot fell. For a moment, my soul rose above my degrading, miserable fears, and I could think about the noble ideas of liberty and self-sacrifice that these sights stood for. For an instant, I dared to throw off my chains and look around with a free, proud spirit. But the iron had already sunk into my flesh, and I sank back again, trembling and hopeless, into my miserable self.\\n\\nWe left Oxford with regret and went on to Matlock, where we stayed next. The countryside around this village looked even more like Switzerland. Still, everything was on a smaller scale, and the green hills lacked the bright crown of distant white Alps that always rise behind the pine-covered mountains of my own country. We visited the amazing cave and the small natural history rooms, where the curiosities were arranged in the same way as in the collections at Servox and Chamounix. When Henry said the name \u201cChamounix,\u201d it made me tremble, and I hurried to leave Matlock, because that frightening scene was now tied to it.\\n\\nFrom Derby, we kept traveling north. We spent two months in Cumberland and Westmorland. Now I could almost pretend I was among the Swiss mountains. The small patches of snow that still lingered on the north sides of the hills, the lakes, and the rush of the rocky streams were familiar sights, and they felt dear to me. We also met some people there, and they almost managed to trick me into being happy. Clerval\u2019s joy was even greater than mine. His mind grew broader in the company of talented men, and he discovered in himself abilities and resources he would never have believed he had while he was around people he considered his inferiors.\"}"
