          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"46cab6c8659e52a89cb76747\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Having seen our friend properly settled in his new office, we turned our attention to Windsor. The closeness of this place to London was such that it eased the idea of a painful separation when we left Raymond and Perdita. We said goodbye to them in the Protectoral Palace. It was quite moving to watch my sister step in, as if she were entering the spirit of the drama, and try to fill her role with the dignity it demanded. Her inner pride and her humility of manner were now more openly at odds. Her shyness wasn\u2019t forced; it came from the fear of not being properly valued, that slight sense of being overlooked by the world\u2014which also characterized Raymond. But Perdita thought more often of others than he did, and part of her bashfulness came from a wish to spare those around her any feeling of inferiority, a feeling that never once crossed her mind. Given the circumstances of her birth and upbringing, Idris would have been better suited to the set formulas of ceremony; yet the ease with which she carried out such actions, because it had become habit, made them feel tedious. Still, despite every drawback, Perdita clearly enjoyed her position. She was too full of new ideas to feel much pain when we departed. She said goodbye to us warmly, promised to visit soon, and yet she did not regret the circumstances that had caused our separation. Raymond\u2019s spirits were boundless. He didn\u2019t know what to do with his new authority; his head was full of plans, and he had not yet settled on any\u2014still, he promised himself, his friends, and the world that the era of his protectorship would be marked by some act of surpassing glory. So we talked about them and moralized, as with fewer people we returned to Windsor Castle. We felt intense relief at escaping political turmoil, and we threw ourselves into our solitude with even greater enthusiasm. We didn\u2019t lack for things to do; but my eager nature was now directed only toward intellectual effort. Hard study proved an excellent remedy for the fever of spirit that, in idleness, I would doubtless have been vulnerable to. Perdita had allowed us to take Clara back with us to Windsor; and she, along with my two lovely infants, was a constant source of interest and amusement.\\n\\nThe only thing that disturbed our peace was Adrian\u2019s health. It clearly declined, without any symptoms that would have led us to suspect his illness\u2014unless, perhaps, his brightened eyes, animated look, and flushed cheeks made us fear consumption. Yet he had neither pain nor fear. He threw himself into books with real ardor, and when he rested from study, it was in the company he loved best: his sister\u2019s and mine.\"}"
