          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c9202c2a5b319ec50b953243\",\"rewritten_text\":\"One day, while I was busy with my books and Mr. Pocket, I received a note in the mail, and the plain outside of it sent me into a great flutter. Even though I had never seen the handwriting it was addressed in, I could tell whose hand it was. It didn\u2019t start in any set way\u2014no \u201cDear Mr. Pip,\u201d or \u201cDear Pip,\u201d or \u201cDear Sir,\u201d or \u201cDear Anything\u201d\u2014but went like this:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am to come to London the day after tomorrow by the midday coach. I believe it was settled that you should meet me. At all events, Miss Havisham has that impression, and I write in obedience to it. She sends you her regard.\\n\\n\u201cYours, ESTELLA.\u201d\\n\\nIf there had been time, I probably would have ordered several suits of clothes for the occasion; but since there wasn\u2019t, I had to make do with what I had. My appetite vanished at once, and I found no peace or rest until the day arrived. But even then it didn\u2019t get any better. I was worse than ever, and started haunting the coach office in Wood Street, Cheapside, before the coach had even left the Blue Boar in our town. I knew perfectly well that I was being ridiculous, yet I still felt as if it wasn\u2019t safe to let the coach office out of my sight for more than five minutes at a time. In that state of irrational worry, I managed the first half-hour of a watch that was supposed to last four or five hours, when Wemmick ran into me.\\n\\n\u201cHalloa, Mr. Pip,\u201d he said. \u201cHow do you do? I wouldn\u2019t have thought this was _your_ beat.\u201d\\n\\nI told him I was waiting to meet someone coming up by coach, and I asked after the Castle and the Aged.\\n\\n\u201cBoth flourishing, thank you,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cand especially the Aged. He\u2019s in wonderful spirits. He\u2019ll be eighty-two next birthday. I\u2019ve got a notion of firing eighty-two times, if the neighborhood doesn\u2019t complain, and if my cannon holds up under the pressure. But this isn\u2019t London talk. Where do you think I\u2019m going?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo the office?\u201d I said, because he was heading that way.\\n\\n\u201cNext thing to it,\u201d Wemmick replied. \u201cI\u2019m going to Newgate. We\u2019re in a banker\u2019s-parcel case just now, and I\u2019ve been down the road taking a look at the scene of action, and I must have a word or two with our client.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid your client commit the robbery?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cBless your soul and body, no,\u201d Wemmick answered, very dryly. \u201cBut he\u2019s accused of it. So could you or I be. Either of us might be accused of it, you know.\u201d\"}"
