          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cb04ef16e11b655954dad0b3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAre you talking about\u2014Mr. Knightley?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course I am. I can\u2019t imagine anyone else\u2014and I thought you knew. When we talked about him, it was as clear as possible.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot quite,\u201d Emma replied, forcing calm, \u201cbecause everything you said then seemed to me to refer to a different person. I could almost say that you had named Mr. Frank Churchill. I\u2019m sure the service Mr. Frank Churchill did for you\u2014protecting you from the gypsies\u2014was what you were talking about.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Miss Woodhouse, how you forget!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Harriet, I remember perfectly well what I said on that occasion. I told you I didn\u2019t wonder at your attachment; that, considering the service he had done for you, it was extremely natural. And you agreed with it, speaking very warmly about your sense of that service, and even mentioning what you felt when he stepped forward to rescue you. The impression of it is still strong in my memory.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, dear,\u201d Harriet cried, \u201cnow I remember what you mean; but I was thinking of something completely different at the time. It wasn\u2019t the gypsies\u2014it wasn\u2019t Mr. Frank Churchill I meant. No!\u201d (with some lift of feeling) \u201cI was thinking of something far more precious\u2014of Mr. Knightley coming to ask me to dance, when Mr. Elton wouldn\u2019t stand up with me, and when there was no other partner in the room. That was the kind action; that was the noble benevolence and generosity; that was the service that made me begin to feel how far superior he was to every other person on earth.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood God!\u201d Emma cried. \u201cThis has been a most unfortunate\u2014most deplorable mistake! What is to be done?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou wouldn\u2019t have encouraged me, then, if you\u2019d understood me. At least, however, I can\u2019t be worse off than I would have been if the other had been the person; and now\u2014it is possible\u2014\u201d\\n\\nShe paused for a few moments. Emma couldn\u2019t speak.\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t wonder, Miss Woodhouse,\u201d she went on, \u201cthat you should feel such a great difference between the two, whether it\u2019s about me or anyone else. You must think one five hundred million times more highly of me than the other. But I hope, Miss Woodhouse, that\u2014supposing\u2014that if\u2014strange as it may seem\u2014. But you know those were your own words, and that more wonderful things had happened: matches of far greater disparity than between Mr. Frank Churchill and me. So it seems as if something like this could have happened before\u2014and if I should be so fortunate, beyond expression, as to\u2014if Mr. Knightley really\u2014if he doesn\u2019t mind the disparity, I hope, dear Miss Woodhouse, you won\u2019t set yourself against it and try to put difficulties in the way.\u201d\"}"
