          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"68237adda8741cd5b0f11111\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No, don\u2019t bring it up by hand then. Not a bit of it!\u201d\\n\\nJoe put forward more gravy, and I was too uneasy to accept it.\\n\\n\u201cHe was a world of trouble to you, ma\u2019am,\u201d Mrs. Hubble said, sympathizing with my sister.\\n\\n\u201cTrouble?\u201d my sister repeated. \u201cTrouble?\u201d\u2014and then she launched into a dreadful roll-call of every illness I\u2019d been blamed for, every stretch of sleeplessness I\u2019d caused, every high place I\u2019d tumbled from, and every low place I\u2019d tumbled into, every injury I\u2019d done myself, every time she\u2019d wished me in my grave, and how stubbornly I\u2019d refused to go there.\\n\\nI dare say the Romans must have worried one another terribly with their noses. Maybe that\u2019s why they turned out so restless. At any rate, Mr. Wopsle\u2019s Roman nose so irritated me while she was listing my misdeeds that I felt like yanking it until he cried out. Still, nothing I\u2019d endured so far could match the dreadful sensations that seized me when the pause came after my sister\u2019s recital\u2014during which everyone looked at me, as I felt keenly, with indignation and disgust.\\n\\n\u201cYet,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, steering the talk gently back to what they\u2019d been discussing, \u201cPork\u2014regarded as biled\u2014is rich, too; ain\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave a little brandy, uncle,\u201d my sister said.\\n\\nOh, heavens\u2014so it had finally arrived! He\u2019d taste it and say it was weak, he would, and then I was done for. I gripped the leg of the table under the cloth with both hands and waited for what would happen.\\n\\nMy sister went for the stone bottle, came back with it, and poured his brandy out\u2014no one else took any. The poor man fidgeted with his glass, lifted it, held it up to the light, set it down again, and kept me in suspense. All the while, Mrs. Joe and Joe were briskly clearing the table for the pie and pudding.\\n\\nI couldn\u2019t take my eyes off him. Always clinging to the table-leg with my hands and feet, I watched him toy with his glass, raise it, grin, throw his head back, and drink the brandy straight. The next moment the whole company was struck with utter consternation: he sprang to his feet, spun round several times in a dreadful, spasmodic whooping-cough dance, and bolted out the door. Through the window we could see him, violently plunging and spitting, making the most awful faces, and acting as though he\u2019d lost his mind.\\n\\nI held on tight as Mrs. Joe and Joe rushed after him.\"}"
