          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cb767e7b8d8892d5ccf0301d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond was thrilled by the strong feelings that came with his recovered health. He believed that, as a leading man among the Athenians, he held a position worthy of his ambition. And in his hope of conquering Constantinople, he expected an event that would stand out through the ages, an achievement unmatched in human history. He imagined that a city with deep historic meaning\u2014whose beautiful setting was the wonder of the world\u2014after many hundreds of years as a stronghold of the Moslems, would be freed from slavery and barbarism, and returned to a people known for genius, civilization, and a love of liberty. Perdita lay in his restored society, in his love, his hopes, and his fame, as a Sybarite rests on a luxurious couch. Every thought carried him away, and every feeling seemed to be warmed and comforted by something kind and soothing.\\n\\nWe arrived at Kishan on the 7th of July. The weather had been calm during our journey. Each day, before dawn, we left our camp for the night and watched the shadows pull back from hill and valley, while the sun\u2019s golden light approached. The soldiers who traveled with us took real pleasure in the beautiful countryside, showing their excitement in a lively, national way. When the sun rose, it was greeted with triumphant music, and the birds, heard now and then, filled the gaps between the songs. At noon, we set up our tents in a shady valley or in a cool wood among the mountains. A stream running over pebbles made a gentle sound that helped us sleep. Our evening march was calmer than the restless feeling of the morning, and it was even more enjoyable. If the band played, they naturally chose tunes with less intense emotion. The music would move from the farewell of love or a lament for being away, and then end with a solemn hymn that matched the quiet beauty of evening and lifted the soul toward something grand and religious. Often