          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ff1dbbb9dc9d47fc013a71e3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He shivered, then went back, closing the window behind him.\\n\\nOnce he reached the door, he turned the key and opened it. He didn\u2019t even look at the murdered man. He felt the secret to the whole thing was not to recognize what was happening. The friend who had painted the fatal portrait that had caused all his misery was gone from his life. That was enough.\\n\\nThen he remembered the lamp. It was a rather curious one, made in Moorish style\u2014dull silver, inlaid with arabesques of burnished steel, and studded with coarse turquoises. Maybe his servant wouldn\u2019t notice it, and no one would ask questions. He hesitated for a moment, then turned back and took it from the table. He couldn\u2019t help looking at the dead thing. How still it was! How horribly white the long hands looked! It was like some dreadful wax figure.\\n\\nAfter locking the door behind him, he moved quietly downstairs. The woodwork creaked and seemed to cry out as if it were in pain. He stopped several times and waited. No\u2014everything was still. It was only the sound of his own footsteps.\\n\\nWhen he reached the library, he saw the bag and coat in the corner. They had to be hidden somewhere. He unlocked a secret press set into the wainscoting\u2014a press where he kept his own strange disguises\u2014and put them inside. He could burn them later. Then he took out his watch. It was twenty minutes to two.\\n\\nHe sat down and began to think. Every year\u2014almost every month\u2014men were strangled in England for what he had done. There had been a kind of murder-madness in the air. Some red star had come too close to the earth.... Still, what evidence was there against him? Basil Hallward had left the house at eleven. No one had seen him come back in. Most of the servants were at Selby Royal. His valet had gone to bed.... Paris! Yes. Basil had gone to Paris, and by the midnight train, just as he\u2019d planned. With his reserved, peculiar habits, it would be months before anyone became suspicious. Months! Everything could be destroyed long before then.\\n\\nA sudden thought struck him. He put on his fur coat and hat and went out into the hall. He paused there, listening to the slow, heavy tread of the policeman outside on the pavement, and watching the flash of the bull\u2019s-eye reflected in the window. He waited and held his breath.\\n\\nAfter a few moments he drew back the latch and slipped out, shutting the door very gently behind him. Then he began ringing the bell.\"}"
