          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c44b589b579090cc03490593\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Suddenly Mr. Podgers let go of Lord Arthur\u2019s right hand and took hold of his left instead, stooping so far to inspect it that the bright gold rims of his spectacles looked as if they might almost rest on the palm. For a moment his expression turned into a stark, white mask of terror, but he quickly regained his _sang-froid_, and, lifting his eyes to Lady Windermere, said with a smile that seemed forced, \u201cIt is the hand of a charming young man.\\n\\n\u201cOf course it is!\u201d returned Lady Windermere. \u201cBut will he be a charming husband? That\u2019s what I want to know.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll charming young men are,\u201d said Mr. Podgers.\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think a husband ought to be too fascinating,\u201d Lady Jedburgh murmured, thoughtful. \u201cIt\u2019s so risky.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear child, they never are too fascinating,\u201d Lady Windermere cried. \u201cBut what I want are details. Details are the only things that interest. What is going to happen to Lord Arthur?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, within the next few months Lord Arthur will go a voyage\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes, his honeymoon, naturally!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd lose a relative.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot his sister, I hope?\u201d said Lady Jedburgh, in a tone full of pity.\\n\\n\u201cCertainly not his sister,\u201d answered Mr. Podgers, with a slight deprecating sweep of his hand. \u201cOnly a distant relative.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I\u2019m dreadfully disappointed,\u201d said Lady Windermere. \u201cI have nothing at all to tell Sybil tomorrow. Nobody cares about distant relatives these days. They went out of fashion years ago. Still, I suppose she\u2019d better have a black silk with her; it always serves for church. And now, let\u2019s go in to supper. They\u2019re sure to have eaten everything, but perhaps we\u2019ll find some hot soup. Fran\u00e7ois used to make excellent soup, but he\u2019s so stirred up about politics at present that I never feel quite sure about him. I do wish General Boulanger would keep still. Duchess, you must be tired?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot at all, dear Gladys,\u201d replied the Duchess, waddling toward the door. \u201cI\u2019ve enjoyed myself immensely, and the cheiropodist\u2014no, I mean the cheiromantist\u2014is quite fascinating. Flora, where can my tortoise-shell fan be? Oh, thank you, Sir Thomas, so much. And my lace shawl, Flora? Oh, thank you, Sir Thomas, how kind\u2014I'm sure\u201d; and at last the worthy woman managed to get downstairs without dropping her scent-bottle more than twice.\\n\\nAll the while Lord Arthur Savile had stayed standing by the fireplace, carrying the same dread in him, the same sickening certainty of evil to come. He gave his sister a sad smile as she passed him on Lord Plymdale\u2019s arm, looking lovely in her pink brocade and pearls, and he scarcely even heard Lady Windermere when she called to him to follow. He kept thinking of Sybil Merton, and the thought that anything might come between them brought tears to his eyes.\"}"
