          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a81ef16b16d37d3daf1e8a74\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once, however, the lieutenant asked why he had traveled so far across the ice in such a strange vehicle.\\n\\nHis face immediately turned to the darkest gloom, and he answered, \u201cTo find the one who ran away from me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd was the man you were chasing traveling in the same way?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen I think we must have seen him. The day before we picked you up, we saw some dogs pulling a sledge, with a man in it, across the ice.\u201d\\n\\nThis made the stranger pay close attention. He asked many questions about the route the d\u00e6mon, as he called him, had taken. Soon after, when he was alone with me, he said, \u201cI\u2019m sure I\u2019ve made you curious, and I\u2019ve made these good people curious too. But you\u2019re kind enough not to ask.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course,\u201d I said. \u201cIt would be very rude and inhuman of me to bother you with my own questions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet you saved me from a strange and dangerous situation. You have kindly brought me back to life.\u201d\\n\\nNot long after that, he asked whether I thought the ice breaking up had destroyed the other sledge. I told him I couldn\u2019t say for sure. The ice didn\u2019t break until close to midnight, and the traveler might have reached safety before then, but I couldn\u2019t know.\\n\\nFrom that point on, a new energy seemed to return to the stranger\u2019s failing body. He was very eager to stay on deck to watch for the sledge that had appeared before. But I persuaded him to remain in the cabin, because he was too weak to handle the cold air. I promised that someone would keep watch for him and let him know right away if anything new came into view.\\n\\nThis is my journal account of what has happened with this strange event up to today. The stranger has slowly started to get better, but he is still very quiet. He also seems uneasy whenever anyone other than me enters his cabin. Still, his manners are so kind and gentle that the sailors all take an interest in him, even though they have hardly spoken with him at all. As for me, I find myself growing to love him like a brother. His constant, deep grief moves me with real sympathy and compassion. He must have been a noble person in better days, because even now, in wreck and ruin, he remains so attractive and pleasant.\\n\\nIn one of my letters, I wrote to you, my dear Margaret, that I would find no friend on the wide ocean. Yet I have found a man who, before his spirit was crushed by misery, I would have been happy to have as the brother of my heart.\u201d}\"}"
