          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b6c9342b54d2cb29a4a22ceb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He shuddered, then withdrew, and shut the window after him.\\n\\nOnce he reached the door, he turned the key and opened it. He didn\u2019t even look toward the murdered man. He told himself that the secret of the whole business was not to acknowledge what was happening. The friend who had painted the fatal portrait to which all his misery had been owed had slipped out of his life. That alone was sufficient.\\n\\nThen the lamp came back to him. It was a rather strange thing, Moorish in workmanship\u2014dull silver, worked with arabesques of burnished steel, and set with rough turquoises. Maybe his servant would fail to notice it, and no questions would follow. He hesitated only a moment, then went back and took it from the table. He couldn\u2019t help staring at the dead object. How motionless it was! How ghastly white the long hands appeared! It looked like some dreadful wax figure.\\n\\nWith the door locked behind him, he moved quietly down the stairs. The woodwork creaked and seemed to cry out, as though it were in pain. He halted more than once and listened. No\u2014everything remained quiet. It was only the sound of his own steps.\\n\\nWhen he got to the library, he saw the bag and coat in the corner. They would have to be put away somewhere. He unlocked a secret press set into the wainscoting, a place where he kept his own odd disguises, and slid them inside. He could burn them later without trouble. Then he took out his watch. It was twenty minutes to two.\\n\\nHe sat down and started to think. Every year\u2014almost every month\u2014men were strangled in England for what he had done. There had been a kind of murderous frenzy in the air. Some red star had drifted too near the earth.... Still, what proof was there against him? Basil Hallward had left the house at eleven. No one had seen him come back in. Most of the servants were at Selby Royal. His valet had gone to bed.... Paris! Yes. Basil had gone to Paris, and by the midnight train, just as he meant to. With his peculiar, reserved ways, it would be months before anyone grew suspicious. Months! Everything could be destroyed long before then.\\n\\nA sudden idea struck him. He put on his fur coat and hat and went into the hall. There he paused, listening to the slow, heavy footfall of the policeman outside on the pavement, and catching the glint of the bull\u2019s-eye in the window. He waited, holding his breath.\\n\\nAfter a few moments he eased the latch and slipped out, closing the door as softly as he could. Then he began ringing the bell.\"}"
