{"request_id":"e5844c1c54b05bb1aec42399","rewritten_text":"Still, even then, I had not yet swallowed the bitter draught down to the last drop; I was not yet convinced that I had truly lost everything; I had not in each throb, in every nerve, in every passing thought, fully felt that I remained alone of my kind—that I was, in fact, the LAST MAN.

The day turned overcast, and a steady drizzle began just as the sun was setting. Even the heavens, I thought, seem to weep; and what dishonor is there, then, in a mortal man spending himself in tears? I recalled the old tales, where people are said to melt away through crying into fountains that gush without end. Would that it were so—then my fate might bear some resemblance to the watery end of Adrian and Clara. Yet grief is strange: it spins a net in which to trace the story of its misery through every shape and change it takes; it clings to all living things; it feeds on every object; it fills everything like light, and, like light, it lends its own colors to all.

In searching, I had wandered some distance from where I’d been cast, and I came upon one of those watch-towers that, at regular intervals, stand along the Italian coast. I welcomed the shelter, glad to find a work of human hands after staring so long at nature’s bleak emptiness; so I went in and climbed the rough, winding stairs up to the guard-room. Fate, for once, had been gentle: nothing dreadful remained of its former occupants. A few planks laid across two iron trestles, scattered with dried Indian-corn leaves, made the bed offered to me; and an open chest, holding some half-rotted biscuits, stirred up an appetite I might have had before, though until now I had not known it. Meanwhile thirst—sharp, burning, and relentless—tormented me, brought on by the sea-water I had swallowed and by the exhaustion of my body. But kind nature had joined these needs to pleasant sensations, so that I—yes, I myself—was refreshed and soothed as I ate this poor fare and drank a little of the sour wine that half-filled a flask left in the deserted place. Then I lay down on the bed, not too proud for the lot of a shipwrecked man. The earthy scent of the dried leaves soothed me after the loathsome smell of seaweed. I let my loneliness slip from my mind. I looked neither back nor ahead; my senses grew quiet for rest, and I fell asleep, dreaming of all the dear scenes inland—of hay-makers, and the shepherd’s whistle to his dog, when he called for help to drive the flock to the fold; of sights and sounds from my boyhood’s mountain life, which I had long since forgotten."}

