{"request_id":"279be710ff78ffd18596a5b5","rewritten_text":"I uncovered it, and a gentle breeze quickly fanned it into a flame. I noticed this as well and made a fan out of branches, which stirred the embers back to life when they were nearly out. When night came again, I was pleased to find that the fire gave light as well as heat, and that this discovery was useful to me in my food. I found that some of the offal the travelers had left behind had been roasted, and it tasted far more savory than the berries I gathered from the trees. So I tried to prepare my food in the same way, placing it on the live embers. I found that the berries spoiled by this method, while the nuts and roots improved.

“Food, however, became scarce, and I often spent the whole day searching in vain for a few acorns to ease the pangs of hunger. When I found this, I decided to leave the place I had been living in up to then and look for somewhere where my few needs would be met more easily. In this move, I was deeply distressed by the loss of the fire I had obtained by accident and knew not how to recreate. I spent several hours seriously thinking through the problem, but I had to give up all attempts to replace it. Then, wrapping myself in my cloak, I headed across the wood toward the setting sun. I wandered for three days, and at last I discovered open country. A heavy fall of snow had happened the night before, and the fields were one unbroken white; the sight was bleak, and I found my feet chilled by the cold, damp substance covering the ground.

“It was about seven in the morning, and I longed for food and shelter. At last I saw a small hut on rising ground, which must have been built for the convenience of some shepherd. This was a new sight to me, and I examined the structure with great curiosity. Finding the door open, I went inside. An old man sat there near a fire, preparing his breakfast. When he heard a noise, he turned, shrieked loudly at seeing me, and then left the hut, running across the fields with a speed his weakened body hardly seemed capable of. His appearance was unlike anything I had ever seen, and his flight surprised me. Still, I was delighted by the hut itself: here snow and rain could not get in; the ground was dry; and it seemed to me then as exquisite and divine a refuge as Pandæmonium appeared to the dæmons of hell after their suffering in the lake of fire.”"}

