          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b19ef2164f8814155722874a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At the stairs where we had first taken him abroad, and ever since, I kept a watchful eye out for any sign that we were suspected. I saw nothing. We certainly were not, and at that moment we were no more attended or followed by any boat than we were suspected. Had a boat been waiting on us, I would have dashed to the shore and made her either go on or show plainly what she meant. As it was, we went on our way without any hint of interference.\\n\\nHe had his boat-cloak on, and, as I\u2019ve said, he looked like a natural part of the scene. It was striking\u2014though perhaps the miserable life he\u2019d been living explained it\u2014that he was the least uneasy of all of us. He wasn\u2019t indifferent; he told me he hoped to live long enough to see his gentleman among the best of gentlemen in a foreign country. Still, he wasn\u2019t the sort to be passive or resigned, as I understood it; he simply had no intention of meeting danger halfway. When danger came, he faced it, but it had to arrive before he would trouble himself.\\n\\n\u201cIf you knew, dear boy,\u201d he said to me, \u201cwhat it\u2019s like to sit here along with my dear boy and have my smoke, after being shut up day by day between four walls, you\u2019d envy me. But you don\u2019t know what it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think I know the pleasures of freedom,\u201d I answered.\\n\\n\u201cAh,\u201d he said, shaking his head gravely. \u201cBut you don\u2019t know it like I do. You must\u2019ve been under lock and key, dear boy, to know it as much as I do,\u2014but I ain\u2019t going to be low.\u201d\\n\\nIt struck me as odd that, for any plan or controlling thought, he should have risked his freedom\u2014and even his life. Yet I considered that perhaps freedom without danger was too far removed from the whole pattern of his existence to mean to him what it would mean to someone else. I was not far off, since after smoking a little he said\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou see, dear boy, when I was over yonder, on the other side of the world, I was always looking back to this side; and it got to feel flat there, even though I was growing rich. Everybody knew Magwitch, and Magwitch could come and Magwitch could go, and no one\u2019s head would be troubled about him. They don\u2019t feel that way about me here, dear boy\u2014wouldn\u2019t, leastwise, if they knew where I was.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf all goes well,\u201d I said, \u201cyou\u2019ll be perfectly free and safe again within a few hours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell,\u201d he replied, drawing a long breath, \u201cI hope so.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you think so?\u201d\"}"
