          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0169748305beddc59163c40e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNo!\u201d the girl answered.\\n\\n\u201cThen tell me why.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFor one reason,\u201d the girl said, steady and resolute, \u201cfor one reason: the lady knows it, and she will stand by me in it\u2014I have her promise. And for another reason besides: for all the bad life he has led, I\u2019ve led a bad life too. There are many of us who have kept the same paths together, and I won\u2019t turn on them\u2014people who might, any of them, have turned on me, but didn\u2019t, though they\u2019re as bad as they are.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen,\u201d said the gentleman at once, as though that were the very point he\u2019d been trying to reach, \u201cput Monks into my hands, and leave him to me to manage.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat if he turns against the others?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI promise you,\u201d he replied, \u201cthat if it comes to that\u2014if the truth is forced out of him\u2014then the matter will end there. There must be circumstances in Oliver\u2019s little history that would be painful to drag before the public, and once the truth is drawn out, they shall go scot free.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd if it isn\u2019t?\u201d the girl suggested.\\n\\n\u201cThen,\u201d the gentleman went on, \u201cthis Fagin shall not be brought to justice without your consent. In that event, I could lay out reasons, I think, that would lead you to agree.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo I have the lady\u2019s promise for that?\u201d asked the girl.\\n\\n\u201cYou do,\u201d said Rose. \u201cMy true and faithful pledge.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMonks would never learn how you came to know what you know?\u201d the girl asked after a short pause.\\n\\n\u201cNever,\u201d answered the gentleman. \u201cThe right sort of intelligence must be brought to bear upon him, so that he could never even guess.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve been a liar, and among liars from a little child,\u201d said the girl after another interval of silence, \u201cbut I\u2019ll take your word.\u201d\\n\\nOnce she had received assurance from both that she might safely do so, she went on in a voice so low that it was often hard for the listener to make out even the meaning of what she said. She described, by name and situation, the public-house where she had been followed that night. From the way she sometimes paused, it seemed as if the gentleman were making quick notes of what she told him. After she had explained the place\u2019s localities, the best spot from which to watch it without drawing notice, and the night and hour when Monks was most accustomed to go there, she stopped for a few moments, as if to fix his face and appearance more firmly in her mind.\\n\\n\u201cHe is tall,\u201d she said, \u201cand strongly built, though not stout. He has a kind of furtive walk; and when he walks he keeps looking over his shoulder\u2014first to one side, then to the other.\u201d\"}"
