          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cfd08e1fc88ae06be21168a2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI have to go, uncertain of what will happen to me; but I will return here, or follow your party, as soon as possible. One word, one look, will be enough to decide whether I enter your father\u2019s house this evening\u2014or never.\u201d\\n\\nSuch a letter was not likely to be recovered from soon. Half an hour of solitude and reflection might have calmed her; but the ten minutes that had passed before she was interrupted\u2014despite all the limits of her situation\u2014could do nothing to bring her peace. Every moment only brought fresh agitation. It was overwhelming happiness. And before she had even moved beyond the first stage of full feeling, Charles, Mary, and Henrietta all came in.\\n\\nThe sheer necessity of seeming like herself caused an immediate struggle; but after a while she could do no more. She stopped understanding a word they said, and had to plead indisposition and excuse herself. Then they could see she looked very ill, and were shocked and concerned, and would not move\u2014would not stir\u2014for anything in the world. This was dreadful. If only they had gone away and left her in quiet possession of that room, it might have cured her; but having them all standing and waiting around her was distracting, and in desperation she said she would go home.\\n\\n\u201cBy all means, my dear,\u201d cried Mrs Musgrove, \u201cgo home directly, and take care of yourself, so that you\u2019ll be fit for the evening. I wish Sarah were here to doctor you, but I\u2019m no doctor myself. Charles, ring and order a chair. She must not walk.\u201d\\n\\nBut a chair would never do. Worse than everything! To lose the chance of saying two words to Captain Wentworth during her quiet, solitary walk up the town (and she felt almost certain she would meet him) was more than she could bear. The chair was earnestly protested against, and Mrs Musgrove\u2014who was thinking only of one kind of illness\u2014after assuring herself with some anxiety that there had been no fall in the matter; that Anne had not, at any time lately, slipped down and struck her head; that she was perfectly convinced there had been no fall\u2014could part with her cheerfully and depend on finding her better at night.\\n\\nAnxious not to miss any possible precaution, Anne struggled and said\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid, ma\u2019am, that it isn\u2019t quite understood. Please be so good as to mention to the other gentlemen that we hope to see your whole party this evening. I\u2019m afraid there has been some mistake; and I want you particularly to assure Captain Harville and Captain Wentworth that we hope to see them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! my dear, it is quite understood. I give you my word.\u201d\"}"
