          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8bcbf4ca8247ed5be95d8a7d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 7th. I got up early, and, to my great joy, I finally saw what there was no reason to doubt was the North Pole itself. It was there, beyond question, right under my feet. But I had already risen so far that I could not make out anything clearly. Still, if I judge by the numbers that show my different heights at different times\u2014between 6 A.M. on April 2 and twenty minutes before 9 A.M. that same day, when the barometer began to fall\u2014it seems reasonable to think that by 4 A.M. on April 7 the balloon had reached a height of at least 7,254 miles above the sea. That height sounds enormous, but the estimate I used was probably lower than the truth. In any case, I clearly saw the whole major diameter of the earth. The entire northern hemisphere lay beneath me like a map laid flat. Even the great circle of the equator formed the edge of my horizon. Your Excellencies can easily imagine that the parts within the Arctic Circle that had not been explored so far, though they were directly under me and therefore did not look stretched out, were still too small and too far away from where I was looking to allow any very accurate study. Even so, what I could see was strange and thrilling. Northward from that huge rim I mentioned\u2014more or less the limit of human discovery in these regions\u2014an unbroken, or almost unbroken, sheet of ice continues. At first, its surface is flattened. Farther on, it sinks into a flatter plane, and then, becoming clearly concave, it ends at the Pole in a circular center that is sharply defined. From the balloon, its apparent diameter made an angle of about sixty-five seconds. Its color was a dark gray that varied in intensity, and it was always darker than any other spot on the visible hemisphere. At times it deepened into the most complete and impenetrable blackness. Beyond that, there was little I could determine. By twelve o\u2019clock the circular center had shrunk noticeably, and by seven P.M. I could no longer see it at all. The balloon passed over the western edge of the ice and then floated away quickly toward the equator.\\n\\n\u201cApril 8th. I noticed the earth\u2019s apparent size had decreased, and its overall color and look had changed as well.\u201d\"}"
