          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1dbfdd82419c45bf2d8bfc46\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma did not regret being so condescending when she went to the Coles. The visit gave her many pleasant memories the next day. And anything she might have seemed to lose by staying quietly and with dignity was more than made up for by the excitement and popularity she received. She must have made the Coles very happy\u2014good, deserving people who deserved to be pleased. And she left behind a name that would not be forgotten soon.\\n\\nPerfect happiness, even when remembered, is rare. Still, there were two things that bothered her. First, she wondered if she had not gone against a woman\u2019s duty by telling Frank Churchill her suspicions about Jane Fairfax\u2019s feelings. It did not seem right, but the idea had been so strong that she could not stop herself. And the fact that he listened to everything she said made it feel like a compliment to her judgment. That, in turn, made it harder for her to be sure she should have kept quiet.\\n\\nThe second thing she regretted also involved Jane Fairfax. Emma had no doubt about this one. Jane truly and clearly regretted how much worse her own playing and singing were. She also sincerely regretted how idle she had been as a child, and then she sat down and practiced hard for an hour and a half.\\n\\nAfter that, Harriet came in. If Harriet\u2019s praise could have satisfied her, Emma would have been comforted quickly.\\n\\n\u201cOh! If I could only play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t put us in the same group, Harriet. My playing isn\u2019t more like hers than a lamp is like sunshine.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! dear\u2014I think you play the best of the two. I think you play just as well as she does. I\u2019m sure I\u2019d rather hear you. Everyone said how well you played last night.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPeople who really knew anything about it would notice the difference. The truth is, Harriet, my playing is only good enough to be praised. Jane Fairfax\u2019s is much better.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I\u2019ll always think you play just as well as she does, or that if there is any difference, nobody would ever notice it. Mr. Cole said how much taste you have. And Mr. Frank Churchill talked a lot about your taste, and he said he values taste more than skill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, but Jane Fairfax has both, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you sure? I saw that she had skill, but I didn\u2019t know she had taste. Nobody ever talked about it. And I hate Italian singing. You can\u2019t understand a word of it. Besides, if she plays so well, you know she has to, because she\u2019ll have to teach.\u201d}\"}"
