{"request_id":"11a47ee1441584753cf45543","rewritten_text":"The little man’s body was broader than it ought to have been, so that his whole appearance came off as comically rotund. His feet, naturally, were entirely out of sight; yet now and then some horny-looking bit of him would push through a tear in the bottom of the car—or, to put it more accurately, through the top of the hat. His hands were of an enormous size. His hair was of an extremely gray hue, gathered into a queue at the back. His nose was prodigiously long, twisted, and inflamed-looking; his eyes were full, bright, and keen; and though his chin and cheeks were wrinkled with age, they were still broad, swollen, and double. As for any ears at all—of whatever sort—there was not the faintest trace to be found anywhere on his head.

This odd little gentleman wore a loose surtout of sky-blue satin, paired with tight breeches of the same, secured at the knees by silver buckles. His vest was made of some vivid yellow stuff; a white taffety cap sat jauntily to one side of his head; and, to finish his outfit, a blood-red silk handkerchief wrapped his throat and dropped down in a neat fashion upon his bosom, tied into a fanciful bow of extraordinary size.

Having descended, as I said before, to roughly one hundred feet above the earth, the little old man was suddenly seized by a fit of trepidation and seemed unwilling to come any nearer to terra firma. So, throwing out a quantity of sand from a canvas bag—one he hauled up with great difficulty—he came to a stop at once. Then, in a hurried, agitated way, he reached into a side-pocket of his surtout and drew out a large morocco pocket-book. He held it up suspiciously, glanced at it with an air of the utmost astonishment, and was plainly struck by how heavy it seemed. At last he opened it and let fall, exactly at the burgomaster’s feet, a huge letter sealed with red sealing-wax and bound carefully with red tape. His Excellency stooped to pick it up. But the aeronaut, still very much rattled, and apparently having no further reason to keep him in Rotterdam, began at that moment to busy himself with preparations to depart; and since he needed to throw off some ballast in order to rise again, the half-dozen bags he tossed out one after another—without the least trouble to empty them—unluckily tumbled, each and every one, onto the burgomaster’s back, rolling him over and over no fewer than twenty-one times, in full view of every man in Rotterdam. Still, it would be wrong to think that great Underduk allowed this sort of impertinence from the little old man to go unanswered."}

