          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2dd6a3e1891f5c0a4fa334ef\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet even now I had not finished drinking the bitter potion. I still did not believe I had truly lost everything. I had not yet felt, in every heartbeat, in every nerve, in every thought, that I was alone\u2014alone of my kind. I was not yet sure, in my bones, that I was the LAST MAN.\\n\\nThe day had turned cloudy, and at sunset a light rain began to fall. Even the sky seems to cry, I thought. If that is true, is it shameful for a human being to spend himself in tears? I remembered old stories, where people are said to dissolve away through crying, turning into fountains that pour and pour. I wished it were like that. Then my fate might be like the watery deaths of Adrian and Clara. But grief is strange. It makes a net you can trace, showing its pain in every change and every form. It clings to everything living. It feeds on every object. Like light, it fills the world, and like light, it gives everything its own colors.\\n\\nI had wandered far from the place where I had been thrown, searching, when I came to one of those watch-towers that stand along the Italian shore at regular intervals. I was glad to find shelter. After staring so long at nature\u2019s empty, bleak land, it felt good to see something made by human hands. So I went in and climbed the rough, winding stairs up to the guard-room. Fate had been kind to me, because there was little left to show what had once lived there. Two iron supports held up a few boards, and dried Indian corn leaves covered the bed. An open chest sat nearby, with some half-rotten biscuits inside, and it stirred up an appetite I did not even know I still had. \\n\\nThirst also tormented me\u2014sharp and burning\u2014because of the sea-water I had drunk and the exhaustion of my body. Still, nature had given these needs pleasant feelings, so that I\u2014yes, I\u2014was refreshed and calmed as I ate this poor food and drank a little of the sour wine from a flask that was left behind in the abandoned place. Then I lay down on the bed, not too proud to rest there, like any shipwrecked man. The earthy smell of the dried leaves soothed me after the awful smell of seaweed. For a while, I forgot I was alone. I did not look back or forward. My senses went quiet, and I fell asleep. In my dreams I saw dear inland scenes again: hay-makers, and the shepherd\u2019s whistle to his dog when he needed help driving the flock into the fold. I heard and saw the things from my boyhood mountain life that I had long forgotten.\"}"
