he soul toward grand, religious thoughts. Often all sound would fall away, so we could listen for the nightingale; while fireflies danced in bright, measured rhythm, and the soft cooing of the aziolo promised fair weather to the travelers. Did we pass through a valley? Gentle shades surrounded us, and the rocks wore beautiful hues. If we crossed a mountain, Greece\u2014a living map\u2014lay spread beneath us, her famed peaks cleaving the sky; her rivers ran through the fertile land in silver lines. Almost afraid to breathe, we English travelers surveyed with ecstasy this splendid landscape, so different from the sober colors and melancholy grace of our own native scenery. When we left Macedonia, the fertile but low plains of Thrace offered fewer beauties, yet our journey remained interesting. An advanced guard gave word of our approach, and the local people quickly moved to honor Lord Raymond.\"}"
