{"request_id":"a9a11c81d605108551031532","rewritten_text":"“I proceeded to think this way—_a posteriori_. The murderers had escaped through one of these windows. If that was true, then they couldn’t have re-fastened the sashes from the inside, since they were found secured; that was the consideration that, through its obviousness, stopped the police from looking any further in this direction. And yet the sashes _were_ fastened. So they _must_ have had the ability to fasten themselves. There was no way around that conclusion. I stepped to the unobstructed casement, withdrew the nail with some difficulty, and tried to raise the sash. It resisted every effort, just as I had expected. A concealed spring must, I now know, exist; and this confirmation of my idea convinced me that at least my premises were correct, even if the circumstances surrounding the nails still seemed mysterious. A careful search soon revealed the hidden spring. I pressed it, and, satisfied with what I’d found, refrained from lifting the sash.

“I now put the nail back and studied it closely. Someone who passed out through this window might have closed it again, and the spring would have caught—but the nail couldn’t have been put back. The conclusion was clear, and it narrowed the scope of my investigations again. The assassins _must_ have escaped through the other window. If, then, the springs on each sash were the same—as was likely—there _must_ be a difference between the nails, or at least between the way they were fastened. Climbing onto the bedstead’s sacking, I examined the second casement minutely over the head-board. Running my hand down behind the board, I quickly found and pressed the spring, which, as I’d supposed, was identical in character to the one beside it. I then looked at the nail. It was as stout as the other, and seemed to fit in the same way—driven in nearly up to the head.

“You’ll say I was puzzled; but if you think that, you must have misunderstood the nature of my inferences. To use a sporting phrase, I wasn’t once ‘at fault.’ The trail had never, for an instant, been lost. There was no break in any link of the chain. I had traced the secret to its final result—and that result was _the nail_. I say it had, in every respect, the same appearance as its counterpart in the other window; but that fact was an absolute nullity (however conclusive it might seem) when compared with the consideration that, at this point, the trail ended. ‘There _must_ be something wrong,’ I said, ‘with the nail.’ I touched it, and the head—along with about a quarter of an inch of the shank—came off in my fingers.”"}

