          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2c3f36e2141c6612bf3c4bdb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, bother my hat! I want Virginia!\u201d cried the little Duke, laughing, and they galloped on to the railway station. There, Mr. Otis asked the station-master whether anyone matching Virginia\u2019s description had been seen on the platform, but he couldn\u2019t get any news of her. The station-master, however, wired up and down the line and assured him that a strict watch would be kept, and after having bought a hat for the little Duke from a linen-draper who was just putting up his shutters, Mr. Otis rode off to Bexley, a village about four miles away. He\u2019d been told it was a well-known haunt of the gypsies, since there was a large common next to it. \\n\\nThey woke up the rural policeman there, but got no information from him. After riding all over the common, they turned their horses\u2019 heads homeward and reached the Chase about eleven o\u2019clock, dead-tired and almost heart-broken. At the gate-house they found Washington and the twins waiting for them with lanterns, because the avenue was so dark. Not the slightest trace of Virginia had been discovered. The gypsies had been caught on Brockley meadows, but she wasn\u2019t with them. They explained their sudden departure by saying they had mistaken the date of Chorton Fair and had left in a hurry for fear they\u2019d be late. Indeed, they\u2019d been quite distressed to hear about Virginia\u2019s disappearance, since they were very grateful to Mr. Otis for letting them camp in his park, and four of their number had stayed behind to help with the search. The carp-pond had been dragged, and the whole Chase had been thoroughly searched, but with no result. \\n\\nIt was clear that, at least for that night, Virginia was lost to them. In the deepest depression, Mr. Otis and the boys walked up to the house, with the groom following behind, leading the two horses and the pony. In the hall they found a group of frightened servants. In the library, lying on a sofa, was poor Mrs. Otis, almost beside herself with terror and anxiety, while the old housekeeper bathed her forehead with eau-de-cologne. Mr. Otis immediately insisted she should have something to eat and ordered supper for the whole party. It was a melancholy meal\u2014hardly anyone spoke\u2014and even the twins were awestruck and subdued, because they were very fond of their sister. \\n\\nWhen they\u2019d finished, Mr. Otis, despite the little Duke\u2019s pleading, ordered them all to bed, saying nothing more could be done that night and that he would telegraph in the morning to Scotland Yard to have some detectives sent down immediately.\"}"
