{"request_id":"185ab6ae664e11b2b59d7f5e","rewritten_text":"It is only four o’clock, but it is winter, and the sun has already set. There are no clouds in the clear, frosty sky to catch and reflect its slanting beams; yet the air itself carries a faint rose-colored tint, which is reflected again on the snow that covers the ground. I live in a lone cottage on a wide, empty heath—no voice of life reaches me. I look out on the desolate plain, all white, except for a few black patches that the midday sun has left at the tops of those sharp, pointed hillocks, where the snow, sliding as it fell, lies thinner than it does across the open ground. A few birds are pecking at the hard ice that covers the pools, for the frost has held on for a long time.

I am in a strange state of mind. I am alone—completely alone—in the world. The curse of misfortune has passed over me and withered me. I know I am about to die, and I feel happy—joyous. I feel my pulse; it races. I press my thin hand to my cheek; it burns. There is a slight, quick spirit within me, and it is now letting out its last sparks. I will never see the snows of another winter. I truly believe I shall never again feel the life-giving warmth of another summer sun. And it is in this belief that I begin to write my tragic history. Perhaps a history such as mine would be better off dying with me, but a feeling I cannot name keeps pulling me forward, and I am too weak—both in body and mind—to resist even the smallest impulse. While life was still strong in me, I thought there was something sacred and terrible in my story, something that made it unfit to speak aloud; now, as I am about to die, I contaminate its mystic terrors. It is like the wood of the Eumenides—only the dying may enter. And Oedipus is about to die.

What am I writing?—I must gather my thoughts. I don’t know that anyone will read these pages except you, my friend, who will receive them at my death. I don’t write to you alone, because it would please me to dwell on our friendship in a way that would be unnecessary if only you were to read what I write. So I will tell my tale as if I were writing for strangers. You have often asked what caused my solitary life—my tears—and above all my impenetrable, unkind silence. In life I dared not; in death I reveal the mystery. Others will toss these pages aside lightly. To you, Woodville—kind, affectionate friend—they will be dear: precious memorials of a heart-broken girl who, dying, is still warmed by gratitude toward you. Your tears will fall on the words that record my misfortunes; I know they will. And while I still have life, I thank you for your sympathy."}

