s enough by itself to create a suspicion. That suspicion should guide everything we do next in investigating this mystery. I said \u201clegitimate deductions,\u201d but I do not mean it in the simple way you might think. I mean that the deductions are the only proper ones, and that the suspicion arises inevitably from them, as the single result. What the suspicion is, however, I will not say yet. For now, just remember that, for me, it was strong enough to give my inquiries in the chamber a clear direction\u2014a certain pull.\\n\\n\u201cNow let us move, in our minds, to this chamber. What should we look for first? The way the murderers got out. It is not too much to say that neither of us believes in supernatural events.\u201d\"}"
