          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cb5556df5e74eec1242b1ed2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Nearly a year had passed since my father\u2019s return, and the seasons had almost completed their cycle. It was now the end of May; the woods were clothed in their freshest green, and the sweet smell of newly mown grass filled the fields. I thought that the warm air and the lovely face of Nature might help me find the right feelings to inspire him\u2014to bring him gentle sensations, and prepare him for the calm peace and love I meant to win from him.\\n\\nSo I chose the evening of one of these days for my attempt. I invited him to walk with me, and led him to a nearby beech wood where the light shade sheltered us from the slanting, dazzling beams of the setting sun. After walking for some time in silence, I sat down with him on a mossy mound\u2014It\u2019s strange, but even now I can still see the place. The slender, smooth trunks were many of them wrapped with ivy, whose shining leaves of the darkest green stood out against the white bark and the light leaves of the young beech shoots growing from their parent trunks. The short grass was mixed with moss and partly covered by the dead leaves of last autumn, which the winds had driven here and there into little heaps. There were a few moss-covered stumps. The leaves were gently stirred by the breeze, and through their green canopy you could see the bright blue sky. As evening came on, the distant trunks were reddened by the sun, the wind died away entirely, and a few birds flew past us to their evening rest.\\n\\nSo it was here that we sat together, and when you hear everything that had happened\u2014everything that tore our souls with such terrible force even in this peaceful spot, which, but for strange passions, might have been a paradise for us\u2014you won\u2019t be surprised that I remember it as I looked at it, hoping its calm might give me calm, and inspire me not only with courage but with persuasive words. I saw all these things and, in a kind of vacant way, noted them in my mind as I tried to arrange my thoughts in the right order for my attempt. My heart beat fast as I worked myself up to speak to him; I was determined not to be turned away, but I trembled to imagine what effect my words might have on him. At last, with great hesitation, I began:\\n\\n\u201cYour kindness to me, my dearest father, and the affection\u2014the excessive affection\u2014that you had for me when you first returned\u2014I hope will excuse me in your eyes for daring to speak to you, even though, with the tender affection of a daughter, I also speak with the freedom of a friend and an equal.\u201d\"}"
