          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"91816e029532d461999da7e4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was early February; and Anne, after a month in Bath, found herself growing impatient for tidings from Uppercross and Lyme. She longed to know far more than Mary had managed to tell her. It had been three weeks since she had heard anything at all. She only knew that Henrietta was back at home again, and that Louisa, though thought to be mending quickly, was still in Lyme; and one evening, as she sat thinking of them all with great intensity, a letter\u2014thicker than usual\u2014came from Mary. To heighten her pleasure and astonishment, it came with the compliments of Admiral and Mrs Croft.\\n\\nThe Crofts must be in Bath! A detail that could not fail to interest her. They were the sort of people whose presence her heart turned toward without effort.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is this?\u201d Sir Walter exclaimed. \u201cThe Crofts have come to Bath? The Crofts who take Kellynch? What have they brought you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA letter from Uppercross Cottage, Sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! those letters are handy enough. They act as passports; they secure an introduction. I should have paid Admiral Croft a visit, though, in any case. I know what is owed to my tenant.\u201d\\n\\nAnne could not listen any longer; she could not even say how the Admiral\u2019s complexion escaped notice\u2014her own letter claimed her attention. It had been written several days earlier.\\n\\n\u201cFebruary 1st.\\n\\n\u201cMY DEAR ANNE,\\n\\n\u201cI make no apology for my silence, because I know how little letters are valued in a place like Bath. You must be far too happy to think much of Uppercross, which, as you know, offers so little to write about. We have had a very dull Christmas; Mr and Mrs Musgrove have not given a single dinner party during the holidays. I do not count the Hayters as anyone. The holidays, however, are at last over: I believe no children ever had such long ones. I am sure I had not. The house was cleared yesterday, except for the little Harvilles; but you will be surprised to learn they have never gone home. Mrs Harville must be an odd mother to keep them so long. I cannot make sense of it. In my opinion, they are not at all nice children; yet Mrs Musgrove seems to like them as much as, if not more than, her own grandchildren.\\n\\n\u201cWhat dreadful weather we have had! It may not be felt in Bath, with your pleasant pavements; but in the country it matters. I have not had a soul call on me since the second week in January, except Charles Hayter, who had been coming much more often than was agreeable. Between ourselves, I think it a pity Henrietta had not stayed at Lyme as long as Louisa; it would have kept her a little out of his way.\u201d\"}"
