{"request_id":"e9a2b494c008d2972e880964","rewritten_text":"How long I stayed in that state, I cannot say. It must have been quite a while, because when I started to come back to awareness, the day was breaking. I saw the balloon floating at an enormous height above a stretch of ocean, and there was no land anywhere in sight, as far as the horizon could reach. Still, when I regained my senses, I did not feel as much pain as I might have expected. In fact, there was a kind of early madness in how calmly I looked over my situation. I pulled my hands up to my eyes, one after the other, and wondered what could have caused my veins to swell and why my fingernails had turned so dark. After that, I examined my head very carefully. I shook it again and again and felt it closely, until I was satisfied it was not, as I had half feared, bigger than my balloon.

Then I reached into both pockets of my breeches. I found that a set of tablets and a toothpick case were missing. I tried to figure out what could have happened, but I could not, and I felt a sharp, wordless annoyance. At that point, I began to notice how much my left ankle hurt, and a faint sense of what was happening to me slowly returned. Strangely enough, I was not shocked or terrified. If I felt anything, it was a quiet, satisfied chuckle at how clever I was going to be in getting myself out of this mess. I never, even for a moment, treated my eventual safety as something that might not happen.

For a few minutes I sat in deep thought. I clearly remember pressing my lips together, putting my forefinger to the side of my nose, and making other gestures and grimaces that men often make when they sit comfortably in their armchairs and think hard about complicated problems. Once I felt I had gathered my thoughts, I did the next step with great care. I put my hands behind my back and unfastened the large iron buckle that was attached to the waistband of my inexpressibles. The buckle had three teeth, and because they were a little rusty, they turned with real difficulty. I managed, after some trouble, to rotate them so they were at right angles to the buckle’s body, and I was relieved to see they stayed there. Holding the tool in my teeth, I then worked to untie the knot of my cravat. I had to pause and try again several times, but in the end I succeeded."}

