          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7687ac1bf3be0faa0dd3070f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After a while, twelve o\u2019clock boomed from the tall tower at Westminster, and with every heavy ring of the bell the night seemed to shake. Then the railway lights went out. Only one lamp stayed on, shining alone like a big ruby on a giant mast, and the city\u2019s noise faded.\\n\\nAt two o\u2019clock he got up and walked toward Blackfriars. Everything looked unreal. It felt like a strange dream. The buildings across the river seemed to be made of darkness. It was as if silver and shadow had reshaped the whole world. St. Paul\u2019s great dome loomed in the dim air like a bubble.\\n\\nWhen he came near Cleopatra\u2019s Needle, he saw a man leaning over the parapet. As he got closer, the man looked up, and the gaslight lit his face.\\n\\nIt was Mr. Podgers, the cheiromantist. No one could mistake him: his fat, soft face, his gold-rimmed spectacles, his weak, sickly smile, and his sensual mouth.\\n\\nLord Arthur stopped. A bright idea flashed through him. He moved quietly up behind the man. In an instant he grabbed Mr. Podgers by the legs and threw him into the Thames. There was a rough curse, a heavy splash, and then everything went still. Lord Arthur looked anxiously over the water, but he could see nothing of the cheiromantist except a tall hat spinning in the moonlit current. After a time even that sank, and there was no sign of Mr. Podgers. Once, he thought he saw the thick, misshapen figure swimming toward the steps by the bridge, and a terrible feeling of failure hit him. But it was only a reflection, and when the moon came out from behind a cloud, it disappeared. At last he seemed to accept what destiny had decided. He let out a deep sigh of relief, and Sybil\u2019s name came to his lips.\\n\\n\u201cHave you dropped anything, sir?\u201d a voice asked behind him.\\n\\nHe turned around and saw a policeman holding a bull\u2019s-eye lantern.\\n\\n\u201cNothing important, sergeant,\u201d he said, smiling. Then he hailed a passing hansom, jumped in, and told the driver to take him to Belgrave Square.\\n\\nFor the next few days he swung between hope and fear. Sometimes he almost expected Mr. Podgers to walk into the room. At other times he felt that Fate could not be so cruel to him. Twice he went to the cheiromantist\u2019s address in West Moon Street, but he could not make himself ring the bell. He wanted certainty, and he was afraid of it.\\n\\nIn the end it arrived. He was sitting in the club\u2019s smoking-room, drinking tea, and listening without much energy as Surbiton talked about the last comic song at the Gaiety. Then the waiter came in with the evening papers.\"}"
