          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3097ce767b29109e93b9381c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Jane admitted she was a little taken aback by the match; yet she spoke less of her surprise than of her sincere wish for their happiness, and Elizabeth could not get her to treat it as unlikely. Kitty and Lydia had no particular envy for Miss Lucas, since Mr. Collins was only a clergyman; and the whole affair touched them in no other way than as fresh news to be passed around at Meryton.\\n\\nLady Lucas, however, could not help feeling pleased at being able to answer Mrs. Bennet with the consolation of a daughter settled well in marriage, and she paid a visit to Longbourn more often than usual to declare how delighted she was, though Mrs. Bennet\u2019s sour looks and ill-natured remarks might have been enough to spoil any happiness.\\n\\nBetween Elizabeth and Charlotte there was a kind of restraint that kept them both silent on the subject; and Elizabeth felt convinced that no real confidence could ever exist between them again. Her disappointment in Charlotte only turned her thoughts with greater warmth toward her sister, whose integrity and delicacy she was sure could never be shaken; and for whose happiness she grew daily more uneasy, as Bingley had now been gone a week, and nothing was heard of his return.\\n\\nJane had written Caroline an early reply, and she was counting the days until she could reasonably expect to hear again. The promised letter of thanks from Mr. Collins reached them on Tuesday, addressed to their father, and composed with all the solemn gratitude that a twelve-month\u2019s residence in the family might have inspired. Having discharged his conscience on that point, he went on, with many rapturous expressions, to tell them how happy he was to have won the affection of their amiable neighbour, Miss Lucas; and then he explained that it was only in order to enjoy her company that he had been so ready to agree to their kind wish of seeing him again at Longbourn, to which he hoped to be able to return on Monday fortnight. Lady Catherine, he added, so warmly approved of his marriage that she wished it to be carried out as soon as possible, which he trusted would be an argument not to be answered with his amiable Charlotte, to name an early day for making him the happiest of men.\\n\\nMr. Collins\u2019s coming back into Hertfordshire was no longer a matter of satisfaction to Mrs. Bennet. On the contrary, she was inclined to complain of it as much as her husband. It was very odd, she thought, that he should come to Longbourn rather than to Lucas Lodge; it was also extremely inconvenient and a great deal of trouble. She disliked having visitors in the house while her health was so poor, and lovers, above all others, were the most disagreeable.\"}"
