          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"57835a7f482834f194808f92\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In all of this there was something strange that drew me in and filled me with wonder. It was as if he had just woken from a long, dream-filled sleep. He felt, in a way, like one of the seven sleepers, or like Nourjahad, in that sweet imitation of an eastern tale. Diana was gone. His friends were changed or dead. And now, with his awakening, I was the only person he had left to love on earth.\\n\\nHow dear the waters, mountains, and woods of Loch Lomond felt to me now that I had such a beloved companion for my walks. I went with my father to every beautiful place we could find, whether on the islands or beside the waterfalls that hid behind trees. I explored every shaded path and every little valley tangled with thick undergrowth and fern. His conversation made my thoughts grow broader. I felt as if I had been made new, surrounded by the fresh energy of a new life. It seemed that, since his arrival, I had been carried from a small patch of earth into a world so wide it could stretch the imagination and the mind. Before, my life had been like a pleasant stream, quietly staying in its own fields, never meant to go far, and then disappearing without leaving a trace. Now it felt to me like a many-sided river flowing through a fertile, beautiful landscape\u2014always changing, always lovely. But I did not know what desert it was heading toward, or the rocks that would tear its waters, or the ugly scene that would show up in its waves in a twisted way. Life was bright then. I began to learn to hope\u2014and I also learned what brings a more bitter despair to the heart than hope destroyed.\\n\\nIs it not strange that grief should come so quickly after such happiness? I drank from an enchanted cup, but there was gall at the bottom of its long, sweet taste. My heart was full of deep affection, yet it was calm, even because of how full it was. I had no idea that misery could come from love. And this lesson\u2014one that everyone must learn in the end\u2014was taught to me in a way that few people are forced to experience. I still mourn those few short months of paradise. I disobeyed no command. I ate no apple. And yet I was driven out without mercy. My companion did it, and I was thrown down with him. But I am getting off track. Let sorrow arrive when it is meant to. At this point in my story, I can still speak of happiness.\\n\\nThree months passed in this delightful closeness, and then my aunt fell ill. I spent a whole month in her room, nursing her, but her illness was deadly. She died, and for some time I was inconsolable. Death is so terrible for those who are still living. Even when affection does not hold you together, the chains of habit are so strong that the heart must suffer when they break.\"}"
