          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"abf02c7e421a32e2596311bf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Jane admitted that she was a little surprised by the match, but she talked less about her shock than about how strongly she wanted everyone to be happy. Elizabeth also couldn\u2019t get her to treat it as unlikely. Kitty and Lydia had no reason to envy Miss Lucas, since Mr. Collins was only a clergyman. For them, the whole thing changed nothing except that it became news to spread around Meryton.\\n\\nLady Lucas couldn\u2019t hide her satisfaction at being able to tell Mrs. Bennet that she had a daughter who was well married. She visited Longbourn more often than usual to say how happy she was, even though Mrs. Bennet\u2019s sour looks and mean remarks might have been enough to spoil anyone\u2019s happiness.\\n\\nBetween Elizabeth and Charlotte there was a kind of distance that kept them from speaking openly about the matter. Elizabeth felt sure that real trust could never exist between them again. Her disappointment in Charlotte made her look at her sister with even more affection. She was certain of Charlotte\u2019s honesty and good manners, and she grew more and more worried about Charlotte\u2019s happiness, especially now that Bingley had been gone for a week and no one had heard anything about his return.\\n\\nJane had already written Caroline back early, and she was counting the days until she could reasonably expect to hear again. On Tuesday, the promised letter of thanks from Mr. Collins arrived. It was addressed to their father and written with all the serious gratitude that a year spent living with the family might have produced. After he had said what he needed to say to satisfy his conscience, he went on, with many excited expressions, to tell them how happy he was to have won the affection of their kind neighbor, Miss Lucas. Then he explained that he had been so quick to agree to their wish that he come back to Longbourn, simply because he wanted to enjoy her company. He hoped to be able to return on Monday fortnight. He added that Lady Catherine approved of his marriage so strongly that she wanted it to happen as soon as possible, and he trusted that this would be an unanswerable reason for his kind Charlotte to choose an early date and make him the happiest man.\\n\\nMr. Collins\u2019s return to Hertfordshire was no longer something that pleased Mrs. Bennet. Instead, she was just as ready to complain about it as her husband. She found it strange that he should come to Longbourn instead of to Lucas Lodge. She also thought it would be inconvenient and a great deal of trouble. She hated having visitors in the house when her health was so poor, and she considered lovers, of all people, the most unpleasant.\"}"
