          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"262f7c0f0c0412d27419b0d2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We dragged through a few miserable hours until eleven o\u2019clock, when the trial was to begin. My father and the rest of the family, being required to attend as witnesses, I went with them to the court. All through that wretched imitation of justice I endured something like living torment. The question was whether the outcome of my idle curiosity and lawless contrivances would bring about the death of two of my fellow creatures: one a smiling infant, innocent and full of gladness; the other murdered in a far more dreadful manner, with every added circumstance of infamy that could make the act stand out in horror. Justine was also a girl of worth, with qualities that promised to make her life a happy one; and now everything was to be wiped out in a shameful grave, and I was the cause. A thousand times over I would have confessed myself guilty of the crime laid to Justine, but I was not there when it was done, and any such admission would have been treated as the ravings of a madman, and would not have freed her, who was suffering because of me.\\n\\nJustine\u2019s appearance was composed. She wore mourning, and her face, which was always attractive, was made even more exquisitely beautiful by the solemnity of her feelings. Still, she seemed certain of her innocence and did not shake, even though she was stared at and cursed by thousands; for whatever kindness her beauty might otherwise have drawn was drowned out in the spectators\u2019 minds by the thought of the enormity she was said to have committed. She remained steady, though her steadiness was clearly forced; and since her earlier confusion had been offered as evidence of guilt, she gathered herself to present an air of courage. When she entered the court, she swept her eyes about and quickly found where we were sitting. A tear appeared to blur her gaze when she saw us, but she soon regained herself, and a look of sorrowful tenderness seemed to declare her complete innocence.\\n\\nThe trial started, and after the advocate against her had set out the charge, several witnesses were called. A number of odd circumstances combined against her\u2014things that might have shaken anyone who did not have proof of her innocence such as I possessed. She had been out all night during the time when the murder was committed, and toward morning she was noticed by a market-woman not far from where the murdered child\u2019s body was later found. The woman asked her what she was doing there, but Justine answered in a very strange way, and only gave back a confused, unintelligible reply. She came back to the house about eight o\u2019clock, and when someone asked where she had spent the night, she said she had been searching for the child and pressed earnestly whether anything had been heard about him.\"}"
