          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b6ac1e8ef84f026c8cfd5283\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 12th. Something unusual happened to the balloon\u2019s direction. Even though I expected it, the change still filled me with clear, unmistakable joy. After we had been traveling on the old route for about the twentieth parallel of southern latitude, the balloon suddenly turned east at a sharp angle. From then on, it kept going through the day, staying almost exactly in the same plane as the lunar ellipse. The most noticeable effect was that the car shook a lot. This shaking came from the change in route and lasted, to a greater or lesser degree, for many hours.\\n\\n\u201cApril 13th. I was again very frightened by the loud, crackling sound that had terrified me on the tenth. I thought about it for a long time, but I couldn\u2019t reach any clear conclusion. The Earth looked much smaller now, and from the balloon it subtended an angle of a little more than twenty-five degrees. I couldn\u2019t see the moon at all, because it was almost straight overhead. I kept traveling in the same plane of the ellipse, but I made little progress to the east.\\n\\n\u201cApril 14th. The Earth\u2019s apparent size dropped very fast. Today I became strongly convinced that the balloon was now moving along the line of apsides toward the point of perigee. In other words, it was following the direct path that would take it immediately to the moon at the part of its orbit closest to Earth. The moon was directly overhead, so I couldn\u2019t see it. I had to work hard for a long time to condense the air.\\n\\n\u201cApril 15th. I could no longer make out the outlines of continents and seas on the Earth in any clear way. Around twelve o\u2019clock, I noticed that terrible sound again for the third time. This time it lasted for several moments, and it grew louder as it continued. At last, while I stood frozen with shock and fear, waiting for some kind of dreadful destruction, the car suddenly vibrated violently. A huge, blazing mass of material\u2014something I couldn\u2019t identify\u2014came roaring and booming past the balloon, with a sound like a thousand thunders. Once my fear and astonishment eased, I had little trouble believing it was some great piece of volcanic matter thrown from the world I was rapidly approaching. It was probably one of those unusual substances sometimes found on Earth and called meteoric stones, simply because there isn\u2019t a better name.\\n\\n\u201cApril 16th. Today, as best I could, I looked up through the side windows one after another. To my great delight, I saw a very small part of the moon\u2019s disk showing on all sides beyond the huge edge of the balloon.\u201d\"}"
