          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0779637af262a33888a0bb9a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now turn to my own story. In the first part of my life there is little worth setting down, and I shall be brief; yet I must be permitted to linger a little over my childhood, so it may be seen how, when one hope failed, all life became a blank; and how, when the only affection I was allowed to value was blasted, my very existence was extinguished with it.\\n\\nI have already said that my aunt was very unlike my father. I believe that, without the faintest taint of a bad heart, she possessed the coldest nature that ever filled a human breast: she was wholly incapable of affection. She took me under her protection because she regarded it as her duty; but she had lived too long alone, undisturbed by the noise and chatter of children, to tolerate my disturbing her peace. She had never married; and for the last five years she had lived entirely by herself on an estate that had come to her through her mother, on the shores of Loch Lomond in Scotland. In his letters, my father had expressed a wish that she should come and live with me at his family mansion, which stood in a beautiful district near Richmond in Yorkshire. She would not agree to this plan; but once she had put in order the affairs her brother\u2019s departure had left to her care, she left England and took me with her to her Scottish estate.\\n\\nThe charge of me, first as a baby and afterwards until I had reached my eighth year, was entrusted to a servant of my mother\u2019s, who had accompanied us in our retirement for that purpose. I was placed in a remote part of the house, and only saw my aunt at fixed times. These came twice each day: once about noon, when she came to my nursery, and once after her dinner, when I was brought to her. She never caressed me, and while I remained in the room she seemed to fear that I might annoy her with some childish whim. My good nurse, however, always prepared me with the greatest care before she would venture into the parlour; and the dread inspired by my aunt\u2019s cold looks and her few, restrained words was so strong that I seldom broke the lessons I had been taught, nor did I betray the exemplary quiet in which I was trained during those brief visits.\\n\\nIn my good nurse\u2019s keeping, I ran wild about our park and the neighboring fields. From my earliest years I showed the offspring of the deepest love, and with it the greatest sensibility of temperament. I cannot say with what fervor I loved everything, even the inanimate objects that surrounded me.\"}"
