{"request_id":"2c92a0a99c1f2091249db06d","rewritten_text":"It is only four o’clock, yet winter has already drawn the sun down; in the clear, sharp air there are no clouds to catch and throw back its slanting light. Still, the atmosphere itself carries a faint rose tint, and that colour is taken up again by the snow spread over everything. I live in a lone cottage set alone on a broad heath—no living sound comes to me. Before my eyes lies the bleak plain, all white, except for a few dark spots where the midday sun has struck the crowns of those narrow, pointed hillocks; there the snow, as it fell, has grown thinner than on the open ground. A few birds are pecking at the hard ice that seals the pools, for the frost has held on long and steadily.

I find myself in a strange frame of mind. I am alone—utterly alone—in the world. The curse of misfortune has passed over me and left me withered; I know I am near to dying, and yet I feel glad—almost joyful. I check my pulse: it races. I press my thin hand to my cheek; it burns. There is a slight, quick spirit in me, now throwing out its last sparks. I shall never look upon the snows of another winter again—I believe I shall never feel the life-giving warmth of another summer sun. It is in that conviction that I begin to set down my tragic history. Perhaps such a history as mine ought to end with me, but some feeling I cannot name drives me on, and I am too weak, both in body and in mind, to resist even the smallest impulse. While life still had strength in me, I thought there was a kind of sacred horror in my story that made it unfit to speak aloud; and now, as I am about to die, I lay bare its mystic terrors. It is like the wood of the Eumenides—none may enter but the dying; and Oedipus is about to die.

What am I writing?—I must gather my thoughts. I do not suppose that anyone will read these pages, save you, my friend, who will take them at my death. I do not write to you alone, because it would please me to linger on our friendship in a way that would be unnecessary if you were the only one to read what I set down. So I will tell my tale as though I were writing for strangers. You have often asked what brought about my solitary life—my tears—and, above all, my silence, so impenetrable and so unkind. In life I dared not; in death I unveil the mystery. Others may fling these pages aside lightly; but to you, Woodville—kind and affectionate friend—they will be precious, the treasured memorials of a heart-broken girl who, even while dying, is still warmed by gratitude toward you. Your tears will fall on the words that record my misfortunes; I know they will—and while I have breath, I thank you for your sympathy."}

