          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0dfd8832cf70116b4c672191\",\"rewritten_text\":\"From late reports out of Rotterdam, it would seem that the place is in a state of lively philosophical excitement. Indeed, there have been happenings there of such a character\u2014so completely out of the ordinary, so wholly new, so at odds with what people had been expecting\u2014that I cannot doubt that, before long, all Europe will be thrown into an uproar, all physics thrown into confusion, and reason and astronomy together will be at loggerheads.\\n\\nIt is said that on the\u2014\u2014 day of\u2014\u2014 (I cannot swear to the date), a vast crowd of persons, assembled for purposes not set down, gathered in the great square by the Exchange in the well-ordered city of Rotterdam. The weather was warm\u2014more than usually so for the season\u2014there was scarcely any air stirring; and the people were not in the least ill-tempered at being, now and then, lightly sprinkled with friendly showers of brief duration, which dropped from great white clouds that broke and shifted across the blue vault of the sky. Still, about noon, a small but notable stir made itself felt in the gathering: the clatter of ten thousand voices took over; and, almost at once, ten thousand faces were tilted upward toward the heavens, ten thousand pipes were lowered in unison from the corners of ten thousand mouths, and a cry\u2014fit to be likened to nothing but the roar of Niagara\u2014rang out, long and loud and furious, through every quarter of Rotterdam.\\n\\nThe source of the commotion soon became plain enough. From behind the great bulk of one of those sharply outlined cloud-masses already mentioned, there was seen to drift slowly into an open patch of blue space a strange, mixed, yet apparently solid substance\u2014so oddly shaped, so capriciously put together\u2014that it could not, in any way, be understood, nor ever be admired enough by the stout burghers standing below with mouths agape. What could it be? In the name of all the vrows and devils in Rotterdam, what could it possibly mean? No one knew; no one could guess; no one\u2014even Mynheer Superbus Von Underduk, the burgomaster\u2014had the least clue to unravel the matter. So, since nothing more sensible seemed available, each man to a man replaced his pipe carefully in the corner of his mouth, and with his right eye cocked upward toward the spectacle, puffed, paused, waddled about, and grunted in a way that was unmistakably significant; then he waddled back, grunted, paused, and at last puffed again.\\n\\nMeanwhile, however, the object of all that curiosity, and the cause of all that smoke, came lower and lower toward the goodly city. In a few minutes it drew near enough to be read with certainty. It appeared to be\u2014yes!\u2014a kind of balloon; but surely no such balloon had ever been seen in Rotterdam before.\"}"
