          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8a6c0755665bcd775134c2a5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Smallpox!\\n\\nI sprang straight to the king and spoke close to his ear:\\n\\n\u201cOut the door at once, sire\u2014the woman is dying of that sickness that ruined the hem of Camelot two years back.\u201d\\n\\nHe didn\u2019t so much as move.\\n\\n\u201cBy my word, I\u2019ll stay\u2014and I\u2019ll lend a hand as well.\u201d\\n\\nI leaned in again, whispering:\\n\\n\u201cKing, it can\u2019t be. You must go.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou mean well, and you\u2019re not speaking foolishly. Still, it would be a disgrace for a king to admit fear, and a disgrace for a knight with a belt to hold back his own hand when there are those who need help. No\u2014I will not go. It\u2019s you who must leave. The Church\u2019s ban is not laid on me; it forbids you to be here, and if word reaches her of your trespass, she\u2019ll deal with you harshly.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a perilous position for him to take, one that might cost him his life, but there was no point arguing. Once he set his mind on his knightly honor being at stake, that was the end of discussion\u2014he would remain, and nothing could turn him. I knew it, so I let the matter drop.\\n\\nThe woman spoke:\\n\\n\u201cGood sir, will you climb that ladder and bring me word of what you find? Don\u2019t be afraid to tell it plain\u2014there are seasons when even a mother\u2019s heart can\u2019t be broken any further, for it\u2019s already been broken\u2014long since.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWait,\u201d said the king, \u201cand give the woman something to eat. I\u2019ll go.\u201d And he set down the knapsack.\\n\\nI started to turn, but the king had already begun. He stopped, looked down at a man lying in a dim light, and only then seemed to notice us.\\n\\n\u201cIs that your husband?\u201d the king asked.\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs he asleep?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod be thanked for that mercy, yes\u2014these three hours. Where do I pay for the full measure of my gratitude? My heart is bursting with it, for the sleep he\u2019s having now.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019ll be gentle. We won\u2019t wake him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, no\u2014you won\u2019t,\u201d she answered, \u201cfor he is dead.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDead?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. What a triumph it is to know it! No one can harm him, no one can insult him again. He\u2019s in heaven now, and happy; or if not, then he waits in hell and is content\u2014because there he\u2019ll find neither abbot nor bishop. We were boy and girl together; we were man and wife these five and twenty years, and never apart until today. Think how long it is to love and suffer side by side. This morning he was out of his mind, and in his fancy we were boy and girl again, roaming the bright fields; and so, in that innocent, glad talk, he wandered farther and farther\u2014into those other places we don\u2019t know\u2014until he was shut away from mortal sight.\u201d\"}"
