          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5f163848af9b0b95f6a7b6b1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter had secured a very fine house in Camden Place\u2014high, stately, and exactly the sort of situation that suited a man of consequence; and there both he and Elizabeth were comfortably established, to their great satisfaction.\\n\\nAnne entered with a heavy heart, expecting to be kept in confinement for many months, and inwardly asking herself, \u201cOh! when shall I be able to leave you again?\u201d Yet the welcome she received was unexpectedly warm, and it did her good. Her father and sister were glad to see her, not only for her own sake, but because they wished to show her their house and furniture, and they met her with real kindness. That she should be the fourth at their dinner-table was remarked upon as an advantage.\\n\\nMrs Clay was exceedingly agreeable and always smiling, though her civilities and grins were, in a way, what one might expect. Anne had always thought that, on her arrival, they would act out what was proper; but the ease and consideration of the others had taken her by surprise. They were evidently in excellent spirits, and she was soon meant to hear the reasons. Still, they had no intention of listening to her. After a few compliments were laid out\u2014regrets for her being so deeply missed in their old neighbourhood, which Anne could not honestly return\u2014they made only some slight inquiries, and then the conversation had to be entirely their own. Uppercross interested them not at all, Kellynch scarcely more; it was all Bath.\\n\\nThey were pleased to assure her that Bath had fully met their hopes in every particular. Their house, they said, was unquestionably the best in Camden Place; their drawing-rooms had advantages that none of the others could match, whether they had seen them or heard of them, and the superiority held no less in the style of the fittings than in the taste of the furniture. Their acquaintance was in great demand. Everyone seemed eager to pay them visits. They had declined many introductions, and yet cards were still being left by people they did not know.\\n\\nThere was no end of enjoyment here. Could Anne doubt that her father and sister were happy? She might not doubt it, but she could not help sighing that her father should feel no loss in the change, should see nothing to regret in the duties and dignity of a resident landholder, should find so much to boast of in the small vanities of a town. She had to sigh, and smile, and wonder too, as Elizabeth threw open the folding-doors and, with lively triumph, moved from one drawing-room to the other, boasting of their space; even at the thought of that woman\u2014who had once been mistress of Kellynch Hall\u2014taking pride in the extent between two walls, perhaps no more than thirty feet apart.\\n\\nBut this was not everything that was meant to make them happy. They had Mr Elliot as well.\"}"
