          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"df97fe2656bd90829433216a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After they had all joined in general lamentations over the dreadful sequel of this event\u2014which Elizabeth felt was nearly certain, and Miss Bennet could not claim was wholly impossible\u2014the former kept the conversation going. \u201cBut tell me everything about it that I haven\u2019t already heard. Give me further particulars. What did Colonel Forster say? Had they no reason to be afraid before the elopement took place? They must have seen them together\u2014forever.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cColonel Forster admitted that he had often suspected some partiality, especially on Lydia\u2019s side, but nothing that should have alarmed him. I am so grieved for him. His behavior was attentive and kind to the utmost. He was coming to us to assure us of his concern before he had any idea that they were not gone to Scotland; when that fear first became known, it only made him hurry his journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd was Denny convinced that Wickham would not marry? Did he know of their plan to run off? Had Colonel Forster seen Denny himself?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; but when Denny was questioned by him, Denny denied knowing anything of their plan, and wouldn\u2019t give his real opinion about it. He didn\u2019t repeat his belief that they wouldn\u2019t marry, and from that I\u2019m inclined to hope he may have been misunderstood before.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd until Colonel Forster came himself, not one of you had any doubt that they were truly married, I suppose?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow could such an idea have entered our minds? I felt a little uneasy\u2014a little afraid for my sister\u2019s happiness with him in marriage, because I knew his conduct had not always been quite right. My father and mother knew nothing of that; they only felt how imprudent the match must be. Kitty then admitted, with a very natural triumph at knowing more than the rest of us, that in Lydia\u2019s last letter she had prepared her for such a step. She had known, it seems, that they had been in love with each other for many weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not before they went to Brighton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, I don\u2019t believe so.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did Colonel Forster seem to think badly of Wickham himself? Does he know his true character?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI must confess that he didn\u2019t speak of Wickham as well as he used to. He believed him to be imprudent and extravagant; and since this sad affair has taken place, it\u2019s said that he left Meryton in great debt: but I hope that may be false.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Jane, if we had been less secret\u2014if we had told what we knew about him\u2014this could not have happened!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps it would have been better,\u201d her sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cBut to expose a person\u2019s earlier faults, without knowing what their present feelings are, seemed unjustifiable.\u201d\"}"
