          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5ad04ddc39307b4b37e32c2d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Other chances to make her observations would not fail to arise. Anne soon found herself in the company of all four together often enough to form an opinion, though she was too wise to admit it openly at home, where she knew it would satisfy neither husband nor wife. While she considered Louisa to be the favorite, she could not help thinking\u2014so far as she dared judge from memory and experience\u2014that Captain Wentworth was not in love with either. They were more in love with him; but that was not love. It was a kind of feverish admiration, and it might, probably must, end in love.\\n\\nCharles Hayter seemed to be aware of being slighted, and yet Henrietta had sometimes the look of being split between them. Anne longed for the power to show them all what they were doing, and to point out the evils they were exposing themselves to. She did not believe any of them were acting with guile. It gave her the greatest comfort to think that Captain Wentworth was, at least, completely unaware of the pain he was causing. There was no triumph in his manner, and no pitiful kind of triumph either. He had, probably, never heard of any claims Charles Hayter might have, and had never thought of them. He was only wrong in accepting\u2014accepting, she supposed, must be the word\u2014the attention of two young women at once.\\n\\nAfter a short struggle, however, Charles Hayter seemed to give up the field. Three days passed without his coming once to Uppercross, a very clear change. He had even refused one regular invitation to dinner; and when Mr Musgrove found him on the occasion with several large books in front of him, Mr and Mrs Musgrove were sure that nothing could be right, and talked, with grave faces, about his studying himself to death. Mary\u2019s hope and belief was that he had received a positive dismissal from Henrietta, and her husband lived under the constant expectation of seeing him tomorrow. Anne could only feel that Charles Hayter was wise.\\n\\nOne morning, about this time, when Charles Musgrove and Captain Wentworth were out shooting together, the sisters in the Cottage were sitting quietly at their work when they were visited at the window by the sisters from the Mansion-house.\\n\\nIt was a very fine November day, and the Miss Musgroves came through the little grounds, stopping for no other reason than to say that they were going to take a long walk, and therefore concluded that Mary could not possibly want to go with them. When Mary immediately replied, with some jealousy at not being thought a good walker, \u201cOh, yes, I should like to join you very much; I am very fond of a long walk,\u201d Anne felt, from the looks of the two girls, that it was precisely what they did not want. She admired again the kind of necessity that family habits seemed to create\u2014how everything had to be shared, and everything had to be done together, even when it was undesired and inconvenient.\"}"
