ement. Still they were always together, and their happiness seemed like Paradise: they studied side by side, laid plans for their future employments, and drank in love and delight from each other\u2019s looks and words, scarcely murmuring at the delay that stood between them and their complete union. Woodville rose ever higher in renown; and Elinor grew more lovely and more wise under the instruction of her accomplished lover.\\n\\nIn two months Elinor would be twenty-one: everything had been arranged for their marriage. How can I set down the catastrophe against so much joy?\u2014for the earth would not be the earth it is, if it were not strewn with blight and sorrow, when such angelic creatures as these had been allowed to exist for one another; search the world over, and you will not find the perfect happiness their marriage would have brought them. There must have been some overturning in the order of things, as it is established among us miserable dwellers on earth, to have admitted such consummate joy.\"}"
