          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2f8e9755420d90d9c7e831f4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell, monsieur le mauvais sujet,\u201d the old lady called out when he came into the room. \u201cWhy haven\u2019t you come to see me all this time?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lady Clem, I never get a moment to myself,\u201d said Lord Arthur, smiling.\\n\\n\u201cI suppose you mean you spend all day with Miss Sybil Merton, buying chiffons and talking nonsense. I can\u2019t understand why people make such a fuss about getting married. In my day, we never dreamed of flirting in public, or even in private.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you I haven\u2019t seen Sybil for twenty-four hours, Lady Clem. From what I can tell, she\u2019s completely taken up with her milliners.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course. That\u2019s the only reason you come to visit an ugly old woman like me. I wonder men don\u2019t take warning. On a fait des folies pour moi, and here I am\u2014a poor, rheumatic creature, with a false front and a bad temper. If it weren\u2019t for dear Lady Jansen, who sends me the worst French novels she can find, I don\u2019t think I could get through the day. Doctors are no help at all, except to take your money. They can\u2019t even cure my heartburn.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve brought you something to help with that, Lady Clem,\u201d said Lord Arthur gravely. \u201cIt\u2019s a wonderful thing, invented by an American.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think I like American inventions, Arthur. I\u2019m sure I don\u2019t. I read a few American novels lately, and they were full of nonsense.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, but there\u2019s no nonsense at all in this, Lady Clem! I promise it\u2019s a perfect cure. You must promise to try it.\u201d Then Lord Arthur took the little box from his pocket and handed it to her.\\n\\n\u201cWell, the box is charming, Arthur. Is it really a gift? That\u2019s very kind of you. And is this the wonderful medicine? It looks like a bonbon. I\u2019ll take it right away.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood heavens, Lady Clem!\u201d Lord Arthur cried, grabbing her hand. \u201cYou mustn\u2019t do that. It\u2019s a homoeopathic medicine, and if you take it when you don\u2019t have heartburn, it could do you a lot of harm. Wait until you have an attack, and take it then. You\u2019ll be amazed by the result.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019d like to take it now,\u201d said Lady Clementina, holding the little transparent capsule up to the light. Inside was a floating bubble of liquid aconitine. \u201cI\u2019m sure it\u2019s delicious. The truth is, even though I hate doctors, I love medicines. Still, I\u2019ll save it for my next attack.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd when will that be?\u201d Lord Arthur asked eagerly.\"}"
