{"request_id":"647c2c811f0c0b019ba85937","rewritten_text":"I could remember what it was like during our great civil war in the nineteenth century. In the North, a carpenter earned three dollars a day, based on gold value. In the South, he earned fifty dollars, but it was paid in Confederate shinplasters that were worth a dollar a bushel. In the North, a set of overalls cost three dollars, which was a day’s wages. In the South, the same overalls cost seventy-five dollars, which was two days’ wages. Everything else worked out the same way. So wages were about twice as high in the North as in the South, because the North’s pay had much more buying power.

Yes, I made various acquaintances in the hamlet, and one thing that pleased me a lot was seeing our new coins in circulation. There were plenty of milrays, plenty of mills, plenty of cents, lots of nickels, and some silver. You could find them among the artisans and ordinary people, and even some gold, though that was at the bank, meaning the goldsmith’s shop. I went in while Marco, the son of Marco, was arguing with a shopkeeper about a quarter of a pound of salt, and I asked for change for a twenty-dollar gold piece. They did it, but only after they had chewed the coin, tapped it on the counter, and tested it with acid. Then they asked me where I got it, who I was, where I was from, where I was going, when I expected to arrive, and probably a couple of hundred more questions. When they finally ran out of questions, I kept going and gave them a lot of information on my own. I told them I owned a dog named Watch. I told them my first wife was a Free Will Baptist, and that her grandfather was a Prohibitionist. I told them I used to know a man who had two thumbs on each hand and a wart on the inside of his upper lip, and who died hoping for a glorious resurrection, and so on, and so on, and so on, until even that hungry village questioner started to look satisfied, and a little less tense. Still, he had to respect a man with my financial strength, so he didn’t give me any trouble. But I noticed he took his frustration out on his workers, which felt like a perfectly normal thing to do.

Yes, they changed my twenty, but I thought it strained the bank a bit. That was to be expected. It was like walking into a small village store in the nineteenth century and suddenly asking the owner to change a two-thousand-dollar bill. He might be able to do it, but he would also wonder how a small farmer ended up carrying that much money around. The goldsmith probably thought the same thing. He followed me to the door and stood there watching me go, with reverent admiration."}

