          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0674092b7f20ed45bbc70e3b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"A few days after that, Virginia and her curly-haired cavalier went riding out on Brockley meadows. In pushing through a hedge, she tore her habit so badly that, when she got home, she decided she would go up by the back staircase so she wouldn\u2019t be seen. As she hurried past the Tapestry Chamber\u2014whose door happened to be open\u2014she thought she saw someone inside. Thinking it was her mother\u2019s maid, who sometimes used to bring her work there, she looked in to ask her to mend her habit. To her immense surprise, it was the Canterville Ghost himself.\\n\\nHe was sitting by the window, watching the ruined gold of the yellowing trees whirl through the air, while red leaves danced wildly down the long avenue. His head rested on his hand, and his whole posture showed extreme depression. In fact, he looked so miserable, so broken down, that little Virginia\u2014whose first thought had been to run away and lock herself in her room\u2014felt pity and decided to try to comfort him. Her footsteps were so light, and his melancholy so deep, that he didn\u2019t notice her until she spoke.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m so sorry for you,\u201d she said. \u201cBut my brothers are going back to Eton tomorrow, and then, if you behave yourself, no one will bother you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s ridiculous to ask me to behave myself,\u201d he replied, turning in astonishment toward the pretty little girl who had dared to address him. \u201cIt\u2019s absurd. I have to rattle my chains, groan through keyholes, and walk about at night\u2014if that\u2019s what you mean. It\u2019s my only reason for existing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s no reason at all to exist,\u201d Virginia said, \u201cand you know you\u2019ve been very wicked. Mrs. Umney told us, the very first day we arrived here, that you killed your wife.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I\u2019ll admit it,\u201d the Ghost said petulantly. \u201cBut it was purely a family matter, and it concerned no one else.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s very wrong to kill anyone,\u201d Virginia said. At times she had a sweet Puritan seriousness she\u2019d picked up from some old New England ancestor.\\n\\n\u201cOh, I hate the cheap severity of abstract ethics!\u201d the Ghost snapped. \u201cMy wife was plain enough\u2014she never had my ruffs properly starched, and she knew nothing about cookery. Why, there was a buck I shot in Hogley Woods\u2014a magnificent pricket\u2014and do you know how she had it sent up to the table? But it doesn\u2019t matter now. It\u2019s all over. And I don\u2019t think it was very nice of her brothers to starve me to death, even though I did kill her.\u201d\"}"
