          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7a586b8327cd5a5eabb953b7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His manner was stubborn to the extreme, and \u201cdat deuced bug\u201d were the only words that escaped his lips during the trip. For my part, I carried a couple of dark lanterns, while Legrand kept himself occupied with the _scarab\u00e6us_, which he had tied to the end of a short whip-cord; he swung it back and forth as he walked, turning it like a conjurer. When I noticed this last, clear sign of my friend\u2019s disturbed state of mind, I could hardly keep from crying. Still, I thought it best to indulge his fancy\u2014for now, at least\u2014until I could take some more forceful measures with a better chance of success. Meanwhile, I tried, but in vain, to get him to talk about the purpose of the expedition. Once I had agreed to accompany him, he seemed unwilling to discuss anything of minor importance, and for every question I asked, the only answer he gave was, \u201cwe shall see!\u201d\\n\\nWe crossed the creek at the head of the island in a skiff, and then, climbing the high ground along the shore of the mainland, we continued in a northwesterly direction through a stretch of country so wild and desolate that there was no sign of any human footstep. Legrand led the way with determination, stopping only for a moment now and then to consult what looked like certain landmarks of his own making from a previous occasion.\\n\\nIn this way we traveled for about two hours, and the sun was just setting when we entered a region far more bleak than anything we had seen yet. It was a kind of plateau near the top of an almost inaccessible hill\u2014densely wooded from base to peak\u2014and broken up by huge crags that seemed to rest loosely on the ground. In many places they were held from crashing down into the valleys below only by the trees they leaned against. Deep ravines running in different directions added an even harsher solemnity to the scene.\\n\\nThe natural platform we had climbed onto was thick with brambles. We soon found that we could not have pushed through them at all without the scythe. And at his master\u2019s direction, Jupiter cleared a path to the base of an enormously tall tulip-tree. It stood on the level ground with eight or ten oaks, but it towered over them all\u2014and over every other tree I had ever seen then\u2014in the beauty of its foliage and shape, in the wide spread of its branches, and in the overall majesty of its appearance.\"}"
