          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4cb6c42341845a8727291e82\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was long past noon when he woke. His valet had slipped into the room several times on tiptoe, just to see whether he was stirring, and had wondered why his young master slept so late. At last the bell sounded, and Victor came in quietly with a cup of tea and a stack of letters on a small tray of old Sevres china. He drew back the olive-satin curtains, with their shimmering blue lining, that hung in front of the three tall windows.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur has slept well this morning,\u201d he said, smiling.\\n\\n\u201cWhat time is it, Victor?\u201d Dorian Gray asked, drowsy.\\n\\n\u201cOne hour and a quarter, Monsieur.\u201d\\n\\nHow late it was! He sat up, sipped his tea, and began to sort through his letters. One of them was from Lord Henry and had been delivered by hand that morning. He hesitated for a moment, then set it aside. The others he opened without much interest. They held the usual assortment of cards\u2014dinner invitations, tickets for private viewings, programs for charity concerts, and the like that are showered on fashionable young men every morning during the season. There was also a rather heavy bill for a chased silver Louis-Quinze toilet set he had not yet had the courage to send to his guardians. They were extremely old-fashioned and didn\u2019t seem to understand that in our day, unnecessary things are the only necessities. Along with that were several very politely worded notes from money-lenders on Jermyn Street, offering to advance any sum at a moment\u2019s notice, at the most reasonable rates of interest.\\n\\nAfter about ten minutes, he got up and threw on an elaborate dressing gown of silk-embroidered cashmere wool. Then he went into the onyx-paved bathroom. The cool water refreshed him after his long sleep. It felt as though he had forgotten everything he had been through. A faint sense that he had taken part in some strange tragedy came back to him now and then, but it was only the unreal feeling of a dream.\\n\\nThe moment he was dressed, he went into the library and sat down to a light French breakfast laid out for him on a small round table near the open window. It was an exquisite day. The warm air seemed full of spices. A bee flew in and buzzed around the blue-dragon bowl in front of him, filled with sulphur-yellow roses. He felt completely happy.\\n\\nThen his eyes fell on the screen he had placed in front of the portrait, and he started.\\n\\n\u201cToo cold for Monsieur?\u201d asked his valet, setting an omelette on the table. \u201cI shut the window?\u201d\\n\\nDorian shook his head. \u201cI\u2019m not cold,\u201d he murmured.\"}"
