          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"363e7d1829e9ad1e0f8ffadc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Should I ask them? Or should I ask Miss Bates? She is even closer.\u2014And I do not know whether Miss Bates might not understand the feelings of the rest of the people as well as anyone. I think we do need a bigger group to decide. What if I go and invite Miss Bates to join us?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014if you please,\u201d said Mrs. Weston, sounding a little unsure, \u201cif you think she will be any help.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will get nothing useful from Miss Bates,\u201d said Emma. \u201cShe will be full of delight and gratitude, but she will tell you nothing. She won\u2019t even listen to your questions. I see no point in consulting Miss Bates.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut she is so amusing\u2014so extremely amusing! I really enjoy hearing Miss Bates talk. And you don\u2019t need to bring the whole family, you know.\u201d\\n\\nThen Mr. Weston joined them. When he heard what was being proposed, he fully agreed.\\n\\n\u201cYes, do, Frank. Go and fetch Miss Bates, and let\u2019s settle it right away. She will enjoy the plan, I\u2019m sure, and I don\u2019t know anyone better for showing us how to get rid of difficulties. Fetch Miss Bates. We\u2019re being a little too careful. She is a constant lesson in how to be happy. But fetch them both. Invite them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBoth, sir! Can the old lady?\u201d...\\n\\n\u201cThe old lady! No, the young lady, of course. I\u2019ll think you a great blockhead, Frank, if you bring the aunt without the niece.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I beg your pardon, sir. I didn\u2019t think of it at once. Of course, if you want it, I will try to persuade them both.\u201d And he ran off.\\n\\nLong before he came back, he returned with the aunt\u2014short, neat, and brisk\u2014and with her elegant niece. Mrs. Weston, being kind and good-tempered, and a good wife, had gone over the passage again. She now found the problems were much smaller than she had first thought\u2014really, they were very trifling. With that, the question of what to do was settled. After that, everything else was smooth, at least in their minds. The small details\u2014table and chair, lights and music, tea and supper\u2014either arranged themselves, or were left as little matters that could be handled anytime between Mrs. Weston and Mrs. Stokes. Everyone who was invited would certainly come. Frank had already written to Enscombe to suggest that he stay a few days longer than his fortnight, and that could not possibly be refused. And it would be a delightful dance.\\n\\nWhen Miss Bates arrived, she agreed with them most warmly. As a counsellor, she was not needed, but as an approver\u2014a much safer role\u2014she was truly welcome. Her approval, both general and detailed, was constant and enthusiastic, and it could not help but please them. For another half-hour they all walked back and forth between the different rooms, some offering ideas, some paying attention, and all enjoying the happy prospect of what was coming.\"}"
