          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c2b36345e1b78d3b57b578af\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was not every person who would have stood out in so considerate a manner as she did, and refused to accept Jane\u2019s answer; yet she plainly insisted that she would not write any such denial yesterday, just as Jane wished. She would wait\u2014and sure enough, by yesterday evening everything was settled, and Jane was to go. What a surprise to me! I had not the least notion!\u2014Jane took Mrs. Elton aside and told her at once that, after thinking over the advantages of Mrs. Smallridge\u2019s situation, she had resolved to accept it. \u201cI did not know a word of it until it was all settled.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid you spend the evening with Mrs. Elton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, all of us. Mrs. Elton would have us come. It was settled that way, upon the hill, while we were walking about with Mr. Knightley. \u2018You must all spend your evening with us,\u2019 said she\u2014\u2018I positively must have you all come.\u2019\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Knightley was there too, then?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, not Mr. Knightley; he refused it from the first. And though I thought he might come, because Mrs. Elton said she would not let him off, he did not. Still, my mother, and Jane, and I, were all there, and we had a very agreeable evening. Such kind friends, you know, Miss Woodhouse\u2014one always finds them agreeable, though everybody seemed rather worn out after the morning\u2019s party. Even pleasure, you know, is fatiguing; and I cannot say that any of them appeared to have enjoyed it very much. Still, I shall always think it a very pleasant party, and I feel extremely obliged to the kind friends who included me in it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Fairfax, I suppose, though you were not aware of it, had been making up her mind all day?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say she had.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhenever the time may come, it must be unwelcome to her and all her friends\u2014but I hope her engagement will have every alleviation that is possible. I mean, as to the character and manners of the family.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, dear Miss Woodhouse. Yes, indeed, there is everything in the world that can make her happy in it. Except the Sucklings and Bragges, there is not such another nursery establishment\u2014so liberal and so elegant\u2014in all Mrs. Elton\u2019s acquaintance. Mrs. Smallridge is a most delightful woman!\u2014a style of living almost equal to Maple Grove. And as for the children: except the little Sucklings and little Bragges, there are not such elegant, sweet children anywhere. Jane will be treated with such regard and kindness!\u2014It will be nothing but pleasure, a life of pleasure. And her salary!\u2014I really cannot venture to name her salary to you, Miss Woodhouse.\u201d\"}"
