          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"93501ac97c669b97db39e4ca\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But there was no joy in the little town that calm Saturday afternoon. The Harpers, along with Aunt Polly\u2019s family, were being put into mourning, with deep grief and many tears. The village felt unusually quiet, even though it was usually quiet enough. People moved around as if something was missing, and they spoke very little, but they sighed again and again. The Saturday holiday felt like a burden to the children. They had no interest in their games, and slowly they stopped.\\n\\nIn the afternoon, Becky Thatcher found herself sitting and brooding in the empty schoolhouse yard. She felt very sad, but there was nothing there that could comfort her. She talked to herself.\\n\\n\u201cOh, if I only had that brass andiron-knob again! But I don\u2019t have anything now to remember him by.\u201d She swallowed hard and held back a sob.\\n\\nAfter a moment, she stopped and said to herself again:\\n\\n\u201cIt was right here. Oh, if I could do it over, I wouldn\u2019t say that\u2014I wouldn\u2019t say it for the whole world. But he\u2019s gone now. I\u2019ll never, never, never see him again.\u201d\\n\\nThat thought broke her down. She walked away with tears running down her cheeks. Then a group of boys and girls came by\u2014Tom\u2019s and Joe\u2019s playmates. They stood by the fence and talked in reverent voices about what Tom did the last time they saw him, and about what Joe said and did, with small details that now felt like terrible warnings. Each person pointed to the exact spot where the boys had stood when they were last seen, and then added something like, \u201cAnd I was standing just like that\u2014just as I am now. If you was him, I was as close as that. And he smiled, just like this. Then something seemed to go through me\u2014awful, you know. I didn\u2019t understand it then, but I can see it now!\u201d\\n\\nAfter that, they argued about who had seen the dead boys last while they were still alive. Many claimed that grim honor and offered proof, though it was more or less changed by the person telling it. When they finally decided who had seen the boys last and who had exchanged the last words with them, the lucky ones acted as if they were especially important. Everyone else stared at them and envied them. One poor boy, who had no other kind of glory to offer, said with clear pride in the memory:\\n\\n\u201cWell, Tom Sawyer beat me once.\u201d\\n\\nBut that attempt at fame did not work. Most of the boys could say the same thing, so it made the distinction feel cheap. The group drifted away slowly, still remembering the lost heroes in hushed, awed voices.\"}"
