          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"64a7ab10d146ac428a3b2b2f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I know she would!\u201d\\n\\nThe Widow Douglas\u2019 splendid hospitality was a tempting lure. It, along with Tom\u2019s arguments, soon won the day. So it was decided to tell nobody anything about the night\u2019s plan. Presently Tom realized that maybe Huck might come this very night and give the signal. The thought drained a lot of the excitement from his anticipation. Still, he couldn\u2019t bring himself to give up the fun at Widow Douglas\u2019. And why should he? he reasoned\u2014the signal hadn\u2019t come the night before, so why would it be any more likely tonight? The certain fun of the evening outweighed the uncertain prize; and, like a boy, he decided to follow the stronger pull and not let himself think about the box of money again that day.\\n\\nThree miles below town, the ferryboat stopped at the mouth of a wooded hollow and tied up. The crowd poured ashore, and soon the forest distances and jagged heights echoed far and near with shouting and laughter. Every way of getting hot and tired was tried out, and by and by the wanderers drifted back to camp with appetites that felt properly earned\u2014then the destruction of the good things began. After the meal came a refreshing stretch of rest and conversation in the shade of spreading oaks. Soon someone shouted:\\n\\n\u201cWho\u2019s ready for the cave?\u201d\\n\\nEveryone was. Bundles of candles were gathered, and right away there was a scramble up the hill. The cave\u2019s mouth was up the hillside\u2014an opening shaped like the letter A. Its massive wooden door stood unbarred. Inside was a small chamber, chilly as an icehouse, and lined by Nature with solid limestone that sweated cold. It was romantic and mysterious to stand in this deep gloom and look out over the green valley shining in the sun. But the impressiveness didn\u2019t last long, and the roughhousing started again. The moment a candle was lit, everyone rushed toward the person holding it; there was a struggle and a brave defense, but the candle was soon knocked down or blown out, and then there was a happy uproar of laughter and a fresh chase.\\n\\nBut everything has an end. By and by the group began filing down the steep descent of the main avenue, the flickering line of lights dimly revealing the tall rock walls almost to where they met\u2014sixty feet overhead. This main avenue was no more than eight or ten feet wide. Every few steps, other lofty but even narrower crevices branched off on either side\u2014for McDougal\u2019s cave was a vast labyrinth of crooked passageways that ran into each other and then out again, leading nowhere.\"}"
