          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f4b33edc7ac80536aae00d18\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cSo what result, then, has followed? What impression have I made on your imagination?\u201d\\n\\nI felt my skin crawl as Dupin asked the question. \u201cA madman,\u201d I said. \u201cSomeone raving, escaped from a nearby _Maison de Sant\u00e9_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn some respects,\u201d he replied, \u201cyour idea isn\u2019t irrelevant. But the voices of madmen\u2014even in their wildest attacks\u2014are never a match for that particular voice heard on the stairs. Madmen belong to some nation, and even when their speech is incoherent, it still has the coherence of syllables. Besides, the hair of a madman isn\u2019t like the tuft I\u2019m holding in my hand. I\u2019ve disentangled this little clump from the rigidly clenched fingers of Madame L\u2019Espanaye. Tell me what you make of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDupin!\u201d I said, completely unnerved. \u201cThis hair is most unusual\u2014this isn\u2019t _human_ hair.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI haven\u2019t claimed that it is,\u201d he said. \u201cBut before we decide this point, I want you to look at the small sketch I\u2019ve traced here on this paper. It\u2019s a _fac-simile_ drawing of what, in one part of the testimony, is described as \u2018dark bruises, and deep indentations of finger nails,\u2019 on the throat of Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye, and in another\u2014by Messrs. Dumas and Etienne\u2014as \u2018a series of livid spots, evidently the impression of fingers.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ll see,\u201d he continued, spreading the paper on the table between us, \u201cthat this drawing suggests a firm, fixed hold. There\u2019s no _slipping_ anywhere. Each finger has kept\u2014perhaps until the victim died\u2014the terrible grip with which it originally dug in. Now try placing all your fingers at the same time into the impressions you see.\u201d\\n\\nI tried, but it was useless.\\n\\n\u201cWe may not be giving this matter a fair test,\u201d he said. \u201cThe paper is laid out on a flat surface, but the human throat is cylindrical. Here\u2019s a piece of wood whose circumference is about the same as the throat. Wrap the drawing around it, and try again.\u201d\\n\\nI did, but the problem was even clearer than before. \u201cThis,\u201d I said, \u201cisn\u2019t the mark of any human hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow read,\u201d Dupin replied, \u201cthis passage from Cuvier.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a minute anatomical and generally descriptive account of the large, tawny Ourang-Outang of the East Indian islands. The enormous size, the prodigious strength and activity, the wild ferocity, and the imitative tendencies of these mammals were well known to everyone. I understood the full horror of the murder at once.\\n\\n\u201cThe description of the digits,\u201d I said, after I finished reading, \u201cmatches this drawing exactly.\u201d\"}"
