          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f0c2a55900d68e33bd1a0fb2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They brought along a very nice-looking old gentleman and a very nice-looking younger one, with his right arm in a sling. And, my souls, how the people yelled and laughed, and kept it up. But I didn\u2019t see anything funny about it, and I thought it would make the duke and the king look bad to watch. I figured they\u2019d turn pale. But no\u2014none of them turned pale. The duke didn\u2019t show any suspicion at all. He just walked around with his eyes wide, acting happy and pleased, like a jug that\u2019s spilling buttermilk. As for the king, he just stared and stared at the new arrivals, looking sad, like the thought of such frauds and rascals in the world gave him a stomachache right in his heart. He carried it off admirably. A lot of the important people gathered around the king so he could see they were on his side.\\n\\nThe old gentleman who had just arrived looked completely confused. Pretty soon he started to speak, and I could tell right away he sounded like an Englishman\u2014not the king\u2019s way, though the king\u2019s was pretty good for an imitation. I can\u2019t repeat the old man\u2019s exact words, and I can\u2019t copy his voice, but he turned to the crowd and said something like this:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a surprise to me. I wasn\u2019t expecting it, and I\u2019ll be honest\u2014I\u2019m not in a good position to answer it. My brother and I have had bad luck. He broke his arm, and our baggage was left behind at a town above here last night because of a mistake. I am Peter Wilks\u2019 brother, Harvey, and this is his brother, William. William can\u2019t hear or speak, and he can\u2019t even make signs very well, because he only has one hand to use. We are who we say we are. In a day or two, when I get the baggage, I can prove it. Until then, I won\u2019t say anything more. I\u2019ll go to the hotel and wait.\u201d\\n\\nSo Harvey and the new dummy started off. The king laughed and talked right away.\\n\\n\u201cBroke his arm\u2014very likely, isn\u2019t it?\u2014and very convenient too, for a fraud who has to make signs and hasn\u2019t learned how. Lost their baggage! That\u2019s mighty good\u2014and mighty clever\u2014under the circumstances!\u201d\\n\\nHe laughed again, and everyone else laughed too, except for three or four, maybe half a dozen. One of them was that doctor. Another was a sharp-looking man with an old-fashioned carpet-bag, who had just come off the steamboat. He was talking to the doctor in a low voice, looking toward the king now and then, nodding his head. That was Levi Bell, the lawyer who had gone up to Louisville. The others included a big, rough, husky man who had come along and listened to everything the old gentleman said, and who was listening to the king now as well.\"}"
