          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"59c2bc80a394866300e99707\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The winters along the latitude of Sullivan\u2019s Island are seldom very severe, and in the fall of the year it is a rare thing indeed for a fire to be considered necessary. About the middle of October, 18\u2014, however, there occurred a day of remarkable chill. Just before sunset I made my way through the evergreens to my friend\u2019s hut\u2014a place I hadn\u2019t visited for several weeks. At that time my home was in Charleston, nine miles from the island, and the means of getting there and back were far behind what they are today. When I reached the hut, I knocked, as I always did, and, getting no answer, searched for the key where I knew it was hidden. I unlocked the door and went in.\\n\\nA fine fire was blazing on the hearth. It was a welcome novelty, and certainly not an ungrateful one. I shrugged off my overcoat, took an armchair by the crackling logs, and waited patiently for my hosts to arrive.\\n\\nSoon after dark they came, and gave me a very warm welcome. Jupiter, grinning from ear to ear, bustled about to prepare some marsh-hens for supper. Legrand was in one of his moods\u2014how else can I describe them?\u2014of excitement. He had found an unknown bivalve, forming a new genus, and, even more than that, he had tracked down and secured, with Jupiter\u2019s help, a _scarab\u00e6us_ that he believed to be totally new. Still, he wanted my opinion on it the next day.\\n\\n\u201cAnd why not tonight?\u201d I asked, rubbing my hands over the blaze, and wishing the whole tribe of _scarab\u00e6i_ would go to the devil.\\n\\n\u201cAh, if I\u2019d only known you were here!\u201d Legrand said. \u201cBut it\u2019s been so long since I saw you, and how could I have guessed you\u2019d come to visit me this very night of all nights? As I was coming home, I met Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014 from the fort, and, very foolishly, I lent him the bug. So you won\u2019t be able to see it until morning. Stay here tonight, and I\u2019ll send Jup down for it at sunrise. It\u2019s the loveliest thing in creation!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat?\u2014sunrise?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing of the sort! No\u2014the bug. It\u2019s a brilliant gold color, about the size of a large hickory nut, with two jet-black spots near one end of the back, and another, somewhat longer, at the other. The _antenn\u00e6_ are\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDey aint no tin in him, Massa Will, I keep a tellin on you,\u201d Jupiter cut in. \u201cDe bug is a goole bug\u2014solid, ebery bit of him, inside and all, sep him wing. Neber feel half so hebby a bug in my life.\u201d\"}"
