          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8463bdfeeb06eee3906b2eee\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Those old, frail people were always seen by the public bowing, and it was widely believed that after they had bowed a customer out, they would keep bowing in the empty office until they bowed another customer in.\\n\\nThe barrister was sharp enough to see that the banker wouldn\u2019t have gone so far in his opinion on anything less solid than moral certainty. Unprepared as he was for the big pill he had to swallow, he got it down. \u201cAnd now,\u201d said Mr. Stryver, shaking his forensic forefinger at the Temple in general, once it was down, \u201cmy way out of this is to put you all in the wrong.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a bit of the craft of an Old Bailey tactician, and it gave him real relief. \u201cYou won\u2019t put me in the wrong, young lady,\u201d said Mr. Stryver; \u201cI\u2019ll do that for you.\u201d\\n\\nSo when Mr. Lorry called that night as late as ten o\u2019clock, Mr. Stryver, amid a pile of books and papers scattered for the purpose, seemed to have nothing on his mind at all about the subject of the morning. He even looked surprised to see Mr. Lorry, and was entirely absent-minded and preoccupied.\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d said that good-natured messenger, after a full half-hour of fruitless attempts to bring him back to the point. \u201cI\u2019ve been to Soho.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo Soho?\u201d Mr. Stryver repeated, coldly. \u201cOh, of course! What was I thinking of!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I have no doubt,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201cthat I was right in the conversation we had. My opinion is confirmed, and I repeat my advice.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you,\u201d Mr. Stryver replied, in the friendliest tone, \u201cthat I\u2019m sorry for it on your account, and sorry for it on the poor father\u2019s account. I know this must always be a painful subject for the family\u2014let\u2019s not say more about it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t understand you,\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI dare say not,\u201d Stryver answered, nodding in a smoothing, final way; \u201cno matter, no matter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut it does matter,\u201d Mr. Lorry insisted.\\n\\n\u201cNo, it doesn\u2019t. I assure you it doesn\u2019t. Having supposed there was sense where there is no sense, and a worthy ambition where there is no worthy ambition, I\u2019m well out of my mistake, and no harm is done. Young women have committed similar follies often before, and have repented them in poverty and obscurity often before. In an unselfish sense, I\u2019m sorry the matter is dropped, because it would have been a bad thing for me in a worldly point of view; in a selfish sense, I\u2019m glad it\u2019s dropped, because it would have been a bad thing for me in a worldly point of view\u2014it\u2019s hardly necessary to say I could have gained nothing from it.\u201d\"}"
