          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c20cf2f059b89078cdaa4328\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNo, no\u2014this is for to-morrow; and I had it in my mind, above all, to see Knightley to-day, for that very reason.\u2014What a dreadful, broiling morning!\u2014I even walked over the fields too\u2014(with a tone of strong complaint,)\u2014and that only made matters worse. And then, to find him not at home! I assure you I am by no means satisfied. There was not a word of apology, not a message for me. The housekeeper said she knew nothing of my being expected.\u2014How very odd!\u2014And not a soul could tell which way he had gone. Perhaps to Hartfield, perhaps to the Abbey Mill, perhaps into his woods.\u2014Miss Woodhouse, this is quite unlike our friend Knightley!\u2014Can you make it out?\u201d\\n\\nEmma found amusement in insisting that it was indeed most extraordinary, and that she had not a syllable to offer in his defence.\\n\\n\u201cI cannot conceive,\u201d said Mrs. Elton, (with the proper sense of being wronged that a wife ought to feel,) \u201chow he could treat you so, of all people in the world! The very last person one should think of as being forgotten!\u2014My dear Mr. E., he must have left you some message; I am sure he must.\u2014Not even Knightley could be so very eccentric;\u2014and his servants have been careless about it. Depend upon it, that is what happened: and it is very likely with the Donwell servants, who, as I have often remarked, are all extremely awkward and remiss. I am sure I would not have such a creature as his Harry standing at our sideboard for any consideration. And as for Mrs. Hodges, Wright values her very little indeed.\u2014She promised Wright a receipt, and never sent it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI met William Larkins,\u201d continued Mr. Elton, \u201cas I came near the house, and he told me I should not find his master at home; but I would not believe him. William looked quite out of sorts. He said he did not know what had come over his master of late, but that he could hardly ever get a word from him. I have nothing to do with William\u2019s affairs, but it is really of the utmost consequence that _I_ should see Knightley to-day; and so it is a matter of very serious inconvenience that I should have taken this hot walk to no purpose.\u201d\\n\\nEmma thought she could do nothing better than go home at once. Most likely she was being waited for there at this very moment; and Mr. Knightley might be kept from sinking still deeper into his resentment against Mr. Elton, if not against William Larkins.\\n\\nWhen she took her leave, she was glad to observe that Miss Fairfax was resolved to accompany her out of the room, and to go with her even downstairs; it gave her an opening which she instantly seized, to say,\u201d\"}"
