          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"755f8f7a567d9a29fa8412ef\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And if he asked me why I wanted it, and why I thought I had any right to it, I would tell him\u2014though he cared very little for dreams like that\u2014that I had loved Estella dearly and for a long time. Even though I had lost her and had to live a bereaved life, anything connected to her was still closer to me and dearer than anything else in the world. Seeing that Mr. Jaggers stood quite still and silent, and apparently just as stubborn as ever, in response to my appeal, I turned to Wemmick and said, \u201cWemmick, I know you to be a man with a gentle heart. I\u2019ve seen your pleasant home, your old father, and all the innocent, cheerful, playful ways you have of refreshing your business life. I beg you to say a word for me to Mr. Jaggers, and to make him understand that, considering all the circumstances, he ought to be more open with me!\u201d\\n\\nI have never seen two men look more strangely at one another than Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick did after that. At first, I feared Wemmick would be dismissed from his employment on the spot; but that worry faded as I saw Mr. Jaggers relax into something like a smile, and Wemmick grow bolder.\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s all this?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers. \u201cYou with an old father, and you with pleasant and playful ways?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d Wemmick replied. \u201cIf I don\u2019t bring \u2019em here, what does it matter?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, putting his hand on my arm and smiling openly, \u201cthis man must be the most cunning impostor in all London.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot a bit of it,\u201d Wemmick shot back, getting bolder and bolder. \u201cI think you\u2019re the other one.\u201d\\n\\nOnce again they exchanged those odd looks of theirs, each still apparently suspicious that the other was letting him in.\\n\\n\u201c_You_ with a pleasant home?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers.\\n\\n\u201cSince it don\u2019t interfere with business,\u201d Wemmick answered, \u201clet it be so. Now, I look at you, sir, and I wouldn\u2019t be surprised if _you_ might be planning and scheming to have a pleasant home of your own one of these days, when you\u2019re tired of all this work.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Jaggers nodded his head two or three times, as if looking back on it, and actually let out a sigh. \u201cPip,\u201d he said, \u201cwe won\u2019t talk about \u2018poor dreams.\u2019 You know more about that sort of thing than I do, having had much fresher experience. But now about this other matter. I\u2019ll put a case to you. Mind\u2014 I admit nothing.\u201d\\n\\nHe waited for me to say that I understood he had expressly said he admitted nothing.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Pip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, \u201cput this case. Put the case that a woman, under circumstances like the ones you\u2019ve mentioned, held her child concealed, and was forced to tell her legal adviser about it\u2014because he represented to her that he must know, with an eye to the latitude of his defence, how things stood regarding that child.\u201d\"}"
