          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e10ad771ef09fc0915118574\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Merton was very upset about the second postponement of the marriage. Lady Julia, who had already ordered her wedding dress, did everything she could to make Sybil call off the match. But Sybil loved her mother too much to turn against her. She had put her whole life in Lord Arthur\u2019s hands, and nothing Lady Julia said could shake her faith. \\n\\nAs for Lord Arthur, it took him days to get over his terrible disappointment, and for a while his nerves were completely wrecked. Still, his good common sense soon returned. His practical mind did not keep him in doubt for long about what to do. Since poison had failed completely, he decided that dynamite, or some other kind of explosive, was the obvious thing to try.\\n\\nSo he went back through the list of friends and relatives he could reach, and after careful thought he decided to blow up his uncle, the Dean of Chichester. The Dean was a learned, well-cultured man who loved clocks. He had a remarkable collection of timepieces, from the fifteenth century up to the present day. Lord Arthur thought this hobby gave him a perfect chance to carry out his plan. Getting an explosive machine, however, was another matter. The London Directory did not help him at all. He also felt there was little point in going to Scotland Yard, because they never seemed to know anything about the dynamite faction\u2019s movements until after an explosion had already happened, and even then they often knew very little.\\n\\nThen he remembered his friend Rouvaloff, a young Russian with very revolutionary ideas. Lord Arthur had met him at Lady Windermere\u2019s in the winter. Count Rouvaloff was said to be writing a life of Peter the Great, and to have come to England to study documents about that Tsar\u2019s residence here as a ship carpenter. But people generally suspected he was a Nihilist agent, and it was well known that the Russian Embassy did not approve of him being in London. Lord Arthur believed Rouvaloff was exactly the sort of person he needed. One morning, he drove to his lodgings in Bloomsbury to ask for advice and help.\\n\\n\u201cSo you are taking up politics seriously?\u201d Count Rouvaloff asked, after Lord Arthur explained what he wanted.\\n\\nLord Arthur hated any kind of swagger, so he had to admit that he had no interest at all in social questions. He only wanted the explosive machine for a private family matter, something that involved no one else but himself.\"}"
