          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"df9bbd6a4e6aa468ea731889\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the morning, she was found dead in her bed, apparently having suffered no pain. Sir Mathew Reid had been sent for at once, but, of course, there was nothing to be done, and she was to be buried on the 22nd at Beauchamp Chalcote. A few days before she died, she had made her will and left Lord Arthur her little house in Curzon Street, along with all her furniture, personal belongings, and pictures\u2014except for her collection of miniatures, which was to go to her sister, Lady Margaret Rufford, and her amethyst necklace, which Sybil Merton was to have. The property wasn\u2019t worth much, but Mr. Mansfield, the solicitor, was extremely anxious for Lord Arthur to return as soon as possible, if he could, because there were many bills to be paid, and Lady Clementina had never kept any regular accounts.\\n\\nLord Arthur was deeply moved by Lady Clementina\u2019s kind remembrance of him, and he felt that Mr. Podgers had a great deal to answer for. His love for Sybil, however, dominated every other feeling, and the knowledge that he had done his duty gave him peace and comfort. When he arrived at Charing Cross, he felt perfectly happy.\\n\\nThe Mertons received him very kindly. Sybil made him promise that nothing would ever come between them again, and the marriage was set for the 7th of June. Life seemed bright and beautiful to him once more, and all his old joy returned.\\n\\nOne day, though, as he was going through the house in Curzon Street with Lady Clementina\u2019s solicitor and Sybil herself, burning packages of faded letters and emptying drawers of odd rubbish, the young girl suddenly gave a little cry of delight.\\n\\n\u201cWhat have you found, Sybil?\u201d Lord Arthur asked, looking up from his work and smiling.\\n\\n\u201cThis lovely little silver bonbonni\u00e8re, Arthur. Isn\u2019t it quaint and Dutch? Please\u2014give it to me! I know amethysts won\u2019t suit me until I\u2019m over eighty.\u201d\\n\\nIt was the box that had held the aconitine.\\n\\nLord Arthur started, and a faint blush rose into his cheek. He had almost entirely forgotten what he had done, and it struck him as a strange coincidence that Sybil\u2014who, for her sake, he had endured all that terrible anxiety\u2014should be the first to remind him of it.\\n\\n\u201cOf course you can have it, Sybil. I gave it to poor Lady Clem myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, thank you, Arthur; and may I have the bonbon too? I had no idea Lady Clementina liked sweets. I thought she was far too intellectual.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur went deadly pale, and a horrible thought crossed his mind.\"}"
