          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cd538e03bbf446386c2f0fee\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The oaks about us were the haunt of a tribe of nightingales\u2014there am I now; Idris, in the very bloom of youth, stands at my side\u2014only remember, I am but twenty-two, and seventeen summers have scarcely gone by since my heart\u2019s beloved. The river, swollen by autumn rains, has overflowed and drowned the low grounds; and Adrian, in his chosen boat, is engaged in the perilous sport of reaching for the highest bough from a submerged oak. Tell me, Adrian\u2014are you weary of life, that you court danger so?\u2014\\n\\nHe has won his prize, and he steers his boat through the flood; our eyes followed him with dread, yet the current bore him away from us. He was compelled to land far lower down, and make a long circuit before he could come back to us. \u201cHe is safe!\u201d Idris called, as he sprang ashore, lifting the bough above his head as proof of success; \u201cwe\u2019ll wait for him here.\u201d\\n\\nWe were alone together; the sun had gone down; the nightingales began their song; the evening star shone clearly in the flood of light, still bright in the west. My angelic girl\u2019s blue eyes were fixed on that gentle emblem of herself: \u201cHow the light seems to throb,\u201d she said, \u201cwhich is, as it were, the star\u2019s own life. Its shifting brightness looks to me like a sign that its condition\u2014like ours upon earth\u2014is unsteady and changeful; it hesitates, I think, and it fears, and it loves.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo not look at the star, dear, kind friend,\u201d I cried; \u201cdo not read love in its trembling rays; do not gaze on distant worlds; do not speak of the mere fancy of a feeling. I have been silent for a long time\u2014so long, indeed, that I have longed even to sickness to speak to you, and to lay my soul, my life, my whole self at your feet. Look not on the star, dear love; let that eternal spark plead for me. Let it be my witness and my advocate, silent as it shines\u2014love is to me what light is to the star; and so long as that light is not blotted out by annihilation, so long shall I love you.\u201d\\n\\nThat moment\u2019s rapture must be veiled forever from the world\u2019s cold gaze. Yet even now I feel her graceful form pressing against my heart, heavy with all its fullness\u2014still do sight, and pulse, and breath grow sick and fail at the recollection of that first kiss. Slowly and in silence we went to meet Adrian, whom we heard drawing near.\\n\\nI begged Adrian to come back to me after he had seen his sister home. And that very evening, as we walked along the moonlit paths of the forest, I poured out my whole heart\u2014its agitation and its hope\u2014to my friend. For a moment he seemed troubled. \u201cI might have guessed this,\u201d he said; \u201cwhat conflict will now follow!\u201d\"}"
