          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c51329124ecf4e7717b52e8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut the moment I saw you become the object of another\u2019s love\u2014when I pictured that you might be cherished in some way other than as a holy emblem of loveliness and excellence; or that you might come to love someone else with a fiercer devotion than the one you once gave me\u2014then the fiend stirred within me. I drove away your lover, and from that hour I have known no peace. I have searched in vain for sleep or rest; my eyes would not close, and my blood has been in constant uproar. I woke into a new life, as if one who dies in hope might rise in Hell. I will not stain your imagination by recounting my struggles, my rage against myself, and my despair. Let a veil be drawn over the unspeakable sensations of a guilty father; the secrets of a heart so tortured must not be made common. Everything was turmoil, wrongdoing, remorse, and hatred\u2014yet still the tenderest love. And what first hardened me into the resolve to master my passion and restore her father to my child was the sight of your bitter, compassionate sorrows. That is what brought me here: I told myself that if I could rouse again in my heart the grief I had felt at the loss of your mother, and the many memories of her that had been sleeping for seventeen years, then all love for her child would finally die out. In a burst of heroic determination I resolved to go alone; to leave you, the life of my life, and not to see you again until I could do so without guilt. But it would not work. I judged my strength too highly, or my love too lightly. I would surely have died if you had not hurried to me. Oh, if only I had truly been extinguished!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd now, Mathilda, I must make you my final confession. I have been wretchedly mistaken in thinking I could conquer my love for you; I never can. The sight of this house, these fields and woods where my first love lived seems only to have increased it. In