{"request_id":"4450e4633ccb319a134bbfe5","rewritten_text":"But this thought, which once carried me at the start of my career, now only drags me deeper into the dust. All my speculations and hopes amount to nothing. Like the archangel who reached for omnipotence, I am bound in an eternal hell. My imagination was vivid, yet my powers of analysis and application were intense; by combining those qualities, I conceived the idea and carried out the creation of a man. Even now, I can hardly recall those moments without passion—my visions while the work was still incomplete. In my mind I walked through heaven: sometimes exulting in my abilities, sometimes burning with the thought of what their effects would be. From my infancy I was filled with high hopes and lofty ambition; but how far I have fallen! Oh, my friend—if you had known me as I once was, you would not recognize me in this state of degradation. Despondency rarely visited my heart. A great destiny seemed to be carrying me forward, until I fell—and never, never again to rise.

“Must I then lose this admirable being? I have longed for a friend. I have searched for someone who would understand me, sympathize with me, and love me. Look—on these desert seas I have found such a one, but I fear I have gained him only to learn his worth and then lose him. I want to bring him back to life, but he rejects the idea.

‘I thank you, Walton,’ he said, ‘for your kind intentions toward such a miserable wretch. But when you talk about new bonds and fresh affections, do you really think anything can replace those who are gone? Can any man be to me what Clerval was, or any woman what Elizabeth was? Even when the affections are not strongly stirred by any superior excellence, the companions of our childhood still hold a certain power over our minds—one that hardly any later friend can ever achieve. They know our earliest dispositions, which may be changed later, but are never truly erased. And they can judge our actions with far more certain conclusions about the integrity of our motives. A sister or a brother can never—unless such signs were shown early—suspect the other of fraud or dishonesty. Another friend, no matter how strongly we are attached, may still be viewed with suspicion, even against his will. But I had friends who I valued not only out of habit and association, but for their own merits. Wherever I am, the comforting voice of my Elizabeth and the conversation of Clerval will always be whispered in my ear. They are dead, and in such solitude only one feeling can persuade me to keep living. If I were engaged in some great undertaking or design—something full of extensive benefit to my fellow creatures—then I could live to carry it out.’"}

