{"request_id":"8f12230356c1b673eaad19dd","rewritten_text":"It was past two o’clock when, after a sudden turn, I found myself close to the lake by a cove where a small skiff was moored. It wasn’t far from our house, and I saw my father and my aunt walking on the lawn. I jumped into the boat, and, well used to such things, I pushed off from shore and put all my strength into rowing swiftly across. As I drew near—dressed in white, covered only by my tartan rachan, my hair streaming over my shoulders, and flying forward with even greater speed than it could be supposed I could give to my boat—my father had often told me that I looked more like a spirit than a human maid. I came up to the shore; my father held the boat; I leapt lightly out, and in a moment I was in his arms.

And now I began to live. Everything around me had changed, from a dull sameness into the brightest scene of joy and delight. The happiness I felt in my father’s company far exceeded my most hopeful expectations. We were together forever, and the subjects of our conversations never ran out. He had spent sixteen years away among nations nearly unknown to Europe; he had wandered through Persia, Arabia, and the north of India, and had moved among the natives’ settlements with a freedom granted to very few Europeans. His accounts of their manners, his anecdotes, and his descriptions of scenery carried us through delicious hours whenever we grew tired of talking about our plans for the future.

The voice of affection was so new to me that I hung on every word when he told me what he had felt about me during those long years of apparent forgetfulness. “At first,” he said, “I couldn’t bear to think of my poor little girl; but later, as grief faded and hope returned, I could only turn to her, and amid cities and deserts her little fairy form—just as I imagined it—kept flitting before me. The northern breeze, as it refreshed me, seemed sweeter and more balmy because it carried some of your spirit along with it. I often thought that I would come back at once and take you with me to some fertile island, where we would live in peace forever. But as I returned, my fervent hopes were dashed by so many fears; my impatience became, in the highest degree, painful. I dared not think that the sun might shine and the moon rise not on your living form, but on your grave. But no—it isn’t so. I have my Mathilda, my consolation, and my hope.””"}

