{"request_id":"0d164de970bde55f801c442a","rewritten_text":"The following day he did not go out at all, and for the most part stayed shut in his own room, ill with a fierce dread of dying, though strangely indifferent to life itself. The sense of being hunted—caught, followed, cornered—had taken hold of him. If the tapestry even quivered in the wind, he would start and shake. The dead leaves skittering against the leaded panes looked to him like his own squandered resolutions and frantic regrets. When he shut his eyes, the sailor’s face returned, peering through the mist-stained glass, and horror seemed again to press a hand to his heart.

Yet maybe it was only his imagination that had summoned vengeance from the night and set those grim shapes of punishment before him. Real life was disorder, but the mind could be frightfully consistent. It was imagination that made remorse trail behind sin. It was imagination that caused each crime to carry its misshapen offspring. In the plain world of facts, the wicked were not punished and the good were not rewarded. Strength met with success; weakness met with failure. That was all. Besides, if some stranger had been prowling about the house, the servants or the keepers would have noticed. If any tracks had been found in the flower-beds, the gardeners would have reported them. Yes—this was only fancy. Sibyl Vane’s brother had not returned to kill him. He had sailed away in his ship to founder in some winter sea; at least, from him he was safe. The man did not even know who he was, and could not know. The mask of youth had protected him.

Still, if it was nothing but illusion, how dreadful to think that conscience could conjure such terrifying phantoms, give them a visible shape, and set them moving before him! What kind of life would he have, day and night, with shadows of his wrongdoing peering at him from silent corners, taunting him from hidden places, whispering in his ear while he sat at table, and waking him with cold, prying fingers when he lay down to sleep? As the thought slid through his mind, he went pale with terror, and the air felt suddenly colder. Oh, in what wild madness he had killed his friend! How awful the memory of that moment was. He saw it all again; every hideous detail returned, each one worse than before. From the black cave of time, wrapped in scarlet, there rose the image of his sin."}

