          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bdcd1ff05ae5913d52e5622f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Soul and body, body and soul\u2014how strange and shadowy they were! In the soul there seemed to be something animal, while the body, in turn, could show flashes of spirituality. The senses might sharpen, and the intellect might sink. Who could say exactly where the pull of the flesh ended, or where the pull of the mind began? How thin and arbitrary were the definitions offered by ordinary psychologists! Still, how hard it was to choose between the competing claims of the different schools. Was the soul merely a shadow sitting within the house of sin? Or was the body truly contained in the soul, as Giordano Bruno had believed? The parting of spirit from matter was itself a mystery, and so too was their joining\u2014spirit with matter.\\n\\nHe found himself wondering whether psychology could ever be made so exact a science that every small spring of life would be laid bare to us. As things stood, we were forever misreading ourselves and seldom understanding others. Experience held no ethical weight. It was only the name people gave to their errors. Moralists, for the most part, treated it as a kind of warning; they claimed it had a certain moral force in shaping character, praising it as what instructs us what to pursue and what to shun. Yet experience had no driving power. It was as inactive a cause as conscience itself. What it showed, in the end, was only this: that our future would repeat our past, and that the sin we had once committed with loathing we would do again, and with pleasure.\\n\\nHe was convinced that the experimental method was the sole route to any scientific account of the passions; and certainly Dorian Gray was a case well suited to him, one that seemed to promise abundant and fruitful findings. His sudden, mad love for Sibyl Vane was a psychological event of no small consequence. There was no denying that curiosity played a large part in it\u2014curiosity and the hunger for fresh experiences\u2014but it was not a simple passion; it was intricate, tangled, and many-layered. Whatever portion of it came from the purely sensuous instinct of boyhood had been reshaped by the imagination\u2019s work, turned into something that appeared to the boy himself to be far from mere sense, and for that very reason became all the more perilous. It was the passions whose beginnings we manage to misjudge that ruled us most fiercely. Our feeblest motives were often the ones we knew best, the ones whose nature we were aware of. And it frequently happened that when we believed we were testing others, we were in truth testing ourselves.\\n\\nWhile Lord Henry sat there, lost in thought, there came a knock at the door; his valet entered and reminded him that it was time to dress for dinner.\"}"
