e, whether he would give them a lift as far as Isleworth.\\n\\n\u201cGet up,\u201d said the man. \u201cIs that your boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; he\u2019s my boy,\u201d answered Sikes, staring hard at Oliver, and slipping his hand, as if absent-minded, into the pocket where the pistol lay.\\n\\n\u201cYour father walks a bit too fast for you, doesn\u2019t he, my man?\u201d the driver asked, noticing Oliver was out of breath.\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d said Sikes, cutting in. \u201cHe\u2019s used to it. Here\u2014take hold of my hand, Ned. In with you!\u201d\\n\\nSo speaking to Oliver, he got him into the cart; and the driver, pointing to a heap of sacks, told him to lie down there and rest.\\n\\nAs they went by the different mile-stones, Oliver kept wondering, more and more, where his companion intended to take him. Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, Brentford\u2014each was passed; yet they went on as steadily as though they had only just started their journey.\"}"
