          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a5b47846d1f5912d238075db\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Almost a year had gone by since my father came back, and the seasons were nearly through with their yearly round. It was now the close of May; the woods wore their freshest green, and the fields were filled with the sweet scent of newly cut grass. I believed that the mild air and the fair face of Nature might soften my mind and help me to awaken in him gentler feelings\u2014peace, and love\u2014so that I might gain from him the confidence I had resolved to win.\\n\\nSo I chose the evening of one of those days for my attempt. I asked him to walk with me, and I led him to a nearby beech wood, where the light shade kept us from the slanting, dazzling rays of the setting sun. After we had been walking for some time in silence, I sat myself down with him on a mossy little mound. Even now it seems to me I can see the very place: many of the slender, smooth trunks were twined with ivy, whose glossy leaves of the deepest green set off the pale bark, and the light leaves of the young beech shoots that sprang from their parent stems. The short grass was mixed with moss, and here and there it was partly covered by the dead leaves of the last autumn, blown by the winds into small heaps. There were a few moss-grown stumps scattered about. The breeze stirred the leaves softly, and through the green canopy the bright blue sky showed itself. As evening drew on, the far trunks took on a reddish tint from the sun, the wind fell entirely still, and only a few birds passed overhead on their way to their evening rest.\\n\\nSo it was that we sat together here; and when you consider all that had happened\u2014everything terrible that had torn at our souls even in this quiet spot, which, but for strange passions, might have been a paradise\u2014you will not be surprised that I remember it as I looked upon it, thinking that its calm might give me calm, and stir me not only to courage but to persuasive words. I saw these things, and in a kind of vacant way I took note of them, while I tried to set my thoughts in proper order for what I meant to do. My heart beat quickly as I gathered myself to speak to him; I was resolved not to be turned away, yet I could not help trembling at the thought of what my words might do to him. At last, with great hesitation, I began:\\n\\n\u201cYour kindness to me, my dearest father, and the affection\u2014the excessive affection\u2014that you showed for me when you first returned, I hope will excuse me in your eyes if I venture to speak to you, though I do so with the tender affection of a daughter, yet also with the freedom of a friend and an equal.\u201d\"}"
