          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"03356bd89f987e5b6cfac5b5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut,\u201d said I, handing him the slip back, \u201cI\u2019m no clearer than I was before. If all the jewels of Golconda were waiting for me on the other side of this enigma, I\u2019m sure I still couldn\u2019t win them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet,\u201d Legrand replied, \u201cthe solution isn\u2019t nearly as hard as you might think from that first quick look at the characters. Those characters, as anyone could guess, make up a cipher\u2014that is, they carry a meaning; but, knowing Kidd, I couldn\u2019t imagine him capable of devising any of the more recondite cryptographs. I decided at once it was of a plain sort\u2014though, as it turned out, it was utterly beyond the sailor\u2019s rough understanding without the key.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you truly worked it out?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course. I\u2019ve solved others that were ten thousand times more intricate. Circumstances, and a certain bent of mind, have drawn me to such puzzles, and it\u2019s hard to doubt that human ingenuity can\u2019t, with the right application, unravel an enigma of the same kind. Once I had set down connected, readable characters, I hardly gave a thought to the mere labor of extracting their import.\\n\\n\u201cIn the present instance\u2014indeed, in every case of secret writing\u2014the first thing to consider is the language of the cipher; the methods of solution, particularly for the simpler ciphers, depend on\u2014and are shaped by\u2014the character of the idiom. In general, there\u2019s nothing for it but to experiment (guided by probabilities) with every language known to the person attempting the deciphering, until the true one is found. But here, with the cipher in front of me, the signature removed nearly all the difficulty. The pun on the word \u2018Kidd\u2019 is only really felt in English. Had it not been for that, I would have started my trials with Spanish and French\u2014the tongues in which a secret of this sort would most naturally have been written by a pirate of the Spanish Main. As it was, I took the cryptograph to be English.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ll notice there are no breaks between the words. If there had been divisions, the job would have been much easier. Then I would have begun with a comparison and analysis of the shorter words, and if a one-letter word had turned up\u2014as is quite likely, (a or I, for instance,)\u2014I should have regarded the solution as essentially certain. But since there were no divisions, my first move was to determine the letters that appeared most often, as well as those that were least frequent.\u201d\"}"
