          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d8f11829b539ab9e332cb51c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Could not be sure whether it was the voice of a man or of a woman. Could not make out what was said, but believed the language to be Spanish. The condition of the room and of the bodies was described by this witness in the same way we described them yesterday.\\n\\n\u201c_Henri Duval_, a neighbor, and by trade a silversmith, states that he was one of the group who first entered the house. He confirms Mus\u00e8t\u2019s testimony in general. As soon as they forced their way in, they shut the door again to keep out the crowd, which gathered very quickly, even though it was late. The shrill voice, this witness thinks, was that of an Italian. He was certain it was not French. He could not be sure whether it was a man\u2019s voice; it might have been a woman\u2019s. He was not familiar with the Italian language. He could not distinguish the words, but was convinced by the speaker\u2019s intonation that the person was Italian. He knew Madame L. and her daughter. He had spoken with both often. He was sure the shrill voice was not either of the deceased.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014_Odenheimer, restaurateur._ This witness offered his testimony. Not speaking French, he was questioned through an interpreter. He is a native of Amsterdam. He was passing the house at the time of the shrieks. They lasted for several minutes\u2014probably ten. They were long and loud\u2014very awful and distressing. He was one of those who entered the building. He confirmed the previous evidence in every respect except one. He was sure the shrill voice was that of a man\u2014of a Frenchman. He could not distinguish the words that were spoken. They were loud and quick\u2014uneven\u2014apparently spoken in fear as well as in anger. The voice was harsh\u2014not so much shrill as harsh. He could not call it a shrill voice. The rough voice said repeatedly \u2018_sacr\u00e9_,\u2019 \u2018_diable_,\u2019 and once \u2018_mon Dieu_.\u2019\\n\\n\u201c_Jules Mignaud_, banker, of the firm of Mignaud et Fils, Rue Deloraine. Is the elder Mignaud. Madame L\u2019Espanaye had some property. She opened an account with his banking house in the spring of that year\u2014(eight years earlier). She made frequent deposits in small amounts. She did not write checks for anything until the third day before her death, when she withdrew in person the sum of 4000 francs. This money was paid in gold, and a clerk went home with it.\\n\\n\u201c_Adolphe Le Bon_, clerk to Mignaud et Fils, states that on the day in question, around noon, he accompanied Madame L\u2019Espanaye to her residence with the 4000 francs, putting it into two bags. When the door was opened, Mademoiselle L. appeared and took one of the bags from his hands, while the old lady took the other from him.\u201d\"}"
