          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bab0e0d84f6057a6b1950dd8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The group was arranged on two benches next to each other. Anne sat among those in the front, and Mr. Elliot had managed it so well, with help from his friend Colonel Wallis, that he ended up with a seat beside her. Miss Elliot, surrounded by her cousins and the main focus of Colonel Wallis\u2019s attention, was perfectly content.\\n\\nAnne\u2019s mind was in the best possible mood for the evening\u2019s entertainment. It kept her busy in just the right way: she could enjoy the tender parts, the lively ones, the scientific explanations, and even the long, tiring sections. She had never liked a concert more\u2014at least during the first act. Near the end of it, during the break after an Italian song, she explained the words to Mr. Elliot. They had the concert program between them.\\n\\n\u201cThis,\u201d she said, \u201cis almost the meaning of the words\u2014though I should say the sense, not that I can truly explain it. You can\u2019t really talk about the meaning of an Italian love-song, but this is as close as I can get. I don\u2019t pretend to understand the language. I\u2019m a very poor Italian scholar.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, I see it. I can tell you know nothing about it. You only have enough knowledge of the language to translate, at once, these Italian lines that are turned around, rearranged, and shortened, into clear, easy, elegant English. You don\u2019t need to say anything more about your ignorance. Here is full proof.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI won\u2019t argue with such kindness,\u201d Anne replied, \u201cbut I would be sorry to be tested by a real expert.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI haven\u2019t had the pleasure of visiting Camden Place for so long,\u201d Mr. Elliot answered, \u201cwithout learning something about Miss Anne Elliot. And I truly believe she is too modest to notice, in the world at large, even half of her accomplishments\u2014and too accomplished for modesty to come