          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4986ef2be369e59b4ef63886\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But in that bitter tirade about Chantilly, which appeared in yesterday\u2019s \u201cMus\u00e9e,\u201d the satirist, making some disgraceful allusions to the cobbler\u2019s change of name after taking up the buskin, quoted a Latin line we have often discussed. I mean the line\\n\\nPerdidit antiquum litera prima sonum.\\n\\n\u201cI told you that this was in reference to Orion, which had once been written Urion; and, from certain clues tied to that explanation, I knew you couldn\u2019t have forgotten it. It was clear, then, that you wouldn\u2019t fail to connect the two ideas of Orion and Chantilly. The fact that you did combine them, I saw in the expression of the smile that crossed your lips. You were thinking of the poor cobbler\u2019s immolation. Up to that point, you had been stooping in your walk; but then I saw you straighten to your full height. I was sure, at that moment, that you were reflecting on Chantilly\u2019s diminutive stature. And that\u2019s when I interrupted your thoughts to say that, since he was in fact a very little man\u2014that Chantilly\u2014he\u2019d do better at the Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s.\u201d\\n\\nNot long after that, we were looking over an evening edition of the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux\u201d when the following paragraphs caught our attention.\\n\\n\u201cExtraordinary Murders.\u2014This morning, at about three o\u2019clock, the residents of the Quartier St. Roch were woken from sleep by a series of terrifying shrieks, apparently coming from the fourth story of a house on the Rue Morgue, known to be occupied solely by Madame L\u2019Espanaye and her daughter, Mademoiselle Camille L\u2019Espanaye. After some delay\u2014caused by a fruitless attempt to gain entry in the usual way\u2014the gate was forced open with a crowbar, and eight or ten of the neighbors entered with two gendarmes. By then the cries had stopped; but as the group rushed up the first flight of stairs, two or more rough voices, arguing angrily, could be heard, and they seemed to come from the upper part of the house. When the second landing was reached, those sounds had also ceased, and everything was perfectly quiet. The party fanned out and hurried from room to room. When they arrived at a large back chamber on the fourth story (the door of which, found locked with the key inside, was forced open), a scene presented itself that struck everyone there with horror no less than with astonishment.\\n\\n\u201cThe apartment was in the wildest disorder\u2014the furniture broken and thrown about in every direction. There was only one bedstead; and from it the bed had been removed and thrown into the middle of the floor. On a chair lay a razor, smeared with blood.\u201d\"}"
