          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b09b50320db65f545fd6647d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No note of condolence had been sent to Ireland. The omission fell back upon the person who had sinned; for when poor Lady Elliot died, no letter of condolence was received at Kellynch, and so there was far too much reason to fear that the Dalrymples regarded the relationship as ended. The point, then, was how to set this troublesome matter right and be taken up again as cousins; and it was a point which, in a more sensible way, neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot considered trivial. \u201cFamily ties are always worth keeping, and good society is always worth seeking. Lady Dalrymple has taken a house, for three months, in Laura Place, and will be living handsomely. She was at Bath the year before, and Lady Russell had heard her spoken of as a charming woman. It is very desirable that the connexion should be renewed, if it can be done, without any breach of propriety on the part of the Elliots.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter, however, would manage matters in his own fashion, and at last wrote a very fine letter\u2014full of explanation, regret, and entreaty\u2014to his right honourable cousin. Neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot could find much to admire in it; but it served its purpose, and brought from the Dowager Viscountess only three lines of scrawl: \u201cShe was very much honoured, and should be happy in their acquaintance.\u201d The labour was at an end; the pleasant part began. They called in Laura Place; they received the cards of Dowager Viscountess Dalrymple and of the Honourable Miss Carteret, to be displayed wherever they might be most noticed; and \u201cOur cousins in Laura Place\u201d\u2014\u201cOur cousin, Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret\u201d\u2014was repeated to everyone.\\n\\nAnne felt mortified. Even if Lady Dalrymple and her daughter had been agreeable, she would still have been ashamed of the commotion they caused; but they were nothing at all. There was no superiority of manner, no real accomplishment, no understanding. Lady Dalrymple had earned the reputation of \u201ca charming woman\u201d merely because she had a smile and a civil answer for everyone. Miss Carteret, who had even less to say, was so plain and so awkward that she would never have been tolerated in Camden Place, had it not been for her birth.\\n\\nLady Russell admitted she had expected something better; yet \u201cit is an acquaintance worth having.\u201d And when Anne ventured to give her opinion of them to Mr Elliot, he agreed that they were nothing in themselves, but still insisted that, as a family connexion, as good company, and as people who would gather good company about them, they had their value. Anne smiled and said,\\n\\n\u201cMy notion of good company, Mr Elliot, is the company of clever, well-informed people, who have a great deal to talk about; that is what I call good company.\u201d\"}"
