{"request_id":"4228e331a38527cf90206b26","rewritten_text":"All the young people were eager to see Lyme. Captain Wentworth spoke of going there again himself; it was only seventeen miles from Uppercross, and though it was November, the weather was by no means unpleasant. In short, Louisa—most eager of the eager—having resolved to go, and, besides the satisfaction of doing as she pleased, now encouraged by the thought of having deservedly kept to her own plan, pressed down every wish of her father and mother that they should wait till summer. To Lyme they were to go: Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth.

At first the plan had been to set out in the morning and come back the same night; but Mr Musgrove would not agree to it, chiefly for the sake of his horses. When the matter was considered more sensibly, it was clear that a day in the middle of November would not allow much time for viewing a new place, after allowing seven hours for the journey there and back, as the character of the country demanded. They were therefore to spend the night there, and were not to be expected back until the next day’s dinner. This was felt to be a notable improvement; and although they all met at the Great House for an early breakfast and started with great punctuality, it was so far past noon before the two carriages—Mr Musgrove’s, with the four ladies, and Charles’s curricle, in which he drove Captain Wentworth—were descending the long hill into Lyme, and then entering the town’s still steeper street, that it was plain they could have little more than time to look about them before the light and warmth of the day were gone.

Once accommodations were secured and dinner ordered at one of the inns, the next step was unquestionably to walk straight down to the sea. They had arrived too late in the year for any amusement or variety that Lyme, as a public resort, might otherwise provide. The rooms were shut up, most of the lodgers had gone, and scarcely any families remained except those of the residents. Since there is nothing particularly to admire in the buildings themselves, it is the town’s remarkable situation that draws the stranger’s attention: the principal street almost hurrying into the water, the walk to the Cobb, winding along the pleasant little bay, which in season is lively with bathing machines and company; the Cobb itself, with its old wonders and new improvements; and the very beautiful line of cliffs stretching eastward from the town. Indeed, it would take a very odd sort of stranger not to find charms in the immediate neighbourhood of Lyme, and not to feel inclined to know it better."}

