ions fill my breast, and even the sight of you brings back thoughts of him. It is for all these reasons that I am moved to deal with you gently now\u2014yes, Edward Leeford\u2014even now\u2014and to blush for the unworthiness of you who bear the name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat has the name to do with it?\u201d asked the other, after weighing the words in half-silence and half-dogged astonishment. \u201cWhat is the name to me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow. \u201cNothing to you. But it was hers; and even after all this time it brings back to me, an old man, the glow and quickening I once felt\u2014only to have it spoken again by a stranger. I am truly glad you have changed it\u2014very\u2014very glad.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is all very fine,\u201d said Monks (to keep up his assumed character) after a long pause, in which he jerked himself back and forth in sullen defiance, while Mr. Brownlow sat with his face shaded by his hand.\"}"
