          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"32e5d44f10562599acbe328d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You see, I\u2019d tucked my handkerchief into my helmet, along with a few other odds and ends; but it was the sort of helmet you can\u2019t take off on your own. I hadn\u2019t thought of that when I put it there, and honestly I didn\u2019t even know. I figured it would be especially handy sitting right there. But now the idea of it being so close and so convenient\u2014yet completely out of reach\u2014made everything worse and harder to stand. Yes, the thing you can\u2019t get is the thing you want most, that\u2019s what everyone notices. Well, it took my attention off everything else, cleared it right away, and fixed it in my helmet; mile after mile it stayed there, with me imagining the handkerchief, picturing it. And it was bitter and maddening to have the salt sweat keep running into my eyes while I couldn\u2019t reach it. It sounds small on paper, but it wasn\u2019t small at all; it was the most genuine sort of misery. I wouldn\u2019t say it if it weren\u2019t true. \\n\\nI decided that next time I\u2019d carry a reticule instead, whatever people might think. Sure, those iron fellows of the Round Table would probably call it scandalous and maybe raise Sheol about it, but for me\u2014comfort first, and style after. So we jogged on, and now and then we hit a patch of dust; it would billow up in clouds, get into my nose, and make me sneeze and weep. And of course I said things I oughtn\u2019t to have said\u2014I won\u2019t pretend otherwise. I\u2019m not any better than the rest.\\n\\nWe couldn\u2019t seem to run into anybody in this lonesome Britain, not even an ogre; and in the mood I was in then, it was a mercy for the ogre\u2014that is, an ogre with a handkerchief. Most knights would\u2019ve been thinking only about getting his armor back, but I managed to take his bandanna; he could keep his gear, for all of me.\\n\\nMeanwhile, it was getting hotter and hotter in there. The sun kept beating down, warming the iron more and more all the time. When you\u2019re hot like that, every little thing starts to irritate you. When I trotted, I rattled like a crate of dishes, and that got on my nerves; and I couldn\u2019t stand the shield slatting and banging, now against my breast, now around my back. If I dropped into a walk, my joints creaked and screeched in that tiresome way a wheelbarrow does, and since we weren\u2019t making any breeze at that pace, I felt like I\u2019d be cooked in that stove. And besides, the quieter I went, the more the iron seemed to settle on me, until it felt like more and more tons, pressing down on me every minute.\"}"
