          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f77feb623241bab404d041e1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAre you talking about\u2014Mr. Knightley?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo be sure I am. I could never imagine anyone else\u2014and I thought you would know that. When we talked about him, it was as clear as I could make it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot quite,\u201d Emma answered, keeping her voice steady. \u201cEven though you said all that, it seemed to me you were talking about a different person. I could almost say you had actually named Mr. Frank Churchill. I\u2019m sure you were speaking about the help Mr. Frank Churchill gave you when he protected you from the gipsies.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Miss Woodhouse, how you forget!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Harriet, I remember perfectly what I said. I told you I didn\u2019t doubt your feelings. And since he had done you that service, it was only natural. You agreed with me, and you spoke about it very warmly, saying how grateful you were for what he did. You even mentioned what you felt when he came forward to rescue you. The whole thing is still very strong in my memory.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, dear,\u201d Harriet said, \u201cnow I remember what you mean. But at the time, I was thinking of something else. It wasn\u2019t the gipsies. It wasn\u2019t Mr. Frank Churchill. No!\u201d She lifted her voice a little. \u201cI was thinking of something far more important\u2014Mr. Knightley coming to ask me to dance, when Mr. Elton wouldn\u2019t dance with me, and when there was no other partner in the room. That was the kind act. That was the noble, generous help. It was that service that made me start to feel how much better he was than anyone else on earth.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood God!\u201d Emma cried. \u201cThis is the most unfortunate\u2014most dreadful\u2014mistake. What can be done?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou wouldn\u2019t have encouraged me, then, if you had understood what I meant. At least, though, I can\u2019t be any worse off than I would have been if the other