          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"64833add98a4790a5086f646\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once, however, the lieutenant asked why he had come so far across the ice in such a strange vehicle.\\n\\nHis face instantly took on the look of the deepest gloom, and he replied, \u201cTo seek one who fled from me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did the man you were pursuing travel in the same way?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen I suppose we\u2019ve seen him. The day before we picked you up, we saw some dogs drawing a sledge, with a man in it, across the ice.\u201d\\n\\nThis stirred the stranger\u2019s attention, and he asked a whole series of questions about the route the d\u00e6mon, as he called him, had taken. Soon after, when he was alone with me, he said, \u201cI\u2019ve no doubt excited your curiosity, as well as that of these good people; but you\u2019re too considerate to ask questions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course; it would be very impertinent and inhuman of me to trouble you with any curiosity of my own.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet you rescued me from a strange and dangerous situation; you\u2019ve kindly brought me back to life.\u201d\\n\\nSoon after, he asked whether I thought the breaking up of the ice had destroyed the other sledge. I told him I couldn\u2019t say for certain, because the ice hadn\u2019t broken until near midnight, and the traveler might have reached a place of safety before then; but I couldn\u2019t judge.\\n\\nFrom that time on, a new spirit of life seemed to animate the stranger\u2019s decaying body. He showed the greatest eagerness to be on deck to watch for the sledge that had appeared before; but I persuaded him to stay in the cabin, because he\u2019s far too weak to endure the harshness of the air. I promised that someone would keep watch for him and give him immediate notice if any new object came into view.\\n\\nSuch is my journal of what has happened in this strange occurrence up to the present day. The stranger has gradually improved in health, but he is very quiet and seems uneasy whenever anyone other than myself enters his cabin. Still, his manners are so agreeable and gentle that the sailors are all interested in him, even though they\u2019ve had very little chance to talk with him. As for me, I\u2019ve begun to love him like a brother, and his constant, deep grief fills me with sympathy and compassion. He must have been a noble creature in better days, since even now, in wreck, he remains so attractive and amiable.\\n\\nI said in one of my letters, my dear Margaret, that I would find no friend on the wide ocean; yet I have found a man who, before his spirit was broken by misery, I would have been happy to have as the brother of my heart.\u201d\"}"
