          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7f08ca2cfe2f516500cecc92\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cJust listen, then! You!\u201d Monks replied. \u201cWhen his father fell ill in Rome, he was joined there by his wife\u2014my mother\u2014after a long separation from him. She came from Paris and brought me along, to attend to his affairs, as far as I can tell; for she cared little for him, and he for her. He knew nothing of us, his senses having failed, and he slept on until the next day, when he died. Among the papers in his desk there were two, both dated the night his illness first began, and both addressed to yourself\u201d; he turned to Mr. Brownlow; \u201cand each enclosed in a few brief lines to you, with a note on the outside saying the package was not to be sent on until after he was dead. One was a letter to this girl, Agnes; the other was a will.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat about the letter?\u201d asked Mr. Brownlow.\\n\\n\u201cThe letter?\u2014It was a page crossed and recrossed, again and again, with a penitent confession, and prayers to God for her help. He had kept up a story to the girl\u2014that some secret matter, to be explained one day, had stopped him from marrying her at that time. So she went on, trusting him patiently, until she trusted too far and lost what no one could ever restore. At that point she was only a few months from her confinement. He told her everything he had intended, to spare her shame if he had lived, and if he died, he begged her not to curse his memory, nor to believe that the consequences of their sin would fall on her or on their young child; for the guilt was all his. He reminded her of the day he had given her the little locket and the ring, with her Christian name engraved upon it, and left a blank for the name he hoped one day to put there\u2014prayed her still to keep it and wear it close to her heart, as she had done before\u2014and then he kept going, wildly, repeating the same words over and over, as though he had lost his mind. I think he had.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow, as Oliver\u2019s tears came fast.\\n\\nMonks said nothing.\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d Mr. Brownlow went on, speaking for him, \u201cwas in the same tone as the letter. He spoke of the miseries his wife had brought him; of the stubborn bent, vice, malice, and early bad passions in you, his only son, whom he had trained to hate him. And he left you, and your mother, each an annuity of eight hundred pounds. He divided the rest of his property into two equal parts\u2014one for Agnes Fleming, and the other for their child, if it should be born alive and live to come of age.\u201d}\"}"
