          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"90541193a15c90095b3465f9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He rose and left the room with my nurse, and after a moment my father entered.\\n\\nAt that instant nothing could have brought me more joy than seeing my father. I reached out to him and cried,\\n\\n\u201cThen you are safe\u2014and Elizabeth\u2014and Ernest?\u201d\\n\\nMy father soothed me with assurances of their welfare, and tried\u2014by dwelling on matters so dear to my heart\u2014to lift my sinking spirits; yet he soon discovered that a prison cannot be a place of cheer. \u201cWhat sort of dwelling is this for you, my son?\u201d he said, casting a mournful look at the barred windows and the miserable state of the room. \u201cYou came to find happiness, but some fatality seems to follow you. And poor Clerval\u2014\u201d\\n\\nThe name of my unfortunate, murdered friend was more than my frail condition could bear; I burst into tears.\\n\\n\u201cAlas! Yes, my father,\u201d I answered; \u201csome destiny of the most dreadful kind hangs over me, and I must live to carry it out\u2014or else I surely should have died upon Henry\u2019s coffin.\u201d\\n\\nWe were not permitted to talk for long, because my health was so precarious that every precaution was needed to keep me tranquil. Mr. Kirwin came in and insisted that my strength must not be worn down by too much effort. Still, my father\u2019s presence felt to me like that of a kindly angel, and my health returned to me little by little.\\n\\nAs the sickness left me, I sank into a gloomy, black melancholy that nothing could dispel. The sight of Clerval remained before my eyes, ghastly and murdered. More than once, the agitation stirred up by these thoughts made my friends fear a dangerous relapse. Alas! Why did they spare such a miserable and hated life for me? It must be so that I might fulfil my destiny, which is now drawing to its end. Soon\u2014oh, very soon\u2014death will put an end to these throbbings and free me from the heavy weight of anguish that drags me to the dust; and, in carrying out the sentence of justice, I shall also sink into rest. At that time the approach of death still seemed far off, though the wish was always present to my mind. Often I sat for hours, perfectly still and silent, longing for some great revolution that would bury both me and my destroyer in its ruins.\\n\\nThe time of the assizes drew near. I had already been three months in prison, and though I was still weak and in constant danger of relapsing, I was required to travel nearly a hundred miles to the country town where the court was to be held. Mr. Kirwin took it upon himself to see to every detail\u2014collecting witnesses and arranging my defence.\u201d}\"}"
