          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5588d1f37424c181d8a0655c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without the usual tools and resources, the analyst throws himself into the mindset of his opponent. He tries to understand how the other person thinks, and he often sees at a glance the only ways\u2014sometimes in a way that is almost absurdly simple\u2014by which he can trick someone into making a mistake or push them into miscalculating.\\n\\nWhist has long been known for its effect on what people call the power to calculate. Even very intelligent men have been known to take real pleasure in it, while dismissing chess as too silly. There is probably nothing else that challenges the ability to analyze in quite the same way. The best chess-player in Christendom may be little more than the best chess-player, but skill at whist suggests a talent for succeeding in other, more important situations where one mind must deal with another. When I say skill, I mean the kind of mastery that includes understanding all the sources of real advantage. Those sources are many and varied, and they often hide in parts of thought that most people cannot reach. Paying close attention helps you remember clearly. So a player who concentrates well at chess will usually do well at whist, and the rules of Hoyle\u2014based mainly on the game\u2019s basic mechanics\u2014are easy enough to understand.\\n\\nSo people often think that having a good memory and playing \u201cby the book\u201d is all that matters. But the analyst\u2019s real skill shows up in areas beyond the simple rules. He makes, in silence, a whole series of observations and guesses. His companions may do the same, but the difference in what they learn does not come as much from whether their guesses are correct as from how good their observations are. The key is knowing what to watch.\\n\\nOur player does not limit himself at all. Even though the game is the focus, he does not throw away deductions from things outside the game. He studies his partner\u2019s face, comparing it carefully with each opponent\u2019s. He looks at how the cards are sorted in each hand, sometimes counting trump card by card and honors by honors, based on the quick looks the players give to those cards. As the play goes on, he notices every change in expression, collecting information from differences in how certain someone looks, how surprised they seem, whether they show triumph, or whether they show frustration. From the way a person gathers up a trick, he judges whether that person can take another trick in the same suit. He also figures out what is being played through feints, by the way it is laid down on the table.\\n\\nEven a casual or careless remark; an accidental dropping or turning of a card, along with the anxiety or carelessness about hiding it; the counting of tricks and the order they are arranged in\u2014these, along with embarrassment, hesitation, eagerness, or nervous fear, all give him signs of what is really going on, as if he can see it intuitively.\"}"
