          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ba551ae640af6fa487d0cb1b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI swear,\u201d he burst out, \u201cby the sun, and by the blue vault of heaven, and by the flame of love that consumes my heart: if you grant my request, then while they endure you shall never see me again. Go on to your home and begin your labours; I shall follow their course with unspeakable dread; and be assured that when you are prepared, I will come.\u201d\\n\\nHaving said this, he suddenly left me, alarmed\u2014perhaps\u2014at any shift in my feelings. I watched him make his way down the mountain with a swifter pace than an eagle\u2019s flight, and soon he was lost among the heaving folds of the sea of ice.\\n\\nHis account had filled the entire day, and the sun was already nearing the horizon when he took his leave. I knew I ought to hurry down toward the valley, for darkness would soon close around me; yet my spirit sank, and my steps dragged. The work of picking my way along the narrow mountain paths, and of planting my feet firmly as I went, held me back and troubled me, while I was still captive to the emotions that the events of the day had stirred. Long after night had set in, I reached the half-way resting-place and sat down beside the fountain. The stars appeared now and then as the clouds drifted aside; the dark pines rose before me, and here and there a shattered tree lay scattered on the ground. It was a spectacle of deep solemnity, and it stirred up strange thoughts within me. I wept bitterly, and with my hands clenched in anguish I cried, \u201cO stars and clouds and winds\u2014ye are all about to mock me. If you truly pity me, crush feeling and memory; let me be nothing. But if you do not, go\u2014go\u2014and leave me in darkness.\u201d\\n\\nThese were wild and wretched thoughts; yet I cannot tell you how the everlasting flicker of the stars pressed upon me, nor how I listened to every gust of wind as though it were a dull, hideous siroc, coming to devour me.\\n\\nMorning broke before I reached the village of Chamounix. I took no rest, but returned at once to Geneva. Even within my own breast I could not put my sensations into words; they bore down on me with the weight of a mountain, and their excess overwhelmed and extinguished my agony. So I went back home, and, entering the house, presented myself to the family. My gaunt and wild appearance struck them with intense alarm, yet I answered no question and scarcely spoke. I felt as though I were under a curse\u2014as if I had no claim to their sympathy\u2014as if I could never again enjoy their company.\u201d\"}"
