          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0d19459f7e1d84e4c43e46a9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI have no nerves for that sort of thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut could you really be comfortable, spending the whole evening away from the poor boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; you see his papa can, so why shouldn\u2019t I? Jemima is so careful, and she could send us word every hour about how he is. I truly think Charles might as well have told his father that we would all come. I\u2019m not more alarmed about little Charles now than he is. I was dreadfully frightened yesterday, but today is quite different.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, if you don\u2019t think it too late to give notice for yourself, suppose you were to go too, along with your husband. Leave little Charles to my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove cannot think it wrong while I remain with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you in earnest?\u201d Mary cried, her eyes brightening. \u201cDear me! That\u2019s a capital idea\u2014an excellent one, indeed. I may as well go as not, for what good am I at home? It only worries me. You, who have not a mother\u2019s feelings, are far the more proper person. You can make little Charles do anything; he always obeys you at a word. It will be much better than leaving him only with Jemima. Oh, I shall certainly go; I\u2019m sure I ought to, if I can, quite as much as Charles, for they want me excessively to be acquainted with Captain Wentworth, and I don\u2019t mind being left alone. What a splendid thought of yours, Anne. I\u2019ll go and tell Charles, and get ready at once. You can send for us at a moment\u2019s notice if anything happens; but I dare say there will be nothing to alarm you. I shouldn\u2019t go, you may be sure, if I didn\u2019t feel quite at ease about my dear child.\u201d\\n\\nThe next moment she was tapping at her husband\u2019s dressing-room door, and as Anne followed her upstairs, she caught the whole conversation. It began with Mary speaking in a tone of great triumph\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI mean to go with you, Charles, for I\u2019m no more use at home than you are. If I were to shut myself up forever with the child, I couldn\u2019t persuade him to do anything he didn\u2019t like. Anne will stay; Anne undertakes to stay at home and take care of him. It\u2019s Anne\u2019s own proposal, so I shall go with you, and it will be a great deal better, because I haven\u2019t dined at the other house since Tuesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is very kind of Anne,\u201d her husband replied. \u201cI should be very glad to have you go; but it seems rather hard that she should be left at home by herself, to nurse our sick child.\u201d\"}"
