          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ff9c5722ee3d5c9e91cadc77\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, that\u2019s nothing at all! It gives me pleasure. I don\u2019t toil for money; I live wholly for my art.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur set down \u00a34, 2s. 6d. on the table, thanked the little German for his courtesy, and having managed to refuse an invitation to join some Anarchists for a meat-tea the next Saturday, he left the house and headed for the Park.\\n\\nOver the next two days he was in a state of the utmost agitation, and on Friday, just after twelve, he drove down to the Buckingham to wait for word. All afternoon the heavy, unmoving hall-porter kept pinning up telegrams from different parts of the country\u2014horse-race results, the outcomes of divorce cases, the state of the weather, and the like\u2014while the tape continued to tick out tedious particulars about an all-night sitting in the House of Commons and a small scare on the Stock Exchange. At four o\u2019clock the evening papers arrived, and Lord Arthur vanished into the library with the _Pall Mall_, the _St. James\u2019s_, the _Globe_, and the _Echo_, to Colonel Goodchild\u2019s great annoyance. Colonel Goodchild wanted to read the report of a speech he had given that morning at the Mansion House, on South African Missions and the wisdom of having black Bishops in every province, and, for reasons of his own, he had a marked dislike for the _Evening News_. None of the papers, though, mentioned Chichester in the slightest way, and Lord Arthur felt the attempt must have failed. It was a crushing disappointment, and for a while he was thoroughly shaken.\\n\\nThe next day he went to see Herr Winckelkopf, who was full of elaborate apologies and proposed to replace the clock for free, or else supply him with a case of nitro-glycerine bombs at cost. Lord Arthur, however, had lost faith in explosives, and Herr Winckelkopf himself admitted that everything was so adulterated these days that even dynamite could hardly be obtained in a clean, untainted state. Still, the little German\u2014while conceding that something must have gone wrong with the mechanism\u2014had not entirely given up hope that the clock might yet go off. He cited, as an example, a barometer he had once sent to the military Governor at Odessa: although it was meant to explode in ten days, it had not done so until nearly three months later. It was true that when it finally did go, it only managed to blow a housemaid to pieces, since the Governor had left town six weeks before; but it at least suggested that dynamite, as a destructive force, could be a formidable thing when kept under the control of machinery\u2014if somewhat unreliable about its timing.\"}"
