ich. This was treasure-hunting with the best possible luck\u2014no annoying uncertainty about where to dig. They kept nudging one another, over and over\u2014meaningful nudges, easy to read, because they were simply saying, \u201cOh, but ain\u2019t you glad we\u2019re here now!\u201d\\n\\nJoe\u2019s knife struck something.\\n\\n\u201cHello!\u201d he said.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is it?\u201d asked his comrade.\\n\\n\u201cHalf-rotten plank\u2014no, it\u2019s a box, I reckon. Here\u2014give me a hand and we\u2019ll see what it\u2019s for. Never mind, I\u2019ve already broken through.\u201d\\n\\nHe reached in and drew it out\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMan, it\u2019s money!\u201d\\n\\nThe two men looked over the handful of coins. They were gold. The boys above were just as stirred up as the men, and just as pleased.\\n\\nJoe\u2019s comrade said, \u201cWe\u2019ll be done with this quick. There\u2019s an old rusty pick out among the weeds in the corner on the other side of the fireplace\u2014I saw it a minute ago.\u201d\"}"
