          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"56642c061002e352513c9d60\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma Woodhouse\u2014good-looking, quick-witted, and well-off\u2014had a comfortable home and a cheerful temper, and it seemed as though she gathered together several of the finest advantages life can offer; she had lived nearly twenty-one years in the world with scarcely anything to trouble or annoy her.\\n\\nShe was the younger of two daughters of a father whose affection was most warm and whose indulgence knew few bounds; and, as a result of her sister\u2019s marriage, she had been mistress of his house from a very early age. Her mother had been gone so long that Emma could keep only a faint recollection of her caresses; her place was filled by an excellent woman, acting as governess, who fell little short of a mother in her tenderness.\\n\\nFor sixteen years Miss Taylor had been in Mr. Woodhouse\u2019s family, less in the strict character of governess than as a friend\u2014fond of both daughters, and especially attached to Emma. Between them, it was more like the closeness of sisters than anything else. Even before Miss Taylor had ceased to hold the mere title of governess, her gentle disposition had scarcely ever allowed her to lay down any real restraint; and with the shadow of authority long since gone, they had been living together as friend with friend, in the most mutual attachment, while Emma did exactly what she pleased\u2014valuing Miss Taylor\u2019s judgment highly, yet guided chiefly by her own.\\n\\nIndeed, the true drawbacks of Emma\u2019s situation were having rather too much her own way, and a tendency to think rather too well of herself; these were the disadvantages that might, one day, mingle with her many enjoyments. But the danger was at present so little perceived that it could not, by any means, take the rank of a misfortune with her.\\n\\nSorrow came\u2014only a mild sorrow, and by no means accompanied by any unpleasant sense of wrong.\u2014Miss Taylor married. It was Miss Taylor\u2019s loss that first brought real grief. On the very day of this beloved friend\u2019s wedding, Emma first found herself sitting in mournful thought about what might come next. When the ceremony was over and the wedding party had gone, her father and she were left to dine together, with no third person to brighten a long evening. After dinner, her father, as usual, settled himself to sleep; and then Emma had nothing to do but sit and consider what she had lost.\\n\\nEverything promised happiness for her friend. Mr. Weston was a man of unexceptionable character, with an easy fortune, an age well suited, and agreeable manners; and there was even some comfort in reflecting on the self-denying, generous friendship with which she had always wished for and encouraged the match. Still, it was a black morning\u2019s work for Emma. The absence of Miss Taylor would be felt every hour of every day. She went back over past kindnesses\u2014the kindness and affection of sixteen years\u2014how she had taught and played with her from the time she was five\u2014how she had devoted all her powers to attach and amuse her in health\u2014and how she had nursed her through the various illnesses of childhood.\"}"
