          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"00fcb35d9292cba1b90ecad3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI need to step out for a moment to say this, Sir Walter,\u201d Mr Shepherd began one morning at Kellynch Hall, as he set down the newspaper. \u201cRight now is working very much in our favour. This peace will send all our rich naval officers back to land. They will all be looking for a home. It could not be a better time, Sir Walter, to choose tenants\u2014proper, responsible ones. Many fortunes have been made during the war. If a rich admiral should come our way, Sir Walter\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe would be a very lucky man, Shepherd,\u201d Sir Walter answered. \u201cThat\u2019s all I have to say about it. Kellynch Hall would be a prize indeed for him\u2014perhaps the greatest prize of all, after all the others he may have taken before. Well, Shepherd?\u201d\\n\\nMr Shepherd laughed, because he knew he had to, at the remark, and then went on\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI would also suggest, Sir Walter, that in business, gentlemen in the navy are worth dealing with. I know something about how they do business. I can honestly say they have very generous ideas, and they are as likely to make good tenants as any group you could meet. So, Sir Walter, what I want to propose is this: if rumours should get out about your plans\u2014which we have to consider possible, because it is so hard to keep one part of the world from watching and being curious about the other\u2014then consequences will have their cost. I, John Shepherd, could hide any family matters I wanted, because nobody would bother to look closely at me. But Sir Walter Elliot is watched, and it may be hard to avoid notice. That is why I venture to say that, even with all our care, it would not surprise me if some rumour of the truth did reach people. And if that happens, as I was going to say, since applications will certainly follow, I think any from our wealthy naval commanders would be especially worth your attention. I would also add that I can come over at any time within two hours, so you won\u2019t have the trouble of replying.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter only nodded. Soon after, though, as he rose and paced the room, he said with a touch of sarcasm\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI suppose there are few among the gentlemen of the navy who would not be surprised to find themselves in a house like this.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey would look around, no doubt, and be grateful for their good fortune,\u201d Mrs Clay said, because Mrs Clay was there. \u201cMy father brought me over, since nothing helps my health like a drive to Kellynch. But I agree with my father that a sailor might be a very desirable tenant.\u201d\"}"
