          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2ad71433e388c5b79ce81e09\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At last it was settled that we should keep house together while I remained in the city; and, since my affairs in the world were a little less cramped than his, I was allowed to cover the cost of renting and fitting out, in a manner that matched the rather fantastic gloom of our shared temper, a timeworn and hideous mansion\u2014one that had been left empty for years, abandoned to superstitions we never bothered to question, and leaning toward ruin in a secluded, desolate corner of the Faubourg St. Germain.\\n\\nIf the pattern of our life in that place had become known to the public, we would have been taken for madmen\u2014though, perhaps, for madmen of a harmless sort. Our withdrawal was complete. We admitted no visitors. In fact, even the situation of our retreat had been kept carefully from my former acquaintances; and it had been many years since Dupin had stopped either knowing or being known in Paris. We lived as if within ourselves alone.\\n\\nIt was a whim of fancy in my friend (what else can I call it?) to love the night for its own sake; and into this _bizarrerie_, as into all his other notions, I slipped quietly, surrendering myself to his wild caprices with a full and easy _abandon_. The sable divinity would not always remain with us in person, yet we could mimic her presence. At the first dawn we shut every troublesome shutter of the old house, and set a couple of tapers burning\u2014heavily perfumed, and throwing off nothing but the most ghastly, thin rays. With their help we then occupied our minds in dreams: reading, writing, or talking, until the clock warned us that the true Darkness was at hand. Then we went out together into the streets, arm in arm, carrying on the day\u2019s talk or wandering far and wide until late, hunting through the city\u2019s crowded lights and shadows for that infinity of mental excitement which only quiet observation can supply.\\n\\nOn such occasions I could not help noticing and admiring (though his rich ideality had already prepared me to expect it) a certain analytic power in Dupin. He seemed, too, to take a keen satisfaction in putting it to use\u2014if not precisely in showing it\u2014and he did not hesitate to own the pleasure he drew from it. With a low chuckling laugh he told me that most men, in regard to themselves, carried windows in their breasts, and that he was accustomed to follow such claims with direct and startling proofs of his intimate knowledge of my own. At those moments his bearing turned cold and abstract; his eyes went vacant in their expression; and while his voice, which was usually a rich tenor, rose into a treble, it would have sounded petulant\u2014had it not been for the deliberate care and the perfectly clear distinctness of his enunciation.\"}"
