          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e581b5b8681bcab4af5723ec\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There comes a time in every properly raised boy\u2019s life when he gets a wild urge to go somewhere and dig for hidden treasure. That desire suddenly hit Tom one day. He set out to find Joe Harper, but it didn\u2019t work out. Next he went looking for Ben Rogers; Ben had gone fishing. Before long, Tom ran into Huck Finn the Red-Handed. Huck would go along. Tom brought him to a private spot and told him everything in confidence. Huck was willing. Huck was always willing to join any adventure that promised entertainment and didn\u2019t require any capital, because he had a troublesome surplus of that kind of time that isn\u2019t money. \u201cWhere\u2019ll we dig?\u201d Huck asked.\\n\\n\u201cOh, just about anywhere.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, is it hidden all around?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, it isn\u2019t. It\u2019s hidden in very specific places, Huck\u2014sometimes on islands, sometimes in rotten chests under the end of a limb of an old dead tree, right where the shadow falls at midnight; but mostly under the floors in haunted houses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho hides it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, robbers, of course\u2014who\u2019d you think? Sunday-school superintendents?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. If it were mine, I wouldn\u2019t hide it; I\u2019d spend it and have a good time.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo would I. But robbers don\u2019t do it that way. They always hide it and leave it there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t they come back for it anymore?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, they think they will, but they usually forget the marks, or else they die. Either way, it sits there a long time and gets rusty; and then, sooner or later, somebody finds an old yellow paper that tells how to find the marks\u2014a paper you have to cipher over for about a week, because it\u2019s mostly signs and hy\u2019roglyphics.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHyro\u2014which?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHy\u2019roglyphics\u2014pictures and things, you know, that don\u2019t seem to mean anything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo you have one of those papers, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell then, how are you going to find the marks?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t want any marks. They always bury it under a haunted house or on an island, or under a dead tree that has one limb sticking out. Well, we\u2019ve tried Jackson\u2019s Island a little, and we can try it again sometime; and there\u2019s that old haunted house up the Still-House branch, and there are lots of dead-limb trees\u2014dead loads of \u2019em.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs it under all of them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow could you say that? No!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen how are you going to know which one to go for?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGo for all of \u2019em!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTom, it\u2019ll take all summer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, what of it? Suppose you find a brass pot with a hundred dollars in it, all rusty and gray, or a rotten chest full of diamonds. How\u2019s that?\u201d\\n\\nHuck\u2019s eyes lit up.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s great. Plenty great enough for me. Just you give me the hundred dollars, and I don\u2019t want no diamonds.\u201d\"}"
