          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"743ed6d1bf0aeb4f6db41157\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The stars were still shining brightly when I woke. Taurus was high in the southern sky, and that meant it was midnight. I had been having troubled dreams. It seemed to me that I had been invited to Timon\u2019s last feast. I arrived with a sharp hunger, the covers were lifted, and hot water was brought in, steaming in a way that never satisfied. Then I ran before the host\u2019s anger, who had taken the form of Raymond. In my sick imagination, the things he threw at me were filled with foul, rotten-smelling vapors. His shape, twisted by a thousand distortions, grew into a huge phantom with the mark of pestilence on its brow. The shadow kept rising and rising, filling everything, as if it meant to break through the hard, unyielding ceiling that arched over and held the world inside.\\n\\nThe nightmare turned into real torture. With a strong effort, I forced myself awake and brought my thoughts back to reason. My first thought was Perdita. I had to return to her. I had to support her, taking whatever food I could from despair to keep her wounded heart going. I tried to bring her back from the wild excess of grief, using the strict rules of duty and the gentle comfort of regret.\\n\\nThe stars\u2019 position was my only guide. I turned away from the terrible ruin of the Golden City, and after a great struggle, I managed to get myself out of it. Outside the walls, I met a group of soldiers. I borrowed a horse from one of them and hurried to my sister. In the short time I was gone, the plain looked different. The camp had been broken up. The remains of the army that had been dismissed were scattered in small groups. Every face was clouded, and every movement showed surprise and fear.\\n\\nWith a heavy heart, I entered the palace. I was afraid to move forward, afraid to speak, afraid to even look. In the middle of the hall sat Perdita. She was on the marble floor, her head resting on her chest, her hair loose and tangled, her fingers busy, twisting together. She was as pale as stone, and her face was pulled tight with pain. When she saw me, she looked up as if asking a question. The small look of hope in her eyes turned to misery. I couldn\u2019t get the words out. I felt a horrible smile pull at my lips. She understood what I meant. Her head fell again, and her fingers kept working restlessly.\\n\\nAt last I found my voice, but it frightened her. The poor girl had already understood my look, and she would not, for anything, have wanted the story of her suffering to be shaped and confirmed by harsh, final words. It seemed, too, that she wanted to distract me from the subject. She rose from the floor and whispered, \u201cHush. After all this weeping, Clara is asleep. We must not disturb her.\u201d\"}"
