          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"02498494d8c43ae4a99957c1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I thanked him and apologised. He said, \u201cNot at all,\u201d and went on.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham was now an heiress, so you can imagine she was treated like a great prize. Her half-brother had plenty of money again, but between old debts and fresh madness, he wasted it all most terribly once more. There were even bigger differences between him and her than there had been between him and his father. It is suspected that he held a deep, deadly grudge against her, believing she had influenced his father\u2019s anger. Now, I come to the cruel part of the story\u2014just stopping for a moment, my dear Handel, to say that a dinner napkin won\u2019t fit into a tumbler.\u201d\\n\\nI have no idea why I was trying to pack mine into my tumbler. I only know that I found myself, with a stubborn determination that should have been used for something better, making the hardest effort to squeeze it into that space. Again I thanked him and apologised, and again he said, in the cheerfullest way, \u201cNot at all, I\u2019m sure!\u201d and continued.\\n\\n\u201cThen a certain man appeared on the scene\u2014at the races, at public balls, or anywhere else you like. He made love to Miss Havisham. I never saw him myself, because this was five-and-twenty years ago, before you and I were Handel, but I have heard my father say he was showy, the sort of man meant for that purpose. Still, my father insists\u2014very strongly\u2014that without ignorance or prejudice, you must not mistake him for a gentleman. He believes that no man who was not truly a gentleman at heart ever was, since the world began, a true gentleman in the way he behaved. He says no varnish can hide the grain of the wood; the more varnish you put on, the more the grain shows through. Well! This man followed Miss Havisham closely and claimed to be devoted to her. I think she hadn\u2019t been very open to anyone at that time, but all the openness she had certainly came out then, and she passionately loved him. There\u2019s no doubt she idolised him completely. He worked on her feelings in a careful, planned way, and he got large sums of money from her. He also persuaded her to buy her brother out of his share in the brewery\u2014which her father had left him, though only weakly\u2014at an enormous price, telling her that when he was her husband he would have to own and run it all. Your guardian wasn\u2019t involved in Miss Havisham\u2019s decisions at that time, and she was too proud and too in love to be guided by anyone.\u201d}\"}"
