          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"64d415c3f5d9a7c28423e9a5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If not, then, since you are asking for my patience and for the mercy of the people you have hurt so deeply, sit down in that chair\u2014quietly, without a word. It has been waiting for you for two full days.\u201d\\n\\nThe monks muttered some words that no one could understand, but they still hesitated.\\n\\n\u201cYou will be on time,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow. \u201cIf I speak once, the other option will be gone forever.\u201d\\n\\nThe man hesitated again.\\n\\n\u201cI have no desire to negotiate,\u201d Mr. Brownlow said. \u201cAnd because I am fighting for other people\u2019s most important interests, I have no right to bargain.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs there\u2014\u201d Monks began, his voice shaking. \u201cIs there\u2014no middle way?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNone.\u201d\\n\\nMonks looked at the old gentleman with anxious eyes. But when he saw only strictness and determination in his face, he went into the room, shrugged his shoulders, and sat down.\\n\\n\u201cLock the door from the outside,\u201d Mr. Brownlow told the attendants. \u201cCome back when I ring.\u201d\\n\\nThe men obeyed, and the two were left alone.\\n\\n\u201cThis is how you treat me, sir,\u201d said Monks, dropping his hat and cloak, \u201cafter my father\u2019s oldest friend.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is because I was your father\u2019s oldest friend, young man,\u201d Mr. Brownlow replied. \u201cIt is because the hopes and wishes of those early, happy years were tied to him\u2014and to that fine woman of his blood and family, who returned to God in her youth and left me here alone, a lonely man. It is because he knelt with me beside his only sisters\u2019 death-bed when he was still a boy, on the morning that\u2014if Heaven had willed it differently\u2014would have made her my young wife. It is because my heart, hardened by pain, held on to him through all his trials and mistakes, until he died. It is because old memories and connections fill my mind, and even seeing you brings back thoughts of him. That is why I am choosing to treat you gently now\u2014yes, Edward Leeford\u2014even now\u2014and why I blush for your unworthiness, since you carry the name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat does the name have to do with it?\u201d the other asked, after watching him in half-silence and half-stubborn confusion. \u201cWhat is the name to me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing,\u201d Mr. Brownlow answered. \u201cNothing to you. But it was hers. And even after all this time, it brings back to me\u2014an old man\u2014the warmth and excitement I once felt, only to have it spoken again by a stranger. I am very glad you changed it. Very glad.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is all very impressive,\u201d said Monks, keeping up his pretending name, after a long silence. During that silence he kept jerking himself back and forth in sullen defiance, while Mr. Brownlow sat with his face shaded by his hand.\u201d}\"}"
