{"request_id":"ab41a0d2f40bd414cde93172","rewritten_text":"When Tom woke up that morning, he couldn’t help wondering where he was. He pushed himself upright, rubbed at his eyes, and took in his surroundings. Then it all clicked into place. It was that cool, gray dawn, and in the deep, steady quiet of the woods there was a satisfying feeling of rest and peace. No leaf moved; no sound intruded on great Nature’s own contemplation. Dew beaded on the leaves and grasses. A pale blanket of ashes lay over the fire, and a narrow ribbon of blue smoke rose straight up into the air. Joe and Huck were still asleep.

Far off in the woods, a bird called, and another answered; soon after, the hammering of a woodpecker carried on the air. Little by little the cool, dim gray of morning brightened, and little by little sounds multiplied and life made itself known. The wonder of Nature shaking off sleep and getting to work unfolded before the thoughtful boy. A small green worm slid along the surface of a dewy leaf, lifting about two-thirds of its body into the air now and then and “sniffing around,” then going on again—Tom said it was measuring—and when the worm drew near, he held perfectly still, his hopes rising and falling as the creature kept coming toward him or seemed ready to drift elsewhere. At last it judged the moment, curved its body in the air, and then came decisively down onto Tom’s leg, beginning to travel over him; and his whole heart was glad, because that meant he was about to get a new suit of clothes—no question about it—a flashy, piratical uniform. Then, as if from nowhere, a line of ants appeared and went about their business. One worked with grim determination, hauling a dead spider in its arms that was five times its own size, and dragged it straight up a tree trunk. A brown-spotted lady-bug climbed to the dizzy top of a grass blade, and Tom leaned close and said, “Lady-bug, lady-bug, fly away home, your house is on fire, your children’s alone,” and she lifted off to see about it—something that didn’t surprise him, since he’d long known the insect was gullible about fires, and he’d tested her with that very trick more than once. Next came a tumblebug, rolling along with its ball, and Tom touched it, watching it tuck its legs in tight against its body and pretend to be dead.

By this time the birds were practically in a frenzy. A catbird—the Northern mocker—settled in a tree above Tom’s head and poured out her imitations of her neighbors with pure delight; then a shrill jay swooped down, flashed a streak of blue, and landed on a twig almost within the boy’s reach. It tilted its head to one side, studying the strangers with intense curiosity. A gray squirrel and a larger “fox” type of fellow came skittering along, pausing at intervals to look and chatter at the boys, for the wild creatures probably hadn’t seen a human being before and could scarcely decide whether they ought to be afraid."}

