{"request_id":"b4f98f644a7941d367b2c7cf","rewritten_text":"Once we had seen our friend properly settled in his new office, we directed our thoughts again toward Windsor. The closeness of that place to London was such that, when we left Raymond and Perdita, the notion of a painful parting was almost taken away. We took our leave of them in the Protectoral Palace. It was a pleasing sight to watch my sister step in, as it were, into the very spirit of the scene, and strive to fill her new station with a dignity that suited it. Her inward pride and her humility of manner were now more openly at odds. Her timidity was not something assumed; it sprang from a fear of not being valued as she ought to be, from that slight estimate of the world’s regard which also marked Raymond. Yet Perdita, for her part, thought more often of others than he did; and much of her bashfulness came from a wish to spare those about her any sense of inferiority—a feeling that never once occurred to her. In the circumstances of her birth and education, Idris would have been better matched to the forms and formulas of ceremony; but the very ease with which she performed such things, through habit, made them seem wearisome, whereas, with all the drawbacks, Perdita plainly enjoyed her situation. She was too full of fresh ideas to suffer greatly when we departed; she gave us an affectionate farewell and promised to come and see us soon, but she did not regret the circumstances that brought about our separation.

Raymond’s spirits were in no way restrained; he scarcely knew what to do with his newly acquired power. His mind was crowded with plans, though as yet he had settled on none; still, he assured himself, his friends, and the world, that the era of his Protectorship should be marked by some deed of surpassing glory. So we spoke of them, and moralized, as with fewer numbers we returned to Windsor Castle. We felt an intense satisfaction at having escaped political agitation, and sought our quiet with renewed eagerness. We wanted for no occupation; yet my own ardour was now turned chiefly toward intellectual exertion, and I found that hard study served as an excellent remedy to calm the fever of spirit which, in idleness, I should doubtless have been subject to. Perdita had allowed us to take Clara back with us to Windsor; and she, together with my two lovely infants, became a constant source of interest and amusement.

The only thing that disturbed our peace was Adrian’s health. It was plain that it declined, without any symptoms that would have led us to suspect his disorder—unless, indeed, his brightened eyes, his animated look, and his flushed cheeks made us fear consumption; but he had neither pain nor fear. He threw himself with ardour into his books, and rested from study in the company he loved best: that of his sister and myself."}

