          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ba50a5eeee4c197ca77502d3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On the morning appointed for Admiral and Mrs Croft\u2019s visit to Kellynch Hall, Anne found it perfectly natural to continue her almost daily walk to Lady Russell\u2019s, keeping out of the way until everything should be finished; and then, when she discovered it was over, she felt it most natural to regret that she had not had the chance of seeing them.\\n\\nThis meeting of the two parties turned out extremely satisfactory, and settled the whole business at once. Each lady had already been disposed to agree, and therefore saw nothing in the other but good manners; while, as for the gentlemen, there was such hearty good humour, such an open, trusting liberality on the Admiral\u2019s part, that it could not fail to affect Sir Walter. He had, besides, been flattered into his very best and most polished behaviour by Mr Shepherd\u2019s assurances that he was, by report, known to the Admiral as a model of good breeding.\\n\\nThe house and grounds, the furniture\u2014everything\u2014was approved; the Crofts were approved; the terms, the time, every point, and every person, were right; and Mr Shepherd\u2019s clerks were set to work, without any single preliminary disagreement to alter what \u201cThis indenture sheweth.\u201d\\n\\nWithout the least hesitation, Sir Walter declared the Admiral the best-looking sailor he had ever met, and even went so far as to say that, if his own man had been the one to arrange his hair, he should not be ashamed to be seen with him anywhere. The Admiral, with sympathetic cordiality, remarked to his wife as they drove back through the park, \u201cI thought we should soon come to a deal, my dear, in spite of what they told us at Taunton. The Baronet will never set the Thames on fire, but there seems to be no harm in him.\u201d\u2014so that the reciprocal compliments would have been judged about equal.\\n\\nThe Crofts were to take possession at Michaelmas; and as Sir Walter meant to remove to Bath during the preceding month, there was no time to lose in arranging every dependent matter.\\n\\nLady Russell, being persuaded that Anne would not be permitted to be of any use, or any consequence, in choosing the house they were about to secure, was very unwilling to have her sent away so soon. She wished to make it possible for Anne to stay behind until she could herself take her to Bath after Christmas; but as she had engagements of her own which must take her from Kellynch for several weeks, she could not give the full invitation she desired. Anne, though dreading the possible heats of September in all the white glare of Bath, and grieving to miss the influence so sweet and so sad of the autumnal months in the country, did not think, on the whole, that she ought to remain.\"}"
