          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"03d1b5527c34ccdca331a373\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In truth, he was dressed up as \u201cJonas the Graveless, or the Corpse-Snatcher of Chertsey Barn,\u201d\u2014one of his most striking impersonations, and one the Cantervilles had every reason to keep in mind, since it was the very first cause of their quarrel with their neighbour, Lord Rufford. It was a quarter past two in the morning, and, so far as he could judge, there was not a soul stirring. Yet as he wandered toward the library to see whether any trace of the blood-stain remained, two figures suddenly sprang at him from a dark corner; they flung their arms about above their heads and shrieked \u201cBOO!\u201d right into his ear.\\n\\nOvercome with panic\u2014quite natural enough under the circumstances\u2014he made for the staircase, only to find Washington Otis waiting there with the big garden-syringe. Thus hemmed in on every side by his enemies, and driven nearly to bay, he disappeared into the great iron stove, which, happily for him, was not lit; and from there he had to pick his way home through the flues and chimneys, reaching his own room in a dreadful condition of dirt, confusion, and despair.\\n\\nAfter that, he was never again seen on any midnight venture. The twins lay in wait for him on several nights, and scattered the passages with nutshells, to the intense annoyance of their parents and the servants, but all to no purpose. It was plain enough that his feelings had been so badly hurt that he would not show himself. Mr. Otis therefore went back to his great undertaking on the history of the Democratic Party, which he had been working at for some years; Mrs. Otis set about a splendid clam-bake that astonished the entire county; the boys took up lacrosse, euchre, poker, and other American national games; and Virginia went riding along the lanes on her pony, with the young Duke of Cheshire at her side, he having come to spend the last week of his holidays at Canterville Chase. Everyone supposed the ghost had gone away, and, in fact, Mr. Otis wrote to Lord Canterville to say so; Lord Canterville, in reply, declared how delighted he was with the news, and sent his best congratulations to the Minister\u2019s worthy wife.\\n\\nThe Otises, however, were mistaken, for the ghost was still in the house; and though he was now almost an invalid, he was by no means inclined to let the matter drop\u2014especially once he learned that among the guests was the young Duke of Cheshire. His grand-uncle, Lord Francis Stilton, had once wagered a hundred guineas with Colonel Carbury that he would play dice with the Canterville ghost, and the next morning he was found lying on the floor of the card-room in such a helpless state of paralysis that, though he lived to a great age, he could never afterward manage anything but \u201cDouble Sixes.\u201d\"}"
