          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"71bec474f85de34fd81265d2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He couldn\u2019t help it. And it drove him crazy to see\u2014at least, as he thought he saw\u2014that Becky Thatcher never once suspected he was even in the land of the living. But she did see, nevertheless; and she knew she was winning her fight, too, and was glad to watch him suffer the way she had suffered.\\n\\nAmy\u2019s cheerful chatter became unbearable. Tom hinted at things he had to take care of\u2014things that had to be done\u2014and that time was running out. But it was useless\u2014the girl kept chirping on. Tom thought, \u201cOh, come on\u2014am I ever going to get rid of her?\u201d At last he had to attend to those things, and she said, carelessly, that she\u2019d be \u201caround\u201d when school let out. He hurried away, hating her for it.\\n\\n\u201cAny other boy!\u201d Tom thought, grinding his teeth. \u201cAny boy in the whole town but that Saint Louis smarty who thinks he dresses so fine and is aristocracy! Oh, sure\u2014I beat you the first day you ever saw this town, mister, and I\u2019ll beat you again! Just wait till I catch you out! I\u2019ll just\u2014\u201d\\n\\nAnd he went through the motions of beating an imaginary boy\u2014pummeling the air, and kicking and stabbing. \u201cOh, you do, do you? You holler enough, do you? All right then\u2014let that teach you!\u201d And so the pretend beating was finished to his satisfaction.\\n\\nTom ran home at noon. His conscience couldn\u2019t take any more of Amy\u2019s grateful happiness, and his jealousy couldn\u2019t bear any more of the other distress. Becky went back to inspecting her pictures with Alfred, but as the minutes dragged by and Tom didn\u2019t come, her triumph began to fade and she lost interest; seriousness and absentmindedness followed, and then melancholy. Two or three times she perked up at a footstep, but it was only a false hope\u2014no Tom came. At last she became completely miserable and wished she hadn\u2019t pushed it so far. When poor Alfred, realizing he was losing her, didn\u2019t know what else to do and kept blurting, \u201cOh, here\u2019s a jolly one! look at this!\u201d she finally lost patience and said, \u201cOh, don\u2019t bother me! I don\u2019t care for them!\u201d Then she burst into tears, got up, and walked away.\\n\\nAlfred fell in beside her, about to try to comfort her, but she said:\\n\\n\u201cGo away and leave me alone, can\u2019t you! I hate you!\u201d\\n\\nSo the boy stopped, wondering what he could have done\u2014because she had said she\u2019d look at pictures all through the nooning\u2014and she kept walking on, crying. Then Alfred went on thinking in the empty schoolhouse. He felt humiliated and angry. He could easily see the truth: the girl had simply used him as a convenient way to vent her spite on Tom Sawyer.\"}"
