          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"11a10cc6f70f5ce222fc0d19\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I do not understand why she wanted to be close to me. Still, if the same situation happened again, I am sure I would be fooled again. Caroline did not come back to see me until yesterday, and in the meantime I received not a note and not a word from her. When she finally did arrive, it was clear that she did not enjoy it. She made a small, formal apology for not calling sooner, said nothing at all about wanting to see me again, and she seemed so changed that, when she left, I decided to stop knowing her.\\n\\nI feel sorry for her, even though I cannot help blaming her. She was wrong to single me out the way she did. I can say for certain that every step toward intimacy started with her. But I pity her because she must know she has been acting wrongly, and I am sure that her brother is the reason. I do not need to explain more. Even if we both know her anxiety is unnecessary, if she feels it, it can explain how she has behaved toward me. And however dear he is to his sister, any worry she has for him is natural and kind.\\n\\nStill, I cannot help wondering why she has any such fears now. If he had cared about me at all, we would have met long ago. She knows I am in town, I am sure, because she said so herself. Yet the way she talks makes it seem as if she is trying to convince herself that he is really interested in Miss Darcy. I cannot understand that. If I were not afraid of judging too harshly, I might almost say that there is a strong look of dishonesty in all of this.\\n\\nI will try to push away every painful thought and focus only on what will make me happy: your affection, and the steady kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Please write to me again very soon. Miss Bingley mentioned that he might never return to Netherfield, and that they might give up the house, but she was not certain. We should not bring it up. I am very glad that you have such pleasant news from our friends at Hunsford. Please go and see them, with Sir William and Maria. I am sure you will be comfortable there.\\n\\n\u201cYours, etc.\u201d\\n\\nThis letter upset Elizabeth. But her spirits returned when she thought that Jane would not be fooled anymore, at least not by the sister. Any hope from the brother was now completely gone.\"}"
