          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"18d90393e1180a3334bf9083\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For years, Dorian Gray found himself unable to shake off the sway of that book. Or, perhaps it would be truer to say he never really tried to break its hold. From Paris he obtained no fewer than nine copies of the first edition, printed on large paper, and he had them bound in different colours, so they could match his shifting moods and the restless whims of a temperament over which, at times, he seemed to have almost completely lost command. The hero\u2014the remarkable young Parisian in whom the romantic and the scientific temperaments were so oddly interwoven\u2014came to him as a kind of shadowing likeness of himself. Indeed, the entire volume felt as though it held the history of his own life, set down before he had even lived it.\\n\\nIn one respect, though, he was luckier than the novel\u2019s fantastic figure. He never learned\u2014never, in fact, had any reason to learn\u2014about that somewhat grotesque terror of mirrors, of polished metal, and of still water that struck the young Parisian so early in his life, and that was brought on by the sudden rotting of a beau who, once, had seemed so extraordinary. With something like a cruel delight\u2014and in nearly every delight, and certainly in every pleasure, cruelty has its share\u2014he would read the later portion of the book, with its genuinely tragic, though a touch too heavily stressed, account of the grief and despair of one who had lost, as he himself had, what others and the world had most dearly prized.\\n\\nThe extraordinary beauty that had captivated Basil Hallward, and many others besides, never seemed to leave him. Even those who had heard the worst things said about him\u2014and from time to time strange rumours about his way of living slipped through London and became the talk of the clubs\u2014could not bring themselves to believe anything that would stain his name when they saw him. He always carried the look of a man who had kept himself untouched by the world. When Dorian Gray entered, men who spoke in coarse terms would fall silent. There was something in the cleanness of his face that checked them. His very presence seemed to stir in them the memory of the innocence they had soiled. They could not understand how someone so captivating and graceful could have escaped the mark of an age at once sordid and sensual.\\n\\nOften, when he returned from one of those secret, drawn-out absences that led his friends\u2014or those who believed themselves to be his friends\u2014to form such odd conjectures, he would slip up himself to the locked room. There he would open the door with the key that never left him, and stand, with a mirror, before the portrait Basil Hallward had painted of him\u2014watching, in turn, the wicked, aging countenance on the canvas and the fair young face that met his gaze with laughter from the polished glass.\"}"
