          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"326f6d2823594d4e3e1f25fc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without giving herself any time to think, and barely knowing what she felt, Elizabeth, as soon as she finished this letter, grabbed the next one. She opened it with the greatest impatience and read on. It had been written a day after the first letter ended.\\n\\n\u201cBy this time, my dearest sister, you have received my hurried letter. I hope this one is clearer, but I\u2019m so confused that I can\u2019t be sure I\u2019m saying things in a sensible way. Dearest Lizzy, I hardly know what to write, but I have bad news for you, and it can\u2019t be delayed. Even though it would be foolish for Mr. Wickham to marry our poor Lydia, we are now desperate to know whether it has actually happened. There is too much reason to fear they have not gone to Scotland.\\n\\nColonel Forster came yesterday. He left Brighton the day before, not many hours after the express. Lydia had sent a short letter to Mrs. F., which made it seem like they were going to Gretna Green. But Denny said something that made them worry. He believed that W. never intended to go there, or to marry Lydia at all. He told Colonel F. this, and Colonel F. immediately became alarmed. He set off from B. to follow their route.\\n\\nHe was able to trace them easily to Clapham, but no farther. When they reached Clapham, they got into a hackney-coach and dismissed the chaise that had brought them from Epsom. After that, all anyone knows is that they were seen continuing along the London road. I don\u2019t know what to think.\\n\\nAfter making every possible inquiry on that side of London, Colonel F. went on into Hertfordshire. He kept trying to find them at every turnpike and at the inns in Barnet and Hatfield, but without success. No one had seen such people pass through. With the greatest kindness, he came on to Longbourn and tried to calm our fears in a way that reflects very well on his heart. I am truly sorry for him and Mrs. F., but no one can blame them.\\n\\nOur distress, my dear Lizzy, is very great. My father and mother believe the worst, but I can\u2019t think so badly of him. There are many reasons they might choose to marry privately in town instead of following their first plan. And even if he could somehow form such a plan against a young woman like Lydia, which is not likely, how could I believe she is so lost to everything? Impossible! Still, I\u2019m sorry to say that Colonel F. doesn\u2019t seem willing to rely on their marriage. When I told him my hopes, he shook his head and said he feared W. was not a man to be trusted.\u201d\"}"
