          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f8606876aed85014cd541ae9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He made it clear to her that Frank admired her very much\u2014he thought her extremely beautiful and charming. With so much to be said in his favor, she felt she shouldn\u2019t judge him too harshly. As Mrs. Weston put it, \u201call young people would have their little whims.\u201d\\n\\nAmong his new acquaintances in Surry, there was one person who did not feel so forgiving. In general, people in the parishes of Donwell and Highbury spoke about him with great honesty. They made allowances for the small mistakes of such a handsome young man\u2014one who smiled so often and bowed so well. But there was one person whose judgment could not be softened by bows or smiles: Mr. Knightley. The story was told to him at Hartfield. For the moment, he was quiet, but Emma heard him almost at once say to himself, while he held a newspaper, \u201cHum! just the trifling, silly fellow I took him for.\u201d She almost wanted to be offended, but a moment\u2019s thought showed her he was only saying it to ease his own feelings, not to cause trouble. So she let it go.\\n\\nEven though Mr. and Mrs. Weston had come this morning with news that was not entirely good, their visit was especially helpful in another way. Something happened while they were at Hartfield that made Emma want their advice, and\u2014what was even better\u2014she wanted exactly the advice they gave.\\n\\nThis was what happened:\u2014The Coles had been settled in Highbury for some years, and they were good people. They were friendly, generous, and not at all showy. But they were of low origin, in trade, and only moderately well-bred. When they first moved into the country, they lived according to what they could afford. They kept to themselves, and they had only a small circle of acquaintances, and even that circle was not expensive. In the last year or two, though, they had gained a great deal of money. The house in town had brought in more profit, and their fortune had clearly improved. With their wealth, their ideas grew too. They wanted a larger house and more company. They added to their house, increased the number of servants, and raised their spending in every way. By now, in both money and the style of living, they were second only to the family at Hartfield. Their love of social life, and their new dining-room, made everyone expect that they would start hosting dinner parties. A few had already taken place, mostly among single men. Emma could hardly imagine that they would dare to invite the best and most regular families. That meant they would not invite Donwell, Hartfield, or Randalls. Nothing would make her go if they did. She also regretted that her father\u2019s well-known habits would make her refusal mean less than she would have liked.\"}"
