          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7eada23f6943d3b3a7f80f4e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was Lady Windermere\u2019s final reception before Easter, and Bentinck House was even more packed than it usually was. Six Cabinet Ministers had arrived from the Speaker\u2019s Lev\u00e9e in their stars and ribands; all the pretty women had turned out in their finest dresses; and at the far end of the picture-gallery stood Princess Sophia of Carlsr\u00fche\u2014a heavy, Tartar-looking woman, with tiny black eyes and splendid emeralds\u2014talking off-colour French at the top of her voice and laughing without restraint at everything that was said to her. Altogether it was a remarkable mixture of people. Handsome peeresses exchanged civilities with fierce Radicals; popular preachers brushed coat-tails with distinguished sceptics; a whole company of bishops kept trailing a stout prima-donna from room to room; on the staircase stood several Royal Academicians, disguised as artists; and it was even said that, at one time, the supper-room was crammed with geniuses. In truth, it was one of Lady Windermere\u2019s best evenings, and the Princess remained until close on half-past eleven.\\n\\nThe moment she had gone, Lady Windermere went back to the picture-gallery, where a celebrated political economist was gravely laying out the scientific theory of music to an indignant virtuoso from Hungary, and she began to speak with the Duchess of Paisley. She looked wonderfully beautiful\u2014her grand ivory throat, her large blue forget-me-not eyes, and her heavy coils of golden hair. Or rather, not that washed-out straw shade which nowadays steals the name of gold, but such gold as is spun into sunbeams or tucked away in strange amber; and it gave her face something of the look of a saint, with no small touch of the fascination of a sinner. She was, in a way, a curious study in psychology. From early on she had grasped the important truth that nothing resembles innocence so much as an indiscretion; and through a run of reckless escapades\u2014half of them perfectly harmless\u2014she had gathered all the advantages of a well-established personality. She had more than once changed her husband; indeed, Debrett credited her with three marriages; but since she had never changed her lover, the world had long since stopped talking scandal about her. She was now forty, childless, and possessed of that excessive passion for pleasure which is the secret of staying young.\\n\\nSuddenly she swept her gaze eagerly round the room and said, in her clear contralto, \u201cWhere is my cheiromantist?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour what, Gladys?\u201d the Duchess cried, with an involuntary start.\\n\\n\u201cMy cheiromantist, Duchess; I can\u2019t get on without him at present.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Gladys! you\u2019re always so original,\u201d the Duchess murmured, trying to recall what a cheiromantist really was\u2014and hoping it wasn\u2019t the same thing as a cheiropodist.\\n\\n\u201cHe comes to see my hand twice a week, regularly,\u201d Lady Windermere went on, \u201cand he\u2019s quite fascinating about it.\u201d\"}"
