          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e0803da13a2ea780ff798c98\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter had taken a very fine house in Camden Place\u2014a lofty, dignified address, the kind that suits a man of consequence. Both he and Elizabeth were settled there, to everyone\u2019s satisfaction.\\n\\nAnne entered with a sinking heart, expecting to be shut up for many months, and anxiously thinking to herself, \u201cOh! when shall I leave you again?\u201d Still, the welcome she received was unexpectedly warm, and it helped her. Her father and sister were glad to see her, not only for her sake, but because they wanted to show her the house and its furnishings; they met her with kindness. The fact that she was now the fourth person at their table, when they sat down to dinner, was treated as an advantage.\\n\\nMrs. Clay was very pleasant and very smiling, but her courtesies and smiles were more or less automatic. Anne had always expected that she would perform whatever was proper on her arrival, but the easy, accommodating attitude of the others was more than she had anticipated. They were clearly in excellent spirits, and she would soon hear the reasons. Yet they had no intention of listening to her. After a few compliments about how deeply they regretted her old neighbourhood, which Anne could not honestly return, they only asked a couple of faint questions before the conversation had to be entirely their own. Uppercross interested them hardly at all; Kellynch very little. It was all Bath.\\n\\nThey took pleasure in assuring her that Bath more than met their expectations in every respect. Their house, they said, was undoubtedly the best in Camden Place; their drawing-rooms had many clear advantages over any others they had either seen or heard of, and the superiority was just as strong in the style of the fittings and in the taste of the furniture. Their acquaintance was in great demand. Everyone wanted to visit them. They had turned down many introductions, and yet cards were still constantly being left by people they didn\u2019t even know.\\n\\nThere was plenty to enjoy. Could Anne really be surprised that her father and sister were happy? She might not be surprised, but she had to sigh that her father felt no sense of humiliation in his change; that he saw nothing to regret in the duties and dignity of a resident landholder; that he found so much to be proud of in the small vanities of town life. And she had to sigh, and smile, and wonder too, as Elizabeth threw open the folding doors and walked with exultation from one drawing-room to the next, boasting of their space\u2014at the thought that woman, who had once been mistress of Kellynch Hall, could now take pride in the extent of rooms between two walls, perhaps only thirty feet apart.\\n\\nBut this was not everything that made them happy. They had Mr. Elliot too.\"}"
