          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"64b37919b70900838c98bb40\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After I had sent them in, I could not stay away from the places where they were, and I felt that they were more hopeful and less desperate when I was near. In my restless pain of mind, I would wander the streets in the evening, passing the offices and houses where I had left the petitions. Even now, the weary western streets of London on a cold, dusty spring night\u2014the stern, shut-up mansions and the long rows of lamps\u2014still feel melancholy to me because of that.\\n\\nThe visits I could make him each day were now shorter, and he was kept under stricter watch. I thought, or feared, that they suspected I might try to poison him, so before I sat down at his bedside I asked to be searched. I told the officer who was always there that I was willing to do anything to prove that my intentions were honest. Nobody was cruel to him or to me. There was duty to be done, and it was done, but not harshly. The officer always assured me that he was worse, and that other sick prisoners in the room, and other prisoners who served as sick nurses\u2014malefactors, but not without kindness, thank God\u2014gave the same report.\\n\\nAs the days went on, I noticed more and more how he lay, calm and still, staring at the white ceiling, with a blank look on his face until some word of mine brightened it for a moment, and then it faded again. Sometimes he could hardly speak at all. Then he would answer me with small presses of my hand, and I learned to understand what he meant.\\n\\nBy the tenth day, I saw a bigger change in him than I had seen before. His eyes were turned toward the door, and they lit up when I came in.\\n\\n\u201cDear boy,\u201d he said, as I sat down by his bed, \u201cI thought you was late. But I knew you couldn\u2019t be that.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is just the time,\u201d I said. \u201cI waited for it at the gate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou always waits at the gate; don\u2019t you, dear boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. I don\u2019t want to lose a moment.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank\u2019ee, dear boy, thank\u2019ee. God bless you! You\u2019ve never deserted me, dear boy.\u201d\\n\\nI held his hand in silence, because I could not forget that I had once meant to desert him.\\n\\n\u201cAnd what\u2019s the best of all,\u201d he said, \u201cis that you\u2019ve been more comfortable with me, since I was under a dark cloud, than when the sun was shining.\u201d\"}"
