{"request_id":"0e5fffc524ad9f237e1488d7","rewritten_text":"“It was not until some time later that I had regained enough composure to attend to the balloon’s usual concerns. I then inspected it carefully, and, to my great relief, found it unhurt. Every one of my instruments was secure, and, happily, I had not lost either ballast or provisions. Indeed, I had fastened them so well in their proper places that no such mishap could reasonably occur. Glancing at my watch, I saw it was six o’clock. I was still rising swiftly, and my barometer reported a height of three and three-quarter miles. Directly beneath me in the ocean there was a small black object, a little oblong, and apparently about the size of, and in all respects much like, one of those childish toys called a domino. Bringing my telescope to bear upon it, I could clearly make out a British ninety-four-gun ship, close-hauled, pitching heavily in the sea with her head to the W.S.W. Beyond that vessel I saw nothing but the ocean, the sky, and the sun, which had already been up for some time.

“It is high time that I should lay before your Excellencies the purpose of my perilous journey. Your Excellencies will recall that the distressing circumstances in Rotterdam had at last driven me to the resolution of taking my own life. Yet it was not that I felt any real loathing for life itself; rather, I was worn down beyond endurance by the accidental miseries that attended my condition. In that frame of mind—wishing to live, but sick of living—the bookstall of a bookseller offered a kind of opening to my imagination. I then at last resolved. I determined to go away, but remain alive—to leave the world, yet continue to exist—in short, to drop all doubts, and, whatever might follow, to force a passage, if I could, to the moon. And to ensure I am not thought more mad than I truly am, I will set down, as clearly as I can, the considerations that led me to believe that an undertaking of this sort, though undoubtedly difficult and unquestionably full of danger, was not absolutely beyond the reach of a bold spirit.

“The moon’s actual distance from the earth was the first matter to be considered. The mean or average interval between the centres of the two planets is 59.9643 of the earth’s equatorial radii, or only about 237,000 miles. I speak of the mean or average interval, but it must be remembered that the moon’s orbit is an ellipse with an eccentricity of no less than 0.05484 of the major semi-axis of the ellipse itself, and that the earth’s centre lies in its focus; so that, if I could in any way contrive to meet the moon, as it were, at its perigee, the distance just mentioned would be reduced in a material degree.”"}

