          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dec1f694f34014c5192945f8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She studied me closely for a moment, and then spoke in a low voice.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you want for them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly,\u201d I said, \u201cthat you won\u2019t mix them up with the others. They may share the same blood, but, believe me, they are not the same in nature.\u201d\\n\\nStill watching me, Miss Havisham asked again, quietly and firmly.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you want for them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m not so clever as to hide things from you, even if I wanted to,\u201d I answered, feeling my face grow warm. \u201cI do want something. Miss Havisham, if you would spare the money to do my friend Herbert a lasting good in life\u2014something that, because of the circumstances, must be done without his knowledge\u2014I could show you how.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy does it have to be done without his knowledge?\u201d she asked. She rested her hands on her stick so she could look at me even more carefully.\\n\\n\u201cBecause,\u201d I said, \u201cI started the work myself more than two years ago, without his knowledge. I don\u2019t want it to be ruined by being found out. I can\u2019t explain why I\u2019m unable to finish it. It\u2019s part of a secret that belongs to someone else, not to me.\u201d\\n\\nShe slowly looked away from me and stared into the fire. She watched it for what seemed like a long time, in the quiet and in the dim light of the candles that were burning down. Then, when some of the red coals collapsed, she turned back toward me. At first her expression was empty, but then her attention sharpened. All this time Estella kept knitting. When Miss Havisham finally fixed her eyes on me again, she spoke as if nothing had interrupted our conversation.\\n\\n\u201cWhat else?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEstella,\u201d I said, turning to her now, and trying to control my shaking voice, \u201cyou know I love you. You know I have loved you for a long time, and with all my heart.\u201d\\n\\nShe looked up at my face when I spoke to her. Her fingers kept working, and her expression stayed completely calm. I saw that Miss Havisham glanced from me to Estella, and then from Estella back to me.\\n\\n\u201cI should have said this sooner,\u201d I continued, \u201cbut I made a long mistake. It made me hope that Miss Havisham meant for us to be together. When I thought you couldn\u2019t help yourself, in a way, I held back. But I have to say it now.\u201d\\n\\nWith her face still unmoved, and her hands still busy, Estella shook her head.\\n\\n\u201cI know,\u201d I said, answering her. \u201cI have no hope that I will ever be able to call you mine, Estella.\u201d\"}"
