          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"39247346defac1f6e71a38d4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI swear,\u201d he cried, \u201cby the sun, by the blue sky above, and by the fire of love burning in my heart, that if you grant my request, as long as they live you will never see me again. Go home and begin your work. I will watch how things go with unbearable worry. Don\u2019t fear that I won\u2019t come when you are ready\u2014I will appear then.\u201d\\n\\nWith that, he suddenly left me. He seemed afraid that my feelings might change. I watched him hurry down the mountain faster than an eagle could fly, and soon I lost sight of him among the shifting waves of the sea of ice.\\n\\nHis story took up the entire day, and the sun was already near the horizon when he left. I knew I should hurry down toward the valley, because darkness would soon surround me. But my heart was heavy, and my steps were slow. I had to work my way along the small paths of the mountain, carefully placing my feet, and that effort kept distracting me, even though I was still full of the emotions the day\u2019s events had stirred in me. \\n\\nIt was well into the night when I reached the halfway resting place. I sat down beside the fountain. The stars shone here and there as the clouds moved past them. Dark pines stood all around, and now and then a broken tree lay on the ground. The whole scene felt deeply solemn, and it brought strange thoughts to my mind. I cried bitterly. Clasping my hands in agony, I said, \u201cOh, stars and clouds and winds\u2014you are all about to mock me. If you truly pity me, crush my feelings and my memories. Let me become nothing. But if you don\u2019t, go\u2014go\u2014and leave me in darkness.\u201d\\n\\nThese were wild and miserable thoughts, but I can\u2019t explain to you how the endless twinkling of the stars weighed on me, or how I listened to every gust of wind as if it were a harsh, ugly sirocco, coming to destroy me.\\n\\nMorning came before I reached the village of Chamounix. I didn\u2019t rest at all. I went straight back to Geneva. Even inside my own heart, I couldn\u2019t put my feelings into words. They pressed on me with the weight of a mountain, and the sheer force of them crushed my suffering beneath it. So I returned home and went into the house to face my family. My haggard, wild look made them fear for me, but I answered no questions and hardly spoke. I felt as if I were under some kind of curse\u2014as if I had no right to ask for their sympathy\u2014as if I would never again be able to enjoy their company.\"}"
