          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"221675c253aa7aad1e9c8aeb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I don\u2019t suddenly rush at him and risk making a clumsy mess of it. No, I move off to one side, and I build up to it slowly, so he never even suspects I\u2019m going to hit him. Then, all at once, in a flash, he\u2019s flat on his back, and he can\u2019t figure out how it happened. That\u2019s how I went for brother Dowley. I started talking in a lazy, easy way, like I was just passing the time. The oldest man in the world couldn\u2019t have guessed where I was going to end up, just by looking at where I began.\\n\\n\u201cBoys, there are a lot of curious things about law, and custom, and the way people do things, when you really look at it. And there\u2019s the way human opinion and human movement drift and change, too. There are written laws\u2014they don\u2019t last. But there are also unwritten laws\u2014those are eternal. Take the unwritten law of wages. It says wages have to rise, little by little, straight through the centuries. Look at how it works. We know what wages are now, in one place and another. We average it out and call that today\u2019s wages. We also know what wages were a hundred years ago, and what they were two hundred years ago. That\u2019s as far back as we can go, but it\u2019s enough to show the law of progress\u2014the pace and rate of the steady increase. So even without any document, we can figure out pretty closely what wages were three, four, and five hundred years ago. So far, so good. Do we stop there? No. We stop looking backward and turn to the future and apply the same law.\\n\\n\u201cMy friends, I can tell you what people\u2019s wages will be at any date in the future you want to name, for hundreds and hundreds of years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat, goodman, what!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. In seven hundred years wages will be six times what they are now, in your region, and farm hands will be allowed 3 cents a day, and mechanics 6.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI