          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eeca9762cb021c6a9f5d596f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After I had sent them in, I couldn\u2019t stay away from the places where they were, but I felt as if they were more hopeful and less desperate when I was near them. In that unreasonable restlessness and mental pain, I would roam the streets in the evening, wandering past the offices and houses where I had left the petitions. Even now, the weary western streets of London on a cold, dusty spring night\u2014those rows of stern, shut-up mansions and the long lines of lamps\u2014are melancholy to me because of that.\\n\\nThe daily visits I could make him were shortened now, and he was kept more strictly. Thinking, or imagining, that I was suspected of intending to carry poison to him, I asked to be searched before I sat down at his bedside, and told the officer who was always there that I was willing to do anything that would assure him of the single-mindedness of my intentions. Nobody was harsh with him or with me. There was duty to be done, and it was done, but not cruelly. The officer always gave me the same assurance: that he was worse, and that some other sick prisoners in the room, and some other prisoners who attended on them as sick nurses\u2014malefactors, but not incapable of kindness, God be thanked!\u2014were joining in the same report.\\n\\nAs the days went on, I noticed more and more that he would lie quietly, staring at the white ceiling, with an absence of light in his face until some word of mine brightened it for an instant, and then it would fade again. Sometimes he was almost, or quite, unable to speak; then he would answer me with slight pressure on my hand, and I came to understand his meaning very well.\\n\\nThe number of days had risen to ten, when I saw a greater change in him than I had yet. His eyes were turned toward the door, and they lit up as I entered.\\n\\n\u201cDear boy,\u201d he said, as I sat down by his bed, \u201cI thought you was late. But I knowed you couldn\u2019t be that.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is just the time,\u201d said I. \u201cI waited for it at the gate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou always waits at the gate; don\u2019t you, dear boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. Not to lose a moment of the time.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank\u2019ee, dear boy, thank\u2019ee. God bless you! You\u2019ve never deserted me, dear boy.\u201d\\n\\nI pressed his hand in silence, because I couldn\u2019t forget that I had once meant to desert him.\\n\\n\u201cAnd what\u2019s the best of all,\u201d he said, \u201cyou\u2019ve been more comfortable alonger me, since I was under a dark cloud, than when the sun shone.\u201d\"}"
