          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aa18eb701f97ab63c98fe862\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThey should interest you, Dorian. Every gentleman cares about his good name. You don\u2019t want people to talk about you as if you were vile and ruined. Of course, you have your position, your wealth, and all that sort of thing. But position and wealth aren\u2019t everything. Still, I don\u2019t believe these rumors at all. At least, I can\u2019t believe them when I look at you. Sin shows itself on a man\u2019s face. It can\u2019t be hidden. People sometimes talk about secret bad habits, but there\u2019s no such thing. If a miserable man has a vice, it appears in the way his mouth looks, in the droop of his eyelids, and even in the shape of his hands.\\n\\nSomeone\u2014I'm not going to say his name, but you know him\u2014came to me last year to have his portrait painted. I had never met him before, and I hadn\u2019t heard anything about him at the time, though I\u2019ve heard plenty since. He offered an outrageous amount of money. I refused him. There was something about the shape of his fingers that I couldn\u2019t stand. Now I know I was right about what I suspected. His life is dreadful. But you, Dorian, with your clean, bright, innocent face, and your wonderful, untroubled youth\u2014I can\u2019t believe anything bad about you.\\n\\nAnd yet I see you so rarely, and you never come down to the studio anymore. When I\u2019m away from you, and I hear all these awful things people are whispering about you, I don\u2019t know what to say. Why does the Duke of Berwick leave a club room as soon as you walk in? Why do so many gentlemen in London refuse to visit your house or invite you to theirs? You used to be friends with Lord Staveley. I saw him at dinner last week. Your name came up in conversation, because of the miniatures you lent to the exhibition at the Dudley. Staveley curled his lip and said you might have the most artistic tastes, but that you were a man no decent girl should be allowed to know, and no pure woman should sit in the same room with.\\n\\nI told him I was your friend, and I asked what he meant. He told me. He said it plainly, right in front of everyone. It was terrible! Why is your friendship so dangerous for young men? There was that poor boy in the Guards who killed himself. You were his closest friend. Then there was Sir Henry Ashton, who had to leave England because his name was stained.\"}"
