          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3afd6d7aef37544f08505830\",\"rewritten_text\":\"How long I stayed in that state, I can\u2019t say. It must, however, have been no small amount of time, because when I partially regained the sense of being alive, I saw that day was breaking, the balloon hovering at an enormous height above a wilderness of ocean, and not a trace of land anywhere within the vast horizon. Yet when I came back to myself, my feelings were by no means as full of agony as I might have expected. In fact, there was a kind of budding madness in the calm way I began to survey my situation. I raised each of my hands to my eyes, one after the other, and wondered what could have caused my veins to swell and my fingernails to turn so horribly black. After that, I examined my head carefully, shaking it again and again and feeling it with minute attention, until I was satisfied it was not\u2014something I\u2019d half suspected\u2014larger than my balloon. Then, in a knowing manner, I reached into both pockets of my breeches; finding that a set of tablets and a toothpick case were missing, I tried to account for their disappearance, and when I couldn\u2019t, I felt an indescribable chagrin.\\n\\nIt now occurred to me that I was in great uneasiness in the joint of my left ankle, and a dim awareness of my condition began to come back into my mind. But, strangely enough, I was neither astonished nor horrified. If I felt any emotion at all, it was a kind of chuckling satisfaction at the cleverness I was about to show in getting myself out of this mess, and I never, for a moment, treated my ultimate safety as something in doubt. For a few minutes I remained wrapped in the deepest meditation. I distinctly remember repeatedly compressing my lips, putting my forefinger to the side of my nose, and making other gestures and grimaces that men make when they\u2019re comfortable in their armchairs, meditating on matters of intricacy or importance. Once I thought I had gathered 