          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f2b2fa15f09acfeb10248fe9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her color changed, and she said no more.\\n\\nMrs. Gardiner was standing a little behind; and when he paused, he asked her if she would do him the honor of introducing him to her friends. It was a moment of politeness she was completely unprepared for, and she could hardly keep from smiling at the fact that he was now trying to meet some of the very people against whom his pride had revolted\u2014especially in his offer to herself. \u201cWhat will be his surprise,\u201d she thought, \u201cwhen he learns who they are! He takes them for people of fashion now.\u201d\\n\\nThe introduction, however, was made at once; and as she named their relationship to herself, she glanced at him to see how he handled it, expecting that he would quickly retreat from such disgraceful companions. That he was surprised by the connection was clear; still, he held his ground with fortitude. Instead of leaving, he turned back with them and joined in conversation with Mr. Gardiner. Elizabeth couldn\u2019t help being pleased, couldn\u2019t help feeling triumphant. It was comforting that he should know she had some relatives for whom there was no need to be ashamed. She listened with the greatest attention to everything that passed between them, and took pride in every expression, every sentence from her uncle, which showed his intelligence, his taste, or his good manners.\\n\\nSoon the conversation turned to fishing; and she heard Mr. Darcy invite him, with the greatest civility, to fish there as often as he liked while he remained in the neighborhood. At the same time, he offered to supply him with fishing tackle and pointed out the parts of the stream where there was usually the best sport. Mrs. Gardiner, who was walking arm in arm with Elizabeth, gave her a look full of wonder. Elizabeth said nothing, but it pleased her greatly; the compliment had to be meant for her. Her astonishment, though, was intense, and she kept repeating to herself, \u201cWhy is he so different? What could have caused it? It can\u2019t be for me, it can\u2019t be for my sake that his manners have become so gentle. My reproaches at Hunsford couldn\u2019t have brought about a change like this. It\u2019s impossible that he still loves me.\u201d\\n\\nAfter they had walked some time in this way, the two ladies in front and the two gentlemen behind, when they resumed their places after descending to the riverbank to inspect some curious water-plant more closely, there was a small change. It began with Mrs. Gardiner, who, tired from the morning\u2019s exercise, found Elizabeth\u2019s arm insufficient for her support and therefore preferred her husband\u2019s.\"}"
