          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fc2184062da224552bc8ca69\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mary never wrote to Bath herself; all the work of maintaining a slow, disappointing correspondence with Elizabeth fell on Anne.\\n\\nBreakfast had barely ended when Captain and Mrs. Harville and Captain Benwick joined them. They had arranged to take their last walk around Lyme together. They ought to be leaving for Uppercross by one, and in the meantime they were to be all together, outdoors as long as they could.\\n\\nAnne noticed Captain Benwick coming close to her as soon as they were properly out in the street. The conversation the evening before had not discouraged him from seeking her again. They walked together for some time, talking as before about Mr. Scott and Lord Byron, and still as unable as before\u2014unable as any other two readers\u2014to judge the merits of either in exactly the same way, until something happened that caused almost everyone in their group to shift. Instead of Captain Benwick, Captain Harville was now walking beside her.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Elliot,\u201d said he, speaking rather softly, \u201cyou\u2019ve done a good deed by getting that poor fellow to talk so much. I only wish he could have company like that more often. I know it\u2019s bad for him to be shut up as he is; but what can we do? We can\u2019t separate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI can easily believe that would be impossible. But in time\u2014perhaps. We know what time does in every kind of affliction. And you must remember, Captain Harville, that your friend may yet be called a young mourner. Only last summer, I understand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, yes\u2014true enough,\u201d he said, with a deep sigh. \u201cOnly June.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd maybe not known to him so soon.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot until the first week of August, when he came home from the Cape\u2014just made into the Grappler. I was at Plymouth, dreading to hear about him. He did send letters, but the Grappler was under orders for Portsmouth. There the news had to follow him, but who was to tell it? Not I. I would as soon have been hauled up to the yard-arm. Nobody could do it, but that good fellow\u201d (pointing to Captain Wentworth). \u201cThe Laconia had come into Plymouth the week before; there was no danger of her being sent to sea again. He had his chance for the rest, wrote for leave of absence, but without waiting for the return, traveled night and day until he reached Portsmouth. Then he rowed straight off to the Grappler and never left the poor fellow for a week. That\u2019s what he did, and nobody else could have saved poor James. You may think, Miss Elliot, whether he is dear to us!\u201d\\n\\nAnne did think about it with complete certainty, and said as much in reply as her own feelings could manage, or as his seemed able to bear. But he was too deeply affected to bring the subject up again, and when he spoke once more, it was about something entirely different.\"}"
