          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"71f4b939dad9ddb3fa32466e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her face changed, and she said no more.\\n\\nMrs. Gardiner stood a little behind her. When she paused, Mr. Gardiner asked her if she would do him the honor of introducing him to her friends. It was a polite gesture he had not prepared her for. She could hardly stop herself from smiling at the fact that he was now trying to meet some of the very people against whom his pride had once rebelled, even as he offered her his attention. \u201cWhat will be his surprise,\u201d she thought, \u201cwhen he finds out who they are! He still thinks they are people of fashion.\u201d\\n\\nThe introductions were made right away. As she named their relationship to herself, Elizabeth watched him out of the corner of her eye to see how he handled it. She expected him to pull away as quickly as he could from such embarrassing companions. That he was surprised by the connection was clear, but he managed it with steadiness. Instead of leaving, he turned back with them and joined Mr. Gardiner in conversation. Elizabeth could not help feeling pleased, could not help feeling triumphant. It was comforting to know that he had relations she did not have to be ashamed of. She listened carefully to everything they said and took pride in every remark from her uncle\u2014every sign of his intelligence, his taste, or his good manners.\\n\\nSoon the talk turned to fishing. Elizabeth heard Mr. Darcy invite him, with the greatest politeness, to fish there as often as he liked while he stayed nearby. At the same time, he offered to provide him with fishing tackle and pointed out the parts of the stream where there was usually the best sport. Mrs. Gardiner, who was walking arm in arm with Elizabeth, looked at her with clear surprise. Elizabeth said nothing, but it pleased her greatly. The compliment had to be meant for her.\\n\\nHer astonishment, however, was intense. Again and again she repeated to herself, \u201cWhy has he changed so much? What could have caused it? It can\u2019t be for me. It can\u2019t be because of my sake that his manners have become so gentle. My remarks at Hunsford could not have brought about a change like this. It\u2019s impossible that he still loves me.\u201d\\n\\nAfter they had been walking like this for some time, the two ladies in front and the two gentlemen behind resumed their places after they had gone down to the river\u2019s edge to inspect a curious water-plant more closely. Then something small changed. It started with Mrs. Gardiner. Tired from the morning\u2019s exercise, she found Elizabeth\u2019s arm was not enough to support her, so she preferred her husband\u2019s instead.\"}"
