          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d235c4c69d76e307d60551b5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On that point, then, I will be completely silent. And you can be sure that no unfair criticism will ever leave my lips once we are married.\u201d\\n\\nIt was absolutely necessary to interrupt him now.\\n\\n\u201cYou are moving too fast, sir,\u201d she said. \u201cYou forget that I have not answered yet. Let me do it right away. Thank you for the compliment you are giving me. I understand the honor of your proposal, but I cannot do anything other than refuse it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am not supposed to be learning,\u201d replied Mr. Collins, making a formal gesture, \u201cthat it is common for young ladies to turn down the man they secretly plan to accept, when he first asks for their favor. And that sometimes they refuse him again a second time, or even a third. So I am not discouraged by what you have just said, and I will hope to bring you to the altar soon.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cReally, sir,\u201d Elizabeth said, \u201cyour hope is rather surprising after what I have just said. I assure you I am not one of those young ladies, if any such young ladies exist, who are bold enough to gamble their happiness on the chance that they will be asked a second time. I mean my refusal. You could not make me happy, and I am sure I am the last woman in the world who would make you happy. And if Lady Catherine were to know me, I believe she would find me, in every way, unfit for the position.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf it were certain that Lady Catherine would think so,\u201d said Mr. Collins, very gravely\u2014\u201cbut I cannot imagine that her Ladyship would disapprove of you at all. And you may be certain that when I have the honor of seeing her again, I will speak of your modesty, your economy, and your other pleasant qualities in the highest terms.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Collins,\u201d Elizabeth said, \u201cpraise of me is not needed. You should let me judge for myself, and you should believe what I say. I wish you to be very happy and very rich, and by refusing your hand, I will do everything I can to make sure you are not otherwise. In offering me your hand, you must have considered the delicacy of your feelings toward my family, and you may take possession of the Longbourn estate whenever it becomes yours, without any self-reproach. So this matter may be considered finally settled.\u201d\\n\\nAs she spoke, she rose to leave the room, but Mr. Collins addressed her again.\\n\\n\u201cWhen I have the honor of speaking to you about this again,\u201d he said, \u201cI hope to receive a more favorable answer than the one you have given me now. I am not accusing you of cruelty at present, though, because I know it is the usual custom for your sex to reject a man on his first request. And perhaps you have even now said as much to encourage my suit, in a way that would still fit the true delicacy expected of women.\u201d\"}"
