          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"12f0e2ecabbbf6a638f7ee5b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If I can\u2019t live on that, I won\u2019t ask anyone to give me a lift. Is it satisfactory?\u201d\\n\\nThere was a tremendous roar of applause, and out of it the king\u2019s voice rose, saying:\\n\\n\u201cTake off his bonds and set him free! Pay him homage, high and low, rich and poor, for he has become the king\u2019s right hand\u2014clothed with power and authority, and seated on the highest step of the throne! Now sweep away this creeping night, and bring back the light and cheer, so that all the world may bless thee.\u201d\\n\\nBut I said:\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s nothing if a common man is shamed before the world; but it would be an insult to the king if anyone who saw his minister naked should not also see him delivered from his shame. If I may ask that my clothes be brought back\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey won\u2019t do,\u201d the king cut in. \u201cFetch clothing of another kind; dress him like a prince!\u201d\\n\\nMy plan worked. I wanted to keep things as they were until the eclipse was total; otherwise they would keep trying to get me to dismiss the darkness, and of course I couldn\u2019t. Sending for the clothes bought me some time, but not enough. So I had to come up with another excuse. I said it would be natural for the king to change his mind and regret, at least to some extent, what he\u2019d done in the heat of the moment; therefore I would let the darkness grow for a while, and if, at the end of a reasonable time, the king had kept his mind the same, then the darkness should be dismissed. Neither the king nor anyone else was happy with that arrangement, but I had to stick to it.\\n\\nIt grew darker and darker, blacker and blacker, while I wrestled with those awkward sixth-century clothes. At last it was pitch dark, and the crowd groaned with horror as they felt the cold, uncanny night breezes sweep through the place and saw the stars come out and twinkle in the sky. Finally the eclipse was total, and I was very glad of it, but everyone else was miserable\u2014which was only natural. I said:\\n\\n\u201cThe king, by his silence, still stands by the terms.\u201d Then I lifted up my hands\u2014held them just so for a moment\u2014and then, with the most awful solemnity, I said, \u201cLet the enchantment dissolve and pass harmless away!\u201d\\n\\nFor a moment there was no response, in that deep darkness and that graveyard hush. But when the silver edge of the sun pushed itself out a moment or two later, the whole assembly erupted with a vast shout and came pouring down like a flood to smother me with blessings and gratitude; and Clarence was not the last of the crowd, of course.\u201d}\"}"
