          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2721ed9389989cd2fef98151\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStory!\u201d\\n\\nHe seemed determined to misunderstand the word she had just repeated, and then, with a quickness that betrayed him, he went on: \u201cYes\u2014customers. In the banking trade we commonly name the connection we have our customers. He was a French gentleman; a scientific gentleman; a man of very fine education\u2014a Doctor.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot of Beauvais?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, yes, of Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, the gentleman was from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, he was a person of standing in Paris. I had the honour of knowing him there. Our dealings were business dealings, but kept confidential. At that time I was in our French House, and I had been\u2014oh!\u2014twenty years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt that time\u2014may I ask, at what time, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI mean, miss, twenty years ago. He married\u2014an English lady\u2014and I served as one of the trustees. His affairs, as with those of many other French gentlemen and French families, were entirely managed by Tellson\u2019s. In a like manner I am, or have been, trustee of one sort or another for scores of our customers. These are only business relations, miss; there\u2019s no friendship in them, no special concern, nothing that can be called sentiment. I move from one to another in the course of my work, just as I move from one customer to another during the day; in short, I have no feelings\u2014I\u2019m nothing but a machine. To continue\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut this is my father\u2019s story, sir; and I begin to think\u201d\u2014the oddly furrowed brow was fixed on him with a kind of earnestness\u2014\u201cthat when I was left an orphan, my mother surviving my father by only two years, it was you who brought me to England. I\u2019m nearly certain it was you.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Lorry took the small, hesitating hand that had come forward so trustingly to take his, and with some ceremony pressed it to his lips. Then he guided the young lady at once back to her chair. Keeping the chair-back steady with his left hand, and with his right he alternated between rubbing his chin, tugging his wig at the ears, or indicating what he meant, he stood looking down at her while she sat looking up.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Manette, it was I. And you will see how truly I spoke of myself just now, when I said I had no feelings, and that all the relations I keep with my fellow-creatures are mere business relations, when you consider that I have never seen you since. No\u2014you have been the ward of Tellson\u2019s House, and I have been occupied with the other business of Tellson\u2019s House besides. Feelings! I have no time for them, no opportunity for them. I spend my whole life, miss, turning an immense pecuniary Mangle.\u201d\"}"
