          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ca826e1c8486a00f818f4655\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Tom woke up in the morning, he couldn\u2019t help wondering where he was. He sat up, rubbed his eyes, and looked around. Then it all clicked. It was a cool gray dawn, and there was a wonderful feeling of rest and peace in the deep, steady calm and silence of the woods. Not a leaf moved; not a sound intruded on great Nature\u2019s quiet thinking. Dewdrops beaded on the leaves and grasses. A white layer of ashes covered the fire, and a thin blue ribbon of smoke rose straight into the air. Joe and Huck were still asleep.\\n\\nNow, far out in the woods, a bird called; another answered; and soon the hammering of a woodpecker could be heard. Little by little, the cool dim gray of morning brightened, and little by little sounds multiplied and life showed itself. The wonder of Nature shaking off sleep and getting to work unfolded before the thoughtful boy. A small green worm crawled over a dewy leaf, lifting about two-thirds of its body into the air now and then and \u201csniffing around,\u201d then going on again\u2014because, Tom said, it was measuring. When the worm came toward him, he stayed as still as stone, his hopes rising and falling in turn as the creature kept coming closer or seemed ready to go somewhere else. And when, at last, it decided on a painful moment with its curved body held up, then came decisively down onto Tom\u2019s leg and began to travel over him, his whole heart was glad\u2014because that meant he was going to get a new suit of clothes, without any doubt a flashy pirate uniform.\\n\\nThen, out of nowhere, a line of ants appeared and went about their work. One struggled bravely with a dead spider five times its size, carrying it straight up a tree trunk. A brown-spotted ladybug climbed to the dizzy height of a grass blade, and Tom leaned down close to it and said, \u201cLady-bug, lady-bug, fly away home, your house is on fire, your children\u2019s alone,\u201d and she took off to go check on it. That didn\u2019t surprise Tom, because he\u2019d known for a long time that this insect was gullible about fires, and he\u2019d tested her on her simplicity more than once. Next came a tumblebug, rolling along with steady effort, pushing its ball. Tom touched it, just to see it tuck its legs in against its body and pretend to be dead.\\n\\nThe birds were really going at it by then. A catbird\u2014the Northern mocker\u2014landed in a tree over Tom\u2019s head and sang out her imitations of her neighbors with pure delight. Then a shrill jay swooped down: a flash of blue, and it stopped on a twig almost within the boy\u2019s reach. It tilted its head to one side, eyeing the strangers with intense curiosity. A gray squirrel and a big \u201cfox\u201d kind of fellow came skittering along, sitting up at intervals to inspect and chatter at the boys, because the wild animals probably had never seen a human being before and barely knew whether they should be afraid.\"}"
