          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a4e74aadcc7041a96c285098\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cCharles Evr\u00e9monde, called Darnay,\u201d was finally brought before the court.\\n\\nHis judges sat on the Bench in feathered hats; but the rough red cap and tricolored cockade were the headgear otherwise most commonly worn. Looking at the Jury and the restless crowd, he might have thought the usual order of things had been turned upside down, and that the criminals were putting the innocent on trial. The lowest, cruelest, and worst people in the city\u2014never without their share of low, cruel, and bad\u2014were the driving force of the whole scene: loudly commenting, applauding, disapproving, anticipating, and pushing the outcome forward, with no restraint. Most of the men were armed in one way or another; among the women, some wore knives, some daggers; some ate and drank as they watched, while many knitted. Among those knitting was one who kept a spare piece of knitting under her arm as she worked. She sat in the front row, beside a man he had never seen since his arrival at the Barrier, but whom he remembered at once as Defarge. He noticed that she leaned toward him once or twice to whisper, and that she seemed to be his wife; but what he noticed most about the two figures was that, though they were posted as close to himself as they could manage, they never looked toward him. They seemed to be waiting for something with stubborn determination, watching the Jury but nothing else.\\n\\nUnder the President sat Doctor Manette, in his usual quiet dress. As far as the prisoner could see, he and Mr. Lorry were the only men there who were not connected with the Tribunal, and who wore their ordinary clothes, without having put on the coarse garb of the Carmagnole.\\n\\nCharles Evr\u00e9monde, called Darnay, was accused by the public prosecutor as an emigrant, whose life was forfeit to the Republic under the decree that banished all emigrants on pain of death. It did not matter that the decree was dated since his return to France. The decree was there, and he was there; he had been taken in France, and his head was demanded.\\n\\n\u201cTake off his head!\u201d the audience cried. \u201cAn enemy to the Republic!\u201d\\n\\nThe President rang his bell to silence those cries, and asked the prisoner whether it was not true that he had lived many years in England.\\n\\nUndoubtedly it was.\\n\\nThen wasn\u2019t he an emigrant? What did he call himself?\\n\\nNot an emigrant, he hoped, in the sense and spirit of the law.\\n\\nWhy not? the President asked.\\n\\nBecause he had voluntarily given up a title that was distasteful to him, and a position that was distasteful to him, and had left his country\u2014he said, before the word emigrant in the Tribunal\u2019s current meaning was even used\u2014to live by his own labor in England, rather than on the labor of the overburdened people of France.\"}"
