          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6794a005c941c6266960fdda\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I don\u2019t suddenly spring at him and risk turning it into a clumsy, half-finished mess; no, I move away over there to one side, and I work up on him slowly, so he never suspects I\u2019m going to hit him at all. Then, little by little, all at once, he\u2019s flat on his back, and he can\u2019t, for the life of him, figure out how it all happened. That\u2019s how I went for brother Dowley. I started talking lazily and comfortably, like I was just passing the time; and the oldest man in the world couldn\u2019t have taken the bearings of where I began and guessed where I was going to end up:\\n\\n\u201cBoys, there are a lot of curious things about law, and custom, and usage, and all that kind of thing, once you really look at it; yes, and about the drift and progress of human opinion and movement, too. There are written laws\u2014they perish; but there are also unwritten laws\u2014_they_ are eternal. Take the unwritten law of wages: it says they\u2019ve got to advance, little by little, straight through the centuries. And notice how it works. We know what wages are now, here and there and yonder; we take an average and say that\u2019s what wages are today. We know what wages were a hundred years ago, and what they were two hundred years ago; that\u2019s as far back as we can get, but it\u2019s enough to show us the law of progress, the measure and rate of the steady increase. And so, without any document to help us, we can come pretty close to determining what wages were three, four, and five hundred years ago. All right, so far. Do we stop there? No. We stop looking backward; we turn around and apply the law to the future. My friends, I can tell you what people\u2019s wages are going to be at any date in the future you want to know, for hundreds and hundreds of years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat, goodman, what!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. In seven hundred years wages will have risen to six times what they are now, here in your region, and farm hands will be allowed 3 cents a day, and mechanics 6.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI wouldn\u2019t I might die now and live then!\u201d Smug, the wheelwright, interrupted, with a fine, greedy glow in his eye.\\n\\n\u201cAnd that isn\u2019t all; they\u2019ll get their board too\u2014such as it is: it won\u2019t bloat them. Two hundred and fifty years later\u2014pay attention now\u2014a mechanic\u2019s wages will be\u2014mind you, this is law, not guesswork; a mechanic\u2019s wages will then be _twenty_ cents a day!\u201d\\n\\nThere was a general gasp of awed astonishment; Dickon the mason murmured, with raised eyes and hands:\"}"
