          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7d99a986bb667b7f1e1a263a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They were bringing along a very nice-looking old gentleman and a nice-looking younger one, with his right arm in a sling. And, my souls, how the people yelled and laughed, and kept it up. But I didn\u2019t see any joke in it, and I figured it would strain the duke and the king to have to watch. I expected they\u2019d turn pale. But no\u2014none of them turned pale. The duke didn\u2019t let on that he suspected anything; he just kept going on and on, happy and satisfied, like a jug that\u2019s gurgling out buttermilk. As for the king, he just stared and stared down at the newcomers, looking so sorrowful, as if it gave him a stomachache right in his heart to think there could be such frauds and rascals in the world. Oh, he did it admirably. A lot of the important people gathered around the king so he could see they were on his side. The old gentleman who had just arrived looked completely baffled. Pretty soon he started to speak, and I could tell right away he pronounced like an Englishman\u2014not the king\u2019s way, though the king\u2019s was pretty good as an imitation. I can\u2019t give the old man\u2019s exact words, and I can\u2019t imitate him either, but he turned to the crowd and said something like this:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a surprise to me that I wasn\u2019t expecting; and I\u2019ll admit, candid and frank, I\u2019m not very well prepared to answer it. My brother and I have had misfortunes. He\u2019s broken his arm, and our baggage was put off at a town above here last night, by mistake. I am Peter Wilks\u2019 brother, Harvey, and this is his brother William, who can\u2019t hear or speak\u2014and can\u2019t even make signs that amount to much, since he\u2019s only got one hand to work them with. We are who we say we are; and in a day or two, when I get the baggage, I can prove it. But until then I won\u2019t say anything more\u2014just go to the hotel and wait.\u201d\\n\\nSo he and the new dummy started off. And the king laughed and talked on:\\n\\n\u201cBroke his arm\u2014_very_ likely, isn\u2019t it?\u2014and very convenient, too, for a fraud who has to make signs and hasn\u2019t learned how. Lost their baggage! That\u2019s _mighty_ good\u2014and mighty ingenious\u2014under the _circumstances_!\u201d\\n\\nSo he laughed again, and everyone else did too, except three or four, or maybe half a dozen. One of them was that doctor. Another was a sharp-looking gentleman with an old-fashioned carpet-bag, who had just come off the steamboat and was talking to him in a low voice, glancing toward the king now and then and nodding his head\u2014it was Levi Bell, the lawyer who had gone up to Louisville. And another was a big, rough, husky fellow who came along and listened to everything the old gentleman said, and was listening to the king too.\"}"
