          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b16c3a78fc9a0ca61858a605\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No letter of condolence had been sent to Ireland. The neglect had been visited on the head of the sinner; for when poor Lady Elliot died herself, no letter of condolence was received at Kellynch, and, consequently, there was far too much reason to fear that the Dalrymples considered the relationship ended. The question was how to set this anxious business right, and be admitted as cousins again\u2014and it was a question which, in a more sensible way, neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot thought unimportant. \u201cFamily connections are always worth keeping, and good company is always worth seeking. Lady Dalrymple has taken a house for three months in Laura Place, and will be living in style. She was at Bath the year before, and Lady Russell has heard her spoken of as a charming woman. It\u2019s very desirable that the connection should be renewed, if it can be done, without any compromise of propriety on the Elliots\u2019 side.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter, however, would choose his own method, and at last wrote a very fine letter\u2014full of explanation, regret, and entreaty\u2014to his right-honourable cousin. Neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot could admire the letter; but it did what was needed, bringing three lines of scrawl from the Dowager Viscountess. \u201cShe was very much honoured, and should be happy in their acquaintance.\u201d The hard part of the business was over; the pleasant part began. They visited in Laura Place. They had the Dowager Viscountess Dalrymple\u2019s cards, and those of the Honourable Miss Carteret, to be arranged wherever they might be most visible: and \u201cOur cousins in Laura Place\u201d\u2014\u201cOur cousin, Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret\u201d\u2014were talked about to everyone.\\n\\nAnne was ashamed. Even if Lady Dalrymple and her daughter had been agreeable, she would still have been ashamed of the agitation they caused; but they were nothing. There was no superiority of manner, accomplishment, or understanding. Lady Dalrymple had earned the reputation of \u201ca charming woman\u201d only because she had a smile and a civil answer for everyone. Miss Carteret, with even less to say, was so plain and so awkward that she would never have been tolerated in Camden Place, except for her birth.\\n\\nLady Russell admitted she had expected something better; but still, \u201cit\u2019s an acquaintance worth having.\u201d And when Anne ventured to share her opinion of them with Mr Elliot, he agreed that they were nothing in themselves, but still insisted that, as a family connection, as good company, and as people who would draw good company around them, they had their value. Anne smiled and said,\\n\\n\u201cMy idea of good company, Mr Elliot, is the company of clever, well-informed people, who have a great deal of conversation; that is what I call good company.\u201d\"}"
