          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e9894fa59290d3b939a32989\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You\u2019ve raised an artificial soul and spirit in him, ma\u2019am\u2014something that doesn\u2019t suit a person of his condition. As the board, Mrs. Sowerberry, who are practical philosophers, will tell you. What do paupers have to do with soul or spirit? It\u2019s quite enough that we let them have living bodies. If you\u2019d kept the boy on gruel, ma\u2019am, this would never have happened.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear, dear!\u201d ejaculated Mrs. Sowerberry, piously lifting her eyes to the kitchen ceiling. \u201cThis comes of being liberal!\u201d\\n\\nMrs. Sowerberry\u2019s \u201cliberality\u201d toward Oliver had consisted of giving him, in generous measure, all the dirty odds and ends that nobody else would eat; so there was a great deal of meekness and self-sacrifice in her choosing to stay under Mr. Bumble\u2019s heavy accusation, of which, to do her justice, she was entirely innocent\u2014in thought, word, or deed.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Bumble, when the lady brought her eyes back down to earth. \u201cThe only thing that can be done now, that I know of, is to leave him in the cellar for a day or so, until he\u2019s a little starved down. Then take him out and keep him on gruel all through his apprenticeship. He comes of a bad family, excitable natures, Mrs. Sowerberry! Both the nurse and the doctor said that his mother made her way here, against difficulties and pain that would have killed any well-disposed woman, weeks before.\u201d\\n\\nAt this point in Mr. Bumble\u2019s speech, Oliver\u2014just hearing enough to know that something was being said about his mother\u2014started kicking again with such force that every other sound was drowned out. Sowerberry returned at that moment. Once Oliver\u2019s offense had been explained to him, with the exaggerations the ladies thought most likely to stir up his anger, he unlocked the cellar door in an instant and dragged his rebellious apprentice out by the collar.\\n\\nOliver\u2019s clothes had been torn by the beating he\u2019d received; his face was bruised and scratched; and his hair hung scattered over his forehead. The angry flush hadn\u2019t gone away, though. When he was pulled from his prison, he scowled boldly at Noah and looked completely unshaken.\\n\\n\u201cNow, you\u2019re a nice young fellow, aren\u2019t you?\u201d said Sowerberry, shaking Oliver and giving him a slap.\\n\\n\u201cHe called my mother names,\u201d Oliver replied.\\n\\n\u201cWell, what if he did, you little ungrateful wretch?\u201d said Mrs. Sowerberry. \u201cShe deserved what he said\u2014and worse.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe didn\u2019t,\u201d said Oliver.\\n\\n\u201cShe did,\u201d said Mrs. Sowerberry.\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s a lie!\u201d said Oliver.\\n\\nMrs. Sowerberry burst into a flood of tears.\\n\\nThat flood of tears left Mr. Sowerberry no choice. If he\u2019d hesitated for even a moment to punish Oliver most severely, it would have been clear to every experienced reader that he would have been\u2014according to all the precedents in disputes of matrimony established\u2014a brute, an unnatural husband, an insulting creature, a base imitation of a man, and various other agreeable characters too numerous to list within the limits of this chapter.\"}"
