          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"07b2f09395149dcfca8fb835\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mary never wrote to Bath herself. All the work of keeping up a slow, disappointing correspondence with Elizabeth fell to Anne.\\n\\nBreakfast had barely finished when Captain and Mrs Harville and Captain Benwick joined them. They had agreed to take their last walk around Lyme together. They were supposed to leave for Uppercross at one o\u2019clock, and until then they would all be together, outside as long as they could.\\n\\nAs soon as they were properly out in the street, Anne noticed Captain Benwick coming close to her. The conversation they had had the previous evening did not make him less eager to speak with her again. They walked together for some time, talking as before about Mr Scott and Lord Byron, and still unable, as before, to agree exactly on the merits of either\u2014at least, not in the same way. Then something happened that led to an almost general change among their group, and instead of Captain Benwick, Captain Harville was walking beside her.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Elliot,\u201d said he, speaking more quietly, \u201cyou have done a good deed by getting that poor fellow to talk so much. I wish he could have company like that more often. I know it is bad for him to be shut up as he is, but what can we do? We can\u2019t leave him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI can\u2019t believe that would be possible. But in time, perhaps. We all know what time can do for every kind of suffering. And you must remember, Captain Harville, that your friend may still be called a young mourner. Only last summer, I understand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, that\u2019s true enough,\u201d he said with a deep sigh. \u201cOnly June.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd maybe not known to him so soon.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot until the first week of August,\u201d he replied. \u201cHe came home from the Cape, and he was just made into the Grappler. I was in Plymouth, dreading to hear about him. He did send letters, but the Grappler was under orders for Portsmouth. There the news had to follow him, but who was supposed to tell it? Not I. I would as soon have been sent up to the yard-arm. Nobody could do it, except that good fellow\u201d (pointing to Captain Wentworth). \u201cThe Laconia had come into Plymouth the week before, so there was no danger of her being sent out to sea again. He had his chance for the rest. He wrote for leave of absence, but without waiting for the reply, he traveled night and day until he reached Portsmouth. Then he rowed straight off to the Grappler and never left the poor fellow for a week. That\u2019s what he did, and nobody else could have saved poor James. You may think, Miss Elliot, whether he is dear to us!\u201d\\n\\nAnne thought about it with complete certainty, and said as much as her own feelings could manage, and as his seemed able to bear. But he was too upset to bring the subject up again. When he spoke once more, it was about something entirely different.\"}"
