          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"504cb9fdf8199438377ecdae\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter Elliot, of Kellynch Hall, in Somersetshire, was a man who, for his own amusement, never picked up any book except the Baronetage. There he found something to occupy an idle hour, and comfort in a time of distress. There his mind was stirred into admiration and respect as he contemplated the limited surviving remnants of the earliest patents. And there, whenever unpleasant feelings came from domestic matters, they naturally turned into pity and contempt as he leafed through the almost endless creations of the last century. Even if every other page were useless, he could still read his own history with an interest that never failed. This was the page where the favorite volume always opened:\\n\\n\u201cELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL.\\n\\n\u201cWalter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married, July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq. of South Park, in the county of Gloucester, by which lady (who died 1800) he has issue Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a still-born son, November 5, 1789; Mary, born November 20, 1791.\u201d\\n\\nPrecisely the same paragraph had originally stood exactly as the printer set it down; but Sir Walter had improved it, adding\u2014so that he and his family would have the information\u2014these words after the date of Mary\u2019s birth: \u201cMarried, December 16, 1810, Charles, son and heir of Charles Musgrove, Esq. of Uppercross, in the county of Somerset,\u201d and by inserting with the greatest accuracy the day of the month on which he had lost his wife.\\n\\nThen came the history and rise of the ancient and respectable family, in the usual terms: how it had first been established in Cheshire; how it was mentioned in Dugdale; how it had served as high sheriff; how it had represented a borough in three successive parliaments; how it had shown loyalty and dignity of baronetcy in the first year of Charles II, with all the Marys and Elizabeths they had married. Altogether it made two fine duodecimo pages, and ended with the arms and motto:\u2014\u201cPrincipal seat, Kellynch Hall, in the county of Somerset,\u201d and Sir Walter\u2019s handwriting again in the final line:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHeir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great grandson of the second Sir Walter.\u201d\\n\\nVanity was the beginning and the end of Sir Walter Elliot\u2019s character\u2014vanity of person and of position. He had been remarkably handsome in his youth, and at fifty-four he was still a very fine man. Few women could think more highly of their personal appearance than he did, nor could the valet of any newly made lord be more pleased with the place he held in society. He believed the blessing of beauty was inferior only to the blessing of a baronetcy; and Sir Walter Elliot, who had both, was the constant object of his warmest respect and devotion.\\n\\nHis good looks and his rank had a strong claim on his attachment, since to them he must have owed a wife of a character far superior to anything he could have deserved on his own.\"}"
