          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5cd715e448ebe6ca9a48ef7a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Col. Grangerford was a gentleman, plain and simple. He was a gentleman from head to toe, and so was his whole family. He was well born, as they say, and that counts for as much in a man as it does in a horse\u2014so the Widow Douglas liked to put it\u2014and nobody in our town ever argued with her, because she was, as she always claimed, among the very first sort of aristocracy. Pap said the same thing, too, though he wasn\u2019t any better quality than a mudcat himself.\\n\\nCol. Grangerford was mighty tall and mighty slim, with a darkish, pale look to his complexion\u2014no red showing anywhere. Every morning he was clean-shaven over his thin face, and his lips were the thinnest kind, and his nostrils the thinnest kind, with a high nose, heavy eyebrows, and eyes so black they seemed to sink back into caverns, like they were staring out from down in there at you, if you can picture it. His forehead ran high, and his hair was black and straight, falling down to his shoulders. His hands were long and thin, and every day of his life he put on a clean shirt and a full suit from top to bottom made of linen so white it hurt your eyes to look at it; and on Sundays he wore a blue tail-coat with brass buttons. He carried a mahogany cane with a silver head. There wasn\u2019t any foolishness about him at all, not a bit, and he wasn\u2019t ever loud. He was as kind as he could manage\u2014you could feel it, and that made you trust him. Sometimes he smiled, and it was pleasant to see; but when he stood up straight like a liberty-pole, and the lightning started to flicker out from under his eyebrows, you\u2019d want to climb a tree first and figure out what was wrong afterward. He never had to tell anybody to mind their manners\u2014everybody was always well behaved when he was around. People liked having him there, too; he was sunshine most of the time, only I mean he made it seem like good weather. When he turned into a cloudbank, it got awful dark for half a minute, and that was enough; nothing would go wrong again for a week.\\n\\nWhen he and the old lady came down in the morning, the whole family got up out of their chairs and said good-day to them, and nobody sat back down until they had sat down. Then Tom and Bob went to the sideboard where the decanter sat, mixed a glass of bitters, and handed it to him. He kept it in his hand and waited until Tom\u2019s and Bob\u2019s were mixed, and then they bowed and said, \u201cOur duty to you, sir, and madam;\u201d and they\u2014Tom and Bob\u2014bowed the smallest bit and said, \u201cThank you,\u201d and so the three of them drank. After that Bob and Tom poured a spoonful of water over the sugar and the little bit of whisky or apple brandy at the bottom of their tumblers, and gave it to me and Buck, and we drank to the old people, too.\"}"
