he marble floor, her head fallen to her chest, her hair disheveled, her fingers busy, one within the other. She was pale as marble, and every feature was tightened by agony. She saw me and looked up, asking without words; that half glance of hope turned to misery. The words died before I could form them. I felt a ghastly smile pull at my lips. She understood my gesture; her head fell again, and her fingers kept working restlessly. At last I found my voice, but it frightened her. The poor girl had understood my look, and for all the world she would not have wanted the story of her heavy misery shaped and confirmed by harsh, irreversible words. No\u2014she seemed to want to distract my thoughts from it. She rose from the floor and whispered, \u201cHush! After much weeping, Clara sleeps. We must not disturb her.\u201d\"}"
