          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6d862c1404233133dd163c4d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAnd do consider, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Oliver, with the tears pressing into his eyes in spite of every effort to keep them back. \u201cOh! think how young and how good she is, and how much pleasure and comfort she brings to everyone about her. I am sure\u2014quite sure\u2014no doubt at all\u2014that, for your sake, who are so kind yourself; and for her own; and for the happiness she makes for all\u2014she will not die. Heaven will never allow her to die so soon.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHush!\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, putting her hand on Oliver\u2019s head. \u201cYou speak like a child, poor boy. Still, you remind me of my duty. I had forgotten it for a moment, Oliver, but I hope I may be forgiven; I am old, and I have seen enough of illness and death to know the agony of parting from those we love. I have seen enough, too, to know that it is not always the youngest and best who are spared to those that cherish them; but this ought to comfort us in our grief. Heaven is just; and such lessons, in a way, teach us there is a brighter world than this, and that the road to it is swift. God\u2019s will be done! I love her\u2014and He knows how truly!\u201d\\n\\nOliver was startled to observe that, as Mrs. Maylie spoke these words, she checked her lamentations as if by a single effort; and, drawing herself up, she became composed and steady. He was even more astonished to learn that this steadiness did not fail her; for, amid all the care and watching that followed, Mrs. Maylie remained ready and collected\u2014carrying out every duty that had fallen to her, steadily, and, so far as anyone could see, even with cheerfulness. Yet Oliver was young, and did not understand what strong minds can endure when circumstances press hard. How could he, when those who possess such strength so rarely know their own power?\\n\\nAn anxious night passed. When morning came, Mrs. Maylie\u2019s fears were only too well confirmed. Rose had entered the first stage of a high and dangerous fever.\\n\\n\u201cWe must be active, Oliver, and not give way to useless grief,\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, touching her finger to her lip as she looked straight into his face. \u201cThis letter must be sent, with the greatest speed, to Mr. Losberne. It must be taken to the market-town\u2014which is no more than four miles away by the footpath across the field\u2014and then, from there, dispatched by an express on horseback straight to Chertsey. The people at the inn will manage it; and I can rely on you to see that it is done, I know.\u201d\\n\\nOliver had no answer to give, but he could not help showing how eager he was for it to be done at once.\"}"
