          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e578b03e5b2b9e8f34ad6d4c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Other chances of observing her could not fail to present themselves. Anne was soon thrown into the company of all four together often enough to form an opinion, though she was too discreet to own it at home, where she knew it would satisfy neither husband nor wife; for though she judged Louisa to be the favourite, she could not help thinking\u2014so far as she dared judge from what she remembered and had seen\u2014that Captain Wentworth was not in love with either. They were, indeed, more in love with him; but that was not love. It was rather a little fever of admiration, which might, and probably must, turn into love. Charles Hayter appeared to be conscious of being slighted; and yet Henrietta had sometimes an air of being split between them. Anne longed for the power of making them all understand what they were doing, and of showing them the mischief they were laying themselves open to. She did not suppose any of them acted from guile. It was her greatest comfort to believe that Captain Wentworth was in no way aware of the pain he was causing. There was no triumph\u2014no poor, pitiable triumph\u2014in his manner. He had, most likely, never heard, and certainly never considered, any claims of Charles Hayter. His fault lay only in accepting\u2014accepting must be the word\u2014the attentions of two young women at once.\\n\\nAfter a short struggle, however, Charles Hayter seemed to give up the contest. Three days went by without his coming once to Uppercross; a very decided alteration. He had even declined one of his usual invitations to dinner; and when Mr Musgrove found him on the occasion with several large books before him, Mr and Mrs Musgrove were persuaded that all could not be right, and talked gravely of his studying himself to death. Mary\u2019s hope and belief was that he had received a plain dismissal from Henrietta; and her husband lived in a constant expectation of seeing him the next day. Anne could only think that Charles Hayter was wise.\\n\\nOne morning, about this time, when Charles Musgrove and Captain Wentworth were out shooting together, the sisters in the Cottage sat quietly at their work, and were visited at the window by the sisters from the Mansion-house.\\n\\nIt was a very fine November day, and the Miss Musgroves came through the little grounds, stopping for no other purpose than to say that they were going to take a long walk, and therefore concluded Mary could not care to go with them. When Mary, at once answering\u2014somewhat with jealousy that she should not be thought a good walker\u2014\u201cOh, yes, I should like to join you very much; I am very fond of a long walk,\u201d Anne felt, from the looks of the two girls, that it was exactly what they did not wish. She admired again the kind of necessity which the family habits seemed to impose, of having everything communicated and everything done together, even when it was undesired and inconvenient.\"}"
