ough, from his rich idealism, I had been prepared to expect it) Dupin\u2019s particular analytic ability. He seemed, too, to take eager pleasure in using it\u2014if not exactly in showing it\u2014and he did not hesitate to admit the satisfaction he got from it. With a low chuckling laugh, he told me that most men, in his view, carried windows in their own chests, and that he liked to follow up such claims with direct and very startling proofs of his intimate knowledge of my own. His manner at those moments was cold and abstract; his eyes looked empty of expression; and while his voice was usually a rich tenor, it would rise into a treble that might have sounded petulant\u2014except that the enunciation was so deliberate and so perfectly clear.\"}"
