          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"759873e9e84d85e43700b0f0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Perdita\u2019s name was never said, but I could not doubt that his love for her was what made him change his mind so often. And who was more deserving of love than my noble-minded sister? Who should have the hand of this self-important king more than she, whose look belonged to a queen among nations? Who loved him as he loved her\u2014despite the disappointment that cooled her passion, while ambition kept fighting hard against it.\\n\\nWe went together to the House in the evening. Raymond knew that their plans and prospects would be discussed and decided in the debate that everyone expected, yet he was cheerful and careless. As we entered the coffee-room, a sound rose up\u2014like the buzzing of ten thousand swarming bees\u2014that stunned us. Groups of politicians stood together, their brows anxious, their voices loud or deep. The aristocratic party\u2014the richest and most influential men in England\u2014seemed less upset than the others, because the question was going to be discussed without their interference. Near the fire were Ryland and his supporters.\\n\\nRyland was a man of unknown or humble birth, but with immense wealth that he had inherited from his father, a manufacturer. As a young man, he had seen the king give up the throne, and the two houses of Lords and Commons join together. He had sympathized with these popular changes, and it had been his life\u2019s work to strengthen and increase them. Since then, the power of the landed gentry had grown. At first, Ryland was not sorry to see Lord Raymond\u2019s schemes, which pulled many of his opponents\u2019 supporters away. But now it had gone too far. The poorer nobility welcomed the return of sovereignty, believing it would bring back the power and rights they had lost. In people\u2019s minds, the half-forgotten spirit of royalty stirred again. They were willing slaves, self-made subjects, ready to bend their necks to the yoke. Some upright, strong men still remained\u2014pillars of state\u2014but the word republic had grown dull to ordinary ears. And many, as the event would show\u2014whether it was a majority or not\u2014still longed for the glitter and show of royalty. Ryland felt the need to resist. He claimed that only his own tolerance had allowed this party to grow, but he said the time for indulgence was over. With one motion of his arm, he would sweep away the cobwebs that blinded his countrymen.\\n\\nWhen Raymond entered the coffee-room, his friends greeted him almost like a shout. They gathered around him, counted their numbers, and explained the reasons they were about to gain an addition of so many members\u2014people who had not yet declared themselves.\"}"
        }
