{"request_id":"4ed0d76e40e70fff1592fff1","rewritten_text":"“April 19th. This morning, to my great joy, at about nine o’clock, with the moon’s surface frightfully close and my fears stirred to the utmost, the pump of my condenser finally gave clear signs that the atmosphere had changed. By ten, I had reason to believe its density had increased considerably. By eleven, very little work was needed at the apparatus; and at twelve o’clock, with some hesitation, I dared to unscrew the tourniquet. Finding no inconvenience in having done so, I finally opened the gum-elastic chamber and unrigged it from around the car. As might have been expected, spasms and violent headache were the immediate results of an experiment so sudden and so full of danger. But these, along with other difficulties connected with breathing, were by no means so severe that they put my life in peril. Still, I decided to endure them as best I could, since I would leave them behind me moment by moment as I approached the denser layers near the moon.

My approach, however, was still extremely forceful, and it soon became alarmingly clear that, although I had probably not been mistaken in expecting an atmosphere dense in proportion to the satellite’s mass, I had been wrong in assuming that this density—even at the surface—was sufficient to support the great weight carried in the car of my balloon. This should have been true, and to the same degree as at the surface of the earth, the actual gravity of bodies at either planet ought to be in the ratio of atmospheric condensation. That it was not so, my sudden plunge proved well enough; why it was not so can only be explained by those possible geological disturbances I mentioned earlier. In any case, I was now close to the planet and descending with the most terrible impetuosity. I lost no time, therefore, in throwing overboard first my ballast, then my water-kegs, then my condensing apparatus and gum-elastic chamber, and finally every item in the car. But it was all to no purpose. I still fell with horrible speed, and was now no more than half a mile from the surface.

As a last resort, having gotten rid of my coat, hat, and boots, I cut loose from the balloon the car itself, which was no small weight. Clinging with both hands to the netting, I had barely time to notice that the whole country, as far as the eye could reach, was thickly dotted with tiny habitations, before I tumbled headlong into the very heart of a fantastical-looking city—and into the middle of a vast crowd of ugly little people. None of them uttered a single syllable, or made the least effort to help me. They stood, like a bunch of idiots, grinning in a ridiculous way, and staring at me and my balloon sideways, with their arms set akimbo.”"}

