          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f025e002c011868c8d2434ea\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter Elliot, of Kellynch Hall, in Somersetshire, was a man who, purely for his own amusement, would never take up any book except the Baronetage. There he found something to occupy an idle hour, and a kind of comfort in times of trouble; there his mind was stirred into admiration and respect by the sight of the scanty remains of the earliest patents; and there, whenever any unpleasant feeling rose out of domestic matters, it slipped away into pity and contempt as he turned over the almost endless productions of the last century. Even if every other leaf proved dull, he could still read his own story with an interest that never failed. That was the very page where the favourite volume always opened:\\n\\n\u201cELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL.\\n\\n\u201cWalter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married, July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq. of South Park, in the county of Gloucester, by which lady (who died 1800) he has issue Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a still-born son, November 5, 1789; Mary, born November 20, 1791.\u201d\\n\\nThe paragraph had stood in precisely that form as it came from the printer; yet Sir Walter had improved it, adding\u2014both for his own information and that of his family\u2014these words after the date of Mary\u2019s birth: \u201cMarried, December 16, 1810, Charles, son and heir of Charles Musgrove, Esq. of Uppercross, in the county of Somerset,\u201d and inserting with the greatest care the day of the month on which he had lost his wife.\\n\\nAfter this came the usual account of the family\u2019s antiquity and rise: how it was first established in Cheshire; how it was mentioned in Dugdale; how it had held the office of high sheriff; how it had returned members for a borough in three successive parliaments; how it had shown loyalty and dignity of baronetcy in the first year of Charles II, with all the Marys and Elizabeths they had married. Altogether it made up two neat duodecimo pages, and ended with the arms and motto:\u2014\u201cPrincipal seat, Kellynch Hall, in the county of Somerset,\u201d and Sir Walter\u2019s handwriting again at the close:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHeir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great grandson of the second Sir Walter.\u201d\\n\\nVanity was the beginning and the end of Sir Walter Elliot\u2019s character\u2014vanity of person and of station. He had been remarkably handsome in his youth, and at fifty-four was still a very fine-looking man. Few women could think more highly of their own appearance than he did; nor could the valet of any newly made lord be more pleased with the place he held in society. He ranked the blessing of beauty as next only to the blessing of a baronetcy; and the Sir Walter Elliot, who possessed both, was the constant object of his warmest respect and devotion.\\n\\nHis good looks and his rank gave him a further claim to attachment; for to them he must have owed a wife of a character far superior to anything his own merits could have warranted.\"}"
