          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1dbf759047242f94a9cdc555\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wish we had a donkey. Then we could all come on donkeys, Jane, Miss Bates, and me\u2014and my caro sposo walking alongside. I really must talk to him about buying a donkey. In the country, I think it\u2019s almost a necessity; because no matter how many resources a woman has, it isn\u2019t possible for her to be shut up at home all the time. And you know\u2014long walks in summer mean dust, and in winter mean dirt.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou won\u2019t find either between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane is never dusty, and it\u2019s perfectly dry right now. Come on a donkey, if you\u2019d rather. You can borrow Mrs. Cole\u2019s. I\u2019d like everything to be as much to your taste as possible.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat I\u2019m sure you would. Indeed, I give you credit for it, my good friend. Under that particular kind of dry, blunt manner, I know you have the warmest heart. As I tell Mr. E., you\u2019re a complete humorist.\u2014Yes, believe me, Knightley, I\u2019m fully aware of your attention to me throughout all of this plan. You\u2019ve hit on exactly what will please me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley had another reason for avoiding a table in the shade. He wanted to persuade Mr. Woodhouse, as well as Emma, to join the party; and he knew that if either of them sat down outdoors to eat, it would inevitably make him ill. Mr. Woodhouse must not, under the flattering excuse of a morning drive and an hour or two spent at Donwell, be tempted away from his misery.\\n\\nHe was invited in good faith. There were no hidden horrors waiting to punish him for his easy trust. He did agree. He hadn\u2019t been to Donwell for two years. \u201cSome very fine morning, he, and Emma, and Harriet, could go very well; and he could sit still with Mrs. Weston while the dear girls walked about the gardens. He didn\u2019t suppose they could be damp now, in the middle of the day. He should like to see the old house again very much, and would be very happy to meet Mr. and Mrs. Elton, and any other of his neighbors.\u2014He couldn\u2019t see any objection at all to his, and Emma\u2019s, and Harriet\u2019s going there some very fine morning. He thought it very well done of Mr. Knightley to invite them\u2014very kind and sensible\u2014much cleverer than dining out. He wasn\u2019t fond of dining out.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley was fortunate in everyone\u2019s ready agreement. The invitation was received everywhere so well that it seemed, as with Mrs. Elton, they were all taking the scheme as a special compliment to themselves. Emma and Harriet spoke of having very high expectations of pleasure from it; and Mr. Weston, without being asked, promised to get Frank to join them if possible\u2014a proof of approval and gratitude that could have been spared. Mr. Knightley then had to say that he would be glad to see him; and Mr. Weston promised to waste no time in writing, and to spare no arguments to persuade him to come.\"}"
