          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9671d90b650d07461ea2b0fa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cDo you play?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think, sir,\u201d said the spy, in the most grudging tone, turning to Mr. Lorry, \u201cI may fairly appeal to a gentleman of your age and kindness\u2014ask you to put it to this other gentleman, so much younger than you\u2014whether, under any circumstances, he can reconcile it with his position to play that Ace he has mentioned. I\u2019ll own that I am a spy, and that it\u2019s a station held in disrepute\u2014though somebody must fill it; but this gentleman is not a spy. Why should he lower himself to become one?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ll play my Ace, Mr. Barsad,\u201d said Carton, taking the answer upon himself, and glancing at his watch, \u201cwithout the least hesitation, in only a few minutes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI had hoped, gentlemen both,\u201d the spy went on, still trying to draw Mr. Lorry into the talk, \u201cthat your regard for my sister\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can show my respect for your sister no better than by at last freeing her from her brother,\u201d said Sydney Carton.\\n\\n\u201cYou don\u2019t think so, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve made up my mind about it.\u201d\\n\\nThe spy\u2019s smoothness\u2014oddly at odds with his showily rough appearance, and no doubt with his usual manner\u2014met with such a check from the inscrutability of Carton, who was a puzzle even to wiser and better men, that it faltered and failed him. While he hesitated, Carton spoke again, falling back into his former look of studying the cards:\\n\\n\u201cAnd now, thinking it over, I\u2019m under a strong impression that I have another good card here, one not yet named. That friend and fellow-sheep who spoke of himself as pasturing in the country prisons\u2014who was he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFrench. You don\u2019t know him,\u201d said the spy, promptly.\\n\\n\u201cFrench, is it?\u201d Carton repeated, musing, and seeming not to notice him at all, though he echoed the word. \u201cWell; he may be.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you,\u201d said the spy, \u201cit\u2019s French\u2014though it isn\u2019t of consequence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot of consequence,\u201d Carton repeated in the same mechanical fashion\u2014\u201cnot of consequence\u2014No, it isn\u2019t of consequence. No. Still, I know the face.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think so. I\u2019m sure not. It can\u2019t be,\u201d said the spy.\\n\\n\u201cIt-can\u2019t-be,\u201d Sydney Carton muttered, looking back on it, and idling his glass again (happily it was a small one). \u201cCan\u2019t be. He spoke good French. Yet, like a foreigner, I thought?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cProvincial,\u201d said the spy.\\n\\n\u201cNo. Foreign!\u201d cried Carton, slapping his open hand on the table, as a light broke in upon his mind. \u201cCly! Disguised, but the same man. We had that fellow before us at the Old Bailey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow, there you go too fast, sir,\u201d said Barsad, with a smile that tipped his aquiline nose a little more to one side; \u201cyou really do give me an advantage over you.\u201d\"}"
