          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a01f11570477d4f73023db0c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 7th. I got up early, and, to my great joy, at last I saw what there could be no hesitation in believing was the northern Pole itself. It was there beyond doubt, immediately beneath my feet; but, alas, I had now risen to such a vast height that nothing could be discerned with any accuracy. Indeed, judging from the sequence of numbers showing my various altitudes at different times\u2014between 6 A.M. on April 2 and twenty minutes before 9 A.M. that same day (when the barometer dropped)\u2014it could fairly be inferred that the balloon had, by 4 A.M. on April 7, reached a height of not less than, certainly, 7,254 miles above the surface of the sea. That elevation may seem immense, but the estimate on which it is based likely gives a result far below the truth. In any case, I undoubtedly beheld the entire major diameter of the earth; the whole northern hemisphere lay beneath me like a chart projected orthographically, and the great circle of the equator itself formed the boundary line of my horizon. Your Excellencies may, however, easily imagine that the confined regions within the Arctic circle\u2014though they were directly beneath me and therefore seen without any apparent foreshortening\u2014were still, in themselves, comparatively too small, and too far from the point of view, to allow any very accurate examination. Still, what could be seen was singular and thrilling. Northward from that huge rim I mentioned\u2014more or less the limit of human discovery in these regions\u2014there continues an unbroken, or nearly unbroken, sheet of ice. In the first few degrees of its progress, its surface is very noticeably flattened; farther on it sinks into a plane, and finally, becoming somewhat concave, it ends at the Pole itself in a sharply defined circular center. Its apparent diameter, as seen from the balloon, subtended an angle of about sixty-five seconds, and its dusky color, varying in intensity, was at all times darker than any other spot on the visible hemisphere, and at times deepened into the most absolute and impenetrable blackness. Beyond that, little could be determined. By twelve o\u2019clock the circular center had decreased materially in circumference, and by seven P.M. I lost sight of it entirely, as the balloon passed over the western edge of the ice and floated away rapidly in the direction of the equator.\\n\\n\u201cApril 8th. I noticed a clear decrease in the earth\u2019s apparent diameter, along with a material change in its overall color and appearance.\u201d\"}"
