          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e8a88c5d9ba4bc4f65859b33\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma Woodhouse\u2014handsome, clever, and rich\u2014had a comfortable home and a cheerful disposition, and she seemed to bring together some of the best blessings life has to offer. She had lived nearly twenty-one years in the world with very little to distress or upset her.\\n\\nShe was the younger of two daughters of a deeply affectionate, indulgent father; and, as a result of her sister\u2019s marriage, she had been in charge of his household from a very early age. Her mother had died long enough ago that Emma could only remember her caresses vaguely; her place had been taken by an excellent woman as governess, who fell little short of a mother in affection.\\n\\nFor sixteen years, Miss Taylor had been in Mr. Woodhouse\u2019s family\u2014not so much as a governess as a friend. She was fond of both daughters, but especially of Emma. Between them, it was more like the closeness of sisters. Even before Miss Taylor had stopped holding the nominal office of governess, her gentle temperament had hardly allowed her to impose any real restraint; and with the shadow of authority long gone, they had been living together as friend and friend, mutually devoted, while Emma did exactly what she pleased. She held Miss Taylor\u2019s judgment in high regard, but was guided chiefly by her own.\\n\\nThe real downsides of Emma\u2019s situation, however, were having rather too much of her own way and a tendency to think a little too highly of herself. Those were the disadvantages that threatened to spoil some of her many enjoyments. The danger, though, was so completely unrecognized at present that it did not, by any means, count as a misfortune to her.\\n\\nSorrow came\u2014a gentle sorrow\u2014but not in the form of any unpleasant awareness. Miss Taylor married. It was Miss Taylor\u2019s loss that first brought grief. On the wedding day of this beloved friend, Emma first found herself sitting in mournful thought about any future without her. After the ceremony was over and the wedding guests had gone, Emma and her father were left to dine together, with no prospect of a third person to make a long evening feel less empty. Her father, as usual, settled himself to sleep after dinner, and then Emma had only to sit and think about what she had lost.\\n\\nThe event promised happiness for her friend in every way. Mr. Weston was a man of unexceptionable character, with an easy fortune, a suitable age, and pleasant manners; and there was some comfort in knowing that Emma had always wished for and encouraged the match with self-denying, generous friendship. Still, it was a bleak morning\u2019s work for Emma. The absence of Miss Taylor would be felt every hour of every day. She remembered the kindness of the past\u2014the kindness and affection of sixteen years\u2014how Miss Taylor had taught and played with her since she was five, how she had devoted all her abilities to attaching and entertaining her in good health, and how she had nursed her through the various illnesses of childhood.\"}"
