          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cf51663f145c944ca29a52ed\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNo bringing it up by hand, then. Not a bit of it!\u201d\\n\\nJoe offered me more gravy, and I was afraid to take it.\\n\\n\u201cHe was nothing but trouble for you, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Mrs. Hubble, speaking with sympathy about my sister.\\n\\n\u201cTrouble?\u201d my sister repeated. \u201cTrouble?\u201d Then she launched into a terrifying list of everything I had done wrong: every illness I was said to have caused, every time I had kept her awake, every time I had fallen from high places, every time I had fallen into low places, every injury I had done to myself, every time she had wished I were in my grave, and how stubbornly I had refused to go there.\\n\\nI think the Romans must have annoyed each other a great deal with their noses. Maybe that\u2019s why they became so restless. In any case, Mr. Wopsle\u2019s Roman nose annoyed me so much while she was listing my mistakes that I wanted to pull it until he screamed. But everything I had endured so far was nothing compared to the awful feelings that took over me when my sister finished and there was a pause. During that pause, everyone looked at me with anger and disgust, and I felt it painfully.\\n\\n\u201cStill,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, gently steering the group back to the subject they had drifted away from, \u201cPork\u2014boiled\u2014is rich, too; isn\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave some brandy, uncle,\u201d my sister said.\\n\\nOh, heavens\u2014so it had finally come. He would taste it and say it was weak, and then I was finished. I gripped the leg of the table under the cloth with both hands and waited for what would happen.\\n\\nMy sister went for the stone bottle, came back with it, and poured his brandy out. No one else took any. The poor man played with his glass\u2014picked it up, held it to the light, set it down again\u2014dragging out my misery. All the while, Mrs. Joe and Joe were busy clearing the table for the pie and pudding.\\n\\nI couldn\u2019t take my eyes off him. Still gripping the table leg with my hands and feet, I watched him finger his glass, lift it, smile, throw his head back, and drink the brandy. Right after that, the whole group was seized with terrible alarm. He sprang to his feet, turned around several times in a shocking, spasmodic coughing dance, and rushed out through the door. Then he appeared at the window, violently coughing and spitting, making the most horrible faces, and acting as if he had lost his mind.\\n\\nI held on tight while Mrs. Joe and Joe ran to him.\"}"
