{"request_id":"076c9d0639605dfeded1e01d","rewritten_text":"Summer advanced, and, crowned with the sun’s powerful rays, plague shot its unerring shafts over the earth. The nations beneath its influence bowed their heads and died. The corn that sprang up in abundance lay in autumn rotting on the ground, while the miserable man who had gone out to gather bread for his children lay stiff and struck down by plague in the furrow. The green woods waved their branches majestically, while the dying were spread beneath their shade, answering the solemn melody with harsh, discordant cries. Painted birds flitted through the shadows; the careless deer rested unharmed upon the fern. The oxen and horses strayed from their unguarded stables and grazed among the wheat, for death fell on man alone.

With summer and mortality, our fears grew. My poor love and I looked at each other, and at our babies. “We will save them, Idris,” I said. “I will save them. Years from now we’ll tell them about our fears, and how they passed with the danger that caused them. Even if only they remain on the earth, they will live; their cheeks won’t turn pale, and their sweet voices won’t fade.” Our eldest, to some extent, understood what was happening around him, and at times—wearing a serious look—he questioned me about the reason for such vast desolation. But he was only ten years old, and the cheerfulness of youth soon drove unreasonable worry from his brow. Evelyn, a laughing cherub, a playful infant with no idea of pain or sorrow, would shake his light curls back from his eyes and make the halls ring with his merriment, drawing our attention to his play in a thousand innocent ways. Clara, our lovely gentle Clara, was our support, our comfort, our delight. She made it her task to attend the sick, comfort the grieving, help the aged, and join in the sports and awaken the young to joy. She moved through the rooms like a good spirit, sent from the celestial kingdom to brighten our dark hour with an alien splendor. Wherever her footsteps went, gratitude and praise followed. Yet when she stood before us in quiet simplicity, playing with our children, or with girlish diligence doing small acts of kindness for Idris, one wondered what fair features of her pure loveliness, what soft tone of her thrilling voice, could contain so much heroism, wisdom, and active goodness.

The summer dragged on, because we trusted that winter would at least slow the disease. That it would vanish entirely was a hope too precious—too deeply felt—to be spoken aloud. When someone said such a thought carelessly, the listeners responded with a rush of tears and passionate sobs, showing how deep their fears were and how small their hopes."}

