          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"35a5ff7515d44a5b36c85b34\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On my return, I found the following letter from my father:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\\n\\n\u201cYou have probably been waiting impatiently for a letter that will set the date of your return to us; and at first I was tempted to write only a few lines, just mentioning the day on which I expected you. But that would be a cruel kindness, and I can\u2019t bring myself to do it. What would be your surprise, my son, if, when you expected a happy and joyful welcome, you were met instead with tears and misery? And how, Victor, can I tell you about our misfortune? Absence cannot have made you indifferent to our joys and griefs; and how could I inflict pain on my long-absent son? I want to prepare you for the dreadful news, but I know it\u2019s impossible. Even now, your eyes skim over the page, searching for the words that will bring you these horrible tidings.\\n\\n\u201cWilliam is dead!\u2014that sweet child, whose smiles delighted and warmed my heart, who was so gentle, yet so full of life! Victor, he is murdered!\\n\\n\u201cI won\u2019t try to comfort you; I\u2019ll simply lay out the circumstances of what happened.\\n\\n\u201cLast Thursday (May 7th), I, my niece, and your two brothers went for a walk in Plainpalais. The evening was warm and peaceful, and we stayed out longer than usual. It was already dusk before we thought of going back; and then we realized that William and Ernest, who had gone ahead, were missing. We sat down on a bench and waited until they returned. Soon after, Ernest came and asked whether we had seen his brother. He said that he had been playing with him, that William had run away to hide, and that he had searched for him in vain; afterward he waited a long time, but William never came back.\\n\\n\u201cThis account alarmed us, and we kept searching for him until night fell. Then Elizabeth suggested that he might have returned home. He was not there. We went back again with torches; I couldn\u2019t rest, because I kept thinking that my sweet boy had gotten lost and was exposed to all the damp and dew of the night. Elizabeth was in terrible anguish as well. About five in the morning I found my lovely boy\u2014whom, the night before, I had seen healthy, blooming, and active\u2014lying on the grass, pale and motionless; the mark of the murderer\u2019s finger was on his neck.\\n\\n\u201cHe was taken home, and the anguish visible in my face revealed the truth to Elizabeth. She was very insistent on seeing the body. At first I tried to stop her, but she wouldn\u2019t listen. She went into the room where it lay, hurriedly examined the neck of the victim, and clasping her hands cried out, \u2018O God!\u2019\"}"
        }
