          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"95dc5a6df7e0bedb2feab02a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou haven\u2019t been in Bath long enough,\u201d he said, \u201cto enjoy the evening parties here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! no. There\u2019s nothing in the usual kind of them that interests me. I don\u2019t play cards.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou didn\u2019t used to,\u201d he replied. \u201cI know you didn\u2019t care for cards before, but time changes a great deal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m not changed that much yet,\u201d cried Anne, stopping, because she feared she might be misunderstood. After a pause, he went on, as if the words came straight from what he felt. \u201cIt really is a long time! Eight and a half years is a long time.\u201d\\n\\nWhether he would have said more was left for Anne to think about later, in a calmer moment. Even while she still heard what he had just said, Henrietta called out to her, eager to use the moment\u2019s leisure to go out. She urged her companions not to waste time, in case someone else came in.\\n\\nThey had to move. Anne said she was perfectly ready, and tried to look that way, but she felt that if Henrietta had known the regret and reluctance in her heart as she left that chair, and then prepared to leave the room, she would have understood it in everything she felt for her cousin. Even in the safety of his affection, she could not help but pity him.\\n\\nBut their plans were cut short. Scary sounds were heard, other visitors arrived, and the door was thrown open for Sir Walter and Miss Elliot. Their entrance seemed to bring a chill into the room. Anne felt it at once. Wherever she looked, she saw the same signs. The comfort, freedom, and lively spirit of the room were gone. Everything settled into cold politeness, stiff silence, or dull conversation meant to match the heartless elegance of her father and sister. It was humiliating to realize it.\\n\\nHer jealous thoughts were satisfied in one respect. Captain Wentworth was recognized again by everyone, and Elizabeth was even more gracious than before. She spoke to him once, and then looked at him more than once. In fact, Elizabeth was working out a plan. The rest soon showed what it was. After wasting a few minutes on the usual empty talk, she began the invitation that would cover all the remaining obligations owed by the Musgroves. \u201cTomorrow evening, to meet a few friends. No formal party.\u201d\\n\\nShe said it very smoothly. The cards she had prepared\u2014the \u201cMiss Elliot at home\u201d\u2014were placed on the table with a polite, welcoming smile for everyone. She gave Captain Wentworth one more smile, and one more card. The truth was that Elizabeth had been in Bath long enough to understand how important it was to have a man with his kind of presence and appearance.\"}"
