          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"12d4045154bf75be4604bba5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But he had wronged no one, and he had jailed no one. So far was he from squeezing out the dues with any harsh hand, that he had surrendered them freely of his own accord; he had thrown himself among a world that offered him no special kindness, found his own private footing there, and earned his own daily bread. Monsieur Gabelle, acting on written directions, had managed the impoverished and entangled estate with the aim of sparing the people\u2014giving them what little there was to give: such fuel as the heavy creditors would allow them in winter, and such produce as could be kept safe from the same grasp in summer. No doubt he had set down the matter as both plea and proof, for his own security, so that it could not help but look so now.\\n\\nThis was the kind of favoured, desperate resolve Charles Darnay had begun to form: that he would go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. As in the old tale of the mariner, the winds and currents had carried him under the pull of the Loadstone Rock; it was drawing him on, and he must go. Whatever rose in his thoughts only drove him forward\u2014faster and faster, more and more surely\u2014toward that dreadful attraction. The uneasiness he had kept hidden within him was that wicked purposes were being carried out in his own unhappy country by wicked instruments; and that he, who could not but know himself better than they, was not there, trying to put a stop to bloodshed and to uphold the claims of mercy and humanity. With that uneasiness half smothered and half reproaching him, he was led to the sharp comparison of himself with the brave old gentleman whose sense of duty was so firm; and from that comparison (to his own hurt) there came at once Monseigneur\u2019s sneers, which had stung him cruelly, and Stryver\u2019s as well\u2014above all, coarse and cutting, for old reasons. After these came Gabelle\u2019s letter: the appeal of an innocent prisoner, facing death, to his justice, his honour, and his good name.\\n\\nHis resolution was fixed. He must go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. The Loadstone Rock was drawing him, and he must keep sailing until he struck. He knew of no rock, and he hardly saw any peril. The very intention with which he had done what he had done\u2014even though he had left it unfinished\u2014presented itself to him as something that, if he appeared in France to assert it, would be received with grateful recognition. Then that bright vision of doing good, which is so often the sanguine mirage of good minds, rose before him too; and he even imagined himself, in the illusion, with some influence to guide this Revolution, running so fearfully wild.\"}"
