          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5aa53c3c2c7f76f7e7d0c1e3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He also had to think about Sybil\u2019s father and mother, who were rather old-fashioned people and might object to the marriage if there was anything that looked like a scandal, though he felt sure that if he told them the full facts of the case, they would be the very first to understand the motives that had driven him. So he had every reason to decide in favor of poison. It was safe, reliable, and quiet, and it removed the need for any painful scenes, to which, like most Englishmen, he had a deep, rooted objection.\\n\\nHe knew absolutely nothing about the science of poisons, however, and since the waiter seemed unable to find anything in the library except Ruff\u2019s Guide and Bailey\u2019s Magazine, he examined the shelves himself. At last he came across a handsomely bound edition of the Pharmacopoeia, and a copy of Erskine\u2019s Toxicology, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, the President of the Royal College of Physicians, and one of the oldest members of the Buckingham\u2014having been elected by mistake for someone else. That contretemps enraged the Committee so much that when the real man came forward, they blackballed him unanimously. Lord Arthur was quite puzzled by the technical terms used in both books, and had begun to regret that he hadn\u2019t paid more attention to his classics at Oxford, when, in the second volume of Erskine, he found a very interesting and thorough account of the properties of aconitine, written in fairly clear English. It seemed to him to be exactly the poison he wanted. It worked fast\u2014almost immediate in its effect\u2014was perfectly painless, and when taken in the form of a gelatine capsule, as Sir Mathew recommended, it was by no means unpleasant.\\n\\nAccordingly, he made a note on his shirt cuff of the amount needed for a fatal dose, put the books back in their places, and strolled up St. James\u2019s Street to Pestle and Humbey\u2019s, the great chemists. Mr. Pestle, who always attended personally to the aristocracy, was rather surprised by the order and, in a very deferential manner, murmured something about needing a medical certificate. But as soon as Lord Arthur explained that it was for a large Norwegian mastiff he had to get rid of\u2014since it showed signs of incipient rabies and had already bitten the coachman twice in the calf of the leg\u2014he said he was perfectly satisfied. He complimented Lord Arthur on his wonderful knowledge of Toxicology and had the prescription made up immediately.\\n\\nLord Arthur put the capsule into a pretty little silver bonbonni\u00e8re he\u2019d seen in a shop window in Bond Street, threw away Pestle and Hambey\u2019s ugly pill-box, and drove off at once to Lady Clementina\u2019s.\"}"
