content.text:
{"request_id":"3819e8190b7cd13ae7222064","rewritten_text":"Driving a peg with careful precision into the ground exactly where the beetle had dropped, my friend now drew from his pocket a tape measure. He secured one end at that point on the trunk of the tree nearest the peg, then unrolled it until it touched the peg, and from there unrolled it onward—along the line already marked out by the two points of tree and peg—for a distance of fifty feet, while Jupiter cleared the brambles away with the scythe. At the place reached, a second peg was driven, and around it, as a center, a rough circle about four feet across was traced. Then Legrand took up a spade for himself, and gave one each to Jupiter and to me, begging us to start digging as quickly as we could.

To be candid, I never had much taste for such pastimes, and at that very moment I would gladly have refused; for night was drawing on, and I felt worn out by the exertions already made. Still, I saw no real way to slip out of it, and I feared that declining might unsettle my poor friend’s temper. If I could have relied on Jupiter’s help, I would have had no scruple about trying to get the madman home by force; but I knew too well the old negro’s temper to expect him to back me in a personal struggle against his master. I had no doubt that the latter had caught some of the countless Southern notions about buried money, and that his fancy had been strengthened by finding the _scarabæus_—or perhaps by Jupiter’s stubborn insistence that it was “a bug of real gold.” A mind already inclined to madness would be easily steered by such talk, particularly when it agrees with ideas it has long cherished; and then I remembered the poor fellow’s words about the beetle being “the index of his fortune.” In the end I was sorely vexed and at a loss, yet I decided to make a virtue of necessity—to dig with all my might, and so, as soon as possible, convince the visionary by plain sight of the emptiness of the beliefs he held.

When the lanterns were lit, we all set to work with a zeal fit for a more sensible cause; and as the light flared over our faces and tools, I couldn’t help thinking how striking a figure we made, and how odd and suspicious our digging must have looked to any passer-by who might, by chance, have come upon us. We dug on steadily for two hours."}

