content.text:
{"request_id":"6cd1704de3232611f3803767","rewritten_text":"Deprived of ordinary resources, the analyst throws himself into the spirit of his opponent, identifies with him, and, not infrequently, sees at a glance the only ways—sometimes, indeed, absurdly simple ones—by which he can seduce someone into error or rush them into miscalculation.

Whist has long been noted for its influence on what is called the calculating power; and men of the highest intellect have been known to take an apparently unexplainable delight in it, while avoiding chess as frivolous. There is, beyond doubt, nothing else of a similar kind so demanding on the faculty of analysis. The best chess-player in Christendom may be little more than the best player of chess; but skill at whist implies the ability to succeed in those more important undertakings where mind struggles with mind. When I say skill, I mean the kind of perfection in the game that includes understanding all the sources from which legitimate advantage can be drawn. These are not only numerous but varied, and they often lie in recesses of thought altogether out of reach for ordinary understanding. To observe attentively is to remember clearly; and, in this respect, the concentrative chess-player will do very well at whist, while the rules of Hoyle (based, as they are, on nothing more than the game’s mechanics) are sufficiently and generally understandable. So having a retentive memory and playing “by the book” are points commonly treated as the whole of good playing. But it is in matters beyond the limits of mere rule that the analyst’s skill shows itself. He makes, in silence, a host of observations and inferences.

So, perhaps, do his companions; and the difference in how much information they obtain does not lie so much in whether the inference is valid as in the quality of the observation. The necessary knowledge is knowing what to observe. Our player does not confine himself at all; and because the game is the object, he does not reject deductions from things outside it. He studies his partner’s face, comparing it carefully with that of each opponent. He considers how the cards are being sorted in each hand; often counting trump by trump, and honors by honors, through the glances their holders give them. He notes every change in expression as the play goes on, collecting a store of thought from differences in the look of certainty, surprise, triumph, or chagrin. From the way a person gathers up a trick, he judges whether they can make another in the same suit. He recognizes what is being played through feint, by the manner in which it is thrown down on the table. A casual or careless word; the accidental dropping or turning of a card, along with the anxiety or carelessness shown about concealing it; the counting of the tricks, including the order in which they are arranged—embarrassment, hesitation, eagerness, or agitation—all provide, to his seemingly intuitive perception, signs of the true state of affairs."}

