          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"946045dea9b9d70efecc4864\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I thanked him and apologized. He said, \u201cBy no means,\u201d and went on.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham was now an heiress, and you can imagine she was being treated like a prize match. Her half-brother had plenty of money again, but between his debts and his new madness, he wasted it all most fearfully once more. There were stronger differences between him and her than there had been between him and his father, and it\u2019s suspected that he carried a deep, mortal grudge against her for influencing the father\u2019s anger. Now, I come to the cruel part of the story\u2014simply pausing, my dear Handel, to point out that a dinner napkin won\u2019t fit into a tumbler.\u201d\\n\\nWhy I was trying to pack mine into my tumbler, I can\u2019t say at all. I only know that I found myself, with a persistence that deserved a much better cause, making the most strenuous efforts to compress it within those limits. Again I thanked him and apologized, and again he said, in the cheeriest manner, \u201cNot at all, I\u2019m sure!\u201d and continued.\\n\\n\u201cThere appeared on the scene\u2014say at the races, or the public balls, or anywhere else you like\u2014a certain man who made love to Miss Havisham. I never saw him (this happened five-and-twenty years ago, before you and I were, Handel), but I\u2019ve heard my father mention that he was showy, the kind of man for the job. But my father insists, with the strongest emphasis, that he must not be mistaken\u2014without ignorance or prejudice\u2014for a gentleman; because it\u2019s a principle of his that no man who wasn\u2019t a true gentleman at heart ever was, since the world began, a true gentleman in manner. He says no varnish can hide the grain of the wood; and the more varnish you put on, the more the grain will show. Well! This man pursued Miss Havisham closely and professed to be devoted to her. I believe she hadn\u2019t shown much susceptibility up to that point; but all the susceptibility she had certainly came out then, and she passionately loved him. There\u2019s no doubt she idolized him completely. He worked on her affection in that systematic way, getting large sums of money from her, and he persuaded her to buy her brother out of his share in the brewery (which had been left to him weakly by his father) at an enormous price, on the pretense that when he was her husband he would have to hold and manage it all. Your guardian wasn\u2019t involved with Miss Havisham\u2019s decisions at that time, and she was too proud and too much in love to be advised by anyone.\u201d\"}"
