          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e9eed38b3d79582cb422a728\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Even reading about the luxury of the dead was wonderful.\\n\\nThen he turned his attention to embroideries and to the tapestries that, in the cold rooms of northern Europe, did the same job frescoes would have done. As he looked into the subject\u2014and he always had a rare ability to become completely absorbed in whatever he was doing\u2014he felt almost sad when he thought about how time ruins beautiful and wonderful things. At least, he had escaped that. Summer came after summer, and the yellow jonquils bloomed and died again and again, while nights of horror repeated the story of their shame, but he stayed the same. No winter ever marred his face or spoiled his flowerlike beauty.\\n\\nMaterial things were so different. Where had they gone? Where was the great crocus-colored robe, on which the gods fought the giants, made by brown girls for the pleasure of Athena? Where was the huge velarium Nero stretched across the Colosseum in Rome, that purple sail of the sky, showing the stars and Apollo driving a chariot pulled by white horses with gold reins? He longed to see the delicate table napkins made for the Priest of the Sun, covered with every kind of food and dish that could be wanted for a feast; the burial cloth of King Chilperic, with its three hundred golden bees; the strange robes that made the Bishop of Pontus so angry, and that were patterned with \u201clions, panthers, bears, dogs, forests, rocks, hunters\u2014everything, in fact, that a painter can copy from nature\u201d; and the coat Charles of Orleans once wore, with verses from a song embroidered on the sleeves. The song began \u201cMadame, je suis tout joyeux,\u201d and the music for the words was stitched in gold thread, with each note shaped like a square, and each note made with four pearls.\\n\\nHe read about the room prepared at the palace in Rheims for Queen Joan of Burgundy, decorated with \u201cthirteen hundred and twenty-one parrots, made in broidery, and blazoned with the king\u2019s arms, and five hundred and sixty-one butterflies, whose wings were similarly ornamented with the arms of the queen, the whole worked in gold.\u201d Catherine de Medicis had a mourning bed made for herself from black velvet dusted with crescents and suns. Its curtains were damask, with leafy wreaths and garlands on a gold and silver background, and the edges were fringed with pearl embroidery. It stood in a room lined with rows of the queen\u2019s symbols, cut in black velvet on cloth of silver. Louis XIV. had gold-embroidered caryatids, fifteen feet tall, in his apartment.\"}"
