          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"26eb6840c4bdb3c176239ddc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You wouldn\u2019t know anything about me unless you\u2019d already read a book called The Adventures of Tom Sawyer; but that\u2019s neither here nor there. That book was put together by Mr. Mark Twain, and for the most part he told the truth. Sure, there were bits he stretched, but mostly it was the truth. That\u2019s nothing\u2014people lie one way or another, and I\u2019ve seen it; except maybe Aunt Polly, or the widow, or possibly Mary. Aunt Polly\u2014Tom\u2019s Aunt Polly, she is\u2014and Mary, and the Widow Douglas are the ones talked about in that book, which is mostly true, with a few stretches, like I said.\\n\\nNow the way the story ends is this: Tom and me found the money the robbers had hidden in the cave, and that made us rich. We each got six thousand dollars\u2014real gold. It was a terrible sight of money piled up. Well, Judge Thatcher took it and put it out on interest, and it paid us a dollar a day apiece, every day of the year\u2014more than any person could say what to do with. The Widow Douglas took me for her son and said she\u2019d civilize me, but living in her house all the time was hard going, what with how gloomy, regular, and proper she was in everything; so when I couldn\u2019t stand it any longer, I slipped back into my old rags and my sugar-hogshead, and I was free and content. But Tom Sawyer tracked me down and told me he was going to start a band of robbers, and I could join if I\u2019d go back to the widow and act respectable. So I went back.\\n\\nThe widow cried over me and called me a poor lost lamb, and she gave me a heap of other names besides, but she didn\u2019t mean any harm by it. She dressed me up in those new clothes again, and I just kept sweating and sweating, feeling all pinched and cramped. Then the same old business started up again. The widow rang a bell for supper, and you had to be there on time. When you got to the table, you couldn\u2019t just start eating right away\u2014you had to sit and wait while the widow bowed her head and grumbled a little over the food, though there wasn\u2019t really anything wrong with it; nothing, except that everything was cooked by itself. In a barrel of odds and ends it\u2019s different\u2014things get mixed together, the juices kind of trade places, and the whole thing goes down better.\\n\\nAfter supper she pulled out her book and taught me about Moses and the Bulrushers, and I was eager to learn all about him; but before long she let on that Moses had been dead for quite a long time. Then I didn\u2019t care about him anymore, because I don\u2019t put any faith in dead people.\"}"
