          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d89a9ce0711b233a31b688ee\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If I can\u2019t live on that, then I won\u2019t ask anyone to give me a lift. Is it satisfactory?\u201d\\n\\nThere was a huge roar of applause, and from the middle of it the king\u2019s voice rose, saying:\\n\\n\u201cTake off his bonds and set him free! Give him honor, high and low, rich and poor, because he has become the king\u2019s right-hand man. He is clothed with power and authority, and his place is on the highest step of the throne! Now sweep away this creeping night and bring back light and cheer, so that the whole world may bless thee.\u201d\\n\\nBut I said:\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s nothing if a common man is shamed in front of the world. But it would be an insult to the king if anyone who saw his minister naked did not also see him freed from his shame. If I may ask, let my clothes be brought back.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey won\u2019t do,\u201d the king interrupted. \u201cGet him different clothes\u2014clothe him like a prince!\u201d\\n\\nMy plan worked. I wanted to keep things the way they were until the eclipse was completely over. Otherwise they would keep trying to get me to send the darkness away, and of course I couldn\u2019t. Getting the clothes took some time, but not enough. So I had to make another excuse. I said it would be natural for the king to change his mind and regret, at least a little, what he had done in the heat of the moment. Therefore I would let the darkness last a while, and if, after a reasonable time, the king still kept the same decision, then the darkness could be dismissed. Neither the king nor anyone else liked that plan, but I had to stick to it.\\n\\nThe darkness kept growing darker and blacker as I struggled in those awkward clothes from the sixth century. At last it became pitch black, and the crowd groaned in horror as they felt the cold, strange night breezes blow through the place and saw the stars come out and twinkle in the sky. Finally the eclipse was total, and I was very glad of it, but everyone else was miserable, which was only to be expected. I said:\\n\\n\u201cThe king, by his silence, still holds to the terms.\u201d Then I raised my hands\u2014held them there for a moment\u2014and with the most solemn seriousness I said, \u201cLet the enchantment dissolve and pass harmlessly away!\u201d\\n\\nFor a moment there was no answer, in that deep darkness and that quiet, graveyard-like hush. But when the silver edge of the sun pushed out again, a moment or two later the whole crowd erupted with a huge shout. They rushed down like a flood to smother me with blessings and gratitude, and Clarence was not the last to join in, of course.\u201d}\"}"
