          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"228212c305c74fc4b2e94d0c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIt must have been about that same stretch of Mr. Elliot\u2019s life,\u201d said Anne, \u201cthe part of it that has always stirred my own particular curiosity. It must have been near the time he came to be known to my father and my sister. I never met him myself; I only heard of him; yet there was something in his behaviour toward my father and sister then, and afterwards in the circumstances of his marriage, that I could never quite bring to terms with the present day. It seemed to point to a different sort of man.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI understand it all, I understand it all,\u201d cried Mrs. Smith. \u201cHe had been introduced to Sir Walter and your sister before I ever knew him, but I heard him talk of them again and again. I know he was invited and encouraged, and I know he chose not to go. I can set you right, perhaps, on matters you would hardly think to ask about; and as for his marriage, I knew everything at the time. I was privy to every turn of it\u2014pros and cons alike; I was the friend to whom he laid out his hopes and plans. And though I had not known his wife before, her lower standing in society made that out of the question; still, I knew her all the rest of her life\u2014at least until the last two years of it\u2014and I can answer any question you may care to put.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNay,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI have no special inquiry to make about her. I have always taken it that they were not a happy pair. But I should like to understand why, at that time of his life, he should have treated my father\u2019s acquaintance so lightly, as he did. My father was certainly disposed to show him every kindness and proper attention. Why did Mr. Elliot step back?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Elliot,\u201d replied Mrs. Smith, \u201cat that period of his life, had one aim: to make his fortune, and by a quicker route than the law. He meant to secure it by marriage. He was resolved, at any rate, not to spoil it with an imprudent match; and I know it was his conviction\u2014whether he was right or not, I cannot say\u2014that your father and sister, with their civilities and invitations, were arranging a connection between the heir and the young lady, and that such a match could not possibly fit his notions of wealth and independence. That was why he drew back, I assure you. He told me the whole affair; there were no secrets between us. It was striking, too, that after you had just left me in Bath, my first and chief acquaintance on my marrying should be your cousin; and that, through him, I should be hearing of your father and sister without end.\u201d\"}"
