          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a9888ebe08fe767298728817\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This brief explanation from Mr. Knightley gave Emma real satisfaction. It belonged among the pleasant recollections of the ball; and the next morning she strolled about the lawn, lingering over it.\u2014She was particularly pleased that matters had come to such a good understanding regarding the Eltons, and that their views of both husband and wife were so closely matched; and his praise of Harriet, together with his yielding to her in that point, was gratifying in a way that could not be mistaken. The Eltons\u2019 impertinence, which for a few minutes had threatened to spoil the rest of her evening, had nevertheless been the very cause of some of its greatest comforts; and she looked forward to another happy consequence\u2014the breaking of Harriet\u2019s infatuation.\u2014From Harriet\u2019s way of speaking of what had occurred before they left the ballroom, Emma felt encouraged. It was as though her eyes had been opened at once, and she were now able to see that Mr. Elton was not the superior creature she had taken him for. The fever was gone, and Emma could scarcely fear that the pulse would be quickened again by any injurious civility. She relied on the Eltons\u2019 ill-will to furnish all the further discipline of pointed neglect that might still be needed.\u2014With Harriet reasoning, Frank Churchill not too deeply in love, and Mr. Knightley not disposed to quarrel with her, what a happy summer must be in store for her!\\n\\nShe was not to see Frank Churchill this morning. He had told her that he could not permit himself the pleasure of stopping at Hartfield, since he was to be at home by the middle of the day. She did not regret it.\\n\\nHaving settled and reviewed everything, and put it all in order, she was just turning toward the house with spirits newly raised for the claims of the two little boys, as well as of their grandfather, when the great iron sweep-gate swung open and two persons entered whom she had least expected to see together\u2014Frank Churchill, with Harriet leaning on his arm\u2014indeed Harriet!\u2014In an instant she was sure that something extraordinary had taken place. Harriet looked pale and frightened, and he was trying to rouse her courage.\u2014The iron gates and the front door were not more than twenty yards apart;\u2014soon all three were in the hall, and Harriet at once sank into a chair and fainted away.\\n\\nA young lady who faints must be brought round; questions must be put, and surprises explained. Such occurrences are certainly engaging, yet the suspense over them cannot last long. In a few minutes Emma learned the whole.\\n\\nMiss Smith, and Miss Bickerton\u2014another parlour boarder at Mrs. Goddard\u2019s, who had been at the ball as well\u2014had gone out together and taken a road, the Richmond road; and though it looked public enough to be safe, it had carried them into alarm.\u2014About half a mile beyond Highbury, after a sudden turn, and deeply shaded by elms on either side, it became for some distance very retired; and when the young ladies had gone some way into it, they suddenly perceived, at a little distance before them, on a broader patch of greensward beside the road, a party of gipsies.\"}"
