          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0d9f33d178244bac209143b2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On my return, I found the following letter from my father:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\\n\\n\u201cYou have probably been waiting with impatience for a note that would set the day of your return to us; at first I was inclined to send you only a few lines, just naming the time when I should expect you. But that would be a cruel sort of kindness, and I cannot bring myself to it. Imagine, my son, what your surprise would be: you come expecting a welcome full of joy and gladness, and instead you find tears and misery. Victor, how am I to tell you of our misfortune? Your absence cannot have made you indifferent to our joys and sorrows; and how could I inflict pain on my long-absent son? I mean to prepare you for the dreadful news, though I know it is impossible. Even now, I can see your eye darting over the page, hunting for the words meant to carry you the terrible tidings.\\n\\n\u201cWilliam is dead!\u2014that dear child, whose smiles warmed and delighted my heart, so gentle, and yet so full of life! Victor, he has been murdered!\\n\\n\u201cI will not try to comfort you; I will only set down the circumstances of what occurred.\\n\\n\u201cLast Thursday (May 7th), I, my niece, and your two brothers went out to take a walk in Plainpalais. The evening was mild and calm, and we lingered longer than usual. It was already getting dusk before we thought of turning back; and then we learned that William and Ernest, who had gone on ahead, were missing. We sat down on a bench and waited for them to return. Soon Ernest came back and asked whether we had seen his brother. He said that he had been playing with him; that William had run off to hide himself; that he searched for him in vain; and that after waiting a long time, William did not come back.\\n\\n\u201cThis account alarmed us, and we kept on looking for him until night came. Then Elizabeth suggested that he might have returned home. He was not there. We went back again with torches; I could not rest, thinking of my sweet boy lost and left to the damp and dews of the night. Elizabeth was in the same distress. About five in the morning I found my lovely child\u2014whom, the night before, I had seen blooming and active in health\u2014lying on the grass, livid and motionless; the mark of the murderer\u2019s hand was on his neck.\\n\\n\u201cHe was taken home, and the anguish I could not hide showed Elizabeth the truth. She was very eager to see the body. At first I tried to stop her, but she would not be denied. She entered the room where it lay, hurriedly examined the neck of the victim, and clasping her hands cried out, \u2018O God!\u2019\u201d\"}"
