{"request_id":"70ce201a7bde1b5daf22fe9b","rewritten_text":"Far off in the distance, the line of flickering gas lamps curved away, and outside a small walled-in house stood a solitary hansom, the driver asleep inside. He walked quickly toward Portland Place, glancing around now and then, as if he feared he was being followed. At the corner of Rich Street, two men stood reading a small notice pinned to a hoarding. An odd feeling of curiosity stirred in him, and he crossed over. As he got closer, the word “Murder,” printed in black letters, caught his eye. He flinched, and a deep flush rose into his cheeks. The notice was an advertisement offering a reward for any information that would lead to the arrest of a man of medium height, between thirty and forty years old, wearing a billycock hat, a black coat, and check trousers, with a scar on his right cheek. He read it again and again, wondering whether the wretched man would ever be caught, and how he had ended up scarred. Maybe, one day, his own name would be posted on the walls of London. Maybe, one day, a price would be set on his head as well.

The thought sickened him with horror. He turned sharply and hurried on through the night.

Where he went, he hardly knew. He had a hazy memory of wandering through a maze of grimy houses, of getting lost in a vast web of dark streets, and it was bright dawn before he finally found himself at Piccadilly Circus. As he strolled home toward Belgrave Square, he passed the great wagons heading for Covent Garden. The white-aproned carters, with their pleasant sunburnt faces and coarse, curly hair, marched on steadily, cracking their whips and calling to one another now and then; on the back of a huge grey horse, the leader of a clattering team, sat a chubby boy with a bunch of primroses in his battered hat, gripping the mane tightly with his small hands and laughing. The great piles of vegetables looked like masses of jade against the morning sky—like masses of green jade against the pink petals of some marvelous rose. Lord Arthur felt strangely affected, though he couldn’t say why. There was something in the dawn’s delicate beauty that seemed to him inexpressibly pathetic, and he thought of all the days that break in beauty and then end in storm. Even these rustic men, with their rough, good-humored voices and their easy, unhurried ways—what a strange London they must see: a London free from the sin of night and the smoke of day, a pale, ghost-like city, a desolate town of tombs. He wondered what they thought of it, and whether they knew anything of its splendor and its shame, of its fierce, fiery-colored joys, and of the terrible hunger of it—everything it makes and ruins from morning to night."}

