{"request_id":"87a18cae14d0cf4cb81694a2","rewritten_text":"He told me that he was wearing away his hours in vain where he then was, and that letters from the friends he had made in London urged him to come back, so that he might finish the negotiation they had begun for his Indian venture. He said he could no longer put off his departure; yet, since his trip to London might be followed—perhaps even sooner than he had imagined—by a longer voyage, he begged me to give him as much of my company as I could spare. He therefore entreated me to leave my lonely island and to meet him at Perth, so that we might go south together. In some measure the letter brought me back to myself, and I resolved to leave my island at the end of two days.

Still, before I set out, there was a duty I had to carry out, and the thought of it made me shudder: I must pack my chemical instruments, and to do so I had to go into the room where my hateful work had taken place, and handle those tools whose very sight sickened me. The next morning, at daybreak, I gathered enough courage, and unlocked the door of my laboratory. The remains of the half-finished creature, which I had destroyed, were strewn across the floor, and I could almost believe I had torn at the living flesh of a man. I paused to steady myself, then entered the chamber. With a hand that trembled, I carried the instruments out of the room; but I reflected that I ought not to leave the relics of my labor where they might rouse the peasants’ horror and suspicion. I accordingly put them into a basket, along with a great quantity of stones, and, having stored them away, I resolved to throw them into the sea that very night. In the meantime, I sat upon the beach, occupied in cleaning and arranging my chemical apparatus.

Nothing could be more thorough than the change that had come over my feelings since the night the dæmon appeared. Before, I had looked upon my promise with a dark despair, as something that must be carried out, whatever the consequences; but now it seemed to me as though a film had been lifted from my eyes, and that I saw clearly for the first time. The notion of resuming my labors never once crossed my mind. The threat I had heard continued to press upon my thoughts, yet I did not consider that any voluntary act of mine could ward it off. I had already determined, in my own mind, that to create another like the fiend I had first made would be an act of the basest and most monstrous selfishness, and I drove away every thought that might lead to any other conclusion."}

