          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"570c394f6228e63b93e98244\",\"rewritten_text\":\"During his brief stay, Emma had hardly seen him at all. Still, it was enough to feel that their first meeting was already over, and to get the impression that he had not improved at all by the mixture of irritation and self-importance now showing in his manner. In fact, she was beginning to wonder that she had ever found him pleasant. His presence was so tied to unpleasant feelings that, unless she looked at it in a moral way\u2014as a kind of penance, a lesson, and a source of humiliating profit to her own thoughts\u2014she would have been glad to be sure she would never see him again. She wished him well, but he caused her pain, and his comfort being twenty miles away would give her the most satisfaction.\\n\\nStill, his continued stay in Highbury would certainly be made easier by his marriage. It would prevent many empty attempts to get attention, and smooth over many awkward moments. A Mrs. Elton would be an excuse for any change in how people related to one another, and the old closeness might fade without anyone noticing. It would be almost like starting their life of politeness again.\\n\\nAs for the woman herself, Emma did not think much of her. She was good enough for Mr. Elton, no doubt; capable enough for Highbury; and handsome enough\u2014though she would probably look plain beside Harriet. As for her family connection, Emma felt completely at ease. She believed that, after all his proud claims and his contempt for Harriet, he had done nothing wrong. On that point, the truth seemed within reach. What she was, might still be uncertain, but who she was could be found out; and aside from the 10,000 pounds, it did not seem that she was in any way superior to Harriet. She brought no name, no bloodline, and no family ties. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol merchant, which was what he would have to be called. But since the profits from his business seemed so modest, it was not unreasonable to think that the status of his trade was modest too. Each winter, she had been used to spending part of her time in Bath, but Bristol was her real home\u2014the very center of Bristol. Her parents had died some years earlier, but an uncle remained, in the law. Nothing about him seemed especially distinguished, except that he was in the law. With him, the daughter had lived. Emma guessed that he worked as a drudge for some attorney and was too dull to rise. And all the importance of the connection seemed to depend on the older sister, who was very well married to a gentleman of great means near Bristol, who kept two carriages. That was the final point of Miss Hawkins\u2019s story, and the height of her glory.\"}"
