{"request_id":"486c5acc54c0a9b3377edc3b","rewritten_text":"I came across several funerals. They were attended by only a small number of mourners, and the people watching treated them as ominous signs of the worst kind. Some stared at the processions with wild eagerness; others hurried away in fear; some wept out loud.

Adrian’s main effort, after he had provided immediate help to the sick, was to hide from the people of London the symptoms and the progress of the plague. He understood that fear and gloomy forebodings were powerful allies to disease; that despairing, brooding care made the human body especially vulnerable to infection. So nothing improper or shocking was visible. The shops were generally open, and the flow of passengers was kept up to some degree. Still, though the city’s appearance was kept from looking infected, London seemed changed enough to me—someone who hadn’t seen it since the visitation began. There were no carriages, and grass had grown high in the streets. The houses looked desolate; most of the shutters were closed; and the people I met had a ghastly, frightened stare, nothing like the usual practical, businesslike manner of Londoners. My solitary carriage drew attention as it rattled along toward the Protectoral Palace, and the fashionable streets leading to it looked even more dreary and empty. I found Adrian’s antechamber crowded—it was his hour for receiving visitors. I didn’t want to interrupt his work, so I waited, watching the petitioners come in and go out. They were mostly people from the middle and lower classes, whose ability to live on had collapsed when trade stopped, along with the restless drive to make money in all its forms that was so typical of our country. There was an atmosphere of anxiety, sometimes outright terror, in the newcomers, sharply contrasted with the resigned—and even satisfied—look of those who had already been heard. I could see my friend’s influence in their quicker movements and brighter faces. At two o’clock the admissions stopped; after that, no one was allowed in. Those who had been turned away left sullenly or sadly, and then I entered the audience chamber.

I was struck by how much Adrian’s health had improved. He was no longer bent to the ground like an over-nursed spring flower, forced to grow beyond its strength and weighed down even by the crown of its blossoms. His eyes were bright, his expression calm, and a sense of concentrated energy seemed to spread through his whole body—very unlike the former languor. He sat at a table with several secretaries, who were arranging petitions or recording the notes made during that day’s audience. Two or three petitioners were still waiting. I admired his justice and patience. Those who had the means to live outside London, he advised to leave immediately, and he made sure they were given the resources to do so."}

