          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e1a35990b0e3d40c63b8ecc9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Moreover, this knot is one that only a few people besides sailors can tie, and it is something that belongs especially to the Maltese. I picked up the ribbon at the foot of the lightning-rod. It could not have belonged to either of the dead men. Now, even if I am wrong about what this ribbon suggests\u2014that the Frenchman was a sailor from a Maltese ship\u2014I have still done no harm by saying what I said in the advertisement. If I am mistaken, he will only think that I was led astray by some detail he will not bother to check. But if I am right, then a major advantage is gained. Even though the Frenchman knows nothing about the murder, he will naturally hesitate to answer the advertisement, and to demand the Ourang-Outang. He will think like this: \u2018I am innocent. I am poor. My Ourang-Outang is worth a great deal to someone in my situation\u2014a fortune by itself. So why would I give it up because of idle fears of danger? It is right here, within my reach. It was found in the Bois de Boulogne, far away from the place where that slaughter happened. How could anyone suspect that a wild animal did it? The police are to blame. They have not found the slightest clue. Even if they trace the animal, they still could not prove that I knew about the murder, or connect me to guilt because of that knowledge. Most of all, I am known. The advertiser names me as the owner of the beast. I do not know how far his information goes. If I avoid claiming something of such great value that everyone knows I have, then at least I will make the animal seem suspicious. It is not my plan to draw attention to myself or to the beast. I will answer the advertisement, get the Ourang-Outang, and keep it close until this whole matter has blown over.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAt that moment, we heard a step on the stairs.\\n\\n\u201cBe ready,\u201d said Dupin. \u201cHave your pistols, but don\u2019t use them and don\u2019t show them until I give the signal.\u201d\\n\\nThe front door of the house had been left open. The visitor came in without ringing and walked several steps up the staircase. Then, however, he seemed to pause. Soon we heard him going back down. Dupin moved quickly toward the door, but then we heard the man coming up again. He did not turn back a second time. Instead, he stepped forward with confidence and knocked on the door of our room.\\n\\n\u201cCome in,\u201d said Dupin, in a cheerful, strong voice.\\n\\nA man entered. He was clearly a sailor\u2014a tall, thickset, muscular person, with a bold, dare-devil look on his face, though not entirely unpleasant.\"}"
