          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9d4a642d4509fe08a24c6788\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I was born in a small corner of land surrounded by the sea, under skies heavy with clouds. When I think of the whole world\u2014its shoreless ocean and its endless, unmarked continents\u2014it seems to me like only a tiny dot in an enormous picture. Yet, when I measure it by the strength of my own mind, it matters far more than countries that are bigger and have more people. It is true that the human mind alone creates what is good or great for people, and that Nature is only the first helper. England, far to the north in the muddy sea, now comes into my dreams as if it were a huge, well-staffed ship. It masters the winds and rides proudly over the waves. In my boyhood, England was the whole world to me. When I stood on my native hills and looked as far as my eyes could reach\u2014over plain and mountain, dotted with the homes of my countrymen, and made fertile by their work\u2014I felt that the center of the earth was fixed right there. Everything else on the globe was like a story I could ignore, and forgetting it would have taken no effort from my imagination or my understanding.\\n\\nMy life has shown, from the start, how much change can affect the different paths a person\u2019s life can take. In my case, it came almost like an inheritance. My father was one of those men who are given, in abundance, the gifts of wit and imagination, but then left to steer his life without reason as a rudder or judgment as a pilot. His family background was not well known, but circumstances brought him into public view early. Soon, his small inheritance was spent away in the bright world of fashion and luxury, where he played his part. In the short years of careless youth, he was adored by the fashionable people of the day, and not least by the young king, who escaped the fights of party politics and the heavy duties of ruling, finding in my father constant amusement and a lively spirit. My father\u2019s impulses, never truly controlled by himself, kept leading him into trouble. Still, it was his own cleverness that pulled him out. The growing pile of debts\u2014both debts of honor and debts of trade\u2014that would have crushed almost anyone else, he carried with a light heart and wild, unstoppable cheer. And because his presence was so needed at the tables and gatherings of the wealthy, his mistakes were treated as forgivable, and he was welcomed with flattering excitement.\\n\\nBut this kind of popularity, like all others, does not last. The problems of every kind he had to face grew faster and worse, in a frightening way, as his small means of escape ran out.\"}"
