{"request_id":"0df73d11d548bce331324e2e","rewritten_text":"I cannot make out at all why she should wish to be so intimate with me; yet if the same circumstances were to occur again, I am just as certain I would be taken in once more. Caroline did not come back to my visit until yesterday, and in the meantime I received neither note nor word. When she finally did appear, it was plain enough that she took no satisfaction in it; she offered a small, formal apology for not calling sooner, said nothing at all about wanting to see me again, and had in every way changed into such a different sort of person that, the moment she left, I was fully resolved to drop the acquaintance. I feel for her, though I cannot help blaming her. She was quite mistaken to single me out as she did; I can say with confidence that every step toward intimacy began on her side. Still, I pity her, because she must know she has been acting wrongly, and because I am convinced that her brother’s situation is what has driven it. I need not go on further; and although we know that anxiety is quite needless, if she feels it, it will readily explain her conduct toward me. And however dear he is to his sister, any anxiety she may have for him is both natural and amiable. I cannot help, though, being surprised that she should have such fears now; for if he had cared for me in the least, we must have met long, long ago. He knows I am in town, I am sure, from something she said herself; and yet, from the way she talks, it seems as if she is trying to persuade herself that he is truly inclined toward Miss Darcy. I cannot make sense of it. If I were not afraid of judging too harshly, I should almost be tempted to say that there is a strong look of duplicity in the whole matter. I will try to put away every painful thought and to think only of what can make me happy—your affection, and the steady kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Write to me very soon. Miss Bingley mentioned that he would never return to Netherfield again, and that they might give up the house, though nothing was certain. We had better not bring it up. I am very glad that you have such pleasant accounts from our friends at Hunsford. Do go and see them, with Sir William and Maria. I am sure you will be perfectly comfortable there.

“Yours, etc.”

This letter troubled Elizabeth; but her spirits rose again when she reflected that Jane would not be deceived any longer, at least not by the sister. Every expectation from the brother was now entirely at an end."}

