          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4f5269cd9f35cc3bbe23e13e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It looked like a set of narrow back lanes, ditches, and small gardens, and it gave the feel of a quiet, rather boring retirement. Wemmick\u2019s house was a small wooden cottage set among garden plots. The top of it had been cut out and painted to look like a battery, with guns.\\n\\n\u201cMy own work,\u201d said Wemmick. \u201cPretty, isn\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\nI agreed. I think it was the smallest house I had ever seen. It had the strangest Gothic windows\u2014most of them were fake\u2014and a Gothic front door that was almost too small to get inside.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s a real flagstaff,\u201d said Wemmick. \u201cAnd on Sundays I raise a real flag. Now look here. After I cross this bridge, I lift it up\u2014like this\u2014and cut off the connection.\u201d\\n\\nThe bridge was just a plank. It crossed a chasm about four feet wide and two feet deep. Still, it was pleasant to watch how proud he was as he lifted it and locked it in place. He smiled while doing it, enjoying himself rather than acting out a routine.\\n\\n\u201cAt nine o\u2019clock every night, Greenwich time,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cthe gun fires. There it is, you see! And when you hear it go, I think you\u2019ll say it\u2019s a Stinger.\u201d\\n\\nThe gun itself was mounted in a separate little fortress made of lattice work. It was protected from the weather by a clever tarpaulin cover, shaped like an umbrella.\\n\\n\u201cThen, at the back,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cout of sight, so it doesn\u2019t get in the way of the idea of fortifications. It\u2019s a rule with me: if you have an idea, carry it out and keep it going. I don\u2019t know if that\u2019s your opinion\u2014\u201d\\n\\nI said it was.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014At the back there\u2019s a pig, and there are fowls and rabbits. Then I put together my own little frame, you see, and grow cucumbers. And at supper you can judge what kind of salad I can make. So, sir,\u