          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4c74aec3b3cd9f9a4b2ff74d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, your father can certainly spare you, if your mother can. Daughters never matter to a father as much as sons do. And if you stay another _month_ in full, I will be able to take one of you as far as London. I plan to go there early in June for a week. Since Dawson doesn\u2019t object to the barouche-box, there will be very good room for one of you. And if the weather happens to be cool, I wouldn\u2019t mind taking you both, since neither of you is very large.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are very kind, madam; but I think we should stick to our original plan.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine looked resigned. \u201cMrs. Collins, you must send a servant with them. You know I always say what I think, and I cannot stand the idea of two young women travelling post by themselves. It\u2019s highly improper. You must arrange for someone to go with them. I strongly dislike that kind of thing. Young women should always be properly protected and accompanied, according to their position in life. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate last summer, I made sure she had two men-servants with her. Miss Darcy, the daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley, and Lady Anne could not have appeared in any other way with propriety. I pay close attention to all these matters. You must send John with the young ladies, Mrs. Collins. I\u2019m glad it occurred to me to mention it, because it would really be a disgrace to _you_ to let them go alone.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy uncle will send a servant for us.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Your uncle? Does he keep a man-servant? I\u2019m very glad you have someone who thinks about these things. Where will you change horses? Oh, Bromley, of course. If you mention my name at the Bell, you\u2019ll be taken care of.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine had many more questions to ask about their journey. Since she didn\u2019t answer all of them herself, Elizabeth thought it was lucky; otherwise, with her mind so busy, she might have forgotten where she was. She decided that she would save her thoughts for when she was alone. Whenever she had time by herself, she let them come, because it felt like the greatest relief. Not a day passed without her taking a solitary walk, where she could enjoy all the comfort of unpleasant memories.\\n\\nShe was soon going to know Mr. Darcy\u2019s letter almost by heart. She studied every sentence, and her feelings about the man changed at times in very different ways. When she remembered the way he wrote to her, she was still full of anger. But when she thought about how unfairly she had judged him and spoken harshly to him, her anger turned back on herself, and his disappointment became something she felt sorry for.\u201d}]}]}}\"}"
