          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4b34418419c8aa7f78194022\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThis must have been around the same time in Mr. Elliot\u2019s life,\u201d said Anne. \u201cIt\u2019s always been the part I\u2019ve been most curious about. It must have been about when he first became known to my father and my sister. I never met him myself; I only heard about him. Still, there was something in how he behaved toward my father and sister then, and later in the circumstances of his marriage, that I could never fully fit with the way things are now. It made him seem like a different kind of man.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know it all, I know it all,\u201d Mrs. Smith cried. \u201cHe had already been introduced to Sir Walter and your sister before I even knew him, but I heard him talk about them again and again. I know he was invited and encouraged, and I know he chose not to go. I can explain things to you on points you probably wouldn\u2019t expect. And as for his marriage, I knew everything about it at the time. I was there for all the good and bad sides of it. I was the friend he trusted with his hopes and plans. And although I didn\u2019t know his wife before, her lower position in society made that impossible. Still, I knew her well for the rest of her life, at least until the last two years of her life, and I can answer any question you want to ask.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot really,\u201d said Anne. \u201cI don\u2019t need to ask anything special about her. I\u2019ve always understood they weren\u2019t a happy couple. But I would like to know why, at that stage of his life, he would ignore my father\u2019s acquaintance the way he did. My father was clearly ready to treat him with kindness and proper attention. Why did Mr. Elliot pull back?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Elliot,\u201d Mrs. Smith replied, \u201cat that time in his life, had one goal: to make his fortune, and to do it by a faster route than the law. He was determined to build it through marriage. He was determined, at least, not to ruin it with an unwise marriage. And I know it was his belief\u2014whether it was right or not, I can\u2019t say\u2014that your father and sister, with all their politeness and invitations, were trying to set up a match between the heir and the young lady. He thought such a match couldn\u2019t possibly fit what he wanted in terms of wealth and independence. That was why he drew back, I assure you. He told me the whole story. He kept nothing from me. It was strange that, after just leaving you with me in Bath, my first and main acquaintance after I married should be your cousin\u2014and that through him I should keep hearing about your father and sister.\u201d\"}"
