          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5591dac5d33988b947587bb3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The winters around Sullivan\u2019s Island are usually mild, and in the fall it is rare for anyone to think they need a fire. But about the middle of October, 18\u2014, there was one unusually cold day. Just before sunset, I picked my way through the evergreens to my friend\u2019s hut. I hadn\u2019t visited in several weeks. At that time I lived in Charleston, nine miles away from the island, and getting back and forth was much harder than it is now.\\n\\nWhen I reached the hut, I knocked the way I always did. No one answered, so I found the key where I knew it was hidden, unlocked the door, and went in. A good fire was burning on the hearth. It was a welcome sight. I took off my overcoat, sat in an armchair near the crackling logs, and waited for my hosts.\\n\\nNot long after dark, they arrived and greeted me warmly. Jupiter, smiling from ear to ear, hurried around to prepare some marsh-hens for supper. Legrand was in one of his moods\u2014what else could I call them?\u2014full of excitement. He had found an unknown bivalve, one that seemed to form a new genus. Even better, he had tracked down and secured, with Jupiter\u2019s help, a scarab\u00e6us that he believed was completely new. Still, he wanted my opinion about it the next day.\\n\\n\u201cAnd why not tonight?\u201d I asked, warming my hands by the fire, and wishing that whole tribe of scarab\u00e6i would go to the devil.\\n\\n\u201cAh, if I had only known you were here!\u201d Legrand said. \u201cIt\u2019s been so long since I saw you, and how could I guess you would come to visit me on this particular night? On my way home I met Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014 from the fort, and, very foolishly, I lent him the bug. So you won\u2019t be able to see it until morning. Stay here tonight, and I\u2019ll send Jup down for it at sunrise. It\u2019s the loveliest thing in creation!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat?\u2014sunrise?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo nonsense! No\u2014the bug. It\u2019s a brilliant gold color, about the size of a large hickory nut. Near one end of its back it has two jet-black spots, and at the other end there\u2019s another spot, a little longer. The antenn\u00e6 are\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDey aint no tin in him, Massa Will, I keep a tellin on you,\u201d Jupiter interrupted. \u201cDe bug is a goole bug, solid, ebery bit of him, inside an\u2019 all, sep him wing\u2014neber feel half so hebby a bug in my life.\u201d\"}"
