          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"72157bef06feab7238a5fd19\",\"rewritten_text\":\"So I went on for two years. Day after day, so many hundreds passed; they brought no visible change, yet a few of them worked slowly upon my mind as I drifted onward toward death. I began to study more; to share more readily the thoughts of others as they are set down in books; to read history, and to lose the individuality that had once belonged to me. Perhaps, as the sense of immediate suffering faded, I grew more human. Even solitude began to lose some of its charms: I found myself wishing again for sympathy\u2014not that I was tempted to throw myself among the crowd, but that I wanted one friend to love me.\\n\\nYou may think, perhaps, that I was gradually becoming fit to return to society. I do not believe it. The sympathy I longed for would have to be so clean and so stripped of any sway from outward circumstances that, in the world, I could not help but be checked by the coarse materials that mingle, without fail, even with its best feelings. Believe me, I was then less prepared for any true communion with my fellow creatures than before. When I left them, they had tormented me, but in the same manner that pain and sickness torment\u2014something foreign to the mind that irritates it, and that I wished to cast off. Now, though, I should have wanted sympathy; I would have tried to bind my soul to one of theirs, and I would have laid up for myself abundant draughts of disappointment and suffering; for I was as tender as the sensitive plant, all nerve. I did not crave sympathy and help in ambition or wisdom, but sweet and mutual affection\u2014smiles to cheer me and gentle words of comfort. I wanted one heart into which I might pour out my complaints without restraint, and from which, by the heavenly nature of the soil, blessed fruit might grow from such bad seed.\\n\\nBut how could I find this? The love that is the very soul of friendship is a soft spirit, rarely met with unless two amiable creatures are joined from early youth, or unless they are bound together by mutual suffering and pursuits. It comes to some of the elect, unsought and unawares; it falls like gentle dew on chosen places that, though barren before, become fertile in all sweet plants under its kindly influence. Yet when it is desired, it flies; it mocks the prayers of its worshippers; it will grant, but it will not be sought.\\n\\nI knew all this, and I did not go out to look for sympathy. Still, there on my solitary heath, under my lowly roof where everything around was desart, it came to me like a sunbeam in winter\u2014meant to adorn while it helps to melt the drifted snow. Alas, the sun shone on blighted fruit; I did not revive in its radiance, for I was too utterly undone to feel its kindly power.\"}"
