{"request_id":"402a052ddd826110a79aa65f","rewritten_text":"What kind of life would be theirs, while Fate still kept this terrifying fortune balanced on its scales? The marriage had to be postponed, at any cost. He was fully resolved on that. He loved the girl with all his heart, and even the simple touch of her fingers—when they sat together—made every nerve in his body leap with exquisite joy. Yet he understood just as clearly where his duty lay, and he was completely aware that he had no right to marry until he had committed the murder. Once that was done, he could stand before the altar with Sybil Merton and give his life into her hands without fear that he had done wrong. Once that was done, he could take her into his arms, knowing she would never have to blush for him, never have to lower her head in shame. But it had to be done first; and the sooner the better for both of them.

Many men in his position would have preferred the easy road of flirtation to the steep climb of duty; but Lord Arthur was too conscientious to put pleasure above principle. There was more than mere passion in his love. Sybil was, to him, a symbol of everything good and noble. For a moment he felt a natural revulsion at what he was being asked to do, but it didn’t last. His heart told him it wasn’t a sin, but a sacrifice; his reason reminded him that there was no other path open. He had to choose between living for himself and living for others. Terrible as the task laid upon him undoubtedly was, he knew he must not let selfishness win over love. Sooner or later, all of us are forced to face the same kind of choice—the same question is put to all of us. For Lord Arthur it came early in life, before his nature had been spoiled by the calculating cynicism of middle age, or his heart corroded by the shallow, fashionable egotism of our day. He felt no hesitation about doing his duty. Fortunately, too, he wasn’t just a dreamer, or an idle dilettante. If he had been, he would have hesitated, like Hamlet, and let indecision ruin his purpose. But he was fundamentally practical. To him, life meant action more than thought. He had, rarest of all, common sense.

The wild, turbulent feelings of the previous night had completely passed by now, and it was almost with a sense of shame that he looked back on his frantic wandering from street to street, his fierce emotional agony. The very sincerity of his suffering made it seem unreal to him now. He wondered how he could have been so foolish as to rant and rage about the inevitable."}

