{"request_id":"2d061c1ae9f5d07c27fa9c23","rewritten_text":"Do not misunderstand me; I was never truly angry. I was always aware of my condition when my wild thoughts seemed to drive me toward madness, and I never gave them away to anyone—only silence and solitude. People around me saw none of it. They only saw a poor girl broken in spirit, speaking in a low, gentle voice, and from beneath her lowered eyelids tears would sometimes slip out as she tried to hide them. Someone who loved being alone and shrank from being observed; who never smiled—oh, no! I never smiled, and that was all.

Well, I escaped. I left my guardian’s house, and I was never heard from again; it was believed, based on the letters I left and other circumstances, that I had destroyed myself. So I was searched for with less care than might otherwise have been the case, and soon every trace and memory of me was lost. I left London on a small vessel bound for a port in the north of England. And now that I had succeeded in my plan, and was entirely alone, peace returned to me. The sea was calm, and the vessel moved gently onward. I sat on deck beneath the open canopy of the sky, and it seemed to me I had become a different person. Not the wild, raving, and most miserable Mathilda, but a young hermitess devoted to seclusion—someone who must strive to keep her heart free from all turmoil and unholy despair. The strange nunlike dress I had adopted; the knowledge that my very existence was a secret known only to myself; the solitude to which I was henceforth destined—all of it nurtured gentle thoughts in my wounded heart. The breeze that stirred my hair revived me, and I watched with quiet eyes the sunbeams glittering on the waves, and the birds darting over the water, just brushing it with their wings. I slept without being disturbed by dreams, and woke refreshed, ready again to enjoy my tranquil freedom.

In four days we arrived at the harbor we had set out for. I would not stay on the seacoast, but went inland at once. I had already planned where I would live. It would be a solitary house on a wide plain, far from any other habitation—where I could see the entire horizon and wander far without being bothered by the sight of my fellow creatures. I was not a misanthrope, but I felt that the gentle current of my feelings depended on being alone. I chose a wide solitude for myself. On a dreary heath strewn with stones, among which short grass grew, and here and there a few rushes beside a small pool."}

