{"request_id":"3fb9910a597b153912cf65bc","rewritten_text":"There were very few who could claim a heart so untainted, and a humility so genuine of spirit, joined to a steady trust in her own uprightness and a conviction of others’ worth. From her very birth she had led a quiet, withdrawn life. She had lost her mother while still quite young, yet her father had devoted himself to her education—he held many singular notions, and these shaped the plan he adopted for her training. She knew well the heroes of Greece and Rome, and also those of England who had lived some hundred years before; yet she was almost ignorant of the events passing from day to day. She had read only a few authors who had written within at least the last fifty years, though, with that exception, her reading was extensive. So, though she seemed less initiated in the workings of life and society than he, her knowledge was of a deeper sort and rested on surer ground; and had even her beauty and sweetness not drawn him in, her understanding would still have kept him captivated. He looked to her as his guide, and his devotion was such that he took pleasure in adding to his own mind the sense of inferiority which she sometimes stirred in him.

When he was nineteen, his mother died. He left college at once, and for a time shook off his old friends, retiring to the neighbourhood of his Diana, where he found his comfort in her gentle voice and still dearer caresses. This brief separation from his companions gave him the courage to stand by his independence. He felt that, however they might mock his intended marriage, they would not dare show their contempt once it had actually taken place; thus, seeking the consent of his guardian—which he obtained with some difficulty—and also that of the father of his mistress, which was granted more readily, and without letting anyone else know his purpose, by the time he reached his twentieth birthday he had become Diana’s husband.

He loved her with ardour, and her tenderness held such a charm for him that he could think of nothing else. He invited some of his college friends to come and see him, but their light-mindedness sickened him. Diana had lifted the veil that had kept him in boyhood: he was now a man, and he was astonished that he could ever have joined in the hollow talk and notions of his fellow collegiates, or that he had once feared the censure of such people. He cast off his former friendships, not out of fickleness, but because they truly were beneath him."}

