{"request_id":"0e8df4497ffd0adc239a6e8a","rewritten_text":"The next morning, when the Otis family gathered for breakfast, they talked at length about the ghost. The United States Minister was naturally a little annoyed to learn that his gift had not been accepted. “I have no wish,” he said, “to do the ghost any personal injury, and I must say that, considering how long he has been in the house, I don’t think it’s at all polite to throw pillows at him”—a very fair point, and, I’m sorry to say, the twins burst into laughter at it. “On the other hand,” he went on, “if he really refuses to use the Rising Sun Lubricator, we’ll have to take his chains from him. It would be quite impossible to sleep, with all that noise going on outside the bedrooms.”

For the rest of the week, however, they were left in peace. The only thing that drew any attention was the constant renewal of the blood-stain on the library floor. That was certainly very strange, since Mr. Otis always locked the door at night, and the windows were kept tightly barred. The stain’s chameleon-like color also sparked plenty of discussion. Some mornings it was a dull (almost Indian) red; then it would turn vermilion; then a rich purple; and once, when they came down for family prayers, following the simple rites of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, they found it a bright emerald-green. These kaleidoscopic changes amused everyone, and bets about what it would be like were made freely every evening. The only person who didn’t join in was little Virginia, who, for reasons no one could explain, was always very upset at the sight of the blood-stain, and nearly cried the morning it turned emerald-green.

The ghost’s second appearance came on Sunday night. Shortly after they had gone to bed, they were suddenly alarmed by a terrible crash in the hall. Rushing downstairs, they found that a large suit of old armor had come loose from its stand and fallen onto the stone floor, while, seated in a high-backed chair, was the Canterville ghost, rubbing his knees with an expression of acute agony. The twins, having brought their pea-shooters with them, immediately fired two pellets at him, with the kind of accuracy that only long, careful practice under a writing-master can produce. The United States Minister covered him with his revolver and, in accordance with Californian etiquette, ordered him to hold up his hands. The ghost sprang up with a wild shriek of rage, swept through them like a mist, and as he passed he put out Washington Otis’s candle, leaving them all in total darkness."}

