{"request_id":"700f7f12edf0ea34880b43e9","rewritten_text":"Storm spirits, receive me! Powers of destruction, open wide your arms and hold me forever! If a kinder power has not decreed some other end—so that after long endurance I may reap my reward, and again feel my heart beating close to the heart of another like to me.

Tiber—the road laid out by nature herself, threading her continent—was at my feet, and many a boat was tied along the banks. With just a few books, provisions, and my dog, I would board one of them and float down the stream into the sea; then, staying near the shore, I would sail along the beautiful coasts and sunny headlands of the blue Mediterranean, pass Naples, go on through Calabria, and dare the twin dangers of Scylla and Charybdis. Then, with fearless intent (for what did I have to lose?), I would skim the ocean’s surface toward Malta and the farther Cyclades. I would avoid Constantinople, whose familiar towers and inlets belong to a different world from the one I’m in now; I would coast Asia Minor and Syria, and, passing the seven-mouthed Nile, steer north again until I lost sight of forgotten Carthage and deserted Libya, and reached the Pillars of Hercules. And then—no matter where—the damp caves and soundless depths of the ocean may be my home, before I complete this long, drawn-out voyage, or the arrow of disease finds my heart while I drift alone on the churning Mediterranean; or, in some place I stop at, I may find what I’m looking for—a companion. But if not—then to endless time, decrepit and gray-headed—youth already in the grave with those I love—the lone wanderer will still unfurl his sail, grip the tiller, and, still obeying the breezes of heaven, circle forever around one promontory and then another, anchoring in bay after bay, still plowing a sea without seed, leaving behind the green land of my native Europe, down the tawny shore of Africa, having weathered the fierce seas of the Cape, I may moor my worn skiff in a creek shaded by the spicy groves of the fragrant islands of the far Indian ocean.

These are wild dreams. Yet since, a week ago, they came over me as I stood on the height of St. Peter’s, they have ruled my imagination. I’ve chosen my boat and laid in my meager supplies. I’ve picked out a few books—the main ones are Homer and Shakespeare—though the libraries of the world are opened to me, and in any port I can restock. I don’t expect things to get better; but the monotonous present is unbearable to me."}

