          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"488f6fc4e0d50765ccbfa5ac\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These deputies had been sent by them to Adrian, who had been chosen arbiter; and they had made the journey from Paris to Calais, passing through empty towns and desolate fields, all the while feeding a fierce hatred toward one another; and now, with no restraint of party feeling, they urged their several cases.\\n\\nBy looking at the deputies one by one, and after a thorough inquiry, we learned the real state of affairs in Paris. Since parliament had named him Ryland\u2019s deputy, all the surviving English had submitted to Adrian. He was our captain, to lead us from our native soil into unknown regions; our lawgiver and our preserver. At the first settling of our emigration plan, no lasting separation of our members had been intended, and the command of the whole body, rising by degrees in authority, reached its height in the Earl of Windsor. But unforeseen circumstances overturned our arrangements, and forced the greater part of our number to be parted from the supreme chief for nearly two months. They had crossed in two separate groups; and when they arrived in Paris, quarrelling sprang up between them.\\n\\nThey found Paris a desert. At the beginning, when the plague first showed itself, the return of travellers and merchants, along with communications by letter, kept us informed of the ravages disease was making on the continent. Yet as mortality increased, that intercourse dwindled and finally stopped. Even within England, communication from one part of the island to another grew slow and uncommon. No vessel ever pushed through the tide that lay between Calais and Dover; or if some miserable passenger, anxious to learn whether relatives still lived, put out from the French shore to come back among us, the greedy ocean would often swallow his small craft, or, after a day or two, he would be infected by the disorder and die before he could tell us the story of France\u2019s desolation. Thus we were, to a great extent, ignorant of what was happening on the continent, and we still had a dim hope of finding many companions in its wide path. But the very causes that had so fearfully thinned the English nation gave the sister land even broader room for mischief. France was a blank; along the long road from Calais to Paris not a single human being was met. In Paris, however, there were a few\u2014perhaps a hundred\u2014who, resigned to what awaited them, drifted about the streets of the capital and gathered to talk of former days, with a liveliness, and even a kind of gaiety, that rarely deserts the individuals of this people.\\n\\nThe English took undisputed possession of Paris. Its tall houses and narrow streets lay lifeless. A few wan figures could be seen at the usual meeting-place at the Tuileries; they wondered why the islanders should come toward their ill-fated city\u2014for in the extremity of misery, those afflicted always fancy that their share of the calamity is the bitterest; so that, while enduring intense pain, we would trade the particular torture we are writhing under for any other that might strike a different part of the body.\"}"
