          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6a0f5676a4ff15c84ad2174a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNo, no\u2014that\u2019s for tomorrow. I especially wanted to see Knightley today, for that very reason. What a dreadful, scorching morning! I even went across the fields too,\u201d she added, sounding very put out, \u201cand that only made it worse. And then he wasn\u2019t at home! I assure you, I\u2019m really not pleased. There was no apology, no message for me. The housekeeper said she had no idea I was expected. How strange! And nobody knew at all where he had gone. Maybe to Hartfield, maybe to the Abbey Mill, maybe into his woods. Miss Woodhouse, this isn\u2019t like our friend Knightley. Can you explain it?\u201d\\n\\nEmma amused herself by saying, with protests, that it was indeed very extraordinary, and that she had nothing at all to say in his defense.\\n\\n\u201cI can\u2019t imagine,\u201d said Mrs. Elton, sounding indignant in the way a wife ought to, \u201cI can\u2019t imagine how he could do something like this to you, of all people in the world. You\u2019re the very last person one would expect to be forgotten. My dear Mr. E., he must have left you a message. He must have. Not even Knightley could be so eccentric\u2014and his servants forgot it. I\u2019m sure that\u2019s what happened. And it\u2019s very likely with the Donwell servants, who, as I\u2019ve often noticed, are all extremely awkward and careless. I\u2019m sure I wouldn\u2019t have someone like his Harry standing at our sideboard for any reason. And as for Mrs. Hodges, Wright thinks very little of her. She promised Wright a receipt and never sent it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI met William Larkins,\u201d Mr. Elton went on, \u201cas I was getting near the house, and he told me I wouldn\u2019t find his master at home. But I didn\u2019t believe him. William seemed rather annoyed. He said he didn\u2019t know what had happened to his master lately, but he could hardly ever get him to speak. I don\u2019t deal with William\u2019s problems, but it really is very important that I should see Knightley today. So it\u2019s a serious inconvenience that I\u2019ve taken this hot walk for nothing.\u201d\\n\\nEmma felt she couldn\u2019t do better than go home at once. She was probably being waited for there right now, and Mr. Knightley might be kept from getting even more aggressive toward Mr. Elton\u2014if not toward William Larkins.\\n\\nWhen she said goodbye, she was pleased to see Miss Fairfax determined to follow her out of the room and even downstairs. It gave her a chance, which she immediately used to say,\u201d\"}"
