{"request_id":"9430d2c183dc0effcebc8463","rewritten_text":"Summer moved in, and, crowned with the sun’s strong rays, the plague sent its deadly arrows over the land. The nations under its power bowed their heads and died. The corn that had grown in great abundance lay rotting on the ground by autumn, while the poor man who had gone out to gather bread for his children lay stiff in the furrow, struck down by the plague. The green woods waved their branches proudly, but the dying were spread beneath their shade, answering the quiet song of the place with harsh, broken cries. Painted birds fluttered through the shadows; the deer rested safely on the fern. The oxen and horses wandered away from their unguarded stables and grazed among the wheat, because death seemed to fall on people alone.

With summer and death came our fear. My poor love and I looked at each other, and at our babies. “We will save them, Idris,” I said. “I will save them. Years from now we will tell them about our fears, and then those fears will be gone. Even if only they remain on the earth, they will live, and their cheeks will not turn pale, nor will their sweet voices fade.” Our eldest understood some of what was happening, and now and then he looked at me with serious eyes and asked why there was so much ruin. But he was only ten, and the bright joy of youth soon drove away any worry from his face. Evelyn, our laughing cherub, our playful little child, had no idea of pain or sorrow. He would shake his light curls back from his eyes and fill the halls with his laughter, drawing everyone’s attention to his games in a thousand simple ways. Clara, our lovely gentle Clara, was our support, our comfort, and our joy. She made it her work to care for the sick, comfort those who were grieving, help the elderly, and join in the children’s play, bringing back their happiness. She moved through the rooms like a good spirit, sent from a heavenly place, to brighten our dark hours with a kind of beauty that felt foreign to us. Wherever her steps went, people showed gratitude and praise. Still, when she stood quietly among us, playing with our children, or with a girl’s steady care doing small kind things for Idris, we wondered where, in her pure loveliness and in the soft sound of her voice, so much courage, wisdom, and active goodness could possibly be found.

Summer dragged on, because we hoped that winter would at least slow the disease. To believe it would disappear completely was a hope too precious, too painful to say out loud. When someone spoke of it carelessly, the listeners burst into tears and sobs, showing just how deep their fears were and how small their hopes."}

