          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fd30537a580278781c2bed14\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The narrow streets and alleys, at last, opened into a large open space. Scattered around it were pens for animals and other signs of a cattle market. When Sikes reached this spot, he slowed down; the girl could barely keep going anymore, not at the fast pace they\u2019d been walking. He turned to Oliver and roughly ordered him to take Nancy\u2019s hand.\\n\\n\u201cDo you hear?\u201d Sikes snarled as Oliver hesitated and looked around.\\n\\nThey were tucked into a dark corner, well out of the way of any passengers.\\n\\nOliver could see, all too clearly, that resisting would do no good. He held out his hand, and Nancy gripped it tightly in hers.\\n\\n\u201cGive me the other,\u201d said Sikes, grabbing Oliver\u2019s free hand. \u201cHere, Bull\u2019s-Eye!\u201d\\n\\nThe dog looked up and growled.\\n\\n\u201cLook here, boy!\u201d Sikes said, pressing his other hand to Oliver\u2019s throat. \u201cIf he says even a soft word, hold him! You understand!\u201d\\n\\nThe dog growled again, and licked its lips, staring at Oliver as if it couldn\u2019t wait to clamp onto his windpipe.\\n\\n\u201cHe\u2019s as willing as a Christian\u2014strike me blind if he isn\u2019t!\u201d Sikes said, watching the animal with grim, ferocious approval. \u201cNow you know what you\u2019ve got to expect, master, so call out as quick as you like; the dog will end that game soon enough. Come on, young \u2019un!\u201d\\n\\nBull\u2019s-Eye wagged his tail in response to this unusually affectionate way of speaking, and after another warning growl meant for Oliver, he led the way onward.\\n\\nThey were crossing Smithfield, though it might as well have been Grosvenor Square, for all Oliver knew. The night was dark and foggy. The lights in the shops could barely push through the thick mist, which seemed to grow heavier by the moment, swallowing the streets and houses in gloom. Everything looked unfamiliar to Oliver, and