          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"241373c975898371379030fd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I call on you, spirits of the dead, and on you, wandering ministers of vengeance, to help me and guide me in my work. Let the cursed, hellish monster drink in deep agony. Let him feel the despair that torments me now.\u201d\\n\\nI began my words with solemn care and a kind of awe, almost certain that the shadows of my murdered friends were listening and approving my devotion. But as I finished, the furies took hold of me, and rage choked my speech.\\n\\nIn the silence of the night, I was answered by a loud, fiendish laugh. It echoed in my ears for a long time, heavy and mocking. The mountains seemed to repeat it, and I felt as if all of hell surrounded me just to laugh at me. I truly thought that in that moment I would lose my mind and destroy my miserable life\u2014except that my vow was heard, and I was meant to live for vengeance. The laughter stopped. Then, from what seemed to be right beside my ear, a voice I knew well and hated spoke in a whisper you could hear: \u201cI am satisfied, miserable wretch! You have decided to live, and I am satisfied.\u201d\\n\\nI rushed toward where the sound came from, but the devil slipped out of my reach. Then the moon rose and shone fully on his ghastly, twisted form as he fled with more speed than any mortal could have.\\n\\nI followed him, and for many months this has been my task. With only a small clue to guide me, I traced his path along the bends of the Rhone, but it led nowhere. I reached the blue Mediterranean, and by some strange chance I saw the fiend enter by night and hide himself in a ship bound for the Black Sea. I took passage on the same ship, but he escaped\u2014somehow I still do not know how.\\n\\nEven in the wild lands of Tartary and Russia, though he keeps slipping away, I have kept on his trail. Sometimes the peasants, terrified by the sight of this horrible apparition, told me where he had gone. Other times, he himself left some sign to guide me, because he feared that if I lost all trace of him I would despair and die. Snow fell on my head, and on the white ground I saw the mark of his huge step. You who are just starting life, to whom worry is new and pain is still unknown\u2014how could you understand what I have felt, and still feel? Cold, hunger, and exhaustion were only the smallest of the sufferings I was forced to endure. I was cursed by some devil, and I carried my eternal hell with me. Still, a spirit of good followed me and guided my steps, and whenever I complained the most, it would suddenly pull me out of troubles that seemed impossible to escape. Sometimes, when hunger overcame my body and I sank into exhaustion, a meal would be prepared for me in the desert. It would restore my strength and give me new spirit.\"}"
