          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"df1579c58145e305d088962a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"All this, and more\u2014far more than I can easily set down\u2014would I gladly lay before you. Yet, to keep it short, I must have my reward. I long to be back with my family and in my own home; and, as the price of any further communication from me\u2014in return for the light I am able to shed upon many matters, both important branches of physical and metaphysical science\u2014I must ask, through the good offices of your honorable body, a pardon for the crime I have committed in the death of the creditors when I left Rotterdam. Such, then, is the purpose of this paper. Its bearer, an inhabitant of the moon, whom I have persuaded, and properly instructed, to act as my messenger to the earth, will wait upon your Excellencies\u2019 pleasure, and return to me with the pardon in question, should it be obtained in any way.\\n\\n\u201cI have the honor to be, etc., your Excellencies\u2019 very humble servant,\\n\\n\u201cHANS PFAALL.\u201d\\n\\nWhen Professor Rub-a-dub had finished reading this most extraordinary document, it is said that, in the height of his astonishment, he let his pipe fall to the ground. Mynheer Superbus Von Underduk, having removed his spectacles, wiped them, and then\u2014so completely did he forget both himself and his usual dignity\u2014turned round three times upon his heel in the very quintessence of astonishment and admiration. There could be no question of it: the pardon must be secured. So, at least, swore Professor Rub-a-dub with a hearty oath; and so, in the end, thought the illustrious Von Underduk, as he took his brother in science by the arm. Without saying a word, he set off at once on his way home, to consider what steps ought to be taken. Yet when they reached the door of the burgomaster\u2019s dwelling, the professor ventured to remark that, since the messenger had chosen to vanish\u2014no doubt terrified out of his wits by the savage look of the burghers of Rotterdam\u2014the pardon would be of little account, because no one but a man of the moon would undertake so immense a journey. The burgomaster agreed that this was true, and the matter was therefore concluded. Not so, however, with rumors and conjectures. Once the letter was published, it stirred up a whole crop of gossip and opinions. Some of the over-wise even made themselves ridiculous by denouncing the affair as a mere hoax. But hoax, with people of that sort, is, I take it, a catch-all name for anything beyond their understanding. As for me, I cannot see what grounds they have for such an accusation. Let us hear what they say:\"}"
