          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0e5770ac2741a06b7df27e0a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI hope I shall soon have the pleasure of introducing my son to you,\u201d said Mr. Weston.\\n\\nMrs. Elton, quite ready to take it as a compliment meant for her, smiled with the utmost graciousness.\\n\\n\u201cYou have heard of a certain Frank Churchill, I presume,\u201d he went on\u2014\u201cand you know him to be my son, though he does not go by my name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! yes, and I shall be very glad of the acquaintance. I am sure Mr. Elton will not delay in calling on him; and we shall both have great satisfaction in seeing him at the Vicarage.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are very kind.\u2014Frank will be exceedingly happy, I am sure.\u2014He is to be in town next week, if not sooner. We have word of it in a letter received to-day. I came upon the letters this morning, and, seeing my son\u2019s hand, I supposed I might open it\u2014though it was not addressed to me\u2014it was meant for Mrs. Weston. She is his chief correspondent, I assure you. I scarcely ever get a letter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd so you actually opened what was meant for her! Oh! Mr. Weston\u2014(laughing in a studied way) I must object to that. It is a very perilous precedent indeed!\u2014Pray do not let your neighbours take your cue.\u2014Upon my word, if this is what I am to expect, married women must begin to look to themselves!\u2014Oh! Mr. Weston, I never could have thought it of you!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAye, we men are poor creatures. You must look out for yourself, Mrs. Elton.\u2014This letter tells us\u2014though it is a short one\u2014written in haste, only to give us notice\u2014that they are all coming up to town at once, on Mrs. Churchill\u2019s account. She has not been well all winter, and thinks Enscombe too cold for her\u2014so they are to move southward without delay.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u2014from Yorkshire, I suppose. Is Enscombe in Yorkshire?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, they are about one hundred and ninety miles from London; it is a long journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, upon my word, a very long one. Sixty-five miles farther than from Maple Grove to London. But what is distance, Mr. Weston, to people of large fortune?\u2014You would be astonished to hear how my brother, Mr. Suckling, will sometimes dart about. You can hardly believe me\u2014but twice in one week he and Mr. Bragge went to London and back again with four horses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe trouble of the distance from Enscombe,\u201d said Mr. Weston, \u201cis, that Mrs. Churchill, _as_ _we_ _understand_, has not been able to leave the sofa for a week at a time. In Frank\u2019s last letter she complained, he said, that she was too weak to get into her conservatory without having both his arm and his uncle\u2019s!\u201d}\"}"
