          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"549076708134d2f7c3d959cb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After everyone had shared their sorrow about the terrible outcome of this event\u2014which Elizabeth thought was almost certain, and Miss Bennet couldn\u2019t say was completely impossible\u2014the first woman went on. \u201cBut tell me everything about it that I haven\u2019t already heard. Give me more details. What did Colonel Forster say? Before the elopement happened, didn\u2019t anyone have any reason to worry? They must have known they were together for good.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cColonel Forster admitted that he had often suspected some feelings, especially on Lydia\u2019s side, but nothing that would make him fear the worst. I\u2019m so sorry for him. His manner was attentive and kind. He was actually on his way to us to show how concerned he was, before he even knew that they weren\u2019t going to Scotland. The moment that fear became public, it made him hurry his trip.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd was Denny sure that Wickham would not marry her? Did he know they planned to run off? Did Colonel Forster speak to Denny himself?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; but when Denny was questioned by him, Denny denied knowing anything about their plan and wouldn\u2019t give his real opinion. He didn\u2019t repeat what he believed about them not marrying, and that makes me hope he may have been misunderstood before.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd until Colonel Forster arrived, none of you had any doubt that they were truly married?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow could such an idea even enter our minds? I felt a little uneasy, a little afraid for my sister\u2019s happiness with him in marriage, because I knew his behavior had not always been completely right. My father and mother knew nothing of it. They only thought the match was foolish. Kitty then admitted, with a very natural sense of pride in knowing more than the rest of us, that in Lydia\u2019s last letter she had warned her to expect something like this. It seems she had known for many weeks that Lydia and Wickham were in love with each other.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not before they went to Brighton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, I don\u2019t think so.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did Colonel Forster seem to think badly of Wickham himself? Does he know what Wickham is really like?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have to admit he didn\u2019t speak of Wickham as well as he used to. He thought him imprudent and extravagant. And since this sad affair, it\u2019s said that he left Meryton with a great deal of debt, though I hope that isn\u2019t true.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Jane, if we had been less secret, if we had told what we knew about him, this could not have happened!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMaybe it would have been better,\u201d her sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cBut to point out someone\u2019s earlier faults, without knowing what their feelings are now, seems unfair.\u201d\"}"
