          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a14626de81fd4edb50f2f269\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But how could I, a poor, dazed village boy, avoid that wonderful inconsistency that even the best and wisest men fall into every day?\\n\\n\u201cIt may be all quite true,\u201d I said to Biddy, \u201cbut I admire her dreadfully.\u201d\\n\\nIn short, when it came to that, I turned over on my face and got a good grip of the hair on each side of my head, and yanked it hard. All the while I knew how mad my heart was to be so mad and so completely misplaced; I was fully aware that it would have served my face right if I\u2019d lifted it up by my hair and knocked it against the pebbles as punishment for being such an idiot.\\n\\nBiddy was the wisest of girls, and she tried not to argue with me any more. She put her hand\u2014comfortable, though roughened by work\u2014on my hands, one after another, and gently pulled them out of my hair. Then she softly patted my shoulder in a soothing way, while my face was pressed against my sleeve and I cried a little\u2014just as I had in the brewery yard\u2014and I felt vaguely convinced that I was being badly treated by someone, or by everyone; I couldn\u2019t say which.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m glad of one thing,\u201d said Biddy. \u201cAnd that is, that you\u2019ve felt you could trust me with your confidence, Pip. And I\u2019m glad of another thing: that of course you know you can depend on me to keep it safe, and to deserve it as far as I can. If your first teacher\u2014dear me, such a poor one, and so much in need of being taught herself\u2014had been your teacher at the present time, she thinks she would know what lesson she\u2019d set you. But it would be a hard one to learn, and you\u2019ve already gone beyond her, so it\u2019s no use now.\u201d With a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank, and then, with a fresh and pleasant change of voice, asked, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I cried, getting up, putting my arm around her neck, and kissing her, \u201cI shall always tell you everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUntil you\u2019re a gentleman,\u201d said Biddy.\\n\\n\u201cYou know I never shall be, so that\u2019s settled. And it\u2019s not as if I have to tell you anything, because you already know everything I know\u2014as I told you at home the other night.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Biddy, in a whisper, as she looked away at the ships. Then, with that same pleasant change of voice, she repeated, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\nI told Biddy we would walk a little farther, and we did. The summer afternoon faded into summer evening, and it was very beautiful.\"}"
