{"request_id":"f53149aba663a7ad5d1ac60d","rewritten_text":"I bored a hole in the end of the keg, right over the pitcher, and then fitted in a plug of soft wood cut into a tapered, cone shape. I pushed the plug in or pulled it out, depending on what I needed, until—after a few tries—it fit tightly enough that the water oozing from the hole would fill the pitcher to the brim in sixty minutes. Of course, I could check this quickly by watching how much of the pitcher filled in a given amount of time. Once I had that set, the rest of the plan was clear. I arranged my bed on the floor of the car so that, when I lay down, my head was directly under the pitcher’s mouth. It was obvious that after an hour the pitcher would be full, and then the water would spill over. It would spill over at the mouth, which sat a little lower than the rim. It was also obvious that water falling from more than four feet up would have to land on my face, and that the result would be that I would wake up at once, even if I were sleeping more soundly than anyone could.

“It was already fully eleven when I finished these arrangements, and I went straight to bed, confident that my invention would work. I was not disappointed. Every sixty minutes, exactly on time, my trusty chronometer woke me. I would then empty the pitcher into the keg through the bung-hole, do the condenser’s work, and go back to bed. These regular interruptions bothered me even less than I expected. When I finally got up for the day, it was seven o’clock, and the sun had risen many degrees above the line of my horizon.

“April 3d. I found the balloon at an immense height, and the earth’s curve looked much more pronounced. Below me in the ocean, I saw a cluster of black specks, which were surely islands. Far to the north, I noticed a thin, white, and extremely bright line, or streak, along the edge of the horizon, and I had no doubt it was the southern edge of the Polar Sea’s ice. My curiosity was strongly stirred, because I hoped to go much farther north and might, at some point, find myself directly above the Pole. I now regretted that my great height would make it harder to take the accurate survey I wanted.”"}

