{"request_id":"d7e4d4be6b2182399f326998","rewritten_text":"They were her life—more precious than any place set aside for love, more precious than anything else the earth held. The boys listened with childish delight to our talk about leaving. Clara asked whether we were going to Athens. “It’s possible,” I said, and her face brightened with joy. There she would see the tomb of her parents, and the land would be filled with memories of her father’s great name. She had been thinking about these scenes in silence, again and again, without stopping. Remembering them had turned her babyish cheer into something serious, and it had filled her with high, restless thoughts.

We had many dear friends we could not leave behind, even though they were humble. There was the spirited, obedient horse Lord Raymond had given his daughter. There was Alfred’s dog, and a pet eagle whose sight had grown dim with age. Still, listing all these favorites we would take with us only made the losses feel heavier. It brought a deep sigh for everything we would have to leave. Idris’s eyes filled with tears. Alfred and Evelyn brought out one favorite rose tree after another, and then a marble vase beautifully carved, insisting that these must go. They kept saying it was a pity we couldn’t take the castle and the forest, the deer and the birds, and all the familiar, cherished things with us. “Tender and foolish ones,” I said, “we have lost treasures far more precious than these. We are leaving them behind so we can protect treasures that, compared to them, are nothing. Let us not forget for even a moment our goal and our hope. Then they will become a solid mound that will stop the flood of our regret for small things.”

The children were easily distracted, and soon they were thinking again about future fun. Idris was gone. She had gone to hide her weakness. Leaving the castle, she went down to the little park, looking for solitude so she could give in to her tears. I found her clinging to an old oak, pressing her rose-colored lips to its rough trunk as her tears fell freely. Her sobs and broken outbursts of feeling could not be held back. With overwhelming grief, I saw this beloved person of my heart lost in sorrow. I pulled her toward me. When she felt my kisses on her eyelids and my arms around her, she returned to the awareness of what still remained. “You are very kind not to blame me,” she said. “I am crying, and a sharp pain of unbearable sorrow tears at my heart. And yet I am happy. Mothers mourn their children, wives lose their husbands, but you and my children are left to me.”"}

