          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0d4d4f6b6501ebd2698ec4ff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I could still recall what it was like during our great civil war in the nineteenth century. In the North, a carpenter earned three dollars a day, reckoned in gold; in the South, he earned fifty\u2014paid out in Confederate shinplasters that were supposed to be worth a dollar a bushel. In the North, a set of overalls cost three dollars, which was a day\u2019s wages; in the South, it ran to seventy-five\u2014two days\u2019 wages. Everything else ran in the same way. So wages were twice as high in the North as in the South, since the one wage bought twice as much as the other.\\n\\nYes, I made a number of acquaintances in the hamlet, and what pleased me quite a bit was to see our new coins actually turning up in circulation\u2014plenty of milrays, plenty of mills, lots of cents, a good many nickels, and some silver; all of it among the artisans and the common people generally. Yes, and even some gold\u2014but that was at the bank, meaning the goldsmith\u2019s. I went in while Marco, the son of Marco, was arguing with a shopkeeper about a quarter of a pound of salt, and I asked them to give me change for a twenty-dollar gold piece. They did\u2014after they had worried it between their teeth, tapped it on the counter, tested it with acid, and then asked me where I\u2019d gotten it, who I was, where I came from, where I was headed, when I expected to arrive, and probably a couple of hundred more questions besides. When they finally ran out of things to try, I kept right on supplying them with information on my own: I told them I owned a dog named Watch, that my first wife was a Free Will Baptist, that her grandfather was a Prohibitionist, that I used to know a man who had two thumbs on each hand and a wart on the inside of his upper lip, and that he died hoping for a glorious resurrection\u2014on and on and on\u2014until even that hungry little village questioner began to look satisfied, and a shade of relief came over him. Still, he had to treat a man with my financial standing, so he didn\u2019t give me any backtalk, though I noticed he took it out on his underlings, which seemed perfectly natural enough. Yes, they changed my twenty, but I figured I\u2019d strained the bank a bit, which was to be expected: it was like walking into a small village store in the nineteenth century and suddenly asking the proprietor to break a two-thousand-dollar bill for you. He might be able to do it, sure\u2014but he\u2019d also wonder how a small farmer happened to be carrying that kind of money in his pocket. That was probably the goldsmith\u2019s thought as well; because he followed me to the door and stood there watching me go, staring with reverent admiration.\"}"
