          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6b9343a6d5c25c8f47937ea4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If, though, you\u2019re after one for use at home, I can provide you with an excellent article, and I\u2019ll see that you\u2019re satisfied with the outcome. May I ask who it\u2019s meant for? If it\u2019s for the police, or for anyone tied to Scotland Yard, I\u2019m afraid I can\u2019t help you. English detectives are really our best friends; I\u2019ve always found that if we lean on their stupidity, we can carry out exactly what we please. I couldn\u2019t spare even one of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you,\u201d said Lord Arthur, \u201cit has nothing to do with the police at all. As a matter of fact, the clock is intended for the Dean of Chichester.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood heavens! I had no idea you cared so much about religion, Lord Arthur. Very few young men feel that way these days.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid you\u2019re overestimating me, Herr Winckelkopf,\u201d Lord Arthur said, blushing. \u201cThe truth is, I really know nothing about theology.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen it\u2019s purely a private affair?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPurely private.\u201d\\n\\nHerr Winckelkopf gave a shrug and left the room, coming back a few minutes later with a round cake of dynamite no bigger than a penny, along with a neat little French clock, topped by an ormolu figure of Liberty trampling on the hydra of Despotism.\\n\\nLord Arthur\u2019s expression brightened as soon as he saw it. \u201cThat\u2019s exactly what I want,\u201d he exclaimed. \u201cNow tell me how it goes off.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! there\u2019s my secret,\u201d replied Herr Winckelkopf, regarding his contrivance with a look of perfectly justified pride. \u201cTell me when you want it to explode, and I\u2019ll set the machine for the moment.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, today is Tuesday, and if you could send it off right away\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat won\u2019t be possible; I have a great deal of important work to do for some friends of mine in Moscow. Still, I might manage to send it tomorrow.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, it will be plenty of time!\u201d Lord Arthur said, courteous as ever. \u201cIf it\u2019s delivered tomorrow night or Thursday morning. For the explosion itself\u2014say Friday at noon, precisely. The Dean is always at home at that hour.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFriday, at noon,\u201d Herr Winckelkopf repeated, and he jotted it down in a large ledger lying on a bureau near the fireplace.\\n\\n\u201cAnd now,\u201d said Lord Arthur, rising from his seat, \u201cpray let me know how much I owe you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s such a small matter, Lord Arthur, that I don\u2019t care to make a charge. The dynamite comes to seven and sixpence, the clock is three pounds ten, and the carriage about five shillings. I\u2019m only too glad to oblige any friend of Count Rouvaloff\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut what about your trouble, Herr Winckelkopf?\u201d\"}"
