{"request_id":"a6a154aaaeaad32ddb2e4777","rewritten_text":"The following morning, when the Otis family gathered for breakfast, they talked at some length about the ghost. The United States Minister, as you might expect, was a bit irritated to learn that his gift had not been taken. ‘I have no intention,’ he said, ‘of causing the ghost any personal harm; and I can’t help thinking that, after how long he’s been in the house, it isn’t exactly good manners to throw pillows at him’—a remark, I regret to say, that sent the twins into loud bursts of laughter. ‘On the other hand,’ he went on, ‘if he truly refuses to make use of the Rising Sun Lubricator, then we’ll have to remove his chains. It would be utterly impossible to sleep, with all that noise carrying on outside the bedrooms.’

For the rest of the week, though, they remained untroubled; the only thing drawing notice was the constant freshening of the blood-stain on the library floor. That was certainly odd, since Mr. Otis always locked the door at night, and the windows were kept tightly barred. Even the stain’s chameleon-like colouring drew plenty of talk. Some mornings it appeared a dull (almost Indian) red; then it would turn vermilion; then a deep purple; and once, when they came down for family prayers—according to the plain observances of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church—they discovered it had become a bright emerald-green. Naturally, these kaleidoscopic shifts amused everyone, and bets were made freely each evening. The only one who didn’t join in was little Virginia, who, for reasons no one could explain, was always quite upset at the sight of the blood-stain, and was close to crying the morning it turned emerald-green.

The ghost appeared again on Sunday night. Not long after they had gone to bed, they were suddenly startled by a dreadful crash out in the hall. They hurried downstairs and found that a large suit of old armour had come loose from its stand and fallen onto the stone floor. Seated in a high-backed chair was the Canterville ghost, rubbing his knees with an expression of intense suffering. The twins, having brought their pea-shooters with them, immediately fired two pellets at him, with the kind of steady accuracy that only long, careful practice under a writing-master can produce, while the United States Minister kept him covered with his revolver and, in keeping with Californian etiquette, demanded that he hold up his hands. The ghost sprang up with a furious shriek, swept through them like a mist, and as he passed he put out Washington Otis’s candle, leaving them all in complete darkness."}

