          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7b86ab92ceb32e355e5e4f8d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Wake up sooner or later and look around to see what caused it. Maybe you\u2019d catch a steamboat coughing along upstream, so far off toward the other side that you couldn\u2019t make out anything about her\u2014only whether she was a stern-wheel or a side-wheel. Then for about an hour there\u2019d be nothing to hear and nothing to see\u2014just solid loneliness. Next you\u2019d see a raft sliding by, way out yonder, and maybe some guy on it chopping, because they\u2019re always doing it on a raft; you\u2019d see the axe flash and come down\u2014you wouldn\u2019t hear a thing. You\u2019d watch that axe go up again, and by the time it\u2019s above the man\u2019s head then you\u2019d hear the _k\u2019chunk!_\u2014it took all that time for the sound to cross the water. So we\u2019d spend the day drifting around, listening to the stillness.\\n\\nOnce there was a thick fog, and the rafts and all the other things going by were beating tin pans so the steamboats wouldn\u2019t run over them. A scow or a raft would pass so close we could hear them talking and cussing and laughing\u2014we heard them plain\u2014but we couldn\u2019t see any sign of them. It made you feel creepy, like spirits carrying on out there in the air. Jim said he believed it was spirits; but I said:\\n\\n\u201cNo; spirits wouldn\u2019t say, \u2018Dern the dern fog.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nThe moment night came, we pushed off. When we got her out to about the middle, we left her alone and let her float wherever the current wanted. Then we lit the pipes, dangled our legs in the water, and talked about all kinds of things\u2014we were always naked, day and night, whenever the mosquitoes would let us. The new clothes Buck\u2019s folks made for me were too good to be comfortable, and besides, I didn\u2019t care much for clothes anyway.\\n\\nSometimes we\u2019d have the whole river to ourselves for the longest time. Over there were the banks and the islands, across the water; and maybe a spark\u2014like a candle in a cabin window. Sometimes on the water you could see a spark or two, on a raft or a scow, you know; and sometimes you could hear a fiddle or a song coming over from one of those crafts. It\u2019s lovely to live on a raft. We had the sky right above us, all speckled with stars, and we\u2019d lie on our backs and look up at them, talking about whether they were made or just happened. Jim said he thought they were made, but I said they just happened. I figured it would\u2019ve taken too long to _make_ that many.\"}"
