          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7af5606bef26fe7dd8a4faed\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Ten minutes later, with his face drained of color by terror and his eyes wild with grief, Lord Arthur Savile burst out of Bentinck House, pushing through the ring of fur-coated footmen around the broad striped awning, as if nothing he saw or heard could reach him. The night was bitterly cold; the gas-lamps about the square flared and fluttered in the sharp wind. Yet his hands burned with fever, and his forehead felt as though it were on fire. On and on he went, almost swaying along like a drunken man. A policeman glanced at him as he passed, and a beggar who had been lounging from an archway to beg for alms grew afraid, seeing a misery greater than his own. He paused beneath a lamp and stared at his hands. He believed he could already make out the stain of blood upon them, and a thin cry slipped from his trembling mouth.\\n\\nMurder! That was what the cheiromantist had read there. Murder! The very night seemed to recognize it, and the desolate wind to carry the word into his ear. The dark corners of the streets were thick with it. It stared at him from the roofs of the houses.\\n\\nFirst he reached the Park, whose gloomy woodland appeared to hold him in thrall. He sank wearily against the railings, cooling his brow on the wet metal, and strained to catch the trembling quiet of the trees. \u2018Murder! murder!\u2019 he kept repeating, as though saying it again and again might blunt the horror of the word. The sound of his own voice made him shiver, but he found himself almost wishing that Echo might hear and rouse the sleeping city from its dreams. A wild urge seized him to stop some ordinary passer-by and tell him everything.\\n\\nThen he drifted across Oxford Street into narrow, shameful alleys. Two women with painted faces jeered at him as he went by. From a dark courtyard came the noise of oaths and blows, and then, with shrill screams, he saw\u2014huddled on a damp door-step\u2014the bent shapes of poverty and age. A strange pity took hold of him. Were these children of sin and misery, like him, already marked for their end? Were they, just as he was, nothing more than the puppets of some monstrous show?\\n\\nStill, it was not the mystery, but the comedy of suffering that struck him\u2014the sheer uselessness of it, the grotesque lack of any meaning. Everything felt so jumbled. Nothing seemed to fit together. He was astonished by the mismatch between the shallow optimism of the day and the real facts of existence. He was still very young.\\n\\nAfter a while he found himself standing before Marylebone Church. The silent road stretched out like a long ribbon of polished silver, broken here and there by dark arabesques of moving shadows.\"}"
