          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"44de8005e9388614250f3c2d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 12th. A remarkable change happened in the balloon\u2019s direction, and though I had fully expected it, it still gave me the clearest possible joy. After reaching, on its former course, about the twentieth parallel of southern latitude, it suddenly turned off at a sharp angle to the east, and so continued throughout the day, staying nearly\u2014if not entirely\u2014in the exact plane of the lunar ellipse. What stood out was that this change of route caused a very noticeable wobble in the car\u2014a wobble that persisted, to a greater or lesser degree, for many hours.\\n\\n\u201cApril 13th. I was again very alarmed by the loud, crackling noise that had terrified me on the tenth. I thought long and hard about it, but I couldn\u2019t reach any satisfactory conclusion. The earth\u2019s apparent diameter had decreased greatly; from the balloon it now subtended an angle of a little more than twenty-five degrees. I couldn\u2019t see the moon at all, because it was nearly straight overhead. I still stayed in the plane of the ellipse, but I made little progress to the east.\\n\\n\u201cApril 14th. The earth\u2019s diameter decreased extremely fast. Today I became strongly convinced that the balloon was now actually running along the line of apsides toward the point of perigee\u2014in other words, holding the direct course that would take it immediately to the moon at that part of its orbit where it is closest to the earth. The moon was directly overhead, and so it was hidden from my view. There was great and sustained labor required to condense the atmosphere.\\n\\n\u201cApril 15th. I could no longer make out even the outlines of continents and seas on the earth with anything close to clarity. Around twelve o\u2019clock, for the third time, I became aware of that terrifying sound that had so startled me before. This time, however, it lasted for several moments and kept growing louder as it continued. At last, while I stood stunned and terrified, waiting for I didn\u2019t know what kind of hideous destruction, the car began to vibrate with extreme violence, and a gigantic, flaming mass of some material I couldn\u2019t make out came roaring and booming past the balloon with a voice like a thousand thunders. Once my fears and astonishment had eased a little, I had little trouble believing it was some great volcanic fragment thrown from the world I was rapidly approaching\u2014most likely one of those unusual substances sometimes found on the earth and called meteoric stones, simply because there\u2019s no better name.\\n\\n\u201cApril 16th. Today, looking upward as best I could through each of the side windows in turn, I saw\u2014much to my delight\u2014a very small portion of the moon\u2019s disk protruding, as it were, on all sides beyond the huge circumference of the balloon.\u201d\"}"
