          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5d7cbeaeec7b740f698e546f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These deputies had been sent by them to Adrian, who had been chosen as arbiter; and they had traveled from Paris to Calais, through empty towns and desolate countryside, all the while indulging their violent hatred for one another; and now they pleaded their separate cases with unrestrained party spirit.\\n\\nBy looking at the deputies one by one, and after a great deal of investigation, we learned the true state of affairs in Paris. Since Parliament had elected him Ryland\u2019s deputy, all the surviving English had submitted to Adrian. He was our captain, to lead us from our native soil to unknown lands; our lawgiver and our preserver. When our emigration plan was first arranged, no continued separation of our members was intended, and the command of the whole body, rising gradually in power, reached its peak in the Earl of Windsor. But unforeseen circumstances changed our plans and forced the greater part of our numbers to be divided from the supreme chief for nearly two months. They had crossed over in two separate groups; and when they arrived in Paris, dissension broke out between them.\\n\\nThey found Paris deserted. When the plague first appeared, the return of travelers and merchants, and communications by letter, kept us regularly informed of how disease was ravaging the continent. But as mortality increased, that contact declined and finally stopped. Even within England itself, communication from one part of the island to the other became slow and rare. No vessel ever pushed through the flood that separated Calais from Dover; or if some sorrowful traveler, hoping to learn whether relatives on the French shore were alive or dead, sailed over and then tried to return among us, the greedy ocean often swallowed his small craft, or, after a day or two, he was infected by the disorder and died before he could tell us the tale of France\u2019s desolation. So we were, to a great extent, ignorant of conditions on the continent, and we were not without some vague hope of finding many companions following the same trail. Yet the very causes that had so fearfully reduced the English nation gave even wider opportunity for harm in the sister land. France was a blank. Along the long road from Calais to Paris, not a single human being was found. In Paris there were a few\u2014perhaps a hundred\u2014who, resigned to their fate, drifted through the streets of the capital and gathered to talk about the past, with a liveliness and even a kind of cheerfulness that rarely leaves individuals of this nation.\\n\\nThe English took undisputed possession of Paris. Its tall houses and narrow streets were lifeless. A few pale figures could be seen at the usual meeting place at the Tuileries; they wondered why the islanders should approach their doomed city\u2014for in the depth of misery, people always imagine that their own portion of the calamity is the bitterest, and, when enduring intense pain, we would trade the particular torture we are suffering for any other that might strike a different part of the body.\"}"
