{"request_id":"e0085805497647ab39b4cf8c","rewritten_text":"FOUR days after these curious incidents, a funeral began at Canterville Chase at about eleven o’clock at night. The hearse was drawn by eight black horses, each of which carried on its head a great tuft of nodding ostrich plumes, and the leaden coffin was covered by a rich purple pall, embroidered in gold with the Canterville coat-of-arms. Alongside the hearse and the coaches walked the servants with lighted torches, and the whole procession was wonderfully impressive. Lord Canterville was the chief mourner; he had come up specially from Wales to attend the funeral, and sat in the first carriage with little Virginia. After them came the United States Minister and his wife, then Washington and the three boys, and in the last carriage was Mrs. Umney. It was generally felt that, since she had been frightened by the ghost for more than fifty years, she had a right to see the end of him. A deep grave had been dug in the corner of the churchyard, just under the old yew tree, and the service was read in the most impressive manner by the Rev. Augustus Dampier. When the ceremony was over, the servants, following an old custom observed in the Canterville family, extinguished their torches, and as the coffin was being lowered into the grave, Virginia stepped forward and laid on it a large cross made of white and pink almond blossoms. As she did so, the moon came out from behind a cloud and flooded the little churchyard with its silent silver, and from a distant copse a nightingale began to sing. She thought of the ghost’s description of the Garden of Death; her eyes grew dim with tears, and during the drive home she could hardly speak a word.

The next morning, before Lord Canterville went up to town, Mr. Otis had an interview with him about the jewels the ghost had given to Virginia. They were perfectly magnificent, especially a certain ruby necklace with an old Venetian setting, which was truly a superb example of sixteenth-century work, and their value was so great that Mr. Otis felt considerable scruples about letting his daughter accept them.

“My lord,” he said, “I know that in this country mortmain applies to trinkets as well as to land, and it is quite clear to me that these jewels are, or should be, heirlooms in your family. I must ask you, accordingly, to take them to London with you, and to regard them simply as part of your property, restored to you under certain strange conditions. As for my daughter, she is merely a child, and, I’m glad to say, has as yet very little interest in such trappings of idle luxury.”"}

