{"request_id":"11b63804a4a4e40f313ad1aa","rewritten_text":"Many years ago, I became intimately involved with Mr. William Legrand. He came from an old Huguenot family and had once been wealthy, but a long run of misfortunes had left him in need. To spare himself the humiliation that followed his disasters, he left New Orleans—the city of his ancestors—and settled at Sullivan’s Island, off the coast near Charleston, South Carolina.

This island is a very strange place. It consists almost entirely of sea sand and is about three miles long. At no point is it more than a quarter of a mile wide. It is separated from the mainland by a creek so slight you can barely notice it, seeping through a wilderness of reeds and slime, a favorite haunt of the marsh hen. As you might expect, the vegetation is sparse, or at least stunted. You don’t see any trees of any real size. Near the western end, where Fort Moultrie stands, and where there are some miserable frame buildings occupied in summer by the refugees from Charleston’s dust and fever, you can indeed find the bristly palmetto; but for the rest of the island—except that western point and a strip of hard, white beach along the seacoast—it is covered with dense undergrowth of sweet myrtle, prized by English horticulturists. The shrub here often grows to fifteen or twenty feet, forming an almost impenetrable thicket that fills the air with its fragrance.

In the deepest recesses of this thicket, not far from the eastern end—or the more remote end—of the island, Legrand had built himself a small hut, where he lived when I first met him, purely by accident. That meeting quickly turned into friendship, because there was much in his reclusive life that invited interest and respect. I found him well educated, with unusual mental powers, yet he was plagued by misanthropy and subject to strange moods that swung between intense enthusiasm and deep melancholy. He had many books with him, but he rarely used them. His main pastimes were hunting and fishing, or wandering along the beach and through the myrtles in search of shells or entomological specimens—his collection of the latter might have drawn envy from Swammerdamm. On these outings he was usually accompanied by an old Black man named Jupiter, who had been freed before the family’s reverses, but who could not be persuaded—by threats or promises—to give up what he considered his right to follow his young “Massa Will.” It is not unlikely that Legrand’s relatives, believing him to be somewhat unsettled in the mind, had managed to put this stubbornness into Jupiter, hoping to keep watch and provide guardianship over the wanderer."}

