          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"30596cf2a04bf10c90fa210e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And then I repeated to myself that beautiful passage about Dante\u2019s entrance into the earthly Paradise, and I thought it would be sweet to wander along those lovely banks and watch the car of light descend, bringing my long-lost parent back to me. While I waited there, full of expectation for that moment, I imagined how, among the lovely flowers that grew there, I would wind myself a chaplet and crown myself with joy. I would sing \u201cSul margine d\u2019un rio,\u201d my father\u2019s favorite song, and my voice, gliding through the air that was perfectly still, would announce to him\u2014wherever he sat, waiting for the moment of our reunion\u2014that his daughter had come. Then the mark of misery would fade from my brow, and I would lift my eyes without fear to meet his, eyes that always shone with the gentle glow of innocent love. When I thought about the spellbinding look of those deep eyes, I wept, but softly, so my sobs wouldn\u2019t disturb the fairy scene.\\n\\nI was so completely caught up in this reverie that I kept walking, paying no attention to where my feet were taking me, until I actually bent down to gather a flower for my wreath on that bleak plain where no flower grew. Then I snapped out of my daydream and realized I didn\u2019t know where I was.\\n\\nThe sun had set, and the rosy tint the clouds had caught from it as it descended was nearly gone. A wind swept across the plain. I looked around and saw nothing that could tell me where I was; I had lost my way, and I tried in vain to find my route. I kept wandering, and as darkness came on, every trace that might have guided me became indistinct. At last everything was swallowed by the deep obscurity of the blackest night. I grew tired, and I knew my servant would be sleeping that night in the neighboring village, so my absence wouldn\u2019t alarm anyone. Since I was safe in this wild spot from any intruder, I decided to spend the night where I was. In fact, I was too weary to walk further. The air was cold, but I didn\u2019t care about the discomfort. I told myself I was used to the weather after my two years of solitude, when no change of seasons had ever stopped my endless wandering.\\n\\nI lay on the grass, surrounded by darkness so complete that not the slightest beam of light could reach me. There was no sound, because the deep night had lulled the insects to sleep\u2014the only creatures that lived on that lonely patch of ground where no tree or shrub offered shelter to anything else. There was a wondrous silence in the air that calmed my senses, yet at the same time stirred my soul; my mind raced from image to image and seemed to grasp an eternity.\"}"
