          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b6ba366170916903ebfbc14e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStory!\u201d\\n\\nHe seemed determined to misunderstand the word she had repeated, when he added, in a rush, \u201cYes, customers; in the banking business we usually call our connection our customers. He was a French gentleman; a scientific gentleman; a man of great learning\u2014a Doctor.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot of Beauvais?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, yes, of Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, the gentleman was from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, the gentleman was well known in Paris. I had the honor of knowing him there. Our relationship was business, but confidential. At that time I was in our French House, and I had been\u2014oh!\u2014for twenty years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt that time\u2014I may ask, at what time, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m speaking, miss, of twenty years ago. He married\u2014an English lady\u2014and I was one of the trustees. His affairs, like the affairs of many other French gentlemen and French families, were entirely in Tellson\u2019s hands. In a similar way I am, or I have been, trustee of one kind or another for scores of our customers. These are only business relations, miss; there\u2019s no friendship in them, no particular interest, nothing like sentiment. I move from one to another, in the course of my business life, just as I move from one of our customers to another during the day; in short, I have no feelings; I\u2019m nothing but a machine. To go on\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut this is my father\u2019s story, sir; and I\u2019m starting to think\u201d\u2014the oddly roughened brow was fixed on him with intense focus\u2014\u201cthat when I was left an orphan because my mother survived my father by only two years, it was you who brought me to England. I\u2019m almost sure it was you.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Lorry took the hesitant little hand that had advanced to take his with such trust, and he placed it, with great ceremony, against his lips. Then he guided the young lady straight back to her chair. Holding the chair-back with his left hand, and using his right in turn to rub his chin, tug his wig at the ears, or point as he spoke, he stood looking down at her while she sat looking up at him.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Manette, it was I. And you\u2019ll see how truly I spoke of myself just now, when I said I had no feelings, and that all the relationships I have with my fellow human beings are only business relations, when you consider that I have never seen you since. No\u2014you\u2019ve been Tellson\u2019s ward ever since, and I\u2019ve been busy with Tellson\u2019s other business ever since. Feelings! I have no time for them, no opportunity for them. I spend my whole life, miss, turning an immense pecuniary Mangle.\u201d\"}"
