          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"176b096847076446bd50320e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The chain of necessity, which has so long been dragging misery after it, must surely have been snapped; and the malignant fate that rules over it would not suffer so much as a breach of her unalterable laws. Yet why should I fret at that? Misery has ever been my element, and nothing but what is miserable could ever draw near to me; had Woodville been happy, I should never have come to know him. And can I\u2014who for many years have been fed on tears, and sustained beneath the dew of grief\u2014stop now to tell a story of woe and death?\\n\\nWoodville was compelled to go on a journey into the country, and was kept from day to day in an irksome absence from his lovely bride. He received a letter from her, saying she was slightly ill, but urging him to hasten to her; from his own eyes, she wrote, health would come, and that his company would be her surest medicine. He was detained three days longer, and then he made all haste to her. His heart, he could not tell why, foretold misfortune; he had heard nothing further from her. He feared she might be worse, and that fear made him restless and impatient for the instant when he should see her again, standing before him in health and beauty; for some sinister voice seemed always to murmur, \u201cYou will never more behold her as she was.\u201d\\n\\nWhen he reached her dwelling, everything was silent. He went through several rooms; in one he saw a servant weeping bitterly. He was so overcome with dread that he could scarcely bring himself to ask, \u201cIs she dead?\u201d and only then to listen to the dreadful reply, \u201cNot yet.\u201d Those words struck him with a force less terrible than what he had been bracing himself to hear; to learn that she still lived, and that hope was not yet extinguished, was some relief. He recalled the very terms of her letter, and gave way to a wild fancy\u2014that his kisses, breathing warm love and life, might infuse new spirit into her; and that, with him at her side, she could not die; that his presence was the talisman of her existence.\\n\\nHe hurried to her sick room. She lay there with her cheeks burning from fever, yet her eyes were closed, and she seemed insensible. He drew her to him in his arms; he pressed breathless kisses upon her burning lips; he called to her in a voice subdued with anguish, using the tenderest names. \u201cReturn, Elinor; I am with you\u2014your life, your love. Return, dearest one\u2014you promised me this boon, that I should bring you health. Let your sweet spirit rise again; you cannot die near me. What is death? To see you no more? To be parted from what is a part of myself\u2014without whom I have neither memory nor any future?\u201d\"}"
