          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1f82be52604b6c00a172e28f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Smallpox!\\n\\nI sprang to the king and said in his ear:\\n\\n\u201cOut the door at once, sire. The woman is dying of that disease that ruined the skirts of Camelot two years ago.\u201d\\n\\nHe didn\u2019t move.\\n\\n\u201cTruly, I shall stay\u2014and help as well.\u201d\\n\\nI whispered again:\\n\\n\u201cKing, it can\u2019t be. You have to go.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou mean well, and you\u2019re not speaking unwisely. But it would be shameful for a king to admit fear, and shameful for a belted knight to hold back his hand when there are people who need help. Peace\u2014I won\u2019t go. It\u2019s you who must go. The Church\u2019s ban isn\u2019t on me, but it forbids you to be here, and she will deal with you harshly if she hears of your trespass.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a desperate position for him to be in, and it might cost him his life, but there was no point arguing with him. If he thought his knightly honor was at stake here, then that was the end of the argument; he would stay, and nothing could stop it. I knew that. So I dropped the subject. The woman spoke:\\n\\n\u201cGood sir, will you climb the ladder there and bring me news of what you find? Don\u2019t be afraid to report. There may come a time when even a mother\u2019s heart can\u2019t break anymore\u2014already broken, as it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cStay,\u201d said the king, \u201cand give the woman something to eat. I will go.\u201d And he set down the knapsack.\\n\\nI turned to leave, but the king had already started. He stopped, looked down at a man lying in a dim light, and hadn\u2019t noticed us until then or spoken.\\n\\n\u201cIs it your husband?\u201d the king asked.\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs he asleep?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod be thanked for that one mercy\u2014yes. For three hours. Where can I ever repay the full measure of my gratitude? My heart is bursting with it for the sleep he\u2019s sleeping now.\u201d\\n\\nI said, \u201cWe\u2019ll be careful. We won\u2019t wake him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, no\u2014you won\u2019t,\u201d she said, \u201cbecause he is dead.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDead?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. What a triumph it is to know it! No one can harm him, no one can insult him anymore. He is in heaven now, and happy; or if not there, he waits in hell and is content\u2014because in that place he\u2019ll find neither abbot nor bishop. We were boy and girl together; we were man and wife for twenty-five years, and never separated until today. Think how long that is\u2014to love and suffer together. This morning he was out of his mind, and in his fancy we were boy and girl again, wandering in the happy fields. And so, in that innocent, joyful conversation, he wandered farther and farther\u2014still lightly talking\u2014until he entered those other fields we don\u2019t know about, and was shut away from mortal sight.\u201d\"}"
