          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5132cc2b8cfb93e473c0b7d4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But there was no laughter to be found in the little town that same calm Saturday afternoon. The Harpers, along with Aunt Polly\u2019s people, were being dressed in mourning, with heavy grief and a great deal of crying. An odd stillness lay over the village, though it was ordinarily quiet enough, if you asked me. The villagers went about their business with a kind of far-off look, speaking little; yet they sighed again and again. The Saturday holiday felt like a weight on the children. They had no spirit for their games, and little by little they let them drop.\\n\\nIn the afternoon Becky Thatcher drifted around the empty schoolhouse yard, looking downcast and feeling very low. Still, she found nothing there that could soothe her. At last she began to talk to herself:\\n\\n\u201cOh, if I only had that brass andiron-knob back again! But I don\u2019t have anything now to keep him in mind.\u201d And she swallowed down a small sob.\\n\\nPresently she paused, and said to herself:\\n\\n\u201cIt was right here. Oh, if I could do it over, I wouldn\u2019t say that\u2014I wouldn\u2019t say it for the whole world. But he\u2019s gone now; I\u2019ll never, never, never see him again.\u201d\\n\\nThat thought broke her, and she wandered off, tears running down her cheeks. Then a whole cluster of boys and girls\u2014Tom\u2019s and Joe\u2019s playmates\u2014came along, and stopped to look over the paling fence, speaking in hushed, reverent tones about what Tom had done the last time they saw him, and about how Joe said this and that little matter (full of dreadful meaning, as they could plainly see now!). Each one pointed out the exact place where the lost lads had stood at the time, and then added something like, \u201cand I was standing just so\u2014just as I am now, and as if you was him\u2014I was as close as that\u2014and he smiled, just this way\u2014and then it seemed like something went through me, like\u2014awful, you know\u2014and I didn\u2019t think what it meant then, of course, but I can see it now!\u201d\\n\\nAfter that, there was a quarrel over who had seen the dead boys last while they were still alive, and many claimed that grim honor, bringing forward their proofs\u2014more or less altered by the teller. And when, at last, it was decided who _had_ seen the departed last, and who had exchanged the final words with them, the fortunate ones took on a kind of sacred importance, and the rest stared at them and envied them. One poor fellow, having no other sort of greatness to offer, said with pretty plain pride in the recollection:\\n\\n\u201cWell, Tom Sawyer he licked me once.\u201d\\n\\nBut that attempt at glory fell flat. Most of the boys could say the same thing, and so the distinction got cheapened too much. The group drifted away again, lingering on their memories of the lost heroes, speaking in awed voices.\"}"
