          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a43999a997da80a0f1323879\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThey ought to matter to you, Dorian. Every gentleman cares about his good name. You wouldn\u2019t want people speaking of you as vile and fallen. Certainly, you have your position, your wealth, and all that sort of thing. Still, position and money aren\u2019t everything. I must tell you plainly, I don\u2019t believe these reports at all. At least, I can\u2019t believe them when I look at you. Sin, once it\u2019s there, shows itself across a man\u2019s face. It can\u2019t be hidden. People sometimes whisper about secret vices, but there\u2019s no such thing. If a miserable fellow has a vice, it comes out in the very lines of his mouth, in the droop of his eyelids, in the way his hands are shaped. Someone\u2014I'll not name him, but you know who I mean\u2014came to me last year to have his portrait painted. I had never met him before, and at the time I\u2019d heard nothing about him, though I\u2019ve heard plenty since. He dangled an extravagant sum before me. I turned him away. There was something in the form of his fingers that I couldn\u2019t stand. I see now that I was perfectly right about what I suspected. His life is dreadful. But you, Dorian\u2014your pure, bright, innocent face, and your wonderful youth, so untroubled\u2014I can\u2019t bring myself to believe anything against you.\\n\\nAnd yet I hardly see you, and you never come down to the studio anymore. When I\u2019m away from you, and I hear all these dreadful things people are murmuring about you, I don\u2019t know what to think. Why is it, Dorian, that the Duke of Berwick leaves a club the moment you enter? Why do so many gentlemen in London neither visit your house nor invite you to theirs? You were once friendly with Lord Staveley. I dined with him last week, and your name came up\u2014connected with the miniatures you\u2019ve lent for the exhibition at the Dudley. Staveley curled his lip and said you might have the most artistic tastes, but that you were a man no pure-minded girl ought to be allowed to know, and no chaste woman should ever sit in the same room with. I reminded him that I was your friend, and I asked what he meant. He told me\u2014told me openly, in front of everyone. It was dreadful! Why is your friendship so ruinous for young men? There was that wretched boy in the Guards who took his own life. You were his great friend. And there was Sir Henry Ashton, who had to leave England with a name that had been stained.\"}"
