{"request_id":"2202c78874676c3b7dca3d9b","rewritten_text":"We were raised together; there was not quite a year difference between our ages. I needn’t say that we were strangers to any kind of division or dispute. Harmony was the soul of our companionship, and the variety and contrast in our characters drew us even closer. Elizabeth had a calmer, more focused temperament; but for all my enthusiasm, I was capable of a more intense effort, and I was more deeply seized by a thirst for knowledge. She kept herself busy following the aerial creations of the poets; and in the majestic and wondrous scenes that surrounded our Swiss home—the sublime shapes of the mountains, the changing seasons, storm and calm, the silence of winter, and the life and turbulence of our Alpine summers—she found plenty to admire and delight in. While my companion contemplated the magnificent appearances of things with a serious, satisfied spirit, I took pleasure in investigating their causes. The world was a secret to me, one I longed to uncover. Curiosity, earnest study to learn the hidden laws of nature, and a joy akin to rapture, as they unfolded before me, are among the earliest feelings I can remember.

When my second son was born—my junior by seven years—my parents gave up their wandering life entirely and settled in their native country. We had a house in Geneva and a campagne on Belrive, on the eastern shore of the lake, more than a league from the city. We lived mostly at the latter, and my parents’ lives passed in a good deal of seclusion. I had a temperament that avoided crowds and attached myself fervently to a few. So I was indifferent, in general, to my schoolmates; but I formed the closest bonds of friendship with one of them. Henry Clerval was the son of a merchant from Geneva. He was a boy of unusual talent and imagination. He loved enterprise, hardship, and even danger for its own sake. He was deeply read in books of chivalry and romance. He wrote heroic songs and began to craft many tales of enchantment and knightly adventure. He tried to get us to act out plays and to take part in masquerades, with characters drawn from the heroes of Roncesvalles, from the Round Table of King Arthur, and from the chivalrous company who shed their blood to redeem the holy sepulchre from the hands of the infidels.

No one could have had a happier childhood than I did. My parents were animated by the very spirit of kindness and indulgence. We felt that they were not tyrants ruling our lives according to their whims, but the agents and creators of all the many delights we enjoyed."}

