          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"32af21f53685ccdd34f635c3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He chose Friday, the 17th of August, for his appearance, and spent most of that day going through his wardrobe, until he finally settled on a large, slouched hat with a red feather, a winding-sheet frilled at the wrists and neck, and a rusty dagger. Toward evening a violent storm of rain set in, and the wind was so strong that every window and door in the old house shook and rattled. In fact, it was exactly the kind of weather he loved. His plan was this: he would move quietly to Washington Otis\u2019s room, gibber at him from the foot of the bed, and stab himself three times in the throat to the sound of slow music. He held Washington a special grudge, knowing full well that it was he who had the habit of removing the famous Canterville blood-stain with Pinkerton\u2019s Paragon Detergent. After he had reduced the reckless, foolhardy youth to a state of abject terror, he would then go to the room occupied by the United States Minister and his wife, and there place a clammy hand on Mrs. Otis\u2019s forehead while he hissed into her trembling husband\u2019s ear the awful secrets of the charnel-house. As for little Virginia, he hadn\u2019t fully decided. She had never insulted him in any way, and she was pretty and gentle. A few hollow groans from the wardrobe, he thought, would be more than enough; or, if that didn\u2019t wake her, he might grab at the counterpane with palsy-twitching fingers. As for the twins, he was determined to teach them a lesson. The first thing to do, of course, was to sit on their chests to create the suffocating sensation of nightmare. Then, since their beds were close together, he would stand between them in the shape of a green, icy-cold corpse until they were paralyzed with fear. Finally, he would throw off the winding-sheet and crawl around the room with white, bleached bones and one rolling eyeball, playing the part of \u201cDumb Daniel, or the Suicide\u2019s Skeleton\u201d\u2014a role in which he had more than once made a great impression, and which he considered every bit as good as his famous act of \u201cMartin the Maniac, or the Masked Mystery.\u201d\\n\\nAt ten-thirty he heard the family go to bed. For a while he was disturbed by wild shrieks of laughter from the twins, who, with the carefree cheer of schoolboys, were clearly entertaining themselves before turning in. But at a quarter past eleven everything was quiet, and when midnight sounded he slipped out. The owl beat against the window panes, the raven croaked from the old yew tree, and the wind wandered moaning around the house like a lost soul; yet the Otis family slept, unaware of their doom, and high above the rain and storm he could hear the steady snoring of the Minister for the United States.\"}"
