          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"512e6561c70bbee5590a67a6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was plenty of cold water left to finish the meal. After a while, Becky suggested they should move on again. Tom was quiet for a moment. Then he said, \u201cBecky, can you stand it if I tell you something?\u201d\\n\\nBecky\u2019s face went pale, but she thought she could handle it.\\n\\n\u201cThen we have to stay here,\u201d Tom said. \u201cWe need the water to drink. That little candle piece is our last one!\u201d\\n\\nBecky broke down and cried out in loud, helpless wails. Tom did what he could to comfort her, but it didn\u2019t help much. After a while Becky said, \u201cTom!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, Becky?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey\u2019ll miss us and come looking!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, they will,\u201d Tom said. \u201cOf course they will!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMaybe they\u2019re looking for us already, Tom.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMaybe they are,\u201d Tom answered. \u201cI hope they are.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhen would they miss us?\u201d Becky asked.\\n\\n\u201cWhen they get back to the boat,\u201d Tom said.\\n\\n\u201cTom, it might be dark by then. Would they notice we didn\u2019t come?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know,\u201d Tom said. \u201cBut your mother would miss you as soon as they got home.\u201d\\n\\nA frightened look crossed Becky\u2019s face, and it made Tom realize he had made a serious mistake. Becky was not supposed to go home that night. The children fell silent, each lost in thought. Soon Becky\u2019s grief surged again, and Tom could see that what he had been thinking had hit her too\u2014that Sunday morning might already be half over before Mrs. Thatcher realized Becky wasn\u2019t at Mrs. Harper\u2019s.\\n\\nThe children stared at their small piece of candle and watched it melt slowly and without mercy. They saw the wick stand alone at last. They watched the weak flame rise and fall, climb the thin column of smoke, linger at the top for a moment, and then\u2014total darkness took over.\\n\\nHow long later it was before Becky slowly came back to herself, crying in Tom\u2019s arms, neither of them could say. All they knew was that after what felt like a long stretch of time, they both woke from a heavy, numb sleep and started suffering again. Tom said it might be Sunday now\u2014or maybe Monday. He tried to get Becky to talk, but her pain was too much. Her hopes were gone.\\n\\nTom said they must have been missed long ago, and the search was probably already underway. He said he would shout, and maybe someone would come. He tried it, but in the dark the distant echoes sounded so terrible that he stopped.\\n\\nThe hours dragged on, and hunger began to torment them again. Some of Tom\u2019s half of the cake was left, and they divided it and ate it. But even after that, they seemed hungrier than before.\"}"
