          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f6531355c6daf86997f0d10f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I want you to be permanently well settled in your connections, and for that reason it would be best to keep as few odd acquaintances as possible; so I say that if you are still in this country when Mr. Martin marries, I hope you won\u2019t be drawn in by your closeness with the sisters\u2014so as to become acquainted with his wife, who will probably be nothing more than some farmer\u2019s daughter, with no education.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly. Yes. I don\u2019t suppose Mr. Martin would ever marry anyone who hadn\u2019t had some education\u2014and been brought up very well. Still, I don\u2019t mean to set my judgment against yours, and I\u2019m sure I shall not want the acquaintance of his wife. I shall always have a great regard for the Miss Martins, particularly Elizabeth, and I should be very sorry to give them up, for they are quite as well educated as I am. But if he marries a very ignorant, vulgar woman, then I had better not go to see her, if I can help it.\u201d\\n\\nEmma watched her as she spoke, and saw nothing that looked like any dangerous sign of love. The young man had been the first admirer, but she believed there was no other hold, and that on Harriet\u2019s part there would be no serious trouble in resisting any friendly arrangement of her own.\\n\\nThey met Mr. Martin the very next day, while they were walking on the Donwell road. He was on foot, and after regarding her with the utmost respect, he looked with the most genuine satisfaction at her companion. Emma was glad of the chance to observe; and, walking a few yards ahead while they talked, she soon made her quick eye sufficiently acquainted with Mr. Robert Martin. He had a very neat appearance, and seemed like a sensible young man, but his person had no other advantage; and when she compared him with gentlemen, she felt he must lose all the ground he had gained in Harriet\u2019s favor. Harriet was not unaware of his manner; she had, of her own accord, noticed her father\u2019s gentleness with admiration as well as wonder. Mr. Martin looked as if he did not know what manner was.\\n\\nThey stayed together only a few minutes, for Miss Woodhouse must not be kept waiting; and then Harriet ran to her with a smiling face, and in a flutter of spirits which Miss Woodhouse hoped she would soon be able to settle.\\n\\n\u201cOnly think of our actually running into him!\u2014How very strange! He said it was quite an accident that he hadn\u2019t gone round by Randalls. He didn\u2019t think we ever walked this road. He thought we went towards Randalls most days.\u201d\"}"
