          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8c6e15fb794c2dd5645ab081\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This little explanation with Mr. Knightley gave Emma a great deal of pleasure. It was one of the pleasant memories of the ball, and the next morning she walked about the lawn to enjoy it.\u2014She was especially glad that they had come to such a good understanding about the Eltons, and that their opinions of both husband and wife were so much alike; and his praise of Harriet, his willingness to support her, was particularly gratifying. The Eltons\u2019 impertinence, which for a few minutes had threatened to ruin the rest of her evening, had also been the cause of some of its greatest satisfactions; and she looked forward to another happy result\u2014the cure of Harriet\u2019s infatuation.\u2014From Harriet\u2019s manner of speaking about the incident before they left the ballroom, Emma had strong hopes. It seemed as if her eyes had been suddenly opened, and she could now see that Mr. Elton was not the superior person she had believed him to be. The fever was over, and Emma could hardly fear that it would be quickened again by harmful politeness. She relied on the Eltons\u2019 bad feelings to provide whatever further discipline of pointed neglect might still be needed.\u2014With Harriet thinking more rationally, Frank Churchill not too deeply in love, and Mr. Knightley not inclined to quarrel with her, what a happy summer must be in store for Harriet!\\n\\nShe was not going to see Frank Churchill this morning. He had told her that he couldn\u2019t allow himself the pleasure of stopping at Hartfield, because he was to be at home by the middle of the day. She didn\u2019t regret it.\\n\\nHaving arranged all these matters, checked them over, and put everything in order, she was just turning back toward the house with her spirits refreshed for the demands of the two little boys, as well as of their grandfather, when the great iron sweep-gate opened and two people entered whom she had never expected to see together\u2014Frank Churchill, with Harriet leaning on his arm\u2014actually Harriet!\u2014In a moment she was convinced that something extraordinary had happened. Harriet looked pale and frightened, and he was trying to cheer her.\u2014The iron gates and the front door were not twenty yards apart;\u2014soon all three were in the hall, and Harriet immediately sank into a chair and fainted away.\\n\\nA young lady who faints must be brought round; questions must be asked, and surprises explained. Such events are very interesting, but the suspense can\u2019t last long. In a few minutes Emma learned everything.\\n\\nMiss Smith, and Miss Bickerton, another parlour boarder at Mrs. Goddard\u2019s, who had also been at the ball, had gone out together and taken a road\u2014the Richmond road\u2014which, though it looked public enough to be safe, had led them into alarm.\u2014About half a mile beyond Highbury, making a sudden turn and deeply shaded by elms on both sides, it became for some distance very secluded; and when the young ladies had gone some way into it, they suddenly saw, at a short distance ahead, on a broader patch of greensward beside the road, a party of gipsies.\"}"
