          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6525cda2464e15ba78e75607\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Saturday noon I went to the well and watched for a while. Merlin was still burning smoke-powders, clawing at the air, and muttering nonsense as hard as ever. But he looked very discouraged, because, of course, he hadn\u2019t even managed to make the water in that well start moving yet. Finally I said:\\n\\n\u201cHow is it going, partner? What does this thing promise by now?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLook,\u201d he replied. \u201cI\u2019m working right now on the strongest enchantment known to the princes of the occult arts in the lands of the East. If it fails, nothing else can help. Wait until I finish.\u201d\\n\\nThis time he raised a cloud of smoke that darkened the whole area. It must have made things uncomfortable for the hermits, because the wind carried it down over their dens, turning everything into thick, rolling fog. He kept talking as if he were filling the air with words, and he twisted his body and sawed at the air with his hands in a strange, dramatic way. After twenty minutes he dropped down, panting and nearly spent.\\n\\nThen the abbot arrived, along with several hundred monks and nuns. Behind them came a crowd of pilgrims and even a couple of acres of foundlings, all drawn by the heavy smoke and all buzzing with excitement. The abbot asked anxiously if there were any results. Merlin said:\\n\\n\u201cIf anything a mortal could do might break the spell that holds these waters, then what I have just tried would have done it. It failed. That means what I feared is true. The sign of this failure is that the most powerful spirit known to the magicians of the East has laid his spell on this well. No one may even speak his name and live. A mortal cannot breathe, and never will, who can uncover the secret of that spell. And without that secret, no one can break it. The water will not flow again, ever, Father. I have done what a man can. Let me go.\u201d\\n\\nOf course, this sent the abbot into real panic. He looked at me, and you could see it on his face. He said:\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ve heard him. Is it true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPart of it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot all of it? Not all? Then what part is true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat the spirit with the Russian name has put his spell on the well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod\u2019s wounds, then we\u2019re ruined!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPossibly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not certainly? You mean not certainly?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s right.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo when he says that none can break the spell\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. When he says that, he\u2019s saying something that isn\u2019t necessarily true. There are conditions where an attempt to break it might have some chance\u2014only a small one, a trifling one\u2014of success.\u201d}\"}"
