{"request_id":"42f339d75ccec0be5d6d6a7e","rewritten_text":"“I went on thinking in this way—_a posteriori_. The murderers had got away through one of these windows. If that was the case, they could not have put the sashes back in place from the inside, since they were found fastened; and that plain fact checked the police’s inquiries in this direction. Still, the sashes _were_ fastened. They _must_, then, have had the means of fastening themselves. There was no escaping that conclusion. I moved to the casement that lay clear, pulled out the nail with some trouble, and tried to lift the sash. It held against every effort, just as I expected. I now knew there must be a hidden spring, and this confirmation of my notion convinced me that, at least as to my premises, I was right, though the circumstances around the nails still looked strange. A careful search soon revealed the concealed spring. I pressed it, and, being satisfied with what I had found, I refrained from raising the sash.

“I then put the nail back and studied it closely. Someone who had gone out through this window might have shut it again, and the spring would have caught—but the nail could not have been put back. The inference was straightforward, and it narrowed my investigations once more. The assassins _must_ have escaped through the other window. If, then, the springs on each sash were the same, as seemed likely, there _must_ be some difference between the nails, or at least between how they were fixed. Climbing onto the sacking of the bedstead, I examined the head-board with care at the second casement. Running my hand down behind the board, I soon found and pressed the spring, which, as I had expected, was identical in character with the first. I looked next at the nail. It was as thick as the other, and seemed to be fitted in the same fashion—driven in nearly to the head.

“You’ll say I was perplexed; but if you think that, you’ve misunderstood the nature of my deductions. In a sporting sense, I had not once been ‘at fault.’ The scent was never lost for a single instant. There was no break in any link of the chain. I had followed the secret to its final outcome—and that outcome was _the nail_. I mean that it bore, in every respect, the look of its counterpart in the other window; yet that fact was an absolute nullity (however convincing it might appear) when set against the consideration that, at this very point, the clew ended. ‘There _must_ be something wrong,’ I said, ‘about the nail.’ I touched it, and the head—together with about a quarter of an inch of the shank—came away in my fingers.”"}

