          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"614939515e3d2c6a6f621562\",\"rewritten_text\":\"During my younger years, discontent never entered my mind, and if I was ever overcome by _ennui_, the sight of what is beautiful in nature or the study of what is excellent and sublime in the works of human beings could always catch my heart and give elasticity to my spirits. But I am a blasted tree; the bolt has entered my soul. And I felt then that I would survive only to show what I shall soon cease to be\u2014a miserable spectacle of wrecked humanity, pitiful to others and unbearable to myself.\\n\\nWe spent a considerable time at Oxford, wandering through its surroundings and trying to identify every place that might connect to the most stirring period of English history. Our little journeys of discovery were often prolonged by the successive sights that presented themselves. We visited the tomb of the illustrious Hampden and the field where that patriot fell. For a moment, my soul rose above my degrading, miserable fears, to contemplate the divine ideas of liberty and self-sacrifice\u2014ideas that these scenes were monuments to and reminders of. For an instant, I dared to throw off my chains and look around with a free, lofty spirit, but the iron had eaten into my flesh, and I sank again, trembling and hopeless, into my miserable self.\\n\\nWe left Oxford with regret and went on to Matlock, which was our next place to rest. The countryside near this village resembled, even more closely, the scenery of Switzerland; but everything was on a smaller scale, and the green hills lacked the crown of distant white Alps that always rise behind the pine-covered mountains of my native country. We visited the wondrous cave and the small natural history rooms, where the curiosities are arranged in the same way as in the collections at Servox and Chamounix. The latter name made me tremble when Henry pronounced it, and I hurried to leave Matlock, since that terrible scene was thus linked to it.\\n\\nFrom Derby, still traveling northward, we spent two months in Cumberland and Westmorland. I could now almost imagine myself among the Swiss mountains. The small patches of snow that still lingered on the northern slopes of the mountains, the lakes, and the rushing of the rocky streams were all familiar and dear sights to me. Here, too, we made some acquaintances, who nearly managed to trick me into happiness. Clerval\u2019s delight was, in proportion, even greater than mine; his mind expanded in the company of talented men, and he found in his own nature greater capacities and resources than he could have believed he possessed while he was with people of lesser ability.\"}"
