{"request_id":"1a37fa0f8402ef7a195a6560","rewritten_text":"The only question that seemed to trouble him was who to get rid of; for he wasn’t blind to the fact that murder, like the religions of the Pagan world, requires both a victim and a priest. Since he wasn’t a genius, he had no enemies. And he felt, besides, that this wasn’t the moment to indulge any personal grudge or dislike. The mission he was on demanded great, grave seriousness. He therefore made a list of his friends and relatives on a sheet of notepaper, and after careful thought decided in favor of Lady Clementina Beauchamp—a dear old lady who lived in Curzon Street and was his own second cousin on his mother’s side. He had always been very fond of Lady Clem, as everyone called her, and because he was very wealthy himself—having inherited all Lord Rugby’s property when he came of age—there was no chance he’d gain any vulgar financial advantage from her death. In fact, the more he considered it, the more she seemed to be exactly the right person. And feeling that any delay would be unfair to Sybil, he decided to make his arrangements at once.

The first thing to be done, of course, was to settle with the cheiromantist. So he sat down at a small Sheraton writing-table near the window, drew a cheque for £105 payable to the order of Mr. Septimus Podgers, put it in an envelope, and told his valet to take it to West Moon Street. He then telephoned the stables for his hansom and dressed to go out. As he was leaving the room, he looked back at Sybil Merton’s photograph and swore that, whatever happened, he would never let her know what he was doing for her sake. He would keep the secret of his self-sacrifice hidden in his heart forever.

On his way to the Buckingham, he stopped at a florist’s and sent Sybil a beautiful basket of narcissus, with lovely white petals and staring pheasants’ eyes. When he arrived at the club, he went straight to the library, rang the bell, and ordered the waiter to bring him a lemon-and-soda and a book on Toxicology. He had fully decided that poison was the best way to handle this troublesome business. Anything like personal violence was extremely distasteful to him. And he was also determined not to murder Lady Clementina in any manner that might draw public attention, because he hated the idea of being lionised at Lady Windermere’s—or of seeing his name appear in the paragraphs of vulgar society—newspapers."}

