{"request_id":"71d5affbf45207dbe75bb2fd","rewritten_text":"Cut off from ordinary means, the analyst throws himself into the very temper of his opponent, takes it on as his own, and not infrequently, at a single glance, discovers the only paths—sometimes absurdly plain ones—by which he can lure him into error or drive him into misjudgment.

Whist has long been remarked for the way it bears upon what people call the calculating faculty; and men of the highest intellect have been known to find in it an inexplicable pleasure, while turning away from chess as too light or trifling. There can hardly be anything else of the same kind that taxes analysis so thoroughly. The best player in Christendom _may_ be little more than the best chess-player; yet skill at whist points to a readiness for those more weighty pursuits where one mind must contend with another. By “skill,” I mean the kind of mastery in the game that includes understanding _all_ the sources from which a legitimate advantage can be drawn. These are not only numerous but varied, and they often hide in corners of thought altogether beyond the reach of ordinary comprehension. To watch closely is to remember clearly; and, in so far as the concentrative chess-player goes, he will do quite well at whist, since the rules of Hoyle (built, in truth, on nothing more than the game’s mechanism) are generally intelligible. Thus, to have a retentive memory and to play “according to the book” are commonly taken as the whole of good playing. Yet it is in matters that lie outside the bounds of mere rule that the analyst’s talent shows itself. In silence he makes a whole store of observations and inferences. Perhaps his companions do something similar; but the difference in how much they learn depends less on whether the inference is sound than on the caliber of the observation. The essential thing is knowing _what_ to observe.

Our player does not confine himself at all. Because the game is the object, he does not therefore discard deductions drawn from things outside it. He studies his partner’s face, setting it carefully beside that of each opponent. He considers how the cards are arranged in every hand; often counting trump by trump, and honors by honors, from the looks given by their owners as they hold them. He marks every change in expression as the play goes on, collecting a store of thought from the differences in the look of certainty, surprise, triumph, or vexation. From the way a trick is gathered up, he judges whether the taker can make another in the same suit. He can tell what is being played through feint, by the manner in which it is thrown down upon the table. A casual or careless remark; the accidental dropping or turning of a card, together with the anxiety or neglect shown about keeping it hidden; the counting of tricks and the order in which they are arranged; embarrassment, hesitation, eagerness, or trepidation—these, to his apparently intuitive perception, all serve as signs of the real state of affairs."}

