          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9b9041922d5ff432fe96fae5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI know she would!\u201d\\n\\nThe Widow Douglas\u2019 fine hospitality was a tempting lure. Between that and Tom\u2019s urging, the matter was soon settled. So they decided to tell no one anything about the night\u2019s plan. After a while Tom found himself wondering whether Huck might show up this very night and give the signal. The idea drained a good deal of the sparkle from his hopes. Still, he couldn\u2019t bring himself to give up the fun at Widow Douglas\u2019. And why should he? The signal hadn\u2019t come the night before, so what made it any more likely to come tonight? The certain enjoyment of the evening beat the uncertain promise of a prize; and, in true boy fashion, he chose to follow the stronger pull and not let himself think about the box of money again that day.\\n\\nThree miles below town, the ferryboat stopped at the mouth of a wooded hollow and made fast. The crowd poured ashore, and soon the forest distances and jagged heights rang with shouts and laughter. They tried every way they could think of to get hot and worn out, and by and by the rovers drifted back to camp with appetites that felt properly earned\u2014then the spoiling of the good things began. After the meal came a pleasant stretch of resting and talking in the shade of spreading oaks. Presently someone called out:\\n\\n\u201cWho\u2019s set for the cave?\u201d\\n\\nEveryone was. Bundles of candles were fetched, and at once there was a general scramble up the hill. The cave\u2019s mouth sat higher on the slope\u2014an opening shaped like the letter A. Its heavy oak door stood open, with no bar across it. Inside there was a small chamber, cold as an icehouse, and lined by Nature with solid limestone, slick and damp as if sweating with chill. It was romantic and mysterious to stand in that deep gloom and look out over the green valley shining in the sun. But the spell didn\u2019t last; the play started up again. The instant a candle was lit, its owner was immediately surrounded; there was a scramble and a brave bit of resistance, yet the candle was soon knocked down or blown out, and then came a cheerful uproar of laughter and a fresh chase.\\n\\nBut everything ends. Soon the group began to file down the steep descent of the main avenue, the flickering lights only dimly showing the high rock walls up to where they met, some sixty feet overhead. This main passage was no more than eight or ten feet wide. Every few steps, other lofty but narrower crevices split off on either side\u2014for McDougal\u2019s cave was really a huge tangle of crooked corridors that kept joining and breaking apart again, leading nowhere.\"}"
        }
      ],
