          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"321614eb32d3d02dcfd08027\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dawn had come when I set the Manuscript aside. The rain had almost stopped. Everything looked gray and sad. The storm, worn out, was still making its last sounds, like it was trying to calm down. I went to the stranger\u2019s room and listened at his door, which was slightly open. I could hear him talking, so I knocked. No one answered, but I kept hearing his voice. I leaned in and looked.\\n\\nHe lay on his back in bed, talking in broken bursts, but with energy. He kept moving his arms restlessly, as sick people do when they\u2019re delirious. I slipped in quietly and bent over him. His mutterings and outbursts continued. I said only a single word, just to get his attention. The moment I spoke, his glassy eyes and ashy face lit up with joy\u2014pleasure, gratitude, happiness, and relief.\\n\\n\u201cOh, Sandy, you\u2019ve finally come. How long I\u2019ve waited for you! Sit by me\u2014don\u2019t go. Don\u2019t leave me. Never leave me again, Sandy. Where is your hand? Give it to me, please. Let me hold it\u2014there. Now everything is all right. Everything is peaceful, and I\u2019m happy again. We\u2019re happy again, aren\u2019t we, Sandy? You look so dim and unclear. You\u2019re only a mist, a cloud, but you\u2019re here\u2014and that\u2019s enough. I have your hand. Don\u2019t take it away. It will only be for a little while. I won\u2019t need it long.\\n\\nWas that the child? Hello\u2014Central! She doesn\u2019t answer. Maybe she\u2019s asleep. Bring her when she wakes. Let me touch her hands, her face, her hair, and tell her goodbye.\\n\\nSandy! Yes, you\u2019re there. I lost track for a moment, and I thought you were gone.\\n\\nHow long have I been sick? It must be. It feels like months. And the dreams\u2014such strange, terrible dreams, Sandy. Dreams that felt as real as life. Of course it\u2019s delirium, but they were so real. I thought the king was dead. I thought you were in Gaul and couldn\u2019t get home. I thought there was a revolution. In the wild confusion of those dreams, I believed Clarence and I, along with a few of my cadets, fought and wiped out all the chivalry of England.\\n\\nBut that wasn\u2019t even the strangest part. It felt like I was a creature from some far age that hasn\u2019t even been born yet\u2014centuries from now. And that felt just as real as everything else. Yes, it was like I flew out of that future time back into this one, and then forward again. Then I was left here, a stranger and alone, in a strange England, with thirteen centuries of emptiness opening between me and you. Between me and my home and my friends. Between me and everything that matters to me\u2014everything that could make life worth living.\u201d\"}"
