          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"42e1e4cbc3b78a05592737d7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The congratulatory letter Elizabeth received from Lydia about her marriage explained that, at least by his wife\u2014and if not by himself\u2014such a hope was cherished. The letter ran as follows:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lizzy,\\n\\nI wish you joy. If you love Mr. Darcy half as well as I do my dear Wickham, you must be very happy. It is a great comfort to have you so rich; and when you have nothing else to do, I hope you will think of us. I am sure Wickham would like a place at court very much; and I do not think we shall have quite enough money to live on without some help. Any place would do, about three or four hundred a year; but, however, do not speak to Mr. Darcy about it, if you would rather not.\\n\\nYours, etc.\\n\\nAs it happened, Elizabeth would much rather not, and in her reply she tried to put an end to every request and expectation of that kind. Whatever relief she could manage\u2014by what might be called economy in her own private expenses\u2014she often sent them. It had always been clear to her that an income like theirs, under the direction of two people so extravagant in their wants and so careless about the future, must be very insufficient for their support; and whenever they changed their quarters, either Jane or she was sure to be asked for some small assistance toward paying their bills. Their way of living was unsettled to the extreme, even when the restoration of peace sent them back to a home. They were always moving from place to place in search of a cheap situation, and always spending more than they ought. His affection for her soon turned to indifference; hers lasted a little longer; and, despite her youth and her manners, she still held on to all the claims to reputation her marriage had given her. Though Darcy could never receive him at Pemberley, he nevertheless helped him further in his profession for Elizabeth\u2019s sake. Lydia was sometimes a