          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4e2aeba8fbba8ba2dbe3bf41\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After they had all joined in those general lamentations over the dreadful sequel of the affair\u2014which Elizabeth regarded as next to certain, and Miss Bennet could not quite bring herself to call wholly impossible\u2014the former went on again. \u201cBut do tell me everything about it that I haven\u2019t already heard. Give me the particulars. What did Colonel Forster say? Had no one any misgivings before the elopement actually occurred? They must have seen them together long enough to be sure of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cColonel Forster admitted that he had often suspected some partiality, particularly on Lydia\u2019s side, but nothing that could alarm him. I am truly sorry for him. His conduct was as attentive and kind as possible. He was on his way to us, intending to show us how concerned he was, before he had the least notion that they were not gone to Scotland; and when that fear first became known, it only made him hurry forward.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd was Denny persuaded that Wickham would not marry? Did he understand that they meant to run off? Had Colonel Forster spoken with Denny himself?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; but when Denny was questioned by him, Denny denied knowing anything of their plan, and would not give his real opinion of it. He did not repeat his belief that they would not marry, and for that reason I am inclined to think he may have been misunderstood before.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd until Colonel Forster came in person, none of you, I suppose, entertained any doubt that they were really married?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow could such an idea have found its way into our heads? I confess I felt a little uneasy\u2014somewhat afraid for my sister\u2019s happiness with him in marriage\u2014because I knew his behaviour had not always been quite right. My father and mother knew nothing of it; they only saw how imprudent the match must be. Kitty then owned, with a very natural triumph at having learned more than the rest of us, that in Lydia\u2019s last letter she had prepared her for such a step. It seems she had known for many weeks that they were in love with one another.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not before they went to Brighton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, I don\u2019t think so.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did Colonel Forster seem to think ill of Wickham himself? Does he know his true character?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI must admit he did not speak of Wickham as he once did. He believed him imprudent and extravagant; and since this unhappy business has come to pass, it is said that he left Meryton with himself greatly in debt: but I hope that report is false.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Jane, if we had been less secret\u2014if we had told what we knew of him\u2014this could not have happened!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps it might have been better,\u201d returned her sister.\\n\\n\u201cBut to lay bare the former faults of any person, without knowing what their present feelings are, seemed to me unjustifiable.\u201d\"}"
