          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d0531f2c1430631a795f9cb7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"During dinner, Mr. Bennet hardly spoke at all. But once the servants had left, he decided it was time to talk with his guest. He brought up a topic he expected would impress him, saying that he seemed very fortunate to have such a patroness. Lady Catherine de Bourgh\u2019s attention to his wishes and her care for his comfort were, he said, truly remarkable. Mr. Bennet could not have chosen a better subject.\\n\\nMr. Collins praised her in an eloquent way. The topic made him even more solemn than usual. With a very serious expression, he declared that he had never in his life seen anyone of high rank behave as Lady Catherine did\u2014her kindness and willingness to treat him as she had, he said, were exactly what he had experienced from her. He added that she had graciously approved of both sermons he had already had the honor of preaching before her. She had also asked him twice to dine at Rosings, and she had sent for him only the Saturday before, so he could take part in her evening game of quadrille.\\n\\nLady Catherine, Mr. Collins said, was thought by many people to be proud, but he had never seen anything in her except politeness. She always spoke to him as she would to any other gentleman. She never raised the slightest objection to him joining the neighborhood\u2019s society, and she did not mind him leaving his parish now and then for a week or two to visit his relations. She had even advised him to marry as soon as he could, as long as he chose wisely. And once she had visited his modest parsonage, where she had approved of all the changes he had been making and had even suggested a few herself\u2014some shelves in the upstairs closets.\\n\\n\u201cThat all sounds very proper and kind,\u201d said Mrs. Bennet. \u201cI\u2019m sure she\u2019s a pleasant woman. It\u2019s a shame that great ladies aren\u2019t more like her. Does she live near you, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe garden where my humble home stands is only separated from Rosings Park by a lane\u2014Lady Catherine\u2019s residence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI thought you said she was a widow, sir. Does she have any family?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe has only one daughter, the heiress of Rosings, and she has a great deal of property.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh,\u201d said Mrs. Bennet, shaking her head, \u201cthen she\u2019s better off than many girls. And what kind of young lady is she? Is she handsome?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe is a most charming young lady, indeed. Lady Catherine herself says that, in true beauty, Miss de Bourgh is far superior to the handsomest girl of her whole group\u2014because there is something in her features that shows she comes from distinguished birth.\u201d\"}"
