          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5e68ed56fd0d5719caa516ec\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dark and storm-tossed was the night. About the throne on high not a single star seemed to tremble; yet the deep, rolling thunder kept thudding upon the ear, while the dreadful lightning, in a fit of furious temper, flashed through the vaulted chambers of heaven, as though it scorned the authority exerted over its terrors by the illustrious Franklin! Even the boisterous winds, all of them, burst from their secret haunts and raged about, as if their help were meant to heighten the wildness of the scene.\\n\\nAt such a time\u2014so black, so dismal\u2014when human sympathy might have been expected to fail, my own spirit sighed; but instead of that,\\n\\n\u2018My dearest friend, my counsellor, my comforter and guide\u2014 My joy in grief, my second bliss in joy,\u2019 came to my side.\\n\\nShe moved like one of those bright beings painted in the sunny lanes of fancy\u2019s Eden by the romantic and the young: a queen of beauty, unadorned except by her own surpassing loveliness. Her step was so gentle that it made no sound at all; and, had it not been for the magical thrill her kindly touch gave, she might, like other quiet beauties, have slipped away unnoticed\u2014unsought. A peculiar sadness lay upon her face, as if icy tears had settled on the robe of December; and when she pointed outward to the battling elements, she bade me consider the two powers set before me.\\n\\nThis nightmare of a piece filled some ten pages of manuscript and ended with a sermon so ruinous to all hope among non-Presbyterians that it won the first prize. The whole thing was judged the finest work of the evening. When the mayor of the village presented the award to the author, he delivered a hearty speech, saying it was by far the most \u201celoquent\u201d thing he had ever heard, and that Daniel Webster himself might well be proud of it.\\n\\nIt may be added, in passing, that the number of compositions in which the word \u201cbeauteous\u201d was over-indulged, and human experience was spoken of as \u201clife\u2019s page,\u201d stood at the usual average.\\n\\nNow the master, softened almost to the edge of geniality, pushed his chair aside, turned his back on the audience, and began to draw a map of America on the blackboard, intending to exercise the geography class. But he managed it badly with his unsteady hand, and a smothered titter ran through the room. He knew what was happening and tried to set it right. He wiped out the lines and redrew them; yet he only made them more distorted, and the tittering grew louder. He fixed his whole attention on the work, as if resolved not to be beaten down by the laughter. He felt that everyone\u2019s eyes were upon him; he told himself he was doing well, and still the tittering went on\u2014indeed, it plainly increased.\"}"
