          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"43f0ebd898c4866bde7efcc9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShe would be such a delightful companion for Emma.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Woodhouse agreed with it all, but added,\\n\\n\u201cOur little friend Harriet Smith, however, is just such another pretty, kind young person. You will like Harriet. Emma could not have a better companion than Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m very happy to hear it\u2014but only Jane Fairfax is known to be so very accomplished and superior!\u2014and exactly Emma\u2019s age.\u201d\\n\\nThis topic was discussed very happily, and others followed in the same spirit, passing away with similar harmony; but the evening did not end without a small return of agitation. The gruel was served and gave them plenty to talk about\u2014much praise and many comments\u2014undoubted confidence in its wholesomeness for every constitution, and pretty severe Philippics against the many houses where it was never served in anything like tolerable form;\u2014but, unfortunately, among the failures the daughter had to mention, the most recent, and therefore most prominent, was in her own cook at South End, a young woman hired for the time, who had never been able to understand what she meant by a basin of nice, smooth gruel\u2014thin, but not too thin. No matter how often she had wished for it and ordered it, she had never been able to get anything tolerable. This was a dangerous opening.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Woodhouse, shaking his head and fixing his eyes on her with tender concern. The exclamation in Emma\u2019s ear conveyed, \u201cAh! there is no end of the sad consequences of your going to South End. It does not bear talking about.\u201d For a little while she hoped he would not bring it up, and that quiet rumination might be enough to restore his appetite for his own smooth gruel. After some minutes, however, he began,\\n\\n\u201cI shall always be very sorry that you went to the sea this autumn instead of coming here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut why should you be sorry, sir?\u2014I assure you, it did the children a great deal of good.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd besides, if you must go to the sea, it had better not have been to South End. South End is an unhealthy place. Perry was surprised to hear you had fixed on South End.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know there\u2019s an idea like that among many people, but it\u2019s quite a mistake, sir. We all had our health perfectly well there, and never found the least inconvenience from the mud; and Mr. Wingfield says it\u2019s entirely wrong to suppose the place unhealthy. I\u2019m sure you can rely on him, because he thoroughly understands the nature of the air, and his own brother and family have been there repeatedly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou should have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you went anywhere. Perry was a week at Cromer once, and he considers it the best of all the sea-bathing places.\u201d\"}"
