          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4e7b0d3cfec76a93f7885657\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We finally made the box lighter by taking out two thirds of what was inside. After some trouble, we were then able to lift it out of the hole. We put the items we removed down among the brambles, and we left the dog there to guard them. Jupiter gave him strict orders: under no circumstances was he to move from that spot, and he was not to open his mouth until we came back. Then we hurried home with the chest. We reached the hut safely, but only after a lot of hard work, at one o\u2019clock in the morning. Even though we were exhausted, we couldn\u2019t do anything else right away. We rested until two, ate supper, and then set off for the hills at once with three strong sacks, which, luckily, were already there.\\n\\nA little before four we arrived at the pit. We divided the rest of the stolen goods among us as fairly as we could. Then we left the holes unfilled and went back to the hut for the second time, where we deposited our golden loads again. We did it just as the first faint light of dawn began to show over the treetops in the east.\\n\\nBy then we were completely worn out, but the excitement of what we had done kept us from resting. After an uneasy sleep of about three or four hours, we got up as if we had planned it together, to examine our treasure.\\n\\nThe chest had been filled to the brim. We spent the whole day, and most of the next night, going through everything carefully. There was no real order. Everything had been piled in without any arrangement. Once we sorted it all out, we realized we had even more wealth than we first thought. In coins, there was a little over four hundred and fifty thousand dollars, based on the value of the pieces as accurately as we could using the tables of the time. There was not a single piece of silver. All of it was gold from old times, and it varied a lot\u2014French, Spanish, and German money, along with a few English guineas and some counters we had never seen before. There were also several very large and heavy coins, worn so badly that we couldn\u2019t read their inscriptions. There was no American money.\\n\\nWe had more trouble estimating the value of the jewels. We found diamonds\u2014some extremely large and fine\u2014one hundred and ten in all, and none of them small. We found eighteen rubies, very bright; three hundred and ten emeralds, all beautiful; and twenty-one sapphires, along with an opal. These stones had all been broken out of their settings and thrown loose into the chest. The settings themselves, which we picked out from among the other gold, looked as if they had been smashed with hammers, as if someone wanted to prevent them from being identified.\"}"
