          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eaf30229b2665d3098433f40\",\"rewritten_text\":\"TEN minutes later, with his face drained of color by terror and his eyes wild with grief, Lord Arthur Savile burst out of Bentinck House, pushing through the crowd of fur-coated footmen gathered around the large striped awning, as if he neither saw nor heard anything. The night was bitterly cold, and the gas lamps around the square flared and flickered in the sharp wind; yet his hands were burning hot with fever, and his forehead felt like it was on fire. He kept going and going, almost staggering along like a drunk. A policeman glanced at him as he passed, and a beggar who had been slouching from an archway to ask for alms grew frightened, seeing misery greater than his own. Once he stopped beneath a lamp and stared at his hands. He thought he could already detect the stain of blood on them, and a faint cry broke from his trembling lips.\\n\\nMurder! That was what the cheiromantist had seen there. Murder! Even the night seemed to know it, and the desolate wind to howl it into his ear. The dark corners of the streets were full of it. It grinned at him from the roofs of the houses.\\n\\nFirst he came to the Park, whose gloomy woodland seemed to hold him in a strange fascination. He leaned wearily against the railings, cooling his brow on the wet metal, and listened to the trees\u2019 trembling silence. \u201cMurder! murder!\u201d he kept repeating, as though saying it over and over could soften the horror of the word. The sound of his own voice made him shudder, yet he almost hoped that Echo might hear him and wake the sleeping city from its dreams. He felt a wild urge to stop any casual passer-by and tell them everything.\\n\\nThen he drifted across Oxford Street into narrow, shameful alleys. Two women with painted faces mocked him as he went by. From a dark courtyard came the sound of oaths and blows, followed by shrill screams; and there, huddled on a damp doorstep, he saw the bent, crooked shapes of poverty and age. A strange pity washed over him. Were these children of sin and misery destined for their end, just as he was? Were they, like him, only puppets in some monstrous show?\\n\\nAnd yet it wasn\u2019t the mystery, but the comedy of suffering that struck him\u2014its absolute uselessness, its grotesque lack of meaning. Everything seemed so incoherent. Everything seemed to be missing any real harmony. He was astonished by the clash between the shallow optimism of the day and the actual facts of existence. He was still very young.\\n\\nAfter a while he found himself standing in front of Marylebone Church. The silent road looked like a long ribbon of polished silver, flecked here and there with the dark arabesques of moving shadows.\"}"
