          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9eaa7aba6860518172846dc6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs Clay\u2019s feelings for the young man were stronger than her interest in other plans. For his sake, she gave up the chance to scheme longer for Sir Walter. Still, she has abilities as well as emotions. Now it is hard to say whether his cleverness or hers will win in the end. Even if he prevents her from becoming Sir Walter\u2019s wife, he may still end up talking her into marrying Sir William, with flattery and gentle attention.\\n\\nThere is no doubt that Sir Walter and Elizabeth were shocked and deeply upset by the loss of their companion, and by the discovery that they had been deceived. They could turn to their great cousins for comfort, but they must still feel that it is only half satisfying to flatter and follow other people, without ever being flattered and followed in return.\\n\\nAnne, long before this, had understood what Lady Russell meant and had decided to love Captain Wentworth the way she should. Because of that, there was no other drawback to her happiness. The only thing that troubled her was the thought that she had no family connections to offer him\u2014nothing that a sensible man would value. She felt her own lack of status very sharply. The difference in their fortunes did not bother her at all, and it did not make her regret anything. But the fact that she had no family to judge him properly, to offer respectability, harmony, and goodwill in return for all the worth and the warm welcome he received from his brothers and sisters, caused her real pain, even though everything else in her situation promised strong happiness. She had only two friends in the world to add to his list: Lady Russell and Mrs Smith. Captain Wentworth was, however, very willing to attach himself to both. Lady Russell, despite her earlier mistakes, was someone he could now truly value. He did not have to say that he believed she was right to separate them at first, but he was ready to say almost everything else in her favor. As for Mrs Smith, she had different reasons that made it easy for him to respect and keep her close.\\n\\nAnne\u2019s recent good offices were enough on their own to bring this about. And when Anne and Captain Wentworth married, it did not take away one friend from Mrs Smith; it actually gave her two. Mrs Smith was the first visitor to their home once their life became settled. Captain Wentworth, by helping her recover her husband\u2019s property in the West Indies\u2014through writing for her, acting on her behalf, and staying with her to deal with all the small problems that came up\u2014paid back, fully and completely, the services she had given, and the services she had always meant to give, to his wife.\"}"
