          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9739f609bc0f9e47c6e0d47f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wish we had a donkey. Then we could all ride on donkeys\u2014Jane, Miss Bates, and me\u2014and my caro sposo walking alongside. I really need to talk to him about buying a donkey. In the country, I think it\u2019s almost necessary. Even if a woman has plenty of resources, she can\u2019t always stay shut up at home. And you know how it is: long walks in summer mean dust, and in winter they mean mud.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou won\u2019t find either between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane is never dusty, and right now it\u2019s perfectly dry. If you want, you can come on a donkey. You can borrow Mrs. Cole\u2019s. I\u2019d like everything to be as much to your taste as possible.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat I\u2019m sure you would. And I truly give you credit for it, my good friend. Under that plain, blunt way of yours, I know you have the warmest heart. As I told Mr. E., you\u2019re a real humorist. Yes, believe me, Knightley, I fully understand your attention to me in all of this plan. You\u2019ve chosen exactly what will please me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley had another reason for avoiding a table in the shade. He wanted to persuade Mr. Woodhouse, as well as Emma, to join the outing. He knew that if any of them sat down outside to eat, it would almost certainly make him ill. Mr. Woodhouse must not, under the friendly excuse of a morning drive and a couple of hours at Donwell, be tempted away from his misery.\\n\\nMr. Woodhouse was invited in good faith. There were no hidden horrors waiting to punish him for trusting too easily. He did agree. He hadn\u2019t been to Donwell for two years. \u201cOne fine morning,\u201d he said, \u201che, Emma, and Harriet could go very well. He could sit quietly with Mrs. Weston while the dear girls walked around the gardens. He didn\u2019t think they could be damp now, in the middle of the day. He would like to see the old house again very much, and he would be very happy to meet Mr. and Mrs. Elton, and any other of his neighbors. He could see no objection at all to his going there, and Emma\u2019s, and Harriet\u2019s, on some very fine morning. He thought it was very thoughtful and sensible of Mr. Knightley to invite them\u2014much better than dining out. He wasn\u2019t fond of dining out.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley was lucky in how quickly everyone agreed. The invitation was received so warmly everywhere that it seemed, as if Mrs. Elton were doing, they were all treating the plan like a special compliment meant for them. Emma and Harriet said they expected a great deal of pleasure from it. And Mr. Weston, without being asked, promised to try to get Frank to join them if possible\u2014an extra show of approval and gratitude that really wasn\u2019t necessary. Mr. Knightley then had to say he would be glad to see him, and Mr. Weston promised to waste no time writing and using every argument to persuade him to come.\"}"
