          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3ea6e7f5810ed8941ec5cbf5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She felt a strong pull toward the outer door; she wanted to know whether it rained. Why should she be made to suspect herself of some other motive? Captain Wentworth must be somewhere out of sight. She rose from her seat\u2014she would go; one half of her ought not always to be so much wiser than the other, nor to keep suspecting it of being worse. She would find out if it rained. In the very moment, though, she was checked by Captain Wentworth\u2019s entrance himself, coming in among a party of gentlemen and ladies\u2014plainly his acquaintances\u2014whom he must have joined a little below Milsom Street.\\n\\nHe was far more visibly struck and disconcerted by seeing her than she had ever noticed him to be; he looked quite red. For the first time since their renewed acquaintance, she felt that she was betraying the smaller share of feeling between them. She had the advantage over him in having prepared herself for the last few moments. The first, overwhelming, blinding, bewildering effects of a sudden surprise were past with her; yet she still had enough to feel. It was agitation, pain, pleasure\u2014something poised between delight and misery.\\n\\nHe spoke to her, and then turned away. The tone of his manner was embarrassment. She could not call it cold, nor friendly, nor anything so plainly as embarrassed.\\n\\nAfter a short interval, however, he came toward her again and spoke once more. Their mutual inquiries on common topics went on: neither of them, perhaps, much the wiser for what they learned, and Anne remained fully aware that he was less at ease than before. By dint of having been so much together, they had managed to talk with a fair show of indifference and calmness; but he could not keep it up now. Time had altered him, or Louisa had altered him. There was a consciousness of some sort or other. He looked very well\u2014no sign of suffering in health or spirits\u2014and