          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3246dcba3eb2660aec5f15a9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It\u2019s a strange thing, but everyone who disappears is said to be seen in San Francisco. It must be a wonderful city, with all the attractions of the next world.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you think happened to Basil?\u201d Dorian asked. He held his Burgundy up to the light and wondered how he could talk about it so calmly.\\n\\n\u201cI have no idea at all. If Basil wants to hide, that\u2019s not my business. If he\u2019s dead, I don\u2019t want to think about it. Death is the only thing that has ever truly frightened me. I hate it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy?\u201d the younger man asked, sounding tired.\\n\\n\u201cBecause,\u201d said Lord Henry, lifting the gilt trellis of an open vinaigrette box and letting it pass near his nose, \u201cthese days you can survive almost anything\u2014except that. Death and vulgarity are the only two facts in the nineteenth century that you can\u2019t just explain away. Let\u2019s have our coffee in the music room, Dorian. You must play Chopin for me. The man my wife ran off with played Chopin beautifully. Poor Victoria\u2014I really did like her. The house feels lonely without her. Of course, marriage is only a habit, and a bad one. Still, you miss even your worst habits. Maybe you miss them the most. They\u2019re such an important part of who you are.\u201d\\n\\nDorian didn\u2019t answer. He got up from the table, went into the next room, and sat at the piano. He let his fingers drift over the white and black keys. When the coffee arrived, he stopped playing and looked at Lord Henry. \u201cHarry,\u201d he said, \u201cdid it ever occur to you that Basil might have been murdered?\u201d\\n\\nLord Henry yawned. \u201cBasil was well liked, and he always wore a Waterbury watch. Why would anyone murder him? He wasn\u2019t clever enough to make enemies. Sure, he had real talent for painting. But a person can paint like Velasquez and still be completely dull. Basil was really rather dull. He only interested me once\u2014years ago\u2014when he told me he had a wild admiration for you, and that you were the main reason for his art.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI cared about Basil,\u201d Dorian said, with a touch of sadness. \u201cBut don\u2019t people say he was murdered?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSome of the papers do,\u201d Lord Henry replied. \u201cI don\u2019t think it\u2019s likely. I know there are terrible places in Paris, but Basil wasn\u2019t the kind of man who would go there. He had no curiosity. That was his biggest fault.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat would you say, Harry,\u201d Dorian asked, \u201cif I told you that I murdered Basil?\u201d\"}"
