{"request_id":"01b391f152779e93c0791622","rewritten_text":"“But, as a matter of fact, when you ascend to any given altitude, the weight of air you have passed through in any further ascent is by no means proportional to the additional height you gain (as can be clearly seen from what I said earlier), but instead follows a ratio that keeps decreasing. So it is evident that, however high we climb, we cannot, in the literal sense, reach a boundary beyond which no atmosphere exists. It must exist, I argued, though it may exist in a state of infinite rarefaction.

“On the other hand, I knew that arguments had been made to show that there is a real, definite limit to the atmosphere—one beyond which there is absolutely no air at all. But a detail that those who support such a limit have overlooked seemed to me, even if it did not amount to a direct refutation of their position, to be a matter worthy of very serious investigation. When I compared the intervals between Encke’s comet’s successive returns at perihelion, after giving due credit, in the most exact way, for all disturbances caused by the planets’ attractions, it appears that the periods are gradually shortening; that is, the major axis of the comet’s ellipse is becoming shorter, in a slow but perfectly regular decline. Now, this is exactly what should happen if we assume that the comet experiences resistance from an extremely rare ethereal medium filling the regions of its orbit. For it is clear that such a medium, by slowing the comet’s velocity, would increase its centripetal effect by weakening its centrifugal force. In other words, the sun’s attraction would be constantly gaining strength, and the comet would be drawn closer with every revolution. Indeed, there is no other way to account for the variation in question. But again:—The real diameter of the same comet’s nebulosity is observed to contract rapidly as it approaches the sun, and to expand just as rapidly as it moves away toward its aphelion. Was I not justified, along with M. Valz, in supposing that this apparent condensation of volume comes from the compression of the same ethereal medium I mentioned before, which becomes denser in proportion to its proximity to the sun?

“The lenticular-shaped phenomenon, also called zodiacal light, was another subject worthy of attention. This radiance, so evident in the tropics and impossible to mistake for any meteoric glow, stretches from the horizon obliquely upward and generally follows the direction of the sun’s equator. It seemed to me clearly to be the kind of rare atmosphere that extends outward from the sun, at least beyond the orbit of Venus, and I believed it reaches indefinitely farther. Indeed, I could not suppose that this medium is confined to the comet’s elliptical path or to the immediate neighborhood of the sun.”"}

