          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"54f3696755105a396ebdd0b4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He never tried again. The boys lay there, counting the dragging moments until it felt to them that time had to be over and eternity was turning gray; and then they were grateful to see that the sun was finally setting.\\n\\nNow one snore stopped. Injun Joe sat up, stared around, and gave his comrade a grim smile, whose head was drooping on his knees. He nudged him awake with his foot and said:\\n\\n\u201cHey! You\u2019re the watchman, aren\u2019t you? All right, then\u2014nothing\u2019s happened.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMe! Have I been asleep?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPartly, partly. Almost time for us to move, pard. What are we going to do with the little swag we\u2019ve got left?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know\u2014leave it here like we always do, I guess. No point taking it with us until we start south. Six hundred and fifty in silver is something to carry.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014fine. It won\u2019t matter if we come back here once more.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo\u2014but I\u2019d say come in at night like we used to. It\u2019s better.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, but look here; it might be a good while before I get the right chance for that job. Accidents could happen. It\u2019s not exactly a good place. We\u2019ll just bury it like we always do\u2014and bury it deep.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood idea,\u201d said the comrade. He crossed the room, knelt down, lifted one of the hearth-stones in the back, and pulled out a bag that jingled pleasantly. He took twenty or thirty dollars for himself and the same amount for Injun Joe, then handed the bag to Injun Joe, who was on his knees in the corner now, digging with his bowie-knife.\\n\\nThe boys forgot every fear, every misery, in an instant. With gloating eyes they watched every movement. Luck!\u2014the splendor of it was beyond imagination. Six hundred dollars was enough to make half a dozen boys rich. This was treasure-hunting under the best possible circumstances\u2014no annoying uncertainty about where to dig. They nudged each other again and again\u2014loud, clear nudges, because they only meant: \u201cOh, but aren\u2019t you glad we\u2019re here now!\u201d\\n\\nJoe\u2019s knife struck something.\\n\\n\u201cHello!\u201d he said.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is it?\u201d asked his comrade.\\n\\n\u201cA half-rotten plank\u2014no, I think it\u2019s a box. Here\u2014help me out, and we\u2019ll see what it\u2019s for. Never mind, I\u2019ve already broken a hole.\u201d\\n\\nHe reached in and drew it out\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMan, it\u2019s money!\u201d\\n\\nThe two men examined the handful of coins. They were gold. The boys above were just as excited as they were, and just as delighted.\\n\\nJoe\u2019s comrade said:\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019ll make quick work of this. There\u2019s an old rusty pick out among the weeds in the corner on the other side of the fireplace\u2014I saw it a minute ago.\u201d\"}"
