{"request_id":"3891b4d2e135b6524e8af806","rewritten_text":"The only thing that really bothered him was who to get rid of; he was not blind to the fact that murder, like the religions of the Pagan world, needs both a victim and a priest. He was not a genius, so he had no enemies. He also felt it was not the moment to satisfy any personal grudge or dislike. The mission he was on was serious and solemn. So he wrote down the names of his friends and relatives on a sheet of notepaper, thought it over carefully, and decided on Lady Clementina Beauchamp, an old woman he liked very much. She lived in Curzon Street, and she was his second cousin on his mother’s side. He had always been fond of Lady Clem, as everyone called her. Since he was very wealthy himself—he had inherited Lord Rugby’s property when he came of age—there was no chance that he would gain any vulgar money from her death. In fact, the more he considered it, the more she seemed to be the right choice. And because he felt that waiting would be unfair to Sybil, he decided to make his plans right away.

First, he had to settle with the cheiromantist. He sat down at a small Sheraton writing-table near the window, wrote a cheque for £105 made out to Mr. Septimus Podgers, put it in an envelope, and told his valet to take it to West Moon Street. Then he called for his hansom from the stables and got dressed to go out. As he left the room, he looked back at Sybil Merton’s photograph and swore that, whatever happened, he would never let her know what he was doing for her sake. He would keep the secret of his sacrifice safe in his heart.

On his way to the Buckingham, he stopped at a florist’s and sent Sybil a beautiful basket of narcissus, with white petals and pheasants’ eyes. When he arrived at the club, he went straight to the library, rang for the waiter, and ordered a lemon-and-soda and a book on Toxicology. He had decided that poison was the best way to handle this difficult business. He found anything like direct violence very distasteful. He was also determined not to kill Lady Clementina in a way that would draw public attention, because he hated the idea of being celebrated at Lady Windermere’s, or of seeing his name printed in the paragraphs of vulgar society—newspapers."}

