          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f5b526d0b4a2741c99e9542d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It is only four o\u2019clock, but it is winter, and the sun has already set. The sky is clear and frosty, with no clouds to catch and reflect the slanting light. Still, the air has a faint pink tint, and that color shows again on the snow covering the ground. I live alone in a lone cottage on a wide heath. No living voice reaches me. I look out at the empty plain, all white, except for a few dark patches where the noon sun has melted the snow at the tops of the sharp little hills. As the snow fell, it slid down those hills, so it is thinner there than on the flat ground. A few birds peck at the hard ice that covers the pools, because the frost has lasted a long time.\\n\\nI am in a strange state of mind. I am alone\u2014completely alone in the world. The curse of misfortune has passed over me and left me withered. I know I am about to die, and I feel happy\u2014joyful. I feel my pulse; it beats fast. I press my thin hand to my cheek; it burns. There is a small, quick spirit in me, and it is now giving out its last sparks. I will never see the snow of another winter. I truly believe I will never again feel the life-giving warmth of another summer sun. It is because of this belief that I begin to write my tragic history. Maybe a story like mine should end with me, but something I cannot explain keeps pulling me forward. I am too weak, in both body and mind, to resist even the smallest impulse. While I was still strong, I thought my tale had a kind of sacred horror that made it unfit to speak aloud. Now, as I am about to die, I spoil its mysterious terror by writing it down. It is like the wood of the Eumenides\u2014only the dying may enter. And Oedipus is about to die.\\n\\nWhat am I writing? I must gather my thoughts. I do not know that anyone will read these pages except you, my friend, who will receive them when I die. I am not writing only to you, because it would please me to dwell on our friendship in a way that would be unnecessary if you were the only one who read what I write. So I will tell my story as if I were writing for strangers. You have often asked me why I live so alone, why I cry, and above all why I have been so silent\u2014so hard to understand and so unkind. In life I dared not. In death I reveal the mystery. Others may toss these pages aside without care. But to you, Woodville\u2014kind and affectionate friend\u2014they will be precious. They will be treasured memories of a heart-broken girl who, even while dying, is still warmed by gratitude toward you. Your tears will fall on the words that record my misfortunes. I know they will. And while I still have life, I thank you for your sympathy.\"}"
