          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ce87f2b5f149c2f371e672b5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYes, it is\u2014and I could\u2019ve had it too, if I\u2019d been big enough. I see him first. Who nailed him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt was an old fellow, a stranger. He sold out his chance in him for forty dollars, because he\u2019s got to go up the river and can\u2019t wait. Think of that! You bet I\u2019d wait, if it was seven years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s me, every time,\u201d I said. \u201cBut maybe his chance isn\u2019t worth more than that, if he\u2019ll sell it so cheap. Maybe something isn\u2019t right.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut it is. It\u2019s straight as a string. I can see the handbill myself. It tells everything about him, down to the details. It describes him like a picture and says the plantation he\u2019s from is below New Orleans. No sir, no way\u2014there\u2019s no trouble about that speculation, you bet. Say, give me a chaw of tobacco, won\u2019t you?\u201d\\n\\nI didn\u2019t have any, so he left. I went to the raft and sat down in the wigwam to think. But I couldn\u2019t come up with anything. I kept thinking until my head hurt, but I still couldn\u2019t see any way out of the trouble. After all this long trip, and after everything we\u2019d done for those scoundrels, it had all come to nothing. Everything was ruined, busted up, because they had the nerve to pull a trick like that on Jim and make him a slave again for the rest of his life\u2014among strangers, too\u2014for forty dirty dollars.\\n\\nI told myself that it would be a thousand times better for Jim to be a slave at home, where his family was, as long as he had to be a slave. So I thought I should write a letter to Tom Sawyer and tell him to tell Miss Watson where Jim was. But I gave up that idea for two reasons. First, she\u2019d be mad and disgusted at his lying and leaving, and then she\u2019d send him straight down the river again. Second, if she didn\u2019t, everyone would naturally look down on an ungrateful Black man, and Jim would feel it all the time. He\u2019d get ornery and ashamed. And then think about me. It would spread around that Huck Finn helped a Black man get his freedom. If I ever saw anyone from that town again, I\u2019d be ready to crawl down and lick their boots for shame. That\u2019s how it is: a person does something low and dirty, and then he doesn\u2019t want to face the consequences. He thinks that if he can hide it long enough, it isn\u2019t a disgrace. That was exactly my situation. The more I thought about it, the more my conscience started grinding me down, and the more wicked, low, and ornery I felt.\"}"
