          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2c0a1f319c67285fe625f67d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the center of this rift, I noticed a white spot, but at first I couldn\u2019t make out what it was. I adjusted the telescope\u2019s focus and looked again, and now I could see it clearly: a human skull.\\n\\n\u201cOnce I made that discovery, I was so confident the mystery was solved that I thought the enigma was finished; for the phrase \u2018main branch, seventh limb, east side\u2019 could only point to the skull\u2019s position on the tree, while \u2018shoot from the left eye of the death\u2019s head\u2019 allowed, in regard to searching for buried treasure, only one interpretation. I realized the plan was to drop a bullet from the skull\u2019s left eye, and that a bee-line\u2014meaning a straight line\u2014drawn from the nearest point of the trunk through \u2018the shot\u2019 (that is, the spot where the bullet fell), and then extended out to a distance of fifty feet, would mark a definite point. Beneath that point, I thought it at least possible that something valuable was hidden.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll of this,\u201d I said, \u201cis exceedingly clear, and although it\u2019s ingenious, it\u2019s still simple and direct. When you left the Bishop\u2019s Hotel, what happened then?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, after carefully taking the tree\u2019s bearings, I headed home. The instant I left \u2018the devil\u2019s seat,\u2019 though, the circular rift disappeared; no matter how I turned afterward, I couldn\u2019t get so much as a glimpse of it again. The main ingenuity in the whole scheme, as I see it, is this: repeated experiments have convinced me it\u2019s true\u2014that the circular opening can be seen from no other place than the narrow ledge on the face of the rock.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn this trip to the \u2018Bishop\u2019s Hotel,\u2019 I was accompanied by Jupiter, who, no doubt, had noticed for some weeks that my behavior had changed and made sure I wasn\u2019t left alone. But the next day, I got up very early, managed to slip away from him, and went into the hills to find the tree. After a lot of effort, I found it. When I got home that night, my valet suggested I should be flogged. As for the rest of the adventure, I believe you know it as well as I do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI suppose,\u201d I said, \u201cyou missed the spot the first time you dug, because Jupiter was too careless to let the bug fall through the left eye of the skull instead of the right.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly. That mistake changed the \u2018shot\u2019 by about two and a half inches\u2014that is, the position of the peg nearest the tree. If the treasure had been beneath the \u2018shot,\u2019 the error wouldn\u2019t have mattered much. But \u2018the shot,\u2019 along with the nearest point of the tree, were only two points used to establish a line of direction. Naturally, even a tiny error at the start grows as you extend the line, and by the time we\u2019d gone fifty feet, it had thrown us completely off the trail.\u201d\"}"
