          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5ef59ad95e7fbc619a7e790e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He then bowed and left. At the time, he did not see anyone in the street. It\u2019s a side street\u2014very lonely.\\n\\n\u201c_William Bird_, tailor, testifies that he was one of the group that entered the house. He is an Englishman. He has lived in Paris for two years. He was among the first to go up the stairs. He heard the voices arguing. The rough voice was that of a Frenchman. He could make out several words, but can\u2019t now remember them all. He distinctly heard \u2018_sacr\u00e9_\u2019 and \u2018_mon Dieu_.\u2019 At the moment, there was a sound as if several people were struggling\u2014a scraping and scuffling noise. The shrill voice was very loud\u2014louder than the rough one. He is sure it was not the voice of an Englishman. It seemed to be a German\u2019s voice. It might have been a woman\u2019s voice. He does not understand German.\\n\\n\u201cFour of the witnesses named above, being recalled, testified that the door of the chamber where the body of Mademoiselle L. was found was locked from the inside when the group reached it. Everything was perfectly silent\u2014no groans or noises of any kind. When the door was forced open, no person was seen. The windows, both in the back and the front room, were down and firmly fastened from within. A door between the two rooms was closed, but not locked. The door leading from the front room into the passage was locked, with the key on the inside. A small room at the front of the house, on the fourth story, at the end of the passage, was open, the door left ajar. This room was crowded with old beds, boxes, and the like. These were carefully removed and searched. There was not an inch of any part of the house that was not carefully searched. Sweeps were sent up and down the chimneys. The house was four stories high, with garrets (_mansardes_). A trapdoor on the roof was nailed down very securely\u2014did not appear to have been opened for years. The time that passed between hearing the voices arguing and breaking open the room door was stated differently by the witnesses. Some said it was as short as three minutes\u2014some as long as five. The door was opened with difficulty.\\n\\n\u201c_Alfonzo Garcio_, undertaker, testifies that he lives in the Rue Morgue. He is a native of Spain. He was one of the group that entered the house. He did not go up the stairs. He is nervous, and was afraid of the consequences of agitation. He heard the voices arguing. The rough voice was that of a Frenchman. He could not make out what was said. The shrill voice was that of an Englishman\u2014he is sure of this.\u201d\"}"
