          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b331355c24d83440c1c54a92\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We proclaimed ourselves freemen so loudly and with such earnestness that we caught the attention of that liberty-sounding orator and his patriotic crowd; they gathered around us and adopted a very fixed, determined posture. The orator said:\\n\\n\u201cIf, indeed, ye are freemen, ye have nought to fear\u2014the God-given liberties of Britain are about ye for your shield and shelter! (Applause.) Ye shall soon see. Bring forth your proofs.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat proofs?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cProof that ye are freemen.\u201d\\n\\nAh\u2014I remembered. I came to myself, and said nothing. But the king flew into a storm and left:\\n\\n\u201cThou'rt insane, man. It were better, and more in reason, that this thief and scoundrel here prove that we are _not_ freemen.\u201d\\n\\nYou see, he knew his own laws the way other people so often know them\u2014by words, not by results. They take a _meaning_, and it grows sharp and vivid the moment you try to apply it to yourself.\\n\\nEvery hand shook its head and looked disappointed; some turned away and lost interest. The orator spoke again\u2014this time with the tone of business, not sentiment:\\n\\n\u201cIf ye do not know your country's laws, it were time ye learned them. Ye are strangers to us; ye will not deny that. Ye may be freemen\u2014we do not deny that; but ye may also be slaves. The law is plain: it doth not call on the claimant to prove ye are slaves; it requireth you to prove ye are not.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cDear sir, give us only time to send to Astolat; or give us only time to send to the Valley of Holiness\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPeace, good man, these are extraordinary requests, and you may not expect them to be granted. It would take much time, and would unwarrantably inconvenience your master\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201c_Master_, idiot!\u201d the king raged. \u201cI have no master; I myself am the m\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSilence, for God's sake!\u201d\\n\\nI got the words out in time to stop the king. We were already in enough trouble; it could not help us to let these people think we were raving mad.\\n\\nThere is no point in dragging out the particulars. The earl housed us and then sold us at auction. This same infernal law had been in force in my own South in my own time, more than thirteen hundred years later, and under it hundreds of freemen who could not prove they were freemen were sold into lifelong slavery, without the circumstance ever making any particular impression on me; but the minute law and the auction block came into my own experience, what had been merely improper before became suddenly hellish. That is how we are made.\\n\\nYes, we were sold at auction, like swine. In a large town with an active market we might have fetched a decent price; but this place was utterly stagnant, and so we were sold for a sum that makes me ashamed whenever I think of it.\"}"
