{"request_id":"b3aca537cc196249e2b81291","rewritten_text":"When he reached the top of the staircase, he steadied himself and decided to deliver his famous peal of demonic laughter. He had found it useful on more than one occasion. People said it had turned Lord Raker’s wig grey in a single night, and it had certainly made three of Lady Canterville’s French governesses give warning before their month was up. So he let out his most horrible laugh until the old vaulted roof rang and rang again; but hardly had the terrifying echo faded when a door opened, and Mrs. Otis stepped out in a light blue dressing-gown. “I’m afraid you’re far from well,” she said, “and I’ve brought you a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. If it’s indigestion, you’ll find it an excellent remedy.” The ghost glared at her in fury and immediately began preparing to turn himself into a large black dog—an ability he was well known for, and one the family doctor always blamed for the permanent idiocy of Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Hon. Thomas Horton. The sound of approaching footsteps, however, made him pause in his grim plan. Instead, he only became faintly phosphorescent and vanished with a deep churchyard groan, just as the twins had come up to him.

When he got to his room, he completely fell apart and was consumed by the most violent agitation. The twins’ vulgarity and Mrs. Otis’s blunt materialism were naturally extremely irritating, but what truly upset him most was that he hadn’t been able to wear the suit of mail. He had hoped that even modern Americans would be thrilled by the sight of a Spectre In Armour—if not for any more sensible reason, then at least out of respect for their national poet Longfellow, whose graceful and appealing poetry he had spent many weary hours enjoying while the Cantervilles were in town. Besides, it was his own suit. He had worn it with great success at the Kenilworth tournament and had received high praise for it from no less a person than the Virgin Queen herself. Yet the moment he put it on, he was completely overwhelmed by the weight of the huge breastplate and steel casque. He fell hard onto the stone pavement, badly hurting both knees, and bruising the knuckles of his right hand.

For some days afterward, he was extremely ill and hardly left his room at all, except to keep the bloodstain in proper repair. However, with careful attention, he recovered and resolved to make a third attempt to frighten the United States Minister and his family."}

