          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"adecb5d80412303a4914efad\",\"rewritten_text\":\"A few days later, Virginia and her curly-haired cavalier went riding on Brockley meadows. While she was pushing through a hedge, she tore her habit so badly that, when she got home, she decided to use the back staircase so no one would see her. As she hurried past the Tapestry Chamber, the door was open, and she thought she saw someone inside. She assumed it was her mother\u2019s maid, who sometimes brought her work there, so she looked in to ask her to mend Virginia\u2019s habit. To Virginia\u2019s great surprise, it was the Canterville Ghost himself. He was sitting by the window, watching the ruined gold of the yellowing trees blow through the air, while the red leaves danced wildly down the long avenue. His head rested on his hand, and his whole posture showed deep depression.\\n\\nHe looked so miserable, and so broken down, that Virginia\u2014who had first thought of running away and locking herself in her room\u2014felt sorry for him. She decided to try to comfort him. Her steps were so light, and his sadness so heavy, that he did not notice her until she spoke.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m so sorry for you,\u201d she said. \u201cBut my brothers are going back to Eton tomorrow. Then, if you behave yourself, no one will bother you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s ridiculous to ask me to behave myself,\u201d he replied, turning in astonishment toward the pretty little girl who had dared to speak to him. \u201cIt\u2019s ridiculous. I must rattle my chains, groan through keyholes, and walk about at night\u2014if that\u2019s what you mean. That is the only reason I exist.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt isn\u2019t any reason at all to exist,\u201d Virginia said. \u201cAnd you know you\u2019ve been very wicked. Mrs. Umney told us, the very first day we arrived here, that you killed your wife.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I admit it,\u201d the Ghost said, sounding annoyed. \u201cBut it was a family matter, and it didn\u2019t concern anyone else.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s very wrong to kill anyone,\u201d Virginia answered. At times she had a calm, serious Puritan way of speaking, something she seemed to have picked up from an old New England ancestor.\\n\\n\u201cOh, I hate the harsh, cheap way people talk about abstract ethics! My wife was plain enough. She never had my ruffs properly starched, and she knew nothing about cooking. And do you know what? There was a buck I shot in Hogley Woods\u2014a magnificent pricket. She had it sent up to the table, and that\u2019s the sort of thing she did. But it doesn\u2019t matter now. It\u2019s all over. Still, I don\u2019t think it was very kind of her brothers to starve me to death, even though I killed her.\u201d\"}"
