          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8fc3e815dee0c4ef9f756f04\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But I must give you fair warning that I mean to tell Sybil everything. She\u2019s coming to lunch with me tomorrow, to talk about bonnets; and if Mr. Podgers learns that you\u2019ve got a bad temper, or a tendency to gout, or a wife who lives in Bayswater, I shall be sure to let her have the whole story.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur smiled and shook his head. \u201cI\u2019m not afraid,\u201d he replied. \u201cSybil knows me as well as I know her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, I\u2019m sorry to hear you say that. The right foundation for marriage is a mutual misunderstanding. No, I\u2019m not in the least cynical\u2014I\u2019ve only had experience, which is much the same thing. Mr. Podgers, Lord Arthur Savile is dying to have his hand read. Don\u2019t tell him that he\u2019s engaged to one of the prettiest girls in London, because that appeared in the _Morning Post_ a month ago.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Lady Windermere,\u201d cried the Marchioness of Jedburgh, \u201cdo let Mr. Podgers stay here a little longer. He\u2019s just told me I ought to go on the stage, and I\u2019m so taken with the idea.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf he\u2019s told you that, Lady Jedburgh, I shall certainly take him away. Come at once, Mr. Podgers, and read Lord Arthur\u2019s hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell,\u201d said Lady Jedburgh, drawing up her face in a small _moue_ as she rose from the sofa, \u201cif I\u2019m not to be allowed to go on the stage, then I must at least be permitted to sit in the audience.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly; we\u2019re all going to be part of the audience,\u201d said Lady Windermere. \u201cAnd now, Mr. Podgers, do be sure to tell us something pleasant. Lord Arthur is one of my special favourites.\u201d\\n\\nBut the moment Mr. Podgers looked at Lord Arthur\u2019s hand, he turned curiously pale and said nothing. A shiver seemed to run through him, and his thick, bushy eyebrows twitched in a convulsive, irritating way\u2014just as they did when he was at a loss. Then great beads of perspiration sprang out on his yellow forehead, like a poisonous dew, and his fat fingers went cold and clammy.\\n\\nLord Arthur noticed every one of these odd signs of agitation, and for the first time in his life he felt fear himself. He wanted to bolt from the room, but he held back. It was better to learn the worst, whatever it might be, than to be left in such a dreadful uncertainty.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m waiting, Mr. Podgers,\u201d he said.\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019re all waiting,\u201d cried Lady Windermere, in her quick, impatient fashion; yet the cheiromantist gave no answer.\\n\\n\u201cI suppose Arthur is going on the stage,\u201d said Lady Jedburgh, \u201cand after your scolding, Mr. Podgers is afraid to tell him so.\u201d\"}"
