          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ebe93dc9a6a79dcac947ad1c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For years, Dorian Gray couldn\u2019t escape the hold this book had on him. Or maybe it\u2019s truer to say he never really tried to escape it. From Paris, he ordered at least nine large-paper copies of the first edition, and had each one bound in a different color, so they could match his changing moods and the shifting desires of a nature that, at times, seemed almost completely out of his control. The hero\u2014the remarkable young Parisian, with romantic and scientific instincts mixed in such a strange way\u2014began to feel like a kind of warning image of himself. In fact, the whole book seemed to him to be the story of his own life, written before he had lived it.\\n\\nIn one respect, though, he was luckier than the novel\u2019s strange hero. He never had to learn\u2014he had no reason to learn\u2014about the same ugly fear of mirrors, polished metal, and still water that struck the young Parisian so early in life. That fear came from the sudden decay of a once extraordinary beau. Dorian used to read the later part of the book with something like cruel pleasure, and in nearly every pleasure there is cruelty. He took in its truly tragic, though slightly exaggerated, account of the sorrow and despair of someone who had lost what he had valued most, while others still had it.\\n\\nThe wonderful beauty that had fascinated Basil Hallward, and many others besides, never seemed to leave him. Even those who had heard the worst things about him\u2014and who, from time to time, passed along strange rumors about his life in London, becoming the talk of the clubs\u2014couldn\u2019t believe anything that would dishonor him when they saw him. He always had the look of someone who had stayed untouched by the world. Men who spoke in crude ways would fall silent as soon as Dorian Gray entered the room. Something in the clean purity of his face seemed to shame them. His very presence made them remember the innocence they had already ruined. They couldn\u2019t understand how someone so charming and graceful could have avoided the stain of an age that was both sordid and sensual.\\n\\nOften, when he returned home from one of those mysterious, long absences that made his friends\u2014or people who thought they were his friends\u2014make such strange guesses, he would slip upstairs himself to the locked room. He would open the door with the key that never left him, then stand with a mirror in front of the portrait Basil Hallward had painted of him. He would look at the evil, aging face on the canvas, and then at the fair young face that smiled back at him from the polished glass.\"}"
