          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"255e7313bef30a34538c740a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStory!\u201d\\n\\nHe seemed determined to misunderstand the word she had repeated. Then, in a hurry, he added, \u201cYes, customers. In banking, we usually call our connection our customers. He was a French gentleman; a scientific gentleman; a man with a great education\u2014a Doctor.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot of Beauvais?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, yes, of Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, the gentleman was from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, he was well known in Paris. I had the honor of knowing him there. Our relationship was business, but confidential. At that time I worked in our French House, and I had been there\u2014oh!\u2014for twenty years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt that time\u2014may I ask, at what time, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI mean, miss, twenty years ago. He married an English lady, and I was one of the trustees. His affairs, like the affairs of many other French gentlemen and French families, were entirely handled by Tellson\u2019s. In the same way, I am, or I have been, a trustee of one kind or another for dozens of our customers. These are only business relationships, miss. There is no friendship in them, no special interest, and nothing like sentiment. In my work, I move from one to another, the way I move from one customer to another during a normal day. In short, I have no feelings. I\u2019m just a machine. To continue\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut this is my father\u2019s story, sir; and I begin to think\u201d\u2014the woman\u2019s forehead, oddly roughened, was fixed on him\u2014\u201cthat when I was left an orphan, because my mother survived my father by only two years, it was you who brought me to England. I\u2019m almost sure it was you.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Lorry took the small, hesitant hand that had reached out to take his. With great ceremony, he put it to his lips. Then he guided the young lady back to her chair. Holding the back of the chair with his left hand, and using his right hand in turn to rub his chin, tug his wig at the ears, or point as he spoke, he stood looking down at her while she sat looking up at him.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Manette, it was I. And you will see how truly I spoke of myself just now, when I said I had no feelings and that all my relationships with other people are only business. Think about this: I have never seen you since. No\u2014you have been Tellson\u2019s ward since then, and I have been busy with Tellson\u2019s other business. Feelings! I have no time for them, no chance for them. I spend my whole life, miss, turning an enormous money Mangle.\u201d\"}"
