content.text:
{"request_id":"64ef6852b051885dc382cd95","rewritten_text":"They were her life—more precious than any place consecrated to love, more precious than all else the earth could offer. The boys listened with childish delight to the talk of our departure. Clara asked whether we were to be going to Athens. “It may be,” I answered; and at once her face brightened with pleasure. There she would see the tomb of her parents, and the land would be crowded with memories of her father’s renown. In quiet, yet without any pause, she had been brooding over these scenes. It was the recollection of them that had turned her babyish cheer into something grave, and had set her thinking on high, restless matters.

There were many dear friends we could not leave behind, even though they were humble. There was the spirited, obedient steed Lord Raymond had given his daughter; there was Alfred’s dog, and a pet eagle, its sight dulled by age. Still, this list of favorites to take with us could not be made without pain at our heavy losses, and a deep sigh for all the things we must abandon. Idris’s eyes filled with tears, while Alfred and Evelyn brought out now a beloved rose-tree, now a marble vase finely carved, insisting that these must go, and lamenting the fact that we could not carry off the castle and the forest, the deer and the birds, and every familiar, cherished object besides. “Tender and foolish ones,” I said, “we have lost forever treasures far dearer than these; and we leave them behind only to keep treasures that, compared with them, are nothing. Let us not for a moment lose sight of our purpose and our hope; and they will rise like an unyielding mound to hold back the flood of our regret for trifles.”

The children were easily pulled away from such thoughts, and soon they were back to imagining their coming amusements. Idris was gone. She had slipped off to hide her weakness; leaving the castle, she went down to the little park, seeking solitude so she might give her tears free rein. I found her clinging to an old oak, pressing her rose-colored lips to its rough trunk, while her tears fell in abundance and her sobs and broken outcries could not be restrained. With a grief that went beyond measure, I saw this beloved one of my heart thus swallowed up in sorrow. I drew her toward me; and when she felt my kisses on her eyelids, when she felt my arms around her, she returned to the awareness of what still remained to her. “You are very kind not to reproach me,” she said. “I weep, and a sharp pang of sorrow so intolerable tears at my heart. Yet I am happy; mothers mourn their children, wives lose their husbands, but you and my children are left to me.”"}

