content.text:
{"request_id":"f56cdc3ca45bacedd852cd50","rewritten_text":"He also had to consider Sybil’s father and mother—people of a rather old-fashioned turn of mind—who might well raise objections to the marriage if there were so much as a hint of scandal, though he felt sure that, once he laid out the whole particulars, they would be the first to understand the motives that had driven him. With that in mind, he had every reason to choose poison. It was safe, certain, and quiet; it spared him the need for any painful scenes, to which, like most Englishmen, he had a deep-seated aversion.

Of the science of poisons, however, he knew nothing at all. And since the waiter appeared quite incapable of finding anything in the library beyond Ruff’s Guide and Bailey’s Magazine, he went to the shelves himself. At last he came upon a handsomely bound copy of the Pharmacopoeia, and a volume of Erskine’s Toxicology, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, the President of the Royal College of Physicians, and one of the oldest members of the Buckingham—having been elected by mistake for someone else; a contretemps that so enraged the Committee that, when the true man was produced, they black-balled him unanimously. Lord Arthur was considerably at a loss for the technical terms in both books, and had begun to regret that he had not given more attention to his classics at Oxford, when, in the second volume of Erskine, he found a clear and thorough account of the properties of aconitine. It struck him as precisely the poison he needed: swift—almost immediate—in its effects; entirely painless; and, taken in the form of a gelatine capsule, as Sir Mathew recommended, by no means unpleasant to the taste. He therefore made a note, on his shirt-cuff, of the quantity required for a fatal dose, replaced the books, and walked up St. James’s Street to Pestle and Humbey’s, the great chemists. Mr. Pestle, who always attended in person to the aristocracy, was much surprised by the order, and in a very deferential tone hinted that a medical certificate would be required. But the moment Lord Arthur explained that it was for a large Norwegian mastiff he had to get rid of—showing signs of incipient rabies, and having already bitten the coachman twice in the calf of the leg—Mr. Pestle declared himself perfectly satisfied, praised Lord Arthur’s remarkable knowledge of Toxicology, and had the prescription made up at once.

Lord Arthur put the capsule into a neat little silver bonbonnière he had seen in a shop window in Bond Street, threw away Pestle and Hambey’s ugly pill-box, and drove off immediately to Lady Clementina’s."}

