          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"edc54b5b0380c8144a63acef\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, I see where I went wrong. I heard you say \u2018anybody,\u2019 and I took it that \u2018anybody\u2019 meant\u2014well, anybody; that is, everybody.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt doth\u2014anybody that is of lofty birth; and the better if he be royal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat, it seemeth, might well be,\u201d said the abbot, seizing his chance to smooth matters and head off disaster. \u201cFor it were not likely that so wondrous a gift as this would be granted for the uncovering of the affairs of lesser folk than those born near the very tops of greatness. Our Arthur the king\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWould you know of him?\u201d the enchanter cut in.\\n\\n\u201cMost gladly, yea, and gratefully.\u201d\\n\\nRight away the whole crowd of incorrigible idiots was struck again with awe and curiosity. They watched the incantations with rapt attention, and then looked at me with a \u201cThere now\u2014what do you say to that?\u201d sort of air, until the announcement came:\\n\\n\u201cThe king is weary with the chase, and lieth in his palace these two hours sleeping a dreamless sleep.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod\u2019s benison upon him!\u201d said the abbot, crossing himself. \u201cMay that sleep be for the refreshment of his body and his soul.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd so it might be, if he were sleeping,\u201d I answered, \u201cbut the king is not sleeping\u2014the king rides.\u201d\\n\\nHere was trouble again, a clash of authority. None of them knew which of us to trust; I still had a little credit left. The magician\u2019s contempt was roused, and he said:\\n\\n\u201cLo, I have seen many wonderful soothsayers and prophets and magicians in my life days, but none before that could sit idle and yet see to the heart of things, with never an incantation to lend a hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou have lived in the woods, and lost much by it. I use incantations myself, as this good brotherhood are aware\u2014but only when the moment calls for it.\u201d\\n\\nWhen it comes to sparring with sarcasm, I reckon I know how to hold my own. That thrust made the fellow fidget. The abbot asked after the queen and the court, and received this:\\n\\n\u201cThey be all on sleep, being overcome by fatigue, like as to the king.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cThat is only another falsehood. Half of them are about their amusements\u2014the queen and the other half are not sleeping; they ride. Now perhaps you can stretch yourself a little and tell us where the king and queen and all the rest are riding to this very moment.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey sleep now, as I said; but on the morrow they will ride, for they go a journey toward the sea.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd where will they be the day after to-morrow at vespers?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFar to the north of Camelot, and half their journey will be done.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is another lie, by the space of a hundred and fifty miles.\u201d\"}"
