          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0063d0036603ff65de20b717\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAnd consider, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Oliver, as tears forced themselves into his eyes despite all his efforts to stop them. \u201cOh! consider how young and good she is, and what pleasure and comfort she brings to everyone around her. I\u2019m sure\u2014certain\u2014quite certain\u2014that, for your sake, since you are so good yourself; and for her own; and for the sake of all the happiness she makes possible, she will not die. Heaven will never allow her to die so young.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHush!\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, placing her hand on Oliver\u2019s head. \u201cYou think like a child, poor boy. But you remind me of my duty, even so. I had forgotten it for a moment, Oliver, but I hope I may be forgiven, because I\u2019m old, and I\u2019ve seen enough illness and death to know the agony of being separated from the objects of our love. I\u2019ve seen enough, too, to know that it is not always the youngest and best who are spared to those who love them; but that should give us comfort in our sorrow. Heaven is just, and these things teach us\u2014powerfully\u2014that there is a brighter world than this, and that the passage to it is swift. God\u2019s will be done! I love her, and He knows how well!\u201d\\n\\nOliver was surprised to see that, as Mrs. Maylie spoke those words, she checked her lamentations as if by a single effort; then, drawing herself up as she continued, she became composed and firm. He was even more astonished to find that this firmness held. Through all the care and watching that followed, Mrs. Maylie remained ready and steady: carrying out every duty that had fallen to her, consistently, and\u2014so far as anyone could see\u2014almost cheerfully. But Oliver was young, and he didn\u2019t know what strong minds can endure under trying circumstances. How could he, when those who possess such strength so seldom understand themselves?\\n\\nAn anxious night followed. When morning came, Mrs. Maylie\u2019s predictions were only too well confirmed. Rose was in the first stage of a high and dangerous fever.\\n\\n\u201cWe must be active, Oliver, and not give in to useless grief,\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, pressing a finger to her lip as she looked steadily into his face. \u201cThis letter must be sent, with all possible speed, to Mr. Losberne. It must be carried to the market town\u2014which is no more than four miles away, across the field by the footpath\u2014and then dispatched, by an express on horseback, straight to Chertsey. The people at the inn will handle it, and I can trust you to make sure it gets done. I know I can.\u201d\\n\\nOliver couldn\u2019t find a reply, but he looked as if he wanted his anxiety to be gone at once.\"}"
