          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"66669d851249a5a75f8d7438\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the meantime, the force of gravity would keep weakening, in proportion to the squares of the distances. With a velocity that would accelerate enormously, I should finally reach those far-off regions where the earth\u2019s pull would be replaced by the moon\u2019s. Based on these ideas, I didn\u2019t think it was worth bringing more provisions than would be enough for forty days.\\n\\n\u201cThere was still, however, another difficulty, one that caused me some real unease. It has been observed that, in balloon ascents to any considerable height, besides the pain that comes with breathing, people often feel intense anxiety in the head and body. This is frequently accompanied by bleeding from the nose and other alarming symptoms, which become more and more troublesome the higher the altitude. This was a thought of a rather startling kind. Wasn\u2019t it likely that these symptoms would keep increasing indefinitely\u2014or at least continue until death ended them? In the end, I decided that it would not. Their cause, I believed, lay in the gradual removal of the usual atmospheric pressure acting on the body\u2019s surface, leading to the distention of the superficial blood vessels\u2014not in any direct breakdown of the animal system, as in the case of breathing difficulty, where the atmospheric density is chemically too low to properly refresh the blood in a ventricle of the heart. Unless that refreshing failed, I could see no reason, then, why life couldn\u2019t be sustained even in a vacuum; for the expansion and compression of the chest\u2014what we commonly call breathing\u2014is an action that is purely muscular. It is the cause of respiration, not its effect. In short, I thought that as the body became accustomed to the lack of atmospheric pressure, the sensations of pain would gradually lessen. And while they lasted, I relied with confidence on the iron toughness of my constitution.\\n\\n\u201cThus, may it please your Excellencies, I have laid out some of the considerations\u2014though by no means all\u2014that led me to form the plan of a lunar voyage. I will now go on to present the results of an attempt so seemingly audacious in its conception, and, in any case, so utterly without precedent in the annals of mankind.\\n\\n\u201cAfter reaching the altitude I mentioned\u2014three miles and three-quarters\u2014I threw a quantity of feathers out of the car and found that I still rose with enough speed. So there was no need to discharge any ballast. I was glad of that, because I wanted to keep with me as much weight as I could carry, for reasons that I will explain later.\u201d\"}"
