          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bf9fc99cacb956ce98d09e9d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I walked with a quick pace, and we soon arrived at my college. Then I stopped to think, and the thought made me shiver: the creature I had left in my apartment might still be there, alive and moving around. I dreaded the moment I might have to see this monster, but I feared even more that Henry might see him. So I urged him to stay a few minutes at the bottom of the stairs, and I darted up toward my own room. My hand was already on the door\u2019s lock before I caught myself. I paused, and a cold shiver ran through me. I threw the door open hard, the way children do when they expect a ghost to be waiting for them on the other side\u2014but nothing was there. I stepped in cautiously: the apartment was empty, and my bedroom was free of its hideous guest as well. I could hardly believe that such a great good fortune could have fallen to me, but once I was certain my enemy had truly fled, I clapped my hands for joy and ran down to Clerval.\\n\\nWe went up into my room, and the servant soon brought breakfast; but I couldn\u2019t contain myself. It wasn\u2019t only joy that possessed me. I felt my skin tingle with an almost unbearable sensitivity, and my pulse hammered rapidly. I couldn\u2019t stay in one place for a single instant; I jumped over the chairs, clapped my hands, and laughed out loud. At first, Clerval thought my unusual spirits were simply the result of joy at his arrival. But when he watched me more closely, he saw a wildness in my eyes he couldn\u2019t explain, and my loud, uncontrolled, heartless laughter frightened and astonished him.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\u201d he cried, \u201cfor God\u2019s sake, what is the matter? Don\u2019t laugh like that. You\u2019re terribly ill\u2014what\u2019s causing all this?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t ask me,\u201d I cried, covering my eyes with my hands, because I thought I saw the dreaded specter glide into the room. \u201cHe can tell. Oh, save me! Save me!\u201d I imagined the monster had seized me; I fought furiously and then collapsed into a fit.\\n\\nPoor Clerval! What must he have felt? A meeting he had expected with such joy had turned so strangely into bitterness. But I wasn\u2019t there to witness his grief\u2014I was lifeless, and I didn\u2019t recover my senses for a long, long time.\\n\\nThis was the beginning of a nervous fever that kept me confined for several months. During all that time, Henry was my only nurse. Later I learned that, knowing my father\u2019s advanced age and his inability to make such a long journey, and knowing how miserable my sickness would make Elizabeth, he spared them this grief by hiding how severe my disorder really was.\"}"
