          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5a7fbdddc5e6713f5cc0a6ef\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The doctor was an old man, and when I got him up he seemed very nice and kind. I told him my brother and I had been over on Spanish Island hunting yesterday afternoon, and that we camped on a piece of raft we found. I also told him that about midnight he must have kicked his gun in his dreams, because it went off and shot him in the leg. We wanted him to go over there and fix it, and we wanted him to say nothing about it and not let anyone know. We were planning to come home this evening and surprise the folks.\\n\\n\u201cWho are your folks?\u201d he asked.\\n\\n\u201cThe Phelpses, down yonder.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he said. After a minute he asked, \u201cHow did you say he got shot?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe had a dream,\u201d I said. \u201cAnd it shot him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA singular dream,\u201d he said.\\n\\nThen he lit up his lantern, got his saddle-bags, and we started. But when he saw the canoe, he didn\u2019t like the look of it. He said it was big enough for one person, but it didn\u2019t look safe for two. I told him, \u201cYou don\u2019t need to be afraid, sir. It carried the three of us easy enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat three?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMe and Sid, and\u2014and\u2014and the guns. That\u2019s what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he said.\\n\\nHe put his foot on the side of the canoe and rocked it, then shook his head and said he thought he\u2019d look around for a bigger one. But all the other canoes were locked and chained. So he took my canoe and told me to wait until he came back. He said I could look around some more, or maybe I should go down home and get everything ready for the surprise if I wanted to. I said I didn\u2019t, so I told him how to find the raft, and then he went.\\n\\nI got an idea pretty soon. I thought to myself, if he can\u2019t fix that leg in three shakes of a sheep\u2019s tail, like they say, and if it takes him three or four days, what are we going to do? Just sit around there until he lets the cat out of the bag? No, sir. I knew what I\u2019d do. I\u2019d wait. And when he came back, if he said he had to go any farther, I\u2019d go down there too, if I had to swim. Then we\u2019d take him, tie him up, keep him, and push off down the river. After Tom was done with him, we\u2019d give him what it was worth, or all we had, and then let him get ashore.\\n\\nSo I slipped into a lumber pile to get some sleep. The next time I woke up, the sun was way up over my head. I jumped up and ran to the doctor\u2019s house, but they told me he\u2019d left sometime during the night and hadn\u2019t come back yet.\"}"
