          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3b3ee0d5cd328033c0a118e3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cJust a tick.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere\u2019d you find him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOut in the woods.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019ll you want for him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. I don\u2019t want to part with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFine. Still, it\u2019s a pretty small tick, all the same.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAny fool can pick up a tick that don\u2019t belong to him. I\u2019m content with this one. It\u2019s good enough for me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSho, there are ticks enough. If I felt like it, I could have a thousand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, why don\u2019t you then? You know as well as I do you can\u2019t. This one\u2019s pretty early, I figure. It\u2019s the first I\u2019ve seen this year.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSay, Huck\u2014I'll trade you my tooth for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet me see it.\u201d\\n\\nTom pulled out some paper and carefully unrolled it. Huckleberry looked at it with longing. The urge was mighty strong. Finally he said:\\n\\n\u201cIs it genuine?\u201d\\n\\nTom lifted his lip and showed the empty spot.\\n\\n\u201cWell, all right,\u201d said Huckleberry. \u201cIt\u2019s a deal.\u201d\\n\\nTom tucked the tick into the percussion-cap box that had only lately been the pinchbug\u2019s jail, and the boys went their separate ways, both feeling richer than they had before.\\n\\nWhen Tom got to the little, stand-alone frame schoolhouse, he went in with a brisk stride, acting like he\u2019d come by nothing but plain, honest speed. He hung his hat on a peg and dropped into his seat with businesslike eagerness. The teacher sat up high in his big splint-bottom chair, dozing, soothed by the drowsy murmur of lessons. The interruption snapped him awake.\\n\\n\u201cThomas Sawyer!\u201d\\n\\nTom knew that when his name was said in full, it meant trouble.\\n\\n\u201cSir!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCome up here. Now, sir\u2014why are you late again, like you always are?\u201d\\n\\nTom was ready to hide behind a lie, but then he saw two long ribbons of yellow hair hanging down a back he recognized at once by the electric pull of love; and there, by that very sight, was the only empty place on the girls\u2019 side of the schoolhouse. Without missing a beat he blurted:\\n\\n\u201c_I stopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn!_\u201d\\n\\nThe master\u2019s pulse seemed to freeze, and he stared as if he couldn\u2019t help himself. The hum of study stopped. The pupils wondered whether the boy had gone out of his mind. The master said:\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2014you did what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cStopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn.\u201d\\n\\nThere was no mistaking it.\\n\\n\u201cThomas Sawyer, this is the most astonishing confession I\u2019ve ever heard. No ordinary switch will cover this. Take off your jacket.\u201d\\n\\nThe master\u2019s arm went to work until it was worn out and the supply of switches had clearly dwindled. Then came the next order:\\n\\n\u201cNow, sir, go sit with the girls! And let this be a warning to you.\u201d\\n\\nThe little ripple of laughter that ran through the room did make the boy look a bit flustered, but it was mostly because, underneath, he was struck with reverent awe for his unknown idol\u2014and with a dread kind of delight at how lucky he\u2019d gotten.\"}"
