          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e2efd908094ebb4e8f758b89\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Merton was quite distressed by the second postponement of the marriage, and Lady Julia, who had already ordered her dress for the wedding, did everything she could to make Sybil call off the match. Deeply as Sybil loved her mother, she had given her whole life into Lord Arthur\u2019s hands, and nothing Lady Julia could say could make her waver in her faith. As for Lord Arthur himself, it took him days to get over his terrible disappointment, and for a time his nerves were completely shattered. His excellent common sense, however, soon asserted itself, and his sound, practical mind did not leave him long in doubt about what to do. Since poison had proved a complete failure, dynamite\u2014or some other kind of explosive\u2014was obviously what he should try.\\n\\nHe therefore went back over the list of his friends and relatives, and, after careful consideration, decided to blow up his uncle, the Dean of Chichester. The Dean, a man of great culture and learning, was extremely fond of clocks, and he had a wonderful collection of timepieces, from the fifteenth century up to the present day. It seemed to Lord Arthur that this hobby of the good Dean\u2019s offered an excellent opportunity for carrying out his plan. Getting hold of an explosive machine, of course, was another matter entirely. The London Directory gave him no information, and he felt there was little point in going to Scotland Yard about it, since they never seemed to know anything about the dynamite faction\u2019s movements until after an explosion had already taken place, and not much even then.\\n\\nSuddenly he thought of his friend Rouvaloff, a young Russian with very revolutionary leanings, whom he had met at Lady Windermere\u2019s in the winter. Count Rouvaloff was supposed to be writing a life of Peter the Great, and to have come to England to study the documents connected with that Tsar\u2019s residence in this country as a ship carpenter; but it was widely suspected that he was a Nihilist agent, and there was no doubt that the Russian Embassy did not look kindly on his presence in London. Lord Arthur felt he was exactly the man for the job, and drove down one morning to his lodgings in Bloomsbury to ask his advice and help.\\n\\n\u201cSo you\u2019re taking up politics seriously?\u201d Count Rouvaloff said when Lord Arthur explained the purpose of his visit; but Lord Arthur, who hated any kind of swagger, felt obliged to admit that he had not the slightest interest in social questions and simply wanted the explosive machine for a purely family matter, in which no one was involved but himself.\"}"
