          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d711898ecdaa5f1fd09beaee\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These deputies had been sent by them to Adrian, who had been chosen as arbiter. They traveled from Paris to Calais, passing through empty towns and lonely countryside, all the while feeding their violent hatred for one another. Now they argued their separate cases with complete party spirit.\\n\\nWhen we looked at the deputies one by one, and after a long investigation, we learned the real situation in Paris. Since Parliament had chosen him as Ryland\u2019s deputy, all the surviving English had submitted to Adrian. He was our captain, to lead us from our home soil to unknown lands; he was our lawgiver and our protector. When we first planned our emigration, no lasting separation of our members was expected, and the command of the whole group, as its power grew step by step, reached its highest point in the Earl of Windsor. But unexpected events changed our plans and forced most of our people to be separated from the supreme chief for nearly two months. They had gone over in two separate groups, and when they arrived in Paris, conflict broke out between them.\\n\\nThey found Paris deserted. When the plague first appeared, the return of travelers and merchants, along with letters, kept us informed about how badly disease was striking the continent. But as the death rate rose, that contact slowed down and then stopped. Even within England, communication between one part of the island and another became slow and rare. No ship crossed the flood that separated Calais from Dover; or if some sad traveler tried to sail from the French shore back to us to learn whether relatives were alive or dead, the greedy ocean often swallowed his small boat, or after a day or two he caught the disease and died before he could tell us what had happened in France. So we were largely ignorant of conditions on the continent, and we still had some unclear hope of finding many companions there. But the same causes that had so severely reduced the English population gave even more room for harm in the sister country. France was a blank. Along the long road from Calais to Paris, not a single person was seen. In Paris there were a few\u2014maybe a hundred\u2014who, resigned to what was coming, moved through the streets of the capital and gathered to talk about the past, with a liveliness and even a kind of cheer that rarely leaves this nation\u2019s people.\\n\\nThe English took control of Paris without resistance. Its tall houses and narrow streets were lifeless. A few pale figures could be seen at the usual place, the Tuileries. They wondered why the islanders would come toward their doomed city, because in the worst misery, people always imagine their own suffering is the bitterst. When we are enduring intense pain, we would trade the particular torture we are feeling for any other that might strike a different part of the body.\"}"
