          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"742b2652d16d04b1b0a08ad0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This was Mrs. Umney, the housekeeper, whom Mrs. Otis\u2014at Lady Canterville\u2019s pressing request\u2014had agreed to let remain in her old post. When they stepped down, she curtsied low to each of them and, in a quaint, old-world way, said, \u201cI bid you welcome to Canterville Chase.\u201d After her, they went through the handsome Tudor hall into the library, a long, low chamber panelled in black oak, where at the far end stood a great stained-glass window. Tea had been laid out for them there; after they had taken off their wraps, they sat down and began to look about, while Mrs. Umney attended to them.\\n\\nAll at once Mrs. Otis noticed a dull red mark on the floor near the fireplace, and without the least idea what it meant, told Mrs. Umney, \u201cI\u2019m afraid something has been spilt there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, madam,\u201d answered the old housekeeper in a low voice. \u201cBlood has been spilt on that spot.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow dreadful,\u201d cried Mrs. Otis. \u201cI don\u2019t care for blood-stains in a sitting-room. It ought to be taken care of at once.\u201d\\n\\nThe old woman smiled and replied in the same subdued, shadowy tone: \u201cIt is the blood of Lady Eleanore de Canterville, who was murdered on that very place by her own husband, Sir Simon de Canterville, in 1575. Sir Simon lived on for nine years after her, and then vanished under circumstances most mysterious. His body has never been found, yet his guilty spirit still haunts the Chase. The stain has been greatly admired by tourists and others, and it cannot be removed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s all nonsense,\u201d Washington Otis snapped. \u201cPinkerton\u2019s Champion Stain Remover and Paragon Detergent will have it cleaned up in a jiffy,\u201d and before the frightened housekeeper could get in a word, he dropped to his knees and began to scrub the floor with a small stick that looked like some sort of black cosmetic. In a few moments not the faintest trace of the blood-stain remained.\\n\\n\u201cI knew Pinkerton would manage it,\u201d he declared, looking around at his delighted family; but no sooner had he spoken than a dreadful flash of lightning lit the gloomy room, followed by a violent peal of thunder that made them all jump to their feet, and Mrs. Umney fainted.\\n\\n\u201cWhat a monstrous climate!\u201d said the American Minister, perfectly composed, as he lit a long cheroot. \u201cI suppose the old country is so overrun that there isn\u2019t decent weather enough for everyone. I\u2019ve always thought emigration is the only remedy for England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Hiram,\u201d Mrs. Otis cried, \u201cwhat are we to do with a woman who faints?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCharge it to her like breakages,\u201d the Minister replied. \u201cShe won\u2019t faint after that.\u201d And in a few moments, Mrs. Umney indeed came to.\"}"
