          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"356d4c48fb5f1645cfa383b4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Many American women, when they leave their home country, start to look as if they are always sick. They think this is a kind of European polish, but Mrs. Otis had never made that mistake. She had a strong constitution and a truly wonderful store of energy. In many ways, she was almost English, and she was a good example of how much America and England have in common these days\u2014except, of course, for the language.\\n\\nHer eldest son, named Washington by his parents in a burst of patriotism, was a fair-haired, good-looking young man. He regretted the name, though he had trained himself for American diplomacy by leading the German at the Newport Casino for three straight seasons. Even in London, he was known as an excellent dancer. Gardenias and the peerage were his only weaknesses. Otherwise, he was extremely sensible.\\n\\nMiss Virginia E. Otis was fifteen, slim and lovely as a fawn, with a free, open look in her large blue eyes. She was a wonderful rider. Once, she raced old Lord Bilton on her pony twice around the park, winning by a length and a half, just in front of the Achilles statue. The young Duke of Cheshire was delighted and proposed to her on the spot. Her guardians sent him back to Eton that same night, in floods of tears. After Virginia came the twins, usually called \u201cThe Stars and Stripes,\u201d because they were always getting into trouble. They were delightful boys, and, aside from the respectable Minister, they were the only true republicans in the family.\\n\\nSince Canterville Chase was seven miles from Ascot, the nearest railway station, Mr. Otis had telegraphed for a waggonette to meet them. They set off for the drive in high spirits. It was a lovely July evening, and the air smelled faintly of pine. Now and then they heard a wood pigeon cooing over its own sweet voice, or they saw, deep in the rustling fern, the pheasant\u2019s shining breast. Little squirrels watched them from the beech trees, and rabbits darted away through the brushwood and over the mossy knolls, their white tails flashing as they ran. But when they entered the avenue of Canterville Chase, the sky suddenly turned cloudy. The air seemed strangely quiet. A large flock of rooks passed silently overhead, and before they reached the house, some heavy drops of rain had already fallen.\\n\\nWaiting on the steps to greet them was an old woman, dressed neatly in black silk, with a white cap and apron.\"}"
