          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5353c072bd9b90cd38e0f224\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWe have been to your home,\u201d said the first. \u201cWe didn\u2019t find you there. We were told you were probably walking this way, so we followed you, hoping to catch up. Will you please get into the carriage?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe way they spoke was forceful, and as they said it they both moved so that I was between them and the carriage door. They were armed. I was not.\\n\\n\u201c\u2018Gentlemen,\u2019 I said, \u2018please excuse me. I usually ask who is asking for my help, and what the matter is that brings me here.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cThe second man answered. \u2018Doctor, your clients are people of standing. As for what the case is, we trust your skill to tell you what it is better than we could explain. That is enough. Will you please get into the carriage?\u2019\\n\\n\u201cI had no choice but to obey, and I got in without speaking. They both got in after me\u2014the last one jumped in after putting up the steps. The carriage turned around and drove on at the same speed as before.\\n\\n\u201cI repeat this conversation exactly as it happened. I have no doubt it is, word for word, the same. I describe everything exactly as it took place, making myself stay focused and not let my mind wander from the task. Where I put the marks that are missing here, I stop for the moment and put my paper back in its hiding place.\\n\\n*****\\n\\n\u201cThe carriage left the streets behind, passed the North Barrier, and came out onto the country road. About two-thirds of a league past the Barrier\u2014at the time I did not measure it, but later I did\u2014it left the main road and soon stopped at a lonely house. All three of us got out. We walked, along a damp, soft path through a garden where a neglected fountain had overflowed, to the door of the house. The door was not opened right away when the bell was rung. One of my two escorts struck the man who opened it across the face with his heavy riding glove.\\n\\n\u201cThis didn\u2019t catch my attention in a special way, because I had seen ordinary people struck more often than dogs. But the other brother, still angry, struck the man in the same way with his arm. Their faces and manner were so alike that I only then realized they were twin brothers.\\n\\n\u201cFrom the time we got out at the outer gate\u2014which we found locked, and which one of the brothers had opened to let us in and then relocked\u2014I had heard cries coming from an upper room.\u201d\"}"
