{"request_id":"73fa09b19fda5a98c74de588","rewritten_text":"I don’t understand at all why she wanted to be intimate with me; but if the same circumstances were to happen again, I’m sure I would be deceived again. Caroline didn’t return my visit until yesterday, and in the meantime I received not a note, not a line. When she finally came, it was very clear that she took no pleasure in it. She offered a small, formal apology for not calling before, said not a word about wishing to see me again, and in every respect she was so changed that, when she left, I was completely resolved to end the acquaintance. I pity her, though I can’t help blaming her. She was very wrong to single me out as she did; I can say safely that every step toward intimacy began on her side. Still, I pity her, because she must know she’s been acting wrongly, and because I’m very sure that her anxiety about her brother is the cause of it. I don’t need to explain myself further; and even though we know this anxiety is quite needless, if she feels it, it will easily explain her behavior toward me. And as dear as he is to his sister, any anxiety she may feel on his behalf is natural and kind. I can’t help wondering, though, why she has any such fears now, because if he had cared about me at all, we would have met long, long ago. He must know I’m in town, I’m certain, from something she said herself; yet her manner of talking makes it seem as if she’s trying to convince herself that he is really partial to Miss Darcy. I can’t understand it. If I weren’t afraid of judging too harshly, I’d almost be tempted to say that there’s a strong appearance of duplicity in all of this. I will try to drive away every painful thought and focus only on what will make me happy—your affection, and the steady kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Let me hear from you very soon. Miss Bingley said something about his never returning to Netherfield again, about giving up the house, but not with any certainty. We’d better not mention it. I’m extremely glad that you have such pleasant accounts from our friends at Hunsford. Please go and see them, with Sir William and Maria. I’m sure you’ll be very comfortable there.

“Yours, etc.”

This letter caused Elizabeth some pain, but her spirits returned when she considered that Jane would no longer be duped—at least not by the sister. All expectation from the brother was now absolutely over."}

