          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8bfed9aa61ef30825ba25fb4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After I had sent them in, I found I could not stay away from the very places where they were lodged, and I had the sense that, when I stood near them, they looked more hopeful and less desperate. In that restless, tormenting state of mind I wandered the streets of an evening, going past those offices and houses where I had left the petitions. Even to this hour, the weary western streets of London on a cold, dusty spring night\u2014the stern, shut-up mansions in their rows, and the long lines of lamps\u2014still weigh on me with melancholy, because of what I had tied to them.\\n\\nThe daily visits I could make him were cut down now, and he was kept under stricter watch. Thinking, or imagining, that I was suspected of intending to carry poison to him, I asked to be searched before I sat down at his bedside, and I told the officer who was always there that I was ready to do anything that might assure him of the single-mindedness of my purpose. Nobody was harsh with him or with me. There was work to be done, and it was done, but without cruelty. The officer would always tell me that he was worse, and that some other sick prisoners in the room, and some other prisoners who attended on them as sick nurses\u2014(villains, to be sure, but not without kindness, thank God!)\u2014were saying the same thing.\\n\\nAs the days went on, I saw more and more that he would lie quietly, staring at the white ceiling, with a blankness on his face until some word of mine lit it up for an instant, and then it would sink back again. At times he could hardly speak at all; then he would answer me with slight pressure of my hand, and I came to understand what he meant.\\n\\nWhen the number of days had reached ten, I noticed a change in him greater than any I had yet seen. His eyes were fixed toward the door, and they brightened the moment I came in.\\n\\n\u201cDear boy,\u201d he said, as I sat down by his bed. \u201cI thought you was late. But I knew you couldn\u2019t be that.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is just the time,\u201d said I. \u201cI waited for it at the gate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou always waits at the gate; don\u2019t you, dear boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. Not to lose a moment of the time.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank\u2019ee, dear boy, thank\u2019ee. God bless you! You\u2019ve never deserted me, dear boy.\u201d\\n\\nI held his hand in silence, because I could not forget that I had once meant to desert him.\\n\\n\u201cAnd what\u2019s the best of all,\u201d he said, \u201cyou\u2019ve been more comfortable alonger me, since I was under a dark cloud, than when the sun shone.\u201d\"}"
