          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"74ea828507bdfe2210d7425b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cCharles Evr\u00e9monde, called Darnay,\u201d was finally brought before the court.\\n\\nThe judges sat on the bench wearing feathered hats, though most people else wore a rough red cap with a tricolored cockade. When Darnay looked at the jury and the angry crowd, it almost seemed as if the usual order had been flipped, and that criminals were judging honest men. The worst, most cruel people in the city\u2014always ready in large numbers\u2014ran the whole scene. They shouted comments, cheered, booed, guessed what would happen, and pushed the outcome along, with no one stopping them. Many of the men were armed in different ways. Among the women, some had knives, some had daggers. Some ate and drank while they watched, and many knitted. One of the women, with a spare piece of knitting under her arm as she worked, sat in the front row beside a man Darnay had never seen since his arrival at the Barrier. Darnay remembered that man at once: Defarge. Darnay noticed that she leaned close and whispered to him once or twice, and that she seemed to be his wife. What he noticed most, though, was that even though they were as close to him as they could be, they never looked at him. They acted as if they were waiting for something, stubbornly determined. They stared at the jury, but at nothing else. Under the President sat Doctor Manette, dressed as quietly as always. As far as the prisoner could tell, he and Mr. Lorry were the only men there who were not connected to the tribunal. They wore their usual clothes and had not put on the rough Carmagnole.\\n\\nCharles Evr\u00e9monde, called Darnay, was accused by the public prosecutor of being an emigrant. His life, the prosecutor said, was forfeit to the Republic under the decree that banished all emigrants on pain of death. It did not matter, the prosecutor argued, that the decree had been written long ago, before Darnay returned to France. The decree still existed, and Darnay was back in France. He had been taken in France, and they demanded his head.\\n\\n\u201cTake off his head!\u201d the crowd shouted. \u201cAn enemy to the Republic!\u201d\\n\\nThe President rang his bell to silence them and asked the prisoner whether it was true that he had lived in England for many years.\\n\\nUndoubtedly it was.\\n\\nThen was he not an emigrant? What did he call himself?\\n\\nNot an emigrant, he said, in the sense and spirit of the law.\\n\\nWhy not? the President asked.\\n\\nBecause he had voluntarily given up a title he disliked, and a position he disliked. He had left his country\u2014he added, before the word emigrant was used in the way the tribunal meant it\u2014so he could live by his own work in England, instead of living off the work of the overburdened people of France.\"}"
