          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a37d66bbda88d1a902c77cca\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sandy and I were back on the road again the next morning, bright and early. It felt so good to open up my lungs and take in whole, luscious barrels of the blessed God\u2019s untainted, dew-made, woodland-scented air once more\u2014after being stuck for two days and nights in the moral and physical stench of that intolerable old buzzard-roost. I mean, for me: of course the place was fine enough and agreeable for Sandy, since she\u2019d been used to high life all her days.\\n\\nPoor girl\u2014her jaws had had a weary rest for a while now, and I was expecting to face the consequences. I was right, but she\u2019d stood by me most helpfully in the castle, and had backed me up with gigantic, foolish things that were worth more for the moment than wisdoms twice their size. So I figured she\u2019d earned the right to run her mill for a while, if she wanted to, and I didn\u2019t feel a pang when she started it up:\\n\\n\u201cNow turn we unto Sir Marhaus that rode with the damsel of thirty winter of age southward\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you going to see if you can work up another half-stretch on the trail of the cowboys, Sandy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEven so, fair my lord.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGo ahead, then. I won\u2019t interrupt this time, if I can help it. Start over again\u2014begin clean, shake out all your reefs, and I\u2019ll load my pipe and pay close attention.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow turn we unto Sir Marhaus that rode with the damsel of thirty winter of age southward. And so they came into a deep forest, and by fortune they were nighted, and rode along in a deep way, and at the last they came into a courtelage where abode the duke of South Marches, and there they asked harbour. And on the morn the duke sent unto Sir Marhaus, and bade him make him ready. And so Sir Marhaus arose and armed himself, and there was a mass sung before him, and he broke his fast, and so mounted on horseback in the court of the castle, there they should do the battle. So there was the duke already on horseback, clean armed, and his six sons with him, and each one had a spear in his hand, and so they encountered\u2014whereas the duke and his two sons broke their spears upon him, but Sir Marhaus held up his spear and touched none of them. Then came the four sons in pairs, and two of them broke their spears, and so did the other two. And all this while Sir Marhaus didn\u2019t touch them. Then Sir Marhaus ran to the duke and struck him with his spear, so that horse and man fell to the earth.\u201d}\"}"
