st any trick or artifice; and by these means I won a greater measure of composure. Indeed, as the time approached, the threat began to seem more like a delusion\u2014something not worth disturbing my peace\u2014while the happiness I expected from my marriage appeared more and more certain, as the day for its solemnisation drew nearer and I heard it spoken of again and again as an event no accident could possibly prevent.\\n\\nElizabeth appeared content; my steady manner helped greatly to quiet her mind. But on the very day meant to fulfil my wishes and my fate, she was cast down, and a presentiment of evil lay upon her; and perhaps she also recalled the dreadful secret I had promised to disclose to her on the next day.\"}"
