          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"65c9317f6600cfe6d88a75fb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Well, three or four months passed, and it was winter by then. I had been to school almost all the time. I could spell and read and write a little, and I could say the multiplication table up to six times seven is thirty-five. I don\u2019t think I could ever get any farther than that, even if I lived forever. I don\u2019t really believe in math anyway.\\n\\nAt first I hated school, but after a while I could stand it. When I got unusually tired, I played hookey. The beating I got the next day helped me feel better and cheered me up. The longer I went, the easier it became. I was also getting used to the widow\u2019s way of doing things, and it wasn\u2019t as rough on me. Living in a house and sleeping in a bed felt too tight for me most of the time. Before the cold weather, though, I would slip out and sleep in the woods sometimes, and that gave me a break. I liked the old ways best, but I was starting to like the new ones a little too. The widow said I was coming along slowly but surely, and that I was doing very well. She said she wasn\u2019t ashamed of me.\\n\\nOne morning at breakfast, I turned over the salt-cellar. I reached for it as fast as I could, trying to throw the salt over my left shoulder to avoid bad luck. But Miss Watson was quicker than me, and she stopped me. She said, \u201cTake your hands away, Huckleberry. You\u2019re always making a mess!\u201d The widow defended me, but that wasn\u2019t going to stop the bad luck. I knew that. After breakfast I went out feeling worried and shaky, wondering where the bad luck would land and what it would be. There are ways to avoid some kinds of bad luck, but this wasn\u2019t one of those. So I didn\u2019t try to do anything. I just kept going, low-spirited and on guard.\\n\\nI went down to the front garden and climbed over the stile where you pass through the tall board fence. There was an inch of fresh snow on the ground, and I