 know, that don\u2019t seem to mean anything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou got one of those papers, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell then, how\u2019re you supposed to find the marks?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t need any marks. They always bury it under a haunted house, or on an island, or under a dead tree with one limb sticking out. We\u2019ve tried Jackson\u2019s Island some already, and we can try it again another time; and there\u2019s that old haunted house on the Still-House branch, and plenty of dead-limb trees\u2014dead loads of \u2019em.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs it under all of them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow can you say that? No!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen how\u2019re you going to know which one to pick?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPick all of \u2019em!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTom, it\u2019ll take all summer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo what? Say you turn up a brass pot with a hundred dollars in it\u2014rusty and gray\u2014or a rotten chest full of diamonds. What do you think of that?\u201d\\n\\nHuck\u2019s eyes lit up.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s great. Plenty great for me. Just you hand me the hundred dollars, and I don\u2019t want no diamonds.\u201d\"}"
