          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"efc620bccda62a0a6d187f12\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For that reason, I decided to get off as soon as we reached the town and move out of his hearing. I carried out the plan without trouble. My small portmanteau was in the boot under my feet. All I had to do was turn a hinge to get it out. I threw it down in front of me, climbed out after it, and ended up standing on the first lamp of the town pavement. As for the convicts, they rode on with the coach, and I knew exactly where they would be taken next, to the river. In my mind, I pictured the boat waiting for them at the slime-washed steps, and I could hear the rough \u201cGive way, you!\u201d again, like a command shouted at dogs. I saw the wicked Noah\u2019s Ark again, sitting on the black water.\\n\\nI couldn\u2019t say what I was afraid of, because my fear didn\u2019t have any clear shape. It was just a vague, heavy dread, but it was strong. As I walked to the hotel, I felt a kind of terror that went far beyond the normal worry of facing something unpleasant or embarrassing. I\u2019m sure it didn\u2019t come with any clear picture, only the return for a few minutes of the fear I had as a child.\\n\\nThe coffee-room at the Blue Boar was empty. I had not only ordered my dinner there, but had already sat down to eat it before the waiter even recognized me. The moment he apologized for forgetting, he asked if he should send Boots for Mr. Pumblechook.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d I said. \u201cCertainly not.\u201d\\n\\nThe waiter looked surprised. He was the same man who, on the day I was set to leave, had brought up the Great Remonstrance from the Commercials. He quickly seized the first chance he had to slide a dirty old local newspaper right in front of me. I picked it up and read the paragraph:\\n\\nOur readers will learn, not altogether without interest, in reference to the recent romantic rise in fortune of a young artificer in iron of this neighbourhood (what a theme, by the way, for the magic pen of our as yet not universally acknowledged townsman TOOBY, the poet of our columns!) that the youth\u2019s earliest patron, companion, and friend, was a highly respected individual not entirely unconnected with the corn and seed trade, and whose eminently convenient and commodious business premises are situate within a hundred miles of the High Street. It is not wholly irrespective of our personal feelings that we record HIM as the Mentor of our young Telemachus, for it is good to know that our town produced the founder of the latter\u2019s fortunes.\"}"
