{"request_id":"994d344884b3bb7fb23b6493","rewritten_text":"As I ran my hand through the valve, holding a cup of water for the old puss, the sleeves of my shirt caught in the loop that held the basket up, and in an instant it slipped free from the bottom. If the whole thing had truly vanished into thin air, it could not have left my sight more suddenly or more completely. There could not have been even the smallest fraction of a second between the basket’s release and its absolute, total disappearance, with everything it contained. My good wishes followed it to the earth, though, naturally, I had no expectation that either cat or kittens would ever live to relate the misfortune.

“At six o’clock, I noticed a large portion of the earth’s visible area to the eastward thrown into thick shadow, and it kept pressing forward with remarkable speed, until, at five minutes before seven, the entire surface in view was wrapped in the darkness of night. Yet it was not until long after that the rays of the setting sun stopped reaching the balloon; and though this was, of course, something I had fully expected, it still gave me an infinite amount of satisfaction. It was clear that in the morning I should see the rising luminary many hours before the citizens of Rotterdam, even though they were so much farther to the east; and so, day after day, as I rose higher, I would gain the sun’s light for a longer and longer stretch. I therefore resolved to keep a journal of my passage, counting the days from one to twenty-four hours continuously, without regard to the intervals of darkness.

“At ten o’clock, feeling drowsy, I decided to lie down for the remainder of the night; but at this point a difficulty arose—plain enough, perhaps, yet one I had overlooked until the very instant I am now describing. If I fell asleep as I intended, how would the air in the chamber be renewed in the meantime? To breathe it for more than an hour, at the most, would be out of the question; and even if the time could be stretched to an hour and a quarter, the consequences might be most disastrous. The thought of this dilemma troubled me greatly; and it will hardly be believed that, after all the dangers I had already faced, I should regard this matter with such seriousness as to abandon my ultimate plan and, at last, decide that I must descend. But this wavering lasted only a moment.”"}

