          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5525b360aedca14dcda4c2cf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cDo you hear me? I know the language of despair. I\u2019ve memorized it all, because I am Despair\u2014strange, joyous, triumphant Despair. But those words are lies, because the wave may be dark, yet it isn\u2019t bitter. We lie down and close our eyes with a gentle good night, and when we wake, we are free. So come\u2014no more delay, you slow one! Look at the pleasant potion. See, I\u2019m a spirit of good, not a human maid who invites you, and with winning words\u2014oh, if only they could win you!\u2014says, Come and drink.\u201d\\n\\nAs I spoke, I kept my eyes fixed on his face. His exquisite beauty, the heavenly compassion shining from his eyes, and his gentle but earnest look of reproach and wonder even before he answered all changed something in me: my high-strung feelings eased, the hard edge of despair fell away, and I was left only with the softest grief. I saw that his eyes were wet as well when he took both my hands. Then, sitting down near me, he said:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a sad deed you would lead me into, dearest friend, and your sorrow must be truly deep to fill you with these unhappy thoughts. You long for death, yet you fear it, and you want me to be your companion. But I have less courage than you, and even with you beside me I still cannot dare to die. Listen to me, and then consider whether you should win me over to your plan\u2014especially if, with the overbearing eloquence of despair, you could make black death so inviting that fair heaven itself would seem like darkness. Listen, I beg you, to the words of someone who has nurtured desperate thoughts and longed with impatient desire for death, but who has finally trampled the phantom underfoot and crushed its sting. Come\u2014since you have played Despair with me, I will play the part of Una with you and bring you out unharmed from his dark cavern. Listen to me, and let yourself be softened by words in which no selfish passion lingers.\\n\\n\u201cWe don\u2019t know what this whole wide world means\u2014its strange mix of good and evil. But we\u2019ve been placed here and told to live and hope. I don\u2019t know what we\u2019re meant to hope for, but there is some good beyond us that we must seek, and that is our earthly task. If misfortune comes against us, we must fight her; we must cast her aside and still go on, to find out what it is our nature to desire. Whether this promise of future good is preparation for another existence, I can\u2019t say; or whether it is simply that, as workers in God\u2019s vineyard, we must lend a hand to smooth the way for those who come after us.\"}"
