          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8d288513aadf91c4f75e58f3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mary never wrote to Bath herself; the whole burden of maintaining that slow, disappointing correspondence with Elizabeth fell to Anne.\\n\\nBreakfast had scarcely ended when Captain and Mrs Harville and Captain Benwick joined them. They had agreed to take their last walk about Lyme together, and they ought to be starting for Uppercross by one; in the meantime they were to remain as long as they could out of doors, all in company.\\n\\nAnne noticed Captain Benwick drawing close to her as soon as they were properly in the street. What they had talked of the previous evening did not seem to discourage him from seeking her again; and they walked together for some time, speaking as before of Mr Scott and Lord Byron, still as unable as ever\u2014like any other two readers\u2014to judge the merits of either in the same way, until something occurred that nearly changed the whole current of their party. Captain Harville then took Captain Benwick\u2019s place beside her.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Elliot,\u201d said he, in a lower voice, \u201cyou have done a good deed in getting that poor fellow to talk so much. I only wish he had such company more often. I know it is hard on him to be shut up as he is; but what are we to do? We cannot separate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI can well believe that would be impossible; yet in time\u2014perhaps. We know what time does in every case of affliction. And you must remember, Captain Harville, that your friend may yet be called a young mourner; only last summer, I understand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAy, that\u2019s true enough,\u201d (with a deep sigh) \u201conly June.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd not known to him, perhaps, so soon.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot till the first week of August. He came home from the Cape, just made into the Grappler. I was at Plymouth, fearing to hear of him. He wrote letters, but the Grappler was under orders for Portsmouth. There the news had to follow him, but who was to tell it? Not I. I would as soon have been hauled up to the yard-arm. Nobody could do it, but that good fellow\u201d (pointing to Captain Wentworth). \u201cThe Laconia had come into Plymouth the week before; there was no risk of her being sent to sea again. He had his chance for the rest, wrote for leave of absence, but without waiting for the return, travelled night and day till he reached Portsmouth, rowed off to the Grappler at once, and never left the poor fellow for a week. That\u2019s what he did, and nobody else could have saved poor James. You may judge, Miss Elliot, whether he is dear to us!\u201d\\n\\nAnne did consider the matter with complete certainty, and said as much as her own feelings could express, or as he seemed able to bear; but he was too much moved to take up the subject again. When he spoke once more, it was of something entirely different.\u201d}\"}"
