          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c9a8d3e129546e0a472f0f6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It is a strange thing, but everyone who disappears is said to be seen in San Francisco. It must be a delightful city, with all the attractions of the next world.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you think has happened to Basil?\u201d Dorian asked, holding up his Burgundy to the light, and wondering how he could possibly talk about it so calmly.\\n\\n\u201cI have no idea at all. If Basil wants to hide himself, it\u2019s not my business. If he\u2019s dead, I don\u2019t want to think about him. Death is the only thing that ever terrifies me. I hate it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy?\u201d the younger man asked wearily.\\n\\n\u201cBecause,\u201d said Lord Henry, moving the gilt trellis of an open vinaigrette box past his nostrils, \u201cthese days you can survive everything except that. Death and vulgarity are the only two facts in the nineteenth century that you can\u2019t talk your way out of. Let\u2019s have our coffee in the music room, Dorian. You must play Chopin for me. The man my wife ran away with played Chopin beautifully. Poor Victoria! I was very fond of her. The house is rather lonely without her. Of course, married life is just a habit\u2014a bad one. But you still miss even your worst habits. Maybe you miss them the most. They\u2019re such an essential part of who you are.\u201d\\n\\nDorian didn\u2019t answer. He got up from the table, went into the next room, and sat down at the piano, letting his fingers drift over the white and black keys. When the coffee was brought in, he stopped and looked over at Lord Henry. \u201cHarry,\u201d he said, \u201cdid it ever occur to you that Basil was murdered?\u201d\\n\\nLord Henry yawned. \u201cBasil was well liked, and he always wore a Waterbury watch. Why would he be murdered? He wasn\u2019t clever enough to make enemies. Of course, he had a wonderful gift for painting. But a man can paint like Velasquez and still be as dull as possible. Basil was really rather dull. He only interested me once\u2014years ago\u2014when he told me he had a wild adoration for you, and that you were the driving force of his art.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI was very fond of Basil,\u201d Dorian said, with sadness in his voice. \u201cBut don\u2019t people say he was murdered?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, some of the papers do. It doesn\u2019t seem at all likely to me. I know there are dreadful places in Paris, but Basil wasn\u2019t the kind of man who would go there. He had no curiosity. That was his main flaw.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat would you say, Harry,\u201d the younger man asked, \u201cif I told you that I had murdered Basil?\u201d\"}"
