          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8fb421646c7a7fc641501e84\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The storm battered on fiercely through the night, yet nothing especially worth remarking took place. In the morning, though, when they came down for breakfast, the dreadful smear of blood was there on the floor again. \u201cI can\u2019t believe it\u2019s the Paragon Detergent\u2019s doing,\u201d Washington said; \u201cI\u2019ve tested it with everything.\u201d So he scrubbed the mark out a second time, but on the following morning it reappeared. The third morning it was still present as well; for the library had been shut up at night by Mr. Otis himself, and the key had been carried upstairs. The entire household was now thoroughly stirred. Mr. Otis began to think he had been too certain in his denial of ghosts; Mrs. Otis declared she meant to join the Psychical Society; and Washington set about writing a long letter to Messrs. Myers and Podmore on the Permanence of Sanguineous Stains when linked with Crime. That night, all lingering doubts about the real, external existence of phantasmata were done with forever.\\n\\nThe day turned out warm and bright, and in the cool of the evening the whole family went out for a drive. They did not get back until nine o\u2019clock, when they had a light supper. The talk never once drifted toward ghosts, so there weren\u2019t even those first conditions of eager expectation that so often come before psychical happenings. As I learned afterward from Mr. Otis, the matters discussed were simply the sort of everyday topics that well-bred Americans of the better sort talk over\u2014Miss Fanny Davenport\u2019s immense superiority to Sarah Bernhardt as an actress; how hard it was to get green corn, buckwheat cakes, and hominy, even in the best English homes; the importance of Boston in the shaping of the world-soul; the benefits of the baggage-check system for railway travel; and how much sweeter the New York accent is than the London drawl. Not a word was said of anything supernatural, and Sir Simon de Canterville was not mentioned in any way. At eleven the family went to bed, and by half-past all the lights were out. Some time later, Mr. Otis was roused by a peculiar noise in the corridor outside his room. It sounded like the clank of metal and seemed to be drawing nearer by the moment. He rose at once, struck a match, and checked the time. It was exactly one o\u2019clock. He remained perfectly composed and even felt his pulse, which was in no way feverish. The odd sound went on, and now he could hear footsteps plainly. He slipped on his slippers, took a small oblong phial from his dressing-case, and opened the door.\"}"
