          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7d8b5022334529f3f2a633e2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To look at a star by quick glances\u2014to view it from the side, by turning toward it the outer portions of the retina (which are more susceptible to faint impressions of light than the inner parts)\u2014is to see the star distinctly: it is to get the best sense of its brilliance, a brilliance that grows dim in direct proportion as we turn our vision fully upon it. In the latter case, more rays actually fall on the eye, but in the former there is a more refined capacity for understanding. By going too deep, we confuse and weaken thought; and it is even possible to make Venus herself disappear from the sky by a scrutiny that is too sustained, too concentrated, or too direct.\\n\\n\u201cAs for these murders, let\u2019s go over the facts ourselves before we form an opinion. An inquiry will be entertaining,\u201d [I thought it was an odd word for what he meant, but I said nothing] \u201cand besides, Le Bon once did me a service for which I\u2019m not ungrateful. We\u2019ll go and see the premises with our own eyes. I know G\u2014\u2014, the Prefect of Police, and I won\u2019t have trouble getting the necessary permission.\u201d\\n\\nThe permission was obtained, and we went at once to the Rue Morgue. It was one of those miserable thoroughfares that run between the Rue Richelieu and the Rue St. Roch. We reached it late in the afternoon, since this part of town was far from where we lived. The house was easy to find, because many people were still standing across the street, staring up at the closed shutters with aimless curiosity. It was an ordinary Parisian house, with a gateway; on one side there was a glazed watch-box, with a sliding panel in the window, indicating a loge de concierge. Before going in, we walked up the street, turned down an alley, and then, turning again, passed behind the building\u2014while Dupin, in the meantime, examined the whole neighborhood as well as the house, with a level of careful attention I couldn\u2019t see any possible purpose for.\\n\\nWalking back, we came again to the front of the dwelling, rang the bell, and, after showing our credentials, were admitted by the agents on duty. We went upstairs\u2014into the chamber where the body of Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye had been found, and where both of the deceased still lay. The room\u2019s disorder had, as usual, been allowed to remain. I saw nothing beyond what had already been stated in the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux.\u201d Dupin scrutinized everything\u2014not excluding the bodies of the victims. We then went into the other rooms and into the yard, with a gendarme accompanying us the whole time.\"}"
