 luck.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Stryver laughed until his early, overgrown belly shook.\\n\\n\u201cYou and your luck, Sydney! Get to work, get to work.\u201d\\n\\nSullenly, the jackal loosened his clothes, went into the next room, and came back with a large jug of cold water, a basin, and a couple of towels. He soaked the towels in the water, wrung them out a bit, folded them on his head in a way that was hard to look at, sat down at the table, and said, \u201cNow I\u2019m ready!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot much boiling down to do tonight, Memory,\u201d said Mr. Stryver cheerfully, as he looked through his papers.\\n\\n\u201cHow much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly two sets.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGive me the worst first.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere they are, Sydney. Fire away!\u201d\\n\\nThen the lion lay back on a sofa on one side of the drinking table, while the jackal sat at his own table, covered with papers, on the other side, with the bottles and glasses right within reach.\"}"
