          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"35c9cfc27ff46fdfaa66401f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But I have to warn you in advance that I\u2019m going to tell Sybil everything. She\u2019s coming to lunch with me tomorrow, to talk about bonnets, and if Mr. Podgers finds out that you have a bad temper, or a tendency to gout, or a wife living in Bayswater, I will certainly let her know all about it.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur smiled and shook his head. \u201cI\u2019m not afraid,\u201d he answered. \u201cSybil knows me as well as I know her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! I\u2019m a little sorry to hear you say that. The right foundation for marriage is a mutual misunderstanding. No, I\u2019m not cynical at all\u2014I\u2019ve just had experience, which, as it happens, is very much the same thing. Mr. Podgers, Lord Arthur Savile is dying to have his hand read. Don\u2019t tell him that he\u2019s engaged to one of the most beautiful girls in London, because that appeared in the _Morning Post_ a month ago.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Lady Windermere,\u201d cried the Marchioness of Jedburgh, \u201cdo let Mr. Podgers stay here a little longer. He has just told me I should go on the stage, and I\u2019m so interested.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf he\u2019s told you that, Lady Jedburgh, I\u2019ll certainly take him away. Come over at once, Mr. Podgers, and read Lord Arthur\u2019s hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell,\u201d said Lady Jedburgh, making a little moue as she rose from the sofa, \u201cif I\u2019m not allowed to go on the stage, then I must at least be allowed to be part of the audience.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course; we\u2019re all going to be part of the audience,\u201d said Lady Windermere. \u201cAnd now, Mr. Podgers, be sure to tell us something nice. Lord Arthur is one of my special favorites.\u201d\\n\\nBut when Mr. Podgers saw Lord Arthur\u2019s hand, he turned curiously pale and said nothing. A shudder seemed to run through him, and his great bushy eyebrows twitched convulsively, in that odd, irritating way they 