          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f16662189ad8cd12a18667ce\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Jane admitted she was a little surprised by the match, but she spoke less about her astonishment than about her sincere desire for their happiness; nor could Elizabeth get her to treat it as unlikely. Kitty and Lydia were far from envying Miss Lucas, since Mr. Collins was only a clergyman, and it affected them in no other way than as news to spread around Meryton.\\n\\nLady Lucas couldn\u2019t help feeling triumphant at being able to answer Mrs. Bennet with the comfort of having a daughter who was well married, and she visited Longbourn more often than usual to say how happy she was, even though Mrs. Bennet\u2019s sour looks and mean remarks might have been enough to drive happiness away.\\n\\nBetween Elizabeth and Charlotte there was a restraint that kept them both silent on the subject, and Elizabeth felt convinced that no real trust could ever exist between them again. Her disappointment in Charlotte made her turn with even greater affection to her sister, whose integrity and delicacy she was sure could never be shaken; and she grew daily more anxious for Charlotte\u2019s happiness, as Bingley had now been gone a week and nothing was heard of his return.\\n\\nJane had sent Caroline an early reply to her letter, and she was counting the days until she could reasonably hope to hear again. The promised letter of thanks from Mr. Collins arrived on Tuesday, addressed to their father, and written with all the solemn gratitude that a year spent in the family might have produced. After satisfying his conscience on that point, he went on, with many rapturous expressions, to tell them how happy he was to have won the affection of their amiable neighbor, Miss Lucas. He then explained that it was only with the intention of enjoying her company that he had been so ready to agree to their kind wish that he return to Longbourn, where he hoped to be able to come back on Monday fortnight. For Lady Catherine, he added, approved of his marriage so wholeheartedly that she wanted it to happen as soon as possible, which he trusted would be an unanswerable argument with his amiable Charlotte to set an early date and make him the happiest of men.\\n\\nMr. Collins\u2019s return to Hertfordshire was no longer a matter of pleasure for Mrs. Bennet. On the contrary, she was just as inclined to complain about it as her husband. It was very strange that he should come to Longbourn instead of to Lucas Lodge; it was also very inconvenient and extremely troublesome. She hated having visitors in the house while her health was so uncertain, and lovers were, of all people, the most disagreeable.\"}"
