          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"52ccfc636b5eee03e47a8b05\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The group was split up and settled on two adjoining benches: Anne was among those seated in the front, and Mr. Elliot had managed so well\u2014helped by his friend Colonel Wallis\u2014that he ended up with a seat beside her. Miss Elliot, surrounded by her cousins and the main focus of Colonel Wallis\u2019s gallantry, was perfectly content.\\n\\nAnne\u2019s mind was in an excellent state for the evening\u2019s entertainment; it was just the right kind of occupation. She had feelings for the tender, spirits for the lively, attention for the scientific, and patience for the tedious\u2014and she had never enjoyed a concert more, at least during the first act. Near the end of it, during the interval after an Italian song, she explained the words of the song to Mr. Elliot. They had a concert program between them.\\n\\n\u201cThis,\u201d she said, \u201cis nearly the sense\u2014or rather the meaning\u2014of the words. Of course, you can\u2019t really talk about the sense of an Italian love-song, but it\u2019s as close to the meaning as I can give; I don\u2019t pretend to understand the language. I\u2019m a very poor Italian scholar.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, I see you are. I see you know nothing about it. You only have enough knowledge of the language to translate, at first glance, these inverted, transposed, shortened Italian lines into clear, understandable, elegant English. You don\u2019t need to say anything more about your ignorance. Here\u2019s complete proof.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI won\u2019t argue with such kind politeness; but I\u2019d be sorry to be examined by a true expert.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI haven\u2019t had the pleasure of visiting Camden Place for so long,\u201d he replied, \u201cwithout learning something about Miss Anne Elliot. And I do consider her someone too modest for the world in general to realize half her accomplishments, and too highly accomplished for modesty to come naturally in any other woman.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFor shame! For shame! This is far too much flattery. I forget what we\u2019re supposed to have next,\u201d she said, turning back to the program.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps,\u201d Mr. Elliot said quietly, \u201cI\u2019ve had a longer acquaintance with your character than you realize.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed! How so? You could only have known it since I came to Bath\u2014unless you might have heard me spoken of earlier in my own family.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI knew you by report long before you came to Bath. I\u2019d heard you described by people who knew you intimately. I\u2019ve been acquainted with you by character for many years. Your person, your disposition, your accomplishments, your manner\u2014they were all present to me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Elliot was not disappointed in the interest he hoped to stir. No one can resist the charm of such a mystery.\"}"
