{"request_id":"dbd720308a1e458f22b65e90","rewritten_text":"It was after two o’clock when, by a sudden turn, I found myself near the lake by a small cove, where a little skiff was tied up. It was close enough to our house that I could see my father and my aunt walking on the lawn. I sprang into the boat; being well used to such things, I shoved off from the shore and threw all my strength into rowing fast across. As I drew near, dressed in white and covered only by my tartan rachan, my hair streaming over my shoulders, and flying forward with a speed that seemed to outpace even my own boat, my father had often told me that I looked less like a human maid and more like a spirit. I came up to the bank; my father took hold of the boat, I leapt out lightly, and in an instant I was in his arms.

And from that moment I began to live. Everything around me had shifted from a dull sameness into the brightest picture of joy and delight. The happiness I felt in my father’s company went far beyond anything I had dared to expect. We were together without end, and the topics of our talk never ran out. He had spent sixteen years away among peoples scarcely known in Europe; he had roamed through Persia, Arabia, and the north of India, and had moved among the natives’ homes with a liberty granted to very few Europeans. His accounts of their ways, his stories, and his descriptions of scenery carried us through delicious hours whenever we grew weary of speaking of our own plans for the future life.

The language of affection was so new to me that I clung to every word with delight when he told me what he had felt about me during those long years of seeming forgetfulness. “At first,” said he, “I could not bear the thought of my poor little girl; but later, when grief had worn off and hope came back to me, I could only turn to her, and in cities and deserts alike her little fairy shape—just as I had pictured it—kept flitting before me forever. The northern breeze, when it refreshed me, seemed sweeter and more gentle, as though it carried some part of your spirit along with it. I often imagined that I would return at once and take you with me to some fertile island, where we might live in peace forever. But when I came back, my ardent hopes were crushed by so many fears; my impatience became, in the highest degree, painful. I dared not think that the sun might shine and the moon rise over anything but your living form, not your grave. Yet no—it is not so. I have my Mathilda, my consolation, and my hope.”—"}

