{"request_id":"d219be0e17439af74ea3ac28","rewritten_text":"I bored a hole in the end of the keg over the pitcher, then fitted in a plug of soft wood cut into a tapered, conical shape. I pushed the plug in or pulled it out, depending on what was needed, until—after a few experiments—it reached the exact degree of tightness where the water, oozing from the hole and falling into the pitcher below, would fill the pitcher to the brim within sixty minutes. Of course, this was something I could check briefly and easily by watching how much of the pitcher filled in any given time. Once I had arranged all of that, the rest of the plan was obvious. I set up my bed on the car floor so that, when I lay down, my head would be directly under the mouth of the pitcher. It was clear that, after an hour, as the pitcher filled, it would overflow—and overflow at the mouth, which sat a little lower than the rim. It was also clear that water falling from a height of more than four feet could only land on my face, and that the certain result would be to wake me up instantly, even from the soundest sleep in the world.

“It was fully eleven by the time I finished these arrangements, and I went straight to bed, with complete confidence in the effectiveness of my invention. I wasn’t disappointed. Every sixty minutes, precisely, my trusty chronometer woke me; then, after I had emptied the pitcher into the bung-hole of the keg and carried out the condenser’s duties, I went back to bed. These regular interruptions to my sleep caused me even less discomfort than I’d expected. When I finally got up for the day, it was seven o’clock, and the sun had risen many degrees above the line of my horizon.

“April 3d. I found the balloon at an immense height, and the earth’s apparent curve increased in a noticeable way. Below me in the ocean lay a cluster of black specks, which were undoubtedly islands. Far to the northward, I could see a thin, white, and extremely brilliant line—or streak—along the edge of the horizon, and I had no hesitation in assuming it was the southern disk of the ice of the Polar Sea. My curiosity was strongly stirred, because I hoped to travel much farther north and might, at some point, find myself directly above the Pole itself. I now regretted that my great elevation would, in that case, prevent me from taking as accurate a survey as I wanted.”"}

