          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2a6c0855aa425517c2c1d762\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But there was no laughter in the little town that same calm Saturday afternoon. The Harpers, along with Aunt Polly\u2019s family, were being put into mourning, with deep grief and many tears. An unusual quiet settled over the village, though it was usually quiet enough, if you\u2019re being honest. The villagers went about their business with a distant, absent look, and talked very little; but they sighed often. The Saturday holiday felt like a burden to the children. They had no heart for their games, and gradually gave them up.\\n\\nIn the afternoon, Becky Thatcher found herself hanging around the empty schoolhouse yard, feeling very down. But she found nothing there to comfort her. She talked to herself:\\n\\n\u201cOh, if I only had that brass andiron-knob again! But I don\u2019t have anything now to remember him by.\u201d And she choked back a small sob.\\n\\nAfter a moment, she stopped and said to herself:\\n\\n\u201cIt was right here. Oh, if I could do it over again, I wouldn\u2019t say that\u2014I wouldn\u2019t say it for the whole world. But he\u2019s gone now; I\u2019ll never, never, never see him again.\u201d\\n\\nThat thought broke her, and she wandered off with tears running down her cheeks. Then a whole group of boys and girls\u2014Tom and Joe\u2019s playmates\u2014came by and stood looking over the paling fence, speaking in reverent tones about how Tom had done this or that the last time they saw him, and how Joe had said this and that small detail (full of awful meaning, as they could see now!). Each person pointed out the exact spot where the lost boys had been standing at the time, and then added something like, \u201cand I was standing just so\u2014just like I am now, and as if you were him\u2014I was as close as that\u2014and he smiled, just this way\u2014and then something seemed to go all over me, like\u2014awful, you know\u2014and I didn\u2019t think what it meant, of course, but I can see now!\u201d\\n\\nThen there was an argument about who had seen the dead boys last in life, and many claimed that grim distinction, offering evidence that was more or less altered by the witness. And when it was finally decided who _did_ see the departed last and exchanged the last words with them, the lucky ones took on a kind of sacred importance, and everyone else stared at them and envied them. One poor fellow, who had no other kind of glory to offer, said with fairly obvious pride in the memory:\\n\\n\u201cWell, Tom Sawyer he beat me once.\u201d\\n\\nBut that attempt at glory didn\u2019t work. Most of the boys could say the same thing, and that made the distinction too cheap. The group drifted away again, still lingering on memories of the lost heroes, speaking in hushed, awed voices.\"}"
