          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"efa05b0f101105765dbd3d79\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The two ladies were overjoyed to meet their dear friend again, and, after calling it quite a long while since they had last seen her, they kept asking what she had been doing with herself during their separation. The rest of the family interested them very little; they tried to avoid Mrs. Bennet as much as possible, said little to Elizabeth, and nothing at all to the others. Before long they were off again, rising from their seats with a briskness that startled their brother, and hurrying away as though they were eager to get out of Mrs. Bennet\u2019s civil attentions.\\n\\nThe Netherfield ball promised pleasure to every young woman in the family. Mrs. Bennet chose to treat it as a compliment paid to her eldest daughter, and felt especially pleased to receive the invitation from Mr. Bingley himself, rather than from some formal card. Jane could already imagine a delightful evening with her two friends, along with the attentions of their brother; and Elizabeth, with real satisfaction, looked forward to dancing a great deal with Mr. Wickham, and to seeing in Mr. Darcy\u2019s looks and conduct a further confirmation of everything she had observed. Catherine and Lydia\u2019s expected happiness depended less on any one happening or any particular person; for though each of them, like Elizabeth, meant to spend half the evening with Mr. Wickham, he was by no means the only partner who could please them, and, after all, a ball was a ball. Even Mary, she could assure her family, had no reluctance about it.\\n\\n\u201cWhile I may have my mornings to myself,\u201d said she, \u201cthat is quite enough. I do not think it any great sacrifice to join now and then in evening engagements. Society has claims upon us all; and I am among those who believe intervals of rest and amusement are desirable for everyone.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth\u2019s spirits were so high that, though she did not often speak unnecessarily to Mr. Collins, she could not help asking whether he meant to accept Mr. Bingley\u2019s invitation, and, if he did, whether he would consider it proper to take part in the evening\u2019s amusements. She was somewhat astonished to find that he had not the slightest hesitation on the matter, and was far from fearing any rebuke\u2014whether from the Archbishop or Lady Catherine de Bourgh\u2014when he ventured to dance.\\n\\n\u201cI am by no means of opinion,\u201d said he, \u201cthat a ball of this sort, given by a young man of character to respectable people, can have any evil tendency; and I am so far from objecting to dancing, that I shall hope to be honoured with the hands of all my fair cousins during the course of the evening. I take this opportunity, therefore, of requesting yours, Miss Elizabeth, for the first two dances especially; a preference which I trust my cousin Jane will understand to arise from the right motive, and not from any want of respect for her.\u201d\"}"
