          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f9829ffe20dc1015e9824598\",\"rewritten_text\":\"So the ring-master made a little speech. He said he hoped there wouldn\u2019t be any disturbance, and that if the man promised he wouldn\u2019t cause any more trouble, the ring-master would let him ride, as long as he thought he could stay on the horse. Everyone laughed and said, \u201cAll right,\u201d and the man got on. The second he was on, the horse started ripping and tearing, jumping and running around, with two circus men gripping the bridle and trying to hold him. The drunk man hung onto his neck, his heels flying into the air with every jump. The whole crowd stood up, shouting and laughing until tears rolled down their faces. \\n\\nAt last, sure enough, no matter what the circus men did, the horse broke free. Off he went, running around the ring like crazy, while the drunk man lay on him and clung to his neck. First one leg dangled closer to the ground on one side, and then the other leg on the other side. The people were wild with excitement. It wasn\u2019t funny to me, though. I was shaking with fear for his safety. But pretty soon he struggled up into the saddle, grabbed the bridle, and wobbled this way and that. The next minute he sprang up, dropped the bridle, and stood there. The horse kept going just as fast, like it was on fire. He stood up there, moving around with the ease and comfort of someone who had never been drunk in his life. Then he started pulling off his clothes and throwing them aside. He shed them so quickly and so many that it seemed to clog the air, and in all he got rid of seventeen suits. \\n\\nThen he was slim and handsome, dressed in the gaudiest, prettiest outfit you could imagine. He took up his whip and went to work on that horse, making him perform so well that he finally skipped off. Then the man left the ring, made his bow, and danced off to the dressing room. Everyone was howling with pleasure and astonishment.\\n\\nThen the ring-master realized he had been fooled, and he was the sickest ring-master you ever saw, I reckon. It turned out it was one of his own men. The joke had been planned by him, right from the start, and he never let on to anyone. I felt pretty foolish for falling for it, but I wouldn\u2019t have wanted to be in that ring-master\u2019s place, not for a thousand dollars. I don\u2019t know\u2014maybe there were bigger, better circuses than that one, but I haven\u2019t found any yet. Anyway, it was good enough for me. And wherever I run across it, it can have all of my custom every time.\"}"
