          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a4c76fb4b0e29c97664c4686\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I walked along with a brisk step, and we soon reached my college. Then, as I reflected, the idea struck me with such force that I shivered: the creature I had left in my apartment might still be there\u2014alive, moving about. I dreaded the thought of seeing this monster, yet I feared even more that Henry might catch sight of him. So I begged him to wait a few minutes at the foot of the stairs, and I hurried up to my own room. My hand was already on the door\u2019s lock before I managed to check myself. I stopped; a cold trembling ran through me. I flung the door open with sudden violence, much as children do when they expect a spectre to be waiting on the other side, but nothing showed itself. I stepped in cautiously: the apartment was empty, and even my bedroom had been cleared of its hideous lodger. I could scarcely credit that such a great good fortune had fallen to me; yet once I was certain my enemy had truly gone, I clapped for joy and hurried down to Clerval.\\n\\nWe went up into my room, and the servant soon brought breakfast, but I could not steady myself. It was not mere happiness that seized me; I felt my skin prickle with an over-sensitiveness, and my pulse hammered quickly. I could not stay in one spot for a moment\u2014I sprang over the chairs, clapped my hands, and burst into loud laughter. At first Clerval took my unusual spirits for nothing more than joy at my arrival; but when he watched me more closely, he saw a wildness in my eyes he could not explain. My loud, unrestrained, heartless laughter both frightened and astonished him.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\u201d he cried, \u201cfor God\u2019s sake, what is it? Don\u2019t laugh like that. You can\u2019t be well\u2014what is the reason for all this?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t ask me,\u201d I exclaimed, covering my eyes with my hands, for I thought I saw the dreaded spectre glide into the room. \u201cHe can tell. Oh, save me! Save me!\u201d I believed the monster had seized me; I fought wildly and then fell into a fit.\\n\\nPoor Clerval\u2014what must his feelings have been? A meeting he had looked forward to with such delight had turned, so strangely, to bitterness. But I was not there to witness his grief, for I was lifeless, and I did not recover my senses for a long, long time.\\n\\nThis was the beginning of a nervous fever that kept me confined for several months. During all that time Henry was my only nurse. I later learned that, knowing my father\u2019s advanced age and his inability to endure so long a journey, and considering how miserable my illness would make Elizabeth, he spared them this sorrow by hiding how severe my disorder really was.\"}"
