          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e94808c8bfb80cdee2b4c882\",\"rewritten_text\":\"What outcome, then, has followed? What sort of impression have I left on your mind?\u201d\\n\\nAs Dupin put the question to me, I felt my skin crawl. \u201cA madman,\u201d I answered, \u201cmust have done it\u2014some raving lunatic, escaped from a nearby _Maison de Sant\u00e9._\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn a few ways,\u201d he returned, \u201cyour notion isn\u2019t without weight. Still, the voices of the insane, even at their wildest moments, never match that particular voice heard on the stairs. Madmen belong to a nation; and though their speech may be incoherent in words, it always keeps the coherence of syllables. Besides, the hair of a madman is not the sort I\u2019m holding in my hand right now. I\u2019ve separated this small tuft from the rigidly clenched fingers of Madame L\u2019Espanaye. Tell me what you make of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDupin!\u201d I said, thoroughly shaken. \u201cThis hair is extraordinary\u2014there\u2019s nothing _human_ about it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI haven\u2019t claimed that it is,\u201d he said. \u201cBut before we settle the point, I want you to look at the little sketch I\u2019ve traced here on this paper. It\u2019s a _fac-simile_ of what, in one part of the testimony, is described as \u2018dark bruises, and deep indentations of finger nails,\u2019 upon the throat of Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye, and, in another account (by Messrs. Dumas and Etienne), as a \u2018series of livid spots, evidently the impression of fingers.\u2019\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ll see,\u201d my friend went on, laying the sheet out on the table between us, \u201cthat the drawing conveys a firm, fixed hold. There\u2019s no sign of any _slipping_. Each finger has kept\u2014perhaps right up to the victim\u2019s death\u2014the dreadful grip with which it first sank in. Now try it: place all your fingers at once into the corresponding impressions you can see.\u201d\\n\\nI tried, but in vain.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps we aren\u2019t testing it properly,\u201d he said. \u201cThe paper lies on a flat surface; yet the human throat is cylindrical. Here\u2019s a piece of wood whose circumference is about the same as the throat. Wrap the drawing around it, then attempt the experiment again.\u201d\\n\\nI did so, but the trouble was even clearer than before. \u201cThis,\u201d I said, \u201cis no mark of a human hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow read,\u201d replied Dupin, \u201cthe passage from Cuvier.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a brief but minute anatomical and descriptive account of the large fulvous Ourang-Outang of the East Indian islands. Everyone knows, in general, the creature\u2019s enormous size, its prodigious strength and activity, its savage ferocity, and its imitative habits. I grasped at once the full horror of the murder.\\n\\n\u201cThe description of the digits,\u201d I said, finishing the reading, \u201cmatches this drawing exactly.\u201d\"}"
