          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"348b1b1160253a26eec9e8fe\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I am not dining at home, so I won\u2019t need you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, sir,\u201d the man said, and left the room.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Alan, don\u2019t waste a moment. This chest is so heavy! I\u2019ll carry it for you. You take the other things.\u201d He spoke fast and in a commanding way. Campbell felt pushed and controlled by him. They left the room together.\\n\\nWhen they reached the top landing, Dorian took out the key and put it in the lock. He turned it, then stopped. A worried look crossed his face. He shuddered. \u201cI don\u2019t think I can go in, Alan,\u201d he whispered.\\n\\n\u201cIt means nothing to me. I don\u2019t need you,\u201d Campbell said coldly.\\n\\nDorian opened the door halfway. As he did, he saw his portrait\u2019s face staring out in the sunlight, sneering. On the floor in front of it, the torn curtain lay in pieces. He remembered that the night before he had forgotten\u2014forgotten, for the first time in his life\u2014to hide the deadly painting. He was about to rush forward, when he pulled back again, shaking.\\n\\nWhat was that disgusting red liquid shining on one of the hands, wet and slick, as if the canvas had sweated blood? It was awful. For the moment, it felt even worse than the silent thing he knew was stretched across the table\u2014the thing whose twisted, misshapen shadow on the spotted carpet told him it hadn\u2019t moved. It was still there, exactly as he had left it.\\n\\nHe took a deep breath, opened the door a little wider, and walked in quickly with his eyes half-closed and his head turned away. He told himself he would not look at the dead man even once. Then, bending down, he grabbed the gold-and-purple cloth and threw it right over the picture.\\n\\nHe stopped there, afraid to turn around. His eyes stayed on the patterns in front of him. He heard Campbell bringing in the heavy chest, the irons, and the other things he needed for his terrible work. He started to wonder whether he and Basil Hallward had ever met, and, if they had, what they would have thought of each other.\\n\\n\u201cLeave me now,\u201d a stern voice said behind him.\\n\\nDorian turned and hurried out. He could only think that the dead man had been pushed back into the chair, and that Campbell was staring at a shiny yellow face. As he went downstairs, he heard the key turn in the lock.\\n\\nIt was long after seven when Campbell returned to the library. He looked pale, but completely calm. \u201cI\u2019ve done what you asked me to do,\u201d he muttered.\"}"
