          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"57858955f6be185ec9fe2ee4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Morning had arrived, and I set the Manuscript aside. The rain had nearly stopped; everything lay gray and heavy, while the spent storm, worn out, kept sighing and sobbing as it tried to settle into rest. I went to the stranger\u2019s room and stood listening at his door, which was left a little open. I could hear his voice, so I knocked. No one answered, yet the voice kept going. I leaned in and peeped. The man lay on his back in bed, speaking in broken bursts but with energy, and he kept punctuating his words with his arms, which he flung about restlessly, like sick men do when delirium takes them. I slipped in quietly and bent over him. His mutterings and outbursts continued. I said only a single word, just to draw his attention. In an instant his glassy eyes and ashen face brightened with pleasure, gratitude, joy, and welcome.\\n\\n\u201cOh, Sandy, you\u2019ve come at last\u2014how I\u2019ve been longing for you! Sit by me\u2014don\u2019t go\u2014never leave me again, Sandy, never again. Where is your hand?\u2014give it here, my dear, let me hold it\u2014there\u2014now everything is right, all is peace, and I\u2019m happy again\u2014_we_ are happy again, aren\u2019t we, Sandy? You look so dim, so shadowy; you\u2019re only a mist, a cloud, yet you\u2019re _here_, and that alone is blessedness. And I have your hand\u2014don\u2019t take it away; it\u2019s only for a little while\u2014I shan\u2019t need it long.... Was that the child?... Hello-Central!... she doesn\u2019t answer. Maybe she\u2019s asleep? Bring her when she wakes, and let me touch her hands, her face, her hair, and tell her good-bye.... Sandy! Yes, you\u2019re there. I drifted for a moment, and I thought you were gone.... How long have I been ill? It must be\u2014months, it feels like. And those dreams! Such strange, dreadful dreams, Sandy! Dreams that seemed as real as life\u2014of course delirium, but _so_ real! I truly thought the king was dead; I thought you were in Gaul and couldn\u2019t manage to get home; I thought there\u2019d been a revolution. In the wild frenzy of it all, I believed Clarence and I, with a small crew of my cadets, fought and wiped out the whole chivalry of England! Yet that wasn\u2019t even the strangest part. I felt like some creature from a far-off age not yet born\u2014centuries ahead\u2014and even that was as real as the rest. Yes, it seemed I\u2019d flown back from that time into this one, and then forward again, and I was left here\u2014alone, a stranger, and forlorn in that strange England\u2014while an abyss of thirteen centuries yawned between me and you; between me and my home and my friends; between me and everything that\u2019s dear to me, everything that could make life worth living.\u201d\"}"
