          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c48cec22662ccee11e872fc2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She had left everything behind her\u2014save only the memory that such things had ever existed.\\n\\nAnne had not gone into Kellynch at all since she left Lady Russell\u2019s house in September. There had been no need for it; and whenever it had been possible for her to visit the Hall, she had contrived to slip away from the chance and avoid it. Her first return was simply to take up again her place in the Lodge\u2019s modern, elegant apartments, and to delight the mistress\u2019s eyes.\\n\\nLady Russell\u2019s joy in seeing her was tempered with some anxiety. She knew who had been coming to Uppercross. Yet, happily, either Anne had improved in fulness and appearance, or Lady Russell chose to believe she had; and when Anne received her compliments on the occasion, she found it amusing to connect them, in her own mind, with her cousin\u2019s silent admiration, while hoping she might be granted a second season of youth and beauty.\\n\\nAs soon as they began to talk, Anne perceived a change in her thoughts. The matters that had filled her heart when she left Kellynch\u2014those she had felt slighted in, and had been obliged to suppress among the Musgroves\u2014had now fallen into the background. Lately she had even lost sight of her father and sister and Bath. Uppercross had swallowed up all those concerns; and when Lady Russell returned to their earlier hopes and fears, speaking of her satisfaction in the house in Camden Place, which had been taken, and of her regret that Mrs Clay was still with them, Anne would have been ashamed to have it known how much more she was thinking of Lyme and Louisa Musgrove, and of everyone she knew there. The home and friendship of the Harvilles and Captain Benwick were far more engaging to her than her father\u2019s house in Camden Place, or her sister\u2019s closeness with Mrs Clay. In fact, she had to force herself to show something like equal care for Lady Russell on subjects that, by their very nature, ought first to claim her attention.\\n\\nAt the beginning there was a slight awkwardness in their conversation on another topic. They must speak of the accident at Lyme. Lady Russell had not been there five minutes the day before, when a complete account of the whole affair had already been poured into her; still, it had to be discussed\u2014she must ask questions, she must express regret for the imprudence, lament the outcome, and both must mention Captain Wentworth\u2019s name. Anne felt she did not manage it as well as Lady Russell. She could not bring herself to say the name and then meet Lady Russell\u2019s eye directly, until she had adopted the expedient of telling her, in a few words, what she thought of the attachment between him and Louisa.\"}"
