          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b5791bfb86af5968f45ecf60\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNext, we built a wire fence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWire fence?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. You suggested it yourself, two or three years ago.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I remember. The time the Church tried to fight us the first time, and then decided to wait for a better moment. So how did you set up the fence?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI start with twelve extremely strong wires\u2014bare, not insulated\u2014running from a big dynamo in the cave. The dynamo has no brushes except one positive and one negative.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, that\u2019s right.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe wires leave the cave and form a circle of level ground about a hundred yards across. They make twelve separate fences, ten feet apart\u2014that is, twelve circles inside circles\u2014and each wire end comes back into the cave.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cRight. Keep going.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fences are tied to heavy oak posts, spaced three feet apart, and each post is sunk five feet into the ground.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s good and strong.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. Outside the cave, the wires have no ground connection. They go out from the positive brush of the dynamo. There is a ground connection only through the negative brush. The other ends of the wires return to the cave, and each one is grounded on its own.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, no, that won\u2019t do!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s too expensive. It wastes power for no reason. You don\u2019t want any ground connection except the one through the negative brush. The other end of every wire has to be brought back into the cave and fastened separately, and without any ground connection. Now, look at the economy of it. A cavalry charge slams into the fence. You\u2019re using no power and spending no money, because there\u2019s only one ground connection until the horses hit the wire. The moment they touch it, they complete the circuit to the negative brush through the ground, and they drop dead. Don\u2019t you see? You\u2019re not using energy until it\u2019s actually needed. Your lightning is there and ready, like the load in a gun, but it doesn\u2019t cost you a cent until you fire it. Oh, yes\u2014the single ground connection\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course! I don\u2019t know how I missed that. It\u2019s not only cheaper, but it works better than the other way. If wires break or tangle, no harm is done.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, especially if we have a warning system in the cave and disconnect the broken wire. All right, then. The gatling guns?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. That\u2019s arranged too. In the center of the inner circle, on a platform six feet high, I\u2019ve grouped thirteen gatling guns and stocked plenty of ammunition.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s it. They\u2019ll cover every approach, and when the Church\u2019s knights arrive, there\u2019ll be music. The edge of the cliff above the cave\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve got a wire fence there, and a gatling gun.\u201d}\"}"
