          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5e3b4ba5484bac466ede5767\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don\u2019t want to get mixed up in anything like that, I tell myself.\\n\\nWhen I went downstairs in the morning, the parlor was shut up, and the watchers were gone. There wasn\u2019t anyone around except the family, Widow Bartley, and our people. I watched their faces to see if anything had happened, but I couldn\u2019t tell.\\n\\nAbout the middle of the day, the undertaker came with his man. They set the coffin in the middle of the room on a couple of chairs, then lined up all our chairs in rows. They borrowed more chairs from the neighbors until the hall, the parlor, and the dining room were full. I could see the coffin lid was the same way it had been before, but I didn\u2019t dare to look under it while people were there.\\n\\nThen people started coming in. The men and the girls took seats in the front row at the head of the coffin. For about half an hour, everyone moved around slowly in a single line, looking down at the dead man\u2019s face for a minute. Some people cried and dropped a tear. Everything was very still and solemn, except the girls and the men holding handkerchiefs to their eyes, keeping their heads bent, and sobbing a little. There wasn\u2019t any sound except feet scraping on the floor and people blowing their noses. At a funeral, people blow their noses more than they do anywhere else, except church.\\n\\nWhen the room was packed, the undertaker slid around in his black gloves, using his gentle, soothing way of doing things. He put the final touches on everything, made sure people and objects were arranged neatly, and made no more noise than a cat. He didn\u2019t talk. He just moved people around, squeezed in the late arrivals, opened up paths for people to get through, and did it all with nods and hand signals. Then he stood by the wall. He was the softest, smoothest, stealthiest man I ever saw, and he didn\u2019t have any more smile than a ham.\\n\\nThey had borrowed a melodeum, and it was a sick one. When everything was ready, a young woman sat down and played it. It sounded sharp and squeaky, like it was choking. Everyone joined in and sang. In my opinion, Peter was the only one who had a good thing going. Then Reverend Hobson came forward, slow and solemn, and started to speak. Right away, the most outrageous noise broke out in the cellar that I ever heard. It was only one dog, but it made a terrible racket, and it kept going the whole time. The parson had to stand there over the coffin and wait. You couldn\u2019t hear yourself think.\"}"
