          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6d3b09a3aaf12da5bfdedd7a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I can\u2019t describe the overwhelming joy with which I turned from political fights at home, and from the physical hardships of far-off countries, to my own dear home\u2014the chosen place of goodness and love; to peace, and the exchange of every sacred sympathy. If I had never left Windsor, these feelings wouldn\u2019t have been so intense; but in Greece I had been the victim of fear and terrible change. In Greece, after a stretch of anxiety and sorrow, I had watched two leave\u2014whose very names were symbols of greatness and virtue. Yet none of those miseries could ever intrude on the domestic circle left to me, while, secluded in our beloved forest, we spent our lives in tranquillity.\\n\\nSome small change, indeed, the passing years brought with it here; and time, as it always does, left the marks of mortality on our pleasures and expectations. Idris\u2014the most affectionate wife, sister, and friend\u2014was a tender, loving mother. Her feelings were not like those of many, a mere pastime; it was a passion. We had had three children; one, the second in age, died while I was in Greece. That loss crushed the triumphant, rapturous emotions of motherhood and replaced them with grief and fear. Before it happened, the little beings she had borne\u2014the young heirs of her brief life\u2014seemed to have a sure lease on existence; now she dreaded that the pitiless destroyer might snatch away her remaining darlings, just as it had taken their brother. The slightest illness brought on waves of terror. She was miserable if she was even briefly away from them. She had gathered her treasure of happiness in their fragile lives and kept it under constant watch, afraid that the insidious thief might steal those precious gems again, as before.\\n\\nFortunately, she had little reason to fear. Alfred, now nine years old, was an upright, manly little boy, with a radiant brow, soft eyes, and a gentle but independent temperament. Our youngest was still an infant; yet his downy cheek was sprinkled with the roses of health, and his tireless liveliness filled our halls with innocent laughter.\\n\\nClara had passed the age when, through her quiet ignorance, she was the source of Idris\u2019s fears. Clara was dear to her, and to everyone. There was so much intelligence joined to innocence, sensibility joined to restraint, and seriousness joined to perfect good humor; a beauty so transcendent, paired with such endearing simplicity, that she seemed like a pearl set in the shrine of all our possessions\u2014a treasure of wonder and excellence.\\n\\nAt the beginning of winter, our Alfred, now nine years old, first went to school at Eton. To him, it seemed like the first real step toward manhood, and he was accordingly pleased. Studying and playing together helped bring out the best in his character\u2014his steady perseverance, his generosity, and his well-governed firmness.\"}"
