          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b51e58add2f8aedbd3043290\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThe stupor and surprise that filled my mind at this extraordinary shift in events was, perhaps after all, the part of the adventure least open to explanation. For the upheaval itself was not only natural and inevitable, but had long been anticipated as something to expect whenever I reached that exact point in my voyage where the planet\u2019s pull would be replaced by the satellite\u2019s pull\u2014or, more precisely, where the balloon\u2019s gravity toward the earth would be weaker than its gravity toward the moon. To be sure, I had woken from a deep sleep, with all my senses in confusion, to stare at a very startling phenomenon\u2014one that, although I had expected it, I had not expected in that moment. The change itself must, of course, have happened gradually and without difficulty, and it is by no means clear that, even if I had been awake at the time, I would have noticed any sign of an inversion from anything internal\u2014any inconvenience or disarrangement, either in my person or in my apparatus.\\n\\n\u201cIt is almost needless to say that, once I came to my senses and emerged from the terror that had seized every faculty of my soul, my attention was directed first of all to the general physical appearance of the moon. It lay beneath me like a chart\u2014and although I judged it to be still at a considerable distance, the grooves of its surface were defined to my vision with a striking, altogether unaccountable clarity. The complete absence of ocean or sea, and indeed of any lake or river, or any body of water at all, struck me at first glance as the most extraordinary feature in its geological condition. Yet, strangely enough, I saw vast level regions of a clearly alluvial character, even though by far the greater portion of the hemisphere in view was covered with countless volcanic mountains\u2014conical in shape, and looking more like artificial structures than 