ns, praise of me is quite unnecessary. You must allow me to judge for myself, and grant me the compliment of believing what I say. I wish you to be very happy and very rich; and, by declining your hand, I do all I can to prevent your being otherwise. In offering me, you must have considered the delicacy of your feelings with regard to my family, and you may take possession of the Longbourn estate whenever it falls to you, without any self-reproach. This business may, therefore, be regarded as finally settled.\u201d\\n\\nAnd rising as she spoke, she would have left the room, had not Mr. Collins then addressed her\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhen I have the honour of speaking to you again on this subject, I shall hope to receive a more favourable answer than the one you have now given me; though I am far from charging you with cruelty at present, because I know it to be the established custom of your sex to reject a man on his first application, and perhaps you have even now said as much as would encourage my suit, in a way consistent with the true delicacy of the female character.\u201d\"}"
