{"request_id":"dc0c3ff9f0278f5e5c5b4493","rewritten_text":"What kind of life would they have, while Fate still kept this terrible fortune balanced on the scales? The marriage had to be delayed, no matter what it cost. He was determined about that. Even though he loved the girl deeply, and even the simple touch of her fingers when they sat together made every nerve in his body light up with perfect joy, he still knew clearly where his duty lay. He was fully aware that he had no right to marry until he had committed the murder. After that, he could stand at the altar with Sybil Merton and give his life to her without fear that he had done wrong. After that, he could hold her in his arms, knowing she would never have to blush for him, never have to lower her head in shame. But it had to happen first—and the sooner the better for both of them.

Many men in his position would have chosen the easy way, giving in to pleasure instead of duty. But Lord Arthur was too careful about his principles to put enjoyment ahead of what was right. There was more than simple passion in his love. To him, Sybil was a symbol of everything good and noble. For a moment, he felt a natural disgust at what he was being asked to do, but it did not last. His heart told him it was not a sin, but a sacrifice. His reason reminded him that there was no other path open. He had to choose between living for himself and living for others. Even though the task placed on him was undeniably terrible, he knew he must not let selfishness win over love. Sooner or later, all of us face the same kind of choice, and the same question is put to everyone. For Lord Arthur, it came early in life—before his nature was spoiled by the cold cynicism that often comes with middle age, and before his heart was worn down by the shallow, fashionable self-importance of our time. He had no hesitation about doing his duty. He was also fortunate in another way: he was not just a dreamer, or an idle person who only enjoys ideas. If he had been, he would have hesitated, like Hamlet, and let indecision ruin his purpose. Instead, he was practical. To him, life meant action more than thought. He had, above all else, common sense.

The wild, stormy feelings from the night before had now completely faded. Looking back, he almost felt ashamed of how he had wandered from street to street, caught up in his frantic emotional pain. The honesty of his suffering made it seem unreal to him now. He wondered how he could have been so foolish as to rant and rage about something that was inevitable."}

