          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8fd5745c747050a928fd1dd7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He bowed and left. At the time, he did not see anyone in the street. It was a side street, very lonely.\\n\\n\u201cWilliam Bird, a tailor, says he was one of the people who entered the house. He is an Englishman. He has lived in Paris for two years. He was among the first to go up the stairs. He heard voices arguing. The rough, gruff voice was that of a Frenchman. He could catch a few words, but he cannot remember them all now. He clearly heard \u2018sacr\u00e9\u2019 and \u2018mon Dieu.\u2019 At the same moment, there was a sound like several people struggling\u2014scraping and scuffling. The shrill voice was very loud, louder than the gruff one. He is sure it was not the voice of an Englishman. It seemed to be German. It might have been a woman\u2019s voice. He does not understand German.\\n\\n\u201cFour of the witnesses named above were called back, and they said that when the group reached the chamber where the body of Mademoiselle L. was found, the door to that room was locked from the inside. Everything was completely silent\u2014there were no groans or any other sounds. When they forced the door open, no one was seen. The windows in both the back and front rooms were down and firmly fastened from inside. The door between the two rooms was closed, but it was not locked. The door that led from the front room into the passage was locked, and the key was on the inside. A small room at the front of the house, on the fourth story, at the end of the passage, was open, with the door left slightly ajar. That room was crowded with old beds, boxes, and similar things. They removed these items carefully and searched them. There was not an inch of any part of the house that was not carefully searched. They sent sweepers up and down the chimneys. The house had four stories, with garrets (mansardes). A trapdoor on the roof was nailed down very securely and did not seem to have been opened for years. The time between hearing the voices arguing and breaking open the room door was described differently by the witnesses. Some said it was as short as three minutes; some said as long as five. The door was opened with difficulty.\\n\\n\u201cAlfonzo Garcio, an undertaker, says he lives in the Rue Morgue. He is from Spain. He was one of the people who entered the house. He did not go up the stairs. He was nervous and feared what might happen because of the agitation. He heard the voices arguing. The gruff voice was that of a Frenchman. He could not make out what was being said. The shrill voice was that of an Englishman, and he is sure of this.\u201d\"}"
