          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eab3ee2233955dd4aa895f87\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the meantime, the pull of gravity would keep waning without pause, falling off in proportion to the squares of the distances; and so, with an astonishingly increasing speed, I should finally reach those far-off regions where the earth\u2019s attraction would give way to that of the moon. Following these thoughts, I saw no real advantage in loading myself with more supplies than would last for forty days.\\n\\n\u201cThere remained, however, one further obstacle, which caused me some uneasiness. It has been remarked that, in balloon ascents to any considerable height, besides the pain that attends respiration, there is often a strong distress felt in the head and throughout the body\u2014frequently with bleeding from the nose and other symptoms of a threatening sort\u2014growing more troublesome the higher one goes. The reflection was of a somewhat startling character. Was it not likely that such symptoms would continue to intensify without limit, or at least only stop when death put an end to them? At last I could not believe it. Their source, I thought, lay in the gradual removal of the usual atmospheric pressure acting on the surface of the body, and the resulting distention of the superficial blood-vessels; it was not, as in the case of difficulty in breathing, a matter of any positive derangement of the animal system, where the chemical deficiency of atmospheric density prevents the proper renewal of blood in a ventricle of the heart. Unless that renewal failed, I could see no reason why life could not be maintained even in a vacuum; for the expansion and compression of the chest\u2014what is commonly called breathing\u2014is an operation purely muscular, and the cause, not the effect, of respiration. In short, I imagined that, as the body became accustomed to the absence of atmospheric pressure, the sensations of pain would lessen little by little; and while they lasted, I trusted in the iron hardiness of my constitution to carry me through.\\n\\n\u201cThus, may it please your Excellencies, I have set down some of the considerations\u2014though by no means all\u2014which brought me to form the plan of a lunar voyage. I shall now go on to present the outcome of an attempt so wildly daring in its conception, and, in any case, so entirely without precedent in the records of mankind.\\n\\n\u201cAfter I had reached the altitude I mentioned\u2014three miles and three-quarters\u2014I threw out from the car a quantity of feathers, and I found that I still rose with enough speed; there was therefore no need to discharge any ballast. I was pleased by this, because I wanted to keep with me as much weight as I could manage, for reasons that I will explain later.\u201d\"}"
