          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"377a964bca4d0db6284be7da\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She left everything behind her, except for the memory that such things had existed.\\n\\nAnne had not gone into Kellynch since she left Lady Russell\u2019s house in September. She did not need to, and whenever it was possible for her to go to the Hall, she managed to avoid it and get away. Her first return was simply to take up her place again in the modern, elegant rooms of the Lodge, and to please the eyes of its mistress.\\n\\nThere was some worry mixed with Lady Russell\u2019s happiness when she met Anne. Lady Russell knew who had been visiting Uppercross. But, luckily, either Anne really looked fuller and healthier, or Lady Russell only imagined it; and when Anne accepted her compliments, she found it amusing to connect them with her cousin\u2019s quiet admiration, and to hope that she might be given a second spring of youth and beauty.\\n\\nAs soon as they began to talk, Anne could tell that her mind had changed. The matters that had filled her heart when she left Kellynch\u2014things she had felt slighted about, and had been forced to hold back among the Musgroves\u2014now seemed less important. She had even lost sight, lately, of her father and sister and Bath. Those concerns had been pushed aside by everything happening in Uppercross. So when Lady Russell returned to their old hopes and fears, and spoke about her satisfaction that the house in Camden Place had been taken, and her regret that Mrs Clay was still living with them, Anne would have been embarrassed if anyone knew how much more she was thinking about Lyme and Louisa Musgrove, and everyone she knew there. She found the home and friendship of the Harvilles and Captain Benwick far more interesting than her father\u2019s house in Camden Place, or her sister\u2019s closeness with Mrs Clay. She even had to force herself to show something like equal care for Lady Russell, and to talk with the right attention about topics that, by their nature, should have come first.\\n\\nAt first there was a little awkwardness in their conversation about another subject. They had to talk about the accident at Lyme. Lady Russell had arrived only the day before, and within five minutes she had already heard the full story. Still, it had to be discussed; Anne had to ask questions; Lady Russell had to regret the imprudence, mourn the outcome, and both of them had to mention Captain Wentworth\u2019s name. Anne felt she did not do it as well as Lady Russell. She could not say his name and look straight at Lady Russell\u2019s eyes at the same time, until she used a simple solution: she told Lady Russell, briefly, what she thought about the attachment between him and Louisa.\"}"
