{"request_id":"7223f482ca370669942f7743","rewritten_text":"My father had been everything to me, and his memory was the center of my life. I might feel gratitude toward someone else, but I could never again love or hope the way I once had. Everything felt like suffering; even my pleasures were endured, not enjoyed. I felt like a lonely place in the mountains, shut in on every side by steep, dark cliffs. No warmth could reach me, and there was no way out to fields that were sunny. So even though the spirit of friendship soothed me for a time, it could not truly bring me back. It came like a gentle visit. It left, and I barely noticed the loss. The spirit of life was dead inside me, so it should not surprise you that when a friend came, I did not welcome them more gladly, and when they left, I did not mourn more bitterly. Still, the best gift of heaven—a friend—was there.

The name of my friend was Woodville. I will tell you a little about his life so you can see how cold my heart must have been, not to be warmed by his eloquent words and tender sympathy. We were both deeply unhappy, and we might have been a comfort to each other, if I had not been hardened into stone by the Medusa head of Misery. Woodville’s troubles were not the same kind as mine. His grief was natural, not the kind that destroys the heart, but the kind that can purify it. When the shadow of it passed, he could shine again, brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman, and he had received a classical education. He was one of the very few people whom fortune favors from birth. She gave him, without limit, every gift of mind and body, and under her special protection nothing could truly touch him—no small flaw, no disappointment, even if it was only temporary. It seemed as if she had shaped his mind into a kind of excellence that no roughness could stain, and his understanding was so steady that no mistake could lead him astray. His genius was transcendent. When it rose like a bright star in the east, everyone looked toward it with admiration. He was a Poet. That word has been used so often and so carelessly that it does not fully show what he was. He was like an old poet whom the muses seemed to crown at birth, and whose lips were fed by bees. When he walked among other men, it seemed as if a heavenly halo surrounded him, separating him from them and lifting him above them. It was his striking beauty, the dazzling fire in his eyes, and the way he spoke—his rich, powerful words—that held the listener in silent, thrilled wonder. Because of that, he seemed to rise above everyone else, and in his presence the others appeared only as people meant to serve his greater excellence."}

