          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f4759f08a271f1f56d663873\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For years, Dorian Gray couldn\u2019t shake off the influence of this book. Or maybe it would be more accurate to say that he never really tried to free himself from it. From Paris he had procured no fewer than nine large-paper copies of the first edition, each bound in a different color, so they could match his shifting moods and the changing whims of a nature over which, at times, he seemed to have almost completely lost control. The hero\u2014the wonderful young Parisian in whom the romantic and the scientific temperaments were so strangely blended\u2014became for him a kind of prefiguring image of himself. In fact, the whole book seemed to him to contain the story of his own life, written before he had lived it.\\n\\nIn one respect, he was luckier than the novel\u2019s fantastic hero. He never knew\u2014never, indeed, had any reason to know\u2014that grotesque dread of mirrors, of polished metal surfaces, and of still water that struck the young Parisian so early in his life, and that was brought on by the sudden decay of a beau who had once, apparently, been so remarkable. With an almost cruel joy\u2014and perhaps in nearly every joy, and certainly in every pleasure, cruelty has its place\u2014he used to read the later part of the book, with its truly tragic, though somewhat overemphasized, account of the sorrow and despair of someone who had lost, himself, what in others and in the world he had most dearly valued.\\n\\nThe wonderful beauty that had fascinated Basil Hallward, and many others besides him, never seemed to leave him. Even those who had heard the worst things about him\u2014and from time to time strange rumors about his way of life slipped through London and became the talk of the clubs\u2014couldn\u2019t believe anything that would dishonor him when they saw him. He always had the look of someone who had kept himself untainted by the world. Men who spoke crudely would fall silent when Dorian Gray entered the room. There was something in the purity of his face that rebuked them. His very presence seemed to bring back to them the memory of the innocence they had tarnished. They wondered how someone so charming and graceful could have escaped the stain of an age that was at once sordid and sensual.\\n\\nOften, when he returned home from one of those mysterious and prolonged absences that made his friends\u2014 or those who thought they were his friends\u2014so full of strange conjecture, he would creep upstairs himself to the locked room. He would open the door with the key that never left him now, and stand there, with a mirror, in front of the portrait Basil Hallward had painted of him\u2014looking at the evil, aging face on the canvas, and then at the fair young face that laughed back at him from the polished glass.\"}"
