          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"269c588089192b002096ee05\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We both agreed on Adrian with love, even though she\u2014still not yet escaped from childhood\u2014couldn\u2019t fully appreciate, as I did, the extent of his merits, nor feel the same sympathy in his pursuits and opinions. I was forever with him. There was a sensibility and sweetness in his nature that gave our conversations a tender, almost otherworldly tone. Then he was as cheerful as a lark, singing from its sky-high tower\u2014soaring in thought like an eagle, innocent as the mild-eyed dove. He could calm Perdita\u2019s seriousness and take the sting from the torturing restlessness of my own nature. I looked back on my restless desires and painful struggles with my fellow beings as if they were only a troubled dream, and I felt myself as changed as though I had been transformed into another form\u2014one with a fresh set of senses and a different mechanism of nerves, so that the apparent universe reflected differently in the mirror of my mind. But it wasn\u2019t that way. I remained the same in strength, the same in my earnest craving for sympathy, the same in my yearning for active exertion. My manly virtues didn\u2019t desert me; the witch Urania spared the locks of Sampson while he lay at her feet. Yet everything was softened and made more human. Nor did Adrian teach me only the cold truths of history and philosophy. At the same time that he taught me, through them, to restrain my own reckless and uncultured spirit, he opened my eyes to the living page of his own heart, and made me feel and understand its wondrous character.\\n\\nThe ex-queen of England had, even during her son\u2019s infancy, tried to plant daring and ambitious designs in his mind. She saw that he had genius and extraordinary talent, and she cultivated those gifts so she could later use them to advance her own plans. She encouraged his hunger for knowledge and his impetuous courage; she even tolerated his untamed love of freedom, hoping\u2014though it so often happens\u2014that it would turn into a passion for command. She tried to raise him with resentment toward, and a desire to get revenge on, those who had been responsible for his father\u2019s abdication. In this, she didn\u2019t succeed. Still, the accounts she provided\u2014however distorted\u2014of a great and wise nation asserting its right to govern itself stirred his admiration. In his early days, he became a republican from principle. Yet his mother didn\u2019t give up. To her love of rule and her proud sense of birth she added determined ambition, patience, and self-control. She devoted herself to studying her son\u2019s disposition. Through praise, censure, and exhortation, she tried to strike the right chords; and though the melody that followed her touch seemed discordant to her, she held on to her hopes, trusting that, at last, she would win him.\"}"
