          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"804c0670b4a89174b35fe682\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But enough of this. I will begin my tale: it is my last task, and I hope I have the strength to carry it through. I record no crimes. My faults can easily be pardoned; they did not come from evil intent, but from a lack of judgment. And I believe few would deny that, with different conduct and greater wisdom, I could have avoided the misfortunes that have made me their victim. My fate has been ruled by necessity\u2014hideous necessity. It demanded hands stronger than mine; stronger, I believe, than any human power, to break the thick, adamantine chain that has bound me. Once I breathed nothing but joy, always warmed by love and delight in goodness\u2014now I am brought only to misery, and it is now about to end, in death. But I lose myself; my tale is still untold. I will pause for a few moments, wipe my dim eyes, and try to let the present, obscure but heavy feeling of unhappiness fade into the sharper emotions of the past.\\n\\nI was born in England. My father was a man of rank. He had lost his father early and was raised by a weak mother, with all the indulgence she believed due to a nobleman of wealth. He was sent to Eton and afterward to college, and from childhood he was allowed the free use of large sums of money; so, from his earliest youth, he enjoyed the independence that a boy with these advantages always comes to acquire at a public school.\\n\\nUnder these circumstances his passions found deep ground where they could take root and flourish, either as flowers or weeds, depending on what they were. Because he was always permitted to act for himself, his character became strongly and early marked, showing a varied surface on which a quick observer might see the seeds of virtues and of misfortunes. His careless extravagance\u2014making him squander immense sums of money to satisfy passing whims, which, because of their apparent energy, he dignified with the name of passions\u2014often showed itself in boundless generosity. Yet while he earnestly concerned himself with the needs of others, his own desires were gratified to the fullest extent. He gave his money, but none of his own wishes were sacrificed to his gifts; he gave his time, which he did not value, and his affections, which he was happy to have called into action.\\n\\nI do not claim that, if his own desires had been set against those of others, he would have shown undue selfishness\u2014but that test was never made. He was raised in prosperity and surrounded by all its advantages; everyone loved him and wanted to please him.\"}"
