content.text:
{"request_id":"db7bac192cb48ce99f85aa98","rewritten_text":"What sort of life would be theirs, while Fate still weighed this dreadful chance in the balance? The marriage must be delayed, at any price. He had made up his mind on that point. Even though he loved the girl with all his heart—and even the light touch of her fingers, when they sat together, sent every nerve of him leaping with a kind of exquisite delight—he saw just as clearly where his duty lay. He was fully aware that he had no right to marry until he had committed the murder. Once that was done, he could face the altar with Sybil Merton and place his life in her hands without fear of having done wrong. Once that was done, he could draw her to his arms, certain that she would never have to blush for him, never have to lower her eyes in shame. Yet it had to be done first; and the sooner, the better—for them both.

Many men in his circumstances would have preferred the easy road of flirtation to the steep climb of duty; but Lord Arthur was too scrupulous to put pleasure before principle. There was more in his affection than mere heat of desire, and Sybil stood for him as a sign of everything that was good and noble. For a moment he felt a natural recoil at what he was being asked to do, but it did not last. His heart insisted that it was not a sin, but a sacrifice; his reason reminded him that no other path was open. He had to choose between living for himself and living for others, and though the burden laid upon him was undeniably dreadful, he knew he must not let selfishness win out over love. Sooner or later, all of us are brought to the same decision, the same question put to each of us. To Lord Arthur it came early in life—before his character had been spoiled by the calculating cynicism of middle age, or his heart eaten away by the shallow, fashionable self-importance of our time—and he hesitated at nothing when it came to doing his duty. And, happily for him, he was no mere dreamer, no idle dilettante. Had he been, he would have wavered, like Hamlet, and let indecision spoil his purpose. As it was, he was essentially practical. To him, life meant action more than thought. He possessed, in the rarest degree, common sense.

The wild, turbulent emotions of the previous night had now entirely ebbed, and he looked back on his frantic wandering from street to street, his fierce emotional torment, almost with a sense of shame. The very earnestness of his suffering made it seem unreal to him now. He could not understand how he had been so foolish as to rant and rage about the inevitable."}

