          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"860bcf5131c0e9b613a4f2ee\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 19th. This morning, to my great delight, about nine o\u2019clock, the moon\u2019s surface being frightfully close, and my fears stirred to the highest pitch, the pump of my condenser at last showed unmistakable signs that the atmosphere had altered. By ten, I had good reason to think its density had risen considerably. By eleven, scarcely any work was needed at the apparatus; and at twelve o\u2019clock, with some hesitation, I went so far as to unscrew the tourniquet. Finding no harm in having done so, I at length threw open the gum-elastic chamber and unfastened it from around the car. As might be expected, spasms and a violent headache followed at once from so sudden and hazardous an experiment. Yet, since these and the other troubles of breathing were by no means so severe as to put my life in jeopardy, I resolved to bear them as best I could, considering that I should leave them behind me moment by moment as I neared the denser layers close to the moon. Still, the descent itself remained extraordinarily headlong; and it soon became painfully clear that, though I had probably not been mistaken in expecting an atmosphere whose density would rise in proportion to the satellite\u2019s mass, I had erred in supposing that this density\u2014even at the surface\u2014was sufficient to carry the great weight lodged in the car of my balloon. That it ought to have been so, and in the same degree as on the earth, the actual gravity of bodies at either planet ought to correspond to the ratio of atmospheric condensation. But that it was not so, my sudden fall proved beyond doubt; why it was otherwise, I can only account for by those possible geological disturbances I mentioned earlier. In any case, I was now close to the planet and dropping with the most dreadful force. I therefore lost no time in throwing overboard, first my ballast, then my water-kegs, then my condensing apparatus and gum-elastic chamber, and at last every item inside the car. It availed me nothing, however; I continued to fall with dreadful speed, and was now less than half a mile from the surface. As a final expedient, having disposed of my coat, hat, and boots, I cut the car loose from the balloon itself\u2014its weight was no small matter\u2014and, gripping the net-work with both hands, I had barely time to notice that the whole country, as far as the eye could reach, was dotted thickly with tiny dwellings, before I tumbled headlong into the very heart of a city of the strangest appearance, and straight into the midst of a vast crowd of ugly little people. Not one of them spoke a single syllable, nor did any of them trouble themselves to offer me the least help; they simply stood, like a bundle of idiots, grinning in a ridiculous fashion, and staring at me and my balloon askance, with their arms planted akimbo.\u201d\"}"
