content.text:
{"request_id":"1566c981924160ee301e0a2f","rewritten_text":"You will be glad to learn that nothing disastrous has marked the start of the enterprise you once feared so greatly. I reached this place yesterday, and my first duty is to reassure my dear sister of my well-being and of the growing confidence I have in the success of my undertaking.

I am already well beyond London, and when I walk through the streets of Petersburgh, a sharp northern breeze brushes my cheeks. It steadies my nerves and gives me real pleasure. Do you know that sensation? This wind, coming from the very regions I am moving toward, lets me taste in advance the coldest latitudes. Encouraged by this promising air, my daydreams grow stronger and clearer. I cannot persuade myself that the pole is only a place of frost and desolation; it keeps appearing to my imagination as a country of beauty and delight. There, Margaret, the sun is always in view—its wide face just grazing the horizon and spreading a constant splendour. There—if you will allow me, my sister, to trust the navigators who came before—snow and frost are driven away; and, sailing over a calm sea, we may be carried to a land whose wonders and beauty surpass anything yet found on the habitable globe. Its productions and its character may have no equal, just as the phenomena of the heavenly bodies must be in those undiscovered reaches. What might we not expect in a country of unending light? I may there uncover the remarkable force that draws the needle, and I may also set in order a thousand celestial observations that, as things stand, require only this voyage to make their apparent irregularities consistent forever. I shall satisfy my eager curiosity by seeing a portion of the world never before visited, and I may walk on ground never before marked by the foot of man. These are my attractions, and they are enough to overcome every fear of danger or death, and to make me begin this hard voyage with the same joy a child feels when he sets off in a small boat with his holiday companions on an expedition of discovery along his home river. Yet even if all these hopes prove wrong, you cannot deny the priceless good I will bring to all mankind, down to the last generation, by finding a passage near the pole to those countries which now take so many months to reach; or by uncovering the secret of the magnet, which—if it can be done at all—can only be achieved by an undertaking like mine.

These thoughts have chased away the uneasiness with which I opened my letter, and I feel my heart warm with an ardour that lifts me toward heaven; for nothing so effectively calms the mind as a steady purpose—one on which the soul can fix its inward, intellectual gaze."}

