nd, believing myself safe in this wild place from any intruder, I resolved to pass the night where I lay. In truth, I was too tired to go farther. The air was cold, yet I scarcely cared for bodily discomfort, thinking myself well used to the weather through my two years of solitude, when no change of seasons ever checked my wandering.\\n\\nI stretched myself on the grass, surrounded by darkness so complete that not the faintest beam of light could enter. There was no sound, for the deep night had lulled the insects to rest, and the only living things in that lonely spot were those that could find no shelter in tree or shrub. There was a wondrous silence in the air: it soothed my senses, yet at the same time quickened my soul. My thoughts darted from picture to picture, and seemed to seize upon an eternity.\"}"
