          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6fe573f09b2f6b2dfe092dfa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The congratulatory letter Elizabeth received from Lydia, on the occasion of her marriage, made it plain to her that, at least by Lydia\u2019s own wife, if not by Lydia himself, such a hope was being cherished. The note ran to this effect:--\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lizzy,\\n\\nI wish you joy. If you love Mr. Darcy half as well as I love my dear Wickham, you must be exceedingly happy. It is a great comfort to have you so well off; and when you have nothing else to occupy you, I hope you will remember us. I am sure Wickham would be very glad of a place at court; and I do not think we shall manage to live without some help, even with what we have. Any situation of about three or four hundred a year would do; but, whatever you do, do not mention it to Mr. Darcy, if you would rather not.\\n\\nYours,\u201d etc.\\n\\nAs it turned out, Elizabeth would far rather not, and in her reply she tried to put an end to every entreaty and expectation of that sort. Still, whatever relief she could manage\u2014by what might be called economy in her own private expenses\u2014she often contrived to send. It had always been clear to her that an income like theirs, under the direction of two people so extravagant in their wants and so careless of what lay ahead, must be far from enough for their support; and whenever they shifted their quarters, either Jane or herself were sure to be asked for some small assistance toward paying their bills. Their way of living, even after the restoration of peace sent them back to a home, remained in the most unsettled state. They were forever changing places in search of a cheap situation, and just as constantly spending more than they ought. His affection for her soon cooled into indifference: hers endured a little longer; and, despite her youth and her manners, she kept all the claims to reputation which her marriage had brought her. Though Darcy could never admit _him_ at Pemberley, yet, for Elizabeth\u2019s sake, he continued to help him in his profession. Lydia was sometimes a visitor there, when her husband had gone to enjoy himself in London or Bath; and with the Bingleys they both stayed so long at a time, that even Bingley\u2019s good humour was worn down, and he went so far as to _talk_ of giving them a hint to be gone.\\n\\nMiss Bingley was very much mortified by Darcy\u2019s marriage; but as she judged it best to keep the privilege of visiting at Pemberley, she let her resentment drop. She became fonder than ever of Georgiana, almost as attentive to Darcy as before, and paid off every arrear of civility to Elizabeth.\"}"
