          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"78bf0da5d6c4e0bd0465f9bf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sydney Carton halted in the street, undecided as to where he ought to go. \u201cAt Tellson\u2019s banking-house at nine,\u201d he said, wearing a thoughtful look. \u201cShall I show myself in the meantime? I think I should. It\u2019s important that these people should understand there\u2019s a man like me about; it\u2019s a prudent precaution, and might well be a necessary step. But\u2014steady, steady, steady! I must work it out.\u201d\\n\\nFeeling his feet, which had begun to drift toward some purpose, he turned once or twice in the already deepening dark and followed the idea in his mind through to what it might lead to. His first notion held true. \u201cIt is best,\u201d he said at last, having settled it, \u201cthat these people should know there is such a man as I here.\u201d With that, he turned his face toward Saint Antoine.\\n\\nThat day Defarge had spoken of himself as the keeper of a wine-shop in the Saint Antoine quarter. For anyone who knew the city, it was no great matter to locate his place without asking questions. Once he had made sure of where it stood, Carton slipped back out of the nearer streets again, dined at a place of refreshment, and after dinner fell into a sound sleep. For the first time in many years, he took no strong drink. Since last night he had had nothing but a little light, thin wine; and last night he had let the brandy go down on Mr. Lorry\u2019s hearth, slowly, as though he were done with it.\\n\\nHe did not wake until nearly seven o\u2019clock, feeling refreshed, and went out once more into the streets. As he made his way toward Saint Antoine, he paused at a shop-window with a mirror in it, and slightly rearranged the untidy set of his loose cravat, his coat-collar, and his wild hair. When that was done, he went straight to Defarge\u2019s and went in.\\n\\nThere happened to be no customer in the shop, only Jacques Three, with his restless fingers and his croaking voice. Carton had seen this man upon the Jury; now he stood at the little counter, drinking, in talk with the Defarges\u2014husband and wife. The Vengeance took part in the conversation as if it were a regular fixture of the place.\\n\\nWhen Carton entered, took his seat, and asked (in very indifferent French) for a small measure of wine, Madame Defarge gave him a careless glance at first, and then a sharper one, and then another still sharper, before coming up to him and asking what he had ordered.\\n\\nHe repeated what he had already said.\\n\\n\u201cEnglish?\u201d asked Madame Defarge, inquisitively lifting her dark eyebrows.\\n\\nAfter studying her for a moment, as though even a single French word were slow to find its way into his mind, he answered in the same strong foreign accent as before.\"}"
