          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"62eaafbe8385f0208e8b5f42\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Ten minutes later, with his face drained of color from terror and his eyes full of grief, Lord Arthur Savile burst out of Bentinck House. He pushed through the crowd of fur-coated footmen gathered around the large striped awning, as if he did not see or hear anything. The night was bitterly cold. Gas lamps around the square flared and flickered in the sharp wind, but his hands were burning hot with fever, and his forehead felt like it was on fire. He kept going and going, almost staggering along like a drunk. A policeman watched him as he passed. A beggar, who had been slouching from an archway to ask for alms, became afraid when he saw a misery even worse than his own.\\n\\nOnce, he stopped under a lamp and stared at his hands. He thought he could already see the stain of blood on them, and a faint cry escaped his trembling lips.\\n\\nMurder! That was what the cheiromantist had seen in his hand. Murder! The night itself seemed to know it, and the empty wind sounded the word in his ear. The dark corners of the streets were filled with it. It seemed to grin at him from the roofs of the houses.\\n\\nFirst, he came to the Park, where the gloomy trees and woodland held him as if by force. He leaned wearily against the railings, pressing his brow to the wet metal to cool it, and listened to the shaky silence of the trees. \u201cMurder! murder!\u201d he kept repeating, as though saying it again and again could lessen the horror of the word. Hearing his own voice made him shudder, but he almost hoped that Echo might hear him and wake the sleeping city from its dreams. He felt a wild urge to stop any casual passer-by and tell them everything.\\n\\nThen he wandered across Oxford Street into narrow, shameful alleys. Two women with painted faces mocked him as he went by. From a dark courtyard came the sound of curses and blows, followed by sharp screams. Huddled on a damp doorstep, he saw bent