          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ca173427d6d560ab37c55d5a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On that point, then, I shall be completely silent; and you can be sure that no ungenerous reproach will ever leave my lips once we are married.\u201d\\n\\nIt was absolutely necessary to interrupt him now.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re moving too fast, sir,\u201d she cried. \u201cYou forget that I haven\u2019t answered yet. Let me do it without wasting any more time. Accept my thanks for the compliment you\u2019re paying me. I\u2019m fully aware of the honor of your proposals, but I cannot do anything other than decline them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m not supposed to be learning,\u201d replied Mr. Collins, with a formal wave of the hand, \u201cthat it\u2019s customary for young ladies to refuse the man they secretly intend to accept, when he first asks for their favor; and that sometimes the refusal is repeated a second time, or even a third. So what you\u2019ve just said doesn\u2019t discourage me at all, and I shall hope to lead you to the altar before long.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUpon my word, sir,\u201d cried Elizabeth, \u201cyour hope is rather extraordinary after my declaration. I assure you I\u2019m not one of those young ladies\u2014if any such young ladies exist\u2014who are bold enough to risk their happiness on the chance of being asked a second time. I mean my refusal entirely. You could not make me happy, and I\u2019m convinced I\u2019m the last woman in the world who would make you happy. And if your friend Lady Catherine were to know me, I\u2019m persuaded she would find me, in every respect, ill qualified for the position.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf it were certain that Lady Catherine would think so,\u201d said Mr. Collins, very gravely\u2014\u201cbut I can\u2019t imagine that her Ladyship would disapprove of you at all. And you may be certain that when I have the honor of seeing her again, I shall speak in the highest terms of your modesty, economy, and other amiable qualities.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, Mr. Collins, praise of me will be unnecessary. You must allow me to judge for myself, and do me the compliment of believing what I say. I wish you every happiness and every bit of wealth; and by refusing your hand, I will do everything I can to prevent you from being otherwise. In making me the offer, you must have been satisfied that your feelings were guided by proper consideration for my family, and you may take possession of the Longbourn estate whenever it falls to you, without any self-reproach. This matter, then, may be considered finally settled.\u201d And as she spoke, she would have left the room, had not Mr. Collins addressed her\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhen I have the honor of speaking to you again on this subject, I shall hope to receive a more favorable answer than the one you\u2019ve given me now; though I\u2019m far from accusing you of cruelty at present, because I know it to be the established custom of your sex to reject a man on his first application. And perhaps you have even now said as much to encourage my suit as would be consistent with the true delicacy of the female character.\u201d\"}"
