          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"db9346908093ddd8e4056615\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, that\u2019s nothing at all! It\u2019s a pleasure for me. I don\u2019t work for money; I live entirely for my art.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur set \u00a34, 2s. 6d. on the table, thanked the little German for his kindness, and, having managed to decline an invitation to meet some Anarchists at a meat-tea the following Saturday, left the house and headed for the Park.\\n\\nFor the next two days he was in a state of intense excitement, and on Friday at twelve o\u2019clock he drove down to the Buckingham to wait for news. All afternoon the stolid hall-porter kept posting up telegrams from different parts of the country, listing the results of horse races, the verdicts in divorce cases, the state of the weather, and similar items, while the tape ticked out tedious details about an all-night sitting in the House of Commons and a small panic on the Stock Exchange. At four o\u2019clock the evening papers arrived, and Lord Arthur disappeared into the library with the _Pall Mall_, the _St. James\u2019s_, the _Globe_, and the _Echo_, to the immense outrage of Colonel Goodchild, who wanted to read the reports of a speech he had delivered that morning at the Mansion House\u2014about South African Missions, and the advisability of having black bishops in every province\u2014and for some reason or other had a strong prejudice against the _Evening News_. None of the papers, however, mentioned even the slightest thing about Chichester, and Lord Arthur felt the attempt must have failed. It was a terrible blow to him, and for a time he was completely thrown.\\n\\nHerr Winckelkopf, whom he went to see the next day, was full of elaborate apologies and offered to provide him with another clock free of charge, or with a case of nitro-glycerine bombs at cost price. But he had lost all faith in explosives, and Herr Winckelkopf himself admitted that everything is so adulterated these days that even dynamite can hardly be obtained in a pure form. The little German, though, while conceding that something must have gone wrong with the machinery, still had hope that the clock might yet go off. He pointed to the case of a barometer he had once sent to the military governor at Odessa: although it was set to explode in ten days, it hadn\u2019t gone off for something like three months. It was true that when it finally did, it only succeeded in blowing a housemaid to atoms, since the governor had left town six weeks earlier\u2014but at least it showed that dynamite, as a destructive force, could be a powerful agent when controlled by machinery, even if it was a somewhat unreliable one.\"}"
