          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7468bf67c4f43cee7072bae6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His account was that, while traveling from his home and passing through Datchet, he was startled to see smoke coming from the inn\u2019s chimney. Thinking he might find fellow travelers for his journey gathered there, he knocked and was let in. There was no one in the house except Lucy and her mother. Her mother had been left unable to use her limbs by an attack of rheumatism, and so, one by one, all the remaining inhabitants of the country had set out, leaving them alone. Lucy urged the man to stay with her. In a week or two her mother would be better, and then they could set out; but they would surely die if they were left helpless and abandoned like this.\\n\\nThe man said that his wife and children were already among the emigrants, and that, in his view, it was impossible for him to remain. As a last resort, Lucy gave him a letter for Idris, to be delivered to her wherever he might meet us. At least he carried out that errand, and Idris received, with emotion, the following letter:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHONOURED LADY,\\n\\n\u201cI am sure you will remember me and pity me, and I dare to hope that you will help me. What other hope do I have? Please forgive the way I write\u2014I am so overwhelmed. A month ago my dear mother was deprived of the use of her limbs. She is already better, and in another month I am sure I will be able to travel, in the way you were so kind as to say you would arrange for us. But now everyone has gone\u2014everyone. As they left, each one said that perhaps my mother would be better before we were completely deserted.\\n\\n\u201cThree days ago I went to Samuel Woods, who, because of his newborn child, stayed as long as he could. And since they have a large family, I thought I could persuade them to wait a little longer for us; but I found the house deserted. I have not seen a soul since this good man came. What will become of us? My mother does not know our situation; she is so ill that I have hidden it from her.\\n\\n\u201cWill you not send someone to us? I am sure we must perish miserably as we are. If I tried to move my mother now, she would die on the road. And if, when she gets better, I were able to do it, I cannot even guess how I would find the roads and make so many miles to the sea. You would all be in France by then, and the great ocean would be between us\u2014which is so terrifying even for sailors.\u201d\"}"
