rame, you see, and grow cucumbers; and you\u2019ll judge at supper what kind of salad I can manage. So, sir,\u201d he added, still smiling, though now with a seriousness that matched his shaking of the head, \u201cif you imagine the small place besieged, it would hold out a devil of a time for provisions.\u201d\\n\\nAfter that, he led me to a bower some dozen yards away, though you had to reach it by such clever twists of path that it took quite a while to get there; and in that retreat our glasses were already laid out. Our punch was cooling in an ornamental pool, and the bower rose from its edge. The water was circular\u2014there was even an island in the middle, which might have served as the salad for supper\u2014and he had built a fountain in it that, once you started a little mill and pulled a cork from a pipe, would play with such force that it made the back of your hand quite wet.\"}"
