          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"26af2de3a284f05b14853ba4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But it is certainly striking that she should hear Jane better than she hears me. Jane speaks so clearly and distinctly! Still, she will not find her grandmama any more deaf than she was two years ago; and that is saying a great deal, considering my mother\u2019s age\u2014indeed, it is full two years, you know, since she was here. We have never gone so long without seeing her, and, as I was telling Mrs. Cole, we shall hardly know how to make enough of her now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you expecting Miss Fairfax here very soon?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes; next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u2014that must be a very great pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you. You are very kind. Yes, next week. Everyone is so surprised, and everyone says the same obliging things. I am sure she will be as happy to see her friends at Highbury as they can be to see her. Yes\u2014Friday or Saturday; she can\u2019t say which, because Colonel Campbell will want the carriage himself on one of those days. It is so good of them to send her the whole way! But they always do, you know. Oh yes, Friday or Saturday next\u2014that is what she writes about. That is why she writes out of rule, as we call it; for, in the usual course, we should not have heard from her before next Tuesday or Wednesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I thought as much. I was afraid there might be little chance of my hearing anything of Miss Fairfax today.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo obliging of you! No, we should not have heard, if it had not been for this particular circumstance\u2014her coming here so soon. My mother is quite delighted\u2014for she is to be three months with us at least. Three months, she says, positively, and I am going to have the pleasure of reading it to you. The matter is this: the Campbells are going to Ireland. Mrs. Dixon has persuaded her father and mother to come over and see her at once. They had not meant to go till the summer, but she is so eager to see them again\u2014for before she married last October, she was never away from them for more than a week, which must make it very odd to be in different kingdoms, I was going to say; but, however, different countries. So she wrote a very urgent letter to her mother\u2014or her father, I declare I do not know which it was, but we shall see presently in Jane\u2019s letter\u2014writing in Mr. Dixon\u2019s name as well as her own, to urge their coming over directly. They will give them the meeting in Dublin, and then take them back to their country seat, Baly-craig, a beautiful place, I fancy.\u201d\"}"
