          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e09175d9de5641de34fcf7e1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With a pounding heart and a fearful sense of not knowing why, I dismissed the servant and locked my door. Then I sat down to read my father\u2019s letter. These were the words it contained.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Child\\n\\n\u201cI have betrayed your confidence; I have tried to poison your mind, and I have made your innocent heart familiar with the looks and language of unlawful and monstrous passion. I must atone for these crimes, and I must try, in some measure, to match my punishment to my guilt. You, I doubt not, are ready for what I am about to tell you: we must separate and be divided forever.\\n\\n\u201cI deprive you of your parent and only friend. You are cast out, shelterless into the world: your hopes are shattered; the peace and security of your pure mind are destroyed; memory will bring you frightful images of guilt, and the anguish of innocent love betrayed. Yet I\u2014who bring all this misery upon you; I\u2014who cast you forth and, without remorse, have stamped the seal of distrust and agony on the heart and brow of my own child, who with devilish levity has tried to steal away her loveliness and replace it with the foul deformity of sin\u2014I, in the overflowing anguish of my heart, beg you to forgive me.\\n\\n\u201cI do not ask for your pity; you must, and you do, abhor me: but forgive me, Mathilda, and do not let your thoughts follow me into my banishment with unrelenting anger. I must never see you again; never hear your voice again; but the gentle whisperings of your forgiveness will reach me and cool the burning of my disordered brain and heart. I am sure I would feel it even in my grave. And I dare enforce this request by telling you how miserably I was led into this net of fiery anguish, and all my struggles to free myself. Indeed, if your soul were less pure and bright, I would not try to clear myself to you; I would fear that if I led you to regard me with less abhorrence, you might hate vice less. But in speaking to you, it feels as though I am appealing to an angelic judge. I cannot leave without your forgiveness, and I must try to earn it\u2014or I must despair. I urge you, therefore, to listen to my words, and if, by the sharp agony and remorse that tears the brain as madness perhaps you may think, the good guilt may be in any degree lessened, though I dare not, that I have some claim to your compassion.\\n\\n\u201cI ask you to remember, if you can, our first happy life on the shores of Loch Lomond. I had returned from a weary wandering of sixteen years, during which, though I had faced many dangers and misfortunes, my affections had been entirely empty.\"}"
