          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2ffaf6112cdaffd242fe0565\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No letter of condolence had been sent to Ireland. This neglect was blamed on the person who had done wrong. When poor Lady Elliot died, no letter of condolence was received at Kellynch, and so there was every reason to believe that the Dalrymples thought the family connection was over. The question was how to fix this awkward situation and be accepted as cousins again. Both Lady Russell and Mr Elliot felt that this was not a small matter.\\n\\n\u201cFamily ties are always worth keeping,\u201d Lady Russell said. \u201cGood company is always worth seeking. Lady Dalrymple has taken a house for three months in Laura Place, and she will be living in style. She was in Bath the year before, and Lady Russell has heard her described as a charming woman. It would be very desirable to renew the connection, if it can be done, without the Elliots having to compromise what is proper.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter, however, would choose his own way. In the end, he wrote a fine letter full of explanation, regret, and pleading to his right honorable cousin. Neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot liked the letter, but it did what was needed. It brought three lines of writing from the Dowager Viscountess: \u201cShe was very much honoured, and should be happy in their acquaintance.\u201d The hard part was done, and now came the pleasant part. They visited in Laura Place, and they had the cards of Dowager Viscountess Dalrymple and the Honourable Miss Carteret, to be shown wherever they would be seen most. Everyone talked about \u201cOur cousins in Laura Place\u201d\u2014\u201cOur cousin, Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret.\u201d\\n\\nAnne felt embarrassed. Even if Lady Dalrymple and her daughter had been very agreeable, she would still have been ashamed of the excitement they caused, but they were not agreeable at all. There was no real superiority in their manners, their accomplishments, or their understanding. Lady Dalrymple had earned the name of \u201ca charming woman\u201d only because she smiled and gave a polite answer to everyone. Miss Carteret had even less to say. She was plain and awkward, and she would never have been accepted in Camden Place if it were not for her birth.\\n\\nLady Russell admitted she had expected something better, but she still said, \u201cIt is an acquaintance worth having.\u201d When Anne tried to share her opinion with Mr Elliot, he agreed that they were nothing in themselves. Still, he insisted that, as a family connection, as good company, and as people who could bring good company around them, they had value. Anne smiled and said,\\n\\n\u201cMy idea of good company, Mr Elliot, is the company of clever, well-informed people, who have a great deal of conversation. That is what I call good company.\u201d\"}"
