          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d08bb675c5a6e549cd2e7b22\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Weeks and weeks passed, and I became more and more absorbed in you. Then something new happened. I had drawn you as Paris in delicate armor, and as Adonis wearing a hunter\u2019s cloak and holding a polished boar-spear. With heavy lotus blossoms on your head, you sat on the prow of Adrian\u2019s barge, looking out over the green, muddy Nile. In another scene, you leaned over the quiet pool of a Greek woodland and watched, in the water\u2019s silent silver, the wonder of your own face. It was exactly what art should be\u2014unconscious, perfect, and far away.\\n\\nThen, one day\u2014one I sometimes call fatal\u2014I decided to paint a portrait of you the way you really are. I wanted it to be in your own clothes and your own time, not in the costumes of long-dead ages. I don\u2019t know whether it was the realism of my method, or simply the strength of your own personality, but you seemed to come to me directly, without any mist or veil. As I worked, I felt that every tiny bit of color was giving away my secret. I became afraid that other people would see my worship of you. I felt, Dorian, that I had said too much\u2014that I had put too much of myself into the painting.\\n\\nThat was when I decided I would never let the picture be shown. You were a little annoyed, but you didn\u2019t understand what it meant to me. Harry, who I told about it, laughed at me. I didn\u2019t mind that. When the painting was finished, and I was alone with it, I felt sure I was right. Then, after a few days, the picture left my studio. Once I had gotten rid of the unbearable pull it had on me, it seemed to me that I had been foolish to think I had seen anything in it at all\u2014except that you were extremely good-looking, and that I could paint.\\n\\nEven now, I can\u2019t help thinking that the passion you feel while creating is never truly shown in the work you make. Art is always more distant and abstract than we imagine. Form and color only tell us about form and color. That is all. It often seems to me that art hides the artist more completely than it ever reveals him. So when I received an offer from Paris, I decided to make your portrait the main piece in my exhibition. It never occurred to me that you would refuse. I understand now that you were right. The picture cannot be shown.\\n\\nYou must not be angry with me, Dorian, for what I have told you.\"}"
