{"request_id":"8f9f3a9ee3c50510eb61f755","rewritten_text":"“I thought this through step by step, using what I already knew. The murderers had escaped through one of these windows. If that was true, they could not have put the window sashes back in place from the inside, because the sashes were still found fastened. That obvious fact stopped the police from looking closely at this part of the case. And yet the sashes really were fastened. So they must have been able to fasten themselves. There was no other way to explain it. I went to the open casement, pulled out the nail with some difficulty, and tried to lift the sash. It resisted me, just as I expected. I now knew there had to be a hidden spring, and finding it confirmed that my basic idea was right, even if the details about the nails still seemed strange. A careful search soon revealed the hidden spring. I pressed it, and once I was satisfied with what I had found, I did not try to lift the sash.

“I put the nail back and studied it closely. Someone who left through this window might have closed it again, and the spring would have caught. But the nail could not have been put back in place. The conclusion was clear, and it narrowed my investigation again. The assassins must have escaped through the other window. If the springs on each sash were the same, as seemed likely, then there had to be a difference between the nails, or at least between the way each nail was attached. I climbed onto the bedstead and examined the second casement carefully over the head-board. I ran my hand down behind the board and quickly found and pressed the spring. As I had expected, it was identical to the one beside it. Then I looked at the nail. It was just as thick as the other one and seemed to fit the same way, driven in nearly to the head.

“You might think I was confused, but if you do, you have misunderstood how I drew my conclusions. To put it in a sporting way, I was never ‘at fault.’ The trail was never lost for even a moment. There was no break in any link of the chain. I had followed the secret all the way to its final result, and that result was the nail. I say it looked, in every respect, like the nail from the other window. But that fact meant nothing compared with the idea that, at this point, the clue ended. ‘There must be something wrong,’ I said, ‘with the nail.’ I touched it, and the head, along with about a quarter of an inch of the shank, came off in my fingers.”"}

