{"request_id":"56c679d518eb1ea8ddbdfc97","rewritten_text":"“It wasn’t until some time afterward that I had regained enough composure to attend to the balloon’s ordinary needs. Then I examined it carefully and, to my great relief, found it uninjured. All my equipment was safe, and, fortunately, I had lost neither ballast nor provisions. In fact, I had secured them so well in their places that such an accident was entirely out of the question. Looking at my watch, I saw it was six o’clock. I was still rising rapidly, and my barometer showed a current altitude of three and three-quarter miles. Directly beneath me in the ocean lay a small black object, slightly oblong in shape—seemingly about the size of, and in every way resembling, one of those childish toys called a domino. Bringing my telescope to bear on it, I could plainly make out a British ninety-four-gun ship, close-hauled, pitching heavily in the sea with her head to the W.S.W. Besides this ship, I saw nothing but the ocean and the sky, and the sun, which had long since risen.

“It is now high time that I should explain to your Excellencies the purpose of my perilous voyage. Your Excellencies will remember that, in the end, the desperate circumstances in Rotterdam drove me to the decision to commit suicide. It wasn’t that I had any real disgust for life itself, but that I was being worn down beyond endurance by the miseries that came with my situation. In that state of mind—wanting to live, yet exhausted by life—the bookstall treatise opened a way for my imagination. I finally made up my mind. I decided to leave, but live—to leave the world, yet continue to exist—in short, to drop my questions and, whatever happened, to force a passage, if I could, to the moon. And so, lest I be thought more of a madman than I truly am, I will set out, as clearly as I can, the considerations that led me to believe that an achievement of this kind, though undoubtedly difficult and full of danger, was not absolutely beyond the possible for a bold spirit.

“The moon’s actual distance from the earth was the first thing to consider. Now, the mean or average interval between the centers of the two planets is 59.9643 of the earth’s equatorial radii—only about 237,000 miles. I say mean or average interval, but it must be kept in mind that the moon’s orbit is an ellipse with an eccentricity of no less than 0.05484 of the major semi-axis of the ellipse itself, and that the earth’s center lies in its focus; so that, if I could in any way manage to meet the moon, as it were, at its perigee, the distance mentioned above would be reduced materially.”"}

