          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8455898cb9d7c45ec0d8b568\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Those old, weak people were always seen by the public bowing. People believed that after they bowed a customer out, they would keep bowing in the empty office until they had another customer to bow in.\\n\\nThe barrister could tell that the banker would not have gone so far in his opinion unless he was sure of it in a moral sense. Even though he was not ready for the big pill he had to swallow, he managed to get it down. \u201cAnd now,\u201d said Mr. Stryver, shaking his finger like a lawyer, pointing toward the Temple in general, \u201cmy way out of this is to put you all in the wrong.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a trick of an Old Bailey tactician, and it gave him real relief. \u201cYou won\u2019t put me in the wrong, young lady,\u201d said Mr. Stryver. \u201cI\u2019ll do that for you.\u201d\\n\\nSo when Mr. Lorry came that night, as late as ten o\u2019clock, Mr. Stryver\u2014surrounded by a pile of books and papers left out for the purpose\u2014seemed to have nothing on his mind at all about the matter from the morning. He even looked surprised to see Mr. Lorry, and he was clearly absent-minded and preoccupied.\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d said that friendly messenger after trying for a full half hour to get him to focus on the subject. \u201cI\u2019ve been to Soho.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo Soho?\u201d Mr. Stryver repeated, coldly. \u201cOh, of course! What was I thinking of!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I have no doubt,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201cthat I was right in the conversation we had. My opinion is confirmed, and I repeat my advice.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you,\u201d Mr. Stryver answered, in the friendliest tone, \u201cthat I\u2019m sorry for it\u2014for your sake, and for the poor father\u2019s sake. I know this must always be a painful subject for the family. Let\u2019s not talk about it anymore.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t understand you,\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI dare say you don\u2019t,\u201d Stryver replied, nodding in a smooth, final way. \u201cNo matter, no matter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut it does matter,\u201d Mr. Lorry insisted.\\n\\n\u201cNo, it doesn\u2019t,\u201d said Stryver. \u201cI assure you it doesn\u2019t. I thought there was sense where there is no sense, and a proper ambition where there isn\u2019t one, and I\u2019m glad I was wrong. No harm is done. Young women have made similar mistakes before, and they have often regretted them later, in poverty and obscurity, just as often. In one way, I\u2019m sorry the matter is dropped, because it would have been a bad thing for me in a worldly sense. In another way, I\u2019m glad it\u2019s dropped, because it would have been a bad thing for me in a worldly sense too. And it hardly needs saying that I could have gained nothing from it.\u201d\"}"
