          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1dcc812f28b7e8ad875879f1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His attitude was stubborn to the extreme, and during the whole trip the only words that came out of his mouth were \u201cdat deuced bug.\u201d As for me, I carried a couple of dark lanterns. Legrand, meanwhile, kept himself busy with the _scarab\u00e6us_, which he had tied to the end of a short piece of whip-cord. He kept twirling it back and forth as he walked, like a conjurer performing a trick. When I noticed that plain sign that my friend\u2019s mind was not right, I could hardly keep from crying. Still, I thought it was better to go along with his ideas for now, at least until I could try something more forceful and hope it would work.\\n\\nIn the meantime, I tried in vain to get him to talk about the purpose of the expedition. Since he had agreed to let me come with him, I expected him to answer at least some of my questions, but he would not discuss anything of minor importance. Every time I asked, the only reply I got was, \u201cwe shall see!\u201d\\n\\nWe crossed the creek at the top of the island in a skiff. Then we climbed up into the high ground on the main land and headed northwest. We went through a stretch of country so wild and empty that there was no sign of any human footstep. Legrand led the way with confidence, stopping now and then for a moment to check what looked like landmarks he had planned for himself on a previous trip.\\n\\nWe traveled like this for about two hours. The sun was just setting when we entered an even more miserable region than anything we had seen before. It was a kind of plateau near the top of a hill that was almost impossible to reach. The whole place was thickly wooded from the bottom to the very top, and it was broken up by huge rocks that seemed to be resting loosely on the ground. In many places, they did not fall into the valleys below only because the trees held them up. Deep ravines ran in different directions, making the scene feel even more grim and serious.\\n\\nThe platform we had climbed onto was covered in brambles. We soon found that we could not push through them without the scythe. At Legrand\u2019s direction, Jupiter cleared a path for us to the base of an enormous tulip-tree. It stood on level ground with eight or ten oaks around it, but it was far taller and more impressive than all of them. Its foliage and shape were beautiful, its branches spread wide, and the whole tree looked majestic in a way I had never seen before.\"}"
