          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b532ebda5a6247930ead3dfd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Had she known that her sister was trying to pull her away from the hopes and realities of a life like this, what would she have felt? Only her mother could have understood it, and her mother might have felt almost the same. Lydia\u2019s going to Brighton was the only thing that comforted her, in spite of the sad belief that her husband never meant to go there himself.\\n\\nBut they did not know anything about what had happened, and their excitement kept going, with hardly any break, right up to the day Lydia left home.\\n\\nElizabeth was now going to see Mr. Wickham for the last time. Since she had been in his company so often after her return, her agitation had mostly settled down. The feelings from her earlier partiality were gone too. She had even learned to notice, in the very gentleness that had first pleased her, a kind of pretending and a sameness that soon made her tired and disgusted. And now, in the way he behaved toward her, she found a new reason to dislike him. The interest he soon showed in starting those attentions again that had marked the beginning of their acquaintance could only, after everything that had happened since, make her even more annoyed. She stopped caring about him altogether when she realized he had chosen her as the target of such empty, silly flirting. Even while she kept her feelings under control, she could not help noticing the insult in his belief that no matter how long his attentions had been away, or for whatever reason, her vanity would still be pleased and her preference would be secured again at any time.\\n\\nOn the very last day the regiment stayed in Meryton, he dined with the other officers at Longbourn. Elizabeth was so willing to stay on good terms with him that, when he asked how she had spent her time at Hunsford, she told him that Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy had both spent three weeks at Rosings. Then she asked him if he knew the colonel.\\n\\nHe looked surprised, annoyed, and alarmed. But after a moment, he remembered, smiled again, and said that he had seen him often before. Then, after remarking that he was a very gentlemanlike man, he asked her what she thought of him. Her answer was strongly in his favor. With an air of indifference, he soon added, \u201cHow long did you say he was at Rosings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNearly three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you saw him often?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, almost every day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis manners are very different from his cousin\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, very different; but I think Mr. Darcy improves with knowing him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u201d Wickham said, with a look that did not escape her. \u201cAnd may I ask\u2014\u201d He stopped himself, then continued in a lighter tone, \u201cIs it in manners that he improves?\u201d\"}"
