          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"333d8b42689545d89ea2aca8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Could not tell whether the sound belonged to a man or to a woman. Could not catch what was being said, though he believed it was Spanish. The condition of the room and of the bodies, this witness said, matched what we had described yesterday.\\n\\n\u201c_Henri Duval_, a neighbor, and by trade a silversmith, states that he was among the first to enter the house. In general, he confirms Mus\u00e8t\u2019s account. As soon as they forced their way in, they shut the door again, to keep the crowd out; the people gathered very quickly, despite how late it was. The sharp voice, this witness thinks, was Italian. He was certain it was not French. He could not be sure whether it was a man\u2019s voice\u2014it might have been a woman\u2019s. He did not know Italian. He could not make out the words, but the intonation convinced him the speaker was Italian. He knew Madame L. and her daughter, and had spoken with both often. He was sure the shrill voice was neither of the two deceased.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014_Odenheimer, restaurateur._ This witness offered his testimony. Not speaking French, he was questioned through an interpreter. He is a native of Amsterdam. He was passing the house when the cries began. They went on for several minutes\u2014probably ten. They were long and loud\u2014very dreadful and distressing. He was one of those who entered the building. He corroborated the earlier evidence in every respect, except that he was sure the shrill voice belonged to a man\u2014a Frenchman. He could not distinguish the words that were uttered. They were loud and quick\u2014uneven\u2014spoken, it seemed, in fear as well as in anger. The voice was harsh\u2014not so much shrill as harsh. He could not really call it a shrill voice. The rough voice kept repeating \u2018_sacr\u00e9_,\u2019 \u2018_diable_,\u2019 and once \u2018_mon Dieu_.\u2019\\n\\n\u201c_Jules Mignaud_, banker, of the firm of Mignaud et Fils, Rue Deloraine. He is the elder Mignaud. Madame L\u2019Espanaye had some property. She had opened an account with his banking house in the spring of that year\u2014(eight years earlier). She made frequent deposits in small sums. She had not drawn anything out until the third day before her death, when she took out, in person, the sum of 4000 francs. That amount was paid in gold, and a clerk went home with the money.\\n\\n\u201c_Adolphe Le Bon_, clerk to Mignaud et Fils, deposes that on the day in question, about noon, he accompanied Madame L\u2019Espanaye to her residence with the 4000 francs, which he had put into two bags. When the door was opened, Mademoiselle L. appeared and took one of the bags from his hands, while the old lady took the other from him.\u201d\"}"
