pping the streets and houses in gloom; the place looked stranger still to Oliver, and his uncertainty became all the more wretched and depressing.\\n\\nThey had gone only a few paces when a deep church-bell struck the hour. At the first peal, both of his conductors halted and turned their heads toward the direction the sound came from.\\n\\n\u201cEight o\u2019clock, Bill,\u201d Nancy said when the bell stopped.\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the use of telling me that; I can hear it, can\u2019t I?\u201d Sikes answered.\\n\\n\u201cI wonder whether _they_ can hear it,\u201d Nancy said.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, of course they can,\u201d Sikes replied. \u201cIt was Bartlemy time when I was put out; and there weren\u2019t a penny trumpet at the fair that I couldn\u2019t hear the squeaking of. After I was locked up for the night, the noise and clatter outside made the old jail so still that I could almost have beaten my brains out against the iron plates of the door.\u201d\"}"
