          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c0bfef20f2e93c1b25501db5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cUnless you call Miss Havisham a he. And I doubt even you would go that far.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham, up town?\u201d said Joe.\\n\\n\u201cIs there any Miss Havisham down town?\u201d my sister shot back.\\n\\n\u201cShe wants this boy to go and play there. And of course he\u2019s going. And he\u2019d better play there,\u201d my sister said, shaking her head at me as encouragement to be extremely light and playful, \u201cor I\u2019ll work him.\u201d\\n\\nI had heard about Miss Havisham up town\u2014everybody for miles around had heard about Miss Havisham up town\u2014as an immensely rich and grim woman who lived in a large, dismal house barricaded against robbers, and who lived a life of seclusion.\\n\\n\u201cWell, of course!\u201d Joe said, astounded. \u201cI wonder how she came to know Pip!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNoodle!\u201d my sister cried. \u201cWho said she knew him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201c\u2014Which some individual,\u201d Joe hinted politely again, \u201cmentioned that she wanted him to go and play there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd couldn\u2019t she ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knows of a boy to go and play there? Isn\u2019t it just barely possible that Uncle Pumblechook might be a tenant of hers, and that he may sometimes\u2014we won\u2019t say quarterly or half-yearly, because that would be asking too much of you\u2014but sometimes\u2014go there to pay his rent? And couldn\u2019t she then ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew of a boy to go and play there? And couldn\u2019t Uncle Pumblechook, being always considerate and thoughtful for us\u2014though you may not think it, Joseph,\u201d she said in the deepest reproachful tone, as if he were the most callous of nephews, \u201cthen mention this boy, standing Prancing here\u201d\u2014which I solemnly declare I was not doing\u2014\u201cthat I have forever been a willing slave to?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood again!\u201d Uncle Pumblechook cried. \u201cWell put! Nicely said! Good indeed! Now, Joseph, you know the situation.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, Joseph,\u201d my sister said, still reproachful, while Joe apologized by wiping the back of his hand across and across his nose, \u201cyou don\u2019t yet\u2014though you may not think it\u2014you don\u2019t know the situation. You may think you do, but you don\u2019t, Joseph. Because you don\u2019t know that Uncle Pumblechook, being sensible that for anything we can tell, this boy\u2019s fortune may be made by going to Miss Havisham\u2019s, has offered to take him into town tonight in his own chaise-cart, and to keep him tonight, and to take him with his own hands to Miss Havisham\u2019s tomorrow morning. And Lor-a-mussy me!\u201d my sister cried, throwing off her bonnet in sudden desperation. \u201cHere I am standing talking to mere Mooncalfs, with Uncle Pumblechook waiting, and the mare catching cold at the door, and the boy all grimed with crock and dirt from the hair of his head to the sole of his foot!\u201d\"}"
