          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"08f02c0a3c286857e8622a52\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You see, I had my handkerchief in my helmet, along with some other things; but it was the kind of helmet you can\u2019t take off by yourself. I hadn\u2019t thought of that when I put it there, and honestly I didn\u2019t even know it. I assumed it would be especially convenient right there. So now, the fact that it was there\u2014so handy and so close by, yet completely out of reach\u2014made everything worse and harder to bear. Yes, the thing you can\u2019t get is the thing you want most, mainly; everyone has noticed that. Well, it took my mind off everything else, cleared it completely, and centered it in my helmet. Mile after mile, it stayed there, imagining the handkerchief, picturing the handkerchief. And it was bitter and aggravating to have the salt sweat keep trickling down into my eyes while I couldn\u2019t get at it. It sounds like a small thing on paper, but it wasn\u2019t a small thing at all; it was the most real kind of misery. I wouldn\u2019t say it if it weren\u2019t true. I decided that next time I\u2019d carry a reticule, no matter what it looked like, and let people say what they wanted. Of course those iron men of the Round Table would think it was scandalous, and maybe raise Sheol about it, but as for me\u2014give me comfort first, and style afterward.\\n\\nSo we jogged along, and now and then we hit a stretch of dust. It would tumble up in clouds, get into my nose, and make me sneeze and cry; and of course I said things I shouldn\u2019t have said\u2014I won\u2019t deny that. I\u2019m not better than anyone else.\\n\\nWe couldn\u2019t seem to meet anybody in this lonely Britain, not even an ogre; and in the mood I was in then, it was better for the ogre\u2014that is, an ogre with a handkerchief. Most knights would have thought only about getting his armor, but I got his bandanna instead. He could keep his hardware, for all of me.\\n\\nMeanwhile, it was getting hotter and hotter in there. You see, the sun kept beating down and warming up the iron more and more all the time. And when you\u2019re hot like that, every little thing irritates you. When I trotted, I rattled like a crate of dishes, and that annoyed me; and on top of that, I couldn\u2019t stand the shield slatting and banging\u2014now against my breast, now around my back. If I dropped into a walk, my joints creaked and screeched in that wearisome way a wheelbarrow does, and since we weren\u2019t creating any breeze at that pace, I was close to getting fried in that stove. Besides, the quieter I went, the heavier the iron seemed to settle on me, and the more and more tons I felt like I weighed every minute.\"}"
