          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c1d5a3ea22b4842db19ddb6b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Thus I passed two years. Day after day, so many hundreds went by; they brought no outward changes, but a few slowly began to work on my mind as I drifted toward death. I started to study more; to sympathize more with the thoughts of others as they were expressed in books; to read history, and to lose my individuality among the crowd that had existed before me. In this way, perhaps, as the sensation of immediate suffering faded, I became more human.\\n\\nSolitude also lost some of its appeal. I began again to wish for sympathy\u2014not because I was ever tempted to seek the crowd, but because I wanted one friend to love me. You might say, perhaps, that I was gradually becoming fit to return to society. I don\u2019t think so. The sympathy I desired had to be so pure, so stripped of any influence from outward circumstances, that in the world I could not fail to be blocked by the coarse materials that constantly mingle even with its best feelings. Believe me, I was then less suited for any communion with my fellow creatures than before. When I left them, they tormented me\u2014but it was the way pain and sickness can torment: something extraneous to the mind that grates on it, and that I wanted to cast off. Now, though, I would have wanted sympathy; I would have wanted to bind my soul to one of theirs, and I would have prepared myself for plenty of disappointment and suffering, for I was tender as a sensitive plant\u2014every nerve. I didn\u2019t want sympathy and help in ambition or wisdom, but sweet and mutual affection: smiles to lift me and gentle words of comfort. I wanted one heart into which I could pour out my complaints without restraint, and from which, by the heavenly nature of the soil, blessed fruit might grow from such bad seed.\\n\\nBut how could I find this? The love that is the soul of friendship is a soft spirit, seldom found except when two amiable creatures are joined from early youth, or when they are bound by mutual suffering and shared pursuits. It comes to some of the elect, unsought and unaware; it falls like gentle dew on chosen places that, however barren they were before, become fertile for all sweet plants under its kindly influence. Yet when it is desired, it flies. It mocks the prayers of those who long for it; it will give, but it will not be sought.\\n\\nI knew all this, and I didn\u2019t go looking for sympathy. But there, on my solitary heath, under my humble roof where everything around was desart, it came to me like a sunbeam in winter\u2014something that adorns while it helps to melt the drifted snow. Alas, the sun shone on blighted fruit. I didn\u2019t revive under its radiance, because I was too utterly undone to feel its kindly power.\"}"
