{"request_id":"08e72eb117d7bd21f2cad9f1","rewritten_text":"With that done, a few more of the loops were unfastened from the rim, a further portion of the cloth was brought in, and then the freed loops were connected to their proper buttons. In this way, I could fit the entire upper part of the bag between the net-work and the hoop. It was clear that the hoop would now drop down inside the car, while the whole weight of the car itself, along with everything in it, would be supported only by the strength of the buttons. At first glance, that might seem like an inadequate reliance, but it was not—because the buttons were not only very strong on their own, but also so close together that only a very small fraction of the total weight fell on any one of them. Indeed, if the car and its contents had been three times heavier than they were, I would not have been uneasy at all. I raised the hoop again within the covering of gum-elastic, propping it at nearly its former height with three light poles prepared for the purpose. This, of course, was to keep the bag distended at the top and to hold the lower part of the net-work in its proper position. All that remained now was to fasten the mouth of the enclosure; I did this easily by gathering the folds of the material together and twisting them up very tightly on the inside with a kind of stationary tourniquet.

“In the sides of the covering, adjusted around the car, three circular panes of thick but clear glass had been inserted. Through them, I could see without difficulty in every horizontal direction. In the portion of the cloth that formed the bottom, there was also a fourth window of the same kind, aligned with a small opening in the floor of the car itself. This let me look straight down; but since I had found it impossible to place any similar arrangement overhead—because of the peculiar way the opening was closed there, and the wrinkles it caused in the cloth—I could not expect to see anything directly in my zenith. That, of course, was of little consequence; even if I had been able to put a window at the top, the balloon itself would have prevented me from making any use of it.

“About a foot below one of the side windows was a circular opening, eight inches across, fitted with a brass rim. Its inner edge was adapted to the windings of a screw. Into this rim was screwed the large tube of the condenser, with the body of the machine, of course, located within the chamber of gum-elastic.”"}

