          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cf8dc8ae1d6ceb4a45049ab0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYes\u2014burglars\u2019 tools. That seems to explain a lot. But what made you change your mind? What did you think we\u2019d find?\u201d\\n\\nHuck was trapped by the man\u2019s questioning look. He would have given anything for an answer that sounded believable, but nothing came to him. The look kept pressing on him, deeper and deeper. He had no time to think it through, so he took a guess and said it weakly:\\n\\n\u201cSunday-school books, maybe.\u201d\\n\\nHuck was too upset to smile, but the old man laughed loudly and happily. He shook himself from head to foot, then said that a laugh like that was good for a man\u2019s pocket, because it cut down the doctor\u2019s bill\u2014like everything else. After that, he added:\\n\\n\u201cPoor old chap\u2014you\u2019re pale and worn out. You\u2019re not well at all. No wonder you\u2019re a little jumpy and out of sorts. But you\u2019ll get better. Rest and sleep should set you right, I hope.\u201d\\n\\nHuck felt irritated that he had been such a fool and had shown suspicious excitement. He had already dropped the idea that the parcel brought from the tavern was the treasure as soon as he heard what people were saying at the widow\u2019s stile. He had only thought it wasn\u2019t the treasure, though\u2014he hadn\u2019t known it wasn\u2019t\u2014and that made the idea of a captured bundle too much for his self-control. Still, he felt better that the whole thing had happened. Now he knew for sure that that bundle wasn\u2019t the one, and his mind was finally at rest. Everything seemed to be moving the right way again. The treasure must still be in No. 2. The men would be caught and jailed that day. Then he and Tom could take the gold that night without any trouble and without fear of being interrupted.\\n\\nJust as breakfast was finished, there was a knock at the door. Huck sprang up to find a hiding place, because he didn\u2019t want to be connected to what had happened. The Welshman let in several ladies and gentlemen, including the Widow Douglas. He also noticed groups of citizens climbing up the hill to stare at the stile. So the news had spread. The Welshman had to tell the story of the night to the visitors, and the widow\u2019s gratitude for being saved was plain to see.\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t say a word about it, madam. There\u2019s another you\u2019re more grateful to than you are to me and my boys, maybe, but he won\u2019t let me tell his name. We wouldn\u2019t have been there if it weren\u2019t for him.\u201d\"}"
