          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e9d52a5e02fde38d79d96213\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I ran to her and lifted her into my arms, showering her with caresses as I begged:\\n\\n\u201cSpeak, darling\u2014speak! What is it?\u201d\\n\\nHer head went heavy against my chest, and she gasped, almost too faint to hear:\\n\\n\u201cHELLO-CENTRAL!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cQuick!\u201d I shouted to Clarence. \u201cCall the king\u2019s homeopath\u2014get him here!\u201d\\n\\nWithin two minutes I was kneeling by the child\u2019s crib, and Sandy was sending servants here, there, and everywhere, all through the palace. I took in the situation at a glance\u2014membranous croup. I leaned down and whispered:\\n\\n\u201cWake up, sweetheart. Hello-Central.\u201d\\n\\nShe opened her soft eyes, sluggishly, and seemed to manage:\\n\\n\u201cPapa.\u201d\\n\\nThat was a comfort. She wasn\u2019t dead yet. I had sulphur prepared; I even got up and took care of the croup-kettle myself. I don\u2019t sit back and wait for doctors when Sandy or the child is sick. I knew how to nurse both of them, and I had experience. This little one had spent much of its short life in my arms, and I often could calm its troubles and make it laugh through the tear-wet shine in its eyelashes\u2014when even its mother couldn\u2019t.\\n\\nSir Launcelot, in his finest armor, strode into the great hall on his way to the stock-board. He was president of the stock-board and occupied the Siege Perilous, which he had bought from Sir Galahad. The stock-board was made up of the Knights of the Round Table, and they used the Round Table for business now. Seats there\u2014well, you\u2019d never believe the number, so it\u2019s pointless to say it. Sir Launcelot was a bear, and he\u2019d staked out a corner in one of the new lines, and was just getting ready to squeeze the shorts today. But what did that matter? He was still the same old Launcelot, and when he glanced in as he passed the door and saw that his pet was sick, that was all it took. Let the bulls and bears fight it out however they wanted\u2014he would come right in here and stand by little Hello-Central as long as he could. And that\u2019s exactly what he did. He tossed his helmet into the corner, and in half a minute he had a fresh wick in the alcohol lamp and was firing up the croup-kettle.\\n\\nBy then Sandy had built a blanket canopy over the crib, and everything was ready.\\n\\nSir Launcelot got the steam going. He and I loaded the kettle with unslaked lime and carbolic acid, adding a touch of lactic acid, then filled it with water and set the steam-spout under the canopy. Everything was set properly now, and we sat down on either side of the crib to keep watch.\"}"
