          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2cfe0bf0aa7e15b5308caeb2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You\u2019ve raised an artificial soul and spirit in him, ma\u2019am\u2014hardly the sort of thing that suits a boy in his state. The board, Mrs. Sowerberry, who are practical philosophers, will tell you as much. What business have paupers with soul or spirit? It\u2019s quite sufficient that we allow them living bodies. Had you kept the boy on gruel, ma\u2019am, this never would have come about.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear, dear!\u201d cried Mrs. Sowerberry, with pious eyes turned up toward the kitchen ceiling. \u201cThis is what comes of being liberal!\u201d\\n\\nMrs. Sowerberry\u2019s liberality toward Oliver had amounted to giving him, in abundance, all the dirty odds and ends that no one else would touch; and so there was no small share of meekness and self-sacrifice in her, in choosing to stand under Mr. Bumble\u2019s heavy accusation, though, to do her justice, she was entirely innocent\u2014neither in thought, word, nor deed.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Bumble, once the lady let her gaze fall back to earth. \u201cThe only thing I can see to do now is to leave him in the cellar for a day or so, until he\u2019s starved down a bit; then take him out and keep him on gruel through the whole apprenticeship. He comes of a bad family, Mrs. Sowerberry\u2014excitable natures! Both the nurse and the doctor said that his mother made her way here, against difficulties and pain that would have killed any woman of good disposition, weeks before.\u201d\\n\\nAt this point in Mr. Bumble\u2019s speech, Oliver\u2014just hearing enough to know that his mother was being spoken of\u2014began kicking again with such force that every other sound was swallowed up. Sowerberry returned at that moment. When Oliver\u2019s offence had been explained to him, with the sort of exaggerations the ladies thought most likely to stir his anger, he unlocked the cellar-door in an instant and dragged the rebellious apprentice out by the collar.\\n\\nOliver\u2019s clothes were torn from the beating he had received; his face was bruised and scratched; and his hair lay scattered over his forehead. The angry flush, however, had not left him. When he was hauled from his prison, he scowled straight at Noah, and looked in no way daunted.\\n\\n\u201cNow, you\u2019re a fine young fellow, aren\u2019t you?\u201d said Sowerberry, giving Oliver a shake and a box on the ear.\\n\\n\u201cHe called my mother names,\u201d answered Oliver.\\n\\n\u201cWell, what if he did, you little ungrateful wretch?\u201d said Mrs. Sowerberry. \u201cShe deserved what he said\u2014and more.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe didn\u2019t,\u201d said Oliver.\\n\\n\u201cShe did,\u201d said Mrs. Sowerberry.\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s a lie!\u201d said Oliver.\\n\\nMrs. Sowerberry broke into a flood of tears.\\n\\nThat flood of tears left Mr. Sowerberry no choice. Had he delayed for even a moment before punishing Oliver most severely, it would have been plain to every seasoned reader that he would, by all the established precedents in disputes of matrimony, have been a brute\u2014an unnatural husband\u2014an insulting creature\u2014a base imitation of a man\u2014and a number of other agreeable descriptions too many to list within the limits of this chapter.\"}"
