y knew this insect believed stories about fires, and he had tested that idea more than once. Next came a tumblebug, pushing its ball along with steady effort. Tom touched it, and the creature pulled its legs in against its body and pretended to be dead.\\n\\nBy this time, the birds were almost everywhere. A catbird, the Northern mocker, landed in a tree above Tom\u2019s head and sang her neighbors\u2019 sounds with pure joy. Then a shrill jay swooped down, flashed blue, and stopped on a twig within the boy\u2019s reach. It tilted its head to one side, stared at the strangers with intense curiosity, and seemed to study them. A gray squirrel and a larger \u201cfox\u201d squirrel skittered along too, stopping now and then to look and chatter. The wild animals probably had never seen a human being before, and they didn\u2019t know whether to be afraid.\"}"
