          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aafd61e81d389ceb484fa57e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Moreover, this knot is one that very few besides sailors can make, and it is peculiar to the Maltese. I picked up the ribbon at the foot of the lightning-rod. It could not have belonged to either of the dead men. Now, if I am mistaken in the inference I draw from this ribbon\u2014that the Frenchman was a sailor attached to a Maltese vessel\u2014then, at any rate, I have done no harm in saying what I put in the advertisement. If I am wrong, he will only think that I have been led astray by some circumstance he will not trouble himself to look into. But if I am right, a great advantage is secured. Though he is aware of nothing of the murder, the Frenchman will naturally pause before replying to the advertisement\u2014before demanding the Ourang-Outang. He will argue to himself thus:\u2014\u2018I am innocent; I am poor; my Ourang-Outang is of immense value\u2014to someone in my position, a fortune in its own right\u2014so why throw it away through idle fears of danger? It lies here within my reach. It was found in the Bois de Boulogne\u2014at a great remove from the scene of that slaughter. How could it ever be suspected that a brute beast committed the deed? The police are to blame; they have not obtained the smallest clue. Even if they were to trace the animal, it would be impossible to show that I knew of the murder, or to connect me with guilt on the ground of such knowledge. Above all, I am known. The advertiser points to me as the owner of the beast. I cannot tell how far his information goes. If I were to avoid claiming a property of such value\u2014one that is known I possess\u2014I would at least make the animal liable to suspicion. It is not my plan to draw attention either to myself or to the beast. I will answer the advertisement, take the Ourang-Outang, and keep it close until this business has blown over.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAt that instant we heard a step on the stairs.\\n\\n\u201cBe ready,\u201d said Dupin, \u201cwith your pistols; but neither use them nor show them until you have a signal from me.\u201d\\n\\nThe front door of the house had been left open, and the visitor had come in without ringing, and gone forward several paces upon the staircase. Now, however, he seemed to waver. Soon we heard him descending. Dupin moved quickly toward the door, when we heard him again coming up. He did not go back a second time, but stepped forward with decision and knocked at the door of our chamber.\\n\\n\u201cCome in,\u201d said Dupin, in a cheerful, hearty voice.\\n\\nA man entered. He was a sailor, evidently\u2014a tall, thick-set, muscular-looking fellow, with a certain dare-devil cast to his face, though not altogether unpleasing.\"}"
