          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"14025cbd15c94d471aaf3c1d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I walked quickly, and we soon reached my college. Then I stopped and thought, and the thought made me shiver: the creature I had left in my apartment might still be there, alive, moving around. I dreaded seeing this monster, but I feared even more that Henry might see him. So I asked him to wait a few minutes at the bottom of the stairs, and I hurried up to my room. My hand was already on the door lock before I caught myself. I paused, and a cold shiver ran through me. I threw the door open hard, the way children do when they expect a ghost to be waiting for them on the other side. But nothing was there. I stepped in carefully. The apartment was empty, and my bedroom was also free of its hideous visitor. I could hardly believe such good luck, but once I was sure my enemy had truly gone, I clapped with joy and ran down to Clerval.\\n\\nWe went into my room, and the servant soon brought breakfast, but I couldn\u2019t calm down. It wasn\u2019t only happiness. I felt my skin tingle as if I were too sensitive, and my pulse raced. I couldn\u2019t stay in one place for even a moment. I jumped over the chairs, clapped my hands, and laughed out loud. At first, Clerval thought my excitement was just from joy at his arrival. But when he watched me more closely, he saw a wild look in my eyes that he couldn\u2019t explain. My loud, uncontrolled laughter frightened and shocked him.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\u201d he cried, \u201cfor God\u2019s sake, what is wrong? Don\u2019t laugh like that. You\u2019re not well. What\u2019s causing all this?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t ask me,\u201d I cried, covering my eyes with my hands, because I thought I saw the dreaded spectre slip into the room. \u201cHe can tell. Oh, save me! Save me!\u201d I imagined the monster had grabbed me. I fought wildly and then fell into a fit.\\n\\nPoor Clerval\u2014what must he have felt? The meeting he had expected with such joy had turned so suddenly into bitterness. But I wasn\u2019t there to see his grief. I was unconscious, and I didn\u2019t regain my senses for a long, long time.\\n\\nThis was the start of a nervous fever that kept me sick for several months. During all that time Henry was my only nurse. Later I learned that, because he knew my father was old and not fit for such a long trip, and because he also knew how much my sickness would hurt Elizabeth, he spared them this pain by hiding how bad my condition really was.\"}"
