          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cd00447cb4ba2d82015b644e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He mounted a fiery horse\u2014the same one that had carried him on to victory in Greece\u2014and, by throwing himself into deadening exertion, he dulled the sharpness of a troubled mind by giving himself over to mere animal sensation.\\n\\nHe recovered himself slowly; and at last, as though emerging from the effects of poison, he raised his head out of the vapours of fever and passion into the quiet air of sober reflection. He began to consider what ought to be done. The first thing that struck him was the length of time that had passed, since it was madness, not any reasonable impulse, that had governed his actions. A month had gone by, and during it he had not seen Evadne. Her influence\u2014tied to only a few of the lasting feelings in his heart\u2014had greatly withered. He was no longer her slave\u2014no longer her lover: he would never see her again, and, by the completeness of his return, would deserve Perdita\u2019s confidence.\\n\\nYet, even as he resolved this, his thoughts conjured the wretched dwelling of the Greek girl. A home she had refused to trade, from noble and lofty principle, for one of greater luxury. He recalled the splendour of her situation and appearance when he first came to know her; he recalled her life at Constantinople, attended by every circumstance of eastern magnificence; and then her present penury, her daily labour, her lonely condition, her faded, famine-struck cheek. Compassion swelled within him. He would see her once more; he would devise some plan to restore her to society, and to the comforts of her station; their separation would then follow, as a matter of course.\\n\\nAgain he considered how, throughout this long month, he had kept away from Perdita, slipping from her as if from the stings of his own conscience. But he was awake now; all this must be put right; and a future devotion would blot out the memory of this one stain upon the serenity of their life. He grew cheerful at the thought, and with sober resolve laid out the course he meant to follow. He remembered that he had promised Perdita to be present this very evening\u2014the 19th of October, the anniversary of his election as Protector\u2014at a festival held in his honour. Surely such a festival ought to be a good omen for the happiness of coming years. First, he would call on Evadne; he would not linger; but he owed her some account, some compensation for his long absence without word. Then, to Perdita, to the world he had neglected, to the duties of society, the splendour of rank, and the enjoyment of power.\\n\\nAfter the scene set down in the pages before, Perdita had expected an entire change in Raymond\u2019s manners and conduct. She looked for freer communication, and a return to those habits of affectionate intercourse that had been the delight of her life.\"}"
