          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1c57e28902b9b092963c265b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She would make such a charming companion for Emma.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Woodhouse agreed with all of it, though he added,\\n\\n\u201cOur little friend Harriet Smith, however, is just as pretty a sort of young person. You will find Harriet delightful. Emma could hardly have a better companion than Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am very glad to hear it\u2014but only Jane Fairfax is known to be so very accomplished and superior!\u2014and she is exactly Emma\u2019s age.\u201d\\n\\nThis subject was talked over with great pleasure, and other remarks of the same kind followed, passing away with much the same ease; yet the evening did not end without a small return of uneasiness. The gruel was brought in and furnished plenty to say\u2014general praise and many observations\u2014about the undoubted wholesomeness of it for every constitution, along with rather sharp remarks on the many houses where it was never served in a way that was tolerable; but, among the instances the daughter had to mention, the latest\u2014and therefore the most noticeable\u2014was in her own cook at South End, a young woman taken on for the time, who could never make sense of what she meant by a basin of nice smooth gruel, thin, but not too thin. However often she had asked for it and ordered it, she had never been able to get anything fit to be called tolerable. That was a dangerous opening.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Woodhouse, shaking his head and looking at her with tender concern. The words in Emma\u2019s ear conveyed, \u201cAh! there\u2019s no end to the sad consequences of your going to South End. It really should not be talked of.\u201d For a little while she hoped he would not return to it, and that his quiet reflection might be enough to bring him back to the comfort of his own smooth gruel. After some minutes, though, he began,\\n\\n\u201cI shall always be very sorry that you went to the sea this autumn, instead of staying here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut why should you be sorry, sir?\u2014I assure you, it did the children a great deal of good.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd besides, if you were determined to go to the sea, it would have been better not to have been to South End. South End is an unhealthy place. Perry was quite surprised to hear you had chosen South End.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know there\u2019s such an opinion among many people, but it\u2019s quite a mistake, sir.\u2014We were all in perfect health there, and never felt the least inconvenience from the mud; and Mr. Wingfield says it is entirely wrong to think the place unhealthy; and I\u2019m sure he can be relied on, for he understands the nature of the air thoroughly, and his own brother and family have been there again and again.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou ought to have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you went anywhere.\u2014Perry was a week at Cromer once, and he thinks it the very best of all the sea-bathing places.\u201d\"}"
