          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7a9303d2a95846fc3513f8dd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Madame and Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye were not destroyed by spirits. The people who carried out the crime were real, and they escaped in a real, physical way. So how did they get away? There is only one sensible way to reason this out, and it must lead us to a clear answer. Let us look, step by step, at every possible way out.\\n\\nIt is clear that the assassins were in the room where Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye was found, or at least in the room next to it, when the group went up the stairs. That means we only need to search for exits from these two rooms. The police have examined the floors, the ceilings, and the walls in every direction. No secret openings could have escaped their attention. Still, I did not rely only on their eyes. I checked myself. There were, then, no secret exits.\\n\\nBoth doors that led from the rooms into the hallway were locked from the inside, with the keys kept inside as well. So we turn to the chimneys. They are fairly normal in width for about eight or ten feet above the hearths, but they cannot fit the body of a large cat anywhere along their length. Since escape through the ways we have already considered is impossible, we are left with only the windows. From the front room, no one could have escaped without being seen by the crowd in the street. The murderers must, then, have gone through the windows of the back room.\\n\\nNow that we have reached this conclusion so clearly, it is not our job, as thinkers, to throw it out just because it seems impossible. We only need to show that these \u201cimpossibilities\u201d are not actually impossible.\\n\\n\u201cThere are two windows in the room. One of them is not blocked by furniture, and you can see it fully. The lower part of the other is hidden from view by the heavy bedstead pushed right up against it. The first window was found locked from the inside. It resisted the strongest efforts of the people who tried to lift it. A large hole had been drilled into the left side of its frame, and a very thick nail was fitted into that hole, almost up to the top. When the other window was checked, a similar nail was found fitted in the same way. A strong attempt to lift that sash also failed. The police were now fully convinced that no one had escaped through those windows. And so, they thought it was unnecessary to remove the nails and open the windows.\\n\\n\u201cMy own examination was more careful, for the reason I just mentioned\u2014because this was the place where I knew that every apparent impossibility had to be proven, in reality, not to be one.\u201d\"}"
