          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e15764dbc6a2eeff2ae88196\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhy, you can\u2019t mean to say\u2014\u201d my sister began.\\n\\n\u201cYes I do, Mum,\u201d said Pumblechook; \u201cbut just hold on. Joseph\u2014go on. Well done! Go on!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat would respectable company think,\u201d Joe continued, \u201cof twenty pounds?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHandsome would be the word,\u201d my sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cWell, then,\u201d said Joe, \u201cit\u2019s more than twenty pounds.\u201d\\n\\nThat abject hypocrite, Pumblechook, nodded once more, and with a patronizing chuckle said, \u201cIt\u2019s more than that, Mum. Good again! Follow her up, Joseph!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen to settle it,\u201d Joe said, pleased as anything, as he handed the bag to my sister; \u201cit\u2019s five-and-twenty pounds.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s five-and-twenty pounds, Mum,\u201d the basest of swindlers echoed, rising to shake her hand; \u201cand it\u2019s no more than your merits (as I said when my opinion was asked), and I wish you joy of the money!\u201d\\n\\nHad the villain stopped there, his wickedness would already have been bad enough; but he deepened his guilt by taking me into custody, with a show of patronage that made all his earlier villainy seem small.\\n\\n\u201cNow you see, Joseph and wife,\u201d said Pumblechook, taking me by the arm above the elbow, \u201cI\u2019m one of those that always see things through once they\u2019ve been started. This boy must be bound\u2014no delay. That\u2019s _my_ way. Bound out of hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGoodness knows, Uncle Pumblechook,\u201d my sister said, clutching the money, \u201cwe\u2019re truly in your debt.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t mind me, Mum,\u201d returned that diabolical cornchandler. \u201cA pleasure\u2019s a pleasure, wherever you find it. But this boy\u2014you know\u2014we\u2019ve got to have him bound. I said I\u2019d look after it\u2014tell you the truth.\u201d\\n\\nThe Justices were sitting in the Town Hall close by, so we went straight over to have me bound apprentice to Joe in the Magisterial presence. I say we went over, but in truth Pumblechook shoved me along, as if I\u2019d just then lifted a pocket or fired a shot; and in fact the general notion in Court was that I\u2019d been caught red-handed. As Pumblechook pushed me through the crowd, I heard people say, \u201cWhat\u2019s he done?\u201d and others, \u201cHe\u2019s a young \u2019un too, but he looks bad, don\u2019t he?\u201d One mild, kindly-looking person even pressed a tract into my hand, with a woodcut of a spiteful young man fitted out with a complete sausage-shop of fetters, and the title TO BE READ IN MY CELL.\\n\\nThe Hall struck me as a strange place. There were higher pews than in a church, and people leaned over them to watch. The Justices\u2014one with a powdered head\u2014sat back in chairs with arms folded, or took snuff, or nodded off, or wrote, or read the newspapers. On the walls hung some bright black portraits, which my untrained eye took for a mash of hardbake and sticking-plaster.\"}"
