          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f6d1fe585e6187312af934b3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I ran to her and lifted her into my arms. I covered her with kisses and caresses, and begged her:\\n\\n\u201cPlease, darling\u2014tell me what\u2019s wrong. Speak!\u201d\\n\\nHer head went heavy against my chest. She gasped, almost too softly to hear:\\n\\n\u201cHELLO-CENTRAL!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow!\u201d I shouted to Clarence. \u201cCall the king\u2019s homeopath and have him come right away!\u201d\\n\\nWithin two minutes, I was kneeling beside the child\u2019s crib. Sandy was sending servants everywhere across the palace. I understood what was happening at once: membranous croup. I leaned close and whispered, \u201cWake up, sweetheart. Hello-Central.\u201d\\n\\nHer eyes opened slowly, and she seemed to struggle to say, \u201cPapa.\u201d\\n\\nThat was a relief. She was still far from dead. I had sulphur prepared, and I got the croup-kettle going myself. I don\u2019t just sit and wait for doctors while Sandy or the child is sick. I knew how to care for both of them, and I had done it before. This little one had spent much of its short life in my arms, and I often managed to calm it and make it laugh again through the tears on its eyelashes\u2014when even its mother couldn\u2019t.\\n\\nSir Launcelot came striding through the great hall in his finest armor, on his way to the stock-board. He was president of it, and he sat in the Siege Perilous, which he had bought from Sir Galahad. The stock-board was made up of the Knights of the Round Table, and they used the Round Table for business now. The seats were worth a fortune\u2014so much that you wouldn\u2019t believe the number, and it wouldn\u2019t help to say it. Sir Launcelot was a bear, and he had a place in one of the new lines; he was getting ready to squeeze the shorts today. But what did that matter? He was still the same old Launcelot. When he glanced in as he passed the door and saw that his pet was sick, that was all he needed. Let the bulls and bears fight it out without him\u2014he came straight in and stood by little Hello-Central as long as he could. That\u2019s exactly what he did. He tossed his helmet into the corner, and in half a minute he had a new wick in the alcohol lamp and was firing up the croup-kettle.\\n\\nBy then, Sandy had set up a blanket canopy over the crib, and everything was ready. Sir Launcelot got the steam going. He and I filled the kettle with unslaked lime and carbolic acid, adding a touch of lactic acid, then filled it with water and placed the steam-spout under the canopy. Everything was set. We sat on either side of the crib to keep watch.\"}"
