          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ae82d07b82b6d83a889e301d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She now felt a strong urge to go to the outer door; she wanted to see whether it was raining. Why should she suspect herself of some other motive? Captain Wentworth must be out of sight. She rose from her seat\u2014she would go; one half of her shouldn\u2019t always be so much wiser than the other half, or always suspecting it of being worse than it was. She would find out if it rained. But she was sent back at once by Captain Wentworth\u2019s entrance himself, among a party of gentlemen and ladies\u2014evidently people he knew\u2014whom he must have joined a little below Milsom Street.\\n\\nHe was more visibly struck and confused by seeing her than she had ever noticed before; he looked quite red. For the first time, since their renewed acquaintance, she felt that she was betraying the least sensibility of the two. She had the advantage over him in the preparation of the last few moments. All the overpowering, blinding, bewildering first effects of strong surprise were over for her. Still, she had enough to feel. It was agitation, pain, pleasure\u2014something caught between delight and misery.\\n\\nHe spoke to her, and then turned away. The tone of his manner was embarrassment. She couldn\u2019t call it cold, or friendly, or anything so clearly as embarrassed.\\n\\nAfter a short interval, however, he came toward her again and spoke once more. Their mutual inquiries on common subjects passed; neither of them, probably, learned much from what they heard, and Anne continued to be fully aware that he was less at ease than before. By dint of being together so much, they had managed to speak to each other with a considerable show of indifference and calmness; but he couldn\u2019t do it now. Time had changed him, or Louisa had changed him. There was some kind of awareness in him. He looked very well\u2014not as if he had been suffering in health or spirits\u2014and he talked of Uppercross, of the Musgroves, even of Louisa, and had even a brief moment of his own arch significance as he mentioned her; yet still it was Captain Wentworth who was not comfortable, not at ease, not able to pretend that he was.\\n\\nIt didn\u2019t surprise Anne, but it grieved her, to see that Elizabeth wouldn\u2019t recognize him. She could tell that he saw Elizabeth, that Elizabeth saw him, and that there was complete internal recognition on both sides. She was convinced he was ready to be acknowledged as an acquaintance, expecting it; and she had the pain of watching her sister turn away with unchanging coldness.\\n\\nLady Dalrymple\u2019s carriage, for which Miss Elliot was growing very impatient, now drew up; the servant came in to announce it.\"}"
