          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c776d4f8c4a223a488195648\",\"rewritten_text\":\"My heart was pounding, and I felt afraid, though I did not know why. I sent the servant away, locked my door, and sat down to read my father\u2019s letter. These were the words it contained.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Child,\\n\\n\u201cI have betrayed your trust. I tried to poison your mind, and I have made your innocent heart learn the look and the words of unlawful, monstrous passion. I must make up for these crimes, and I must try to match my punishment to my guilt. You are, I doubt not, ready for what I am about to tell you. We must separate, and be divided forever.\\n\\n\u201cI take away your parent and your only friend. You are left with no shelter in the world. Your hopes are destroyed. The peace and safety of your pure mind are gone. When you remember, you will see terrible images of guilt, and you will suffer the pain of innocent love betrayed. Yet I am the one who brings all this misery on you. I am the one who casts you out, and without mercy puts the seal of distrust and agony on the heart and brow of my own child. With devilish carelessness, I tried to steal away her beauty and replace it with the ugly shape of sin. In the deep anguish of my heart, I beg you to forgive me.\\n\\n\u201cI do not ask for your pity. You must, and you do, hate me. But forgive me, Mathilda, and do not let your thoughts follow me into my exile with unrelenting anger. I must never see you again. I must never hear your voice again. Still, the gentle whispers of your forgiveness will reach me and cool the burning of my confused mind and heart. I am sure I would feel it even in my grave. And I dare to ask this of you by telling you how badly I was tricked into this net of burning anguish, and how I struggled to free myself. In truth, if your soul were less pure and bright, I would not try to defend myself to you. I would be afraid that if I made you think of me with less hatred, you might hate vice less. But when I speak to you, it feels as if I am appealing to an angelic judge. I cannot leave without your forgiveness. I must try to earn it, or I must despair. So I urge you to listen to my words. And if, by some sharp agony and remorse that tears the mind apart like madness, my guilt can be lessened in any way, then perhaps you will think I deserve some compassion.\\n\\n\u201cI ask you to remember our first happy life on the shores of Loch Lomond. I had returned after sixteen years of weary wandering. During that time, even though I faced many dangers and misfortunes, my feelings were completely empty.\"}"
