          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7aa91bfb46a7895d61cadd0d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After a while, twelve o\u2019clock boomed from the tall tower at Westminster, and with each strike of the deep, resonant bell the night seemed to shudder. Then the railway lights went out; only one solitary lamp remained, shining like a large ruby on a giant mast, and the roar of the city faded.\\n\\nAt two o\u2019clock he got up and strolled toward Blackfriars. Everything looked unreal. Everything felt like some strange dream. The houses across the river seemed built out of darkness. It was as if silver and shadow had remade the world. The huge dome of St. Paul\u2019s loomed like a bubble in the dim air.\\n\\nAs he neared Cleopatra\u2019s Needle, he saw a man leaning over the parapet. When he came closer, the man looked up, and the gaslight fell full on his face.\\n\\nIt was Mr. Podgers, the cheiromantist. No one could mistake the fat, flabby face, the gold-rimmed spectacles, the sickly, weak smile, the sensual mouth.\\n\\nLord Arthur stopped. A brilliant idea flashed through him, and he moved quietly up behind. In an instant he had seized Mr. Podgers by the legs and thrown him into the Thames. There was a coarse oath, a heavy splash, and then everything went still. Lord Arthur looked anxiously over, but he could see nothing of the cheiromantist except a tall hat, spinning in an eddy of moonlit water. After a time even that sank, and no trace of Mr. Podgers was visible. Once he thought he caught sight of the bulky, misshapen figure striking out for the staircase by the bridge, and a horrible feeling of failure swept over him, but it turned out to be only a reflection. When the moon slipped out from behind a cloud, it was gone. At last it seemed he had accepted the decree of destiny. He let out a deep sigh of relief, and Sybil\u2019s name came to his lips.\\n\\n\u201cHave you dropped anything, sir?\u201d a voice said behind him suddenly.\\n\\nHe turned around and saw a policeman holding a bull\u2019s-eye lantern.\\n\\n\u201cNothing of importance, sergeant,\u201d he replied, smiling. Then, hailing a passing hansom, he jumped in and told the driver to take him to Belgrave Square.\\n\\nFor the next few days he swung between hope and fear. There were moments when he almost expected Mr. Podgers to walk into the room, and yet at other times he felt that Fate couldn\u2019t be so unjust to him. Twice he went to the cheiromantist\u2019s address in West Moon Street, but he couldn\u2019t bring himself to ring the bell. He wanted certainty, and he was afraid of it.\\n\\nFinally it came. He was sitting in the club\u2019s smoking-room, having tea, listening rather dully to Surbiton\u2019s account of the last comic song at the Gaiety, when the waiter came in with the evening papers.\"}"
