out it. I told myself I was used to the weather after my two years of solitude, when no change of seasons ever stopped my endless wandering.\\n\\nI lay on the grass, surrounded by darkness so complete that not even the smallest light could reach me. There was no sound, because the deep night had put the insects to sleep, and the only living things were those that could survive on that lonely spot where no tree or shrub offered shelter. The silence was so strange and wonderful that it calmed my senses, yet it also stirred my soul. My thoughts raced from one image to the next, and it felt as if my mind could grasp an eternity.\"}"
