          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a258c666fd8626689037e2f6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With my heart pounding and with a fear I could not explain, I drove the servant away, locked my door, and sat down to read my father\u2019s letter. These were the words it contained.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Child\\n\\n\u201cI have betrayed your confidence; I have tried to corrupt your mind, and I have brought your innocent heart to know the looks and language of unlawful and monstrous passion. I must atone for these crimes, and I must, as far as I can, measure my punishment against my guilt. You are, I doubt not, ready for what I am about to tell you; we must separate and be divided for ever.\\n\\n\u201cI deprive you of your parent and only friend. You are turned out upon the world without shelter: your hopes are shattered; the peace and security of your pure mind are destroyed; memory will return to you with frightful pictures of guilt, and with the torment of innocent love betrayed. Yet I\u2014who bring all this misery upon you; I\u2014who cast you off and, with remorseless levity, have set the seal of distrust and agony upon the heart and brow of my own child, who has sought, with devilish lightness, to steal away her loveliness and put in its place the foul deformity of sin; I, in the overflowing anguish of my heart, beg you to forgive me.\\n\\n\u201cI do not ask for your pity; you must, and you do, abhor me: but pardon me, Mathilda, and do not let your thoughts pursue me in my banishment with unrelenting anger. I must never more see you; never more hear your voice; yet the gentle whisperings of your forgiveness will reach me and cool the burning of my disordered brain and heart. I am sure I should feel it even in my grave. And I dare press this request by telling you how miserably I was drawn into this net of fiery anguish, and by describing all my efforts to free myself. Indeed, if your soul were less pure and bright, I would not try to clear myself to you; I should fear that, if I made you regard me with less abhorrence, you might hate vice less. But in speaking to you, I feel as though I were appealing to an angelic judge. I cannot go away without your forgiveness, and I must strive to obtain it\u2014or I must despair. I conjure you, therefore, to listen to my words; and if, by the good guilt, anything may be lessened by sharp agony and by remorse that tears the brain as madness perhaps you may think, though I dare not say it, that I have some claim to your compassion.\\n\\n\u201cI entreat you to recall our first happy life on the shores of Loch Lomond. I had come after a weary wandering of sixteen years, during which, though I had passed through many dangers and misfortunes, my affections had been a complete blank.\"}"
