          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"58305c1d6ec29b898292fa79\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To look at a star by quick glances, or to see it from the side by turning your eye so that the outer parts of the retina take in the light, is to see the star clearly. This gives you the best sense of its brightness, and that brightness fades the more directly you look at it. In the second case, more rays actually enter the eye, but in the first you have a more careful way of understanding what you see. If you stare too deeply, you confuse and weaken your thoughts. It is even possible to make Venus disappear from the sky if you look at it too steadily, too intensely, or too directly.\\n\\n\u201cAs for these murders, let\u2019s examine things for ourselves before we decide what we think. An inquiry will be entertaining,\u201d [I thought it was a strange word to use, but I said nothing] \u201cand, besides, Le Bon once did me a favor that I\u2019m not going to forget. We\u2019ll go and see the place with our own eyes. I know G\u2014\u2014, the Prefect of Police, so getting the permission won\u2019t be hard.\u201d\\n\\nThe permission was obtained, and we went at once to the Rue Morgue. It was one of those miserable streets that lies between the Rue Richelieu and the Rue St. Roch. We arrived late in the afternoon, since this part of town was far from where we lived. We found the house easily, because many people were still standing across the way, staring up at the closed shutters with empty curiosity. The building was an ordinary Parisian house, with a gateway. On one side there was a glazed watch-box, and in the window a sliding panel showed that there was a loge de concierge. Before we went in, we walked up the street, turned down an alley, and then, turning again, went around behind the building. All the while, Dupin studied the whole neighborhood and the house itself with such careful attention that I could not see what purpose it could serve.\\n\\nWe retraced our steps, came back to the front, rang the bell, and, after showing our credentials, were admitted by the agents on duty. We went upstairs into the room where Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s body had been found, and where both bodies still lay. The room\u2019s disorder had, as usual, been allowed to remain. I saw nothing beyond what had already been reported in the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux.\u201d Dupin examined everything, not leaving out the bodies of the victims. Then we went into the other rooms and into the yard, with a gendarme accompanying us the whole time.\"}"
