          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3b567144423405ac395d5b8f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAnd think about it, ma\u2019am,\u201d Oliver said, with tears filling his eyes even though he tried to stop them. \u201cOh, think how young and good she is, and how much pleasure and comfort she brings to everyone around her. I\u2019m sure\u2014absolutely sure\u2014that, for your sake, since you are so kind yourself; and for her sake; and for everyone she makes happy, she will not die. Heaven will not allow her to die so young.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHush!\u201d Mrs. Maylie said, putting her hand on Oliver\u2019s head. \u201cYou talk like a child, poor boy. Still, you remind me of my duty. I had forgotten it for a moment, Oliver, but I hope you will forgive me. I am old, and I have seen enough illness and death to know the pain of being separated from the people we love. I have also seen enough to know that it is not always the youngest and best who are spared. But this should comfort us in our sorrow, because Heaven is fair. These things teach us, in a powerful way, that there is a better world than this\u2014and that the road to it is short. God\u2019s will be done! I love her, and He knows how well.\u201d\\n\\nOliver was surprised to see that, as Mrs. Maylie spoke those words, she stopped her crying as if she had forced herself to do it. She straightened up and became calm and steady. He was even more astonished to learn that this steadiness lasted. Through all the care and watching that followed, Mrs. Maylie remained ready and composed, doing every duty that fell to her, steadily\u2014and, from the outside, even cheerfully. But Oliver was young, and he did not understand what strong minds can handle in hard times. How could he, when those people so rarely even understand their own strength?\\n\\nAn anxious night passed. When morning came, Mrs. Maylie\u2019s fears were confirmed. Rose was in the first stage of a high and dangerous fever.\\n\\n\u201cWe must act, Oliver, and not give in to useless grief,\u201d Mrs. Maylie said. She put a finger to her lip and looked straight at him. \u201cThis letter must be sent as quickly as possible to Mr. Losberne. It has to be carried to the market town, which is only about four miles away. You can take the footpath across the field, and then it must be sent on by an express on horseback straight to Chertsey. The people at the inn will handle it from there, and I can trust you to make sure it gets done. I know I can.\u201d\\n\\nOliver had no answer. He only looked as if he wanted his anxiety to be gone at once.\"}"
