          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6c674d23ecbe7ed0b2ea2b88\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She studied me closely for a moment, and then said quietly,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you want for them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly,\u201d I said, \u201cthat you won\u2019t mix them up with the others. They may share the same blood, but, believe me, they\u2019re not of the same nature.\u201d\\n\\nStill looking at me closely, Miss Havisham repeated,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you want for them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou see, I\u2019m not so clever,\u201d I answered, aware that I was blushing a little. \u201cI can\u2019t hide from you, even if I wanted to, that I do want something. Miss Havisham, if you\u2019d spare the money to do my friend Herbert a lasting service in life\u2014something that, by its very nature, must be done without his knowledge\u2014I could show you how.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy must it be done without his knowledge?\u201d she asked, resting her hands on her stick so she could look at me even more carefully.\\n\\n\u201cBecause,\u201d I said, \u201cI started the service myself more than two years ago, without his knowledge, and I don\u2019t want to be betrayed. I can\u2019t explain why I\u2019ve failed to finish it. It\u2019s part of a secret that belongs to someone else, not me.\u201d\\n\\nShe slowly pulled her eyes away from me and turned them to the fire. After watching it for what seemed like a long time in the silence, and by the light of the candles burning down, she was stirred by the collapse of some of the red coals. She looked back toward me\u2014first blankly, then with attention that steadily sharpened. All this time Estella kept knitting. When Miss Havisham finally fixed her attention on me, she said, as if there had been no break in our conversation,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhat else?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEstella,\u201d I said, turning to her now, and trying to control my trembling voice, \u201cyou know I love you. You know I\u2019ve loved you for a long time, and with all my heart.\u201d\\n\\nShe lifted her eyes to my face as I spoke, and her fingers kept working. She looked at me with an expressionless face. I saw that Miss Havisham glanced from me to her, and from her back to me.\\n\\n\u201cI should have said this sooner, but for my long mistake. It made me hope that Miss Havisham meant us for one another. While I thought you couldn\u2019t help yourself, in a way, I held back from saying it. But I have to say it now.\u201d\\n\\nWith her unmoved expression still in place, and her fingers still going, Estella shook her head.\\n\\n\u201cI know,\u201d I said in response to that gesture. \u201cI have no hope that I will ever call you mine, Estella.\u201d\"}"
