          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c36579e28eb74717df228b7c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I want you to be permanently well connected, and for that reason it would be best to have as few odd acquaintances as possible. So I say this: if you are still in this country when Mr. Martin marries, I hope you won\u2019t be pulled in by your closeness to the sisters\u2014so that you end up meeting his wife. She will probably be nothing more than some farmer\u2019s daughter, with no education.\\n\\n\u201cOf course. Yes. I don\u2019t think Mr. Martin would ever marry anyone who hadn\u2019t had some education, and been very well brought up. Still, I don\u2019t mean to argue against your opinion. I\u2019m sure I won\u2019t want to know his wife. I will always have a great regard for the Miss Martins, especially Elizabeth, and I would be very sorry to give them up, because they are just as well educated as I am. But if he marries a very ignorant, vulgar woman, then I had better not visit her, if I can avoid it.\u201d\\n\\nEmma watched her as she spoke and saw nothing that looked like love. The young man had been the first admirer, but she believed there was no other reason for Harriet to be taken with him, and that nothing serious would get in the way of any friendly arrangement Harriet might want.\\n\\nThey met Mr. Martin the very next day while they were walking on the Donwell road. He was on foot. After looking at her very respectfully, he seemed genuinely pleased with her companion. Emma was glad to have the chance to study him. As she walked a few steps ahead while they talked, her quick eyes soon learned what Mr. Robert Martin was like. He looked neat and like a sensible young man, but he had no other real advantage. When she compared him with other gentlemen, she felt he would lose the ground he had gained in Harriet\u2019s favor. Harriet noticed his manner too. She had already seen her father\u2019s gentleness and admired it with both wonder and approval. Mr. Martin looked as if he didn\u2019t really know what to do with his manners.\\n\\nThey stayed together only a few minutes, because Miss Woodhouse couldn\u2019t be kept waiting. Then Harriet ran to her with a smiling face, full of excitement, which Miss Woodhouse hoped she would calm down soon.\\n\\n\u201cJust think of it\u2014we actually met him! How strange! He said it was a lucky thing he hadn\u2019t gone by Randalls. He didn\u2019t think we ever walk this road. He thought we walk toward Randalls most days.\u201d\"}"
