          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"71e271d2878a8d847118cbcc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don\u2019t want to get mixed up in any such business as that, I says to myself.\\n\\nWhen I come down the stairs in the morning, the parlor was shut up tight, and the watchers was gone. There weren\u2019t nobody about except the family, the widow Bartley, and our tribe. I kept my eyes on their faces to see if anything had been going on, but I couldn\u2019t make out.\\n\\nAbout the middle of the day the undertaker showed up with his man, and they set the coffin down in the middle of the room on a couple of chairs. Then they lined up all our chairs in rows, and borrowed more from the neighbors till the hall and the parlor and the dining-room was full. I could see the coffin lid was just as it had been before, but I didn\u2019t dare to go look under it, with folks standing around.\\n\\nAfter that, people started coming in, and the beats and the girls took seats in the front row right at the head of the coffin. For about half an hour folks filed past slow in a single line, looking down at the dead man\u2019s face a minute at a time; some dropped a tear. Everything stayed very still and solemn, except the girls and the beats holding their handkerchiefs to their eyes, keeping their heads bowed, and giving a little sob. There wasn\u2019t any other sound besides the scraping of feet on the floor and people blowing their noses\u2014because at a funeral folks blow them more than they do anywhere else, save church.\\n\\nWhen the place was packed, the undertaker slid around in his black gloves with his soft, soothing ways, putting on the last touches and getting everything shipshape and comfortable, and making no more noise than a cat. He didn\u2019t speak; he just shifted people around, squeezed in the late ones, opened up little passages, and did it all with nods and signs with his hands. Then he stood over against the wall. He was the softest, glidingest, stealthiest man I ever saw; and there wasn\u2019t any more smile on him than there is on a ham.\\n\\nThey had borrowed a melodeum\u2014an ill one; and once everything was ready, a young woman sat down and worked it. It was pretty screechy and colicky, and everybody joined in and sang, and Peter was the only one that had a good thing, as far as I could tell. Then Reverend Hobson opened up, slow and solemn, and started talking; and right off the most outrageous row broke out down in the cellar that I ever heard. It was only one dog, but he made a powerful racket, and he kept at it the whole time. The parson had to stand there over the coffin and wait\u2014you couldn\u2019t hear yourself think.\"}"
