{"request_id":"e95beabf2d62913e38a019e6","rewritten_text":"Climbing to the top of the stairs, he steadied himself and set out to unleash his famous peal of demoniac laughter. More than once he had found it astonishingly effective. It was said to have turned Lord Raker’s wig grey in a single night, and it had certainly made three of Lady Canterville’s French governesses give notice before their month was out. So he let loose his most dreadful laugh until the old vaulted ceiling rang and rang again; but scarcely had the terrible echo faded when a door swung open and Mrs. Otis appeared in a light blue dressing-gown. “I’m afraid you’re far from well,” she said, “and I’ve brought you a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. If it’s indigestion, you’ll find it an excellent remedy.” The ghost stared at her with burning rage, and at once began to ready himself to turn into a great black dog—a feat he was well known for, and one the family doctor had always blamed for the permanent dullness of Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Hon. Thomas Horton. Yet the sound of footsteps drawing nearer made him pause in his grim plan. He settled instead for becoming faintly phosphorescent, and vanished with a deep churchyard groan, just as the twins came up to him.

When he reached his room, he completely fell apart and was seized by the fiercest agitation. The twins’ vulgarity and Mrs. Otis’s blunt, practical way of thinking were naturally irritating enough, but what truly upset him was that he hadn’t managed to wear the suit of mail. He had been counting on even modern Americans being stirred by the sight of a Spectre In Armour—if only because it would be fitting, at least in deference to their national poet Longfellow, whose elegant and engaging verses he had spent many a weary hour reading while the Cantervilles were in town. Besides, it was his own suit. He had worn it with great success at the Kenilworth tournament, and had received high praise for it from no less a figure than the Virgin Queen herself. Still, the moment he tried it on, the weight of the massive breastplate and steel casque overwhelmed him; he went down hard on the stone pavement, snapped both his knees painfully, and bruised the knuckles of his right hand.

For several days afterward he was very ill and barely left his room at all, except to keep the blood-stain in proper order. Eventually, with careful attention, he recovered and decided to make a third attempt at frightening the United States Minister and his family."}

