          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a2183aa7b767cf002af8781c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was something wonderfully satisfying about tidying up and then taking a quiet pause before going on with life again, and in our village on Saturday nights it made Joe bold enough to linger half an hour longer on Saturdays than he did at other times. When that half-hour was up, and the rum and water ran out together, Joe rose to go and took me by the hand.\\n\\n\u201cHold on a half moment, Mr. Gargery,\u201d said the odd man. \u201cI believe I\u2019ve got a bright new shilling somewhere in my pocket; and if I have, the boy shall have it.\u201d\\n\\nHe fished it out from a handful of small change, tucked it into some crumpled paper, and handed it to me. \u201cYours!\u201d he said. \u201cMind\u2014your own.\u201d\\n\\nI thanked him, staring at him well past what good manners would allow, and kept a firm grip on Joe. He said good-night to Joe, and good-night to Mr. Wopsle as well (who went out with us), and to me he gave nothing but a look from his aiming eye\u2014no, not exactly a look, because he shut it up; still, wonders can be done with an eye by hiding it.\\n\\nOn the way home, if I\u2019d been in the mood to talk, the conversation would have been entirely on my side, for Mr. Wopsle left us at the door of the Jolly Bargemen, and Joe went all the way home with his mouth wide open, trying to blow the rum out with as much air as he could manage. But I was so dazed by the sudden return of my old wrongdoing and my old acquaintance that I couldn\u2019t think of anything else.\\n\\nWhen we presented ourselves in the kitchen, my sister wasn\u2019t in a particularly ill temper, and that unusual calm encouraged Joe to tell her about the bright shilling. \u201cA bad one, I\u2019ll warrant,\u201d said Mrs. Joe, with triumph, \u201cor he wouldn\u2019t have given it to the boy! Let\u2019s see it.\u201d\\n\\nI took it from the paper, and it turned out to be a good one. \u201cBut what\u2019s this?\u201d said Mrs. Joe, dropping the shilling and snatching up the paper. \u201cTwo One-Pound notes?\u201d\\n\\nNothing less than two thick, hot, sweltering one-pound notes, as if they\u2019d been on the friendliest terms with every cattle-market in the county. Joe grabbed his hat again and ran with them to the Jolly Bargemen to return them to their owner. While he was gone, I sat down on my usual stool and stared vacantly at my sister, feeling pretty certain the man wouldn\u2019t be there.\\n\\nAt last Joe came back, saying the man had gone, but that Joe had left word at the Three Jolly Bargemen about the notes.\"}"
