{"request_id":"f77841528c78219c8db8d48f","rewritten_text":"Mr. Attorney-General was obliged to tell the jury that the prisoner before them, though still young in years, had long been old in the treasonable practices that, in the end, would cost him his life. He insisted that this correspondence with the public enemy was not something of to-day, nor of yesterday, nor even of last year, nor of the year before that. It was, he said, certain the prisoner had been passing and repassing between France and England for longer than that, on secret business of which he could not offer any honest account. And if it were the sort of thing traitorous conduct to prosper (which, happily, it never was), the true wickedness and guilt of his undertaking might have stayed hidden. Providence, however, had put it into the heart of a person who was beyond fear and beyond reproach to ferret out the nature of the prisoner’s schemes; and, struck with horror, to lay them before his Majesty’s Chief Secretary of State and the most honourable Privy Council. This patriot, he added, would be brought before them. His bearing and his whole manner, on the whole, were sublime.

He had been the prisoner’s friend, Mr. Attorney-General continued, but at once—at an hour both auspicious and evil—when he had detected the man’s infamy, he had resolved to sacrifice the traitor he could no longer cherish in his bosom, upon the sacred altar of his country. If Britain decreed statues for public benefactors, as in ancient Greece and Rome, that bright citizen would surely have one. Since such decrees were not made, he probably would not. Virtue, as the poets had remarked (in many passages which he knew the jury would have, word for word, ready on their tongues), was in a manner contagious; and especially the clear virtue called patriotism, or love of country. The lofty example of this spotless, unimpeachable witness for the Crown—mentioning whom, however unworthily, was an honour—had taken hold upon the prisoner’s servant as well, and had stirred him to a holy resolve: to search his master’s table-drawers and pockets, and to hide away his papers.

Mr. Attorney-General said he was ready to hear some attempt to lessen the servant’s merit; yet, in general, he preferred him to his own brothers and sisters, and honoured him more than his father and mother. With confidence he called upon the jury to do the same. The testimony of these two witnesses, together with the documents of their discovery that would be produced, would show that the prisoner had been supplied with lists of his Majesty’s forces and of their disposition and preparation, both by sea and land; and it would leave no doubt that he had habitually conveyed such information to a hostile power."}

