{"request_id":"2249ab7a8db162b0ab10d5b1","rewritten_text":"There were few who could boast of so pure a heart, and so much genuine humility of soul, joined to a steady trust in her own integrity and a belief in that of others. From her birth she had lived a withdrawn, private life. She lost her mother when she was very young, but her father devoted himself to caring for her education—he had many peculiar ideas, which shaped the system he adopted for her. She knew well the heroes of Greece and Rome, or of England from some hundred years ago, while she was nearly ignorant of the day’s current events. She had read few authors who had written within at least the last fifty years, but—apart from that—her reading was extensive. So, although she seemed less initiated in the mysteries of life and society than he, her knowledge was deeper and rested on firmer ground; and if even her beauty and sweetness had not captivated him, her understanding would still have held him in its power. He looked up to her as his guide, and such was his devotion that he took pleasure in strengthening in his own mind the sense of inferiority she sometimes made him feel.

When he was nineteen, his mother died. He left college because of it, and after shaking off his old friends for a while, he withdrew to the neighborhood of his Diana, drawing all his comfort from her sweet voice and even dearer caresses. This brief separation from his companions gave him the courage to assert his independence. He felt that, however they might mock his intended marriage, they would not dare show their ridicule once it had happened; so, seeking the consent of his guardian—which he obtained with some difficulty—and of the father of his mistress, which was granted more easily, and without telling anyone else of his plan, by the time he reached his twentieth birthday he had become Diana’s husband.

He loved her with passion, and her tenderness had a charm for him that would not let him think of anything but her. He invited some of his college friends to come see him, but their frivolity disgusted him. Diana had torn away the veil that had kept him in boyhood: he was now a man, and he was surprised how he could ever have joined in the shallow, empty words and ideas of his fellow collegiates, or how for even a moment he had feared the judgment of people like that. He abandoned his old friendships not out of fickleness, but because they truly were unworthy of him."}

