          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"751e02ef44179944869b965b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGood-bye, Uncle George. I\u2019ll be late for lunch if I stay any longer. Thanks for telling me what I wanted to know. I always like to know everything about my new friends, and nothing about my old ones.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere are you having lunch, Harry?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt Aunt Agatha\u2019s. I\u2019ve asked myself and Mr. Gray. He\u2019s her newest prot\u00e9g\u00e9.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHumph! Tell your Aunt Agatha, Harry, not to bother me with her charity appeals anymore. I\u2019m tired of them. She really seems to think I have nothing to do but write cheques for her silly causes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right, Uncle George. I\u2019ll tell her, but it won\u2019t change anything. People who are always talking about philanthropy lose all sense of humanity. That\u2019s what makes them different.\u201d\\n\\nThe old gentleman growled in approval and rang the bell for his servant. Lord Henry walked up through the low arcade into Burlington Street, then turned toward Berkeley Square.\\n\\nSo that was the story of Dorian Gray\u2019s parentage. Even though it was told to him in a rough way, it still stirred him. It suggested a strange kind of romance, almost modern. A beautiful woman risking everything for a wild passion. A few reckless weeks of happiness cut short by a horrible, treacherous crime. Months of silent suffering, and then a child born in pain. The mother taken by death, the boy left alone under the control of an old man who had no love for him. Yes; it was an interesting background. It shaped the boy and, in a way, made him even more perfect. Behind every beautiful thing there was something tragic. Whole worlds had to be in turmoil so that even the smallest flower could bloom.... And how charming the boy had been at dinner the night before. With startled eyes and parted lips, he had sat opposite him at the club, looking frightened and pleased at the same time, while the red candle shades made the wonder on his face seem even richer. Talking to him was like playing an exquisite violin. The boy responded to every touch and every change in the music. There was something terribly gripping about the act of influencing someone. No other activity was like it. To put your own soul into some graceful form and let it stay there for a moment. To hear your own ideas reflected back to you, with the added music of passion and youth. To pass your own temperament into another person, as if it were a subtle fluid or a strange perfume. There was real joy in that\u2014maybe the greatest joy left to us in an age as limited and vulgar as our own, an age that is grossly focused on the body in its pleasures and grossly ordinary in its goals.... This was a remarkable type, too, this lad whom he had met in Basil\u2019s studio by such a strange chance, or at least could be made into a remarkable type.\"}"
