in, \u201cbut may I? _may_ I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nBefore long it began to feel needless to repeat the form of asking, so he did it at once. How he managed to do it so often without cutting himself with my knife, I can\u2019t say.\\n\\n\u201cAnd your sister,\u201d he went on, after a little steady eating, \u201cwho had the honour of bringing you up by hand! It\u2019s a sad picture to think she\u2019s no longer able to understand the honour in full. May\u2014\u201d\\n\\nI saw he was about to press on again, and I stopped him.\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019ll drink her health,\u201d said I.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d cried Mr. Pumblechook, leaning back in his chair, quite limp with admiration, \u201cthat\u2019s the way you know \u2019em, sir!\u201d (I don\u2019t know who Sir was, but it certainly wasn\u2019t I, and there was no third person there); \u201cthat\u2019s the way you know the noble-minded, sir!\u201d\"}"
