          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0971872029d22027340750d0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The chain of necessity that keeps bringing misery must have been broken, and the malignant fate that rules it would not allow this breach of her eternal laws. But why should I resent that? Misery was my element, and only what was miserable could draw near to me; if Woodville had been happy, I should never have known him. And can I\u2014who for many years have been sustained by tears, and nourished by the dew of grief\u2014stop now to tell a tale of woe and death?\\n\\nWoodville was forced to make a journey into the country, and he was kept away day after day in an exhausting absence from his lovely bride. He received a letter from her saying she was only slightly ill, but urging him to hurry to her; she told him that from his eyes she would receive health, and that his company would be her surest medicine. He was detained three days longer, and then he rushed to her. His heart, he could not say why, foretold misfortune. He had not heard from her again; he feared she might be worse, and that fear made him impatient and restless for the moment when he could see her once more, standing before him in health and beauty. Still, a sinister voice seemed always to whisper to him, \u201cYou will never more behold her as she was.\u201d\\n\\nWhen he arrived at her home, everything was silent. He moved through several rooms; in one he saw a servant weeping bitterly. He was so overcome with fear that he could hardly bring himself to ask, \u201cIs she dead?\u201d and he only listened to the terrible answer, \u201cNot yet.\u201d Those astonishing words struck him as less dreadful than what he had expected. To learn that she was still alive\u2014and that he might still hope\u2014was some relief. He remembered the words of her letter, and he let himself fall into a wild idea: that his kisses, breathing warm love and life, would infuse new spirit into her; and that with him near her, she could not die\u2014that his presence was a charm for her life.\\n\\nHe hurried to her sick room. She lay there, her cheeks burning with fever, yet her eyes were closed and she seemed senseless. He wrapped her in his arms; he pressed breathless kisses on her burning lips. He called to her in a voice of restrained anguish, using the tenderest names: \u201cReturn, Elinor; I am with you; your life, your love. Return, dearest one\u2014you promised me this boon, that I should bring you health. Let your sweet spirit revive; you cannot die near me. What is death? To see you no more? To be parted from what is a part of myself\u2014without whom I have no memory and no future?\u201d\"}"
