{"request_id":"b3f7748098237f77bc4242d6","rewritten_text":"I came upon it, and a light breeze soon coaxed it into a flame. I noticed the same thing again, and I contrived a kind of fan made of branches, which stirred the embers back to life when they were almost gone. When night returned, I was pleased to find that the fire gave light as well as warmth, and that this discovery served me well in my meals. For I found that some of the scraps the travellers had left behind had been roasted, and they tasted far more savoury than the berries I gathered from the trees. I therefore set myself to cook my food in the same fashion, laying it on the live embers. The berries, however, spoiled by the process, while the nuts and roots came out much better.

“Food, though, grew scarce, and I would often spend the entire day searching in vain for a few acorns to quiet the pangs of hunger. When I did manage to find them, I resolved to leave the place I had hitherto lived in, and to look for another where my few needs might be met more easily. In making this change, I lamented bitterly the loss of the fire I had obtained by accident, and I did not know how to bring it back. I devoted several hours to thinking seriously over the matter, but I was forced to abandon any attempt to recover it; then, wrapping myself in my cloak, I crossed the wood toward the setting sun. I wandered for three days, and at last I reached open country. A heavy fall of snow had come down the night before, and the fields lay in one unbroken whiteness; the scene was bleak, and I felt my feet grow chilled by the cold, damp covering of the ground.

“It was about seven in the morning, and I longed for food and shelter. At length I saw a small hut on rising ground, which must have been built for the convenience of some shepherd. It was a new sight to me, and I studied the building with great curiosity. Finding the door standing open, I went in. An old man was seated there near a fire, preparing his breakfast over it. He turned at the sound, and when he saw me he shrieked loudly, then left the hut and fled across the fields with a speed that his worn-out frame could hardly seem to allow. His look was unlike anything I had ever seen, and his flight somewhat astonished me. Yet I was taken with the hut itself: here the snow and rain could not get in; the ground was dry; and it seemed to me then as exquisite and heavenly a refuge as Pandæmonium appeared to the dæmons of hell after their sufferings in the lake of fire.”"}

