          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f5ffe7ba587c0d28b395ea90\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShucks, Tom,\u201d I says, \u201cI reckon you could tell a person. What\u2019s a bar sinister?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, _I_ don\u2019t know. But he\u2019s got to have it. All the nobility does.\u201d\\n\\nThat was just the way he carried on. If it didn\u2019t suit him to explain something to you, he wouldn\u2019t. You could pester him for a week and it wouldn\u2019t make no difference.\\n\\nHe\u2019d already got all that coat of arms business squared away, so now he went on to finish the rest of that work\u2014planning out a mournful inscription, the kind Jim was supposed to have, same as they all done. He made up a bunch, wrote them out on paper, and read them off, like so:\\n\\n1. _Here a captive heart busted._\\n\\n2. _Here a poor prisoner, forsook by the world and friends, fretted out his sorrowful life._\\n\\n3. _Here a lonely heart broke, and a worn spirit went to its rest, after thirty-seven years of solitary captivity._\\n\\n4. _Here, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of bitter captivity, perished a noble stranger, natural son of Louis XIV._\\n\\nTom\u2019s voice shook while he was reading them, and he pretty near fell apart. When he was done he couldn\u2019t make up his mind which one Jim ought to scratch on the wall\u2014because they were all so good\u2014but in the end he decided he\u2019d let him scratch all of them on. Jim said it would take him a year to scratch that much stuff onto the logs with a nail, and he didn\u2019t know how to make letters besides; but Tom said he\u2019d block them out for him, and then Jim wouldn\u2019t have anything to do but follow the lines. Then pretty soon Tom says:\\n\\n\u201cCome to think, the logs ain\u2019t going to do it. There ain\u2019t log walls in a dungeon. We got to dig the inscriptions into a rock. We\u2019ll fetch a rock.\u201d\\n\\nJim said a rock was worse than the logs; he said it would take him such a pison long time to dig those words into a rock he\u2019d never get out. But Tom said he\u2019d let me help him with it. Then he looked over to see how me and Jim was getting along with the pens. It was awful tedious and hard, and slow, and it didn\u2019t give my hands any chance to heal up the sores\u2014we didn\u2019t seem to make no headway at all. So Tom says:\\n\\n\u201cI know how to fix it. We got to have a rock for the coat of arms and mournful inscriptions, and we can kill two birds with that same rock.\u201d\"}"
