{"request_id":"b11d9240e1be3d79612427cd","rewritten_text":"I bored a hole in the far end of the keg, over the pitcher, and then fitted into it a plug of soft wood, shaped to a tapering, conical form. I pushed that plug in or drew it out, according to what seemed to be required, until, after trying it a few times, it reached the precise tightness where the water, oozing through the opening and dropping into the pitcher beneath, would bring the pitcher up to the brim within sixty minutes. Of course, this could be checked quickly and without trouble, simply by observing how much of the pitcher was filled in any given span of time. Once that was settled, the remainder of the scheme was plain. I arranged my bed on the car’s floor so that, when I lay down, my head would be directly under the pitcher’s mouth. It was clear that, after the hour was up, the pitcher—having filled—would overflow, and would overflow at the mouth, which sat a little below the rim. It was equally clear that water falling from a height of more than four feet could do nothing but strike my face, and that the certain result would be to rouse me at once, even from the deepest sleep imaginable.

“It was fully eleven when I finished these arrangements, and I went straight to bed, confident in the working of my contrivance. I was not disappointed. Exactly every sixty minutes my faithful chronometer brought me up; then, after I had emptied the pitcher into the bung-hole of the keg and carried out the condenser’s duties, I lay down again. These steady interruptions troubled me even less than I had expected, and when I rose at last for the day, it was seven o’clock, and the sun had climbed many degrees above the line of my horizon.

“April 3d. I found the balloon at an immense height indeed, and the earth’s apparent convexity grew in a noticeable degree. Below me, in the ocean, lay a cluster of black specks, which could only be islands. Far to the northward I could make out a thin, white, and intensely bright line, or streak, along the edge of the horizon, and I had no hesitation in thinking it the southern disk of the Polar Sea’s ice. My curiosity was strongly stirred, for I hoped to go much farther north, and might, at some point, find myself directly over the Pole. I now regretted that my great elevation would, in that case, keep me from taking as exact a survey as I wished.”"}

