{"request_id":"560372c779e3c2c59c1d7833","rewritten_text":"Elizabeth was sad and hopeless. She no longer enjoyed her usual tasks, and every kind of pleasure felt wrong to her, as if it dishonored the dead. She believed that endless grief and tears were the proper tribute to an innocence that had been ruined and destroyed. She was no longer the happy young woman who, in earlier years, had walked with me along the lake and talked with excitement about our future. The first of the sorrows meant to pull us away from the earth had reached her, and its dulling power had taken the light out of her favorite smiles.

“When I think, my dear cousin,” she said, “about the miserable death of Justine Moritz, I can’t see the world the way I used to. Before, when I read in books or heard from others about vice and injustice, I treated it as something from long ago or as imaginary evil. At least it felt distant, and easier for reason to handle than for imagination. But now suffering has come close, and men seem to me like monsters who thirst for each other’s blood. Still, I know I’m being unfair. Everyone believed that poor girl was guilty. And if she had really done the crime she was punished for, then she would have been the most depraved person alive. For a few jewels, to have murdered the son of her benefactor and friend—a child she had cared for since birth, and who she seemed to love as if he were her own! I could never accept the death of any human being. But I would certainly have thought that such a person had no place among people. Yet she was innocent. I know it. I feel it. You agree with me, and that makes me feel even more certain. Oh, Victor, when lies can look so much like the truth, how can anyone be sure of happiness? It feels as if I’m standing on the edge of a cliff, with thousands pressing in behind me, trying to push me into the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, and the killer gets away. He walks through the world free, and maybe even respected. Even if I were sentenced to die on the scaffold for the same crimes, I wouldn’t trade places with such a person.”

I listened to her words with unbearable pain. I was the true murderer—not in what I had done, but in what it caused. Elizabeth saw my suffering in my face. She took my hand gently and said, “My dearest friend, you must calm yourself. These events have hurt me, God knows how deeply, but I’m not as miserable as you are.”"}

