          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fe53155ed66c4877dc05c073\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShe would be such a delightful companion for Emma.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Woodhouse agreed, but added,\\n\\n\u201cOur little friend Harriet Smith is just as pretty and kind as any young person. You will like Harriet. Emma could not have a better companion than Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am very happy to hear it,\u201d said Emma, \u201cbut only Jane Fairfax is known to be so accomplished and superior\u2014and she is exactly Emma\u2019s age.\u201d\\n\\nThey talked about it very pleasantly, and the conversation moved on to other similar topics, all with the same easy harmony. Still, the evening did not end without a small return of worry. The gruel was brought in, and it gave them plenty to say\u2014everyone praised it, made comments, and agreed it was wholesome for every kind of person. They also made sharp remarks about the many houses where it was never served in a way that was at all tolerable. Unfortunately, among the failures Emma had to mention, the most recent\u2014and therefore the most important\u2014was in her own cook at South End. A young woman had been hired there for the time, and she never seemed to understand what Emma meant by a basin of nice, smooth gruel: thin, but not too thin. No matter how often Emma had asked for it and ordered it, the cook had never been able to make it come out right. This was a dangerous opening.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Woodhouse, shaking his head and looking at her with gentle concern. The sound he made in Emma\u2019s ear said, \u201cAh, there is no end to the sad results of your going to South End. It shouldn\u2019t be talked about.\u201d For a little while, Emma hoped he would not bring it up, and that his quiet worry might be enough to let him enjoy his own smooth gruel again. After a few minutes, however, he began,\\n\\n\u201cI shall always be very sorry that you went to the sea this autumn instead of coming here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut why should you be sorry, sir?\u201d Emma asked. \u201cI assure you it did the children a great deal of good.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd besides,\u201d Mr. Woodhouse continued, \u201cif you had to go to the sea, it should not have been to South End. South End is an unhealthy place. Perry was surprised to hear you had chosen South End.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know many people think that,\u201d Emma replied, \u201cbut it is quite a mistake. We were all perfectly healthy there, and we never had the least trouble from the mud. And Mr. Wingfield says it is entirely wrong to suppose the place is unhealthy. I am sure you can trust him, because he understands the air so well, and his own brother and family have been there again and again.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou should have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you went anywhere,\u201d said Mr. Woodhouse. \u201cPerry was there for a week once, and he thinks it is the best place of all for sea-bathing.\u201d\"}"
