          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"464ccdf6c2a7e72e7010d474\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet, despite all that, the boy only sank deeper into gloom\u2014growing more and more melancholy, wan, and cast down. She tried hot baths, sitz baths, shower baths, and even plunges. Still he stayed as cheerless as a hearse. Next she began to \u201cdoctor\u201d the water with a thin oatmeal diet and blister-plasters. She measured his \u201ccapacity\u201d the way one would judge a jug, and every day she filled him up with quack cure-alls.\\n\\nBy this time Tom had grown indifferent to persecution. That stage alarmed the old lady and set her heart in consternation. Indifference, she decided, had to be checked at any cost. Then she heard of Pain-killer for the first time. She ordered a great quantity at once. When she tasted it, she felt nothing but gratitude; it was, as she put it, fire in liquid form. She abandoned the water treatment and everything else, and pinned her hopes entirely on Pain-killer. She gave Tom a teaspoonful and waited with the keenest anxiety to see what would happen. Her worries were soothed at once, her soul settled again; for the \u201cindifference\u201d had been broken. The boy couldn\u2019t have shown a more eager, hearty interest if she\u2019d actually lit a fire under him.\\n\\nTom decided it was time to wake up. This kind of life might have seemed romantic enough for his ruined condition, but it was beginning to feel short on sentiment and long on distracting variety. So he weighed different schemes for relief, and finally settled on pretending to be fond of Pain-killer. He asked for it so often he became a nuisance, and his aunt finally told him to help himself and stop bothering her. If it had been Sid, she would have been delighted without a trace of doubt; but with Tom, she watched the bottle in secret. She saw that the medicine truly did lessen, yet it never occurred to her that the boy was using it to patch up a split in the sitting-room floor.\\n\\nOne day Tom was in the middle of dosing the crack when his aunt\u2019s yellow cat wandered in, purring and eyeing the teaspoon with greedy interest, begging for a taste. Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t ask for it unless you want it, Peter.\u201d\\n\\nBut Peter made it plain that he did want it.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019d better be sure.\u201d\\n\\nPeter was sure.\\n\\n\u201cNow that you\u2019ve asked, I\u2019ll give it to you, because there ain\u2019t anything mean about me; but if you find you don\u2019t like it, you mustn\u2019t blame anybody but your own self.\u201d\\n\\nPeter agreed. So Tom pried his mouth open and poured in the Pain-killer. Peter sprang a couple of yards into the air, then let out a war-whoop and ran round and round the room, crashing into furniture, knocking over flower-pots, and making a general mess.\"}"
