          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9a006344e4ca8aa05344ea9f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cSo, now, let\u2019s know all about it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, you see, it was like this. Old Missus\u2014Miss Watson\u2014she picks on me all the time and treats me pretty rough, but she always said she wouldn\u2019t sell me down to Orleans. Still, I noticed there was a slave trader hanging around the place a lot lately, and I started getting uneasy. Well, one night I slipped out to the door pretty late, and it wasn\u2019t quite shut. I heard Missus telling the widow she was going to sell me down to Orleans. She didn\u2019t want to, but she could get eight hundred dollars for me, and it was such a big pile of money she couldn\u2019t resist. The widow tried to get her to promise she wouldn\u2019t do it, but I didn\u2019t wait to hear the rest. I took off fast, I can tell you.\\n\\n\u201cI got out and headed down the hill. I was going to steal a skiff along the shore somewhere above town, but people were still moving around, so I hid in the old tumble-down cooper shop on the bank and waited for everyone to go away. I stayed there all night. There was somebody around all the time. Sometime around six in the morning the skiffs started passing, and about eight or nine every skiff that went by was talking about how your father came over to town and said you\u2019d been killed. Those last skiffs were full of ladies and gentlemen going over to see the place. Sometimes they\u2019d pull up at the shore and take a rest before they started across, so from all the talk I learned everything about the killing. I\u2019m mighty sorry you\u2019re killed, Huck, but I ain\u2019t no more.\\n\\n\u201cI lay there under the shavings all day. I was hungry, but I wasn\u2019t scared, because I knew old Missus and the widow were going to start off to the camp meeting right after breakfast and be gone all day. They know I go off with the cattle at daylight, so they wouldn\u2019t expect to see me around the place, and they wouldn\u2019t miss me until after dark in the evening. The other servants wouldn\u2019t miss me either, because they\u2019d slip out and take a holiday as soon as the old folks were out of the way.\\n\\n\u201cWell, when it got dark I went out up the river road and walked about two miles or more to where there weren\u2019t any houses. I\u2019d already decided what I was going to do. You see, if I kept trying to get away on foot, the dogs would track me. And if I stole a skiff to cross, they\u2019d notice that skiff was missing, you see, and they\u2019d know where I landed on the other side and where to pick up my trail.\"}"
