          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b3c265af518190fe0d861955\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mental traits spoken of as analytical are, by their very nature, scarcely open to being analyzed. We come to know them chiefly through what they produce. Among other things, we observe that, when a person indulges them immoderately, they become for their owner a wellspring of the keenest pleasure. Just as the robust man rejoices in his bodily powers, taking delight in exercises that set his muscles working, so the analyst exults in that moral labor which _disentangles_. He finds satisfaction even in the smallest employments, so long as his gift can be brought to bear. He is drawn to riddles, conundrums, and hieroglyphics, and in solving each he shows a degree of _acumen_ that seems, to ordinary understanding, almost uncanny. His outcomes\u2014shaped by the very soul and substance of method\u2014carry, in truth, the look of something like intuition.\\n\\nThe power of re-solution may be greatly sharpened by mathematical study, and especially by the highest branch of it which, unfairly and merely because it works backward, has been dubbed, as if _par excellence_, analysis. Still, to calculate is not, in itself, to analyze. A chess-player, for instance, can do one thing without the other. It follows that chess, in the way it affects mental character, is widely misread. I am not setting out to write a treatise; I am only introducing a somewhat singular narrative with remarks offered at random. I will therefore take the liberty of saying that the higher faculties of the reflective mind are more distinctly and more profitably engaged by the plain, unassuming game of draughts than by all the elaborate, empty play of chess. In the latter, where the pieces move in different and _bizarre_ ways and carry various, shifting values, what is merely complicated is taken for what is profound (a mistake not uncommon). Here the _attention_ is brought powerfully into action. If it slips for even a moment, an oversight follows, and injury or defeat is the result. Since the possible moves are not only numerous but also intricate, the likelihood of such oversights is increased; and in nine cases out of ten it is the more concentrating, rather than the more keenly acute, player who wins. In draughts, however, where the moves are _unique_ and allow little variation, the chances of inadvertence are reduced, and with attention left comparatively idle, the advantage each side gains comes from superior _acumen_. To make it less abstract, suppose a game of draughts reduced to four kings, where, naturally, no oversight is to be expected. Then, with the players otherwise equal, it is plain that victory can be decided only by some _recherch\u00e9_ maneuver\u2014the product of a vigorous exertion of the intellect.\"}"
