          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fe2004c0950169e44d484bf6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 12th. A remarkable change occurred in the balloon\u2019s direction, and though I had fully expected it, nothing could have given me more certain pleasure. After reaching, along its former path, about the twentieth parallel of southern latitude, it suddenly swung off at a sharp angle toward the east, and so went on through the day, keeping nearly\u2014if not entirely\u2014in the same plane as the lunar ellipse. Worth noting, the car showed a very noticeable trembling as a result of this altered route; the motion continued, in greater or lesser degree, for many hours.\\n\\n\u201cApril 13th. I was again greatly alarmed by the loud, crackling noise that had frightened me on the tenth. I thought it over at length, yet could reach no satisfying conclusion. The earth\u2019s apparent size kept shrinking; from the balloon it now subtended an angle a little over twenty-five degrees. The moon could not be seen at all, since it lay almost in my zenith. I remained in the ellipse\u2019s plane, but I made little headway to the east.\\n\\n\u201cApril 14th. The earth\u2019s diameter diminished with extraordinary speed. Today I became strongly convinced that the balloon was now actually running along the line of apsides toward the point of perigee\u2014in other words, maintaining the direct course that would carry it at once to the moon at that portion of its orbit nearest the earth. The moon was directly overhead and therefore out of my sight. There was great and prolonged labor required for the condensation of the atmosphere.\\n\\n\u201cApril 15th. Even the outlines of continents and seas could no longer be traced on the earth with anything like distinctness. About twelve o\u2019clock I became aware, for the third time, of that dreadful sound which had so startled me before. This time, however, it lasted for some moments and grew louder as it continued. At last, while I stood, dazed and terrified, waiting\u2014though for what I could not say\u2014the car shook with extreme violence, and a vast, blazing mass of some substance, which I could not make out, swept past the balloon with a voice like a thousand thunders, roaring and booming overhead. When my fears and astonishment had somewhat settled, I found little difficulty in believing it to be some great volcanic fragment hurled from the world I was approaching so rapidly, and, most likely, one of those peculiar materials sometimes gathered on the earth and called meteoric stones for want of a better name.\\n\\n\u201cApril 16th. Today, looking upward as best I could through each of the side windows in turn, I saw, to my great delight, a very small part of the moon\u2019s disk protruding, as it were, on every side beyond the enormous rim of the balloon.\u201d\"}"
