{"request_id":"362425a598ed206b47b7fee8","rewritten_text":"He said that he was wearing away his time in that place without purpose, and that letters from the friends he had made in London urged his return so he could complete the negotiation they had begun for his Indian venture. He could no longer delay his departure; yet, since his trip to London might be followed—perhaps even sooner than he now guessed—by his longer voyage, he begged me to give him as much of my company as I could spare. He therefore urged me to leave my solitary island and meet him at Perth, so that we might go south together. This letter, in some measure, brought me back to life, and I decided to leave my island after two days.

But before I departed, there was a task I had to carry out—one that made me shudder to think about. I had to pack up my chemical instruments, and to do that I had to enter the room that had been the scene of my hateful work, and handle those tools whose very sight sickened me. The next morning, at daybreak, I gathered enough courage and unlocked the door of my laboratory. The remains of the half-finished creature, whom I had destroyed, lay scattered across the floor, and I almost felt as though I had torn apart living human flesh. I paused to steady myself, then went into the chamber. With a trembling hand, I carried the instruments out of the room; but I told myself I shouldn’t leave the relics of my work where they might stir up the peasants’ horror and suspicion. So I put them into a basket, along with a large quantity of stones, and, having arranged them, I decided to throw them into the sea that very night. In the meantime, I sat on the beach, cleaning and arranging my chemical apparatus.

Nothing could be more complete than the change that had taken place in my feelings since the night the dæmon appeared. Before, I had viewed my promise with grim despair, as something that—whatever the consequences—had to be carried out; but now it felt as if a film had been lifted from my eyes, and that for the first time I saw clearly. The thought of resuming my labors never once crossed my mind. The threat I had heard weighed on my thoughts, but I did not consider that a voluntary act of mine could prevent it. I had already decided, in my own mind, that creating another like the fiend I had first made would be the basest and most atrocious selfishness, and I drove from my thoughts every idea that could lead to a different conclusion."}

