          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9e0882c762124ad004858adc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGet up! What are you up to?\u201d\\n\\nI opened my eyes and looked around, trying to figure out where I was. It was after sunrise, and I\u2019d been sound asleep. Pap was standing over me, looking sour and sick, too. He said:\\n\\n\u201cWhat were you doing with this gun?\u201d\\n\\nI figured he didn\u2019t know anything about what he\u2019d been doing, so I said:\\n\\n\u201cSomebody tried to get in, so I was waiting for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy didn\u2019t you wake me up?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI tried, but I couldn\u2019t. I couldn\u2019t move you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, all right. Don\u2019t stand there talking all day\u2014get out and see if there\u2019s a fish on the line for breakfast. I\u2019ll be along in a minute.\u201d\\n\\nHe unlocked the door, and I headed out along the riverbank. I noticed bits of limbs and other things floating down, and a scattering of bark, so I knew the river had started to rise. I figured I\u2019d have a great time now if I was over in town. The June rise used to always be good luck for me\u2014because as soon as the water starts rising here comes cordwood floating down, and pieces of log rafts, sometimes a dozen logs together. All you have to do is catch them and sell them to the wood yards and the sawmill.\\n\\nI walked up the bank with one eye out for Pap and the other watching what the rise might bring in. Then, all at once, a canoe came drifting in\u2014what a beauty, too, about thirteen or fourteen feet long, riding high like a duck. I launched myself headfirst off the bank like a frog, clothes and all, and paddled hard for the canoe. I expected someone might be lying in it to fool people, because sometimes when a man pulled a skiff out, they\u2019d pop up and laugh at him. But that wasn\u2019t what happened this time. It was a real drift-canoe, and I climbed in and paddled it ashore.\\n\\nI thought, the old man will be glad when he sees this\u2014it\u2019s worth ten dollars. But when I got to shore, Pap wasn\u2019t in sight yet. As I was pushing the canoe into a little creek like a narrow gully, all tangled with vines and willows, another idea hit me: I figured I\u2019d hide it well. Then, instead of running off into the woods when I got away, I\u2019d go down the river about fifty miles and camp in one place for good, so I wouldn\u2019t have to keep dealing with the rough time of walking on foot.\\n\\nIt was pretty close to the shanty, and I thought I could hear the old man coming the whole time. But I got the canoe hidden, then stepped out and looked around through the willows\u2014and there was the old man down the path a ways, aiming his gun at a bird, drawing a bead.\"}"
