ery could spring from love; and this lesson\u2014one that all at last must learn\u2014was taught me in a way that few are forced to endure. I lament now, and must always lament, those few short months of blissful Paradise. I broke no command; I ate no apple; and still I was driven from it without mercy. Alas! my companion did, and I was hurled down with him. But I stray from my account\u2014let sorrow arrive when its appointed time comes; at this point in my story I may still speak of happiness.\\n\\nThree months passed in this delightful companionship, when my aunt fell ill. I spent a whole month in her chamber, nursing her, yet her sickness was mortal, and she died, leaving me for some time inconsolable. Death is so dreadful to the living; the chains of habit are so strong even when affection does not bind them, that the heart must be tormented when they snap.\"}"
