          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d9b4f2a67160fffaf5cebc87\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was a wonderful feeling of tidying up and then taking a quiet pause before going on with life again, in our village on Saturday nights. It made Joe dare to stay out half an hour longer on Saturdays than he did at other times. When that half-hour was up, and the rum and water ran out together, Joe stood up to go and took me by the hand.\\n\\n\u201cWait just half a moment, Mr. Gargery,\u201d said the strange man. \u201cI think I\u2019ve got a bright new shilling somewhere in my pocket, and if I do, the boy gets it.\u201d\\n\\nHe dug it out from a handful of small change, folded it up in some crumpled paper, and handed it to me. \u201cIt\u2019s yours!\u201d he said. \u201cMind\u2014your own.\u201d\\n\\nI thanked him, staring at him well beyond what good manners would allow, and holding tight to Joe. He said good-night to Joe, and good-night to Mr. Wopsle (who went out with us), and to me he gave only a look from his aiming eye\u2014no, not a look, because he shut it up; but wonders can be done with an eye when you hide it.\\n\\nOn the way home, if I\u2019d been in the mood to talk, the conversation would have been all on my side, because Mr. Wopsle left us at the door of the Jolly Bargemen, and Joe went all the way home with his mouth wide open, trying to rinse the rum out with as much air as possible. But I was so stunned by the sudden return of my old wrongdoing and my old acquaintance that I couldn\u2019t think of anything else.\\n\\nWhen we presented ourselves in the kitchen, my sister wasn\u2019t in a particularly bad temper, and Joe was encouraged by that unusual fact to tell her about the bright shilling. \u201cA bad one, I\u2019ll bet,\u201d said Mrs. Joe triumphantly. \u201cOr he wouldn\u2019t have given it to the boy! Let\u2019s look at it.\u201d\\n\\nI took it out of the paper, and it turned out to be a good one. \u201cBut what\u2019s this?\u201d said Mrs. Joe, dropping the shilling and snatching up the paper. \u201cTwo two-pound notes?\u201d\\n\\nNothing less than two thick, sweaty one-pound notes, as if they\u2019d been on the closest terms with every cattle-market in the county. Joe grabbed his hat again and ran to the Jolly Bargemen to return them to their owner. While he was gone, I sat down on my usual stool and stared vacantly at my sister, pretty sure the man wouldn\u2019t be there.\\n\\nPresently, Joe came back, saying the man was gone, but that Joe had left word at the Three Jolly Bargemen about the notes.\"}"
