          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"28d6b2d4701bef5320f00de5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI don\u2019t have the nerves for that kind of thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut could you really be comfortable, spending the whole evening away from the poor boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. You see his father can do it, so why shouldn\u2019t I? Jemima is so careful, and she could send us word every hour about how he is. I really think Charles might as well have told his father that we would all come. I\u2019m not more worried about little Charles now than Charles is. I was terribly worried yesterday, but today is different.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, if you don\u2019t think it\u2019s too late to give notice for yourself, suppose you go too, along with your husband. Leave little Charles to my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove can\u2019t think it wrong as long as I stay with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you serious?\u201d Mary cried, her eyes brightening. \u201cDear me! That\u2019s a very good idea\u2014an excellent one, indeed. I might as well go as not, because I\u2019m no use at home, am I? It only makes me more restless. You, who don\u2019t have a mother\u2019s feelings, are much more suited for this. You can make little Charles do anything; he always listens to you when you speak. It will be much better than leaving him only with Jemima. Oh, I will certainly go. I\u2019m sure I ought to, if I can, just as much as Charles, because they want me to meet Captain Wentworth, and I don\u2019t mind being left alone. What a wonderful thought, Anne. I\u2019ll go tell Charles and get ready right away. You can send for us at any moment if anything goes wrong, but I don\u2019t think there will be anything to worry you. I wouldn\u2019t go at all if I didn\u2019t feel fairly at ease about my dear child.\u201d\\n\\nThe next moment she was tapping at her husband\u2019s dressing-room door. As Anne followed her upstairs, she arrived in time to hear the whole conversation. It began with Mary speaking in a tone full of excitement.\\n\\n\u201cI mean to go with you, Charles, because I\u2019m no more use at home than you are. If I were to shut myself up forever with the child, I wouldn\u2019t be able to make him do anything he doesn\u2019t want. Anne will stay. Anne will take care of him at home. It\u2019s Anne\u2019s own idea, so I\u2019ll go with you, which will be much better, because I haven\u2019t dined at the other house since Tuesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is very kind of Anne,\u201d her husband answered. \u201cI would be glad for you to go, but it seems rather unfair that she should be left at home by herself to nurse our sick child.\u201d\"}"
