          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"628d34252d2c99838cff5b2d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat\u2019s wrong?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTen o\u2019clock, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTen o\u2019clock\u2014at night?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, sir. Your honour told me to come and fetch you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I see. Very good, very good.\u201d\\n\\nAfter a few lifeless attempts to get back to sleep\u2014efforts the man skillfully thwarted by keeping the fire stirred for five minutes\u2014he rose, flung his hat on, and went out. He turned into the Temple, and, having set himself right again by pacing twice along the King\u2019s Bench-walk and the Paper-buildings, he made for the Stryver rooms.\\n\\nThe Stryver clerk, who never took part in these meetings, had already gone home, and the Stryver principal opened the door. He was in his slippers, with a loose bed-gown on, and his throat was uncovered for his comfort. There was that rather wild, strained, scorched look about the eyes\u2014something to be seen in all the free livers of his set, from Jeffries\u2019 portrait down\u2014and it could be traced, under all manner of artistic disguises, through the portraits of every Drinking Age.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re a bit late, Memory,\u201d said Stryver.\\n\\n\u201cAbout the usual time; perhaps a quarter of an hour later.\u201d\\n\\nThey went into a dingy room, lined with books and strewn with papers, where a fire was burning. A kettle hissed on the hob, and amid the ruin of papers a table gleamed, well supplied with wine, and brandy, and rum, and sugar, and lemons.\\n\\n\u201cI take it you\u2019ve had your bottle, Sydney,\u201d said Stryver.\\n\\n\u201cTwo tonight, I suppose. I\u2019ve been dining with the day\u2019s client\u2014or else watching him dine; it\u2019s much the same!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat was a fine stroke, Sydney, the way you handled the identification. How did it come to you? When did it first occur?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI figured he was a pretty handsome sort, and I thought I\u2019d