          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"38350597f090005633e8f83d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I can\u2019t fully describe the joy I felt when I left behind the noisy fights of politics at home, and the physical suffering I had seen in faraway places, and turned instead to my own dear home\u2014the place chosen for goodness and love. I felt peace there, and the chance to share every sacred feeling with others. If I had never left Windsor, these emotions wouldn\u2019t have been so strong. But in Greece I had been trapped by fear and by terrible change. In Greece, after a time of worry and sorrow, I watched two people leave\u2014people whose names alone stood for greatness and virtue. Still, none of those troubles could reach the family life I had left behind. While we lived quietly in our beloved forest, shut away from the world, we were safe.\\n\\nOf course, time brought small changes, as it always does. It left signs of how mortal we are on our pleasures and hopes. Idris\u2014my most loving wife, sister, and friend\u2014was a tender, affectionate mother. Her love was not like a casual hobby for her, as it is for many. It was a true passion. We had three children. One of them, the second oldest, died while I was in Greece. That loss crushed the proud joy of motherhood and filled me and her with grief and fear. Before that tragedy, the little ones she had borne\u2014the young heirs of her brief life\u2014seemed to have a secure future. Now she feared that the cruel destroyer might take her remaining children, just as it had taken their brother.\\n\\nThe smallest illness brought her terror. She was miserable whenever she was away from them. Her happiness was built on their fragile lives, and she kept watch constantly, afraid that the same thief might steal those precious treasures again. Luckily, she had little reason to fear. Alfred was now nine years old. He was upright and strong in spirit, with a bright forehead, soft eyes, and a gentle but independent nature. Our youngest was still an infant, but his cheeks were smooth and healthy, touched with the pink of good health, and his constant energy filled our halls with innocent laughter.\\n\\nClara had passed the age when her quiet ignorance would have caused Idris to worry. Clara was loved by Idris, and by everyone. She had so much intelligence mixed with innocence, so much feeling mixed with restraint, and such seriousness joined to perfect good humor. Her beauty was extraordinary, and it came with such simple, lovable ways that she seemed like a pearl kept in the heart of our home\u2014a treasure of wonder and excellence.\\n\\nAt the start of winter, Alfred, now nine, went to school at Eton for the first time. To him, it felt like the first real step toward manhood, and he was pleased. Studying and playing with others helped bring out the best in him. He became steady and persistent, generous, and firm in a way that was well controlled.\"}"
