          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a5b63b62022e8395e7cbb0e6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You will be glad to hear that nothing terrible has happened as I began this journey, even though you worried so much about it. I arrived here yesterday, and my first duty is to tell my dear sister that I am safe and that I feel more and more confident about the success of what I am doing.\\n\\nI am already far north of London. As I walk through the streets of Petersburgh, a cold northern wind touches my cheeks. It steadies my nerves and makes me feel happy. Do you know that feeling? This breeze has come from the far regions I am moving toward, and it gives me a preview of those freezing places. Because of this promising wind, my daydreams grow stronger and clearer. I cannot convince myself that the pole is only a place of ice and emptiness. Instead, it always appears to me as a land of beauty and joy. There, Margaret, the sun stays in view all the time. Its bright disk sits just above the horizon and spreads a constant, dazzling light. There, with your permission, my sister, I will trust what earlier sailors said\u2014there, snow and frost are gone. And if we sail over a calm sea, we may be carried to a land that is more wonderful and beautiful than anything anyone has found so far on the habitable earth. Its plants and features may be unlike anything else, just as the wonders of the sky must be unlike anything in those still unknown regions. What could we not expect in a country of endless daylight? I may discover the strange power that draws the compass needle, and I may also be able to make a thousand observations of the heavens that would only make sense after this one voyage, so that their apparent oddities stay consistent forever. I will satisfy my eager curiosity by seeing part of the world no one has visited before, and I may walk on land that no human foot has ever touched. These are the reasons that pull me forward, and they are enough to drive away all fear of danger or death. They make me start this hard journey with the same joy a child feels when he sets off in a small boat with friends on an expedition to explore his home river.\\n\\nBut even if all my guesses are wrong, you still cannot deny the great benefit I will bring to all people, even for the last generation. I will either find a route near the pole to reach those countries that now take so many months to get to, or I will learn the secret of magnetism\u2014something that, if it is possible at all, can only be discovered through an undertaking like mine.\\n\\nThese thoughts have calmed the worry I felt when I began my letter. I feel my heart warm with excitement, as if it lifts me toward heaven. Nothing helps the mind as much as a steady purpose\u2014something the soul can fix its attention on, like a clear point for the mind to look at.\"}"
