          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d1ee493366256b8eadab3511\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At dinner, Mr. Bennet hardly said a word; but once the servants had withdrawn, he judged it the right moment to speak with his visitor, and so he began a topic on which he expected him to shine\u2014remarking that he seemed extremely fortunate in his patroness. Lady Catherine de Bourgh\u2019s attention to his wishes, and her care for his comfort, struck him as especially noteworthy. Mr. Bennet could not have picked a better subject. Mr. Collins grew eloquent in praising her. The matter put him into a more than ordinary gravity of manner, and with a most consequential air he declared that he had never, in all his life, seen such conduct in any person of rank\u2014so much affability and condescension as he had personally experienced from Lady Catherine. She had been gracious enough to approve of both sermons he had already had the honour of delivering before her. She had also asked him twice to dine at Rosings, and had sent for him only the Saturday before, so that he might make up her party for quadrille in the evening. Lady Catherine was thought proud by many people, he knew, but he had never observed anything in her except affability. She had always spoken to him as she would to any other gentleman; she raised not the slightest objection to his mixing with the neighbourhood, nor to his leaving his parish now and then for a week or two to see his relations. She had even condescended to advise him to marry as soon as he could, as long as he chose with discretion; and she had once paid him a visit at his modest parsonage, where she had approved every alteration he had been making, and had even deigned to suggest a few herself\u2014some shelves in the upstairs closets.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s all very proper and civil, I\u2019m sure,\u201d said Mrs. Bennet. \u201cAnd I dare say she\u2019s a very agreeable woman. It\u2019s a pity that great ladies in general aren\u2019t more like her. Does she live near you, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe garden where my humble home stands is separated from Rosings Park by nothing more than a lane\u2014her Ladyship\u2019s residence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI thought you said she was a widow, sir. Has she any family?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe has only one daughter\u2014the heiress of Rosings, and possessed of very extensive property.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh,\u201d cried Mrs. Bennet, shaking her head, \u201cthen she\u2019s better off than many girls. And what kind of young lady is she? Is she handsome?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe is, indeed, a most charming young lady. Lady Catherine herself says that, in point of true beauty, Miss de Bourgh is far superior to the handsomest of her sex; for there is something in her features that marks her as a young woman of distinguished birth.\u201d\"}"
