          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"682fabb9514daeb02319fac2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In truth, he had been completely unaware of any influence he might have had. He followed her into the shrubbery without any plan to try anything. He had come, driven by anxiety to see how she handled Frank Churchill\u2019s engagement, with no selfish motive at all\u2014indeed, with no clear motive beyond wanting, if she gave him the chance, to calm her or offer advice. What happened next was simply the result of the moment and of what he heard right then. Her confident assurance that she felt no interest in Frank Churchill\u2014her heart being entirely free of him\u2014gave rise to the hope that, over time, he might win her affection himself. But at that instant, he did not even think in terms of time; in the rush of eagerness over good judgment, he only wanted to be told that she would not forbid him to try to win her. The hopes that opened up to him gradually were even more thrilling. The affection he had been asking to be allowed to create was already his. Within half an hour, he had gone from being deeply distressed to something so close to perfect happiness that it could not be called anything else.\\n\\n_Her_ change matched his. This same half-hour gave each of them the precious certainty of being loved, and removed from each the same ignorance, jealousy, or distrust. On his side, there had been jealousy for a long time, starting with Frank Churchill\u2019s arrival\u2014or even with the expectation of it. He had been in love with Emma and jealous of Frank Churchill from about the same period, and one feeling likely helped him understand the other. It was his jealousy of Frank Churchill that made him leave the country. The Box Hill party decided him to go away. He wanted to protect himself from having to witness again those attentions that were allowed and encouraged. He had gone to learn to be indifferent. But he had gone to the wrong place. There was too much domestic happiness in his brother\u2019s house; the women there wore too pleasant a face; Isabella was too much like Emma, differing only in those obvious, lesser faults that always made the other seem brighter to him, even if he had had more time to do something about it. Still, he stayed on, day after day, until this very morning\u2019s post brought him the story of Jane Fairfax. Then, with the gladness he had every right to feel\u2014gladness he did not hesitate to admit, since he had never believed Frank Churchill deserved Emma\u2014he found that there was so much real concern for her, so much sharp anxiety on her behalf, that he could not stay any longer. He rode home through the rain, and after dinner he went straight to see how this best and sweetest of all creatures, faultless despite all her faults, handled the discovery.\"}"
