          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6c9a6a3412604a54a20c2563\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cJupiter,\u201d said he, as we reached the base, \u201ccome here! Tell me\u2014was the skull fixed to the limb with the face turned outward, or turned toward the limb?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDe face was out, massa, so de crows could get at de eyes good, widout any trouble.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen which eye was it\u2014this one or that one\u2014through which you let the beetle drop?\u201d Here Legrand touched each of Jupiter\u2019s eyes in turn.\\n\\n\u201c\u2019Twas dis eye, massa\u2014de lef eye\u2014jis as you tell me,\u201d and at that the negro pointed out his right eye.\\n\\n\u201cThat will do. We\u2019ll have to try it again.\u201d\\n\\nHere my friend\u2014whose madness I had begun to see, or thought I saw, showed some sort of method\u2014pulled out the peg that marked where the beetle had fallen, and set it down about three inches farther west than before. Then, taking the tape measure from the nearest point of the trunk to the peg as we had done earlier, and carrying the line straight on for fifty feet, we were led to a spot that lay several yards away from where we\u2019d been digging.\\n\\nA circle, a bit larger than the last, was drawn around this new place, and we started in again with the spades. I was terribly tired, yet, without quite knowing what had brought about the shift in my thoughts, I no longer felt any strong dislike for the work. I had become, in the strangest way, absorbed\u2014indeed, even stirred. Maybe it was something in all the wildness of Legrand\u2014some hint of forethought, some deliberation\u2014that carried me along. I dug with eagerness, and now and then I caught myself actually staring, with an expression very like expectation, for the imagined treasure\u2014the very vision that had driven my poor companion out of his mind. At a time when these fancies held me most completely, and after we had been at it perhaps an hour and a half, we were cut off