          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0851110f9363b2b7f4dd3f82\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Biddy cried; the darkening garden, the lane, and the stars that were beginning to show were blurred in my own sight.\\n\\n\u201cNothing was ever discovered, Biddy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo you know what has become of Orlick?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019d say, from the color of his clothes, that he\u2019s working in the quarries.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen you\u2019ve seen him?\u2014Why are you staring at that dark tree in the lane?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI saw him there, on the night she died.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat wasn\u2019t the last time either, Biddy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo; I\u2019ve seen him there since we\u2019ve been walking here. \u2014It\u2019s no use,\u201d said Biddy, laying her hand on my arm as I was about to run out. \u201cYou know I wouldn\u2019t deceive you; he wasn\u2019t there for a minute, and he\u2019s gone.\u201d\\n\\nIt flared my anger to realize that this man was still pursuing her, and I felt a deep, stubborn hatred toward him. I told her so, and told her that I would spend any money or take any trouble to drive him out of that country. Bit by bit, she brought the conversation back to calmer ground, and she told me how Joe loved me, and how Joe never complained about anything\u2014she didn\u2019t say anything about me; she didn\u2019t need to; I understood what she meant. He always did his duty in his own way of life, with a firm hand, a quiet tongue, and a gentle heart.\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, it would be hard to say too much for him,\u201d I said. \u201cAnd, Biddy, we\u2019ll have to talk about these things often, because I\u2019ll be down here a great deal now. I\u2019m not going to leave poor Joe alone.\u201d\\n\\nBiddy didn\u2019t say a single word.\\n\\n\u201cBiddy, don\u2019t you hear me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, Mr. Pip.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot to mention your calling me Mr. Pip\u2014which, to me, sounds like bad taste, Biddy\u2014what do you mean?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do I mean?\u201d Biddy asked, timidly.\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I said, in a self-righteous way, \u201cI need to know what you mean by this.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBy this?\u201d said Biddy.\\n\\n\u201cNow, don\u2019t echo,\u201d I snapped. \u201cYou didn\u2019t used to, Biddy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUsed not!\u201d said Biddy. \u201cOh, Mr. Pip! Used!\u201d\\n\\nWell! I figured I\u2019d have to give up that point too. After another silent turn in the garden, I fell back on my main argument.\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I said, \u201cI made a remark about how often I come down here to see Joe, and you responded with a very noticeable silence. Please, Biddy, tell me why.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you quite sure, then, that you WILL come to see him often?\u201d Biddy asked, stopping in the narrow garden path and looking at me under the stars with a clear, honest gaze.\"}"
