{"request_id":"7955649ba10e349356897733","rewritten_text":"Indeed, I had no fear on her account. She was what people call a notable woman, and she could handle matters in the world without my help. To be honest, I think she always saw me as an idle boy—a mere extra, good for nothing but building castles in the air—and she was rather pleased to be rid of me. It was a dark night when I said goodbye to her. Taking with me, as aides-de-camp, the three creditors who had given me so much trouble, we carried the balloon—along with the car and all the equipment—by a roundabout route to the station where the other items had been stored. There we found everything untouched, and I went straight to work.

“It was the first of April. The night, as I said before, was dark—there wasn’t a star to be seen—and a drizzling rain, coming and going, made us miserable. But my main worry was the balloon. Despite the varnish meant to protect it, it was starting to grow heavy with moisture, and the powder was also at risk of being damaged. So I kept the three duns busy with relentless diligence: pounding ice around the central cask and stirring the acid in the others. They didn’t stop, though, from badgering me with questions about what I planned to do with all this apparatus, and they made it clear they were unhappy with the terrible labor I was forcing on them. They couldn’t see, as they put it, what good could come from getting soaked to the skin just to play a part in such horrible incantations. I began to feel uneasy. I truly believed the idiots thought I had made a deal with the devil, and that what I was doing now was no better than it should be. So I was afraid they would abandon me altogether. Still, I managed to calm them by promising to pay every debt in full as soon as I could bring the current business to an end. Of course, they interpreted my words in their own way—assuming, no doubt, that in any case I would end up with a huge amount of ready money. And if I paid them everything I owed, plus a little extra for their trouble, I dare say they cared very little what happened to my soul or my body.

“In about four and a half hours, I found the balloon inflated enough. I attached the car, then, and put all my tools in it—without forgetting the condensing apparatus, a plentiful supply of water, and a large quantity of provisions, such as pemmican, which contains a lot of nourishment in comparatively little bulk.”"}

