          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2ad1304bdea501b708dda1c3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The following day the ghost was faint with weariness. The dreadful excitement of the past four weeks was at last beginning to tell upon him; his nerves were utterly in pieces, and he started at the smallest sound. For five days he kept to his room, and then, after much turning over the matter, decided to abandon the whole business of the blood-stain on the library floor. If the Otis family did not want it, then, plainly, they did not deserve it. They seemed to him to live on a low, material level, and to be quite unable to grasp the symbolic force of sensuous appearances. The matter of phantasmal manifestations, and the shaping of astral bodies, was another question altogether\u2014indeed, something beyond his own control. His duty, solemn and fixed, was to show himself in the corridor once a week, and to gibber from the great oriel window on the first and third Wednesday of each month; and he could not see how he might, with any propriety, slip out of such obligations.\\n\\nIt was true enough that his life had been wicked, yet he was, on the other hand, scrupulous in everything connected with the supernatural. Accordingly, for the next three Saturdays, he went through the corridor as usual between midnight and three o\u2019clock, taking every precaution so that he should neither be heard nor seen. He took off his boots, stepped as lightly as he could on the old worm-eaten boards, drew a heavy black velvet cloak about him, and made sure to apply the Rising Sun Lubricator when it came to oiling his chains. I must admit it was no small struggle for him to bring himself to adopt this last safeguard. Still, one night\u2014while the family were at dinner\u2014he slipped into Mr. Otis\u2019s bedroom and made off with the bottle. At first he felt a little ashamed of himself, but later he was sensible enough to recognize that there was a great deal to be said for the invention, and that, to a certain extent, it served his turn.\\n\\nYet even so, he was not left in peace. Strings were constantly stretched across the corridor; he caught his foot on them in the dark, and on one occasion, when he was dressed for the part of \u201cBlack Isaac, or the Huntsman of Hogley Woods,\u201d he came down hard after stepping on a butter-slide. The twins had laid it from the entrance of the Tapestry Chamber up to the top of the oak staircase. This fresh affront so inflamed him that he resolved to make one last attempt to assert his dignity and social standing, and he determined to call upon the impertinent young Etonians the next night in his famous character of \u201cReckless Rupert, or the Headless Earl.\u201d\\n\\nHe had not worn that disguise for more than seventy years; in truth, not since it had so frightened pretty Lady Barbara Modish. By means of it she had broken off her engagement with the present Lord Canterville\u2019s grandfather, and fled to Gretna Green with handsome Jack Castleton, declaring that nothing in the world would ever persuade her to marry into a family that permitted so dreadful a phantom to pace the terrace at twilight.\"}"
