          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d6a2c481e233138c9eae7261\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was in Warwick Castle that I came across the curious stranger I\u2019m going to talk about. He drew me in for three reasons: his candid simplicity, his marvelous familiarity with ancient armor, and the calm, restful feeling of his company\u2014for he did all the talking. We fell into step together, as modest people do, at the back of the group being led through, and he immediately began saying things that genuinely interested me. As he spoke\u2014softly, pleasantly, smoothly\u2014he seemed to drift, almost without my noticing, out of this world and out of its time, and into some far-off era and an old, forgotten country. Slowly he wove such a spell around me that I felt as if I were moving among the specters and shadows and dust and mold of a gray antiquity, holding conversation with something from it! Just as I would talk about my closest personal friends or enemies, or my most familiar neighbors, he spoke of Sir Bedivere, Sir Bors de Ganis, Sir Launcelot of the Lake, Sir Galahad, and all the other great names of the Round Table\u2014and how old, old, unspeakably old, and faded, and dry, and musty, and ancient he seemed to become as he went on.\\n\\nAt last he turned to me and said, as casually as one might mention the weather\u2014or any other ordinary subject\u2014\\n\\n\u201cDo you know about the transmigration of souls? Do you know about the transposition of epochs\u2014and bodies?\u201d\\n\\nI told him I hadn\u2019t heard of it. He was so little concerned\u2014just as people are when they talk about the weather\u2014that he didn\u2019t seem to notice whether I answered him or not. There was a brief half-moment of silence, immediately broken by the droning voice of the paid cicerone:\\n\\n\u201cAncient hauberk, dating from the sixth century, time of King Arthur and the Round Table; said to have belonged to the knight Sir Sagramor le Desirous; observe the round hole through the chain-mail in the left breast; can\u2019t be accounted for; supposed to have been made with a bullet since the invention of firearms\u2014perhaps maliciously by Cromwell\u2019s soldiers.\u201d\\n\\nMy acquaintance smiled\u2014not a modern smile, but one that must have gone out of common use many, many centuries ago\u2014and muttered, as if to himself:\\n\\n\u201cWit ye well, I saw it done.\u201d Then, after a pause, he added: \u201cI did it myself.\u201d\\n\\nBy the time I had recovered from the electric shock of that remark, he was gone.\\n\\nAll that evening I sat by my fire at the Warwick Arms, sunk in a dream of the olden time, while the rain hammered at the windows and the wind roared around the eaves and corners. From time to time I dipped into old Sir Thomas Malory\u2019s enchanting book, feeding on its rich feast of prodigies and adventures, breathing in the fragrance of its obsolete names, and dreaming again.\"}"
