          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dc90f2d2949a62a467cedbb1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI hope I shall soon have the pleasure of introducing my son to you,\u201d said Mr. Weston.\\n\\nMrs. Elton, very willing to assume that this hope was meant as a compliment to her, smiled most graciously.\\n\\n\u201cYou have heard of a certain Frank Churchill, I presume,\u201d he continued\u2014\u201cand you know him to be my son, even though he doesn\u2019t bear my name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! yes, and I shall be very happy to make his acquaintance. I\u2019m sure Mr. Elton will waste no time in calling on him; and we shall both have great pleasure in seeing him at the Vicarage.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are very kind.\u2014Frank will be extremely happy, I\u2019m sure.\u2014He\u2019s to be in town next week, if not sooner. We\u2019ve had notice of it in a letter today. I came across the letters this morning, and seeing my son\u2019s handwriting, I presumed to open it\u2014though it wasn\u2019t addressed to me\u2014it was addressed to Mrs. Weston. She is his main correspondent, I assure you. I hardly ever get a letter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd so you actually opened what was meant for her! Oh, Mr. Weston\u2014(laughing in a showy way) I must protest against that.\u2014It\u2019s a most dangerous precedent indeed!\u2014I beg you won\u2019t let your neighbors follow your example.\u2014Upon my word, if this is what I\u2019m to expect, married women will have to start looking after themselves!\u2014Oh, Mr. Weston, I couldn\u2019t have believed it of you!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAye, we men are poor creatures. You must take care of yourself, Mrs. Elton.\u2014This letter tells us\u2014it\u2019s a short letter\u2014written in a hurry, just to give us notice\u2014it says that they\u2019re all coming up to town at once, on Mrs. Churchill\u2019s account\u2014she hasn\u2019t been well all winter, and thinks Enscombe too cold for her\u2014so they\u2019re all to move south without delay.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u2014from Yorkshire, I think. Enscombe is in Yorkshire?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, they are about one hundred and ninety miles from London\u2014a considerable journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, upon my word, very considerable. Sixty-five miles farther than from Maple Grove to London. But what is distance, Mr. Weston, to people of large fortune?\u2014You\u2019d be amazed to hear how my brother, Mr. Suckling, sometimes flies about. You would hardly believe me\u2014but twice in one week he and Mr. Bragge went to London and back again with four horses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe trouble with the distance from Enscombe,\u201d said Mr. Weston, \u201cis that Mrs. Churchill, _as_ _we_ _understand_, hasn\u2019t been able to leave the sofa for a week at a time. In Frank\u2019s last letter she complained, he said, of being too weak to get into her conservatory without both his arm and his uncle\u2019s!\u201d}\"}"
