          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fd208e7ac3753755d62c7d87\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Nearly a year had passed since my father returned, and the seasons were almost through their cycle. It was now the end of May. The woods were at their greenest, and the fields smelled sweet from the new grass that had been cut. I hoped that the warm air and the beautiful face of Nature would help me. I wanted them to soften him, and to bring him a gentle feeling of peace and love, before I tried to win his confidence.\\n\\nSo I chose one of these evenings for my attempt. I asked him to walk with me, and I led him to a nearby beech wood, where the light shade kept us from the slanting, dazzling rays of the setting sun. After we had walked in silence for some time, I sat down with him on a mossy hillock. Even now, I can still picture the place. Many of the slim, smooth trunks were wrapped with ivy. Its leaves were a deep, shining green, which made a strong contrast against the white bark and the pale new leaves of the young beech shoots growing from the older trunks. The short grass was mixed with moss, and it was partly covered by dead leaves from last autumn. Those leaves, blown by the wind, had gathered here and there into small piles. There were a few moss-covered stumps around us. The breeze moved the leaves gently, and through the green cover you could see the bright blue sky. As evening came, the distant trunks turned red in the last sunlight. The wind finally stopped, and a few birds flew past us on their way to settle for the night.\\n\\nWe sat there together. And if you knew everything that had happened\u2014everything terrible that had torn at our souls even in this quiet spot, which might have been a paradise for us if not for strange passions\u2014you would not be surprised that I remember it with the hope that its calm could give me calm too. I wanted it to help me, not only with courage, but with words that could persuade him. I noticed all these things in a blank, careful way as I tried to put my thoughts in the right order for what I meant to say. My heart beat fast as I worked myself up to speak. I was determined not to be turned away, but I still trembled at the thought of how my words might affect him. At last, after a long pause, I began:\\n\\n\u201cYour kindness to me, my dearest father, and the affection\u2014the excessive affection\u2014that you showed me when you first returned, I hope will excuse me in your eyes for daring to speak to you. Even though I speak with a daughter\u2019s tender love, I also speak with the freedom of a friend and an equal.\u201d\"}"
