{"request_id":"d5bdcd3d2cb903a434c2a3fe","rewritten_text":"We were brought up together; there was scarcely a year between our ages. I need not remark that we were strangers to any kind of separation or quarrel. The very essence of our companionship was harmony, and the variety and contrast in our temperaments drew us even closer. Elizabeth had a steadier, more collected nature; yet, for all my warmth, I could apply myself with greater intensity, and I was more deeply seized by a hunger for knowledge. She occupied herself with tracing the airy inventions of the poets; and in the grand and astonishing spectacles that surrounded our Swiss dwelling—the lofty forms of the mountains, the shifting of the seasons, storm and calm, the stillness of winter, and the life and commotion of our Alpine summers—she found abundant room for admiration and pleasure. While my companion regarded the magnificent appearances of things with a grave and contented mind, I took delight in probing their causes. To me the world was a hidden thing I longed to penetrate. Curiosity, earnest inquiry to learn the secret laws of nature, and a joy akin to rapture, as they unfolded themselves before me, were among the earliest sensations I can recall.

When my parents had a second son, my junior by seven years, they gave up their wandering life altogether and settled in their native country. We owned a house in Geneva, and a _campagne_ at Belrive, on the eastern shore of the lake, more than a league from the city. We lived chiefly at the latter place, and my parents’ days were spent in a good deal of retirement. I had a disposition to shun crowds and to devote myself ardently to a small number. I was therefore indifferent, in general, to my schoolmates; but I formed the closest friendship with one of them. Henry Clerval was the son of a merchant of Geneva. He was a boy of uncommon talent and imagination. He loved enterprise, hardship, and even danger purely for their own sake. He was profoundly versed in books of chivalry and romance. He composed heroic songs and began to write many a tale of enchantment and knightly adventure. He tried to get us to act plays and to take part in masquerades, where the characters were drawn from the heroes of Roncesvalles, from the Round Table of King Arthur, and from the chivalrous band who shed their blood to redeem the holy sepulchre from the hands of the infidels.

No one could have had a happier childhood than I. My parents were animated by nothing but kindness and indulgence. We believed that they were not tyrants ruling our lot by mere whim, but the agents and makers of all the many delights we enjoyed."}

