          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3682849c2bd65e2126a206f1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Suddenly Mr. Podgers let go of Lord Arthur\u2019s right hand and took hold of his left instead, bending so low to examine it that the gold rims of his spectacles seemed almost to touch the palm. For a moment his face turned into a pale mask of horror, but he quickly regained his sang-froid, and looking up at Lady Windermere, said with a forced smile, \u201cIt is the hand of a charming young man.\\n\\n\u201cOf course it is!\u201d Lady Windermere replied. \u201cBut will he be a charming husband? That is what I want to know.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll charming young men are,\u201d said Mr. Podgers.\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think a husband should be too fascinating,\u201d Lady Jedburgh murmured, thoughtful. \u201cIt\u2019s so dangerous.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear child, they never are too fascinating,\u201d cried Lady Windermere. \u201cBut what I want are details. Details are the only things that interest me. What is going to happen to Lord Arthur?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, within the next few months Lord Arthur will go on a voyage\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes, his honeymoon, of course!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd lose a relative.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot his sister, I hope?\u201d Lady Jedburgh asked, in a voice full of pity.\\n\\n\u201cCertainly not his sister,\u201d Mr. Podgers answered, waving a hand dismissively. \u201cA distant relative, merely.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I\u2019m dreadfully disappointed,\u201d Lady Windermere said. \u201cI have absolutely nothing to tell Sybil tomorrow. No one cares about distant relatives nowadays. They went out of fashion years ago. Still, I suppose she\u2019d better have a black silk with her; it always works for church, you know. And now let\u2019s go to supper. They\u2019re sure to have eaten everything, but we might find some hot soup. Fran\u00e7ois used to make excellent soup once, but he\u2019s so agitated about politics at present that I never feel quite certain about him. I do wish General Boulanger would keep quiet. Duchess, I\u2019m sure you\u2019re tired?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot at all, dear Gladys,\u201d the Duchess answered, waddling toward the door. \u201cI\u2019ve enjoyed myself immensely, and the cheiropodist\u2014I mean the cheiromantist\u2014is most interesting. Flora, where can my tortoiseshell fan be? Oh, thank you, Sir Thomas, so much. And my lace shawl, Flora? Oh, thank you, Sir Thomas, that\u2019s very kind. I\u2019m sure\u201d; and the worthy woman finally managed to get downstairs without dropping her scent-bottle more than twice.\u201d\\n\\nAll this time Lord Arthur Savile had remained standing by the fireplace, with the same feeling of dread over him, the same sickening sense of coming evil. He smiled sadly at his sister as she swept past him on Lord Plymdale\u2019s arm, looking lovely in her pink brocade and pearls, and he hardly heard Lady Windermere when she called to him to follow her. He thought of Sybil Merton, and the idea that anything could come between them made his eyes fill with tears.\"}"
