          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ece14b5ab620e2ea29fbedb8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I know she would!\u201d\\n\\nThe Widow Douglas\u2019 wonderful hospitality was a tempting lure. Tom\u2019s arguments soon won the day. So they decided to tell no one anything about the plan for the night. Then Tom suddenly wondered if Huck might come that very night and give the signal. The thought drained a lot of the excitement from him. Still, he couldn\u2019t bring himself to give up the fun at Widow Douglas\u2019. And why should he? He told himself that since the signal hadn\u2019t come the night before, it probably wouldn\u2019t come tonight either. The certain fun of the evening mattered more than the uncertain chance of finding money. Like a boy, he chose to follow the stronger pull and not think about the box of money again that day.\\n\\nThree miles below town, the ferryboat stopped at the mouth of a wooded hollow and tied up. The crowd poured ashore, and soon the forest paths and rocky heights echoed with shouts and laughter. They tried every way they could to get hot and tired, and then, little by little, the men and boys drifted back to camp with appetites that felt well earned. After that, the good food and treats began to disappear. After the meal, everyone rested and chatted in the shade of spreading oaks. Soon someone called out:\\n\\n\u201cWho\u2019s ready for the cave?\u201d\\n\\nEveryone was. Bundles of candles were gathered up, and right away people scrambled up the hill. The cave entrance was on the hillside, shaped like the letter A. Its heavy wooden door stood open, with no bars on it. Inside was a small chamber, cold as an icehouse, with walls of solid limestone that looked wet and cold, as if it were sweating. It was romantic and mysterious to stand in the deep dark and look out over the green valley shining in the sun. But the mood didn\u2019t last. Soon the playing started again. The moment a candle was lit, the person holding it was immediately surrounded. There was a struggle and a brave defense, but the candle was soon knocked down or blown out, and then everyone laughed loudly and chased each other again.\\n\\nBut everything ends. After a while, the group moved in a line down the steep descent of the main passage. The flickering lights only dimly showed the high rock walls, almost up to where they met about sixty feet overhead. This main passage was only about eight or ten feet wide. Every few steps, other tall but narrower openings branched off to either side. McDougal\u2019s cave was really a huge maze of crooked hallways that connected and split again and again, leading nowhere.\"}"
