          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ba0cf36608a7aeaffad83ab9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShucks, Tom,\u201d I said. \u201cI think you might tell a person. What\u2019s a bar sinister?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I don\u2019t know. But he\u2019s got to have it. All the nobility does.\u201d\\n\\nThat was just the way he was. If he didn\u2019t feel like explaining something to you, he wouldn\u2019t. You could ask him for a week, and it wouldn\u2019t make any difference.\\n\\nHe\u2019d already gotten the coat of arms part figured out, so now he started finishing the rest of that work. That meant planning a mournful inscription\u2014Jim was supposed to have one, like they all did. He made up a lot, wrote them out on paper, and read them off.\\n\\n\u201cHere a captive heart busted.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHere a poor prisoner, forsook by the world and friends, fretted out his sorrowful life.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHere a lonely heart broke, and a worn spirit went to its rest, after thirty-seven years of solitary captivity.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHere, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of bitter captivity, perished a noble stranger, natural son of Louis XIV.\u201d\\n\\nTom\u2019s voice shook while he read them, and he broke down completely when he finished. After that, he couldn\u2019t decide which one Jim should scratch onto the wall, because they were all so good. In the end, he decided Jim would scratch all of them on.\\n\\nJim said it would take him a year to scratch that much stuff onto the logs with a nail, and he didn\u2019t know how to make letters. But Tom said he would block the letters out for him, and then Jim wouldn\u2019t have to do anything except follow the lines.\\n\\nThen Tom said, \u201cCome to think of it, the logs aren\u2019t going to work. There aren\u2019t log walls in a dungeon. We have to dig the inscriptions into a rock. We\u2019ll fetch a rock.\u201d\\n\\nJim said a rock would be worse than the logs. He said it would take him so long to dig the words into a rock that he\u2019d never get out. But Tom said he would let me help him do it.\\n\\nTom looked at how me and Jim were getting on with the pens. The work was slow and tedious, and it didn\u2019t help my hands heal the sores. We didn\u2019t seem to be making much progress at all. So Tom said, \u201cI know how to fix it. We need a rock for the coat of arms and the mournful inscriptions, and we can kill two birds with that same rock.\u201d\"}"
