{"request_id":"e9f5c6f3803ae2b731b02538","rewritten_text":"The stars were still shining with great brightness when I woke, and Taurus high in the southern sky showed me that it was midnight. I had risen out of troubled dreams. It seemed to me that I had been invited to Timon’s last feast; I went with a sharp appetite, the coverings were drawn back, and the hot water was brought up, steaming in a way that left me unsatisfied. Then I fled, before the host’s anger—who had taken the shape of Raymond. In my sickly fancy, the vessels he hurled after me were heavy with foul vapors, and my friend’s form, warped by a thousand distortions, swelled into a vast phantom, crowned with the mark of pestilence. The shadow kept rising and rising, filling the air, and then as if it would strain to burst through the adamantine vault that arched overhead, holding, sustaining, and enclosing the world. The nightmare turned into torment; with a hard effort I shook off sleep and brought reason back to her proper office. My first thought was Perdita. To her I must return; I must stand by her, drawing from despair whatever might best keep her wounded heart alive; I must bring her back from the wild excesses of grief, under the stern laws of duty, and the gentle tenderness of regret.

The stars’ position was my only guide. I turned away from the dreadful ruin of the Golden City, and, after much strain, managed to free myself from its bounds. Outside the walls I met a company of soldiers; I borrowed a horse from one of them and hurried to my sister. In the short time it took, the look of the plain had altered: the encampment had been broken up; the remains of the dispersed army lay in little groups here and there; every face was overcast, and every gesture declared astonishment and dismay.

With a heavy heart I entered the palace, afraid to move forward, afraid to speak, afraid even to look. In the middle of the hall sat Perdita. She was on the marble pavement, her head resting on her bosom, her hair disordered, her fingers busy, one within the other. She was pale as stone, and every feature was drawn tight by agony. When she saw me, she lifted her eyes in inquiry; the half-glance that held hope turned to misery. The words failed me before I could form them. I felt a ghastly smile crease my lips. She understood my gesture; once more her head sank, and once more her fingers worked restlessly. At last I found my voice, but it frightened her. The poor girl had already understood what my look meant, and she would not—no, not for anything—have wanted the story of her crushing misery to be shaped and confirmed by harsh, final words. Indeed, she seemed to try to divert my thoughts from it: she rose from the floor and whispered, “Hush! After much weeping, Clara sleeps; we must not disturb her.”"}

