          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"118b21195449cd8cec614faa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He couldn\u2019t help it. And it drove him wild to think\u2014at least as he believed\u2014that Becky Thatcher never once suspected he was even in the world of the living. Still, she did see him; and she knew she was carrying her point, too, and was pleased to watch him suffer the way she had suffered.\\n\\nAmy\u2019s cheerful chatter soon became unbearable. Tom let fall hints about things he had to take care of\u2014matters that must be done\u2014and that time was slipping away. But it was all to no purpose; the girl kept chirping on. Tom thought, \u201cOh, bother her\u2014ain\u2019t I ever going to be rid of her?\u201d At last he had to attend to those very things, and she said, with a kind of innocent ease, that she\u2019d be \u201caround\u201d when school let out. He hurried off, furious with her for it.\\n\\n\u201cAny other boy!\u201d Tom thought, grinding his teeth. \u201cAny boy in the whole town but that Saint Louis smart aleck who thinks he dresses so fine and is some kind of aristocrat! Oh, sure\u2014I licked you the first day you ever set foot here, and I\u2019ll lick you again! Just you wait till I catch you out! I\u2019ll just\u2014\u201d\\n\\nHe then went through the business of beating an imaginary boy, pounding the air and making kicks and savage jabs. \u201cOh, you do, do you? You holler enough, do you? Well then\u2014let that teach you!\u201d And with that, the pretend thrashing was finished to his liking.\\n\\nTom slipped home at noon. His conscience couldn\u2019t stand any more of Amy\u2019s thankful happiness, and his jealousy couldn\u2019t bear the other girl\u2019s pain any longer. Becky went back to studying pictures with Alfred; but as the minutes dragged and Tom didn\u2019t come, her triumph started to dim and she lost interest. Then came heaviness and absent-mindedness, and after that melancholy. Two or three times she perked up at the sound of footsteps, but each time it was only a false hope\u2014no Tom arrived. At last she became thoroughly miserable and wished she hadn\u2019t pushed it so far. When poor Alfred, realizing he was losing her, kept blurting, \u201cOh, here\u2019s a jolly one! just look at this!\u201d she snapped at him, \u201cOh, don\u2019t bother me! I don\u2019t care for them!\u201d and burst into tears, then rose and walked away.\\n\\nAlfred drifted alongside, intending to comfort her, but she said:\\n\\n\u201cGo on away and leave me alone, can\u2019t you! I hate you!\u201d\\n\\nSo the boy stopped, wondering what he could have done\u2014since she had said she\u2019d be looking at pictures clear through the nooning\u2014and she went on, crying. Then Alfred sat there, thinking, in the empty schoolhouse. He felt ashamed and angry. He could see his way to the truth easily enough: the girl had simply used him as a handy target for her spite against Tom Sawyer.\"}"
