          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6261c728b9fd02b91995ab5a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The next day the ghost felt very weak and tired. The frightening excitement of the last four weeks was starting to catch up with him. His nerves were completely wrecked, and he jumped at even the smallest sound. For five days he stayed in his room, and then, at last, he decided to stop worrying about the bloodstain on the library floor. If the Otis family didn\u2019t want it, then they clearly didn\u2019t deserve it. They seemed to live on a low, practical level, and he believed they were too shallow to understand the symbolic meaning of sensuous things.\\n\\nThe matter of ghostly visions and the making of astral bodies was different, of course, and not really his choice. It was his duty to appear in the corridor once a week, and to make his loud, gibbering display from the large oriel window on the first and third Wednesday of every month. He couldn\u2019t see any honorable way to escape those obligations. It was true that his life had been very evil, but he was also very careful about anything connected with the supernatural.\\n\\nSo, for the next three Saturdays, he went through the corridor as usual between midnight and three o\u2019clock, taking every precaution so that he would not be heard or seen. He took off his boots, stepped as lightly as he could on the old boards that were eaten up by worms, and wore a large black velvet cloak. He was also careful to use the Rising Sun Lubricator to oil his chains. I have to admit it wasn\u2019t easy for him to get himself to use that last kind of protection. Still, one night, while the family were at dinner, he slipped into Mr. Otis\u2019s bedroom and stole the bottle. At first he felt a little ashamed, but soon he understood that the invention had a lot going for it, and it did help him, to a certain extent.\\n\\nEven so, he wasn\u2019t left alone. Strings were stretched across the corridor again and again, and he kept tripping over them in the dark. On one occasion, while he was getting ready for his part as \u201cBlack Isaac, or the Huntsman of Hogley Woods,\u201d he fell hard because he stepped on a butter-slide. The twins had made it from the entrance of the Tapestry Chamber all the way to the top of the oak staircase. This insult made him so angry that he decided to make one last attempt to prove his dignity and his social standing. The next night he planned to visit the rude young Etonians in his famous disguise as \u201cReckless Rupert, or the Headless Earl.\u201d\\n\\nHe hadn\u2019t worn that costume for more than seventy years. In fact, it was the first time since he had frightened pretty Lady Barbara Modish with it. She had broken off her engagement to the present Lord Canterville\u2019s grandfather, then ran away to Gretna Green with handsome Jack Castleton. She said nothing in the world would make her marry into a family that let such a horrible phantom walk up and down the terrace at twilight.\"}"
