{"request_id":"6089c3c9670b65e7c636f515","rewritten_text":"Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not destroyed by spirits. The people who carried out the deed were real, and they escaped in a real, physical way. So how? Fortunately, there is only one way to reason through this, and it must lead us to a clear decision. Let us examine, one by one, the possible ways they could have gotten out. It is clear that the assassins were in the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was found—or at least in the adjoining room—when the party went up the stairs. That means we have to look for exits from just those two apartments. The police have exposed the floors, the ceilings, and the masonry of the walls in every direction. No secret openings could have escaped their vigilance. But I didn’t trust only their eyes; I examined it myself. There were, then, no secret openings. Both doors that led from the rooms into the passage were securely locked, with the keys kept inside. Let us turn to the chimneys. These are of ordinary width for about eight or ten feet above the hearths, but they will not allow, anywhere along their length, the body of a large cat to pass through. Since the impossibility of escape by the means already stated is therefore absolute, we are left with only the windows. Through the windows of the front room, no one could have escaped without being noticed by the crowd in the street. The murderers must, then, have gone out through the windows of the back room. Now that we have reached this conclusion in such an unmistakable way, it is not our job, as people who reason, to dismiss it because of apparent impossibilities. The only thing left is to prove that these apparent “impossibilities” are, in reality, not that at all.

“There are two windows in the chamber. One of them is unobstructed by furniture, and is fully visible. The lower part of the other is hidden from view by the head of the bulky bedstead pushed right up against it. The first window was found securely fastened from within. It resisted the greatest force used by those who tried to lift it. A large gimlet-hole had been drilled in its frame to the left, and a very stout nail was found fitted into it, nearly up to the head. When the other window was examined, a similar nail was seen fitted in it in the same way; and a strong attempt to raise that sash also failed. The police were now entirely convinced that there had been no exit in those directions. And, therefore, they thought it was unnecessary to remove the nails and open the windows.

“My own examination was somewhat more careful, and was so for the reason I’ve just given—because this was where, I knew, all apparent impossibilities had to be proven not to be real.”"}

