          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"51ba1c0f54f01987557593ec\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs Clay\u2019s feelings had carried her beyond her own interest, and, for the young man\u2019s sake, she had given up the chance of plotting longer in Sir Walter\u2019s favour. Yet she has talents as well as attachments; and it remains uncertain whether his craft or hers will at last prevail\u2014whether, after he has managed to keep her from becoming Sir Walter\u2019s wife, he may not still be persuaded, coaxed, and fondly treated into making her the wife of Sir William.\\n\\nThere can be no doubt that Sir Walter and Elizabeth were both startled and deeply vexed by the loss of their companion, and by the discovery of the deception practised upon them. They had, to be sure, their great cousins to turn to for comfort; but they must still feel, for a long time, that to praise and follow others, while one is not praised and followed in return, can amount to no more than a kind of half-enjoyment.\\n\\nAnne, long before, had understood Lady Russell\u2019s intention well enough to be in earnest about loving Captain Wentworth as she ought; and there was no other drawback to the happiness of her prospects than the thought that she could offer him no relations\u2014nothing that a man of sense would value. There she felt her own inferiority most sharply. The mismatch in their fortunes gave her not a moment\u2019s regret; it was not that. But having no family to receive and judge him properly, no respectable, harmonious, well-disposed connections to offer in return for all the worth and the ready welcome which met her in his brothers and sisters\u2014this was a pain as lively as her mind could well feel, even amid circumstances otherwise rich in felicity. She had only two friends in the world to add to his list: Lady Russell and Mrs Smith. Still, he was disposed to attach himself closely to both. Lady Russell, despite all her earlier offences, he could now prize from his heart. Though he did not feel bound to declare that he believed her right in originally separating them, he was willing to say almost everything else in her favour; and as for Mrs Smith, she had claims of several sorts that made her worth recommending at once and securing permanently.\\n\\nAnne\u2019s recent good offices had been enough in themselves; and their marriage, far from costing her one friend, actually gave her two. She was the first visitor in their settled life; and Captain Wentworth, by putting her in the way of recovering her husband\u2019s property in the West Indies\u2014by writing for her, acting for her, and carrying her through all the small troubles of the business with the energy and steadiness of a bold man and a resolute friend\u2014fully repaid the services she had rendered, or ever meant to render, to his wife.\"}"
