          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3c75e511f081803daf243361\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Great God! If, for even an instant, I had considered the hellish intent of my fiendish adversary, I would rather have banished myself forever from my native country and wandered the earth as a friendless outcast than have agreed to this miserable marriage. But it was as if the monster had magic powers; he blinded me to his true intentions. And when I believed I had only prepared my own death, I hurried that of a far dearer victim.\\n\\nAs the time fixed for our marriage drew nearer, whether from cowardice or from some prophetic feeling, I felt my heart sink within me. Still, I hid my feelings behind an outward show of cheerfulness. It brought smiles and joy to my father\u2019s face, though it hardly deceived Elizabeth\u2019s ever-watchful, sharper eye. She looked forward to our union with calm contentment, mixed with a little fear\u2014fear shaped by past misfortunes\u2014that what now seemed like certain, tangible happiness might soon vanish into an airy dream and leave behind nothing but deep, everlasting regret.\\n\\nPreparations were made for the event; congratulatory visits were received; and everyone wore a smiling expression. I shut up, as best I could, the anxiety that preyed on my mind, and it entered into my father\u2019s plans with an air of earnestness, even if those plans were only meant to serve as the decorations of my tragedy. Through my father\u2019s efforts, part of Elizabeth\u2019s inheritance was restored to her by the Austrian government. A small property on the shores of Como belonged to her. It was agreed that, immediately after our union, we would go to Villa Lavenza and spend our first days of happiness beside the beautiful lake near which it stood.\\n\\nMeanwhile, I took every precaution to defend myself in case the fiend should openly attack me. I carried pistols and a dagger constantly, and I stayed on guard to prevent any trickery. By these means I gained a greater measure of calm. In fact, as the time approached, the threat seemed more like a delusion\u2014something not worth disturbing my peace\u2014while the happiness I hoped for in my marriage appeared more and more certain as the day for its solemnization drew closer. I heard it spoken of again and again as an event that no accident could possibly prevent.\\n\\nElizabeth seemed happy, and my steady, composed manner helped calm her mind. But on the day that was meant to fulfill my wishes and my destiny, she was melancholy, and a presentiment of evil weighed on her. Perhaps she also thought of the dreadful secret I had promised to reveal to her the following day.\"}"
