          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"daa46abc73c0fc6975d12585\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cUnless you mean Miss Havisham as a he. And I doubt even you would go that far.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham, up town?\u201d Joe asked.\\n\\n\u201cIs there any Miss Havisham down town?\u201d my sister shot back.\\n\\n\u201cShe wants this boy to go and play there. And of course he will. And he\u2019d better play there,\u201d my sister said, shaking her head at me as if to encourage me to be light and playful, \u201cor I\u2019ll work him.\u201d\\n\\nI had heard about Miss Havisham up town. Everyone for miles around had heard about her. She was an extremely rich, grim woman who lived in a large, gloomy house, shut up tight against robbers, and who kept to herself.\\n\\n\u201cWell, of course!\u201d Joe said, clearly shocked. \u201cI wonder how she came to know Pip!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNoodle!\u201d my sister cried. \u201cWho said she knew him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201c\u2014Someone,\u201d Joe suggested politely again, \u201csaid she wants him to go and play there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd why can\u2019t she ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knows a boy who could go and play there? Isn\u2019t it just possible that Uncle Pumblechook is one of her tenants, and that sometimes\u2014though we won\u2019t say every quarter or every half-year, because that would be asking too much of you\u2014but sometimes he goes there to pay his rent? And then couldn\u2019t she ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knows a boy to go and play there? And couldn\u2019t Uncle Pumblechook, who is always kind and thoughtful for us\u2014though you may not think it, Joseph,\u201d she said with the deepest reproach, as if he were the most heartless nephew, \u201cthen mention this boy, standing here prancing\u201d\u2014which I solemnly declare I was not doing\u2014\u201cthat I have been a willing slave to?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood again!\u201d Uncle Pumblechook cried. \u201cWell said! Nicely put! That\u2019s right! Now, Joseph, you know the situation.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, Joseph,\u201d my sister said, still sounding reproachful, while Joe apologized by wiping the back of his hand across and across his nose. \u201cYou don\u2019t yet\u2014though you may not think it\u2014know the situation. You may think you do, but you don\u2019t, Joseph. You see, Uncle Pumblechook knows that, as far as we can tell, this boy\u2019s future might be made by going to Miss Havisham\u2019s. So he has offered to take him into town tonight in his own chaise-cart, to keep him there tonight, and then to take him with his own hands to Miss Havisham\u2019s tomorrow morning. And Lor-a-mussy me!\u201d my sister cried, suddenly desperate, throwing off her bonnet. \u201cHere I am talking to mere Mooncalfs, while Uncle Pumblechook is waiting, the mare is catching cold at the door, and the boy is covered in grime\u2014crock and dirt\u2014from the hair on his head to the sole of his foot!\u201d\"}"
