{"request_id":"98f33abb2f142b57d57bcf73","rewritten_text":"Ye spirits of storm, take me up! ye powers of ruin, fling wide your arms, and hold me fast for ever! Unless some gentler will has ordained a different end—so that, after long endurance, I may gather my reward, and feel my heart beat again close to the heart of another, like to my own.

Tiber, the road spread out by nature’s own hand, running through her continent, lay at my feet, and many a boat was moored along the banks. With only a few books, some provisions, and my dog, I would step aboard one of them and drift with the stream down into the sea; then, keeping close to shore, I would sail past the lovely coasts and bright headlands of the blue Mediterranean, go on by Naples, along Calabria, and dare the twin dangers of Scylla and Charybdis; after that, with a bold resolve—what could I possibly lose?—skim the ocean’s surface toward Malta and the farther Cyclades. I would steer clear of Constantinople, whose familiar towers and inlets belong to a different order of being than my present one; I would coast Asia Minor and Syria, and, passing the seven-mouthed Nile, turn northward again, until I lose sight of forgotten Carthage and deserted Lybia, and come at last to the pillars of Hercules. And then—wherever it may be—the oozy caves and silent depths of the ocean may be my dwelling, before I finish this long-spun voyage, or the arrow of sickness strikes my heart while I float alone upon the heaving Mediterranean; or, in some port where I put in, I may find what I’m after—a companion; and if not, then to endless time, worn and grey with age, with youth already in the grave beside those I love—the solitary wanderer will still unfurl his sail, grip the tiller, and, ever obeying the breezes of heaven, go on round promontory after promontory, anchoring in bay after bay, still cutting through the ocean that bears no seed, leaving behind the green land of native Europe, down the tawny coast of Africa, having weathered the fierce seas of the Cape, I may moor my battered skiff in some creek, sheltered by the spicy groves of the fragrant islands far out in the Indian ocean.

These are wild dreams. Yet since, only a week ago, they seized me while I stood upon the height of St. Peter’s, they have governed my imagination. I have picked out my boat and laid in my meagre stores. I have chosen a few books—the chief are Homer and Shakespeare—though the libraries of the world are opened to me, and in any port I can replenish my supply. I look for no change for the better; but the dull sameness of the present is unbearable to me."}

