{"request_id":"1eb03673802689e2c1fbc31d","rewritten_text":"Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not undone by spirits. The hands that carried out the deed were flesh-and-blood, and they slipped away in the same plain, material fashion. But how, then? Happily, there is only one way to reason through the matter, and that way has to bring us to a clear conclusion. Let us look, one by one, at every possible way of getting out. It is plain that the assassins were in the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was found—or, at the very least, in the adjoining room—when the party went up the stairs. So our search for exits is limited to those two apartments. The police have stripped the floors, the ceilings, and the masonry of the walls, in every direction. No secret openings could have escaped their watchfulness. Still, I did not rely on their eyes alone; I checked for myself. There were, then, no secret exits. Both doors from the rooms into the passage were locked fast, with the keys left inside. Let us consider the chimneys next. Though they are of ordinary width for about eight or ten feet above the hearths, they will not, anywhere along their length, allow the body of a large cat to pass. Since escape by the means already named is therefore out of the question, we are driven to the windows. From the front-room windows, no one could have escaped without being seen by the crowd in the street. The murderers must therefore have gone out by the windows of the back room. And once we reach this conclusion in so unmistakable a manner, it is not for us, as reasoners, to discard it merely because it seems to clash with appearances. The only remaining task is to show that these apparent “impossibilities” are, in truth, not such.

“There are two windows in the chamber. One is clear of furniture and entirely in view. The lower part of the other is hidden by the head of the bulky bedstead pushed right up against it. The first was found fastened from within. It held against the greatest force used to lift it. A large gimlet-hole had been bored in its frame to the left, and a very stout nail was fitted there, nearly up to the head. When the other window was examined, a similar nail was found fitted in the same way; and a strong effort to raise that sash failed as well. The police were now fully convinced that there had been no escape by those directions. And, therefore, they considered it needless to pull the nails out and open the windows.

“My own inspection was a bit more exacting, and was so for the reason I’ve just given—because here, I knew, all apparent impossibilities had to be proved not to be impossibilities at all in reality.”"}

