tle without much expense; but the last year or two had brought them a very considerable increase of fortune\u2014the house in town had turned a better profit, and their circumstances in general had prospered. With their wealth, their notions grew bolder; their wish for a larger house, and their inclination for more company. They enlarged their house, increased their number of servants, and raised their expenses of every kind; and by this time, in both fortune and manner of living, they stood next only to the family at Hartfield. Their fondness for society, and their new dining-room, had prepared everyone for their giving dinner-parties; and a few gatherings, chiefly among the single men, had already occurred. The principal and most regular families Emma could hardly suppose they would venture to ask\u2014neither Donwell, nor Hartfield, nor Randalls. Nothing could persuade her to go, if they did; and she regretted that her father\u2019s well-known habits would make her refusal carry less meaning than she could wish.\"}"
