{"request_id":"11f9b1d195fb01f82b8aba30","rewritten_text":"I am a native of a sea-surrounded nook, a land shadowed by clouds, which—when I look at the surface of the globe, with its shoreless ocean and trackless continents—appears to my mind only as an insignificant speck in the immense whole; and yet, when weighed against the scale of mental power, it far outweighs countries of greater extent and more numerous population. So true it is that man’s mind alone created everything good or great for man, and that Nature herself was only his first servant. England, seated far to the north in the turbid sea, now visits my dreams in the shape of a vast, well-manned ship—one that mastered the winds and rode proudly over the waves. In my boyhood, she was the universe to me. When I stood on my native hills and saw plain and mountain stretch out to the farthest limits of my vision, speckled with the dwellings of my countrymen, and made fertile by their labor, the earth’s very center was fixed for me in that spot, and the rest of her orb was like a fable—something it would have cost neither my imagination nor my understanding to forget.

My fortunes, from the beginning, have been an example of how mutability can affect the varied course of a man’s life. In my case, it came almost by inheritance. My father was one of those men whom nature had endowed with, in lavish measure, the envied gifts of wit and imagination, and then left his bark of life to be driven by those winds, without adding reason as the rudder or judgment as the pilot for the voyage. His origins were obscure; but circumstances brought him early into public notice, and his small paternal property was soon squandered in the splendid spectacle of fashion and luxury in which he acted as a participant. In the brief years of thoughtless youth, he was adored by the high-bred triflers of the day, and not least by the youthful sovereign, who escaped from the intrigues of party and the demanding duties of kingly business to find in his society a never-failing source of amusement and exhilaration. My father’s impulses, never truly under his own control, continually led him into difficulties from which his ingenuity alone could get him out; and the growing pile of debts of honor and of trade, which would have crushed any other man, he met with a light spirit and untamed hilarity. His presence was so necessary at the tables and gatherings of the rich that his failures were treated as forgivable, and he was welcomed with intoxicating flattery.

This kind of popularity, like every other, is fleeting; and the difficulties of every kind he had to face increased in a frightful proportion to the small means he had for extricating himself."}

