          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6f0b5ec235c73ec4ea092325\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In fact, he was dressed as \u201cJonas the Graveless, or the Corpse-Snatcher of Chertsey Barn,\u201d which was one of his best impersonations. The Cantervilles had every reason to remember it, because it was the real start of their fight with their neighbor, Lord Rufford. It was a little after two in the morning, and, as far as he could tell, no one was moving. Still, as he walked toward the library to look for any traces of the bloodstain, two figures suddenly burst out of a dark corner. They waved their arms wildly over their heads and shrieked \u201cBOO!\u201d right into his ear.\\n\\nHe panicked, as anyone would in that situation. He ran for the stairs, but Washington Otis was already there, holding a big garden syringe. With his enemies all around him and nowhere to go, he slipped into the large iron stove. Luckily for him, it was not lit. He then had to work his way home through the flues and chimneys, and when he finally reached his own room, he was in a terrible mess\u2014dirty, disordered, and full of despair.\\n\\nAfter that, he was not seen again on any night-time adventure. The twins waited for him on several occasions and, every night, scattered nutshells in the passages, which annoyed their parents and the servants a great deal. But it did not help. It was clear that his feelings were hurt enough that he would not show himself again. Mr. Otis therefore went back to his big project on the history of the Democratic Party, which he had been working on for years. Mrs. Otis planned a wonderful clam-bake that amazed the whole county. The boys took up lacrosse, euchre, poker, and other American national games. Virginia rode around the lanes on her pony, with the young Duke of Cheshire beside her. He had come to spend the last week of his holidays at Canterville Chase.\\n\\nEveryone assumed the ghost had gone away. In fact, Mr. Otis wrote a letter to Lord Canterville to say so