          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b8e159f91118a2175b5977a9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was well beyond noon when he woke. His valet had slipped in more than once, moving on tiptoe, to see whether his young master had stirred, and he had been puzzled by what kept him asleep so late. At last the bell rang, and Victor entered quietly with a cup of tea and a stack of letters, all carried on a small tray of old Sevres china. He drew back the olive-satin curtains, whose blue lining caught the light with a faint shimmer, that hung before the three tall windows.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur has slept very well this morning,\u201d he said, smiling.\\n\\n\u201cWhat time is it, Victor?\u201d Dorian Gray asked, still drowsy.\\n\\n\u201cOne hour and a quarter, Monsieur.\u201d\\n\\nHow late it was! He sat up, took a sip of tea, and began to sort through his letters. One had come from Lord Henry, delivered by hand that morning. He paused, then set it aside. The rest he opened with a kind of indifference. They were the usual assortment\u2014cards, invitations to dinner, tickets for private views, notices of charity concerts, and all the other things that are sent to fashionable young men every morning during the season. There was also a rather heavy bill for a chased silver Louis-Quinze toilet-set, which he had not yet found the nerve to forward to his guardians. They were extremely old-fashioned, and could not see that we live in an age where unnecessary things are the only necessities. Along with that were several messages from the money-lenders of Jermyn Street, written with the utmost courtesy, offering to advance any sum at a moment\u2019s notice and on the most reasonable terms of interest.\\n\\nAfter about ten minutes he rose, slipped into an elaborate dressing-gown of silk-embroidered cashmere wool, and went into the onyx-paved bathroom. The cool water revived him after his long sleep. It was as though he had forgotten everything he had been through. Now and then a vague feeling returned\u2014that he had somehow taken part in some strange tragedy\u2014but it came with the same unreality as a dream.\\n\\nOnce he was dressed, he went into the library and sat down to a light French breakfast laid out for him on a small round table near the open window. It was an exquisite day. The warm air seemed thick with spices. A bee wandered in and buzzed around the blue-dragon bowl before him, filled with sulphur-yellow roses. He felt perfectly content.\\n\\nThen his gaze caught the screen he had placed in front of the portrait, and he started.\\n\\n\u201cToo cold for Monsieur?\u201d asked his valet, setting an omelette on the table. \u201cI shut the window?\u201d\\n\\nDorian shook his head. \u201cI am not cold,\u201d he murmured.\"}"
