          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ee03639ae4414a637ba54714\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Col. Grangerford was a gentleman, you see. He was a gentleman in every way, and so was his family. He was well born, as people say, and that mattered as much for a man as it did for a horse, the Widow Douglas liked to say. Nobody in our town ever argued with her, either, because she was considered one of the top families. Pap always said the same thing, too, even if he wasn\u2019t any better than a mudcat himself.\\n\\nCol. Grangerford was very tall and very thin. His complexion was dark and pale, with no red anywhere. Every morning he was clean-shaven across his thin face. His lips were thin, his nostrils were thin, and he had a high nose. He had heavy eyebrows and the blackest eyes, set so deep that they looked like they were staring at you from caverns, as you might imagine. His forehead was high. His hair was black and straight, and it fell down to his shoulders. His hands were long and thin. Every day of his life he put on a clean shirt and a full suit from head to foot made of linen so white it hurt your eyes to look at it. On Sundays he wore a blue tail-coat with brass buttons. He carried a mahogany cane with a silver head.\\n\\nThere was nothing silly about him at all, and he was never loud. He was as kind as he could be, and you could feel it, so you trusted him. Sometimes he smiled, and it was nice to see. But when he stood up straight like a flagpole, and the lightning seemed to flicker out from under his eyebrows, you wanted to climb a tree first and figure out what was wrong afterward. He never had to tell anyone to mind their manners, because everyone was always well behaved when he was around. People loved having him there, too. He was like sunshine most of the time\u2014at least, he made it seem like good weather. When he turned into a cloud, though, it got terribly dark for about half a minute, and that was enough. Nothing bad would happen again for a week.\\n\\nWhen he and the old lady came down in the morning, the whole family got up from their chairs to greet them and didn\u2019t sit back down until they had sat down. Then Tom and Bob went to the sideboard where the decanter was. They mixed a glass of bitters, handed it to him, and he held it while Tom\u2019s and Bob\u2019s were mixed. After that, they bowed and said, \u201cOur duty to you, sir, and madam.\u201d They bowed a little themselves and said, \u201cThank you,\u201d and then all three drank. Bob and Tom poured a spoonful of water onto the sugar and the tiny bit of whisky or apple brandy at the bottom of their glasses, and then they gave it to me and Buck. We drank to the old people, too.\"}"
