          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"36a05b260204f4a39c0bc3fa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mental traits discussed as the analytical are, in themselves, only slightly open to analysis. We understand them mainly through their effects. Among other things, we know that they are always, for the person who has them in an excessive degree, a source of the liveliest enjoyment. Just as the strong man takes pride in his physical ability\u2014delighting in exercises that bring his muscles into action\u2014so the analyst takes pride in that moral activity which disentangles. He finds pleasure even in the most trivial tasks, so long as his talent can be brought to bear. He likes enigmas, conundrums, and hieroglyphics; in solving each, he shows a level of acumen that seems supernatural to ordinary understanding. His results, produced by the very soul and essence of method, truly have the whole look of intuition.\\n\\nThe faculty of re-solution may well be strengthened by mathematical study, and especially by that highest branch of it which, unjustly, and merely because its operations run backward, has been called\u2014so to speak\u2014analysis par excellence. Still, to calculate is not, by itself, to analyze. A chess player, for example, can do one without the other. It follows that chess, in its effects on mental character, is greatly misunderstood. I am not writing a treatise now, but simply prefacing a somewhat peculiar narrative with observations drawn very much at random; I will therefore take the opportunity to claim that the higher powers of the reflective intellect are more decidedly and more usefully exercised by the unshowy game of draughts than by all the elaborate frivolity of chess. In the latter, where the pieces have different and bizarre moves, with various and changeable values, what is merely complex is mistaken (a not uncommon mistake) for what is profound. Here attention is called powerfully into play. If it slips for even an instant, an oversight is made that leads to injury or defeat. Since the possible moves are not only numerous but also intricate, the chances of such oversights are multiplied; and in nine cases out of ten it is the more concentrative rather than the more acute player who wins. In draughts, by contrast, where the moves are unique and have little variation, the likelihood of inadvertence is reduced, and because mere attention is left comparatively idle, the advantages gained by either side come from superior acumen. To make this less abstract, let us suppose a game of draughts in which the pieces are reduced to four kings, and where, of course, no oversight is to be expected. It is clear that here victory can be decided (since the players are equally matched) only by some recherch\u00e9 move\u2014the result of a strong exertion of the intellect.\"}"
