          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c4e0e91ce57cdf43ab63db8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Watching him in such moods, I would often fall into a meditative turn over the old notion of the Bi-Part Soul, and I would amuse myself with the conceit of a double Dupin\u2014the one inventive, the other quick to resolve.\\n\\nNor let it be imagined, from what I have just set down, that I am laying out any mystery, or composing any romance. What I have said of the Frenchman was simply the outcome of an excited\u2014perhaps even a disordered\u2014intelligence. Yet, as to the nature of his remarks at those moments, an example will serve best to show the point.\\n\\nWe were walking one night along a long, dirty street near the Palais Royal. Both of us, as it seemed, were taken up with our thoughts; for at least fifteen minutes neither of us had spoken a single syllable. Then, all at once, Dupin burst out with these words:\\n\\n\u201cHe is a very little fellow, that\u2019s true, and would do better for the _Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere can be no doubt of that,\u201d I answered, before I had even noticed\u2014so deep was I in my own reverie\u2014the odd way in which the speaker had fallen in with my meditations. In the next instant, however, I checked myself, and my astonishment was profound.\\n\\n\u201cDupin,\u201d said I, gravely, \u201cthis is beyond my understanding. I do not hesitate to say that I am amazed, and can scarcely bring myself to believe my senses. How could you possibly know that I was thinking of \u2014\u2014?\u201d I paused, to be sure\u2014beyond any doubt\u2014that he truly knew whom I had in mind.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014 of Chantilly,\u201d said he. \u201cWhy do you stop? You were just remarking to yourself that his small figure did not suit tragedy.\u201d\\n\\nThat, in truth, was exactly what had been occupying my thoughts. Chantilly had once been a cobbler of the Rue St. Denis; having grown stage-mad, he had tried to take the _r\u00f4le_ of Xerxes in Cr\u00e9billon\u2019s so-called tragedy, and had been notoriously Pasquinaded for his pains.\\n\\n\u201cTell me, for Heaven\u2019s sake,\u201d I cried, \u201cthe method\u2014if there is any method\u2014by which you have managed to sound my mind in this matter.\u201d Indeed, I was more shaken than I cared to admit.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the fruiterer,\u201d replied my friend, \u201cwho led you to the conclusion that the mender of soles was not tall enough for Xerxes _et id genus omne_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fruiterer!\u2014you surprise me\u2014I know of no fruiterer at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe man who brushed against you as we entered the street\u2014it can\u2019t have been more than fifteen minutes ago.\u201d\\n\\nI remembered then that, as we passed from the Rue C\u2014\u2014 into the very thoroughfare where we were standing, a fruiterer with a large basket of apples balanced on his head had nearly knocked me down by accident; but I could not see how that had anything to do with Chantilly.\"}"
