{"request_id":"81a15ca9907a62ec1914a60f","rewritten_text":"But the very thought that once steadied me at the start of my career now drags me still farther into the mire. All my conjectures and hopes amount to nothing; and, like the archangel who longed for omnipotence, I find myself bound to an endless hell. My imagination burned bright, yet my powers of reasoning and practical execution were equally fierce; it was through the joining of these gifts that I conceived the plan and carried out the making of a man. Even now I cannot recall, without agitation, the visions I had while the work remained unfinished. In my mind I walked through heaven—now triumphant in what I could do, now consumed by the thought of what those powers would bring about. From my earliest years I was filled with lofty hopes and a proud ambition; yet see how far I have sunk! Oh, my friend—if you had known me as I once was, you would not recognize me in this condition of disgrace. Despondency seldom found a place in my heart; some high destiny seemed to bear me onward, until I fell—and never, never again to rise.

Must I then lose this admirable being? I have longed for a friend; I have searched for someone who would share my feelings and love me. Look—on these desert seas I have found such a one, yet I fear I have gained him only to learn his worth and then to lose him. I would persuade him to take up life again, but he rejects the very notion.

“I thank you, Walton,” he said, “for your kind intentions toward so wretched a creature; but when you speak of new bonds and fresh affections, do you suppose that anything can take the place of those who are gone? Can any man be to me what Clerval was, or any woman what Elizabeth was? Even when the heart is not stirred by some superior excellence, the companions of our childhood still hold a certain sway over our minds that scarcely any later friend can win. They know the temper of our earliest days, which, though it may be altered afterward, is never truly wiped away; and they can weigh our actions with far surer judgment regarding the honesty of our motives. A brother or sister cannot, unless such signs were shown early, suspect the other of fraud or deceit, whereas another friend—however strongly one may be attached—may, despite himself, be viewed with suspicion. Yet I had friends who were dear not merely from habit and association, but from their own merits; and wherever I am, the gentle voice of my Elizabeth and the talk of Clerval will be heard in my ear. They are dead, and in such a solitude only one feeling can persuade me to keep my life. If I were engaged in some great undertaking, some design of extensive benefit to my fellow creatures, then I might live to carry it out.”"}

