          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"722f62cc061a5ba2d89c889e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her predicament was, in truth, unlike any ordinary trial and full of perplexity. Though she burned with the keenest longing to pierce the mystery that shrouded Oliver\u2019s past, she could not help but treat as sacred the trust which the wretched woman she had just spoken with had placed in her\u2014trust given to her as a young, artless girl. Her words and manner had gone straight to Rose Maylie\u2019s heart; and, blended with her affection for her young charge, and no less fervent in its sincerity, was her earnest desire to bring the outcast back toward repentance and hope.\\n\\nThey meant to stay in London only three days before setting out, for some weeks, to a far part of the coast. It was now midnight of the first day. What plan could she settle upon that might be carried out within forty-eight hours? Or how could she delay the journey without arousing suspicion?\\n\\nMr. Losberne was with them, and would remain so for the next two days; yet Rose knew too well the impetuous temper of the excellent gentleman, and she saw too clearly the anger with which, at the first burst of his indignation, he would view the very means of Oliver\u2019s recapture, to entrust him with the secret\u2014especially when her plea in the girl\u2019s behalf could not be supported by any seasoned person. All this called for the utmost caution and the most careful circumspection in what she communicated to Mrs. Maylie, whose first impulse would surely be to call a consultation with the worthy doctor. As for turning to any legal adviser, even had she known how to do so, it was hardly to be considered for the same reason. Once the idea came to her of seeking help from Harry; but at once she remembered their last parting, and it seemed to her unworthy to summon him back\u2014when, as the tears rose to her eyes and she followed the thought further\u2014he might already have learned to forget her, and to be happier away.\\n\\nBothered by these shifting reflections\u2014now leaning toward one course, now recoiling from it, and then from all again as each new consideration presented itself\u2014Rose spent a sleepless, troubled night. After thinking it over again the next day, she reached a desperate decision: she would consult Harry.\\n\\n\u201cIf it hurts him,\u201d she told herself, \u201cto return here, then it will hurt me too! But perhaps he won\u2019t come. He may write, or he may come and yet, with studied care, avoid meeting me\u2014as he did when he left. I hardly expected he would; still, it is better for both of us.\u201d With that, Rose let the pen fall and turned aside, as though the very paper that was to serve as her message ought not to witness her tears.\"}"
