          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bde794a0ddc249e104d9069b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Among the public buildings in a certain town\u2014one I will not name, for reasons I won\u2019t bother to explain\u2014there is one old and common place found in nearly every town, large or small: a workhouse. In that workhouse, Oliver Twist was born. It happened on a day and date I don\u2019t need to repeat, since it can\u2019t matter to the reader at this point. What does matter is the death that is mentioned at the start of this chapter.\\n\\nFor a long time after Oliver was brought into the world of sorrow and trouble, the parish surgeon could not say for sure whether the child would live long enough to have any name at all. If he had not, then these memoirs would never have been written. And if they had been, they would have been only a couple of pages long, and would have been the shortest and truest biography ever seen.\\n\\nI don\u2019t mean to claim that being born in a workhouse is the best or most enviable thing that can happen to anyone. But in Oliver\u2019s case, it was the best outcome that could possibly have happened. The truth is, there was real trouble getting Oliver to start breathing. It was a difficult process, but it is something custom has made necessary for us to live. For a while he lay gasping on a small flock mattress, balanced between this world and the next, with the next world clearly winning.\\n\\nIf, during that short time, Oliver had been surrounded by careful grandmothers, anxious aunts, experienced nurses, and doctors who knew exactly what to do, he would almost certainly have died right away. But no one like that was there. Instead, there was only a pauper old woman, made a little confused by an unusual amount of beer, and a parish surgeon who handled these things for pay. Oliver and Nature fought it out between them. After a few struggles, Oliver finally breathed, sneezed, and then made it clear to everyone in the workhouse that the parish now had a new burden. He did this by crying as loudly as could reasonably be expected from a newborn boy who did not yet have that very useful part, and he kept crying for longer than three minutes and a quarter.\"}"
