          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"527bc5f74ce98799b180c5f7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I want to see you permanently well connected, and for that reason it would be best to have as few odd acquaintances as possible. So I say that if you should still be in this country when Mr. Martin marries, I hope you won\u2019t be pulled in by your closeness with the sisters\u2014especially not to become acquainted with his wife, who will probably be some plain farmer\u2019s daughter, with no education.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course. Yes. I don\u2019t mean to say that I think Mr. Martin would ever marry anyone who hadn\u2019t had some education\u2014and been very well brought up. Still, I\u2019m not trying to set my opinion against yours, and I\u2019m sure I won\u2019t want to know his wife. I\u2019ll always have a great regard for the Miss Martins, especially Elizabeth, and I\u2019d be very sorry to give them up, because they\u2019re just as well educated as I am. But if he marries a very ignorant, vulgar woman, then certainly I\u2019d better not visit her, if I can help it.\u201d\\n\\nEmma watched her as she spoke, and saw no alarming signs of love. The young man had been Harriet\u2019s first admirer, but Emma trusted there was no other hold on her, and that there would be no serious difficulty on Harriet\u2019s side in opposing any friendly arrangement of her own.\\n\\nThey met Mr. Martin the very next day, as they were walking on the Donwell road. He was on foot, and after looking at her with the utmost respect, he looked with the most genuine satisfaction at her companion. Emma was not sorry to have the chance to study him; and as she walked a few yards ahead while they talked, her quick eye soon became familiar enough with Mr. Robert Martin. He looked very neat, and he seemed like a sensible young man, but his appearance had no other advantage; and when she compared him with gentlemen, she thought he would lose all the ground he had gained in Harriet\u2019s favor. Harriet was not unaware of his manner; she had noticed her father\u2019s gentleness with admiration as well as wonder. Mr. Martin looked as if he didn\u2019t really know what to do with his manners.\\n\\nThey stayed together only a few minutes, because Miss Woodhouse must not be kept waiting; and then Harriet came running to her with a smiling face, in a flutter of spirits that Miss Woodhouse hoped she would settle down very soon.\\n\\n\u201cJust think of it\u2014we happened to meet him! How strange! He said it was quite a chance that he hadn\u2019t gone round by Randalls. He didn\u2019t think we ever walked this road. He thought we walked toward Randalls most days.\u201d\"}"
