          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c592614671d2e23824151d5c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without giving herself any time to reflect, and scarcely knowing what she felt, Elizabeth, the moment she had finished this letter, snatched up the next; and opening it with the utmost impatience, read on: it had been written a day after the close of the first.\\n\\n\u201cBy this time, my dearest sister, you have received my hurried letter; I hope this may be more intelligible, though I am not so much pressed for time as my head is so bewildered that I cannot answer for being coherent. Dearest Lizzy, I hardly know what I should write, but I have bad news for you, and it cannot be put off. Imprudent as a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now anxious to be assured it has actually taken place, for there is too much reason to fear they have not gone on to Scotland. Colonel Forster came yesterday, having left Brighton the day before, not many hours after the express.\\n\\nThough Lydia\u2019s short letter to Mrs. F. had led them to understand that they were going to Gretna Green, something was dropped by Denny, expressing his belief that W. never intended to go there, nor to marry Lydia at all; which was repeated to Colonel F., who, instantly alarmed, set out from B., meaning to trace their route. He traced them with ease as far as Clapham, but no farther; for on entering that place, they took themselves into a hackney-coach, and dismissed the chaise that had brought them from Epsom. After that, all that is known is that they were seen to keep on along the London road.\\n\\nI know not what to think. After making every inquiry possible on that side of London, Colonel F. went on into Hertfordshire, anxiously renewing his search at every turnpike, and at the inns in Barnet and Hatfield, but without success\u2014no such people had been seen to pass through. With the kindest concern he came on to Longbourn, and relieved our fears in a way that does him and Mrs. F. the highest credit. I am truly grieved for him and Mrs. F.; but no one can lay blame on them.\\n\\nOur distress, my dear Lizzy, is very great. My father and mother believe the worst, but I cannot bring myself to think so ill of him. Many circumstances might make it more suitable for them to be married privately in town than to pursue their first plan; and even if _he_ could entertain such a design against a young woman of Lydia\u2019s connections\u2014which is not likely\u2014how can I suppose her so lost to everything? Impossible! I grieve, however, to learn that Colonel F. is not disposed to rely on their marriage: when I spoke of my hopes, he shook his head and said he feared W. was not a man to be trusted.\u201d\"}"
