          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"334a2ea2233cc697f8d8d9c1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNever!\u201d the girl answered.\\n\\n\u201cTell me why?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFor one reason,\u201d the girl said firmly, \u201cbecause the lady knows it and will stand by me. I know she will, because I have her promise. And there\u2019s another reason too. Even though he has lived a bad life, I have lived a bad one as well. There are many of us who have stayed on the same path together, and I won\u2019t turn on them. People who might have turned on me\u2014any of them\u2014didn\u2019t, even though they were just as bad.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen,\u201d the gentleman said quickly, as if this was the exact point he had been trying to reach, \u201cput Monks into my hands, and leave him to me to deal with.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat if he turns against the others?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI promise you that if he does, and the truth is forced out of him, then the matter will end there. There must be things in Oliver\u2019s little history that would be painful to bring into public view, and once the truth is brought out, they will all be left alone.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd if it isn\u2019t?\u201d the girl asked.\\n\\n\u201cThen,\u201d the gentleman continued, \u201cthis Fagin will not be brought to justice without your consent. If that happens, I can show you reasons, I think, that would make you agree.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo I have the lady\u2019s promise for that?\u201d the girl asked.\\n\\n\u201cYou do,\u201d Rose replied. \u201cMy true and faithful pledge.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMonks would never learn how you knew what you know?\u201d the girl said after a short pause.\\n\\n\u201cNever,\u201d the gentleman answered. \u201cThe information should be used in a way that he could never even guess.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve been a liar, and I\u2019ve been among liars since I was a child,\u201d the girl said after another silence, \u201cbut I will take your word.\u201d\\n\\nAfter both of them assured her that she could do so safely, she spoke in a very low voice. It was often hard for the listener to catch even what she meant as she named, one by one, the public-house where she had been followed that night. From the way she sometimes paused, it seemed the gentleman was making quick notes of what she told him. When she had explained the place fully\u2014the best spot to watch it without drawing attention, and the night and hour when Monks was most likely to go there\u2014she stopped for a few moments, as if trying to bring his face and appearance back more clearly.\\n\\n\u201cHe is tall,\u201d the girl said. \u201cHe\u2019s strongly built, but not fat. He walks in a way that makes you notice him, and as he walks he keeps looking over his shoulder, first to one side and then to the other.\u201d\"}"
