          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8a6002df831adb8bff403eca\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sandy and I were back on the road the next morning, fresh and early. It felt wonderful to draw in air and fill one\u2019s lungs with whole, luscious barrels-ful of the blessed God\u2019s pure, dew-borne, woodland-scented breath again, after being cramped in body and mind for two days and nights in the moral and physical stink of that wretched old buzzard-roost! I mean, for me; of course the place suited Sandy well enough, since she\u2019d been raised on high life all her days.\\n\\nPoor girl\u2014her jaws had had a weary spell of rest for a while, and I expected to pay for it in the end. I was right, though she\u2019d stood by me most loyally in the castle, and had propped me up with vast, ridiculous foolishnesses that were worth more for the moment than wisdoms twice their size. So I figured she\u2019d earned the right to run her mill for a bit, if she wanted, and I didn\u2019t feel so much as a pang when she started:\\n\\n\u201cNow turn we unto Sir Marhaus that rode with the damsel of thirty winter of age southward\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you going to see if you can work up another half-stretch on the trail of the cowboys, Sandy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEven so, fair my lord.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGo on, then. I won\u2019t interrupt this time, if I can manage it. Begin over again; start fair, and shake out all your reefs, and I\u2019ll load my pipe and give it my best attention.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow turn we unto Sir Marhaus that rode with the damsel of thirty winter of age southward. And so they came into a deep forest, and by fortune they were nighted, and rode along in a deep way, and at the last they came into a courtelage where abode the duke of South Marches, and there they asked harbour. And on the morn the duke sent unto Sir Marhaus, and bad him make him ready. And so Sir Marhaus arose and armed him, and there was a mass sung afore him, and he brake his fast, and so mounted on horseback in the court of the castle, there they should do the battle. So the duke was already on horseback, clean armed, with his six sons beside him, and each had a spear in his hand; and thus they encountered it, where the duke and his two sons brake their spears upon him, but Sir Marhaus held up his spear and touched none of them. Then came the four sons, two and two, and two of them brake their spears, and so did the other two. And all this while Sir Marhaus touched them not. Then Sir Marhaus ran to the duke and smote him with his spear, so that horse and man fell to the earth.\u201d}\"}"
