          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"08d1a65adb755f03721f852c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His body was short, but he stood with remarkable uprightness, and his voice was the sweetest I had ever heard. He began his lecture with a recap of the history of chemistry and the various improvements made by different learned men, pronouncing with real fervor the names of the most distinguished discoverers. He then gave a quick overview of the science as it stood at present and explained many of its basic terms. After a few preparatory experiments, he ended with a praise of modern chemistry\u2014words I will never forget:\\n\\n\u201cThe ancient teachers of this science,\u201d said he, \u201cpromised the impossible and delivered nothing. The modern masters promise very little; they know that metals cannot be transmuted and that the elixir of life is a chimera. Yet these philosophers, whose hands seem made only to dabble in dirt, and whose eyes are meant to pore over the microscope or crucible, have truly performed miracles. They go into the hidden recesses of nature and show how she works in her hiding-places. They rise into the heavens; they have discovered how the blood circulates and the nature of the air we breathe. They have gained new powers\u2014almost unlimited. They can command the thunders of heaven, imitate an earthquake, and even mock the invisible world with shadows of its own.\u201d\\n\\nThose were the professor\u2019s words\u2014no, let me say the words of fate\u2014spoken to destroy me. As he continued, I felt as though my soul were wrestling with a tangible enemy; one by one, the different keys were turned that made up the mechanism of my being. Chord after chord was struck, and soon my mind was filled with a single thought, a single idea, a single purpose. \u201cSo much has been done,\u201d exclaimed the soul of Frankenstein; \u201cmore, far more, will I accomplish. Walking in the path already marked, I will blaze a new way, explore unknown powers, and unfold to the world the deepest mysteries of creation.\u201d\\n\\nI did not close my eyes that night. My inner self was in a state of rebellion and turmoil; I felt that order would come from it, but I had no power to bring it about. Gradually, after the morning\u2019s dawn, sleep finally came. I woke, and the thoughts of the previous night seemed like a dream. What remained was only a resolution: to return to my old studies and devote myself to a science for which I believed I had a natural talent. That same day I went to see M. Waldman. In private, his manners were even gentler and more appealing than in public; there was a certain dignity in his bearing during his lecture, but in his own home it was replaced by the greatest affability and kindness.\"}"
