          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8f1f34af5372574dfb52ccfe\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But enough of this. I shall begin my story: it is my last duty, and I pray I have strength enough to carry it through. I set down no crimes; whatever faults I have can readily be forgiven. They did not spring from wicked intent, but from a lack of judgment; and I think few would deny that, with a different course and wiser understanding, I might have escaped the misfortunes that now pursue me. My lot has been ruled by necessity\u2014by a dreadful necessity. It demanded hands stronger than mine; stronger, I believe, than any human power, to break the thick, adamantine chain that has held me. Once I breathed nothing but joy, always warmed by love and delight in goodness\u2014now it is misery alone, and it is about to end, in death. Yet I lose myself; my tale is still untold. I will stop for a few moments, wipe my dim eyes, and try to let the present, dim and heavy unhappiness fade into the sharper emotions of the past.\\n\\nI was born in England. My father was a man of rank. He had lost his father early, and was brought up by a weak mother, with every indulgence she believed proper for a gentleman of wealth. He was sent to Eton and afterward to college; and from childhood he was allowed the free use of large sums of money, so that, from his earliest years, he enjoyed that independence which a boy with such advantages at a public school will always come to possess.\\n\\nUnder these conditions his passions found rich ground to take root and grow, whether as flowers or weeds, according to what they truly were. Because he was always permitted to act for himself, his character was marked early and deeply, and showed a shifting surface on which a sharp observer might discern the seeds of virtues and of misfortunes. His careless extravagance\u2014making him throw away immense sums to satisfy passing whims, which, by their show of force, he called by the grand name of passions\u2014often appeared as boundless generosity. Still, while he earnestly attended to the needs of others, his own desires were indulged to the fullest. He gave his money, but he sacrificed none of his own wishes to those gifts; he gave his time, which he did not value, and his affections, which he was glad to have stirred into action in any way.\\n\\nI do not claim that, had his own desires been set against those of others, he would have shown an undue selfishness; but that test was never put to him. He was raised in prosperity and surrounded by all its advantages; everyone loved him and wanted to gratify him.\"}"
