          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"868242924e1f3601d894baa3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mental traits people call \u201canalytical\u201d are, by themselves, not very easy to break down and explain. We understand them mainly by what they do. We also know that, when someone has them too strongly, they become a steady source of the most intense pleasure. Just as a strong man enjoys his physical power and takes delight in exercises that make his muscles work, the analyst takes pride in that moral activity that \u201cuntangles.\u201d He enjoys even small, ordinary tasks as long as they let him use his talent. He likes puzzles, riddles, and even hieroglyphs, and in solving each one he shows a sharpness that seems almost beyond normal understanding. His results come from the very core of his method, and they often feel like intuition.\\n\\nThe ability to solve again and again may be strengthened by studying mathematics, especially its highest branch, which has been unfairly named \u201canalysis,\u201d as if that word were the best and most special one. Still, to calculate is not the same as to analyze. A chess player, for instance, can do one without the other. That is why chess is often misunderstood in how it shapes the mind. I am not writing a formal treatise; I am only setting up a somewhat unusual story with observations that come to mind at random. So I will say this: the higher powers of careful thinking are more clearly and more usefully challenged by the quiet, unshowy game of draughts than by all the elaborate, empty tricks of chess. In chess, the pieces move in different and strange ways, and they have different, shifting values. Because of that, what is merely complicated is mistaken for what is truly deep. Here, attention is pushed hard into action. If it slips for even a moment, a mistake follows, and that mistake can lead to injury or defeat. Since the possible moves are not only many but also hard to untangle, the chances of such errors grow. In nine cases out of ten, the player who wins is the one who can concentrate more, not necessarily the one who is the most sharp. In draughts, however, the moves are unique and have little variation, so the chances of careless mistakes are smaller. With attention left comparatively free, the advantage goes to the player with better real understanding. To make this less abstract, imagine a game of draughts where the pieces are reduced to four kings, so that no oversight can really happen. Then, with both players equal, it is clear that the winner must be decided by some rare, carefully chosen move\u2014something that requires real effort from the mind.\"}"
