          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9b7cf90109b1e3284efa8be6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After a while, twelve o\u2019clock thundered out from the high tower at Westminster, and with every heavy, ringing blow of the bell the night seemed to shudder. Then the railway lights died down; only one lone lamp remained, burning on like a great ruby atop a towering mast, and the din of the city thinned away.\\n\\nAt two o\u2019clock he rose and wandered toward Blackfriars. Everything looked unreal\u2014so unreal, it might as well have been a dream. The buildings across the river appeared to have been made of darkness. One might have thought that silver and shadow had remade the world. St. Paul\u2019s vast dome loomed up like a bubble in the dim air.\\n\\nAs he neared Cleopatra\u2019s Needle, he noticed a man leaning over the parapet; and when he drew closer, the fellow looked up, the gas-light falling straight on his face.\\n\\nIt was Mr. Podgers, the cheiromantist! No one could fail to recognize the fat, flabby countenance, the gold-rimmed spectacles, the sickly, weakly smile, the sensual mouth.\\n\\nLord Arthur halted. A bright notion struck him, and he slipped quietly behind. In an instant he had seized Mr. Podgers by the legs and hurled him into the Thames. There was a coarse oath, a heavy splash, and then silence. Lord Arthur peered anxiously over, but could make out nothing of the cheiromantist except a tall hat, spinning in an eddy of moonlit water. After a time even that sank, and no trace of Mr. Podgers could be seen. Once he fancied he caught sight of the bulky, misshapen form making for the stairway by the bridge, and a dreadful sense of failure seized him; but it proved only to be a reflection, and when the moon slipped out from behind a cloud it vanished. At last he seemed to grasp the decree of destiny. He let out a deep breath of relief, and Sybil\u2019s name came to his lips.\\n\\n\u201cHave you dropped anything, sir?\u201d a voice asked behind him suddenly.\\n\\nHe wheeled round and saw a policeman holding a bull\u2019s-eye lantern.\\n\\n\u201cNothing of consequence, sergeant,\u201d he replied, smiling; and hailing a passing hansom, he jumped in and told the driver to go to Belgrave Square.\\n\\nFor the next few days he swung between hope and dread. There were times when he nearly expected Mr. Podgers to come walking into the room, and yet at other moments he felt that Fate could not be so cruel to him. Twice he went to the cheiromantist\u2019s place in West Moon Street, but he could not summon the courage to ring the bell. He craved certainty, and feared it at the same time.\\n\\nAt last it arrived. He was seated in the club\u2019s smoking-room, taking tea and listening\u2014rather listlessly\u2014to Surbiton\u2019s account of the latest comic song at the Gaiety, when the waiter entered with the evening papers.\"}"
