content.text:
{"request_id":"2a9d53d3156ad3fa89e6143a","rewritten_text":"Perdita’s name was never spoken; still, I could not doubt that his wavering of purpose came from love for her. And who deserved love more than my noble-minded sister? Who could claim the hand of this self-important king better than she whose look belonged to a queen among nations—who loved him as he loved her—though disappointment had checked her ardor, and ambition fought on, in open contest with his.

We went together to the House in the evening. Raymond, though he knew their plans and prospects were to be weighed and settled in the debate expected, was light-hearted and careless. The moment we entered the coffee-room, a hum—like the sound of ten thousand hives of swarming bees—dazed us. In knots, politicians stood gathered, with anxious brows and voices loud or low. The aristocratical party, the richest and most influential men in England, seemed less stirred than the rest, because the matter was to be discussed without their interference. By the fire sat Ryland and those who backed him. Ryland was a man of little-known birth, but with immense wealth, which he had inherited from his father, a manufacturer. As a young man he had seen the king’s abdication and the joining of the two houses of Lords and Commons; he had sympathized with these popular encroachments, and it had been the work of his life to consolidate and increase them. Since then, the landed proprietors’ influence had grown; and at first Ryland had not been sorry to watch Lord Raymond’s schemes draw off many of his opponents’ partisans. But now it was going too far. The poorer nobility welcomed the return of sovereignty as an event that would put them back in possession of power and rights they had lost. The half-extinguished spirit of royalty stirred again in men’s minds; and they, willing slaves and self-made subjects, were ready to bend their necks to the yoke. Yet some upright, manly spirits still held their ground—pillars of state. Still, the word republic had grown stale to the common ear; and many, as the event would show whether it was a majority, longed for the glitter and pageantry of royalty. Ryland was stirred to resistance; he insisted that his own forbearance alone had allowed this party to grow, but the season for indulgence was over, and with a single motion of his arm he would sweep away the cobwebs that blinded his countrymen.

When Raymond entered the coffee-room, his friends greeted him with near-shouts. They closed around him, counted their numbers, and laid out the grounds for the addition of so many members, who had not yet declared themselves."}

