{"request_id":"cd7acb50e2ea7a315aef554e","rewritten_text":"Elizabeth was cast down and miserable; she no longer found pleasure in her usual employments; every kind of delight seemed to her a profanation of the dead; she believed that endless grief and tears were the proper offering due to innocence so cruelly marred and destroyed. She was no longer the same bright-hearted girl who, in earlier days, had wandered with me along the lake’s banks and spoken with rapture of our future hopes. The first of those sorrows that are sent to draw us away from the earth had come upon her, and its dulling power had stilled her dearest smiles.

“Whenever I reflect, my dear cousin,” said she, “on the wretched death of Justine Moritz, I can no longer look on the world and its affairs as I once did. Before, when I read in books, or heard from others, of vice and injustice, I took such accounts for stories of long-ago times or for evils that were only imagined; at least they were distant, and therefore more manageable to reason than to fancy. But now misery has reached me, and men appear to me as monsters who thirst for one another’s blood. Still, I must confess I am unjust. Everyone was persuaded that poor girl was guilty; and if she had truly committed the crime for which she suffered, she would have been, beyond doubt, the most depraved creature among mankind. For the sake of a few jewels, to have murdered the son of her benefactor and friend—a child whom she had nursed from its birth, and who she seemed to love as though it were her own! I could never consent to the death of any human being; yet I should certainly have thought such a person unfit to remain in the company of men. But she was innocent. I know it—I feel it. You share the same view, and that only strengthens me. Alas, Victor: when falsehood can wear so much the face of truth, who can promise themselves any certain happiness? It seems to me as though I were walking along the brink of a precipice, while thousands press toward it, striving to hurl me into the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, and the murderer goes free; he moves through the world unpunished, and perhaps even respected. Yet even if I were condemned to endure death upon the scaffold for the same crimes, I would not trade places with such a wretch.”

I listened to her words with the most intense anguish. Though not in deed, I was, in effect, the true murderer. Elizabeth saw my distress in my face, and, taking my hand with gentleness, said, “My dearest friend, you must try to steady yourself. These events have affected me—God knows how deeply—but I am not as wretched as you are.”"}

