          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8bc8d99940e4be4bf4f5dd33\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Not a word, however, passed his lips in reference to it until their visitor took his leave for the night; but the moment he was gone, he turned to his daughter and said,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cJane, I congratulate you. You will be a very happy woman.\u201d\\n\\nJane went to him at once, kissed him, and thanked him for his kindness.\\n\\n\u201cYou are a good girl,\u201d he replied, \u201cand I take real pleasure in believing you will be so happily settled. I have no doubt you will do very well together. Your tempers are by no means unlike. You are both so agreeable that nothing will ever be decided; so easygoing that every servant will cheat you; and so generous that you will always end up exceeding your income.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI hope not so. Any imprudence or thoughtlessness in money matters would be unforgivable in _me_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExceed their income! My dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d cried his wife, \u201cwhat are you talking about? Why, he has four or five thousand a year, and very likely more.\u201d Then, turning to her daughter, \u201cOh, my dear, dear Jane, I am so happy! I\u2019m sure I won\u2019t get a wink of sleep tonight. I knew how it would turn out. I always said it must be so at last. I was sure you couldn\u2019t be so beautiful for nothing! I remember, the moment I saw him when he first came into Hertfordshire last year, I thought it was likely you would end up together. Oh, he is the handsomest young man that ever was seen!\u201d\\n\\nWickham and Lydia were all forgotten. Jane was beyond competition her favorite child. At that moment she cared about nothing else. Her younger sisters soon began to press her with ideas of future happiness\u2014things she might one day be able to provide.\\n\\nMary petitioned for the use of the library at Netherfield; and Kitty begged very hard for a few balls there every winter.\\n\\nFrom this time, Bingley was, of course, a daily visitor at Longbourn; he came frequently before breakfast and always stayed until after supper\u2014unless some barbarous neighbor, whom he could not possibly detest enough, had given him an invitation to dinner, which he felt obliged to accept.\\n\\nElizabeth now had very little time to talk with her sister; for while he was there, Jane had no attention to spare for anyone else. But she found herself considerably useful to both of them during those hours of separation that must sometimes occur. In Jane\u2019s absence, he always attached himself to Elizabeth for the pleasure of talking about her; and when Bingley was gone, Jane constantly sought the same kind of relief.\\n\\n\u201cHe has made me so happy,\u201d said she one evening, \u201cby telling me that he was completely unaware of my being in town last spring!\u201d\"}"
