          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f6f018860805b4dc312de93b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs. Clay\u2019s feelings had overwhelmed her interest, and she had given up, for the young man\u2019s sake, the chance to scheme longer for Sir Walter. She has abilities, though, as well as feelings; and it is now a doubtful question whether his cunning or hers will finally carry the day\u2014whether, after preventing her from being Sir Walter\u2019s wife, he may not at last be persuaded, coaxed, and coddled into making her the wife of Sir William.\\n\\nThere can be no doubt that Sir Walter and Elizabeth were shocked and mortified by the loss of their companion, and by the discovery of their deception in her. They had their great cousins to turn to for comfort, to be sure, but they must still feel for a long time that to flatter and follow others, without being flattered and followed in return, is only a kind of half-enjoyment.\\n\\nAnne, satisfied very early on of what Lady Russell meant\u2014namely, that she should love Captain Wentworth as she ought\u2014had no other bitterness mixed into the happiness of her prospects than what came from the awareness that she had no relations to offer him that a sensible man would value. There she felt her own inferiority very sharply. The mismatch in their fortunes meant nothing; it gave her not a moment\u2019s regret. But to have no family to receive and properly judge him, no respectability, no harmony, no goodwill to offer in return for all the worth and the prompt welcome that met her in his brothers and sisters\u2014this was a source of pain as lively as her mind could well feel, even under circumstances of otherwise strong felicity. She had only two friends in the world to add to his list: Lady Russell and Mrs. Smith. To them, however, he was very disposed to attach himself. Lady Russell, despite all her earlier wrongdoings, he could now value from his heart. While he was not required to say that he believed her to have been right in originally separating them, he was ready to say almost everything else in her favor. And as for Mrs. Smith, she had claims of various kinds that recommended her quickly and permanently.\\n\\nHer recent good offices to Anne had been enough on their own, and their marriage, instead of costing her one friend, secured her two. She was their earliest visitor in their settled life. And Captain Wentworth, by putting her in the way of recovering her husband\u2019s property in the West Indies\u2014by writing for her, acting for her, and seeing her through all the small difficulties of the case with the energy and determination of a fearless man and a steadfast friend\u2014fully repaid the services she had rendered, or ever meant to render, to his wife.\"}"
