          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d99fad4db62be6dd602de716\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The next day was set for the event, so that our honor would not be harmed by delay. The next day Drummle showed up with a polite little confession in Estella\u2019s hand. He had the \u201chonour\u201d of dancing with her several times. That left me with no real choice but to regret that I had been \u201cbetrayed into a warmth which,\u201d and, overall, to reject the idea that I could be found anywhere. Drummle and I then sat facing each other, snorting, for an hour. Meanwhile, the Grove kept contradicting itself, and at last it was announced that the promotion of good feeling had moved forward at an astonishing speed.\\n\\nI say this lightly, but it was not a small thing for me. I cannot properly explain the pain I felt at the thought of Estella showing any kindness to such a contemptible, clumsy, sulky fool, far below the average. Even now, I believe my suffering came from the pure generosity and unselfish love I felt for her. I could not bear the idea of her lowering herself to that kind of dog. Of course, I would have been miserable no matter who she chose; but if she had chosen someone more worthy, my distress would have been different in kind and in degree.\\n\\nIt was not hard for me to learn what was happening, and I soon found out that Drummle had started following her closely, and that she let him. After a little while, he was always chasing her. He and I crossed paths every day. He kept at it in a dull, stubborn way, and Estella kept him there\u2014sometimes encouraging him, sometimes discouraging him, sometimes almost flattering him, sometimes openly despising him, sometimes acting as if she knew him very well, and sometimes barely remembering who he was.\\n\\nThe Spider\u2014Mr. Jaggers\u2019s name for him\u2014was used to waiting in ambush, and he had the patience of his kind. He also had a thick confidence in his money and in his family\u2019s importance, which sometimes did him good service, almost in place of real focus and steady purpose. So the Spider, watching Estella with stubborn persistence, outlasted many brighter insects. He would often hold back until the exact right moment, then suddenly drop in.\\n\\nAt one Assembly Ball in Richmond\u2014there were Assembly Balls in most places then\u2014Estella had outshone every other beauty. Yet this clumsy Drummle hovered around her so constantly, and she tolerated it so much, that I decided to speak to her about him. I took the next chance, when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home. She was sitting apart among some flowers, ready to leave.\"}"
