          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e0ad5441a7854a78006ead09\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Right in front of him, in the weak moonlight, he saw an old man with a frightening look. His eyes burned red, like coals. Long grey hair hung over his shoulders in tangled, matted locks. His clothes were cut in an old-fashioned style, but they were dirty and torn. Heavy manacles and rusty chains hung from his wrists and ankles.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear sir,\u201d said Mr. Otis, \u201cI really must insist that you oil those chains. I\u2019ve brought you a small bottle of the Tammany Rising Sun Lubricator for that purpose. They say it works completely the first time you use it, and there are several testimonials on the label from some of our most important local divines. I\u2019ll leave it here for you by the bedroom candles, and I\u2019ll be glad to get you more if you need it.\u201d With that, the United States Minister set the bottle down on a marble table, shut his door, and went to bed.\\n\\nFor a moment, the Canterville ghost stood perfectly still, filled with natural anger. Then he grabbed the bottle and smashed it hard on the polished floor. He ran down the corridor, groaning in a hollow way and casting out a sickly green light. But just as he reached the top of the great oak staircase, a door flew open. Two small figures in white robes appeared, and a large pillow shot past his head. Clearly, there was no time to waste. He quickly used the Fourth Dimension of Space to escape, vanished through the wall paneling, and the house fell silent.\\n\\nWhen he reached a small secret room in the left wing, he leaned against a patch of moonlight to catch his breath and start thinking clearly about what had happened. In his long, uninterrupted career of three hundred years, he had never been insulted so badly. He remembered the Dowager Duchess, whom he had scared into a fit while she stood before the mirror in her lace and diamonds. He remembered the four housemaids, who had broken down into hysterics simply because he grinned at them through the curtains of one of the spare bedrooms. He remembered the parish rector, whose candle he had blown out one night when he came home late from the library, and who had been under Sir William Gull\u2019s care ever since, a true martyr to nervous troubles. And he remembered old Madame de Tremouillac, who, after waking up early one morning and seeing a skeleton sitting in an armchair by the fire reading her diary, had been stuck in bed for six weeks with brain fever. After she recovered, she returned to the Church and cut ties with that notorious skeptic, Monsieur de Voltaire.\"}"
