ght\u2019s absence on a tour to the seaside, which was the plan. John and Isabella, and every other friend, agreed in approving it. But Mr. Woodhouse\u2014how was Mr. Woodhouse to be brought to consent?\u2014he, who had never yet spoken of their marriage except as something distant.\\n\\nAt first, when the subject was raised, he was so wretched that they were nearly without hope. A second mention, however, caused him less pain. He began to believe it was to be, and that he could not prevent it\u2014a hopeful movement of the mind toward resignation. Yet even then he was not happy. Indeed, he seemed so much the contrary, that Emma\u2019s courage failed. She could not bear to see him suffering, or to know him imagining himself neglected; and though her reason almost agreed with the assurances of both Mr. Knightleys, that once the event was over his distress would soon be over too, she still faltered\u2014she could not go on.\"}"
