          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"60898ae0ee7efe3a191587b8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Well, three or four months went by, and it was well into winter now. I\u2019d been to school most of the time, and I could spell and read and write a little, and I could say the multiplication table up to six times seven is thirty-five. I don\u2019t think I could ever get any farther than that, even if I lived forever. I don\u2019t put much faith in mathematics anyway.\\n\\nAt first I hated school, but after a while I got to where I could stand it. Whenever I got unusually tired, I played hooky, and the hiding I got the next day did me good and cheered me up. So the longer I went to school, the easier it got. I was getting used to the widow\u2019s ways, too, and they weren\u2019t so rough on me. Living in a house and sleeping in a bed pulled on me pretty hard most of the time, but before the cold weather I used to slip out and sleep in the woods sometimes, and that was a relief. I liked the old ways best, but I was starting to like the new ones a little too. The widow said I was coming along slow but sure, and doing very satisfactory. She said she wasn\u2019t ashamed of me.\\n\\nOne morning I happened to tip over the salt-cellar at breakfast. I reached for it as fast as I could, to throw it over my left shoulder and keep off the bad luck, but Miss Watson was already there and stopped me. She said, \u201cTake your hands away, Huckleberry; you\u2019re always making a mess!\u201d The widow spoke up for me, but that wasn\u2019t going to keep off the bad luck, I knew that well enough. After breakfast I went out feeling worried and shaky, wondering where it was going to land on me and what it was going to be. There are ways to ward off some kinds of bad luck, but this wasn\u2019t one of those kinds, so I never tried to do anything. I just kept going, low-spirited and on the lookout.\\n\\nI went down to the front garden and climbed over the stile where you go through the high board fence. There was an inch of new snow on the ground, and I saw somebody\u2019s tracks. They had come up from the quarry and stood around the stile for a while, and then went on around the garden fence. It was strange they hadn\u2019t come in after standing there so long. I couldn\u2019t figure it out. Somehow it felt very curious. I was going to follow them, but I bent down to look at the tracks first.\"}"
