          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5159225bbfdf978ae0d6457a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was Lady Windermere\u2019s last reception before Easter, and Bentinck House was even more crowded than usual. Six Cabinet Ministers had arrived from the Speaker\u2019s Lev\u00e9e, wearing their stars and ribands, and all the pretty women were dressed in their finest clothes. At the end of the picture-gallery stood the Princess Sophia of Carlsr\u00fche, a heavy, Tartar-looking woman with tiny black eyes and wonderful emeralds. She was talking bad French loudly and laughing too much at everything people said to her. It was, without doubt, a strange mix of people. Beautiful peeresses chatted easily with loud Radicals, popular preachers brushed past eminent sceptics, and a group of bishops kept moving from room to room, following a stout prima-donna. On the staircase stood several Royal Academicians, disguised as artists, and it was said that at one time the supper-room was packed with geniuses. In fact, it was one of Lady Windermere\u2019s best nights, and the Princess stayed until nearly half past eleven.\\n\\nThe moment the Princess left, Lady Windermere went back to the picture-gallery. There, a celebrated political economist was solemnly explaining the scientific theory of music to an angry virtuoso from Hungary, and Lady Windermere began talking to the Duchess of Paisley. She looked wonderfully beautiful, with her grand ivory throat, her large blue forget-me-not eyes, and her heavy coils of golden hair. Or perhaps not\u2014she wasn\u2019t that pale straw color that people call gold these days. Her hair was real gold, like gold woven into sunlight or hidden in amber. It gave her face the look of a saint, with just enough of the pull of a sinner. She was a fascinating study of psychology. Early in life she had learned an important truth: nothing looks so innocent as an indiscretion. Through a series of reckless adventures\u2014many of them harmless\u2014she had gained all the benefits of having a reputation. She had changed her husband more than once; Debrett even credits her with three marriages. But since she had never changed her lover, the world had long stopped talking about her as a scandal. Now she was forty, had no children, and had that strong passion for pleasure that is the secret of staying young.\\n\\nSuddenly she looked around the room eagerly and said, in her clear contralto voice, \u201cWhere is my cheiromantist?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour what, Gladys?\u201d the Duchess asked, startled before she could help it.\\n\\n\u201cMy cheiromantist, Duchess. I can\u2019t live without him right now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Gladys! You\u2019re always so original,\u201d the Duchess murmured, trying to remember what a cheiromantist really was, and hoping it wasn\u2019t the same thing as a cheiropodist.\\n\\n\u201cHe comes to see my hand twice a week, regularly,\u201d Lady Windermere went on. \u201cAnd he\u2019s very interesting about it.\u201d\"}"
