          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c143db9be48dc6be68e2cbf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the morning, she was found dead in her bed, and it seemed she had suffered no pain. Sir Mathew Reid was sent for right away, but there was nothing anyone could do. She was to be buried on the 22nd at Beauchamp Chalcote. A few days before she died, she made her will. She left Lord Arthur her small house in Curzon Street, along with all her furniture, personal belongings, and pictures. The only exceptions were her collection of miniatures, which were to go to her sister, Lady Margaret Rufford, and her amethyst necklace, which Sybil Merton was to have. The property itself was not worth much, but Mr. Mansfield, the solicitor, was very worried that Lord Arthur should come back as soon as possible, if he could. There were many bills to pay, and Lady Clementina had never kept careful accounts.\\n\\nLord Arthur was deeply moved by Lady Clementina\u2019s thoughtful kindness toward him, and he felt that Mr. Podgers had a lot to answer for. Still, his love for Sybil was stronger than anything else. The fact that he believed he had done his duty gave him peace and comfort. When he reached Charing Cross, he felt completely happy.\\n\\nThe Mertons welcomed him warmly. Sybil made him promise that nothing would ever come between them again, and they set the wedding for the 7th of June. To him, life felt bright and wonderful again, and all his old happiness returned.\\n\\nBut one day, while he was going through the house in Curzon Street with Lady Clementina\u2019s solicitor and Sybil herself\u2014burning old packets of faded letters and emptying drawers of odd rubbish\u2014Sybil suddenly let out a little cry of delight.\\n\\n\u201cWhat have you found, Sybil?\u201d Lord Arthur asked. He looked up from what he was doing and smiled.\\n\\n\u201cThis lovely little silver bonbonni\u00e8re, Arthur. Isn\u2019t it quaint and Dutch? Please, give it to me! I know amethysts won\u2019t suit me until I\u2019m over eighty.\u201d\\n\\nIt was the box that had held the aconitine.\\n\\nLord Arthur started, and a faint blush rose in his cheeks. He had almost forgotten what he had done. It felt like a strange coincidence that Sybil\u2014who he had done all that terrible worrying for\u2014should be the first to bring it back to his mind.\\n\\n\u201cOf course you can have it, Sybil. I gave it to poor Lady Clem myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, thank you, Arthur. And may I have the bonbon too? I didn\u2019t know Lady Clementina liked sweets. I thought she was far too intellectual.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur went deadly pale, and a terrible thought crossed his mind.\"}"
