 had brought in better profits, and their fortune in general had smiled on them. With their wealth, their expectations grew; their lack of a larger house, and their desire for more company. They added to their house, to the number of servants, and to their expenses of every kind; and by now, in both fortune and style of living, they were second only to the family at Hartfield. Their love of society, and their new dining-room, prepared everyone for their hosting dinner-parties; and a few gatherings, mostly among the single men, had already taken place. The regular and best families Emma could hardly imagine they would presume to invite\u2014neither Donwell, nor Hartfield, nor Randalls. Nothing should tempt her to go, if they did; and she regretted that her father\u2019s well-known habits would make her refusal carry less meaning than she would have liked.\"}"
