          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"14cbfcdf84fcb54ea2ce3256\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No bringing it up by hand, then. Not a bit of it!\u201d\\n\\nJoe offered me more gravy, which I was afraid to take.\\n\\n\u201cHe was a world of trouble to you, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Mrs. Hubble, sympathizing with my sister.\\n\\n\u201cTrouble?\u201d my sister repeated. \u201cTrouble?\u201d Then she launched into a terrifying list of every illness I\u2019d been guilty of, every act of sleeplessness I\u2019d committed, every high place I\u2019d tumbled from, every low place I\u2019d tumbled into, every injury I\u2019d done myself, every time she\u2019d wished me in my grave\u2014and how stubbornly I\u2019d refused to go there.\\n\\nI think the Romans must have aggravated one another a great deal with their noses. Maybe that\u2019s why they became so restless. In any case, Mr. Wopsle\u2019s Roman nose so aggravated me during the recital of my misdeeds that I wanted to pull it until he howled. But everything I\u2019d endured up to then was nothing compared with the awful feelings that took hold of me when the pause came after my sister finished\u2014during which pause everyone looked at me (as I felt painfully aware) with indignation and disgust.\\n\\n\u201cYet,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, gently steering the conversation back to the subject they\u2019d drifted from, \u201cPork\u2014regarded as biled\u2014is rich, too; ain\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave a little brandy, uncle,\u201d my sister said.\\n\\nOh, heavens\u2014it had finally come! He\u2019d find it was weak, he\u2019d say it was weak, and I was finished! I gripped the leg of the table under the cloth with both hands and waited for what would happen.\\n\\nMy sister went for the stone bottle, came back with it, and poured his brandy out\u2014no one else took any. The poor man fidgeted with his glass, picked it up, looked at it in the light, set it down again, and dragged out my misery. All the while, Mrs. Joe and Joe were briskly clearing the table for the pie and pudding.\\n\\nI couldn\u2019t take my eyes off him. Always holding tight to the table leg with my hands and feet, I watched the miserable man finger his glass playfully, lift it, smile, throw his head back, and drink the brandy straight down. Immediately afterward, the whole company was seized with unspeakable alarm, because he sprang to his feet, turned around several times in an appalling, spasmodic whooping-cough dance, and rushed out the door. Then he became visible through the window, violently plunging and coughing up everything, making the most hideous faces, and acting as if he\u2019d completely lost his mind.\\n\\nI held on tight while Mrs. Joe and Joe ran to him.\"}"
