          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5cf65dbd26eb11ca8d494939\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The day went by much as the one before it had. Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley had spent several hours of the morning with the invalid, who continued, though only slowly, to get better; and in the evening Elizabeth joined their party in the drawing-room. The loo table, however, was not to be seen. Mr. Darcy was at his writing, and Miss Bingley, seated close by, watched the progress of his letter, while repeatedly diverting his attention with messages to her sister. Mr. Hurst and Mr. Bingley were engaged at piquet, and Mrs. Hurst sat by, observing their play.\\n\\nElizabeth took up some needlework, and found herself entertained enough by what passed between Darcy and his companion. The lady\u2019s constant praises\u2014of his hand-writing, of the evenness of his lines, of the length of his letter\u2014delivered with a perfect air of indifference to the effect they produced, made a sort of dialogue, and matched exactly her own estimate of each point.\\n\\n\u201cHow pleased Miss Darcy must be to receive such a letter!\u201d\\n\\nHe offered no reply.\\n\\n\u201cYou write astonishingly fast.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re mistaken. I write rather slowly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow many letters you must have occasion to put down in the course of a year! Letters of business, too! I should think them dreadful.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen it is lucky, at least, that they fall to my share rather than to yours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPray, tell your sister that I long to see her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have already told her once, at your request.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid you don\u2019t like your pen. Let me fix it for you. I mend pens remarkably well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you\u2014but I always mend my own.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow do you manage to write so evenly?\u201d\\n\\nHe was silent.\\n\\n\u201cTell your sister I am delighted to hear of her improvement on the harp, and do let her know that I am quite carried away by her pretty little design for a table; I think it infinitely better than Miss Grantley\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you allow me to postpone your raptures until I write again? At present I have not space enough to do them justice.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, it matters nothing. I shall see her in January. But do you always send her such charming long letters, Mr. Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey are generally long; but whether they are always charming, that is not for me to decide.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is a maxim with me that anyone who can write a long letter with ease cannot write badly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat won\u2019t do as a compliment to Darcy, Caroline,\u201d cried her brother; \u201cbecause he does not write with ease. He thinks too much about words of four syllables. Don\u2019t you, Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy manner of writing is very different from yours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d said Miss Bingley, \u201cCharles writes in the most careless fashion imaginable.\u201d\"}"
