{"request_id":"00632a69cd19bf114a14b2a1","rewritten_text":"My father had been everything to me, and his memory was the life of my life. I might feel gratitude toward someone else, but I could never again love or hope the way I had; it was all suffering. Even my pleasures were endured, not enjoyed. I felt like a solitary place among mountains, shut in on every side by steep, black precipices—where no ray of heat could reach, and from which there was no way out to sunnier fields. So it was that although the spirit of friendship soothed me for a while, it could not restore me. It came like a gentle visitation; it left, and I hardly noticed the loss. The spirit of existence was dead within me; so don’t be surprised that when it came I did not welcome it more gladly, or when it departed I did not lament more bitterly the best gift of heaven—a friend.

The name of my friend was Woodville. I will briefly tell you his history so you can judge how cold my heart must have been—not to be warmed by his eloquent words and tender sympathy. And you will see how, since he was also deeply unhappy, we were well suited to be mutual consolation to each other, if I had not been hardened into stone by the Medusa head of Misery. Woodville’s misfortunes were not rooted in the very core of his heart like mine. His was a natural grief: it did not destroy, but purified the heart, and from it—once the shadow had passed—he might shine forth brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and had received a classical education. He was one of the very few whom fortune favors from birth—on whom she bestows, without limit, all gifts of intellect and person, and who, under her special protection, is not allowed to be touched by even the smallest imperfection or by any disappointment, however brief. It seemed she had shaped his mind into an excellence that no dross could tarnish, and his understanding was such that no error could twist it. His genius was transcendent; when it rose like a bright star in the east, all eyes turned toward it in admiration. He was a Poet. That name has been degraded so often that it cannot convey what he truly was. He was like a poet of old whom the muses had crowned in his cradle, and on whose lips bees had fed. As he walked among other men, he seemed surrounded by a heavenly halo that separated him from them and lifted him above them. It was his surpassing beauty, the dazzling fire of his eyes, and his words—rich in accents that wrapped the listener in mute and ecstatic wonder—that made him rise above all others, so that in his presence they seemed only formed to serve his superior excellence."}

