          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6e4cf99afc0ef0418e6286c0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"One day, while I was busy with my books and Mr. Pocket, a note arrived in the mail. Even before I opened it, the outside of the envelope made me feel wildly nervous. I had never seen the handwriting before, but I knew whose it was. The note did not start in any usual way, like \u201cDear Mr. Pip,\u201d or \u201cDear Pip,\u201d or \u201cDear Sir,\u201d or anything like that. Instead, it said:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am to come to London the day after to-morrow by the midday coach. I believe it was settled you should meet me. At all events, Miss Havisham has that impression, and I write to you because of it. She sends you her regard.\\n\\n\u201cYours, ESTELLA.\u201d\\n\\nIf I had had time, I would probably have bought several new suits of clothes for the occasion. But since I didn\u2019t, I had to make do with what I already owned. My appetite disappeared at once, and I couldn\u2019t find any peace or rest until the day finally came. But even when it arrived, I was no better. I became so restless that I kept going to the coach office in Wood Street, Cheapside, before the coach had even left the Blue Boar in our town. I knew, in a sensible way, that I understood this perfectly well, yet I still felt it was not safe to let the coach office out of my sight for more than five minutes. In that state of irrational worry, I had already spent the first half-hour of a watch that was supposed to last four or five hours when Wemmick ran into me.\\n\\n\u201cHalloa, Mr. Pip,\u201d he said. \u201cHow do you do? I wouldn\u2019t have thought this was your job.\u201d\\n\\nI told him I was waiting to meet someone who was coming by coach, and I asked how things were at the Castle and with the Aged.\\n\\n\u201cBoth are doing well, thank you,\u201d Wemmick said. \u201cAnd especially the Aged. He\u2019s in wonderful spirits. He\u2019ll be eighty-two next birthday. I\u2019m thinking of firing my cannon eighty-two times, if the neighborhood doesn\u2019t complain, and if that cannon of mine can handle the strain. But this isn\u2019t London talk. Where do you think I\u2019m going?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo the office?\u201d I said, because he was heading that way.\\n\\n\u201cNext thing to it,\u201d Wemmick replied. \u201cI\u2019m going to Newgate. We\u2019re in a banker\u2019s-parcel situation right now, and I\u2019ve been down the road to take a look at what\u2019s going on. After that, I had to have a word or two with our client.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid your client commit the robbery?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cBless your soul and body, no,\u201d Wemmick answered, very dryly. \u201cBut he\u2019s accused of it. So could you or I be. Either of us might be accused of it, you know.\u201d\"}"
