{"request_id":"f63543c663e3aaa3689abc46","rewritten_text":"“By eight o’clock, I had actually risen to seventeen miles above the earth. It seemed clear to me that my speed of ascent was not only increasing, but that the change would have been noticeable even if I had not released the ballast, which I did. My head and ears hurt again, coming back in sharp bursts, and I was still bleeding from my nose now and then. Still, overall I felt much less pain than I might have expected. Breathing, however, became harder and harder, and every breath came with an unpleasant, sudden spasm in my chest. I took out the condensing apparatus and got it ready for immediate use.

“The view of the earth at this point in my climb was truly beautiful. To the west, north, and south, as far as I could see, there was a vast stretch of ocean that looked calm. With every moment it grew a deeper blue and began to look slightly curved. Far to the east, the islands of Great Britain were still clearly visible, along with the entire Atlantic coasts of France and Spain, and a small part of the northern continent of Africa. Not a single building could be seen, and even the proudest cities of mankind had completely disappeared from the earth. From the rock of Gibraltar, now only a faint speck, the dark Mediterranean Sea spread out to the east as far as my eyes could reach. It was dotted with shining islands, like stars in the sky. The whole mass of water seemed, at last, to pour straight over the edge of the horizon, and I found myself leaning forward, listening for the echoes of a great waterfall. Above me, the sky was a deep, jet-black, and the stars were bright and clear.

“Since the pigeons seemed to be suffering a lot at this time, I decided to let them go. First, I untied one of them, a beautiful gray-mottled pigeon, and set him on the rim of the wicker basket. He looked extremely uneasy. He kept glancing around anxiously, fluttering his wings, and making loud cooing sounds, but he would not trust himself to leave the car. I finally picked him up and threw him about half a dozen yards from the balloon. As I expected, I thought he would try to descend, but he did not. Instead, he fought hard to get back, crying out in very sharp, piercing screams. In the end, he managed to return to his old spot on the rim. But almost immediately, his head fell onto his breast, and he died inside the car.”"}

