          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"82f3c84f748f662e6480bfff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Shall I speak to them? Or to Miss Bates?\u2014She is still the nearer one. And I cannot help thinking that Miss Bates may be just as likely as anyone to catch the temper of the rest of the people. Still, we do want a larger council. Why not go and ask Miss Bates to come in with us?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014if you please,\u201d said Mrs. Weston, with a little hesitation, \u201cif you believe she will be of any service.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ll get nothing worth the trouble from Miss Bates,\u201d Emma replied. \u201cShe\u2019ll be all smiles and gratitude, but she won\u2019t tell you anything. She won\u2019t even listen to your questions. I see no use in consulting Miss Bates.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut she is so entertaining\u2014so very entertaining! I do enjoy hearing Miss Bates talk. And you needn\u2019t bring the whole family, you know.\u201d\\n\\nAt this moment Mr. Weston joined them; and when he heard what was proposed, he gave it his full approval.\\n\\n\u201cAye, do, Frank.\u2014Go and fetch Miss Bates, and let us settle it at once. She\u2019ll be pleased with the plan, I\u2019m sure; and I don\u2019t know a better person for showing us how to get rid of difficulties. Fetch Miss Bates. We\u2019re getting a little too particular. She\u2019s a standing example of how to be happy. But fetch them both\u2014invite them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBoth, sir! Can the old lady?\u201d...\\n\\n\u201cThe old lady! No, the young lady, of course. I shall think you a great blockhead, Frank, if you bring the aunt without the niece.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I beg your pardon, sir. I didn\u2019t at once remember. Undoubtedly, if you wish it, I will try to persuade them both.\u201d And off he ran.\\n\\nLong before he came back, bringing the short, neat, brisk-moving aunt, and her elegant niece, Mrs. Weston\u2014sweet-tempered as she was, and a good wife besides\u2014had gone over the passage again, and found the troubles in it far less than she had first imagined; indeed, they were very trifling. So the question of decision was at an end. As for everything else, at least in their thoughts, it was perfectly smooth. The smaller arrangements\u2014table and chair, lights and music, tea and supper\u2014either arranged themselves, or were left as mere trifles to be settled at any time between Mrs. Weston and Mrs. Stokes. Everyone invited was certainly to come; Frank had already written to Enscombe to propose staying a few days beyond his fortnight, which could not possibly be refused. And it would be a delightful dance.\\n\\nMost cordially, when Miss Bates arrived, did she agree that it must be so. As a counsellor she was not needed; but as an approver\u2014(a far safer part)\u2014she was truly welcome. Her approval, at once general and minute, warm and unceasing, could not fail to please; and for another half-hour they were all walking from room to room, some suggesting, some attending, and all in happy expectation of what was to come.\"}"
