          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eeb67ce3ba204a18573484e4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Thus I spent two years. Day after day, so many hundreds of days passed; nothing obvious changed on the outside, but a few things slowly worked on my mind as I drifted closer to death. I began to study more. I began to understand others more, through the thoughts they expressed in books. I read history, and in doing so I lost the sense of being only myself, the individuality I had once had. Perhaps, as the feeling of immediate suffering faded, I became more human. Even solitude began to lose some of its appeal. I started to wish again for sympathy. Not because I wanted to join the crowd, but because I wanted one friend to love me.\\n\\nYou might say that I was gradually becoming ready to return to society. I do not think so. The sympathy I wanted had to be pure, free from the pull of outside circumstances. In the world, I could not help running into the rough, everyday things that mix even with the best feelings. Believe me, at that time I was less fit for any real closeness with my fellow people than before. When I left them, they had tormented me in the same way that pain and sickness can torment\u2014something outside the mind that hurts it, and that I wanted to throw off. But now I would have wanted sympathy. I would have wanted to tie my soul to someone else\u2019s, and I would have had to prepare myself for disappointment and suffering. I was as delicate as a sensitive plant, all nerve. I did not want sympathy and help for ambition or wisdom. I wanted sweet, mutual affection\u2014smiles to lift me and gentle words of comfort. I wanted one heart where I could pour out my complaints without restraint, and where, if the soil were heavenly, good fruit might grow even from such bad seed.\\n\\nBut how could I find this? The love that is the soul of friendship is a soft spirit, rarely found unless two kind people are joined from early youth, or unless they are bound together by shared suffering and shared pursuits. It comes to a few, chosen ones, without being asked for or even noticed. It falls like gentle dew on places that had been barren before, and then those places become fertile for all sweet plants. But when it is desired, it flies. It mocks the prayers of those who long for it. It will give, but it will not be sought.\\n\\nI knew all this, and I did not go looking for sympathy. Yet there, on my lonely heath, under my humble roof, with nothing around me but desert, it came to me like a winter sunbeam\u2014something that brightens and helps dissolve the snow that has piled up. Alas, the sun shone on blighted fruit. I did not come back to life under its light, because I was too completely broken to feel any kindness in it.\"}"
