          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fbe7cb9b3f6ef6f0b70e8f09\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without giving herself time to think, and scarcely knowing what she felt, Elizabeth, having finished this letter, immediately grabbed the other one. She opened it with the utmost impatience and read as follows: it had been written a day later than the end of the first.\\n\\n\u201cBy this time, my dearest sister, you have received my hurried letter; I hope this one is clearer, but though I\u2019m not short on time, my head is so confused that I can\u2019t answer for being coherent. Dearest Lizzy, I hardly know what I would write, but I have bad news for you, and it cannot be delayed. As imprudent as a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now anxious to be sure it has taken place, for there is far too much reason to fear they are not gone to Scotland.\\n\\nColonel Forster came yesterday. He left Brighton the day before, not many hours after the express. Although Lydia\u2019s short letter to Mrs. F. made it clear they were going to Gretna Green, something Denny said led them to believe W. never intended to go there, or to marry Lydia at all. He repeated this to Colonel F., who, instantly alarmed, set off from B. intending to trace their route. He traced them easily as far as Clapham, but no farther; for when they entered that place, they got into a hackney-coach and dismissed the chaise that had brought them from Epsom.\\n\\nAll that is known after this is that they were seen to continue along the London road. I don\u2019t know what to think. After making every possible inquiry on that side of London, Colonel F. went on into Hertfordshire, anxiously renewing his search at every turnpike and at the inns in Barnet and Hatfield, but without success\u2014no such people had been seen to pass through.\\n\\nWith the kindest concern he came on to Longbourn and broke his fears to us in a way that reflects most creditably on his heart. I am sincerely grieved for him and Mrs. F.; but no one can blame them. Our distress, my dear Lizzy, is very great. My father and mother believe the worst, but I can\u2019t think so badly of him. Many circumstances might make it more suitable for them to be married privately in town than to follow their first plan; and even if he could form such a design against a young woman of Lydia\u2019s connections\u2014which is not likely\u2014can I suppose her so lost to everything? Impossible! I grieve, however, to find that Colonel F. is not disposed to rely on their marriage: when I expressed my hopes, he shook his head and said he feared W. was not a man to be trusted.\"}"
