{"request_id":"ac08700d9864c212e8952489","rewritten_text":"Do not misunderstand me. I was never truly angry. I was always aware of what was happening to me when my wild thoughts seemed to push me toward madness, and I never showed them to anyone. I kept them to myself, in silence and in solitude. People around me did not see any of this. They only saw a poor girl with a broken spirit, who spoke in a low, gentle voice. From under her lowered eyelids, tears would sometimes slip out, even though she tried to hide them. She was someone who liked being alone and who shrank from being watched. She never smiled—no, I never smiled. That was all they could see.

Well, I escaped. I left my guardian’s house, and I was never heard of again. From the letters I left and other circumstances, it was believed that I had planned my own death. Because of that, people searched for me with less care than they otherwise might have. Soon every trace of me, and every memory of me, was gone. I left London on a small ship heading for a port in the north of England. Now that I had succeeded in my plan, and was completely alone, peace returned to me. The sea was calm, and the vessel moved gently forward. I sat on deck under the open sky, and I felt like a changed person. Not the wild, raging, most miserable Mathilda, but a young hermit devoted to staying apart. I tried to keep my heart free from all turmoil and hopeless despair. Even the strange nunlike dress I had chosen helped. I knew that my very life was a secret known only to me. The solitude I would have from then on softened my wounded heart and gave it gentle thoughts. The breeze that moved through my hair revived me. I watched quietly as sunlight flashed on the waves, and I saw birds fly over the water, brushing it lightly with their wings. I slept without being disturbed by dreams, and when I woke, I felt refreshed, ready to enjoy my calm freedom again.

After four days, we reached the harbor we were headed for. I did not want to stay near the sea, so I went inland right away. I had already planned where I would live. It would be a solitary house on a wide plain, far from other homes, where I could see the whole horizon and walk far without being bothered by other people. I was not a misanthrope, but I felt that my feelings stayed gentle only when I was alone. I chose a place of wide solitude. It was on a dreary heath scattered with stones, where short grass grew. Here and there, a few rushes grew beside a small pool."}

