          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e84907deaf5a989c00be15ed\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She and the children might stay very comfortably.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! papa\u2014that is exactly what you have never managed to do, and I don\u2019t believe you ever will. Isabella cannot bear to remain behind her husband.\u201d\\n\\nThis was too true to be contradicted. Though it was unwelcome, Mr. Woodhouse could only give a resigned little sigh; and the moment Emma noticed how much his spirits were affected by his daughter\u2019s attachment to her husband, she steered the conversation into a channel that would lift them.\\n\\n\u201cHarriet must give us as much of her company as she can while my brother and sister are here. I\u2019m sure she\u2019ll be delighted with the children. We are very proud of them, aren\u2019t we, papa? I wonder which she\u2019ll think the handsomest\u2014Henry or John?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAye, I wonder which she will. Poor little dears\u2014how happy they\u2019ll be to come. They\u2019re very fond of being at Hartfield, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say they are, sir. I\u2019m sure I don\u2019t know anyone who isn\u2019t.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHenry is a fine boy, but John is very much like his mother. Henry is the eldest; he was named after me, not after his father. John, the second, is named after his father. Some people, I believe, are surprised the eldest wasn\u2019t; but Isabella would have him called Henry, which I always thought very pretty of her. And he is a very clever boy, indeed. They are all remarkably clever, and they have so many engaging ways. They\u2019ll come and stand by my chair, and say, \u2018Grandpapa, can you give me a bit of string?\u2019\u2014and once Henry asked me for a knife; but I told him knives were only made for grandpapas. I think their father is too rough with them far too often.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe seems rough to you,\u201d said Emma, \u201conly because you are so very gentle yourself; but if you compared him with other fathers, you wouldn\u2019t think him rough. He wants his boys to be active and hardy; and if they misbehave, he can give them a sharp word now and then; but he is an affectionate father\u2014at least, Mr. John Knightley is an affectionate father. The children are all fond of him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd then their uncle comes in and tosses them up to the ceiling in the most dreadful fashion!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut they like it, papa; there\u2019s nothing they enjoy more. It\u2019s such pleasure to them that, if their uncle didn\u2019t insist on taking turns, whoever began would never give way to the other.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I can\u2019t make sense of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s just the way with us all, papa. One half of the world can\u2019t understand the pleasures of the other.\u201d\"}"
