          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0d99b1133e5be286ad73b776\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In my day, in my own country, this money was collected from imposts. The citizen believed that the foreign importer paid it, and that belief made him feel comfortable. In truth, though, it was paid by the American people, and it was divided among them so evenly and exactly that the yearly cost to the 100-millionaire and the yearly cost to the day laborer\u2019s sucking child was the same\u2014each paid $6. I don\u2019t see how anything could be more equal than that.\\n\\nWell, Scotland and Ireland were under Arthur\u2019s rule, and the combined population of the British Isles was a little under 1,000,000. A mechanic\u2019s average wage was 3 cents a day, after he paid for his own living. Using that rule, the national government\u2019s expenses came to $90,000 a year, or about $250 a day. So, by replacing nickels for gold on a king\u2019s-evil day, I didn\u2019t harm anyone, didn\u2019t upset anyone, and yet pleased everyone involved. I also saved four-fifths of that day\u2019s national expense. In my day in America, that saving would have been the equivalent of $800,000.\\n\\nWhen I made that substitution, I drew on wisdom from a very distant source\u2014the wisdom of my boyhood. A true statesman doesn\u2019t ignore any wisdom, no matter how humble its origin. As a boy, I had always saved my pennies and contributed buttons to the foreign missionary cause. The buttons would work just as well for the ignorant savage as the coin would; the coin would work better for me than the buttons. Everyone was happy, and nobody was hurt.\\n\\nMarinel took the patients as they arrived. He examined the candidate. If the person couldn\u2019t qualify, he was turned away. If the person could qualify, he was sent on to the king. A priest then said, \u201cThey shall lay their hands on the sick, and they shall recover.\u201d While the reading continued, the king pressed and stroked the ulcers. Finally, the patient was approved, received his nickel\u2014the king even hung it around his neck himself\u2014and was dismissed. Would you think that would cure them? It certainly did. Any kind of show or ritual will cure if the patient\u2019s faith is strong.\\n\\nUp by Astolat there was a chapel where the Virgin had once appeared to a girl who used to herd geese there. The girl said so herself, and they built the chapel on that spot and hung a picture inside showing the event. You might think it would be dangerous for a sick person to go near it. Yet, on the contrary, thousands of lame and sick people came every year, prayed before it, and left whole and well. Even people who were healthy could look at it and live.\"}"
