          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c794ed58821247b0dd5c3bdd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But he had oppressed no one, imprisoned no one. He was so far from having harshly collected what was owed that he had given up his dues of his own free will, thrown himself into a world with no favors in it, secured his own private place there, and earned his own bread. Monsieur Gabelle had held the impoverished, entangled estate under written instructions, meant to spare the people\u2014meant to give them whatever little there was to give: such fuel as the heavy creditors would allow them in the winter, and such produce as could be saved from the same grip in the summer. No doubt he had set it down in his plea and proof, for his own safety, so that it could not but seem true now.\\n\\nThis was the kind of advantage that fed the desperate decision Charles Darnay had begun to make: that he would go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. Like the mariner in the old story, the winds and streams had driven him within the pull of the Loadstone Rock, and it was drawing him in, and he had to go. Everything that rose in his mind carried him forward\u2014faster and faster, more and more steadily\u2014toward the terrible attraction. His buried uneasiness had been that bad aims were being carried out in his own unhappy country by bad instruments, and that the man who could not fail to know he was better than they were was not there, trying to stop bloodshed and assert the claims of mercy and humanity. With that uneasiness half smothered and half reproaching him, he had been pushed into a sharp comparison of himself with the brave old gentleman whose sense of duty was so strong. From that comparison\u2014injurious to himself\u2014there had immediately come Monseigneur\u2019s sneers, which stung him bitterly, and Stryver\u2019s, which above all were coarse and hard to bear, for old reasons. After that came Gabelle\u2019s letter: the appeal of an innocent prisoner, facing death, to his justice, honor, and good name.\\n\\nHis resolution was made. He had to go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. The Loadstone Rock was drawing him, and he had to keep sailing on until he struck. He knew of no rock; he saw hardly any danger. The intention with which he had done what he had done\u2014even though he had left it incomplete\u2014presented itself to him in a way that, when he appeared in France to assert it, would be gratefully recognized. Then that glorious vision of doing good, which is so often the sanguine mirage of so many good minds, rose before him, and he even saw himself under the illusion that he might have some influence to guide this raging Revolution, running so wildly.\"}"
