          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d3a856630038fe6db902dcba\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet he kept one secret from these dear friends\u2014something he had carried and tended since his earliest years. Though he truly loved his fellow collegiates, he would not entrust it to the delicacy or sympathy of any one of them. He loved; he feared. He feared that the force of his passion might become sport for their ridicule, and he could not endure the thought that they might profane it by treating as trifling and fleeting what, to him, was the very substance of his life.\\n\\nNear his family mansion there lived a gentleman of small means with three lovely daughters. The eldest was by far the most beautiful, yet her beauty was only an added grace to her other gifts: her understanding was clear and firm, and her temper was angelically mild. She and my father had been playmates from infancy. Diana\u2014so even in childhood had she been a particular favourite with his mother\u2014grew more and more dear as the years went on; and so, during his school and college vacations, they were almost always together. Novels, and the many ways by which young people in civilized society are brought to a knowledge of passions before they truly feel them, had made a strong impression on him, for he was unusually open to every influence. At eleven, Diana was already his favourite companion, though he spoke the language of love. Though she was nearly two years older than he, the kind of education she had received left her, at least in her knowledge and expression of feeling, more childish. She took his warm protestations with innocence, and answered them without knowing what they meant. She had read no novels and kept to her younger sisters; what could she know of the difference between love and friendship? And when her understanding finally revealed the real nature of their intimacy, her affections were already claimed by her friend; all she worried about was that other attractions or a changeable heart might cause him to break the vows of his childhood.\\n\\nStill, day by day they grew more ardent and more tender. It was a passion that had grown alongside his own growth; it had woven itself into every faculty and every sentiment, and could be lost only with life itself. No one knew of their love except their two hearts. Yet in everything else\u2014and even in this, though he dreaded the judgment of his companions\u2014he remained fixed in his purpose of joining himself to her as soon as he could gather courage enough to face the obstacles before him. For he was determined to overcome those difficulties, even though he loved someone of less fortune than his own.\\n\\nDiana was fully worthy of his deepest affection.\"}"
