          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dac61ad2a7fe36d65704360e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The powerful voice\u2014something we might call inorganic, since we could in no way connect it to the workings of a pipe or a key\u2014fell silent, and the girl, turning to help her elderly companion, finally noticed us.\\n\\nIt was her father; and since childhood she had guided his darkened steps. They were Germans from Saxony, and, having emigrated there only a few years earlier, had formed new ties with the villagers around them. Around the time the pestilence broke out, a young German student had joined them. Their story was easy to piece together. He was a nobleman who loved the fair daughter of the poor musician, and he followed them in their flight from the persecution of his friends; but soon the mighty leveler came with an unblunted scythe to mow down, along with the grass, the tall flowers of the field. The youth was an early victim. She kept herself alive for her father\u2019s sake. His blindness allowed her to keep up a delusion\u2014at first, born of accident\u2014and now, as the only two survivors left in the land, he remained unaware of the change. He did not know that when he listened to his child\u2019s music, the mute mountains, the senseless lake, and the unconscious trees\u2014except for himself\u2014were her only listeners.\\n\\nThe very day we arrived, she had been attacked by a sickness that showed itself in the usual way. She was paralyzed with horror at the thought of leaving her aged, sightless father alone on the empty earth; but she lacked the courage to tell the truth. And the very excess of her desperation drove her into extraordinary effort. At the usual hour for vespers, she led him to the chapel; and, though she trembled and wept for him, she played the hymn meant to celebrate the creation of the adorned earth\u2014without fault in timing or any mistake in note\u2014soon to be her tomb.\\n\\nWe came to her as if from heaven itself. Her intense courage, her firmness barely held together, vanished the moment she saw relief. With a shriek she rushed toward us, threw her arms around Adrian\u2019s knees, and, saying only, \u201cO save my father!\u201d while sobs and hysterical cries poured out of her, she finally opened the long-closed floodgates of her grief.\\n\\nPoor girl!\u2014she and her father now lie side by side beneath the tall walnut tree where her lover rests, and which, in her dying moments, she had pointed out to us. Her father, at last aware of his daughter\u2019s danger, could not see the changes in her dear face; he stubbornly held her hand until it grew cold and stiff with death. He did not move or speak even then, until twelve hours later, kindly death carried him to his endless rest. They lie beneath the sod, the tree their monument. The sacred spot is still clear in my memory, pale beside the craggy Jura and the far, immeasurable Alps; the church spire they used to attend still points above the surrounding trees. And though her hand is cold, I still think the sounds of the divine music they loved drift around, comforting their gentle ghosts.\"}"
