          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f2e70010faf1d68ab99fdab1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He was delivered to you. You see, I\u2019m informed of the circumstances?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSuch is the fact, certainly,\u201d said Defarge. He had been told, in an accidental brush of his wife\u2019s elbow as she knitted and hummed her little song, that he would do best to answer\u2014always briefly.\\n\\n\u201cIt was to you,\u201d said the spy, \u201cthat his daughter came; and it was from your care that his daughter took him, accompanied by a neat brown monsieur. What\u2019s his name\u2014wearing a little wig\u2014Lorry, from the bank of Tellson and Company\u2014over to England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSuch is the fact,\u201d Defarge repeated.\\n\\n\u201cVery interesting memories!\u201d said the spy. \u201cI\u2019ve known Doctor Manette and his daughter in England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes?\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cYou don\u2019t hear much about them now?\u201d asked the spy.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cIn effect,\u201d madame broke in, looking up from her work and her little song, \u201cwe never hear about them. We received the news of their safe arrival, and perhaps another letter\u2014or perhaps two; but since then, they\u2019ve gradually gone on with their lives\u2014us with ours\u2014and we\u2019ve had no correspondence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPrecisely so, madame,\u201d the spy replied. \u201cShe\u2019s going to be married.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGoing?\u201d madame echoed. \u201cShe was pretty enough to have been married long ago. It seems to me the English are cold.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! You know I\u2019m English.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI see your tongue is,\u201d madame returned; \u201cand I suppose the man is, too.\u201d\\n\\nHe didn\u2019t take the identification as a compliment, but he made the best of it and brushed it off with a laugh. After sipping his cognac to the end, he added:\\n\\n\u201cYes, Miss Manette is going to be married. But not to an Englishman; to someone who, like herself, is French by birth. And speaking of Gaspard\u2014ah, poor Gaspard! It was cruel, cruel\u2014there\u2019s something curious about it: she\u2019s going to marry the nephew of Monsieur the Marquis, for whom Gaspard was raised to that height of so many feet\u2014in other words, the present Marquis. But he lives unknown in England; he isn\u2019t a marquis there. He\u2019s Mr. Charles Darnay. D\u2019Aulnais is the name of his mother\u2019s family.\u201d\\n\\nMadame Defarge kept knitting steadily, but the information had a clear effect on her husband. No matter what he did behind the little counter\u2014whether striking a light, or lighting his pipe\u2014he was troubled, and his hand wasn\u2019t steady. The spy would have been no spy if he hadn\u2019t noticed it, or if he hadn\u2019t stored it away in his mind.\\n\\nHaving made, at least, this one hit\u2014whatever it might prove to be worth\u2014and with no customers coming in to help him with anything else, Mr. Barsad paid for what he\u2019d drunk and took his leave. Before departing, he took the opportunity, in a polite manner, to say that he looked forward to the pleasure of seeing Monsieur and Madame Defarge again.\"}"
