{"request_id":"5a72111d9af05a9d9d91edb4","rewritten_text":"Poor Jack was later shot in a duel by Lord Canterville on Wandsworth Common, and Lady Barbara died of a broken heart at Tunbridge Wells before the year was out, so, in every way, it had been a great success. It was, however, an extremely difficult “make-up,” if I may use such a theatrical expression in connection with one of the greatest mysteries of the supernatural, or, to put it in more scientific terms, the higher-natural world, and it took him a full three hours to make his preparations. At last everything was ready, and he was very pleased with how he looked. The big leather riding-boots that came with the costume were just a little too large for him, and he could only find one of the two horse-pistols, but, on the whole, he was quite satisfied. At a quarter past one he slipped out of the wainscoting and crept down the corridor.

On reaching the room occupied by the twins—which, I should mention, was called the Blue Bed Chamber because of the color of its hangings—he found the door just ajar. Hoping to make an effective entrance, he flung it wide open, and a heavy jug of water came down on him at once, soaking him to the skin, and missing his left shoulder by only a couple of inches. At the same moment he heard muffled shrieks of laughter coming from the four-poster bed. The shock to his nerves was so great that he bolted back to his room as fast as he could, and the next day he was laid up with a severe cold. The only thing that consoled him at all was the fact that he hadn’t brought his head with him; had he done so, the consequences might have been very serious.

He now gave up all hope of ever frightening this rude American family, and, as a rule, contented himself with creeping about the passages in list slippers, with a thick red muffler around his throat to guard against drafts, and a small arquebus in case the twins attacked him. The final blow came on September 19th. He had gone downstairs to the great entrance hall, feeling sure that at least there he would not be bothered, and he amused himself by making satirical remarks about the large Saroni photographs of the United States Minister and his wife, which had now replaced the Canterville family pictures. He was simply dressed but neatly, in a long shroud spotted with churchyard mould; he had tied up his jaw with a strip of yellow linen, and he carried a small lantern and a sexton’s spade."}

