ven after it eased, I could draw breath only at long intervals, and with a kind of gasping, while I continued to bleed freely from the nose and ears, and even a little from the eyes. The pigeons seemed in the utmost distress and struggled to get away; while the cat mewed in a piteous tone, with her tongue hanging out, and staggered from side to side in the car as if poisoned. Only then, too late, did I recognize how rash I had been in casting out the ballast, and my agitation was extreme. I expected nothing less than death\u2014death within a few minutes.\u201d\"}"
