          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"28d2214055819e7a3911fda2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"_PS._\u2014Do tell me about the bows. Jennings insists they\u2019re the fashion.\\n\\nLord Arthur looked so serious and miserable over the letter that the Duchess burst into laughter.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Arthur,\u201d she said, laughing, \u201cI\u2019ll never show you a young lady\u2019s letter again! But what am I supposed to say about the clock? I think it\u2019s a capital invention, and I\u2019d like one for myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think much of them,\u201d Lord Arthur said, wearing a sad smile. After kissing his mother, he left the room.\\n\\nWhen he went upstairs, he threw himself onto a sofa, and his eyes filled with tears. He had done his best to carry out this murder, but on both occasions he had failed, and not through any fault of his own. He had tried to do his duty, yet it felt as though Destiny herself had turned traitor. He was weighed down by the emptiness of good intentions, by the pointlessness of trying to be fine. Maybe it would be better to end the marriage altogether. Sybil would suffer, of course, but suffering couldn\u2019t truly ruin a nature as noble as hers. As for himself\u2014what did it matter? There\u2019s always some war in which a man can die, some cause to which a man can give his life, and since life held no pleasure for him, death held no terror. Let Destiny work out his doom. He wouldn\u2019t lift a hand to help her.\\n\\nAt half past seven he dressed and went down to the club. Surbiton was there with a party of young men, and he had to dine with them. Their petty talk and idle jokes bored him, and the moment coffee was brought he left them, making up some engagement so he could get away. As he was going out, the hall-porter handed him a letter. It was from Herr Winckelkopf, asking him to call the next evening and look at an explosive umbrella that went off as soon as it was opened. It was the very latest invention and had just arrived from Geneva. He tore the letter into fragments. He\u2019d decided he wouldn\u2019t try any more experiments.\\n\\nThen he wandered down to the Thames Embankment and sat by the river for hours. The moon pushed through a mane of tawny clouds, as if it were a lion\u2019s eye, and countless stars glittered across the dark vault, like gold dust scattered over a purple dome. Now and then a barge swung out into the muddy stream and drifted away with the tide, while the railway signals changed from green to scarlet as the trains shrieked across the bridge.\"}"
