{"request_id":"dfa7b4062669c2d33d3315e3","rewritten_text":"Indeed, I had no cause to be afraid for her. She was, as people say, a woman of consequence, and knew how to handle affairs in the world without any help from me. If I’m honest, she seemed to regard me as nothing more than a lazy boy—a mere extra weight, fit only for building castles in the air—and she was quite pleased to be rid of me. It was a gloomy night when I took my leave of her; and, with three creditors—those who had given me the most trouble—as aides-de-camp, we carried the balloon, along with the car and all its gear, by a roundabout route to the station where the other articles had been put away. There we found everything safe and untouched, and I set about my work at once.

“It was the first of April. As I said, the night was dark—no star to be seen—and a light rain, coming and going, made us thoroughly uncomfortable. Still, my chief worry was the balloon itself, which, despite the varnish meant to protect it, began to feel rather heavy from the moisture; the powder, too, was at risk of being spoiled. So I kept the three duns hard at it, with great diligence: they pounded ice around the central cask and stirred the acid in the others. They did not stop, though, from badgering me with questions about what I meant to do with all this apparatus, and they complained bitterly about the dreadful labor I was forcing on them. They couldn’t see, as they put it, what good could come of getting wet through, just to take part in such dreadful rites. I started to feel uneasy, and kept working with all my strength, for I truly believe the fools thought I had made a bargain with the devil, and that what I was doing now was no better than it ought to be. I was, therefore, in real danger that they would abandon me entirely. I managed, however, to calm them by promising that I would settle every account in full as soon as I could bring the present business to an end. Of course, they took these words in their own way—imagining, no doubt, that at the very least I would come into possession of large sums of ready money; and if I paid them what I owed, with a little more besides, they probably cared very little what became of either my soul or my body.

“In about four and a half hours I found the balloon inflated enough. I then fastened the car to it and put all my tools inside—without forgetting the condensing apparatus, a plentiful supply of water, and a large store of provisions, including pemmican, which holds a great deal of nourishment in a small space.”"}

