{"request_id":"67893626da924a73366868ad","rewritten_text":"There were few who could claim to have such a pure heart, and so much true humility, along with a steady trust in her own honesty and a belief in the honesty of others. From birth, she had lived a quiet, private life. She lost her mother when she was very young, but her father devoted himself to her education. He had many unusual ideas, and they shaped the system he used for raising her. She knew well the heroes of Greece and Rome, and also the English heroes from about a hundred years earlier, while she was almost ignorant of what was happening in the world around her. She had read only a few authors who had written within the last fifty years, though aside from that, her reading was wide and thorough. So even though she seemed less familiar with the day-to-day mysteries of life and society than he was, her knowledge was deeper and rested on firmer ground. And if her beauty and kindness had not already captivated him, her understanding would still have kept him completely under her influence. He looked to her as his guide, and his love was so strong that he was always eager to add to his own mind the feeling of inferiority that she sometimes made him feel.

When he was nineteen, his mother died. He left college because of it, and after shaking off his old friends for a time, he withdrew to the area near his Diana. He found his comfort in her sweet voice and in the affection he received from her. This brief separation from his companions gave him the courage to act on his independence. He believed that even if they mocked his plan to marry, they would not dare show their disapproval once it had actually happened. So, without telling anyone else what he intended, he sought the consent of his guardian, which he managed to obtain with some difficulty, and also the consent of Diana’s father, which was granted more easily. By the time he turned twenty, he had become Diana’s husband.

He loved her with passion, and her tenderness held his attention so completely that he could not think of anything else. He invited some of his college friends to visit him, but their careless, shallow behavior disgusted him. Diana had lifted the veil that had kept him in boyhood. He was now a man, and he was surprised that he could ever have joined in the empty talk and ideas of his fellow students, or that he had once feared their judgment. He stopped seeing his old friends not out of fickleness, but because they truly were not worthy of him."}

