          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"75b1756ef5de5f35cae8f474\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI hurry now to the more stirring portion of my narrative. I will set down occurrences that made a deep impression on me\u2014impressions that, as I look back, have shaped what I have become.\\n\\n\u201cSpring moved on apace; the weather turned fair, and the sky remained clear of clouds. I was struck by the change: what had lately been desert and cheerless now seemed to burst into bloom, covered with the fairest flowers and rich verdure. My senses were gratified and refreshed by a thousand delightful scents and a thousand pleasing sights.\\n\\n\u201cIt was on one of those days, when my cottagers, at intervals, rested from their labour\u2014the old man playing on his guitar while the children listened\u2014that I noticed Felix\u2019s face was weighed down with a melancholy beyond measure. He sighed again and again; and once his father paused in his music, I judged from his manner that he was asking after the cause of his son\u2019s sorrow. Felix answered in a cheerful tone, and the old man was beginning his playing once more when someone tapped at the door.\\n\\n\u201cIt was a lady on horseback, with a countryman to guide her. She wore a dark suit and was wrapped in a thick black veil. Agatha put a question; the stranger answered only by speaking, in a sweet accent, the name of Felix. Her voice was musical, yet it did not resemble that of either of my friends. At the sound of that name, Felix hurried forward to her; and when she saw him, she lifted her veil, so that I beheld a countenance of angelic beauty and expression. Her hair was a shining raven black, curiously braided; her eyes were dark, but gentle, though full of life. Her features were of regular proportion, and her complexion wonderfully fair, with each cheek touched by a tender pink.\\n\\n\u201cFelix appeared transported with joy at the sight of her; every trace of sorrow vanished from his face, and it at once showed a kind of rapturous delight that I could scarcely have imagined possible. His eyes brightened, his cheek reddened with pleasure; and in that instant I thought him as beautiful as the stranger. Yet she seemed moved by other feelings as well. Wiping away a few tears from her lovely eyes, she extended her hand to Felix, who kissed it with ardent eagerness and called her\u2014so far as I could make out\u2014his sweet Arabian. She did not seem to understand him, but only smiled. He helped her to dismount, dismissed her guide, and led her into the cottage. Some conversation followed between him and his father; and the young stranger, kneeling at the old man\u2019s feet, would have kissed his hand, but he raised her and embraced her with affectionate warmth.\\n\\n\u201cI soon perceived that, though the stranger uttered distinct sounds and appeared to have a language of her own, she was neither understood by the cottagers, nor did she herself understand them.\u201d\"}"
