          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"35dbbb20c734980726fc0b14\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Re-reading his letter and replying to it had such a softening effect that it was especially necessary to steady her with a few decisive words. She was so worried about the idea of making him unhappy, and cared so much about what his mother and sisters would think and say, and was so anxious that they shouldn\u2019t see her as ungrateful, that Emma believed that if the young man had happened to come by at that moment, he would still have been accepted after all.\\n\\nBut the letter was written, sealed, and sent. The business was finished, and Harriet was safe. She was rather low all evening, but Emma could make room for her kind regrets, and sometimes eased them by talking about her own affection, sometimes by bringing up the idea of Mr. Elton.\\n\\n\u201cI shall never be invited to Abbey-Mill again,\u201d Harriet said, in a tone that sounded quite sorrowful.\\n\\n\u201cNor, if you were, could I ever bear to be separated from you, my Harriet. You\u2019re far too necessary at Hartfield to be spared to Abbey-Mill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I\u2019m sure I should never want to go there; I\u2019m never happy anywhere except at Hartfield.\u201d\\n\\nAfter some time, Harriet said, \u201cI think Mrs. Goddard would be very surprised if she knew what had happened. I\u2019m sure Miss Nash would be too\u2014for Miss Nash thinks her own sister very well married, and it\u2019s only a linen-draper.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne should feel sorry to see more pride or refinement in the teacher of a school, Harriet. I dare say Miss Nash would envy you the chance to be married. Even this \u2018conquest\u2019 would probably look valuable to her. As for anything better for you, I suppose she\u2019s completely in the dark. The attentions of a certain person can hardly be among the gossip of Highbury yet. Up to now, I think you and I are the only ones whose looks and manners have made it clear what he means.\u201d\\n\\nHarriet blushed and smiled, and said something about being surprised that people liked her so much. The idea of Mr. Elton was certainly cheering; but after a while, she became tender-hearted again toward the man who had been rejected, Mr. Martin.\\n\\n\u201cNow he has got my letter,\u201d she said softly. \u201cI wonder what they\u2019re all doing\u2014whether his sisters know. If he\u2019s unhappy, they\u2019ll be unhappy too. I hope it won\u2019t trouble him so much.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet\u2019s think about those among our absent friends who are more cheerfully occupied,\u201d Emma cried. \u201cAt this moment, perhaps, Mr. Elton is showing your picture to his mother and sisters, telling them how much more beautiful the original is\u2014and after being asked for it five or six times, he\u2019s letting them hear your name, your own dear name.\u201d\"}"
