{"request_id":"5245d4437401f4d2e1beeb2f","rewritten_text":"Summer pushed on, and crowned with the sun’s fierce rays, the plague sent her sure, unerring darts over the land. The peoples beneath that influence bowed their heads and perished. The corn that had risen in abundance lay in autumn, rotting on the ground, while the miserable man who had gone out to gather bread for his children lay rigid and plague-stricken in the furrow. The green woods stirred their branches with stately grace, yet the dying were laid beneath their shade, answering the grave music with discordant cries. Painted birds fluttered through the gloom; the heedless deer rested unharmed among the fern. Meanwhile the oxen and horses wandered from their unguarded stalls and grazed among the wheat, for death struck mankind alone.

With summer and mortality, our dread only grew. My poor love and I looked at one another, and at our babes.—“We will save them, Idris,” I said. “I will save them. Years from now we shall tell them of our fears, when that time has passed. Even if only they remain on earth, they shall live; their cheeks shall not turn pale, nor their sweet voices grow faint.” Our eldest, to some extent, grasped what was happening about him, and now and then, with grave looks, questioned me about the cause of so vast a desolation. But he was only ten; and the bright spirits of youth soon drove off any unreasonable care. Evelyn—laughing cherub, playful infant—without any notion of pain or sorrow, would shake back his light curls from his eyes and make the halls ring again with his merriment, drawing our notice in a thousand artless ways to his play. Clara, our lovely gentle Clara, was our support, our comfort, our delight. She took it upon herself to tend the sick, console the grieving, help the aged, and join in the sports, stirring the young to gladness. She moved through the rooms like a kindly spirit, as if sent from the celestial kingdom, to cast an alien brightness over our dark hour. Wherever her steps went, gratitude and praise were seen. Still, when she stood before us in quiet simplicity, playing with our children, or with girlish diligence performing small acts of kindness for Idris, one could not help wondering in what fair form of her pure loveliness, in what gentle, thrilling tone of her voice, so much heroism, wisdom, and active goodness could be found.

The summer dragged on, for we clung to the belief that winter would at least restrain the disease. To think it would wholly disappear was a hope too precious—too deeply felt—to be spoken aloud. Whenever such a thought was carelessly voiced, those who heard it answered with a rush of tears and passionate sobs, showing how great their fears were and how slight their hopes."}

