{"request_id":"bb0fe240ca9a9006a6970e5e","rewritten_text":"He slipped stealthily out of the wainscoting, an evil smile on his cruel, wrinkled mouth, while the moon hid her face behind a cloud as he crept past the great oriel window, where his own arms and those of his murdered wife were displayed in azure and gold. On and on he glided, like an evil shadow; even the darkness seemed to resent him as he passed. Once he thought he heard something calling, and he stopped, but it was only the baying of a dog from the Red Farm, and he went on, muttering strange curses from the sixteenth century, now and then brandishing the rusty dagger in the midnight air.

At last he reached the corner of the passage that led to luckless Washington’s room. For a moment he paused there, the wind whipping his long grey locks about his head, twisting them into grotesque, fantastic folds as it seemed to wrap the nameless horror of the dead man’s shroud. Then the clock struck the quarter, and he felt the time had come. He chuckled to himself and turned the corner—but no sooner had he done so than, with a pitiful wail of terror, he stumbled back and hid his blanched face in his long, bony hands. Right in front of him stood a horrible specter, motionless as a carved figure, monstrous as a madman’s dream. Its head was bald and burnished; its face round, fat, and white; and hideous laughter seemed to have twisted its features into an eternal grin. From its eyes streamed rays of scarlet light; its mouth was a wide well of fire; and a hideous garment, like his own, draped the Titan form in silent snow. On its breast was a placard with strange writing in antique characters—some scroll of shame, some record of wild sins, some awful calendar of crime—and with its right hand it held aloft a falchion of gleaming steel.

Having never seen a ghost before, he was naturally terrified. After a second, frantic look at the dreadful phantom, he fled back to his room, tripping over his long winding-sheet as he hurried down the corridor, and finally dropping the rusty dagger into the Minister’s jack-boots, where it was found in the morning by the butler. Once he was alone in his own apartment, he threw himself down on a small pallet-bed and buried his face under the clothes. After a time, however, the brave old Canterville spirit asserted itself, and he decided he would go and speak to the other ghost as soon as it was daylight. So, just as dawn began to touch the hills with silver, he returned toward the spot where he had first laid eyes on the grisly phantom, thinking that, after all, two ghosts were better than one, and that with the help of his new friend he might safely grapple with the twins."}

