          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fbb89021d81df0b7e5f7f77a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell, well, Mr. Bumble,\u201d he said at length, \u201cyou can\u2019t deny that since the new way of feeding was introduced, the coffins have become narrower and shallower than they used to be. But we still have to make some profit, Mr. Bumble. Well-seasoned timber is expensive, sir, and all the iron handles are shipped in by canal from Birmingham.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well,\u201d said Mr. Bumble, \u201cevery trade has its downsides. A fair profit is, of course, allowed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course, of course,\u201d replied the undertaker. \u201cAnd if I don\u2019t make profit on one item or another, I make it up in the long run, you see\u2014he! he! he!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cJust so,\u201d said Mr. Bumble.\\n\\n\u201cStill,\u201d the undertaker went on, picking up the point the beadle had interrupted, \u201cI have to deal with one big disadvantage. The stout people go first. The people who have been better off, and have paid rates for many years, are the first to sink once they come into the house. And let me tell you, Mr. Bumble, that being even three or four inches off your estimate can make a big difference to your profits\u2014especially when you have a family to support, sir.\u201d\\n\\nAs Mr. Sowerberry said this, with the proper anger of a man who feels he has been treated unfairly, and as Mr. Bumble felt it sounded like a criticism of the parish\u2019s honor, Mr. Bumble decided it was best to change the subject. Oliver Twist was on his mind, so he brought him up.\\n\\n\u201cBy the bye,\u201d said Mr. Bumble, \u201cdo you know anyone who wants a boy? A parish apprentice\u2014he\u2019s just dead weight at the moment. Like a millstone around the parish\u2019s neck, as you might say. Liberal terms, Mr. Sowerberry\u2014liberal terms?\u201d\\n\\nAs he spoke, he lifted his cane to the board above him and tapped three times on the words \u201cfive pounds,\u201d printed in huge Roman capitals.\\n\\n\u201cGadso!\u201d said the undertaker, grabbing Mr. Bumble by the gilt-edged lapel of his official coat. \u201cThat\u2019s exactly what I wanted to talk to you about. You know\u2014dear me, what an elegant button this is, Mr. Bumble! I never noticed it before.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I think it\u2019s rather fine,\u201d said the beadle, looking proudly down at the large brass buttons on his coat. \u201cThe design is the same as the parish seal\u2014the Good Samaritan healing the sick and bruised man. The board gave it to me on New Year\u2019s morning, Mr. Sowerberry. I remember putting it on for the first time to attend the inquest on that poor tradesman who died in a doorway at midnight.\u201d\"}"
