          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b9e0590971504b53181cfa5e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"_PS._\u2014Do tell me about the bows. Jennings says they\u2019re the latest fashion.\\n\\nLord Arthur looked so serious and miserable about the letter that the Duchess burst into laughter.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Arthur,\u201d she said, laughing, \u201cI\u2019ll never show you a young lady\u2019s letter again! But what should I say about the clock? I think it\u2019s a wonderful invention, and I\u2019d like one for myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think much of them,\u201d Lord Arthur replied, with a sad smile. After he kissed his mother, he left the room.\\n\\nWhen he went upstairs, he threw himself onto a sofa, and tears filled his eyes. He had done his best to carry out this murder, but on both occasions he had failed, and not because of anything he did wrong. He had tried to do his duty, yet it felt as if Destiny herself had turned against him. He was weighed down by the emptiness of good intentions, and by how pointless it was to try to be \u201cfine.\u201d Maybe it would be better to end the marriage altogether. Sybil would be hurt, of course, but suffering couldn\u2019t truly ruin a nature as noble as hers. As for himself, what did it matter? There\u2019s always some war where a man can die, some cause where he can give his life. And since life held no pleasure for him, death held no fear. Let Destiny carry out his doom. He wouldn\u2019t move to stop her.\\n\\nAt half past seven he got dressed and went down to the club. Surbiton was there with a group of young men, and Lord Arthur had to dine with them. Their small talk and silly jokes bored him, and the moment coffee was served he left, saying he had some engagement so he could get away. As he was heading out, the hall-porter handed him a letter. It was from Herr Winckelkopf, asking him to call the next evening and look at an explosive umbrella that would go off as soon as it was opened. It was the newest invention and had just arrived from Geneva. Lord Arthur tore the letter into pieces. He had decided not to try any more experiments.\\n\\nThen he walked down to the Thames Embankment and sat by the river for hours. The moon pushed through a mane of tawny clouds, as if it were a lion\u2019s eye, and countless stars glittered across the dark sky, like gold dust scattered over a purple dome. From time to time a barge swung out into the muddy water and drifted away with the tide. The railway signals changed from green to scarlet as the trains roared across the bridge.\"}"
