          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d9ef57e6767a71ec380ee03f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI only wish we had a donkey. Then we could all go together on donkeys, Jane, Miss Bates, and me\u2014while my caro sposo walks alongside. I really ought to speak with him about getting a donkey. In country life, I take it to be almost a necessity; for, no matter how many comforts a woman may have, she can\u2019t always be shut up at home;\u2014and you know how very long walks are\u2014there\u2019s dust in summer, and in winter there\u2019s dirt.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou won\u2019t meet with either between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane is never dusty, and it\u2019s perfectly dry at present. If you like, come on a donkey. You may borrow Mrs. Cole\u2019s. I should like everything to be as much to your liking as possible.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat I\u2019m sure you would. Indeed, I do you justice, my dear friend. Beneath that particular kind of dry, blunt way, I know you have the warmest heart. As I told Mr. E., you are a complete humourist.\u2014Yes, believe me, Knightley, I\u2019m perfectly aware of how attentive you\u2019ve been to me throughout all of this plan. You\u2019ve chosen exactly what will please me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley had another motive for steering clear of a table in the shade. He wanted to persuade Mr. Woodhouse, as well as Emma, to join the party; and he knew that if either of them sat down out of doors to eat, it would almost certainly make him unwell. Mr. Woodhouse must not, under the flattering pretext of a morning drive and an hour or two spent at Donwell, be drawn away from his misery.\\n\\nHe was asked in good faith. There were no hidden terrors meant to reproach him for being so easily persuaded. He did agree. He hadn\u2019t been at Donwell for two years. \u201cSome very fine morning, he, and Emma, and Harriet, could go very well; and he could sit quietly with Mrs. Weston while the dear girls walked about the gardens. He didn\u2019t think they could be damp now, in the middle of the day. He should be delighted to see the old house again, and would be very glad to meet Mr. and Mrs. Elton, and any other of his neighbours.\u2014He could see no objection at all to his, and Emma\u2019s, and Harriet\u2019s going there some very fine morning. He thought it very well done of Mr. Knightley to invite them\u2014so kind and sensible\u2014far better than dining out.\u2014He wasn\u2019t fond of dining out.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley was lucky in having everyone\u2019s ready assent. The invitation was taken up so readily everywhere, that it seemed as though, like Mrs. Elton, they were all treating the scheme as a special compliment to themselves. Emma and Harriet declared they expected the greatest pleasure from it; and Mr. Weston, without being asked, promised to try to get Frank to come along, if possible\u2014a mark of approval and gratitude that might well have been spared. Mr. Knightley then found it necessary to say that he would be glad to see him; and Mr. Weston undertook to write at once, and spare no arguments to bring him.\"}"
