{"request_id":"0fb1cb1ee8bd489ddf306f31","rewritten_text":"We grew up together, and there was less than a year between our ages. We were never strangers to disagreement or conflict of any kind. Our companionship was built on harmony, and the differences in our personalities brought us even closer. Elizabeth was calm and steady, but for all my enthusiasm, I could focus more intensely, and I was even more eager to learn. She spent her time following the poets’ “aerial” ideas, and in the grand and amazing scenes around our Swiss home—the towering mountains, the changing seasons, storms and clear weather, the quiet of winter, and the lively, restless summers in the Alps—she had plenty to admire and enjoy. While she looked at the world with a serious, satisfied mind, I took pleasure in figuring out why things happened. To me, the world was a mystery I wanted to understand. Curiosity, honest effort to discover the hidden laws of nature, and a joy that felt close to excitement—these were among the first feelings I can remember.

When my parents had a second son, seven years younger than me, they gave up their wandering life completely and settled in their home country. We had a house in Geneva and a country place on Belrive, on the eastern shore of the lake, a little more than a league from the city. We lived mostly at the country place, and my parents kept to themselves for the most part. I preferred to avoid crowds and to devote myself deeply to a few people. So I was not very interested in most of my schoolmates, but I formed a very close friendship with one of them. Henry Clerval was from Geneva and was the son of a merchant. He had unusual talent and a lively imagination. He loved adventure, hardship, and even danger, simply because it was exciting. He read widely in books of chivalry and romance. He wrote heroic songs and began to create many tales full of magic and knightly adventures. He even tried to get us to act out plays and join in masquerades, where the characters came from the heroes of Roncesvalles, the Round Table of King Arthur, and the chivalrous men who shed their blood to redeem the holy sepulchre from the hands of the infidels.

No one could have had a happier childhood than I did. My parents were full of kindness and indulgence. We felt they were not cruel rulers who decided our lives by their moods, but the ones who made it possible for us to enjoy all the pleasures we had."}

