          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9d63e1e5843208bc33816856\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Now that he had no work to keep him busy, he pressed the knuckles of his right hand into the hollow of his left, then the knuckles of his left into the hollow of his right. After that, he ran his hand across his bearded chin, and then did the same kind of thing again, switching back and forth in a steady rhythm, without stopping for even a moment. Trying to bring him back from the wandering life he always slipped into after he spoke was like trying to wake someone very weak from a faint, or like trying to hold on to a dying man\u2019s spirit, hoping for some last clear sign.\\n\\n\u201cDid you ask me for my name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I did.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that all?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\nWith a tired sound that was not a sigh and not a groan, he bent down to work again, until the silence was broken once more.\\n\\n\u201cYou are not a shoemaker by trade?\u201d said Mr. Lorry, staring at him.\\n\\nHis hollow, worn eyes shifted to Defarge, as if he wanted to pass the question to him. But since no help came, his gaze returned to Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cNot a shoemaker by trade? No. I was not a shoemaker by trade. I\u2014I learned it here. I taught myself. I asked permission to\u2014\u201d\\n\\nHe trailed off, even for minutes, while he kept making those careful, measured movements with his hands. His eyes slowly came back at last to the face they had left. When they landed there, he startled, then went on again, like someone who has just woken up and returns to what he was thinking about last night.\\n\\n\u201cI asked permission to teach myself, and I got it only after a long time, and with a lot of trouble. And I\u2019ve been making shoes ever since.\u201d\\n\\nAs he held out his hand for the shoe that had been taken from him, Mr. Lorry said, still watching him closely:\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette, do you remember nothing about me?\u201d\\n\\nThe shoe fell to the ground. He sat there, staring at Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette,\u201d Mr. Lorry said, putting his hand on Defarge\u2019s arm; \u201cdo you remember nothing about this man? Look at him. Look at me. Is there no old banker, no old business, no old servant, no old time coming back to you, Monsieur Manette?\u201d\\n\\nWhile the prisoner of many years stared, first at Mr. Lorry and then at Defarge, faint signs of an intelligence that had once been active slowly pushed through the dark fog on his forehead. Then the signs faded again, grew weaker, and disappeared. Still, they had been there.\"}"
