{"request_id":"fd51fd11cd46b67bc4f9866a","rewritten_text":"The little man’s body was more than proportionately broad, making his whole figure absurdly rotund. His feet, of course, could not be seen at all, though a horny, suspicious-looking substance was occasionally pushed out through a tear in the bottom of the car—or, to put it more accurately, through the top of the hat. His hands were enormously large. His hair was extremely gray, gathered into a queue behind. His nose was prodigiously long, crooked, and inflamed; his eyes were full, brilliant, and sharp; and though his chin and cheeks were wrinkled with age, they were broad, puffy, and doubled. But there was not the slightest trace of any ears—of any kind or character—anywhere on his head.

This odd little gentleman was dressed in a loose surtout of sky-blue satin, with tight breeches to match, fastened with silver buckles at the knees. His vest was made of some bright yellow material; a white taffeta cap sat jauntily to one side of his head; and to finish his outfit, a blood-red silk handkerchief wrapped his throat and fell down in a dainty way upon his chest, tied into a fantastic bow-knot of superlative size.

Having descended, as I said before, to about one hundred feet above the surface of the earth, the little old gentleman was suddenly seized with a fit of trepidation and seemed unwilling to come any nearer to terra firma. So, throwing out a quantity of sand from a canvas bag—which he lifted with great difficulty—he became stationary at once. Then, in a hurried and agitated manner, he proceeded to pull from a side pocket in his surtout a large morocco pocket-book. He held it up suspiciously, then stared at it with an air of extreme surprise, clearly astonished by its weight. At last he opened it and, drawing out a huge letter sealed with red sealing wax and tied carefully with red tape, let it drop precisely at the feet of the burgomaster, Superbus Von Underduk. His Excellency stooped to pick it up. But the aeronaut, still very much rattled, and apparently having no further reason to keep him in Rotterdam, began at that moment to make preparations to leave; and since he needed to discharge some ballast in order to rise again, the half dozen bags he threw out, one after another, without bothering to empty their contents, unfortunately tumbled—every single one—onto the burgomaster’s back, rolling him over and over no fewer than twenty-one times, in full view of every man in Rotterdam. It is not to be supposed, however, that the great Underduk allowed this impertinence on the part of the little old man to go unpunished."}

