          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"727a5e7deff297f1ea806cc6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But in that sharp _tirade_ against Chantilly, which had appeared in yesterday\u2019s \u2018_Mus\u00e9e_,\u2019 the satirist, with some shameful insinuations about the cobbler\u2019s change of name when he took up the buskin, quoted a Latin line that we have often discussed. I mean the line\\n\\nPerdidit antiquum litera prima sonum.\\n\\n\u201cI told you, you remember, that it was meant to point to Orion, once written Urion; and, from the particular turns of phrase tied to that explanation, I knew you could not possibly have forgotten it. It was plain, then, that you would bring together the two notions of Orion and Chantilly. That you did so, I judged from the sort of smile that flickered across your lips. You were thinking of the cobbler\u2019s miserable sacrifice. Up to then you had been bending in your walk; but now I saw you straighten to your full height. I was convinced, at that moment, that you were reflecting on Chantilly\u2019s small, diminutive stature. And it was at this point that I cut short your thoughts to say that, since he was indeed a very little fellow\u2014that Chantilly\u2014he would be better suited to the _Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s_.\u201d\\n\\nNot long afterward, we were going through an evening issue of the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux,\u201d when the following paragraphs caught our notice.\\n\\n\u201c_Extraordinary Murders_.\u2014This morning, about three o\u2019clock, the residents of the Quartier St. Roch were roused from sleep by a run of dreadful shrieks, apparently coming from the fourth story of a house in the Rue Morgue, which is known to be occupied solely by Madame L\u2019Espanaye and her daughter, Mademoiselle Camille L\u2019Espanaye. After some delay\u2014caused by a fruitless attempt to gain entry by the usual means\u2014the gate was forced open with a crowbar, and eight or ten of the neighbors went in, with two _gendarmes_. By then the cries had stopped; but as the group hurried up the first flight of stairs, two or more rough voices, raised in angry dispute, could be heard, and seemed to come from the upper part of the house. When the second landing was reached, those sounds had also ended, and everything was perfectly still. The party fanned out and searched room after room. At last, arriving at a large back chamber on the fourth story\u2014(the door of which, found locked with the key inside, was forced)\u2014they were met by a scene that struck everyone present with horror no less than with astonishment.\\n\\n\u201cThe place was in the wildest confusion\u2014the furniture smashed and scattered in every direction. There was only one bedstead; and from it the bed had been taken and flung into the middle of the floor. On a chair lay a razor, smeared with blood.\u201d\"}"
