          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c0580956e9062137b3eb451e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond was stirred by the sharp, exhilarating return of health; he believed that, as commander among the Athenians, he had secured a place worthy of his ambition; and, in his expectation of the conquest of Constantinople, he looked for an event that would stand out like a milestone in the long ruin of ages\u2014an achievement without equal in the history of mankind. He imagined a city of vast and venerable associations, whose site\u2019s beauty had long been the wonder of the world, rescued after many hundreds of years from the Moslems\u2019 stronghold, freed from slavery and barbarism, and restored to a people renowned for genius, civilization, and a love of liberty. Perdita lay in his restored society, in his affection, his hopes, and his reputation, much as a Sybarite reclines on a luxurious couch; every thought carried him away, and every feeling seemed to be steeped in a kindred, gentle warmth.\\n\\nWe reached Kishan on the 7th of July. Throughout our journey the weather stayed calm. Each morning, before dawn, we left our night encampment, and observed how the shadows withdrew from hill and valley, while the sun\u2019s first golden blaze drew near. The soldiers who accompanied us met the sight of beautiful country with lively national enthusiasm. When the star of day rose, it was greeted with triumphant music; and whenever the birds were heard in fragments, they filled the pauses between the notes. At noon we pitched our tents in some shaded valley, or sheltered in the green embrace of woods among the mountains, where a stream murmuring over pebbles lulled us into grateful sleep. Our evening march, quieter still, was even more pleasing than the restless stir of spirit we had felt in the morning. If the band played, they could not help choosing tunes of tempered feeling; the parting of love, or a lament for absence, was followed and ended by some grave hymn, which matched the peaceful loveliness of evening and lifted the mind toward lofty, religious thoughts. Often every sound fell away, so we might listen to the nightingale; then the fire-flies danced in bright order, and the soft cooing of the aziolo promised fair weather to travellers. Did we pass through a valley? Gentle shades gathered around us, and the rocks wore beautiful tints. If we climbed among the mountains, Greece\u2014like a living map\u2014lay spread beneath us, her famous pinnacles cleaving the sky, while her rivers ran in silver threads across the fertile ground. Almost afraid to draw breath, we English travellers gazed with rapture on this splendid landscape, so unlike the sober colors and melancholy grace of our own native scenery. When we left Macedonia, the fertile but low plains of Thrace offered fewer attractions, though the journey remained engaging. An advanced guard gave notice of our approach, and the local people were soon moving to pay honour to Lord Raymond.\"}"
