          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c0c39e17f6aba3c5a0288853\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You have to learn some of my philosophy. Think only of the past, because remembering it is what gives you pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can\u2019t give you credit for any philosophy like that. Your retrospection must be so completely free of blame that the comfort it brings isn\u2019t philosophy at all, but\u2014what\u2019s much better\u2014ignorance. With me, it\u2019s not like that. Painful memories keep intruding; they can\u2019t be pushed away, and they ought not to be. I\u2019ve been selfish all my life in practice, even if not in principle. As a child, I was taught what was right, but I wasn\u2019t taught to correct my temper. I was given good principles, but I was left to follow them out of pride and conceit. Unfortunately, as an only son\u2014for many years an only child\u2014I was spoiled by my parents. They were good themselves, especially my father, who was particularly benevolent and kind, but they allowed, encouraged, and almost taught me to be selfish and overbearing: to care for no one beyond my own family circle, to think poorly of everyone else in the world, and even to wish that I could think their sense and worth were inferior to my own. That was who I was, from eight to eight-and-twenty; and I might still have been that way, if not for you, dearest, loveliest Elizabeth. What do I not owe you? You taught me a lesson\u2014hard at first, but most beneficial. Because of you, I was properly humbled. I came to you without any certainty of how I would be received. You showed me how inadequate all my pretensions were to please a woman who was truly worth being pleased.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid you then persuade yourself that I would?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed I did. What will you think of my vanity? I believed you were wishing and expecting my addresses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy manners must have been at fault, but not intentionally, I assure you. I never meant to deceive you, though my spirits might often have led me astray. How could you not have hated me after that evening!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHate you! I was angry, perhaps, at first, but my anger soon began to take the right direction.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m almost afraid to ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. You blamed me for coming?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, indeed. I felt nothing but surprise.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour surprise couldn\u2019t be greater than mine at being noticed by you. My conscience told me I didn\u2019t deserve any extraordinary politeness, and I confess I didn\u2019t expect to receive more than what I was due.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy purpose then,\u201d Darcy replied, \u201cwas to show you, through every civility I could manage, that I was not so petty as to resent the past. And I hoped to win your forgiveness, to reduce your bad opinion, by letting you see that your criticisms had been taken to heart.\u201d\"}"
