          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"65915808bb131af7c11e7f2b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He stood up and left the room with my nurse, and in a moment my father entered.\\n\\nNothing at this moment could have given me greater pleasure than my father\u2019s arrival. I reached out my hand to him and cried,\\n\\n\u201cAre you then safe\u2014and Elizabeth\u2014and Ernest?\u201d\\n\\nMy father soothed me with assurances that they were well, and tried\u2014by dwelling on matters so dear to my heart\u2014to lift my spirits, which were sinking. But he soon realized that a prison can\u2019t be a place of cheer. \u201cWhat kind of place is this that you live in, my son?\u201d he said, looking sadly at the barred windows and the miserable state of the room. \u201cYou traveled to find happiness, but it seems a fatality follows you. And poor Clerval\u2014\u201d\\n\\nThe name of my unfortunate, murdered friend was more than I could bear in my weakened condition; I burst into tears.\\n\\n\u201cAlas! Yes, my father,\u201d I answered. \u201cSome destiny of the most horrible kind hangs over me, and I must live to carry it out\u2014or surely I should have died on the coffin of Henry.\u201d\\n\\nWe weren\u2019t allowed to talk for long, because my health was so precarious that every precaution was needed to keep me calm. Mr. Kirwin came in and insisted that my strength not be drained by too much exertion. But my father\u2019s presence felt to me like that of a good angel, and I gradually regained my health.\\n\\nAs my sickness left me, I fell into a gloomy, black melancholy that nothing could dispel. The image of Clerval was always before me\u2014ghastly and murdered. More than once, the agitation these thoughts stirred in me made my friends fear a dangerous relapse. Alas! Why did they preserve such a miserable, detested life for me? It must be so that I can fulfill my destiny, which is now drawing to a close. Soon\u2014oh, very soon\u2014death will extinguish these throbbings and relieve me from the immense weight of anguish that drags me to the dust; and in carrying out the award of justice, I will also sink to rest. At that time, death still seemed far off, even though the wish was always in my mind. I often sat for hours, motionless and silent, longing for some great revolution that would bury both me and my destroyer in its ruins.\\n\\nThe season of the assizes approached. I had already been three months in prison, and although I was still weak and in constant danger of relapsing, I was required to travel nearly a hundred miles to the country town where the court was held. Mr. Kirwin took it upon himself to handle every detail of collecting witnesses and arranging my defense.\"}"
