          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d3912ddd7b8eda1f2f8e621c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once that was done, I unfastened a few more of the loops from the rim, brought in a further section of the cloth, and then connected the loosened loops to their proper buttons. In this way, I could slide the entire upper part of the bag between the net-work and the hoop. It was clear that the hoop would now drop down inside the car, while the car\u2019s own weight, along with everything inside it, would be supported only by the buttons. At first, this might seem like too little support, but it really was not. The buttons were very strong, and they were also so close together that any one button carried only a small share of the total weight. In fact, even if the car and its contents had been three times heavier than they were, I would not have been worried at all. \\n\\nI lifted the hoop again, placing it back inside the gum-elastic covering, and propped it up to nearly its former height using three light poles set up for that purpose. This was done, of course, to keep the bag stretched tight at the top and to hold the lower part of the net-work in the right position. The only remaining step was to close the opening of the enclosure. I did this by gathering the folds of the material together and twisting them tightly on the inside with a stationary tourniquet.\\n\\n\u201cIn the sides of the covering, now fitted around the car, three circular panes of thick but clear glass had been inserted. Through them, I could see in every horizontal direction without difficulty. In the cloth that formed the bottom, there was also a fourth window of the same kind, aligned with a small opening in the floor of the car itself. This let me look straight down. But I could not place any similar opening overhead, because of the special way the top part had to be closed and the wrinkles that would form in the cloth. So I did not expect to see anything directly above me, at the zenith. That was not very important anyway, because even if I had been able to put a window at the top, the balloon would have blocked my view and made it useless.\\n\\n\u201cAbout a foot below one of the side windows was a circular opening, eight inches across, fitted with a brass rim. The rim\u2019s inner edge matched the windings of a screw. The large tube of the condenser was screwed into this rim, while the main body of the machine was, of course, inside the chamber of gum-elastic.\u201d\"}"
