{"request_id":"246f00b638753a8fc3ee5e2f","rewritten_text":"Each person compares it—not to the voice of any individual from a nation whose language he knows, but to the opposite. The Frenchman thinks it was the voice of a Spaniard, and “might have distinguished some words had he been acquainted with the Spanish.” The Dutchman insists it was that of a Frenchman; but we’re told that “not understanding French this witness was examined through an interpreter.” The Englishman believes it was the voice of a German, and “does not understand German.” The Spaniard is “sure” it belonged to an Englishman, but “judges by the intonation” altogether, “as he has no knowledge of the English.” The Italian thinks it was the voice of a Russian, but “has never conversed with a native of Russia.” A second Frenchman disagrees with the first, and is positive the voice was that of an Italian; but, “not being cognizant of that tongue,” he, like the Spaniard, is “convinced by the intonation.” Now, how strangely unusual must that voice really have been, about which testimony like this could be drawn—whose very tones, even, residents of the five great divisions of Europe could recognize nothing familiar in! You might say it could have been the voice of an Asiatic—of an African. Neither Asiatics nor Africans are common in Paris; but without denying the inference, I will now simply ask you to consider three points. One witness calls the voice “harsh rather than shrill.” Two others say it was “quick and unequal.” No words—no sounds resembling words—were mentioned by any witness as distinguishable.

“I know not,” continued Dupin, “what impression I may have made, so far, on your own understanding; but I do not hesitate to say that legitimate deductions even from this portion of the testimony—the portion about the gruff and shrill voices—are in themselves enough to create a suspicion that should guide all further progress in investigating the mystery. I said ‘legitimate deductions;’ but my meaning is not fully expressed that way. I meant to imply that the deductions are the sole proper ones, and that the suspicion arises inevitably from them as the single result. What that suspicion is, however, I won’t say just yet. I only want you to keep in mind that, for me, it was strong enough to give a definite shape—a certain direction—to my inquiries in the chamber.

“Now let us transport ourselves, in imagination, to this chamber. What shall we look for first? The means of escape used by the murderers. It is not too much to say that neither of us believes in præternatural events.”"}

