          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8e671634867c489962c8efc3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The doctor was an old man\u2014an awfully nice, kind-looking old man, at least the way he seemed when I got him up. I told him me and my brother had been over on Spanish Island hunting yesterday afternoon, and we\u2019d camped on a bit of a raft we\u2019d found. And about midnight, I said, he must\u2019ve kicked his gun in his sleep, because it went off and shot him in the leg. We wanted him to come over there and fix it, and we didn\u2019t want him saying a word about it or letting anybody know, because we meant to be home this evening and surprise the folks.\\n\\n\u201cWho are your folks?\u201d he asks.\\n\\n\u201cThe Phelpses, down yonder.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he says. Then, after a minute, he says:\\n\\n\u201cHow\u2019d you say he got shot?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe had a dream,\u201d I says, \u201cand it shot him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA singular dream,\u201d he says.\\n\\nSo he lit up his lantern, grabbed his saddle-bags, and we set out. But when he saw the canoe, he didn\u2019t like the look of it. He said it was big enough for one, but it didn\u2019t look pretty safe for two. I says:\\n\\n\u201cOh, you needn\u2019t be afeard, sir. She carried the three of us easy enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat three?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, me and Sid, and\u2014and\u2014and _the guns;_ that\u2019s what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he says.\\n\\nStill, he put his foot on the gunnel, rocked her, and shook his head, saying he reckoned he\u2019d go look for a bigger one. But all the others were locked up and chained, so he took my canoe instead. He told me to wait till he came back, or I could go hunt around further, or maybe I better go down home and get everything ready for the surprise if I wanted. But I told him I wouldn\u2019t, so I explained to him how to find the raft, and then he started off.\\n\\nI got an idea pretty soon. I says to myself, if he can\u2019t fix that leg in three shakes of a sheep\u2019s tail, as the saying goes\u2014if it takes him three or four days\u2014what are we going to do? Just sit there and wait till he lets the cat out of the bag? No, sir; I knew what _I\u2019ll_ do. I\u2019ll wait, and when he comes back, if he says he\u2019s got to go any more, I\u2019ll go down there too, if I have to swim. Then we\u2019ll take and tie him up, keep him, and shove out down the river. And when Tom\u2019s done with him, we\u2019ll give him what it\u2019s worth\u2014or all we\u2019ve got\u2014and then let him get ashore.\\n\\nSo I slipped into a lumber-pile to get some sleep. Next time I woke up, the sun was way up over my head. I jumped out and ran for the doctor\u2019s house, but they told me he\u2019d gone away in the night some time or other, and he hadn\u2019t come back yet.\"}"
