          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c30b1d9f18a606d7c2469710\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 7th. I rose early, and, to my great delight, at last saw what could admit of no doubt\u2014nothing less than the northern Pole itself. It lay there, beyond question, directly under my feet; yet, having climbed to so enormous a height, I could discern nothing with any certainty. Indeed, judging from the sequence of figures that marked my different altitudes at various times\u2014between six A.M. on the second of April and twenty minutes before nine A.M. of the same day (when the barometer fell)\u2014one might fairly conclude that, by four o\u2019clock in the morning of April the seventh, the balloon had attained a height of at least 7,254 miles above the surface of the sea. Such an elevation may seem staggering, but the estimate on which it rests almost certainly falls short of the truth. At all events, I unquestionably beheld the whole of the earth\u2019s greatest diameter; the entire northern hemisphere lay beneath me like a map projected orthographically, and the great circle of the equator itself traced the very edge of my horizon. Your Excellencies will, however, easily understand that the limited tracts hitherto unexplored within the Arctic circle\u2014though situated straight beneath me and therefore seen without any appearance of being foreshortened\u2014were still, in themselves, comparatively too small, and too far from the point of view, to allow any close or accurate inspection. Still, what I could make out was of a singular and stirring character. Northward from that vast rim just mentioned\u2014which, with slight qualification, may be taken as the boundary of human discovery in these parts\u2014there runs one continuous, or nearly continuous, expanse of ice. In the first few degrees of its course its surface is very noticeably flattened; farther on it sinks into a plane, and finally, becoming somewhat concave, it ends at the Pole in a circular centre, sharply defined. Its apparent diameter, as seen from the balloon, subtended an angle of about sixty-five seconds; and its dusky tint, varying in strength, was at all times darker than any other spot on the visible hemisphere, and at intervals deepened into the most absolute and impenetrable blackness. Beyond this, little could be determined. By twelve o\u2019clock the circular centre had sensibly diminished in circumference, and by seven P.M. I lost sight of it altogether; the balloon passing over the western edge of the ice and drifting away swiftly toward the equator.\\n\\n\u201cApril 8th. I observed a marked decrease in the earth\u2019s apparent diameter, along with a material change in its general color and aspect.\u201d\"}"
