          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"78b9062389c2f8f9e9572d8c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNow,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, laying his hand once more, gently, on the Doctor\u2019s arm, after a brief pause from both of them, \u201cto what, then, would you attribute this attack?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think,\u201d replied Doctor Manette, \u201cthat there was a powerful and extraordinary return of the train of thought and recollection which first brought on the illness. Certain intense associations\u2014of the most painful kind\u2014were, I believe, brought back with vivid force. It is likely that there had been a long-standing dread in his mind, that those associations would be recalled\u2014perhaps under particular circumstances\u2014perhaps on some specific occasion. He tried in vain to brace himself; and maybe the very attempt to brace himself only left him less able to withstand it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWould he remember what occurred during the relapse?\u201d asked Mr. Lorry, pausing in his question.\\n\\nThe Doctor looked about the room in a desolate way, shook his head, and answered, in a low voice, \u201cNot at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow, regarding what lies ahead,\u201d Mr. Lorry suggested.\\n\\n\u201cAs for the future,\u201d said the Doctor, regaining steadiness, \u201cI have every reason to hope. Since it pleased Heaven, in its mercy, to restore him so soon, I should have great hope. He, having yielded under the pressure of some complicated thing\u2014long dreaded, long vaguely foreseen, and long struggled against\u2014and then recovering after the cloud had burst and passed, I should hope that the worst is over.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well! That is good comfort. I\u2019m thankful!\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m thankful!\u201d echoed the Doctor, bowing his head with reverence.\\n\\n\u201cThere are two other matters,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201con which I am anxious to be instructed. May I go on?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou cannot do your friend a better service.\u201d The Doctor took his hand.\\n\\n\u201cThen to the first. He is given to study, and unusually active; he throws himself with great ardour into the gaining of professional knowledge, into the conducting of experiments, into many things. Now\u2014does he do too much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think so. It may be the temper of his mind to be always in some singular need of occupation. That may be partly natural to it, and partly the consequence of affliction. The less it was taken up with wholesome things, the more it would be in danger of turning toward the unhealthy. He may have noticed it in himself, and found the truth.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you certain he is not under too great a strain?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think I am quite sure of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Manette, if he were overworked now\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lorry, I doubt whether that could easily be. There has been a violent stress in one direction, and it requires a counterweight.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPardon me, as a man of business who persists. Suppose, for the moment, that he _was_ overworked; would it not show itself in some fresh return of this disorder?\u201d\"}"
