          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0857a64e0710ac67c62fd73d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cSo what result has followed, then? What impression have I made on your imagination?\u201d\\n\\nAs Dupin asked me the question, I felt my skin crawl. \u201cA madman,\u201d I said. \u201cSomeone raving, escaped from a nearby Maison de Sant\u00e9.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn some ways,\u201d he answered, \u201cyour idea isn\u2019t wrong. But the voices of madmen, even when they\u2019re at their wildest, never match that particular voice heard on the stairs. Madmen belong to some nation, and even when their words are a mess, their speech still has a clear pattern of syllables. And besides, the hair of a madman isn\u2019t like the hair I\u2019m holding in my hand right now. I pulled this small tuft free from the fingers of Madame L\u2019Espanaye, which were clenched so tightly. Tell me what you think of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDupin!\u201d I said, completely shaken. \u201cThis hair is very unusual. It isn\u2019t human hair.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI haven\u2019t claimed that it is,\u201d he replied. \u201cBut before we decide, I want you to look at the small sketch I\u2019ve drawn here on this paper. It\u2019s a fac-simile of what one part of the testimony describes as \u2018dark bruises, and deep indentations of finger nails\u2019 on Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s throat, and what another part describes, by Messrs. Dumas and Etienne, as \u2018a series of livid spots, evidently the impression of fingers.\u2019\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ll see,\u201d Dupin continued, spreading the paper on the table between us, \u201cthat the drawing shows a firm, steady grip. There\u2019s no sign of slipping. Each finger seems to have kept the terrible hold it first made\u2014perhaps right up until the victim died. Now try placing all your fingers at once into the impressions you see there.\u201d\\n\\nI tried, but I couldn\u2019t do it.\\n\\n\u201cWe may not be testing this in the right way,\u201d he said. \u201cThe paper lies flat, but a human throat is round. Here is a piece of wood with a circumference about the same as the throat. Wrap the drawing around it, then try again.\u201d\\n\\nI did, but the problem was even clearer than before. \u201cThis,\u201d I said, \u201cis not the mark of a human hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow read,\u201d Dupin replied, \u201cthis passage from Cuvier.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a detailed anatomical description of the large, tawny Ourang-Outang from the East Indian islands. Everyone knows the creature\u2019s huge size, its extraordinary strength and energy, its wild ferocity, and its ability to imitate. I understood the full horror of the murder at once.\\n\\n\u201cThe description of the digits,\u201d I said after I finished reading, \u201cmatches this drawing exactly.\u201d\"}"
