          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"37b047da8bc3b4bce0fb239a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma did not feel any remorse for her condescending manner in going to the Coles. The visit left her with many agreeable recollections the following day; and whatever she might be thought to have sacrificed by keeping herself in dignified seclusion, was more than made up to her by the brilliance of her popularity. She must have pleased the Coles\u2014fine, deserving people, who ought to be made happy!\u2014and she would leave behind a name that would not soon fade.\\n\\nPerfect happiness, even when recalled, is not something one often meets; and there were two matters on which she was not entirely at ease. She wondered whether she had not overstepped a woman\u2019s duty by passing on to Frank Churchill her suspicions about Jane Fairfax\u2019s feelings. It was hardly proper; yet the notion had taken such hold of her that it would slip out, and his readiness to submit to everything she told him was, in a way, a compliment to her discernment\u2014so that it was hard for her to be fully certain she ought to have kept her silence.\\n\\nThe other point of regret also concerned Jane Fairfax; and there she had no doubt. She regretted, plainly and without any qualification, the inferiority of her own playing and singing. She truly mourned the idleness of her childhood, and then sat down and practised with vigour for an hour and a half.\\n\\nShe was then interrupted by Harriet\u2019s coming in; and if Harriet\u2019s praise could have satisfied her, she might soon have been comforted.\\n\\n\u201cOh! if I could only play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t put us together, Harriet. My playing isn\u2019t any more like hers than a lamp is like sunshine.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! dear\u2014I think you play the best of the two. I\u2019m sure you play quite as well as she does. I know I\u2019d rather hear you. Everybody last night said how well you played.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThose who understood anything about it must have noticed the difference. The truth is, Harriet, that my playing is just good enough to be praised, but Jane Fairfax\u2019s goes much farther.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I shall always think you play quite as well as she does, or that if there is any difference, no one would ever be able to tell. Mr. Cole said how much taste you had; and Mr. Frank Churchill talked a great deal about your taste, and said he valued taste far more than execution.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! but Jane Fairfax has both, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you sure? I saw she had execution, but I didn\u2019t know she had any taste. No one ever spoke of it. And I hate Italian singing\u2014it\u2019s impossible to make out a word of it. Besides, if she plays so very well, you know, it\u2019s no more than she\u2019s required to do, because she\u2019ll have to teach.\u201d}\"}"
