          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e67d07c3e56aca8271f19b35\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Wickham was so thoroughly content with what had passed that he troubled himself no more about it, nor once again annoyed his dear sister Elizabeth by bringing the matter up; and she was glad to feel that she had said enough to keep him quiet.\\n\\nThe day of his and Lydia\u2019s departure soon arrived; and Mrs. Bennet had to endure a separation which, since her husband by no means shared her plan of having them all go to Newcastle, was likely to last at least a twelvemonth.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear Lydia,\u201d she cried, \u201cwhen shall we meet again?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Lord! I don\u2019t know. Perhaps not for these two or three years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWrite to me very often, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAs often as I can. But you know married women never have much time to write. My sisters may write to _me_. They will have nothing else to do.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Wickham\u2019s parting words were far more tender than his wife\u2019s. He smiled, looked very handsome, and said a great many flattering things.\\n\\n\u201cHe is as fine a fellow,\u201d said Mr. Bennet as soon as they were out of the house. \u201cHe simpers, and smirks, and makes love to us all. I am prodigiously proud of him. I defy even Sir William Lucas himself to produce a better son-in-law.\u201d\\n\\nThe loss of her daughter left Mrs. Bennet dull for several days.\\n\\n\u201cI often think,\u201d said she, \u201cthat nothing is so dreadful as being separated from one\u2019s friends. Without them one feels so forlorn.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is the natural result, you see, madam, of marrying off a daughter,\u201d said Elizabeth. \u201cIt ought to make you more content that your other four are still single.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is no such thing. Lydia does not leave me because she is married; she goes only because her husband\u2019s regiment happens to be so far away. If it had been nearer, she would not have gone so soon.\u201d\\n\\nYet the languid state into which this event threw her was soon eased, and her thoughts opened again to the stir of expectation, by a piece of news that had just begun to be talked of. The Netherfield housekeeper had received orders to get ready for the arrival of her master, who was to come down in a day or two, to shoot there for several weeks. Mrs. Bennet was all in a flutter. She looked at Jane, smiled, and shook her head, now one way and now the other.\\n\\n\u201cWell, well, and so Mr. Bingley is coming down, sister,\u201d (for Mrs. Philips was the first to tell her). \u201cWell, so much the better. Not that I care about it, though. He is nothing to us, you know, and I am sure I never want to see him again. But, if he chooses it, he is very welcome to come to Netherfield.\u201d\"}"
