{"request_id":"c2c7288cce3f30cf9811c3e4","rewritten_text":"I came upon several funerals; they were attended by only a small number of mourners, and the onlookers took them for warnings of the most dreadful kind. Some watched the processions with a fierce, eager curiosity—others slipped away in fear—some wept openly.

Adrian’s chief work, once the sick had been given immediate relief, was to conceal from the people of London the symptoms and the course of the plague. He understood that fear and gloomy forebodings were powerful allies to disease; that a mind given over to despondency and brooding made the body, in its very constitution, unusually prone to infection. For that reason, nothing improper or shocking could be seen: the shops were generally open, and the stream of passengers was kept up, at least in some measure. Yet though the outward look of an infected town was avoided, London still seemed to me—who had not witnessed it since the visitation began—changed enough to be unmistakable. There were no carriages, and grass had grown high in the streets; the houses wore a desolate aspect; most of the shutters were shut; and the people I met had a ghastly, startled stare, quite unlike the usual businesslike manner of Londoners. My single carriage drew attention as it rattled on toward the Protectoral Palace, and the fashionable streets leading to it looked even more cheerless and empty. I found Adrian’s antechamber crowded; it was his appointed time for receiving visitors. I did not wish to interrupt his labours, and so waited, observing the coming and going of the petitioners. They were mostly from the middle and lower ranks, whose means of support failed when trade stopped, and from that restless spirit of money-making in every form so characteristic of our country. There was an air of anxiety, sometimes outright terror, in the newcomers, set sharply against the resigned—indeed, at times satisfied—look of those who had already been admitted. I could see the effect of my friend in the quicker movements and brighter faces of his applicants. At two o’clock the doors were closed; after that, no one was admitted. Those turned away went off sullenly or in sorrow, and I then entered the audience-chamber.

I was struck by the improvement in Adrian’s health. He was no longer bent to the ground, like an over-nursed spring flower, that, thrusting up beyond its strength, is weighed down even by the crown of its own blossoms. His eyes were bright, his expression steady; a sense of concentrated purpose seemed to spread through his whole person, very unlike the former languor. He sat at a table with several secretaries, who were arranging petitions or recording the notes taken during that day’s audience. Two or three petitioners still remained. I admired his justice and patience. Those who had the means to live outside London, he advised at once to leave it, and he provided them with the means of doing so."}

