          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5dd8b7835dba2bdaec6b7f94\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was something wonderful about tidying up and then stopping for a quiet moment before starting life again. In our village, on Saturday nights, that feeling made Joe stay out about half an hour longer than he did on other nights. When the half hour was up, and the rum and water ran out at the same time, Joe stood up to go and took me by the hand.\\n\\n\u201cWait just half a moment, Mr. Gargery,\u201d said the strange man. \u201cI think I\u2019ve got a bright new shilling somewhere in my pocket. If I do, the boy gets it.\u201d\\n\\nHe searched through his small pile of change, found it, folded it up in some crumpled paper, and handed it to me. \u201cIt\u2019s yours,\u201d he said. \u201cJust remember\u2014your own.\u201d\\n\\nI thanked him, staring at him in a way that was far beyond good manners, while I held on tightly to Joe. He said good-night to Joe, and good-night to Mr. Wopsle too, who went out with us. Then he only gave me a look with his steady aiming eye\u2014no, not exactly a look, because he shut it away. Still, you could tell wonders could be done with an eye by hiding it.\\n\\nOn the way home, if I had felt like talking, I would have had all the talking to myself. Mr. Wopsle left us at the door of the Jolly Bargemen, and Joe walked all the way home with his mouth wide open, trying to rinse the rum out with as much air as he could. But I was so stunned by this sudden return of my old mistake and my old acquaintance that I couldn\u2019t think of anything else.\\n\\nWhen we got into the kitchen, my sister wasn\u2019t in a very bad mood. That made Joe feel bold enough to tell her about the bright shilling. \u201cA bad one, I\u2019ll bet,\u201d said Mrs. Joe, pleased with herself. \u201cIf he didn\u2019t mean it, he wouldn\u2019t have given it to the boy. Let\u2019s see it.\u201d\\n\\nI took it out of the paper, and it turned out to be a good one. \u201cBut what is this?\u201d Mrs. Joe said, dropping the shilling and picking up the paper again. \u201cTwo one-pound notes?\u201d\\n\\nThey were nothing less than two thick, hot-looking one-pound notes, as if they had been on the closest terms with every cattle market in the county. Joe grabbed his hat again and ran to the Jolly Bargemen to return them to their owner. While he was gone, I sat on my usual stool and stared at my sister, feeling sure the man wouldn\u2019t be there.\\n\\nSoon Joe came back. He said the man had gone, but that Joe had left word at the Three Jolly Bargemen about the notes.\"}"
