          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0efa8ccd713725bdd9e23bb6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Were our proud dreams meant to fade like this? Our name had been written \u201ca little lower than the angels,\u201d and now we were no better than something brief and fragile. We had called ourselves the \u201cparagon of animals,\u201d and yet we were only \u201cthe quint-essence of dust.\u201d We were bitter that the pyramids had lasted longer than the embalmed body of their builder. Even the small shepherd\u2019s hut of straw we passed on the road had, in its simple structure, a kind of lasting power greater than the whole human race. How could we accept this painful change from our earlier hopes, from the strength we seemed to have?\\n\\nThen an internal voice, clear and unmistakable, seemed to speak:\u2014So it was decided from eternity. The horses that carry Time forward are bound to this hour and its ending, because the void brought forth its burden. Would you try to read backward the unchangeable laws of Necessity?\\n\\nMother of the world! Servant of the Omnipotent! eternal, unchanging Necessity! You sit with busy hands, weaving the unbreakable chain of events. I will not complain about what you do. If my human mind cannot accept that everything that is, is right, then I will still accept this: what is, must be. I will sit among the ruins and smile. We were not born to enjoy; we were born to submit and to hope.\\n\\nWill the reader grow tired if I describe our long journey from Paris to Geneva in detail? If, day by day, I wrote down the many miseries of our life as a journal, could my hand, or my language, find words for the full range of our suffering\u2014how one dreadful event crowded in on the next? Be patient, reader, whoever you are, wherever you live, whether you belong to some spiritual race or you come from some surviving pair. Your nature will still be human, and your home will still be the earth. Here you will read about the deeds of a people long gone, and you will wonder whether those who suffered what you now read were made of the same frail flesh and soft organization as you. That is true\u2014so weep. You are alone, and you will be gentle; you will shed tears of compassion. Still, pay attention to the story and learn the acts and sufferings of those who came before.\\n\\nBut the last events that marked our progress through France were so full of strange horror and dark misery that I cannot linger on them. If I broke down each incident, every small moment would hold a terrible tale, and even the smallest word would make the blood in your young veins run cold. It is right that I should raise this monument for the instruction of those who are gone, but it is not right to drag you through the wards of a hospital or into the hidden rooms of the charnel-house.\"}"
