          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"175fabb1f7f8058f5cd9f8b4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His account was this: that, while travelling from his home and passing through Datchet, he was startled to see smoke rising from the inn\u2019s chimney; thinking he might find fellow-travellers for his journey gathered there, he knocked, and was admitted. There was no one in the house except Lucy and her mother. The latter had been struck by rheumatism and had lost the use of her limbs, and so, one after another, all the remaining inhabitants of the country had gone on, leaving them behind. Lucy begged the man to remain with her; in a week or two her mother would be better, and then they could set out together; yet, she said, they must surely perish if they were left in such helplessness and loneliness. The man replied that his wife and children were already among the emigrants, and that, in his judgment, he could not stay. As a last resort, Lucy gave him a letter for Idris, to be delivered to her wherever he might meet us. He carried out this errand at least, and Idris received, with feeling, the letter that followed:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHONOURED LADY,\\n\\n\u201cI am persuaded you will remember me and have pity on me, and I venture to hope you will help me; what other hope is there for me? Forgive the way I write\u2014I am so bewildered. A month ago my dear mother was deprived of the use of her limbs. She is already better, and in another month I am sure I shall be able to travel, as you were so kind as to say you would arrange for us. But now everyone has gone\u2014everyone\u2014as they left, each one said that perhaps my mother would be better before we were quite deserted. Yet three days ago I went to Samuel Woods, who, because of his new-born child, stayed to the last; and, as they had a large family, I thought I might persuade them to wait a little longer for us. Instead, I found the house empty. I have not seen a living soul since, until this good man came. \u2014What will become of us? My mother does not know our condition; she is so ill that I have kept it from her.\\n\\n\u201cWill you not send someone to us? I am sure we must perish miserably as we are. If I were to try to move my mother now, she would die on the road; and if, when she is better, I were able, I cannot tell how I should find the roads, and make so many miles to the sea\u2014by then you would all be in France, and the great ocean would lie between us, which is dreadful even to sailors.\u201d\"}"
