          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6c2e534ce0eb05bd9ca6b738\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Shall I be carried up to the skies, on flowery beds of comfort, while others fight to win the prize and sail through blood-y seas?\\n\\nHe was known as a wonderful reader. At church \u201csociables,\u201d he was always asked to read poetry. When he finished, the ladies would lift their hands, let them fall helplessly into their laps, and \u201cwall\u201d their eyes, shaking their heads as if to say, \u201cWords cannot express it; it is too beautiful, TOO beautiful for this mortal earth.\u201d\\n\\nAfter the hymn, Rev. Mr. Sprague turned himself into a bulletin-board and read out \u201cnotices\u201d about meetings, societies, and other things, until it felt as if the list would go on until the end of the world. It was a strange custom, and it still exists in America, even in cities, even in this age when newspapers are everywhere. Often, the less there is to justify a traditional custom, the harder it is to get rid of it.\\n\\nThen the minister prayed. It was a good, generous prayer, full of details. He prayed for the church and for the little children in it; for the other churches in the village; for the village itself; for the county; for the State; for the officers of the State; for the United States; for the churches of the United States; for Congress; for the President; for the officers of the government; for poor sailors, tossed by stormy seas; for oppressed millions suffering under European monarchies and Oriental despots; for those who have the light and the good news but still do not have eyes to see or ears to hear; for the heathen in far islands of the sea. He ended with a request that the words he was about to speak might find grace and favor, and be like seed planted in fertile ground, producing a grateful harvest of good in time. Amen.\\n\\nThere was a rustling of dresses, and the congregation sat down. The boy whose story this book tells did not like the prayer. He only endured it, if he even did that much. He was restless the whole time. He kept track of the details of the prayer without meaning to, because he was not really listening. He knew the usual ground and the clergyman\u2019s regular route through it. When a small new detail was added, his ear caught it at once, and his whole nature pushed back. He thought the additions were unfair and scoundrelly.\\n\\nDuring the prayer, a fly landed on the back of the pew in front of him and tormented his spirit by calmly rubbing its hands together, holding its head with its arms, and polishing itself so vigorously that it seemed to almost separate from its body, leaving a thin thread of a neck visible. It scraped its wings with its hind legs and smoothed them back onto its body as if they were coat-tails. It went through its whole grooming routine as calmly as if it knew it was completely safe.\"}"
