          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a8cff365d086a2d32df07230\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cMr. Elliot looked up with so much respect.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cQuite delightful!\u201d cried Mrs. Clay, though she did not dare to look at Anne. \u201cIt\u2019s exactly like father and son! Dear Miss Elliot, may I not say father and son?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I don\u2019t forbid anyone from saying what they like. If you have those ideas! But, honestly, I can\u2019t see that his attention is any more than what other men show.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Miss Elliot!\u201d exclaimed Mrs. Clay. She raised her hands and eyes, and then kept the rest of her surprise to herself in a convenient silence.\\n\\n\u201cWell, my dear Penelope, you don\u2019t need to be so worried about him. You know I invited him. I sent him away with smiles. And when I learned he was really going to his friends at Thornberry Park for the whole day tomorrow, I felt sorry for him.\u201d\\n\\nAnne admired how well her friend acted. She could show so much pleasure, both in the expectation and in the actual arrival of the very person whose presence would truly interfere with her main goal. It was impossible not to think that Mrs. Clay must hate seeing Mr. Elliot. Still, she could put on a friendly, calm expression and seem perfectly satisfied with having to spend only half as much time with Sir Walter as she would have otherwise.\\n\\nFor Anne, it was very upsetting to watch Mr. Elliot enter the room. It was also painful to have him come up to her and speak. She had been used to the idea that he could not always be completely sincere, but now she saw insincerity in everything. His careful respect for her father, compared with what he had said before, was disgusting. And when she remembered how cruel he had been to Mrs. Smith, she could hardly bear to look at his present smiles and gentle manner, or to hear his practiced kind words.\\n\\nShe meant to avoid any change in her manners that might make him feel the need to correct her. Her main goal was to avoid any questions or attention. Still, she planned to be as clearly cool to him as she could, while keeping their relationship. She also meant to quietly step back, as much as she could, from the few moments of unnecessary closeness that had been slowly drawn out of her. So she was more guarded and more cool than she had been the night before.\\n\\nHe wanted to spark her curiosity again about how and where he had heard her praised before. He wanted very much to be encouraged with more attention. But the charm was gone. He found that the lively warmth of a public room was what helped kindle his modest cousin\u2019s vanity. He also found that, at least now, he could not do it with any of the attempts he might make, given how strongly the others demanded attention.\"}"
