          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b46b35df784c6c75216b96d7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And if he asked me why I wanted it, and why I thought I had any right to it, I would tell him\u2014though he cared very little for such small hopes\u2014that I had loved Estella dearly for a long time. Even though I had lost her and would have to live as a grieving man, anything connected to her was still closer and more important to me than anything else in the world. Seeing that Mr. Jaggers stayed perfectly still and silent, and seemed unmoved by my plea, I turned to Wemmick and said, \u201cWemmick, I know you\u2019re a man with a gentle heart. I\u2019ve seen your comfortable home, your old father, and all the innocent, cheerful, playful ways you have of making your work feel brighter. I beg you to say something to Mr. Jaggers for me, and to tell him that, given all the circumstances, he should be more open with me.\u201d\\n\\nI had never seen two men stare at each other so strangely as Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick did after that. At first, I worried that Wemmick would be fired at once. But that fear faded when I saw Mr. Jaggers relax into something like a smile, and when Wemmick grew more confident.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is all this?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers. \u201cYou, with an old father? You, with pleasant and playful ways?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d Wemmick answered. \u201cIf I don\u2019t bring them here, what difference does it make?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, putting his hand on my arm and smiling openly, \u201cthis man must be the cleverest impostor in all London.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d Wemmick replied, getting bolder and bolder. \u201cI think you\u2019re the impostor.\u201d\\n\\nThey exchanged those same odd looks again, both still seeming to doubt that the other was really accepting him.\\n\\n\u201cYou, with a pleasant home?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers.\\n\\n\u201cOnly if it doesn\u2019t get in the way of business,\u201d Wemmick said. \u201cLet it be so. And now, sir, I wouldn\u2019t be surprised if you\u2019re planning to have a pleasant home of your own one of these days, when you\u2019re tired of all this work.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Jaggers nodded his head two or three times, as if looking back on it, and even let out a sigh. \u201cPip,\u201d he said, \u201cwe won\u2019t talk about \u2018poor dreams.\u2019 You know more about that sort of thing than I do, since you have had fresher experience. But now, about this other matter. I\u2019ll put a case to you. Mind\u2014 I admit nothing.\u201d\\n\\nHe waited for me to say that I understood he had clearly meant that he admitted nothing.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Pip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, \u201cput this case. Imagine that a woman, under the kind of circumstances you\u2019ve described, kept her child hidden, and had to tell her legal adviser about it. She did so because he told her that, for the sake of the range of his defense, he needed to know how the situation really stood with that child.\u201d\"}"
