          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"66f3a2f598a0442b507c6b27\",\"rewritten_text\":\"During dinner, Mr. Bennet barely spoke at all; but once the servants had withdrawn, he thought it was time to have some conversation with his guest. So he brought up a topic he expected would let him shine, remarking that he seemed very fortunate in his patroness. Lady Catherine de Bourgh\u2019s attention to his wishes and her consideration for his comfort struck him as truly remarkable. Mr. Bennet could not have chosen better. Mr. Collins was eloquent in praising her. The subject put him into more than his usual solemn mood; and with an air of the utmost importance, he protested that he had never in his life witnessed such conduct in a person of rank\u2014such affability and condescension\u2014as he had personally experienced from Lady Catherine. She had been gracious enough to approve both sermons he had already had the honor of preaching before her. She had also asked him twice to dine at Rosings, and had sent for him only the Saturday before, so he could take his place in her evening quadrille. Many people, he knew, considered Lady Catherine proud, but he had never seen anything in her except affability. She had always spoken to him as she would to any other gentleman; she had raised not the slightest objection to his joining the neighborhood\u2019s society, nor to his leaving his parish now and then for a week or two to visit his relations. She had even condescended to advise him to marry as soon as he could, as long as he chose with discretion. And once she had visited his humble parsonage, where she had approved everything he had been doing to make alterations, and had even deigned to suggest a few herself\u2014some shelves in the upstairs closets.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s all very proper and civil, I\u2019m sure,\u201d said Mrs. Bennet. \u201cAnd I dare say she\u2019s a very agreeable woman. It\u2019s a pity that great ladies in general aren\u2019t more like her. Does she live near you, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe garden where my humble home stands is separated from Rosings Park by only a lane\u2014her Ladyship\u2019s residence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI thought you said she was a widow, sir. Does she have any family?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe has one only daughter\u2014the heiress of Rosings\u2014and of very extensive property.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh,\u201d cried Mrs. Bennet, shaking her head, \u201cthen she\u2019s better off than many girls. And what kind of young lady is she? Is she handsome?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe is a most charming young lady, indeed. Lady Catherine herself says that, in terms of true beauty, Miss de Bourgh is far superior to the handsomest woman of her sex; because there\u2019s something in her features that marks her as a young woman of distinguished birth.\u201d\"}"
