          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8ca3b2352da94aa47b1ae606\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But I have to warn you first: I will tell Sybil everything. She\u2019s coming to lunch with me tomorrow to talk about bonnets. And if Mr. Podgers finds out that you have a bad temper, or a tendency to gout, or a wife living in Bayswater, I will certainly tell her all about it.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur smiled and shook his head. \u201cI\u2019m not afraid,\u201d he said. \u201cSybil knows me as well as I know her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, I\u2019m sorry to hear you say that,\u201d the Marchioness of Jedburgh replied. \u201cThe best foundation for marriage is a mutual misunderstanding. No, I\u2019m not cynical. I just have experience, which is basically the same thing. Mr. Podgers, Lord Arthur Savile is dying to have his hand read. Don\u2019t tell him that he\u2019s engaged to one of the most beautiful girls in London, because that was printed in the _Morning Post_ a month ago.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Lady Windermere,\u201d cried the Marchioness of Jedburgh, \u201cplease let Mr. Podgers stay here a little longer. He just told me I should go on the stage, and I\u2019m so interested.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf he told you that, Lady Jedburgh, I will take him away for sure,\u201d said Lady Windermere. \u201cCome here right now, Mr. Podgers, and read Lord Arthur\u2019s hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell,\u201d said Lady Jedburgh, making a small pout as she stood up from the sofa, \u201cif I can\u2019t be allowed to go on the stage, then I should at least be allowed to be part of the audience.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course,\u201d said Lady Windermere. \u201cWe\u2019re all going to be part of the audience. Now, Mr. Podgers, be sure to tell us something nice. Lord Arthur is one of my favorites.\u201d\\n\\nBut the moment Mr. Podgers saw Lord Arthur\u2019s hand, he turned strangely pale and said nothing. A shiver seemed to run through him, and his thick, bushy eyebrows twitched in a nervous, irritating way, the way they did when he was confused. Then huge drops of sweat appeared on his yellow forehead, like a poisonous mist, and his fat fingers went cold and clammy.\\n\\nLord Arthur noticed every one of these signs of distress. For the first time in his life, he felt fear. He wanted to rush out of the room, but he held back. It was better to find out the worst, whatever it was, than to be stuck in this awful uncertainty.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m waiting, Mr. Podgers,\u201d he said.\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019re all waiting,\u201d Lady Windermere said quickly and impatiently, but the cheiromantist still didn\u2019t answer.\\n\\n\u201cI think Arthur is going on the stage,\u201d said Lady Jedburgh. \u201cAnd after your scolding, Mr. Podgers is afraid to tell him so.\u201d\"}"
