          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"da38170f2d07bfa134775ab7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In fact, he was dressed as \u201cJonas the Graveless, or the Corpse-Snatcher of Chertsey Barn,\u201d one of his most remarkable impersonations\u2014one the Cantervilles had every reason to remember, since it was the real spark for their quarrel with their neighbor, Lord Rufford. It was about a quarter past two in the morning, and, as far as he could tell, no one was moving. Still, as he strolled toward the library to check whether any traces remained of the bloodstain, two figures suddenly sprang out at him from a dark corner. They flailed their arms wildly above their heads and shrieked \u201cBOO!\u201d right into his ear.\\n\\nOvercome by panic\u2014which, given the circumstances, was only natural\u2014he bolted for the staircase, but Washington Otis was waiting there with the big garden syringe. Hemmed in by his enemies on every side, driven almost to the edge, he disappeared into the large iron stove, which, fortunately for him, was not lit. From there he had to make his way home through the flues and chimneys, arriving in his own room in a terrible state of dirt, chaos, and despair.\\n\\nAfter that, he was not seen again on any night-time expedition. The twins lay in wait for him on several occasions and every night scattered the passages with nutshells, to the great annoyance of their parents and the servants, but it made no difference. It was clear that his feelings were so badly hurt that he wouldn\u2019t show up again. Mr. Otis therefore resumed his major work on the history of the Democratic Party, which he had been working on for some years; Mrs. Otis organized a wonderful clam-bake that amazed the whole county; the boys took up lacrosse, euchre, poker, and other American national games; and Virginia rode around the lanes on her pony, accompanied by the young Duke of Cheshire, who had come to spend the last week of his holidays at Canterville Chase. Everyone assumed the ghost had gone away. In fact, Mr. Otis wrote a letter to Lord Canterville saying as much. In reply, Lord Canterville expressed his great pleasure at the news and sent his best congratulations to the Minister\u2019s worthy wife.\\n\\nThe Otises, however, were being fooled, because the ghost was still in the house. Though now almost an invalid, he was in no mood to let things rest\u2014especially once he heard that among the guests was the young Duke of Cheshire. His grand-uncle, Lord Francis Stilton, had once bet a hundred guineas with Colonel Carbury that he would play dice with the Canterville ghost. The next morning, he was found lying on the floor of the card room in such a helpless paralytic state that, though he lived to a great age, he was never able to say anything again except \u201cDouble Sixes.\u201d\"}"
