          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4177fbf0f5e09105fccfcd7c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Far off, the flickering line of gas-lamps bent away into the distance, and just beyond a small, walled-in house a lone hansom waited, the driver asleep within. He set off briskly toward Portland Place, glancing over his shoulder now and then, as if he were afraid someone might be trailing him. At the corner of Rich Street two men stood reading a small notice pinned to a hoarding. A strange prickle of curiosity seized him, and he crossed over.\\n\\nAs he drew near, the word \u201cMurder,\u201d printed in bold black letters, caught his eye. He gave a start, and a hot flush spread up into his cheek. It was an advertisement offering a reward for any information that would lead to the arrest of a man of medium height, between thirty and forty years old, wearing a billy-cock hat, a black coat, and check trousers, with a scar on his right cheek. He read it again and again, unable to stop himself, and found his thoughts turning to whether the poor wretch would ever be caught, and how he had come by that scar. Maybe, some day, his own name would be pasted up on the walls of London. Maybe, too, a price would be put on his head.\\n\\nThe idea sickened him with sheer horror. He wheeled about and hurried on into the night.\\n\\nWhere he went, he could scarcely say. He had only a hazy recollection of threading through a maze of mean houses, of getting swallowed up in a tangle of gloomy streets, and it was bright dawn before he finally emerged in Piccadilly Circus. As he wandered back toward Belgrave Square, he met the great waggons bound for Covent Garden. The carters, in their white smocks, with sunburnt faces and thick, curly hair, marched on with steady purpose, cracking their whips and calling to one another now and then; on the back of a huge grey horse, the leader of a clattering team, sat a chubby boy with a battered hat and a bunch of primroses, gripping the mane tightly with his small hands and laughing. The towering heaps of vegetables looked like blocks of jade against the morning sky\u2014like masses of green jade set against the pink petals of some extraordinary rose. Lord Arthur felt oddly stirred, though he could not have said why. There was something in the dawn\u2019s delicate beauty that struck him as inexpressibly pathetic, and he thought of all the days that break in loveliness and then fall into storm. Even these rustics, with their rough, good-natured voices and their easy, careless ways, seemed to him to show him a strange London: one free of the sin of night and the smoke of day, a pale, ghostly city, a desolate place of tombs. He wondered what they made of it, and whether they knew anything of its splendour and its shame\u2014of its fierce, blazing-coloured joys, and of everything it both creates and ruins from morning to night.\"}"
