          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b636c8dae31ac9b9c4198131\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Many years back, I entered into an intimacy with Mr. William Legrand. He belonged to an old Huguenot line, and had once been well off; yet a run of misfortunes had brought him down to poverty. To spare himself the humiliation that followed upon his losses, he quit New Orleans\u2014his ancestors\u2019 city\u2014and took up residence at Sullivan\u2019s Island, off near Charleston, South Carolina.\\n\\nThis Island is a singular place indeed. It is made up almost entirely of sea sand, and runs to about three miles in length. Nowhere does it widen beyond a quarter of a mile. From the mainland it is divided by a creek so slight you can scarcely make it out, which works its way through a tangle of reeds and slime, a haunt of the marsh hen. As you might expect, the growth is thin, or at least stunted. You see no trees of any size. Near the western end, where Fort Moultrie stands, and where there are some wretched frame buildings occupied in summer by the refugees from Charleston\u2019s dust and fever, you may, in fact, find the bristly palmetto; but save for that western corner, and for a strip of hard white beach along the seacoast, the island is thickly mantled with a dense undergrowth of sweet myrtle, prized by English horticulturists. The shrub there often reaches fifteen or twenty feet, and makes an almost impenetrable thicket, loading the air with its fragrance.\\n\\nWithin the deepest parts of this coppice, not far from the eastern\u2014more remote\u2014end of the island, Legrand had put up a small hut, and it was there that I first came to know him, purely by chance. The meeting soon ripened into friendship, for there was much in the recluse to draw both interest and respect. I learned that he was well educated, endowed with uncommon powers of mind, yet plagued by misanthropy and given to strange alternations of ardor and gloom. He kept many books with him, though he seldom made use of them. His chief pleasures were shooting and fishing, or wandering along the beach and among the myrtles in search of shells or entomological specimens\u2014his insect collection might well have been the envy of a Swammerdamm. On these outings he was generally accompanied by an old negro named Jupiter, who had been manumitted before the family\u2019s reverses, but who could not be persuaded\u2014by threats or by promises\u2014to leave what he regarded as his proper right to follow the steps of his young \u201cMassa Will.\u201d It is not unlikely that Legrand\u2019s relatives, judging him to be somewhat unsteady in intellect, had managed to instill this stubbornness in Jupiter, so as to keep watch and provide guardianship over the wanderer.\"}"
