          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1186d4aae3fa3a04b7c5f7b2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You will be glad to hear that no disaster has marked the start of an enterprise you have viewed with such dark forebodings. I arrived here yesterday, and my first task is to reassure my dear sister of my well-being and of my growing confidence in the success of what I\u2019m setting out to do.\\n\\nI\u2019m already far north of London, and as I walk through the streets of Petersburgh, I feel a cold northern breeze touch my cheeks. It steadies my nerves and fills me with delight. Do you understand that feeling? This breeze, coming from the regions I\u2019m moving toward, gives me a preview of those icy climates. Encouraged by this promising wind, my daydreams become more intense and vivid. I try in vain to convince myself that the pole is the place of frost and desolation; it always appears in my imagination as a region of beauty and joy. There, Margaret, the sun is always visible\u2014its broad disk just skimming the horizon and spreading a constant brilliance. There\u2014if you\u2019ll allow me, my sister, I\u2019ll trust in the navigators who came before\u2014snow and frost are gone. And sailing over a calm sea, we may be carried to a land that surpasses every region discovered so far in wonders and beauty on the habitable globe. Its features and products may have no equal, just as the phenomena of the heavenly bodies undoubtedly do in those undiscovered places. What could we not expect in a country of endless light? I may there uncover the remarkable power that draws the needle and be able to regulate a thousand celestial observations that, with only this voyage, would make their apparent irregularities consistent forever. I will satisfy my intense curiosity by seeing a part of the world never before visited, and I may walk on land never before marked by the foot of man. These are my reasons for being drawn in, and they\u2019re enough to overcome every fear of danger or death and to make me begin this difficult voyage with the same joy a child feels when he sets off in a small boat with his holiday friends on an expedition of discovery up his home river.\\n\\nBut even if all these guesses are wrong, you can\u2019t deny the priceless benefit I will bring to all mankind, even to the last generation, by finding a passage near the pole to those countries that, for now, require so many months to reach\u2014or by uncovering the secret of magnetism, which, if it\u2019s possible at all, can only be learned through an undertaking like mine.\\n\\nThese thoughts have driven away the agitation I felt when I started my letter, and I feel my heart warm with an enthusiasm that lifts me toward heaven. Nothing helps to calm the mind so much as a steady purpose\u2014a point on which the soul can fix its intellectual gaze.\"}"
