er, as if he had lost his mind. I think he had.\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d Mr. Brownlow said, as Oliver\u2019s tears fell fast.\\n\\nMonks said nothing.\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d Mr. Brownlow continued, speaking for him, \u201cwas written in the same spirit as the letter. He talked about the misery his wife had brought him. He blamed you, his only son, for your rebellious nature, your vice, your malice, and your early bad passions\u2014because you were trained to hate him. He left you, and your mother, each an annuity of eight hundred pounds. He divided the rest of his property into two equal parts: one for Agnes Fleming, and the other for their child, if it was born alive and ever reached adulthood.\u201d\"}"
