          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"da4ad88b41ab24d461fe0a72\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma was never, by any later discovery, made to withdraw her harsh opinion of Mrs. Elton. What she had observed the first time had been pretty much right. As Mrs. Elton presented herself to her on this second interview, so she appeared whenever they met again\u2014self-important, forward, familiar, ignorant, and ill-bred. She had a little beauty and a little accomplishment, but so scanty a share of judgment that Emma believed her to imagine she was bringing superior knowledge of the world to enliven and improve a country neighbourhood; and she even supposed Miss Hawkins to have held a place in society that Mrs. Elton\u2019s consequence alone could outdo.\\n\\nThere was no reason to think Mr. Elton differed in his thoughts from his wife. He seemed not only content with her, but proud of her. He had the look of congratulating himself on having brought such a woman to Highbury, as if not even Miss Woodhouse could match her; and most of the new acquaintance, disposed to praise or not accustomed to judge, fell in with Miss Bates\u2019s good humour, or took it for granted that the bride must be as clever and as agreeable as she claimed to be. Thus Mrs. Elton\u2019s commendations passed from one person to another as they ought to, without any check from Miss Woodhouse, who continued with ready good grace her first contribution, and talked of her being \u201cvery pleasant and very elegantly dressed.\u201d\\n\\nIn one respect, Mrs. Elton became even worse than she had seemed at first. Her feelings shifted towards Emma.\u2014Offended, most likely, by the slight encouragement her proposals of intimacy received, she drew back in her turn and grew gradually colder and more distant; and though the change was agreeable in its effect, the ill-will that caused it could only be increasing Emma\u2019s dislike. Their manners, too\u2014Mrs. Elton\u2019s and Mr. Elton\u2019s\u2014were disagreeable towards Harriet. They were sneering and careless. Emma hoped it would soon bring Harriet\u2019s cure about; but the very temper that could lead to such conduct lowered them both in her estimation.\u2014There was no doubting that poor Harriet\u2019s attachment had been offered up as a kind of conjugal indiscretion, and that her own part in the affair, given under the least favourable colouring to her and the most soothing to him, had in all likelihood been supplied as well. Harriet, of course, was the object of their joint dislike.\u2014When they had nothing else to say, it was always easy to begin by abusing Miss Woodhouse; and the enmity they dared not show in open disrespect to her found a wider outlet in their contemptuous treatment of Harriet.\\n\\nMrs. Elton took a strong fancy to Jane Fairfax; and from the very beginning. Not only when a state of rivalry with one young lady might be thought to make the other more desirable, but from the first moment itself; and she was not content merely to express a natural and reasonable admiration\u2014but without being asked, without excuse, without any claim or privilege, she must be eager to assist and befriend her.\u2014Before Emma had lost her confidence, and about the third time of their meeting, she heard the whole of Mrs. Elton\u2019s knight-errantry on the subject.\"}"
