          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6c6c5e01eaafa086d124fb81\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Emma read over her letter again, and then wrote her reply, it made her feel softer. Because of that, she had to steady herself with a few firm words. She was very worried about the idea of making him unhappy, and she kept thinking about what his mother and sisters would think and say. She was also afraid they would think she was ungrateful. So Emma believed that if the young man had happened to come by at that moment, he would still have been accepted.\\n\\nBut the letter was written, sealed, and sent. The matter was finished, and Harriet was safe. Harriet was rather low all evening, but Emma could understand her gentle regrets. Sometimes she eased them by talking about her own affection, and sometimes she brought up the idea of Mr. Elton.\\n\\n\u201cI shall never be invited to Abbey-Mill again,\u201d Harriet said, in a rather sad tone.\\n\\n\u201cNor, if you were, could I ever bear to be separated from you, my Harriet. You are far too important at Hartfield to be spared to Abbey-Mill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I\u2019m sure I would never want to go there,\u201d Harriet replied. \u201cI\u2019m never happy anywhere except at Hartfield.\u201d\\n\\nAfter some time, Harriet said, \u201cI think Mrs. Goddard would be very surprised if she knew what happened. I\u2019m sure Miss Nash would be surprised too. Miss Nash thinks her own sister is very well married, and it\u2019s only a linen-draper.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne should feel sorry to see more pride or refinement in a schoolteacher, Harriet,\u201d Emma said. \u201cI dare say Miss Nash would envy you the chance to be married. Even this match would look valuable to her. As for anything better for you, I suppose she has no idea. The attention of a certain person can hardly be part of the gossip in Highbury yet. So far, I think you and I are the only ones whose eyes and manners have made it clear.\u201d\\n\\nHarriet blushed and smiled, and said something about being surprised that people liked her so much. The thought of Mr. Elton was certainly cheering, but after a while she became tender-hearted again toward the man who had been rejected, Mr. Martin.\\n\\n\u201cNow he has got my letter,\u201d Harriet said softly. \u201cI wonder what they are all doing. Do his sisters know? If he is unhappy, they will be unhappy too. I hope it won\u2019t trouble him too much.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet\u2019s think about those among our friends who are away and are being kept busy in a happier way,\u201d Emma said. \u201cAt this moment, perhaps, Mr. Elton is showing your picture to his mother and sisters. He is telling them how much more beautiful the original is, and after being asked for it five or six times, he keeps letting them hear your name\u2014your own dear name.\u201d\"}"
