          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"36cf5a2940a0f9fdde77a772\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The congratulatory letter Elizabeth received from Lydia about her marriage told her that, at least by Lydia\u2019s wife, and perhaps by Lydia herself, such a hope was being held. The letter said this:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lizzy,\\n\\nI wish you joy. If you love Mr. Darcy half as well as I love my dear Wickham, you must be very happy. It is a great comfort to have you so rich; and when you have nothing else to do, I hope you will think of us. I am sure Wickham would like a place at court very much, and I do not think we will have quite enough money to live on without some help. Any place would do, as long as it pays about three or four hundred a year; but, whatever you do, don\u2019t speak to Mr. Darcy about it, if you would rather not.\\n\\nYours, etc.\\n\\nAs it happened, Elizabeth would much rather not, so in her reply she tried to end every request and expectation of that kind. Still, whenever she could, she offered whatever relief she could manage by being careful with her own private spending. She had always known that their income would not be enough, given how extravagant their needs were and how little they thought about the future. Every time they moved to new quarters, either Jane or Elizabeth was sure to be asked for a little help to pay their bills. Even after peace was restored and they could finally settle at home, their life was still unsettled. They were always searching for a cheap place to live, and they were always spending more than they should. His affection for her soon turned into indifference; hers lasted a little longer. Even so, despite her youth and her manners, she kept all the claims to reputation that her marriage had given her. Darcy could never receive him at Pemberley, but for Elizabeth\u2019s sake he still helped him in his profession.\\n\\nLydia sometimes visited Pemberley when her husband was away enjoying himself in London or Bath. With the Bingleys, they stayed there so long that even Bingley\u2019s good humor was worn down, and he went so far as to talk about giving them a hint to leave.\\n\\nMiss Bingley was very upset by Darcy\u2019s marriage. But since she thought it best to keep the right to visit Pemberley, she dropped her resentment. She became even more fond of Georgiana, was almost as attentive to Darcy as before, and paid Elizabeth back for every bit of civility she owed her.\u201d}]}]}\"}"
