yes and manners have made his meaning plain.\u201d\\n\\nHarriet blushed and smiled, and said something about being surprised that people should like her so much. The thought of Mr. Elton was certainly cheering; but after a while she grew tender-hearted again towards the rejected Mr. Martin.\\n\\n\u201cNow he has got my letter,\u201d she said softly. \u201cI wonder what they are all doing\u2014whether his sisters know\u2014if he is unhappy, they will be unhappy too. I hope it will not trouble him so very much.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet us rather think of those among our absent friends who are more cheerfully occupied,\u201d cried Emma. \u201cAt this very moment, perhaps, Mr. Elton is showing your picture to his mother and sisters, telling them how much more beautiful is the original, and, after being asked for it five or six times, consenting at last to let them hear your name\u2014your own dear name.\u201d\"}"
