{"request_id":"f77a027a497bc26485b6a974","rewritten_text":"Many years ago, I became close with a man named Mr. William Legrand. He came from an old Huguenot family and had once been wealthy, but a run of bad luck had left him in need. To spare himself the shame that came with his troubles, he left New Orleans, the city his family came from, and moved to Sullivan’s Island, near Charleston, South Carolina.

This island is strange in its own way. It is made mostly of sea sand and is about three miles long. At no point is it wider than a quarter of a mile. It is separated from the mainland by a small creek that is barely noticeable, slipping through reeds and slime. Marsh hens often gather there. As you would expect, there is not much plant life, and what does grow is small. You do not see any large trees. Near the western end, where Fort Moultrie stands, there are a few miserable frame buildings. In summer, fugitives from Charleston come there to escape the dust and fever. You can also find the bristly palmetto in that area. But everywhere else—except that western point and a strip of hard, white beach along the sea—the island is thick with sweet myrtle. English gardeners value it highly. The shrub can grow to fifteen or twenty feet tall, forming a dense thicket that is almost impossible to push through, and it fills the air with a strong, sweet smell.

Deep inside this thicket, not far from the eastern end of the island, Legrand had built a small hut. That is where I first met him, and I did so by accident. Our meeting soon turned into friendship, because there was something about his life alone that made him interesting and worth respecting. I learned that he was well educated and had unusual mental abilities, but he was also a misanthrope and could swing between intense excitement and deep sadness. He owned many books, but he rarely used them. His main pleasures were hunting and fishing, or walking along the beach and through the myrtles, looking for shells or for insects and other small creatures. His collection of those specimens could have made even Swammerdamm jealous. On these trips, he was usually with an old Black man named Jupiter. Jupiter had been freed before Legrand’s family fell on hard times, but nothing—threats or promises—could make him leave. He believed he had the right to follow his young “Massa Will.” It is also possible that Legrand’s relatives, thinking he was a bit unstable in his mind, had encouraged Jupiter’s stubbornness so that the wanderer would be watched and guided."}

