          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6c430ec288c2757bedecd22b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI swear,\u201d he cried, \u201cby the sun, and by the blue sky of heaven, and by the fire of love that burns in my heart, that if you grant my prayer, while they exist you shall never see me again. Go on to your home and begin your work; I shall watch their progress with unbearable anxiety; and don\u2019t fear that when you are ready I won\u2019t appear.\u201d\\n\\nWith that, he suddenly left me, frightened\u2014perhaps\u2014of any change in my feelings. I watched him descend the mountain faster than an eagle\u2019s flight, and soon he was lost among the rolling waves of the sea of ice.\\n\\nHis story had taken up the whole day, and the sun was already near the horizon when he departed. I knew I should hurry down toward the valley, because darkness would soon surround me; but my heart was heavy, and my steps were slow. The work of winding through the mountain\u2019s narrow paths and planting my feet firmly as I went left me perplexed, because I was still caught up in the emotions the day\u2019s events had stirred. Long after night had fallen, I reached the halfway resting-place and sat down beside the fountain. The stars shone here and there as the clouds passed over them; dark pines rose before me, and now and then a broken tree lay on the ground. It was a scene of profound solemnity, and it stirred strange thoughts in me. I wept bitterly, and with my hands clasped in agony I cried, \u201cOh, stars and clouds and winds\u2014you\u2019re all about to mock me. If you truly pity me, crush feeling and memory; let me become nothing. But if you don\u2019t, go, go, and leave me in darkness.\u201d\\n\\nThese were wild and miserable thoughts, but I can\u2019t tell you how the endless twinkling of the stars weighed on me, and how I listened to every blast of wind as if it were a dull, ugly sirocco on its way to consume me.\\n\\nMorning came before I reached the village of Chamounix. I took no rest, but returned at once to Geneva. Even in my own heart I couldn\u2019t put my feelings into words\u2014they pressed on me with the weight of a mountain, and their sheer force crushed my agony beneath them. So I went back home, entered the house, and presented myself to the family. My haggard, wild appearance caused intense alarm, but I answered no questions and scarcely spoke. I felt as if I were under a curse\u2014as if I had no right to ask for their sympathy\u2014as if I would never again be able to enjoy their company.\u201d\"}"
