{"request_id":"1cac56c7c3a98d56748f9f95","rewritten_text":"When I had stopped crying and reason and memory returned to me, I began to think more calmly about what had happened and about what it meant for me to act. Only a few hours had passed, yet a huge change had taken place in my life. The natural work of years had been completed since that morning: my father was as good as dead to me, and for a moment I felt as if, with his white hair, he were laid in his coffin while I—youth, vanishing as I approached age—was weeping at his timely death. But it was not so. I was still young—oh, far too young—and he was not dead to others; but I, most miserable of all, must never see or speak to him again. I must flee from him with more determination than from my greatest enemy. In solitude or in cities, I must never again behold him. The thought left me breathless with anguish, and once it took hold of my imagination, I couldn’t for a time follow any line of thought. From then on, I decided, I would live in the dreariest seclusion. I would go to the Continent and become a nun—not for the sake of religion, because I was not a Catholic—but so that I could be shut out from the world forever. There I would find solitude where I could weep, and the voices of life would never reach me.

But my father—my beloved and most wretched father—would he die? Would he never overcome the fierce passion that now ruled him without pity? Could he not, many, many years from now, when age had cooled the burning feelings he has now, become a father to me again? This thought smoothed my brow, and I could feel it—yes, I wept to feel it—draw a half-melancholy smile from my lips, showing the expression of suffering. I dared to hope better for my future life. Years would pass, and they would fly by lightly, carried on by hope; and if they passed heavily, still they would pass, and I would not have lost my father forever. Let him spend another sixteen years wandering in desolation. Let him once more shout his wild complaints to the vast woods and the tremendous cataracts of another country. Let him again face fearful danger and soul-crushing hardships. Let the hot sun of the south burn his passion-worn cheeks again, and let the cold night rains fall on him and chill his blood.

To this life, miserable father, I devote you!—Go!—Let your days be spent among savages, and your nights beneath the canopy of heaven! Let your limbs be worn and your heart chilled, and let all youth be dead within you!"}

