          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"39bbc5ece3993ef5460deb36\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Then they passed by lonely brickworks. The fog was thinner here, and he could make out the strange, bottle-shaped kilns, their orange, fanlike tongues of fire. A dog barked as they went by, and far off in the darkness some wandering sea-gull screamed. The horse caught in a rut, stumbled, then veered aside and broke into a gallop.\\n\\nAfter a while they left the clay road and rattled again over rough-paved streets. Most of the windows were dark, but now and then fantastic shadows were cut out against a blind lit by lamplight. He watched them with interest. They moved like monstrous marionettes and made gestures like living things. He hated them. A dull rage sat in his chest. As they turned a corner, a woman yelled something at them from an open doorway, and two men ran after the hansom for about a hundred yards. The driver lashed at them with his whip.\\n\\nThey say passion makes you think in circles. Certainly, with hideous repetition, the bitten lips of Dorian Gray shaped and reshaped those subtle words about soul and sense, until he had found in them the full expression\u2014so it seemed\u2014of his mood, and could justify, with intellectual approval, passions that, without such justification, would still have ruled his temper. From cell to cell of his mind crept the single thought; and the wild desire to live, the most terrible of all human appetites, surged into strength, quickening every trembling nerve and fiber. What ugliness had once disgusted him with because it made things real became dear to him now for that very reason. Ugliness was the only reality. The coarse brawl, the loathsome den, the crude violence of disordered life, the sheer filth of thief and outcast\u2014these were more vivid, more intensely real in the force of their impression, than all the graceful forms of art, than the dreamy shadows of song. They were what he needed for forgetfulness. In three days he would be free.\\n\\nSuddenly the man pulled up with a sharp jerk at the top of a dark lane. Over the low roofs and jagged chimney-stacks of the houses rose the black masts of ships. Wreaths of white mist clung to the yards like ghostly sails.\\n\\n\u201cSomewhere around here, sir, ain\u2019t it?\u201d he asked hoarsely through the trap.\\n\\nDorian started and looked around. \u201cThis will do,\u201d he said, and after getting out quickly and handing the driver the extra fare he had promised, he walked fast toward the quay. Here and there a lantern glimmered at the stern of some huge merchantman. The light trembled and broke apart in the puddles. A red glare came from an outward-bound steamer that was coaling. The slimy pavement looked like a wet mackintosh.\"}"
