          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"afa57a2fb3dd78d2ed753d9a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The day went by much the same as the day before. Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley spent several hours of the morning with the invalid, who continued, though slowly, to improve; and in the evening Elizabeth joined their group in the drawing-room. The loo table, however, was not there. Mr. Darcy was writing, and Miss Bingley, seated near him, watched the progress of his letter, repeatedly trying to draw his attention away by sending messages to her sister. Mr. Hurst and Mr. Bingley were playing piquet, and Mrs. Hurst sat observing their game.\\n\\nElizabeth picked up some needlework and was entertained enough by watching what passed between Darcy and his companion. The lady\u2019s constant praise\u2014of his handwriting, of the evenness of his lines, of the length of his letter\u2014delivered with perfect unconcern at how it was received, made a curious kind of conversation, and matched exactly her own opinion of each.\\n\\n\u201cHow delighted Miss Darcy will be to receive such a letter!\u201d\\n\\nHe did not answer.\\n\\n\u201cYou write remarkably fast.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re mistaken. I write rather slowly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow many letters you must have to write over the course of a year! Business letters, too! How dreadful I should think them!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen it\u2019s fortunate that they fall to my lot instead of yours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPlease tell your sister that I long to see her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve already told her once, at your request.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid you don\u2019t like your pen. Let me fix it for you. I mend pens remarkably well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you\u2014but I always mend my own.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow do you manage to write so evenly?\u201d\\n\\nHe was silent.\\n\\n\u201cTell your sister I\u2019m delighted to hear of her improvement on the harp, and please let her know that I\u2019m quite in raptures with her beautiful little design for a table, and that I think it infinitely better than Miss Grantley\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you let me postpone your raptures until I write again? Right now I don\u2019t have room to do them justice.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, it doesn\u2019t matter. I\u2019ll see her in January. But do you always write such charming long letters to her, Mr. Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey\u2019re generally long; but whether they\u2019re always charming, that isn\u2019t for me to decide.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s a rule with me that a person who can write a long letter with ease can\u2019t write badly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat won\u2019t do as a compliment to Darcy, Caroline,\u201d her brother cried, \u201cbecause he does not write with ease. He studies too much for words of four syllables. Don\u2019t you, Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy way of writing is very different from yours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d Miss Bingley exclaimed, \u201cCharles writes in the most careless way imaginable.\u201d\"}"
