          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"df0f7fde44447d776d646745\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Master Charles Bates, struck with horror at Sikes\u2019s wickedness, fell to thinking whether, after all, an upright life might not be the better choice. Convinced that it was, he turned away from the old scenes, resolved to set himself to some fresh work. He laboured long and endured much for a time; yet, with a steady temper and a sincere intention, he came at last to success. And so, from being a farmer\u2019s drudge and a carrier\u2019s lad, he is now the merriest young grazier in all Northamptonshire.\\n\\nAnd now the hand that writes these lines hesitates as it nears the end of its duty, and would gladly spin the thread of these adventures a little farther.\\n\\nI would like to linger still among some of those with whom I have so long been moving, and to share their happiness by trying to set it forth. I would show Rose Maylie in the full bloom and quiet grace of early womanhood, casting along her secluded path a gentle, kindly light\u2014soft to all who walk with her, and shining into their hearts. I would portray the life and gladness of the fireside circle and the bright summer company; I would follow her through the sultry fields at noon, and catch the low, tender notes of her voice on the moonlit evening walk; I would observe her in all her goodness and charity abroad, and the cheerful, untiring performance of domestic duties at home. I would paint her, and her dead sister\u2019s child, content in their love for one another, spending long hours together in imagining the friends they had so sadly lost; I would bring again before me those happy little faces that gathered round her knee, and hear their merry chatter. I would recall the sound of that clear laugh, and call up the sympathetic tear that shone in the soft blue eye. These, and a thousand looks and smiles, and turns of thought and speech\u2014I would fain have them all back again.\\n\\nAs for how Mr. Brownlow went 