before the death of any member of our family.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Why, the family doctor does the same, Lord Canterville, for that matter. But there\u2019s no such thing as a ghost, sir, and I don\u2019t expect the laws of Nature to be set aside for the British aristocracy.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018You\u2019re certainly very sensible in America,\u2019 answered Lord Canterville, who didn\u2019t quite catch what Mr. Otis meant by his last remark; \u2018and if you don\u2019t mind a ghost in the house, then it\u2019s perfectly fine. Only remember\u2014I did warn you.\u2019\\n\\nA few weeks later, the purchase was finished, and when the season ended the Minister and his family went down to Canterville Chase. Mrs. Otis, who, as Miss Lucretia R. Tappan of West 53rd Street, had once been a celebrated New York belle, was now a striking, middle-aged woman, with bright eyes and a magnificent profile.\u2019\"}"
