          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dea9b0a0738c4dc3d312a7bd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma did not regret her condescension in going to the Coles. The visit gave her many pleasant memories the next day; and whatever she might be thought to have lost by keeping herself at a dignified distance, would be more than repaid by the brilliance of her popularity. She must have delighted the Coles\u2014worthy people, who deserved to be made happy!\u2014and left behind a name that would not soon fade.\\n\\nPerfect happiness, even in memory, is not common; and there were two things about it that made her feel uneasy. She wondered whether she had not crossed the duty of one woman toward another by telling Frank Churchill her suspicions about Jane Fairfax\u2019s feelings. It hardly seemed right; but the idea had been so strong that it slipped her, and his readiness to accept everything she told him was a compliment to her insight\u2014something that made it hard for her to be completely sure she should have kept quiet.\\n\\nThe other cause for regret also concerned Jane Fairfax; and there she had no doubt. She truly, clearly regretted the inferiority of her own playing and singing. She grieved most sincerely over the idleness of her childhood\u2014and then sat down and practiced vigorously for an hour and a half.\\n\\nShe was then interrupted by Harriet coming in; and if Harriet\u2019s praise could have satisfied her, she might soon have been comforted.\\n\\n\u201cOh! if I could only play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t put us in the same category, Harriet. My playing isn\u2019t any more like hers than a lamp is like sunshine.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! dear\u2014I think you play the best of the two. I think you play just as well as she does. I\u2019m sure I\u2019d much rather hear you. Everyone last night said how well you played.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThose who knew anything about it would have noticed the difference. The truth is, Harriet, that my playing is just good enough to be praised, but Jane Fairfax\u2019s is far beyond that.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I\u2019ll always think you play quite as well as she does, or that if there is any difference, nobody would ever be able to tell. Mr. Cole said how much taste you have; and Mr. Frank Churchill talked a great deal about your taste, and he values taste much more than execution.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! but Jane Fairfax has both, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you sure? I saw she had execution, but I didn\u2019t know she had any taste. Nobody talked about it. And I hate Italian singing.\u2014You can\u2019t understand a word of it. Besides, if she plays so very well, you know, it\u2019s no more than she\u2019s required to do, because she\u2019ll have to teach.\u201d}\"}"
