          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4039f591d588dfc11d6dc94d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the morning she was discovered dead in her bed, apparently without having felt any pain. Sir Mathew Reid was sent for immediately, though, naturally, there was nothing to be done; she was to be buried on the 22nd at Beauchamp Chalcote. A few days before her death she had drawn up her will, leaving Lord Arthur her small house in Curzon Street, together with all her furniture, personal belongings, and pictures\u2014save for her set of miniatures, which were to go to her sister, Lady Margaret Rufford, and her amethyst necklace, which Sybil Merton was to receive. The estate itself was not worth much, but Mr. Mansfield, the solicitor, was intensely anxious for Lord Arthur to come back at once, if he could, since there were many bills to settle, and Lady Clementina had never kept any proper accounts.\\n\\nLord Arthur was deeply moved by Lady Clementina\u2019s thoughtful remembrance of him, and he felt that Mr. Podgers had a great deal to answer for. Yet his love for Sybil ruled everything else, and the knowledge that he had done his duty brought him peace and comfort. When he reached Charing Cross, he felt entirely happy.\\n\\nThe Mertons welcomed him with great kindness. Sybil made him promise that nothing would ever come between them again, and the marriage was set for the 7th of June. Life appeared to him bright and beautiful once more, and all his old joy returned.\\n\\nOne day, though, while he was going through the house in Curzon Street with Lady Clementina\u2019s solicitor and Sybil herself\u2014burning bundles of faded letters and emptying drawers of odd scraps\u2014the young girl suddenly gave a little cry of pleasure.\\n\\n\u2018What have you found, Sybil?\u2019 Lord Arthur asked, looking up from what he was doing, and smiling.\\n\\n\u2018This lovely little silver bonbonni\u00e8re, Arthur. Isn\u2019t it quaint and Dutch? Do let me have it! I know amethysts won\u2019t suit me