          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"531f4bd996025cecc9b773b6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"While these matters were going on in the country workhouse, Mr. Fagin sat in the old den\u2014the very one from which Oliver had been taken away by the girl\u2014sitting and brooding over a lifeless, smoky fire. On his knee he had a pair of bellows, which he seemed to be trying to coax into a brighter, more cheerful blaze; yet his mind had gone elsewhere. With his arms folded over them and his chin resting on his thumbs, he stared, in a far-off way, at the rusty bars.\\n\\nBehind him, at a table, were the Artful Dodger, Master Charles Bates, and Mr. Chitling, all absorbed in a game of whist. The Artful played dummy against Master Bates and Mr. Chitling. The face of the first-named gentleman\u2014always sharp and shrewd\u2014took on even more interest as he watched the game closely, and studied Mr. Chitling\u2019s hand; whenever the moment called for it, he fixed earnest looks upon it, and from those observations he managed his own play with careful judgment. Since it was a cold night, the Dodger kept his hat on, as he often did even indoors. He also had a clay pipe clenched between his teeth, taking it out only for a short spell when he thought it time to ask for a fresh drink from the quart pot on the table, which stood ready, filled with gin-and-water for the company.\\n\\nMaster Bates, too, followed the play; but being more hot-blooded than his accomplished companion, it was plain he reached for the gin-and-water more often, and filled the pauses with plenty of jokes and off-topic remarks\u2014utterly out of place for a serious rubber. The Artful, relying on their close friendship, had more than once taken the chance to speak to him gravely about these missteps; yet Master Bates took every remonstrance in the best possible spirit, only asking his friend to be \u201cblowed,\u201d or to put his head in a sack, or answering with some other neatly turned bit of wit of the same sort. The effect of it was such that Mr. Chitling could hardly contain his admiration. It was also notable that Mr. Chitling and his partner always lost; but instead of vexing Master Bates, the fact only seemed to heighten his enjoyment, for he roared with laughter at the end of every deal and swore he had never in all his born days seen such a jolly game.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s two doubles and the rub,\u201d said Mr. Chitling, with a very long face, as he drew half-a-crown from his waistcoat-pocket. \u201cI never see such a feller as you, Jack; you win everything.\"}"
