          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eb9d69828151171d6d622f00\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With nothing left to do to keep him steady, he set the knuckles of his right hand into the hollow of his left, then the knuckles of his left into the hollow of his right; after that he drew a hand over his bearded chin, and went on making the same measured changes, without the least pause. To drag him back from the wandering state into which he always slipped after he had spoken was like trying to rouse some very feeble creature out of a swoon, or to hold the spirit of a man who is dying fast, in the hope that something might yet be disclosed.\\n\\n\u201cDid you ask me for my name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly I did.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that all?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\nWith a tired sound that was neither sigh nor groan, he bent himself to his work again, until the quiet was broken once more.\\n\\n\u201cYou are not a shoemaker by trade?\u201d said Mr. Lorry, staring at him.\\n\\nHis gaunt eyes swung to Defarge, as though he meant to pass the question on to him; but finding no help there, they returned to the man who had asked, and they searched for the ground.\\n\\n\u201cI am not a shoemaker by trade? No, I was not a shoemaker by trade. I-I learned it here. I taught myself. I asked leave to\u2014\u201d\\n\\nHe trailed off, even for minutes, continuing those steady alternations of his hands all the while. At last his eyes drifted back, slowly, to the face they had left; when they settled on it, he started, and went on as a sleeper does when he has just awakened, falling back upon a matter from last night.\\n\\n\u201cI asked leave to teach myself, and I got it after a long while, with great difficulty; and I have been making shoes ever since.\u201d\\n\\nAs he held out his hand for the shoe that had been taken from him, Mr. Lorry said, still fixing his gaze upon him:\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette, do you remember nothing of me?\u201d\\n\\nThe shoe fell to the ground, and he sat staring straight at the questioner.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette\u201d; Mr. Lorry laid his hand upon Defarge\u2019s arm; \u201cdo you remember nothing of this man? Look at him. Look at me. Is there no old banker, no old business, no old servant, no old time coming back into your mind, Monsieur Manette?\u201d\\n\\nWhile the prisoner of many years sat staring, now at Mr. Lorry and now at Defarge, some long obliterated marks of an intelligence that had once been keen and active began, little by little, to force themselves through the black mist that had settled over him. They were clouded again; they grew fainter; they vanished; yet they had been there.\"}"
