          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d7ec601b4bba96b731ef92be\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The great voice\u2014what we might call inorganic, since no pipe or key could be made to account for it\u2014fell silent in its sonorous strength; and the girl, turning to offer help to her elderly companion, at last noticed us.\\n\\nIt was her father. And she, from childhood onward, had been the guide of his dimmed steps. They were Germans from Saxony, and, having emigrated there only a few years before, had formed fresh connections with the villagers round about. Not long after the pestilence began, a young German student joined them. Their plain story was not hard to read: he was a gentleman who loved the fair daughter of the poor musician, and he followed them in their flight from the persecutions of his friends; but soon the mighty leveller came with an unsharpened scythe\u2014mowing down, with the grass, the tall flowers of the field. The youth was taken early. She kept herself alive for her father\u2019s sake. His blindness allowed her to keep up a delusion; at first it was only the child of accident, and now, as solitary beings, the two remained\u2014sole survivors in the land. He did not know that, when he listened to her music, the mute mountains, the senseless lake, and the unconscious trees, save himself, were her only listeners.\\n\\nThe very day we arrived, she had been struck by a sickness that showed itself in symptoms. She was seized with horror at the thought of leaving her aged, sightless father alone upon the empty earth; yet she lacked the courage to tell the truth, and the very excess of her despair drove her to extraordinary exertions. At the usual hour for vespers, she led him to the chapel; and though she trembled and wept for his sake, she played the hymn\u2014written to celebrate the creation of the adorned earth\u2014without a fault in time or a mistake in note, soon to be her own requiem.\\n\\nWe came to her as if from heaven itself. Her strained courage, her firmness barely held together, broke and slipped away at the sight of relief. With a shriek she sprang toward us, threw her arms about Adrian\u2019s knees, and, uttering nothing but, \u201cO save my father!\u201d with sobs and frantic cries, she let loose the long-closed floodgates of her misery.\\n\\nPoor girl!\u2014she and her father now lie side by side beneath the high walnut-tree where her lover rests, and which, in her last moments, she had pointed out to us. Her father, at last realizing her peril, could not see the changes in her dear face; still he stubbornly kept hold of her hand until death chilled it and made it stiff. Even then he did not stir or speak until, twelve hours later, kindly death carried him to his unbroken repose. They lie beneath the sod; the tree is their monument. The sacred place is clear in my memory, though it is pale beside the craggy Jura and the far, immeasurable Alps; the church spire they had frequented still rises above the sheltering trees; and though her hand is cold, I still fancy the sounds of the divine music they loved wandering about, comforting their gentle ghosts.\"}"
