          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ac8d4577fc3b70aec227bf43\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When we got back to the raft, he counted what he had collected and found it came to eighty-seven dollars and seventy-five cents. He had also taken a three-gallon jug of whisky, which he found under a wagon when he was heading home through the woods. The king said it was worth more than any day he\u2019d ever spent in the missionary work. He said it wasn\u2019t worth talking, because heathens didn\u2019t amount to much compared to pirates, who could work a camp meeting.\\n\\nThe duke thought he\u2019d been doing pretty well until the king showed up, and then he didn\u2019t feel that way anymore. He had set up and printed two small jobs for farmers in the printing office\u2014horse bills\u2014and he took four dollars for them. He also got ten dollars\u2019 worth of advertisements for the paper. He said he would put them in for four dollars if they paid in advance, and they agreed. The paper cost two dollars a year, but he brought in three subscriptions at half a dollar each, as long as they paid him up front. They were going to pay him in cordwood and onions as usual, but he said he had just bought the business and cut the price as low as he could afford, and he was going to run it for cash. He also set up a little piece of poetry he wrote himself, out of his own head\u2014three verses, kind of sweet and sad. It was called \u201cYes, crush, cold world, this breaking heart.\u201d He left it all set and ready to print in the paper and didn\u2019t charge anything for it. In the end, he took in nine dollars and a half, and he said he\u2019d done a pretty fair day\u2019s work.\\n\\nThen he showed us another small job he\u2019d printed and hadn\u2019t charged for, because it was for us. It had a picture of a runaway man with a bundle on a stick over his shoulder, and \u201c$200 reward\u201d under it. The text was all about Jim and described him exactly. It said he ran away from St. Jacques\u2019 plantation, forty miles below New Orleans, last winter, and was likely headed north. It said that whoever caught him and sent him back would get the reward and the expenses.\\n\\n\u201cNow,\u201d says the duke, \u201cafter tonight we can run during the day if we want to. Whenever we see someone coming, we can tie Jim hand and foot with a rope, put him in the wigwam, and show this handbill. We\u2019ll say we captured him up the river, and we were too poor to travel on a steamboat, so we got this little raft on credit from our friends and are going down to get the reward.\u201d\"}"
