{"request_id":"14d4bfab111702a6b6a4d386","rewritten_text":"And Sir Arnold and Sir Gauter, knights of the castle, met Sir Brandiles and Sir Kay; and these four knights encountered fiercely, and broke their spears in their hands. Then Sir Pertolope came from the castle, and there he met Sir Lionel; and there Sir Pertolope, the green knight, smote down Sir Lionel, brother to Sir Launcelot. All this was watched and set down by noble heralds, who bore witness of it and named them. Then Sir Bleobaris brake his spear upon Sir Gareth, but at that stroke Sir Bleobaris fell to the earth. When Sir Galihodin saw it, he bade Sir Gareth keep him, and Sir Gareth smote him down to the earth. Then Sir Galihud took up a spear to avenge his brother, and in like manner Sir Gareth served him; and Sir Dinadan and his brother La Cote Male Taile, and Sir Sagramore le Disirous, and Sir Dodinas le Savage—these all did he bear down with one spear.

When King Aswisance of Ireland saw how Sir Gareth fared, he marvelled what manner of man he might be; for at one moment he seemed green, and at his coming again he seemed blue. And so, at every course he rode to and fro, he changed his color, so that neither king nor knight could have ready knowledge of him. Then Sir Agwisance, King of Ireland, encountered with Sir Gareth; and there Sir Gareth smote him from his horse, saddle and all. And then came King Carados of Scotland, and Sir Gareth smote him down, horse and man. In the same fashion he served King Uriens of the land of Gore. And then there came in Sir Bagdemagus, and Sir Gareth smote him down, horse and man, to the earth. And Bagdemagus’s son Meliganus brake a spear upon Sir Gareth mightily and knightly.

Then Sir Galahault the noble prince cried aloud, “Knight of the many colors, well hast thou justed; now make thee ready, that I may just with thee.” Sir Gareth heard him, and he took up a great spear; so they encountered together, and there the prince brake his spear, but Sir Gareth smote him on the left side of the helm, so that he reeled this way and that, and would have fallen had not his men recovered him. “Truly,” said King Arthur, “that knight with the many colors is a good knight.” Wherefore the king called unto him Sir Launcelot and prayed him to encounter with that knight. “Sir,” said Launcelot, “I may as well find it in my heart to forbear him at this time; for he hath had travail enough this day. And when a good knight doth so well upon some day, it is no part of a good knight to let him of his worship—especially when he seeth a knight hath done so great labour. For peradventure, said Sir Launcelot, his quarrel is here this day, and peradventure he is best beloved with this lady of all that be here; for I see well he pains himself and enforceth him to do great deeds. Therefore, said Sir Launcelot, as for me, this day he shall have the honour; though it lay in my power to put him from it, I would not.”"}

