          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cc84bdec23079446feebabb1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter Elliot, of Kellynch Hall in Somersetshire, was a man who, for his own entertainment, never picked up any book except the Baronetage. There he could pass an idle hour, and find comfort when things at home went badly. Looking at the early patents, he felt his mind stirred with admiration and respect, because they showed only a small, surviving beginning. As he turned through the almost endless records of the last century, any unpleasant feelings that came from domestic matters would naturally turn into pity and contempt. And even if every other page failed him, he could still read his own family history with an interest that never stopped. This was the page where his favorite volume always opened:\\n\\n\u201cELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL.\\n\\n\u201cWalter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married, July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq. of South Park, in the county of Gloucester, by which lady (who died 1800) he has issue Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a still-born son, November 5, 1789; Mary, born November 20, 1791.\u201d\\n\\nThe paragraph had originally been printed exactly like that, but Sir Walter had improved it. For the information of himself and his family, he added, after the date of Mary\u2019s birth, the words: \u201cMarried, December 16, 1810, Charles, son and heir of Charles Musgrove, Esq. of Uppercross, in the county of Somerset,\u201d and he also inserted the exact day of the month when he had lost his wife.\\n\\nAfter that came the usual account of the family\u2019s history and rise\u2014how it was first established in Cheshire; how it was mentioned in Dugdale; how they served as high sheriff; how they represented a borough in three straight parliaments; how they showed loyalty and dignity as baronets in the first year of Charles II; and how, through all those generations, they married Marys and Elizabeths. Altogether it filled two neat duodecimo pages, and ended with the arms and motto: \u201cPrincipal seat, Kellynch Hall, in the county of Somerset,\u201d followed by Sir Walter\u2019s handwriting again at the end:\\n\\n\u201cHeir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great grandson of the second Sir Walter.\u201d\\n\\nVanity was the start and the end of Sir Walter Elliot\u2019s character\u2014vanity about his looks and his position. In his youth he had been remarkably handsome, and at fifty-four he was still a very fine-looking man. Few women could think more highly of their appearance than he did, and no valet of a newly made lord could be more pleased with the place he held in society. He believed that the blessing of beauty was only less important than the blessing of a baronetcy. Sir Walter Elliot, who had both, was the constant object of his own warm respect and devotion.\\n\\nHis good looks and his rank gave him another reason to be attached to himself, because they must have earned him a wife of a character far superior to anything he could claim on his own.\"}"
