          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2f141a720ae265821023436a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGood heavens!\u201d the Duchess said to herself. \u201cSo he really is a sort of cheiropodist after all. How dreadful. I do hope he\u2019s a foreigner, at any rate. Then it wouldn\u2019t be quite so bad.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI must certainly introduce him to you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIntroduce him!\u201d the Duchess exclaimed. \u201cYou don\u2019t mean to tell me he\u2019s here?\u201d And she immediately began to hunt for a small tortoise-shell fan and a very shabby lace shawl, as if she might have to be off at once.\\n\\n\u201cOf course he\u2019s here; I wouldn\u2019t dream of giving a party without him. He tells me I have a pure psychic hand, and that if my thumb had been even a little shorter, I should have been a confirmed pessimist and gone into a convent.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I see!\u201d said the Duchess, feeling considerably relieved. \u201cHe tells fortunes, then?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd misfortunes as well,\u201d Lady Windermere replied. \u201cAny number of them. Next year, for instance, I\u2019m in great danger, both by land and sea; so I\u2019m going to live in a balloon, and have my dinner drawn up in a basket every evening. It\u2019s all written down on my little finger\u2014or on the palm of my hand, I forget which.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut surely that\u2019s tempting Providence, Gladys.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Duchess, surely Providence can resist temptation by now. I think everyone ought to have their hands read once a month, just to know what not to do. Of course one does it all the same, but it\u2019s so pleasant to be warned. Now, if someone doesn\u2019t go and fetch Mr. Podgers at once, I shall have to go myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet me go, Lady Windermere,\u201d said a tall, handsome young man who was standing by, listening with an amused smile.\\n\\n\u201cThank you so much, Lord Arthur; but I\u2019m afraid you wouldn\u2019t recognise him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf he\u2019s as wonderful as you say, Lady Windermere, I couldn\u2019t very well miss him. Tell me what he\u2019s like, and I\u2019ll bring him to you at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, he isn\u2019t at all like a cheiromantist. I mean he isn\u2019t mysterious, or esoteric, or romantic-looking. He\u2019s a little, stout man with a funny bald head and big gold-rimmed spectacles\u2014something between a family doctor and a country attorney. I\u2019m truly sorry, but it isn\u2019t my fault. People are so provoking. All my pianists look exactly like poets, and all my poets look exactly like pianists; and last season I asked a most dreadful conspirator to dinner\u2014a man who had blown up so many people, and who always wore a coat of mail and carried a dagger up his shirt-sleeve. And when he arrived, do you know he looked just like a nice old clergyman, and cracked jokes all evening?\u201d\"}"
