          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d7c1be6ee14d9391bd386e68\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I saw several funerals. They were attended by only a few mourners, and the people watching treated them like bad signs of the worst kind. Some stared at the processions with eager, wild curiosity. Others ran away in fear. Some cried out loud.\\n\\nAfter he had helped the sick right away, Adrian\u2019s main goal was to hide the plague\u2019s symptoms and how it was spreading from the people of London. He understood that fear and gloomy thoughts could help the disease. When people gave in to despair and kept worrying, the body became more likely to catch infection. So there were no disturbing sights to be seen. Most shops stayed open, and the streets still had some traffic. Even so, London looked changed to me, since I had not seen it since the outbreak began. There were no carriages. Grass had grown high in the streets. The houses looked empty and worn down. Most shutters were closed. And the people I passed had a pale, frightened stare that felt nothing like the usual, practical way Londoners carried themselves. My own carriage drew attention as it rattled toward the Protectoral Palace, and the fashionable streets leading to it looked even more empty and bleak. \\n\\nAdrian\u2019s waiting room was crowded. It was his time to see visitors. I did not want to interrupt his work, so I stayed and watched people come in and go out. They were mostly from the middle and lower classes, whose money ran out when trade stopped. There was also the restless drive to make money in every form, which was so common in our country. The newcomers looked anxious, sometimes even terrified, which stood in sharp contrast to the calm, resigned, and even pleased faces of those who had already been heard. I could tell how my friend was affecting them by the way they moved faster and looked happier. At two o\u2019clock the door closed, and no one else was admitted. Those who were turned away left in a sour or 