          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c815836182ff2618150dd7fa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond paced the room in angry strides. I stood prepared to take in his reproaches and answer them. Adrian rested against the wall. \u201cThis is utterly ridiculous,\u201d he burst out; \u201cif you were school-boys, you could not behave more irrationally.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou do not understand,\u201d said Raymond. \u201cThis is only one part of a system\u2014a contrivance of tyranny to which I will never submit. Because I am Protector of England, must I be the only slave in its empire? My privacy invaded, my actions watched and censured, my friends insulted? But I\u2019ll be rid of the whole at once.\u2014Be you witnesses,\u201d and he drew the star, the insignia of his office, from his breast and flung it upon the table. \u201cI renounce my office, I abdicate my power\u2014let whoever will assume it!\u201d\u2014-\\n\\n\u201cLet him assume it,\u201d Adrian cried, \u201cwho can claim it for himself, or whom the world will declare to be your superior. There does not exist in England the man with sufficient presumption. Know yourself, Raymond, and your indignation will quiet; your former self-satisfaction will return. A few months ago, whenever we prayed for the prosperity of our country, or our own, we prayed at the same time for the life and welfare of the Protector, as inseparably bound up with it. Your hours were spent for our good; your ambition aimed at earning our approval. You adorned our towns with buildings, you gave us useful establishments, you made the soil yield in abundant fertility. The powerful and unjust shrank from the steps of your judgment-seat, and the poor and oppressed rose like flowers awakened by morning under the sunshine of your protection.\\n\\n\u201cCan you wonder, then, that we are all stricken with grief and dismay when this seems altered? But, come\u2014this bitter mood is already over. Take up your functions again; your partizans will greet you; your enemies will be silenced; our love, honour, and duty will be shown to you once more. Master yourself, Raymond, and the world will be subject to you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll this would be very good sense, if it were spoken to someone else,\u201d Raymond answered, darkly. \u201cTake the lesson to yourself, and you, the first peer of the land, may become its sovereign. You, the good, the wise, the just, may rule all hearts. But I see\u2014too soon for my own happiness, too late for England\u2019s good\u2014that I have undertaken a task I cannot carry. I cannot govern myself. My passions are my masters; my smallest impulse is my tyrant. Do you think that I renounced the Protectorate\u2014(and I have renounced it)\u2014in a fit of spleen? By the God who lives, I swear I will never take up that bauble again; never again to burden myself with the weight of care and misery, of which that is the visible sign.\u201d\"}"
