          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2a25c2b3cd0986f66f3fd54e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don\u2019t want to get mixed up in any business like that, I tell myself.\\n\\nWhen I went downstairs in the morning, the parlor was shut up, and the watchers were gone. There wasn\u2019t anybody around except the family, Widow Bartley, and our people. I watched their faces to see if anything had been going on, but I couldn\u2019t tell.\\n\\nToward the middle of the day, the undertaker came with his man. They set the coffin in the middle of the room on a couple of chairs, then arranged all our chairs in rows. They borrowed more from the neighbors until the hall, the parlor, and the dining room were full. I could see the coffin lid was the same way it had been before, but I didn\u2019t dare go look underneath it with people standing around.\\n\\nThen the people started to come in. The beats and the girls took seats in the front row at the head of the coffin. For half an hour, people filed around slowly in a single line, looking down at the dead man\u2019s face for a minute. Some people shed a tear, and everything was very still and solemn. Only the girls and the beats held their handkerchiefs to their eyes, kept their heads bent, and sobbed a little. There wasn\u2019t any other sound except the scraping of feet on the floor and people blowing their noses\u2014because at a funeral they blow their noses more than they do anywhere else, except church.\\n\\nWhen the place was packed, the undertaker slid around in his black gloves with his soft, soothing ways, putting on the last touches and getting everything shipshape and comfortable, making no more noise than a cat. He never spoke. He moved people around, squeezed in the late arrivals, opened up passageways, and did it all with nods and signs with his hands. Then he took his place against the wall. He was the softest, glidingest, stealthiest man I ever saw, and there wasn\u2019t any more smile on him than there is on a ham.\\n\\nThey had borrowed a melodeum\u2014a sick one. When everything was ready, a young woman sat down and played it. It was pretty screechy and colicky, and everybody joined in and sang, and Peter was the only one that had a good thing, in my opinion. Then Reverend Hobson opened up, slow and solemn, and started talking. Right away, the most outrageous row broke out in the cellar\u2014something like a body ever heard. It was only one dog, but he made a powerful racket, and he kept it up the whole time. The parson had to stand there over the coffin and wait. You couldn\u2019t hear yourself think.\"}"
