          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f545e260c3b25fc7a69e2cc1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Tom tried to use it to scarify the cupboard, and he was getting ready to start on the bureau when he was called away to get dressed for Sunday-school.\\n\\nMary handed him a tin basin of water and a bar of soap. He went outside the door, set the basin on a small bench, dipped the soap into the water, and laid it down. Then he rolled up his sleeves, poured the water onto the ground carefully, and went back into the kitchen to wash his face. He scrubbed at it hard, using the towel that hung behind the door. But Mary took the towel away and said,\\n\\n\u201cNow, ain\u2019t you ashamed, Tom. You mustn\u2019t be so bad. Water won\u2019t hurt you.\u201d\\n\\nTom felt a little embarrassed. The basin was filled again, and this time he stood over it for a moment, gathering himself. He took a big breath and started. When he went back into the kitchen, both eyes shut and reaching for the towel with his hands, suds and water were dripping from his face. When he came out again, he still wasn\u2019t clean enough. The soap had only reached up to his chin and jaws, like a mask; below that line, his neck and the area around it were still dark and dirty, with no water on them. Mary took him in hand, and when she was finished he looked like a man and a brother, with no difference in color. His hair was soaked but brushed neatly, and the short curls were shaped into a neat, even style. [He quietly fixed the curls himself, with effort and difficulty, and pressed his hair close to his head. He thought curls looked effeminate, and his own hair always made him bitter.] Then Mary brought out a suit of his clothes that had only been worn on Sundays for two years. They were simply called his \u201cother clothes,\u201d and that\u2019s how we know what his wardrobe looked like.\\n\\nAfter Tom dressed himself, Mary \u201cput him to rights.\u201d She fastened his roundabout up to his chin, turned his large shirt collar down over his shoulders, brushed him off, and set his speckled straw hat on his head. Now he looked much better, but also very uncomfortable. He was as uncomfortable as he looked, because he hated the feeling of being forced into clean clothes and keeping himself neat. He hoped Mary would forget his shoes, but that hope didn\u2019t last. She rubbed them thoroughly with tallow, as was the custom, and brought them out. He lost his temper and said he was always being made to do things he didn\u2019t want to do.\"}"
