{"request_id":"65f7ec5fda0d287addfa2223","rewritten_text":"At last we managed to lighten the box by taking out about two thirds of what it held, and only then—after some difficulty—we were able to lift it clear of the hole. The things we removed were laid among the brambles, and the dog was left to keep watch over them, with strict instructions from Jupiter that, under no circumstances, he was to move from the spot or open his mouth until our return. We then made for home as fast as we could with the chest, and though we reached the hut safely, it was not until one o’clock in the morning. Even so, worn out as we were, we could not bring ourselves to do more right away. We lay down until two, took supper, and set off for the hills immediately after, carrying three sturdy sacks that—by good fortune—were already there on the premises. A little before four we reached the pit, divided what remained of the haul as fairly as we could among ourselves, and, leaving the holes unfilled, went back once more to the hut, where we deposited our golden burdens a second time, just as the first faint light of dawn began to show above the treetops in the East.

We were thoroughly spent now, yet the sharp excitement of the moment would not let us rest. After an uneasy sleep of three or four hours, we got up, as though we had agreed beforehand, to examine our treasure.

The chest had been filled to the brim, and we spent the whole day, and most of the next night, going through its contents. There was nothing like order or arrangement; everything had been piled together without any method. Once we had sorted it all carefully, we found ourselves possessed of even greater wealth than we had first imagined. In coin there was a little more than four hundred and fifty thousand dollars, reckoning the worth of the pieces as accurately as we could by the tables of the time. There was not a scrap of silver. All of it was antique gold of many kinds—French, Spanish, and German money, along with a few English guineas, and some counters of which we had never seen examples before. Several coins were very large and heavy, yet so worn that we could not make out their inscriptions. There was no American money at all. We had more trouble estimating the value of the jewels. We found diamonds—some of them extremely large and fine—one hundred and ten in all, and not one small; eighteen rubies of remarkable brilliance; three hundred and ten emeralds, all very beautiful; and twenty-one sapphires, with an opal. These stones had all been broken out of their settings and thrown loose into the chest. The settings themselves, which we picked out from among the other gold, seemed to have been beaten up with hammers, as if to prevent identification."}

