any kinds\u2014French, Spanish, and German money, along with a few English guineas, and some counters of which we had never seen examples before. Several coins were very large and heavy, yet so worn that we could not make out their inscriptions. There was no American money at all. We had more trouble estimating the value of the jewels. We found diamonds\u2014some of them extremely large and fine\u2014one hundred and ten in all, and not one small; eighteen rubies of remarkable brilliance; three hundred and ten emeralds, all very beautiful; and twenty-one sapphires, with an opal. These stones had all been broken out of their settings and thrown loose into the chest. The settings themselves, which we picked out from among the other gold, seemed to have been beaten up with hammers, as if to prevent identification.\"}"
        }
