{"request_id":"05dd6c4d71dbbe1453382199","rewritten_text":"With that finished, several more of the loops were loosened from the rim, a further section of the cloth was brought in, and then the freed loops were joined to their proper buttons. By this means the entire upper portion of the bag could be slipped between the net-work and the hoop. It is plain that the hoop would now fall down inside the car, while the whole burden of the car itself—together with everything in it—would be sustained only by the buttons. At first glance this might look like a thin reliance; yet it was by no means so, since the buttons were not only very strong in themselves, but also set so close together that each one carried only a small share of the total weight. Indeed, had the car and its contents been three times as heavy as they were, I should not have felt the least uneasiness. I raised the hoop again within the gum-elastic covering, and propped it to nearly its former height by means of three light poles prepared for the purpose. This, of course, was done to keep the bag stretched at the top, and to hold the lower part of the net-work in its proper position. Nothing remained now but to secure the mouth of the enclosure; and this was easily done by drawing the folds of the material together and twisting them up tightly on the inside with a sort of fixed tourniquet.

“In the sides of the covering, adjusted round the car in this way, three circular panes of thick but clear glass had been inserted, through which I could see without trouble in every horizontal direction. In the cloth that made the bottom there was also a fourth window, of the same sort, answering to a small opening in the floor of the car itself. This let me look straight down; but, having found it impossible to contrive anything similar overhead—owing to the particular way the upper opening was closed and the wrinkles that would follow in the cloth—I could not expect to see any objects situated directly in my zenith. This, naturally, mattered little; for even if I had managed to place a window at top, the balloon would have prevented me from making any use of it.

“About a foot below one of the side windows there was a circular opening, eight inches across, fitted with a brass rim, whose inner edge was made to match the windings of a screw. Into this rim was screwed the large tube of the condenser; the body of the machine, of course, lay within the chamber of gum-elastic.”"}

