          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"614c62a21bde086f8cc875f0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The young people were all eager to see Lyme. Captain Wentworth even talked about going again himself. It was only seventeen miles from Uppercross, and although it was November, the weather was not bad at all. Louisa, who was the most eager of the eager, had decided to go. She wanted to do what she liked, and she also felt proud of being able to stick to her own plan. Because of this, she pushed aside all her father and mother\u2019s wishes to wait until summer. So they were going to Lyme\u2014Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth.\\n\\nAt first, the plan had been to leave in the morning and come back the same night. But Mr Musgrove would not agree, mainly for the sake of his horses. When they thought it through more sensibly, it was clear that a day in the middle of November would not give them much time to see a new place. After allowing seven hours for traveling there and back, there would be little left for anything else. So they would stay the night, and they would not be expected back until the next day\u2019s dinner. Everyone felt this was a real improvement.\\n\\nThey met at the Great House for breakfast a little earlier than usual, and they left very punctually. Still, it was well past noon before the two carriages came down the long hill into Lyme. Mr Musgrove\u2019s coach carried the four ladies, and Charles\u2019s curricle carried Captain Wentworth. Once they reached the town, they had to go down even farther, into the steeper street itself. It was clear that they would have only a short time to look around before the light and warmth of the day were gone.\\n\\nAfter they had found places to stay and ordered dinner at one of the inns, the next thing to do was to walk straight down to the sea. They had arrived too late in the year for any of the usual fun or variety that Lyme, as a public place, might offer. The rooms were shut up, most of the lodgers had already left, and there were hardly any families there except the residents. Since there is nothing especially impressive about the buildings themselves, what a visitor would notice most is the town\u2019s remarkable setting: the main street seems to hurry right toward the water. The walk to the Cobb goes along a pleasant little bay, which in season is lively with bathing machines and people. The Cobb itself has its old attractions and its newer improvements, and there is also a beautiful line of cliffs stretching to the east of the town. Any stranger who does not see the charms of the area around Lyme would have to be very strange indeed, because it would surely make them want to know the place better.\"}"
