          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ba209d70eaca74a12ce42d51\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Well, three or four months went by, and winter was well set in by then. I\u2019d been at school nearly all the time, and I could spell and read and write a little, and I could say the multiplication table up to six times seven is thirty-five; beyond that, I don\u2019t reckon I\u2019d ever get, even if I lived forever. I don\u2019t put much faith in mathematics, anyway.\\n\\nAt first I hated school, but after a while I got to where I could bear it. Whenever I got unusually tired, I\u2019d play hookey, and the punishment I got the next day did me good and put me in a better humor. The longer I kept going, the easier it became. I was getting used to the widow\u2019s ways, too, and they weren\u2019t so sharp on me. Living in a house and sleeping in a bed held me pretty tight most of the time, but before the cold weather I\u2019d slip out and sleep in the woods sometimes, and that was a kind of rest. I liked the old ways best, but I was starting to like the new ones some, too. The widow said I was coming along slow but sure, and doing very satisfactory. She said she wasn\u2019t ashamed of me.\\n\\nOne morning, at breakfast, I turned over the salt-cellar. I reached for it as quick as I could, to throw some of the salt over my left shoulder and ward off the bad luck, but Miss Watson was already there and beat me to it. She says, \u201cTake your hands away, Huckleberry; you\u2019re always making a mess!\u201d The widow spoke up for me, but that wasn\u2019t going to stop the bad luck, I knew that well enough. I went out after breakfast feeling worried and shaky, wondering where it was going to land on me and what it was going to be. There are ways to keep off certain kinds of bad luck, but this wasn\u2019t one of those, so I didn\u2019t try to do anything\u2014just drifted along low-spirited, and on the lookout.\\n\\nI went down to the front garden and climbed over the stile where you pass through the high board fence. There was an inch of fresh snow on the ground, and I saw somebody\u2019s tracks. They\u2019d come up from the quarry and lingered around the stile a while, and then went on around the garden fence. It struck me as odd they hadn\u2019t gone in after standing there so long. I couldn\u2019t make it make sense. It was curious, somehow. I was going to follow them, but I bent down first to look closely at the tracks.\"}"
