          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0a4e21668412e0954d5feee8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma was not later forced, by any new discovery, to take back her harsh opinion of Mrs. Elton. Her judgment had been pretty accurate. As Mrs. Elton seemed to her in this second interview, so she seemed whenever they met again\u2014self-important, presumptuous, familiar, ignorant, and badly bred. She had a little beauty and a little accomplishment, but so little judgment that Emma believed she thought herself bringing superior knowledge of the world to enliven and improve a country neighborhood; and she concluded that Miss Hawkins must have held a place in society that Mrs. Elton\u2019s importance could only surpass.\\n\\nThere was no reason to suppose Mr. Elton thought any differently from his wife. He did not seem merely happy with her, but proud. He had the air of congratulating himself on having brought such a woman to Highbury\u2014so much so that even Miss Woodhouse could not equal her; and most of her new acquaintances, either inclined to praise or not in the habit of judging, followed Miss Bates\u2019s good will, or simply assumed that the bride must be as clever and as agreeable as she claimed. As a result, everyone was very well satisfied; so that Mrs. Elton\u2019s praise passed from one mouth to another as it should, unimpeded by Miss Woodhouse, who readily continued her first contribution and talked, with good grace, about how \u201cvery pleasant and very elegantly dressed\u201d Mrs. Elton was.\\n\\nIn one respect, Mrs. Elton grew even worse than she had seemed at first. Her feelings changed toward Emma. Offended, probably, by the little encouragement her proposals of intimacy received, she pulled back in her turn and gradually became much more cold and distant; and though the effect was agreeable, the ill will that caused it necessarily increased Emma\u2019s dislike. Her manners, too\u2014and Mr. Elton\u2019s\u2014were unpleasant toward Harriet. They were sneering and careless. Emma hoped it would quickly work Harriet\u2019s cure; but the feelings that could lead to such behavior weighed on them both very heavily. It was not to be doubted that poor Harriet\u2019s attachment had been an offering to conjugal openness, and that her own part in the story, presented in the least favorable light to her and the most soothing to him, had likely been given as well. She was, of course, the object of their shared dislike. When they had nothing else to say, it would always be easy to start by criticizing Miss Woodhouse; and the enmity they could not show in open disrespect to her found a wider outlet in their contemptuous treatment of Harriet.\\n\\nMrs. Elton took a strong fancy to Jane Fairfax\u2014and from the very beginning. Not only when a state of war with one young lady might be expected to make the other seem more desirable, but from the first. And she was not content merely to express a natural and reasonable admiration\u2014without being asked, without pleading, without any claim of privilege, she had to offer help and friendship. Before Emma had lost her confidence, and about the third time of their meeting, she heard all of Mrs. Elton\u2019s knight-errantry on the subject.\"}"
