          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"da4ab8be2da733a40c66cf6c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If I can\u2019t manage on that, I won\u2019t ask anyone to give me a lift. Is it satisfactory?\u201d\\n\\nThere came a tremendous roar of applause, and from the middle of it the king\u2019s voice rose, saying:\\n\\n\u201cLoose him from his bonds, and set him free! and let high and low, rich and poor, do him homage, for he has become the king\u2019s right hand\u2014clothed with power and authority, and seated on the highest step of the throne! Now sweep away this creeping night, and bring back light and cheer, so that all the world may bless thee.\u201d\\n\\nBut I answered:\\n\\n\u201cThat a common man should be shamed before the world is nothing; yet it would be dishonor to the king, if anyone who saw his minister naked were not also to see him delivered from his shame. If I might request that my clothes be brought back\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey are not fit,\u201d the king cut in. \u201cGo fetch raiment of another sort; clothe him like a prince!\u201d\\n\\nMy plan worked. I meant to keep things as they were until the eclipse was fully over; otherwise they\u2019d keep trying to get me to dismiss the darkness, and of course I couldn\u2019t. Getting the clothes sent for bought me some time, but not enough. So I had to invent another excuse. I said it would be only natural for the king to change his mind and repent, at least in part, for what he\u2019d done in the heat of the moment; therefore I would allow the darkness to linger a while, and if, by the end of a reasonable stretch, the king still held to the same mind, then the darkness should be dismissed. Neither the king nor anyone else liked that arrangement, but I had to stand by it.\\n\\nIt kept growing darker and darker, blacker and blacker, while I wrestled with those awkward sixth-century clothes. At last it became pitch dark, and the crowd groaned with horror as they felt the cold, uncanny night breezes sweep through the place and saw the stars come out and twinkle overhead. At last the eclipse was total, and I was glad of it, though everyone else was miserable\u2014which was only to be expected. I said:\\n\\n\u201cThe king, by his silence, still holds to the terms.\u201d Then I raised my hands\u2014kept them just so for a moment\u2014and then, with the most dreadful solemnity, I declared: \u201cLet the enchantment dissolve and pass harmless away!\u201d\\n\\nFor a moment there was no answer, in that deep darkness and graveyard hush. But when the silver rim of the sun pushed itself out, a moment or two later, the whole gathering burst loose with a vast shout and came pouring down like a flood to smother me with blessings and gratitude; and Clarence was not the last of the rush, not by any means.\u201d\"}"
