t a partner.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy, with grave propriety, requested the honour of her hand, but in vain. Elizabeth remained firm; nor did Sir William, by any attempt at persuasion, succeed in shaking her resolve.\\n\\n\u201cYou dance so remarkably well, Miss Eliza,\u201d said he, \u201cthat it is cruel to deny me the happiness of seeing you; and though this gentleman dislikes the amusement in general, he can have no objection, I am sure, to oblige us for half an hour.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Darcy is all politeness,\u201d said Elizabeth, smiling.\\n\\n\u201cHe is, indeed; but, my dear Miss Eliza, with such an inducement, we cannot be surprised at his readiness; for who could refuse a partner like this?\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth glanced with an air, and turned away. Her refusal had not displeased the gentleman; he was even thinking of her with some satisfaction, when Miss Bingley accosted him,\u2014\"}"
