          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d90605daadc236409ba1e7bd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The chain of events that always brings misery must have been broken, and the cruel fate that rules it would not allow this break in her eternal laws. But why should I complain? Misery was my nature, and only something miserable could draw me near. If Woodville had been happy, I would never have known him. And can I, who for many years have been fed by tears and kept alive by the dew of grief, stop now and tell a story of sorrow and death?\\n\\nWoodville had to travel into the country, and he was kept away from his lovely bride day after day, in a way that felt unbearable. He received a letter from her saying she was only slightly ill, but urging him to come quickly. She told him that her health would return from the look in his eyes, and that his company would be the best medicine. He was delayed three more days, and then he hurried to her. His heart, for reasons he could not explain, warned him of trouble. He had not heard from her again. He feared she might be worse, and that fear made him restless and impatient, because he could not wait to see her again\u2014healthy and beautiful, as she had been. Still, a dark voice seemed to whisper to him again and again, \u201cYou will never see her as she was.\u201d\\n\\nWhen he reached her home, everything was silent. He moved through several rooms. In one, he found a servant crying bitterly. He was so frightened he could hardly ask, \u201cIs she dead?\u201d Then he only heard the terrible answer, \u201cNot yet.\u201d Those words hit him as less frightening than what he had expected. Knowing she was still alive, and that he might still hope, eased him a little. He remembered what her letter had said. He let himself believe wildly that his kisses, full of warm love and life, could bring her back. He told himself that she could not die while he was near her, and that his presence was like a charm that kept her alive.\\n\\nHe rushed to her sick room. She lay there with her cheeks burning from fever, but her eyes were closed, and she seemed unaware. He held her in his arms. He pressed breathless kisses on her burning lips. In a voice filled with restrained anguish, he called her by the gentlest names. \u201cCome back, Elinor. I am here with you. Your life, your love. Come back. My dearest one, you promised me this gift\u2014that I would bring you health. Let your sweet spirit return. You cannot die near me. What is death? To never see you again? To be separated from what is part of myself\u2014without whom I have no memory and no future?\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
      "phase": "final_answer",
      "role": "assistant"
    }
  ],
