{"request_id":"40d9308e80192bb0f3c585f8","rewritten_text":"As I ran my hand through the valve, with a cup of water for the old cat, the sleeves of my shirt got caught in the loop that held the basket in place, and in an instant it came loose from the bottom. If the whole thing had actually vanished into thin air, it couldn’t have left my sight more abruptly or more instantaneously. There could not have been even a tenth of a second between the basket’s release and its complete, total disappearance, along with everything in it. My good wishes followed it to the earth, but, of course, I had no hope that either the cat or the kittens would ever live to tell what happened.

“At six o’clock, I noticed a large portion of the earth’s visible area to the east becoming covered in thick shadow. The shadow kept moving forward with great speed, until, five minutes before seven, the entire surface in view was enveloped in the darkness of night. It was not, however, until long after that that the rays of the setting sun stopped illuminating the balloon; and although this was fully expected, it still gave me an infinite amount of pleasure. It was clear that in the morning I would see the rising sun many hours before the citizens of Rotterdam, even though they were so much farther to the east. Day after day, as I rose higher and higher, I would enjoy the light of the sun for a longer and longer period. I now decided to keep a journal of my journey, counting the days from one to twenty-four hours continuously, without paying attention to the intervals of darkness.

“At ten o’clock, feeling sleepy, I decided to lie down for the rest of the night; but a problem presented itself—obvious as it may seem—that I had somehow overlooked until the very moment I am describing. If I fell asleep as I planned, how would the air in the chamber be replenished in the meantime? Breathing it for more than an hour, at the most, would be impossible; and even if that time could be stretched to an hour and a quarter, the consequences could be disastrous. Thinking through this dilemma made me very uneasy. And it will hardly be believed that, after all the dangers I had already faced, I would treat this matter so seriously that I would give up hope of carrying out my ultimate plan and finally decide that I had no choice but to descend. But this hesitation lasted only a moment."}

