          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1636c3567ecd12c645df5456\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This small explanation from Mr. Knightley gave Emma a great deal of pleasure. It was one of the nicest things she remembered from the ball. The next morning she walked around the lawn to enjoy it.\u2014She was especially glad that they had reached such a good understanding about the Eltons, and that their opinions of both Mr. and Mrs. Elton were so much the same. Mr. Knightley\u2019s praise of Harriet, and his willingness to support her, was particularly satisfying. The Eltons\u2019 rudeness, which for a few minutes had threatened to spoil the rest of her evening, had actually led to some of its best moments. And she was looking forward to another happy result\u2014the end of Harriet\u2019s infatuation.\u2014From the way Harriet had spoken about what happened before they left the ballroom, Emma felt very hopeful. It was as if Harriet\u2019s eyes had been suddenly opened, and she could now see that Mr. Elton was not the superior person she had always believed him to be. The excitement had passed, and Emma could hardly fear that his quickened attention would return through any harmful politeness. She expected the Eltons\u2019 bad feelings to provide all the lessons she might still need, through careful, pointed neglect.\u2014If Harriet could stay sensible, if Frank Churchill were not too much in love, and if Mr. Knightley did not want to quarrel with her, what a happy summer Harriet must have ahead of her!\\n\\nEmma was not going to see Frank Churchill this morning. He had told her he could not stop at Hartfield, because he had to be at home by the middle of the day. She did not regret it.\\n\\nAfter she had arranged everything, checked it all, and put matters in order, she was just turning toward the house, with her spirits lifted for the demands of the two little boys and their grandfather, when the great iron sweep-gate opened. Two people entered whom she had never expected to see 