          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"55b87a4e04128fb6fa4eebf8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Elliot looked up with so much respect.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cQuite delightful!\u201d cried Mrs. Clay, though she didn\u2019t dare to turn her eyes toward Anne. \u201cExactly like father and son! Dear Miss Elliot, may I not say father and son?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I\u2019m putting no limits on anyone\u2019s words. If you have such ideas! But, upon my word, I can hardly see that his attentions are any more than those of other men.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Miss Elliot!\u201d exclaimed Mrs. Clay, lifting her hands and eyes, and letting all the rest of her astonishment settle into a convenient silence.\\n\\n\u201cWell, my dear Penelope, you needn\u2019t be so alarmed about him. You know I invited him. I sent him away with smiles. When I learned he was really going to his friends at Thornberry Park for the whole day tomorrow, I felt sorry for him.\u201d\\n\\nAnne admired how well her friend could act\u2014how she managed to show such pleasure, both in the expectation and in the actual arrival of the very person whose presence must truly interfere with her main objective. It was impossible not to think that Mrs. Clay must hate seeing Mr. Elliot; and yet she could adopt the most obliging, calm expression and look perfectly satisfied with the reduced freedom of devoting herself only half as much to Sir Walter as she otherwise would have.\\n\\nFor Anne herself, it was most distressing to watch Mr. Elliot enter the room; and it was even more painful to have him come up to her and speak. She had been used to the feeling that he couldn\u2019t always be entirely sincere, but now she saw insincerity in everything. His attentive deference to her father, set against his former words, was disgusting; and when she remembered his cruel conduct toward Mrs. Smith, she could hardly bear to see his present smiles and gentle manner, or even to hear his rehearsed good sentiments.\\n\\nShe meant to avoid any change in her manners that might give him reason to remonstrate. Her great aim was to escape all inquiry or attention; but she intended to be as clearly cool to him as could be reconciled with their relationship, and to step back\u2014quietly, as far as she could\u2014from the few degrees of unnecessary intimacy she had been gradually drawn into. Accordingly, she was more guarded and more reserved than she had been the night before.\\n\\nHe wanted to spark her curiosity again about how and where he might have heard her praised before; he wanted very much to be encouraged by more solicitation. But the spell was broken. He found that the warmth and liveliness of a public room were needed to feed his modest cousin\u2019s vanity; he found, at least, that it couldn\u2019t be done now by any of the attempts he might make under the too-strong claims of the others.\"}"
