          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f00110a59d4ab547ddf7eb49\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This was invitation enough.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, my dear, you must understand it. Mrs. Long says Netherfield has been taken by a young man with a very large fortune from the north of England. He arrived on Monday in a chaise and four to look at the place, and was so taken with it that he agreed with Mr. Morris at once. He means to take possession before Michaelmas, and some of his servants are to be in the house by the close of next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat is his name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBingley.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs he married, or single?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, single, my dear\u2014of course! A single man of large fortune; four or five thousand a year. What a splendid prospect for our girls!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow so? In what way can it matter to them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d returned his wife, \u201chow can you be so tiresome? You ought to know I\u2019m thinking of his marrying one of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that what he\u2019s aiming at, settling here?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAim? Nonsense\u2014how can you talk like that! Still, it\u2019s very possible he may fall in love with one of them, so you must call on him as soon as he arrives.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can see no need for that. You and the girls can go\u2014or you can send them on their own, which might be even better; for since you\u2019re as pretty as any of them, Mr. Bingley may well prefer you above the rest.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear, you do me a kindness. I admit I\u2019ve had my share of beauty, but I don\u2019t pretend to be remarkable now. When a woman has five grown-up daughters, she ought to stop worrying about her own looks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn that sort of situation, a woman doesn\u2019t usually have much beauty left to consider.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut you must, my dear, go and see Mr. Bingley the moment he comes into the neighbourhood.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid I can\u2019t promise that.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cJust think of your daughters. Consider what an advantage it would be for one of them. Sir William and Lady Lucas are resolved to go, simply for that reason; for, as you know, they don\u2019t pay visits to new arrivals in general. Indeed, you must go, because it will be impossible for us to visit him if you don\u2019t.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re being far too particular. I dare say Mr. Bingley will be pleased to see you; and I will send a few lines by you to let him know how heartily I approve of his marrying whichever girl he chooses\u2014though I must add a good word for my little Lizzy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t want you to do anything of that sort. Lizzy isn\u2019t any better than the others, and I\u2019m sure she isn\u2019t half as handsome as Jane, nor half so good-tempered as Lydia.\u201d\"}"
