{"request_id":"ec8c5a4f4cce321be62fd182","rewritten_text":"“But, as a matter of fact, if you climb to any given height, the amount of air you pass through on any further climb is not at all proportional to the extra height you gain, as can be seen from what I said earlier. Instead, that amount keeps getting smaller and smaller. So it is clear that, no matter how high we go, we cannot, in the strict sense, reach a point beyond which there is no atmosphere at all. I argued that it must exist, even if it exists in a state of infinite thinning.

“On the other hand, I knew that some people had argued for a real, definite limit to the atmosphere, beyond which there is absolutely no air. But there was one detail that those who support such a limit seemed to overlook. I did not have a direct refutation of their view, but I thought this detail was worth serious study. When I compared the time intervals between Encke’s comet returning to perihelion, and allowed as carefully as possible for all the disturbances caused by the planets’ gravity, I found that the periods are gradually getting shorter. In other words, the comet’s elliptical orbit has a major axis that becomes shorter in a slow but perfectly regular way. This is exactly what we would expect if the comet meets resistance from an extremely rare, ethereal medium spread through the region of its orbit. Such a medium would slow the comet down, and by doing so it would increase the comet’s centripetal pull by weakening its centrifugal force. Put simply, the sun’s attraction would keep becoming more effective, and the comet would be drawn closer with every revolution. There is really no other way to explain the change.

“But there was more. The real diameter of the same comet’s nebulosity is seen to shrink quickly as it approaches the sun, and to expand just as quickly as it moves away toward aphelion. Was I not justified, then, in agreeing with M. Valz that this apparent shrinking of volume comes from the compression of the same ethereal medium I mentioned before, which becomes denser the closer it is to the sun? The lentil-shaped phenomenon, also called zodiacal light, also deserved attention. This glow is very clear in the tropics, and it cannot be mistaken for any meteor light. It stretches from the horizon upward at an angle, and it generally follows the direction of the sun’s equator. To me, it seemed clearly like a rare atmosphere spreading outward from the sun, at least beyond Venus’s orbit, and I believed it extended much farther. I also could not assume that this medium was limited to the comet’s orbital path or only to the immediate area near the sun.”"}

