          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fbf7e05e584a932c8e04a46e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You must take in some of my way of thinking. Only look back on the past, since remembering it brings you pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can\u2019t grant you credit for philosophy of that sort. Your backward-looking thoughts must be so entirely free of blame that the satisfaction they give you isn\u2019t philosophy at all, but\u2014something far better\u2014mere ignorance. With me, it\u2019s not like that. Painful recollections press in, ones I cannot, and ought not, try to drive away. I\u2019ve been selfish all my life in practice, though not in principle. As a child I was taught what was right, yet I wasn\u2019t taught to govern my temper. I was handed good principles, but I was left to follow them with pride and conceit. Worse still, being an only son\u2014indeed, for many years an only child\u2014I was spoiled by my parents. They were good themselves, (my father especially, all kindness and good nature,) yet they let me be, encouraged me, almost trained me to be selfish and overbearing: to care for no one beyond my own family circle; to think meanly of everyone else in the world; and even to wish that I could think just as meanly of their understanding and worth compared with my own. That was how I was, from eight to eight-and-twenty; and I might still have been so, if not for you, dearest, loveliest Elizabeth. What do I not owe you? You gave me a lesson\u2014hard at first, but of the greatest use. Through you I was brought down properly. I came to you with no confidence at all in how I\u2019d be received. You showed me how empty all my pretensions were, when it came to pleasing a woman who was truly meant to be pleased.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen did you persuade yourself that I would?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHonestly, I did. What do you think of my vanity? I thought you were wishing and expecting my addresses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy manners must have been at fault, though not on purpose, I assure you. I never meant to deceive you, but my spirits might easily have led me astray. How you must have hated me after that evening!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHate you! I was angry, perhaps, at first, but my anger soon found its proper direction.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m almost afraid to ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. You blamed me for coming?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, indeed\u2014I felt nothing but surprise.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour surprise couldn\u2019t be greater than mine was, in being noticed by you. My conscience told me I deserved no extraordinary politeness, and I admit I didn\u2019t expect to receive more than my due.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy purpose then,\u201d Darcy replied, \u201cwas to show you, by every civility I could manage, that I was not so low as to resent what was past; and I hoped to win your forgiveness, to soften your poor opinion, by letting you see that your reproofs had been taken to heart.\u201d}\"}"
