          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4e76f3a95da95a1771ed514d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cDo you hear me? I know the language of despair. I have it memorized, because I am Despair\u2014and I am a strange thing: joyful, triumphant Despair. But those words are not true. The wave may be dark, but it is not bitter. We lie down and close our eyes for a gentle good night, and when we wake, we are free. So come\u2014no more delay, you slow one! Look at this pleasant potion. See, I am a spirit of good, not a human maid who calls you in. With words that would persuade you\u2014oh, if only they could\u2014she says, \u2018Come and drink.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAs I spoke, I stared at his face. His beauty was striking, and the compassion in his eyes shone through. Even before he answered, his quiet, sincere look\u2014half regret, half amazement\u2014changed my own tight, strained feelings. It took away the hard edge of despair and left only the gentlest kind of grief. I saw that his eyes were wet too. When he took both my hands, he sat down near me and said:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a sad plan you are trying to lead me into, my dearest friend. Your pain must be very deep if it has brought you to these thoughts. You want death, but you fear it, and you still want me to be with you. But I have less courage than you. Even if I am with you, I cannot dare to die. Listen to me, and then think carefully about whether you should try to win me over to your plan. Even if, with the forceful speech of despair, you could make black death seem inviting\u2014so that fair heaven itself would look like darkness\u2014listen to the words of someone who has fed desperate thoughts and longed, with impatient desire, for death, but who has finally crushed that phantom and broken its power.\\n\\n\u201cCome, since you have played Despair with me, I will play the part of Una with you, and I will bring you out safely from his dark cave. Listen to me, and let yourself be softened by words that carry no selfish feeling.\\n\\n\u201cWe do not know what this wide world really means. It is a strange mix of good and evil. But we have been placed here, and we have been told to live and to hope. I do not know exactly what we should hope for, but there is some good beyond us that we must search for. That is our work here on earth. If misfortune comes against us, we must fight it. We must push it aside and keep going, still trying to reach what it is in our nature to desire. I do not know whether this promise of future good is meant to prepare us for another life, or whether it simply means that, as workers in God\u2019s vineyard, we must help smooth the way for those who come after us.\u201d\"}"
