          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0787af823a4fb6c169a03a0c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And he always acted like Peter wrote him those things, but that was a lie. He got every blessed one of them out of that young flathead we canoed up to the steamboat.\\n\\nThen Mary Jane went and fetched the letter her father had left behind, and the king read it out loud and cried over it. It gave the girls the dwelling-house and three thousand dollars in gold. It gave the tannery (which was doing a good business), along with some other houses and land worth about seven thousand, and three thousand dollars in gold to Harvey and William, and it told where the six thousand in cash was hidden down in the cellar. So these two frauds said they\u2019d go and fetch it up, get everything square and above-board, and told me to come with a candle. We shut the cellar door behind us, and when they found the bag, they spilled it out on the floor, and it was a lovely sight\u2014every one of those yellow boys. My, the way the king\u2019s eyes did shine! He slaps the duke on the shoulder and says:\\n\\n\u201cOh, this ain\u2019t bully or anything! Oh, no, I reckon not! Why, Bilji, it beats the Nonesuch, don\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\nThe duke agreed. They pawed the yellow boys, sifted them through their fingers, and let them jingle down on the floor; and the king says:\\n\\n\u201cIt ain\u2019t no use talking. Being brothers to a rich dead man and representatives of foreign heirs that\u2019s got left is the line for you and me, Bilge. This is what comes of trusting in Providence. It\u2019s the best way in the long run. I\u2019ve tried them all, and there ain\u2019t no better way.\u201d\\n\\nMost everybody would\u2019ve been satisfied with the pile and taken it on trust, but no\u2014they had to count it. So they counted it, and it came out four hundred and fifteen dollars short. The king says:\\n\\n\u201cDarn him. I wonder what he done with that four hundred and fifteen dollars?\u201d\\n\\nThey worried over that for a while and ransacked all around for it. Then the duke says:\\n\\n\u201cWell, he was a pretty sick man, and likely he made a mistake. That\u2019s probably the way of it. The best way\u2019s to let it go and keep quiet about it. We can spare it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, shucks, yes, we can spare it. I don\u2019t care nothin\u2019 about that\u2014it\u2019s the count I\u2019m thinking about. We want to be awful square and open and above-board here, you know. We want to lug this here money upstairs and count it before everybody, then there ain\u2019t nothin\u2019 suspicious. But when the dead man says there\u2019s six thousand dollars, you know, we don\u2019t want to\u2014\u201d\"}"
