          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"124042166bf84c1ca44e43b1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But he had oppressed no one and imprisoned no one. He was so far from demanding payment harshly that he had given up what was owed to him of his own choice. He threw himself into a world that offered him no special help, found his own place there, and earned his own bread. Monsieur Gabelle had been holding the poor, troubled estate under written instructions meant to protect the people: to give them whatever little could be given\u2014fuel that the heavy creditors would allow them to have in winter, and whatever produce could be saved from the same tight grip in summer. No doubt he had also stated and proved the same point in his defense, for his own safety, so that it could now seem unavoidable.\\n\\nThis helped shape the desperate decision Charles Darnay had already started to make: that he would go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. Like the mariner in an old story, the winds and currents had carried him into the pull of the Loadstone Rock. It was drawing him in, and he had to go. Everything that came to his mind pushed him forward, faster and faster, more and more steadily, toward the terrible attraction. His hidden uneasiness was that bad plans were being carried out in his own unhappy country by bad people, and that the man who knew he was better than they were was not there, trying to stop the bloodshed and to stand up for mercy and humanity. With that uneasiness half silenced and half accusing him, he was led to compare himself to the brave old gentleman whose sense of duty was so strong. That comparison, which hurt him, was followed at once by Monseigneur\u2019s sneers, which stung him bitterly, and then by Stryver\u2019s, which were worse of all\u2014coarse and especially painful for old reasons. After that came Gabelle\u2019s letter: the plea of an innocent prisoner, facing death, asking for justice, honor, and a good name.\\n\\nHis decision was final. He had to go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. The Loadstone Rock was pulling him, and he would keep sailing until he struck. He knew of no rock, and he saw hardly any danger. Even though he had left what he had done unfinished, the purpose behind it still showed itself to him in a way that, in France, would be welcomed when he went there to claim it. Then that bright vision of doing good\u2014so often the hopeful illusion of good-minded people\u2014rose before him. He even imagined himself, under some guiding influence, able to steer this raging Revolution, which was running so wildly out of control.\"}"
