          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2518d8eee4a1fdda8d66b173\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The oaks around us were home to a tribe of nightingales\u2014there I am now; Idris, in the prime of youth, is at my side\u2014remember, I am only twenty-two, and seventeen summers have scarcely passed over the beloved of my heart. The river, swollen by autumn rains, has flooded the lowlands, and Adrian, in his favorite boat, is engaged in the dangerous pastime of plucking the topmost bough from a submerged oak. Are you weary of life, O Adrian, that you play with danger like this?\u2014\\n\\nHe has won his prize, and he pilots his boat through the flood; our eyes followed him with fear, but the current carried him away from us. He was forced to land far downstream and make a long circuit before he could join us. \u201cHe is safe!\u201d Idris said as he leapt ashore, waving the bough over his head as proof of success. \u201cWe\u2019ll wait for him here.\u201d\\n\\nWe were alone together; the sun had set; the nightingales began to sing; the evening star shone clearly in the flood of light, which still lingered in the west. The blue eyes of my angelic girl were fixed on this sweet emblem of herself. \u201cHow the light palpitates,\u201d she said, \u201cwhich is that star\u2019s life. Its shifting brilliance seems to say that its condition, even like ours on earth, is wavering and changeable; it fears, I think, and it loves.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t look at the star, dear, generous friend,\u201d I cried. \u201cDon\u2019t read love in its trembling rays; don\u2019t look at distant worlds; don\u2019t speak of the mere imagination of a feeling. I have been silent for a long time\u2014so long, even to sickness, have I wanted to speak to you and lay my soul, my life, my entire being at your feet. Don\u2019t look at the star, dear love, or let that eternal spark plead for me; let it be my witness and my advocate, silent as it shines. Love is to me what light is to the star; as long as it is