rkey\u2014and set out for Germany.\\n\\n\u201cShe reached safely a town some twenty leagues from De Lacey\u2019s cottage, but there her attendant fell gravely ill. Safie tended her with the utmost devotion; yet the poor girl died, and the Arabian was left alone\u2014ignorant of the tongue of the country and wholly unacquainted with the ways of the world. Still, she came into good hands. The Italian had already named the place they were bound for, and after her death the mistress of the house where they had lodged took care that Safie should be brought safely to the cottage of her lover.\u201d\"}"
