{"request_id":"3b1b54e7875efeeef678ef11","rewritten_text":"During his brief stay, Emma had barely seen him; but just enough to feel that their first meeting was already over, and to get the impression that he had not been improved by the mixture of pique and pretension now showing in his manner. She was, in fact, beginning to wonder that she had ever thought him pleasing at all. His presence was so tightly tied to some very disagreeable feelings that, except in a moral sense—as a penance, a lesson, a source of profitable humiliation to her own mind—she would have been glad to be assured she would never see him again. She wished him well; but he caused her pain, and his welfare, twenty miles away, would give her far more satisfaction.

The pain of his continued residence in Highbury, however, must certainly be eased by his marriage. Many vain solicitations would be prevented, and many awkwardnesses smoothed out. A Mrs. Elton would be an excuse for any change in how they interacted; any former intimacy might fade without anyone noticing. It would be almost like starting their life of civility again.

As for the lady herself, Emma thought very little of her. She was good enough for Mr. Elton, no doubt; accomplished enough for Highbury—handsome enough, too—to look plain, probably, beside Harriet. As for the connection, Emma felt completely at ease; she was convinced that, after all his own grand claims and his disdain for Harriet, he had done nothing. On that point, the truth seemed within reach. What she was, might be uncertain; but who she was could be found out. And setting aside the 10,000 pounds, it did not seem that she was at all Harriet’s superior. She brought no name, no blood, no alliance. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol merchant—of course he had to be called that; but since the profits of his mercantile life seemed so very modest, it was not unfair to guess that the dignity of his line of trade was equally modest. Part of every winter she had been used to spend in Bath; but Bristol was her home, the very heart of Bristol. Though her father and mother had died some years ago, an uncle remained—in the law line—nothing more distinctly honorable was risked about him than that he was in the law line; and with him the daughter had lived. Emma guessed he was the drudge of some attorney, too dull to rise. And all the grandeur of the connection seemed to depend on the elder sister, who was very well married—to a gentleman in a great way, near Bristol, who kept two carriages. That was the final chapter of Miss Hawkins’s story; that was the glory of Miss Hawkins."}

