          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c91d716e9a4c8af51cc76989\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It is not everyone who would have handled it the way she did\u2014standing up for herself in such a kind way, and refusing to accept Jane\u2019s answer. But she was absolutely clear that she would not write any such denial yesterday, as Jane wished; she would wait. And sure enough, yesterday evening it was all settled that Jane should go. Quite a surprise to me! I had no idea at all. Jane took Mrs. Elton aside and told her at once that, after thinking through the advantages of Mrs. Smallridge\u2019s situation, she had decided to accept it. \u201cI didn\u2019t know a word of it until everything was settled.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou spent the evening with Mrs. Elton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, all of us. Mrs. Elton insisted that we come. It was decided up on the hill while we were walking about with Mr. Knightley. \u2018You must all spend your evening with us,\u2019 she said\u2014\u2018I positively must have you all come.\u2019\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Knightley was there too, was he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, not Mr. Knightley. He refused it from the first. And though I thought he might come, because Mrs. Elton said she wouldn\u2019t let him off, he didn\u2019t. But my mother, and Jane, and I were all there, and we had a very agreeable evening. Such kind friends, you know, Miss Woodhouse\u2014you always find them agreeable, even if everyone seemed rather worn out after the morning\u2019s party. Even pleasure, you know, is tiring, and I can\u2019t say any of them seemed to have enjoyed it very much. Still, I shall always think it a very pleasant party, and I feel extremely grateful to the kind friends who included me in it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Fairfax, I suppose, though you weren\u2019t aware of it, had been making up her mind all day?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say she had.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhenever the time may come, it must be unwelcome to her and all her friends\u2014but I hope her engagement will have every alleviation that\u2019s possible. I mean, as far as the character and manners of the family go.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, dear Miss Woodhouse. Yes, indeed, there is everything in the world that can make her happy in it. Except the Sucklings and Bragges, there isn\u2019t such another nursery establishment\u2014so liberal and so elegant\u2014in all of Mrs. Elton\u2019s acquaintance. Mrs. Smallridge is a most delightful woman. The style of living is almost equal to Maple Grove. And as for the children\u2014except the little Sucklings and the little Bragges\u2014there aren\u2019t such elegant, sweet children anywhere. Jane will be treated with such regard and kindness. It will be nothing but pleasure\u2014a life of pleasure. And her salary! I really can\u2019t bring myself to name her salary to you, Miss Woodhouse.\u201d\"}"
