          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0ecdf47da9fe35239b9bc3bb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Wickham was so completely satisfied with this conversation that he never again bothered himself, or provoked his dear sister Elizabeth, by bringing up the subject; and she was pleased to discover that she had said enough to keep him quiet.\\n\\nThe day of his and Lydia\u2019s departure soon arrived; and Mrs. Bennet was forced to accept a separation, which, since her husband by no means agreed with her plan of having them all go to Newcastle, was likely to last at least a year.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear Lydia,\u201d she cried, \u201cwhen shall we meet again?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Lord! I don\u2019t know. Not for these two or three years, perhaps.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWrite to me very often, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAs often as I can. But you know married women never have much time for writing. My sisters may write to me. They have nothing else to do.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Wickham\u2019s goodbyes were far more affectionate than his wife\u2019s. He smiled, looked handsome, and said many flattering things.\\n\\n\u201cHe is as fine a fellow,\u201d said Mr. Bennet as soon as they were out of the house, \u201cas ever I saw. He simpers, and smirks, and makes love to us all. I\u2019m prodigiously proud of him. I defy even Sir William Lucas himself to produce a more valuable son-in-law.\u201d\\n\\nThe loss of her daughter left Mrs. Bennet very dull for several days.\\n\\n\u201cI often think,\u201d said she, \u201cthat there is nothing so bad as parting with one\u2019s friends. You feel so forlorn without them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is the consequence, you see, madam, of marrying a daughter,\u201d said Elizabeth. \u201cIt ought to make you more satisfied that your other four are still single.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is no such thing. Lydia isn\u2019t leaving me because she\u2019s married, but only because her husband\u2019s regiment happens to be stationed so far away. If it had been nearer, she wouldn\u2019t have gone so soon.\u201d\\n\\nBut the listless state this event left her in was soon relieved, and her mind opened again to the agitation of hope, by a piece of news that then began to circulate. The housekeeper at Netherfield had received orders to prepare for the arrival of her master, who was coming down in a day or two to shoot there for several weeks. Mrs. Bennet was quite restless. She looked at Jane, smiled, and shook her head in turn.\\n\\n\u201cWell, well, and so Mr. Bingley is coming down, sister,\u201d (for Mrs. Philips was the one who first brought her the news). \u201cWell, so much the better. Not that I care about it, though. He is nothing to us, you know, and I\u2019m sure I never want to see him again. But, however, he is very welcome to come to Netherfield, if he likes it.\"}"
