          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1ba379f1cf2a561a03251a12\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma did not have to change her opinion of Mrs. Elton after anything she later learned. Her first judgment had been pretty accurate. The way Mrs. Elton seemed to her in this second visit was the same way she seemed whenever they met again: self-important, presumptuous, too familiar, ignorant, and badly brought up. Mrs. Elton had a little beauty and a little accomplishment, but so little judgment that Emma believed she thought she knew more about the world than everyone else. She imagined herself improving and brightening a country neighborhood. Emma also thought Miss Hawkins must have had a place in society that only Mrs. Elton\u2019s status could possibly surpass.\\n\\nThere was no reason to think Mr. Elton felt any differently from his wife. He did not just seem happy with her; he seemed proud. He had the look of someone congratulating himself for bringing such a woman to Highbury, a woman even Miss Woodhouse could not match. Most of the people who were now meeting her were either ready to praise her or not used to judging for themselves. They followed Miss Bates\u2019s friendly attitude, or simply assumed that the bride must be as clever and as pleasant as she claimed to be. Because of this, Mrs. Elton\u2019s praise moved from one person\u2019s mouth to another, just as it should, without being interrupted by Miss Woodhouse. Miss Woodhouse, in fact, kept up her first kind contribution and talked easily about how \u201cvery pleasant and very elegantly dressed\u201d Mrs. Elton was.\\n\\nIn one way, Mrs. Elton became even worse than she had seemed at first. Her feelings toward Emma changed. She was probably offended by the small amount of encouragement she got when she tried to become more intimate. So she pulled back, and gradually became much colder and more distant. The result was pleasant for Emma, but the bad feeling that caused it could only make Emma dislike her more. Their manners were also unpleasant toward Harriet. Mrs. Elton and Mr. Elton were sneering and careless. Emma hoped it would quickly help cure Harriet, but the kind of behavior that could produce such treatment showed how much they disliked her, and it made both of them seem even worse. It was not in doubt that Harriet\u2019s attachment had been offered as a kind of openness in marriage, and that their story about it\u2014presented in the least favorable way to Harriet and the most comforting way to him\u2014had likely included Harriet\u2019s own part as well. Harriet, of course, was the target of their shared dislike. When they had nothing else to say, it was always easy to start criticizing Miss Woodhouse. And since they could not show their hostility toward her openly, they let it out more freely by treating Harriet with contempt.\\n\\nMrs. Elton soon took a strong fancy to Jane Fairfax, and she did so from the beginning. Not only because a rivalry with one young woman might make her want to support another, but from the very first. She was not content with simply showing a natural, reasonable admiration. Without being asked, without making any request, and without any special right or permission, she felt she must help and befriend her. Before Emma had lost Mrs. Elton\u2019s confidence, and about the third time they met, Emma heard all of Mrs. Elton\u2019s \u201cknight-errantry\u201d on the subject.\"}"
