          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9036f3d7c73ac5b43aaddae7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In fact, he had been completely unaware of any influence of his own. He had followed her into the shrubbery without any intention of trying to do anything. He had come, driven by anxiety to see how she handled Frank Churchill\u2019s engagement, with no selfish motive at all\u2014indeed, with no motive in particular\u2014except to try, if she gave him an opening, to soothe or advise her.\u2014What happened next was simply the work of the moment, the immediate effect of what he heard on his feelings. Her delightful certainty of being entirely indifferent to Frank Churchill, her heart completely disengaged from him, had sparked the hope that, over time, he might win her affection himself;\u2014but it was not a hope for the present. In the brief rush of eagerness overpowering judgment, he had only wanted to be told that she would not forbid his attempt to attach her.\u2014The stronger hopes that gradually opened up were all the more enchanting.\u2014The affection he had been asking permission to create, if he could, was already his!\u2014Within half an hour, he had gone from a state of mind that was thoroughly distressed to something so close to perfect happiness that it could have no other name.\\n\\n_Her_ change matched his. This same half-hour had given each of them the precious certainty of being loved, and had cleared from each the same degree of ignorance, jealousy, or distrust.\u2014On his side, there had been a long-standing jealousy, dating back to Frank Churchill\u2019s arrival\u2014or even the expectation of it. He had been in love with Emma, and jealous of Frank Churchill, from about the same period; one feeling had probably helped him understand the other. It was his jealousy of Frank Churchill that had pulled him away from the country.\u2014The Box Hill party had decided him to go. He would protect himself from having to witness again those attentions that were allowed and encouraged. He had gone to learn to be indifferent.\u2014But he had gone to the wrong place. There was too much domestic happiness in his brother\u2019s house; the women there wore too amiable a face; Isabella was too much like Emma\u2014differing only in those striking, inferior qualities that always made the other seem brighter by comparison, even though there would have been plenty to do, had his time been longer. He stayed on, though, vigorously, day after day\u2014until this very morning\u2019s post brought him the story of Jane Fairfax. Then, with the gladness that had to be felt\u2014indeed, which he did not hesitate to feel, since he had never believed Frank Churchill deserved Emma at all\u2014there was so much tender concern, so much sharp anxiety for her, that he could not stay any longer. He rode home through the rain; and after dinner he walked straight up, to see how this sweetest and best of all creatures\u2014faultless despite all her faults\u2014handled the discovery.\"}"
