          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"331fb48f35469640e3038a08\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNow,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, gently laying his hand on the Doctor\u2019s arm again, after a short silence from both sides, \u201cwhat would you say caused this attack?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe,\u201d returned Doctor Manette, \u201cthat there was a strong and extraordinary revival of the train of thought and remembrance that was the first cause of the illness. Some intense associations of the most distressing kind were vividly brought back, I think. It\u2019s probable that there had long been a dread in his mind\u2014that those associations would be recalled\u2014say, under certain circumstances\u2014say, on a particular occasion. He tried to prepare himself in vain; perhaps the effort to prepare made him less able to bear it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWould he remember what happened during the relapse?\u201d asked Mr. Lorry, with natural hesitation.\\n\\nThe Doctor looked desolately around the room, shook his head, and answered, in a low voice, \u201cNot at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow, about the future,\u201d Mr. Lorry suggested.\\n\\n\u201cAs for the future,\u201d said the Doctor, regaining firmness, \u201cI have great hope. Since it pleased Heaven, in its mercy, to restore him so soon, I have great hope. He, yielding under the pressure of a complicated something\u2014long dreaded, long vaguely foreseen, and long fought against\u2014and recovering after the cloud had burst and passed, I should hope that the worst is over.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well! That\u2019s good comfort. I\u2019m thankful!\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m thankful!\u201d repeated the Doctor, bowing his head with reverence.\\n\\n\u201cThere are two other points,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201con which I\u2019m anxious to be instructed. May I go on?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou can\u2019t do your friend a better service.\u201d The Doctor took his hand.\\n\\n\u201cTo the first, then. He\u2019s studious by habit, and unu