          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c8712be03ca6da888d9949c6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"A few days later, Virginia and her curly-haired cavalier went out riding over Brockley meadows. In forcing their way through a hedge, she tore her habit so badly that, when she came back home, she decided to take the back staircase, so she would not be seen. As she passed the Tapestry Chamber\u2014whose door happened to be open\u2014she thought she caught sight of someone within. Believing it to be her mother\u2019s maid, who sometimes brought her work there, she peeped in to ask that her habit be mended. To her utter astonishment, it was the Canterville Ghost himself. He sat by the window, watching the ruined gold of the yellowing trees whirl and fly through the air, while the red leaves danced furiously down the long avenue. His head rested on his hand, and his whole posture spoke of the deepest depression. Indeed, he looked so forlorn, and so much out of repair, that little Virginia\u2014whose first impulse had been to run away and lock herself in her room\u2014felt pity and resolved to try to comfort him. Her step was so light, and his misery so heavy, that he did not notice her until she spoke.\\n\\n\u201cI am so sorry for you,\u201d she said. \u201cBut my brothers are going back to Eton to-morrow; and then, if you behave yourself, no one will annoy you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is absurd to ask me to behave myself,\u201d he replied, turning his head in astonishment toward the pretty little girl who had dared to address him. \u201cQuite absurd. I must rattle my chains, and groan through keyholes, and pace about at night\u2014if that is what you mean. It is my only business in existing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is no business at all to exist for that,\u201d said Virginia, with at times a sweet Puritan seriousness she had caught from some old New England ancestor. \u201cAnd you know you have been very wicked. Mrs. Umney told us, the very first day we arrived here, that you had killed your wife.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell,\u201d said the Ghost, with petulance, \u201cI do allow it. But it was a purely family affair, and it concerned no one else.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is very wrong to kill anyone,\u201d Virginia insisted.\\n\\n\u201cOh, I detest the cheap severity of abstract ethics!\u201d he cried. \u201cMy wife was plain enough\u2014she never had my ruffs properly starched, and she knew nothing about cookery. Why, there was a buck I shot in Hogley Woods\u2014a magnificent pricket\u2014and do you know how she had it sent up to table? But it is all over now, and I don\u2019t think it was very kind of her brothers to starve me to death, though I did kill her.\u201d\"}"
