          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f4c95e961e612aa23a7221bc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Should I be carried up to the skies, on flowery beds of ease,\\n\\nWhile others fight to win the prize, and sail through blood-y seas?\\n\\nHe was regarded as a wonderful reader. At church \u201csociables\u201d he was always called on to read poetry; and when he finished, the ladies would lift up their hands and let them fall helplessly into their laps, and \u201cwall\u201d their eyes, and shake their heads, as much as to say, \u201cWords cannot express it; it is too beautiful, TOO beautiful for this mortal earth.\u201d\\n\\nAfter the hymn had been sung, the Rev. Mr. Sprague turned himself into a bulletin-board, and read off \u201cnotices\u201d of meetings and societies and things until it seemed the list would stretch out to the crack of doom\u2014a queer custom that is still kept up in America, even in cities, out here in this age of abundant newspapers. Often, the less there is to justify a traditional custom, the harder it is to get rid of it.\\n\\nAnd now the minister prayed. It was a good, generous prayer, and it went into details: it pleaded for the church, and the little children of the church; for the other churches of the village; for the village itself; for the county; for the State; for the State officers; for the United States; for the churches of the United States; for Congress; for the President; for the officers of the Government; for poor sailors, tossed by stormy seas; for the oppressed millions groaning under the heel of European monarchies and Oriental despotisms; for those who have the light and the good tidings, and yet have not eyes to see nor ears to hear; for the heathen in the far islands of the sea; and it closed with a supplication that the words he was about to speak might find grace and favor, and be as seed sown in fertile ground, yielding in time a grateful harvest of good. Amen.\\n\\nThere was a rustling of dresses, and the standing congregation sat down. The boy whose history this book tells did not enjoy the prayer; he only endured it\u2014if he even did that much. He was restless all through it; he kept tally of the details of the prayer, without meaning to\u2014for he wasn\u2019t listening, but he knew the ground of it, and the clergyman\u2019s regular route over it\u2014and when a little trifle of new matter was slipped in, his ear caught it and his whole nature resented it; he thought the additions unfair, and downright scoundrelly. In the middle of the prayer a fly had landed on the back of the pew in front of him and tortured his spirit by calmly rubbing its hands together, embracing its head with its arms, and polishing it so vigorously that it seemed to almost separate from the body, and the slender thread of a neck was exposed to view; scraping its wings with its hind legs and smoothing them back to its body as if they were coat-tails; going through its whole grooming as tranquilly as if it knew it was perfectly safe.\"}"
