          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0f696628d1bad106f9d6433f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I will now tell my own story. In the early part of my life there is not much to say, so I will be brief. Still, I need to spend a little time on my childhood, because it should be clear how, when one hope failed, life became empty, and how, when the only affection I was allowed to hold onto was taken away, my life ended with it.\\n\\nI have already said that my aunt was nothing like my father. I believe she had the coldest heart a person can have, with no trace of cruelty or bad feeling\u2014she simply could not feel affection. She took me under her protection because she thought it was her duty. But she had lived alone for so long, undisturbed by the noise and chatter of children, that she could not bear for me to disturb her peace. She had never married. For the last five years she had lived by herself on an estate that had come to her from her mother, on the shores of Loch Lomond in Scotland.\\n\\nIn his letters, my father had asked that she come live with me at his family home, a large mansion in the beautiful countryside near Richmond in Yorkshire. She refused. But once she had dealt with the matters her brother\u2019s leaving had put in her hands, she left England and took me with her to her Scottish estate.\\n\\nWhen I was a baby, and later until I turned eight, my care was handled by a servant from my mother\u2019s household. This servant had come with us when we withdrew to live there. I was placed in a quiet, remote part of the house, and I only saw my aunt at set times. She came to me twice a day. Once, around noon, she visited my nursery. The second time was after her dinner, when she took me to her. She never hugged or comforted me, and whenever I was in the room she seemed to worry that I might annoy her with some childish act. My nurse always prepared me carefully before she would bring me into the parlour. The fear I felt from my aunt\u2019s cold looks and her few, careful words was so strong that I rarely broke the rules, and I was never caught failing to be as quiet and well-behaved as I was taught to be during those short visits.\\n\\nWith my nurse\u2019s care, I ran freely around the park and the nearby fields. From my earliest years, I showed the deepest love, and it made me very sensitive. I cannot even say how strongly I loved everything, even the lifeless things around me.\"}"
