          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d64684de27233b67fd2bd594\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We both agreed in loving Adrian: yet though she had not escaped childhood, she could not grasp, as I did, the full measure of his merits, nor share the same sympathy in his pursuits and opinions. I was forever with him. There was a sensibility and sweetness in his temper that lent our talk a tender, almost unearthly music. And then he was as gay as a lark, singing from its skyward height\u2014thoughtful as an eagle, innocent as the mild-eyed dove. He could quiet the seriousness of Perdita, and draw the sting from the tormenting activity of my nature. I began to look back on my restless desires and my painful struggles with my fellow beings as though they were only the stuff of a troubled dream, and I felt myself altered as if I had passed into another shape, with a new set of senses and nerves that changed how the apparent universe was reflected in the mind\u2019s mirror. Yet it was not so: I remained the same in strength, in my earnest craving for sympathy, in my longing for active exertion. My manly virtues did not desert me; for Urania spared the locks of Sampson while he lay at her feet, though everything was softened and made more human. Nor did Adrian teach me only the cold truths of history and philosophy. While he trained me, through those lessons, to master my own reckless and uncultivated spirit, he also turned my gaze to the living page of his own heart, so that I could feel and understand its wondrous character.\\n\\nThe ex-queen of England, even in her son\u2019s earliest years, had tried to plant daring and ambitious designs in his mind. She saw that he was endowed with genius and uncommon talent, and she cultivated these qualities so they could later be used to advance her own purposes. She encouraged his hunger for knowledge and his impetuous courage; she even put up with his untamed love of freedom, hoping\u2014what so often happens\u2014that it would grow into a passion for command. She sought to raise him with resentment toward, and a desire to revenge himself upon, those who had helped bring about his father\u2019s abdication. In that aim she failed. Still, the accounts she provided him\u2014however skewed\u2014of a great and wise nation asserting its right to govern itself stirred his admiration; in his early days he became a republican from principle. Yet his mother did not give up. To her love of rule and her proud sense of birth she added determined ambition, patience, and self-command. She set herself to study the turn of her son\u2019s disposition. By means of praise, censure, and exhortation she tried to strike the right chords; and though the music that followed her touch seemed discordant to her, she held fast to her hopes in his talents, convinced that she would at last win him.\"}"
