o had never mentioned their marriage except as a distant event.\\n\\nWhen the subject was first brought up, he was so miserable that they were almost without hope. A second mention, however, caused less pain. He began to think it was going to happen, and that he could not stop it\u2014a promising step of the mind on its way toward resignation. Still, though, he was not happy. Indeed, he seemed so much otherwise that Emma\u2019s courage failed. She could not bear to see him suffering, or to know him imagining himself neglected; and though her understanding nearly agreed with the assurance from both Mr. Knightleys that once the event was over, his distress would soon end too, she hesitated\u2014she could not go on.\"}"
