          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5fc2c04cc6d9e6462c4e66d2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She was right about the rats. Every so often, you\u2019d see one stick its nose out from a hole in the corner. She told me she had to keep things ready to throw at them when she was alone, or they wouldn\u2019t leave her in peace. She showed me a bar of lead twisted into a knot. She said she was a good shot with it, usually, but she\u2019d hurt her arm a day or two ago and didn\u2019t know if she could throw straight now. Still, she watched for a chance, and then she struck at a rat. She missed it badly and cried out, \u201cOuch!\u201d because it hurt her arm so much.\\n\\nThen she told me to try for the next one. I wanted to get away before the old man came back, but I didn\u2019t let on. I took the lead, and the first rat that showed its nose, I threw at it. If it had stayed where it was, it would have been a pretty sick rat. She said that was excellent and that she thought I\u2019d hit the next one too.\\n\\nShe went and got the lump of lead again, brought it back, and also brought a hank of yarn. She wanted me to help her with it. I held up my two hands, and she put the yarn over them while she kept talking about her and her husband\u2019s business. But then she stopped and said, \u201cKeep your eye on the rats. You\u2019d better have the lead in your lap, ready.\u201d\\n\\nRight then she dropped the lump into my lap. I pressed my legs together over it, and she went on talking. Only about a minute later, she took the yarn off my hands and looked straight at me. In a very kind voice she asked, \u201cCome now. What\u2019s your real name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWh\u2014what, ma\u2019am?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s your real name? Is it Bill, or Tom, or Bob\u2014what is it?\u201d\\n\\nI think I shook like a leaf. I didn\u2019t know what to do. Then I said, \u201cPlease don\u2019t make fun of a poor girl like me, ma\u2019am. If I\u2019m in the way, I\u2019ll\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, you won\u2019t. Sit down and stay where you are. I\u2019m not going to hurt you, and I\u2019m not going to tell on you either. You just tell me your secret, and trust me. I\u2019ll keep it. And I\u2019ll help you, too. My old man will help you if you want him to. You see, you\u2019re a runaway apprentice. That\u2019s all. It isn\u2019t anything. There\u2019s no harm in it. You\u2019ve been treated badly, and you decided to leave. Bless you, child. I wouldn\u2019t tell on you.\u201d\"}"
