          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c1ddb3d0978533705082e13c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Could not tell if the voice belonged to a man or a woman. Could not understand what was being said, but believed the language was Spanish. This witness described the condition of the room and the bodies in the same way we described them yesterday.\\n\\n\u201c_Henri Duval_, a neighbor, and by trade a silversmith, states that he was one of the group that first entered the house. He confirms Mus\u00e8t\u2019s testimony in general. As soon as they forced their way in, they shut the door again to keep the crowd out. The crowd gathered very quickly, even though it was late. The witness thinks the shrill voice was Italian. He was sure it was not French. He could not be certain whether it was a man\u2019s voice; it might have been a woman\u2019s. He did not know Italian. He could not make out the words, but he was convinced from the way the speaker sounded that the person was Italian. He knew Madame L. and her daughter and had spoken with both often. He was sure the shrill voice was not either of the two deceased.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014_Odenheimer, restaurateur._ This witness offered his testimony. He does not speak French, so an interpreter questioned him. He is from Amsterdam. He was passing the house when the shrieks began. They lasted for several minutes\u2014probably ten. They were long and loud, very awful and upsetting. He was one of the people who entered the building. He confirmed the earlier evidence in every way except one. He was sure the shrill voice was a man\u2019s voice\u2014a Frenchman\u2019s. He could not make out the words. The voice was loud and fast, uneven, and it sounded as if the speaker was afraid as well as angry. The voice was harsh. It was not so much shrill as harsh. He could not really call it a shrill voice. The rough voice said again and again \u2018_sacr\u00e9_,\u2019 \u2018_diable_,\u2019 and once \u2018_mon Dieu_.\u2019\\n\\n\u201c_Jules Mignaud_, a banker with the firm of Mignaud et Fils, Rue Deloraine. He is the older Mignaud. Madame L\u2019Espanaye had some property. She opened an account with his bank in the spring of that year\u2014eight years earlier. She made frequent deposits in small amounts. She did not write checks for anything until the third day before her death, when she took out, in person, 4000 francs. She paid this amount in gold, and a clerk went home with the money.\\n\\n\u201c_Adolphe Le Bon_, clerk to Mignaud et Fils, states that on the day in question, around noon, he accompanied Madame L\u2019Espanaye to her home with the 4000 francs, carrying it in two bags. When the door was opened, Mademoiselle L. appeared and took one of the bags from his hands, while the old lady took the other.\u201d\"}"
