          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"71afe2fd1eec9746b684042e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once, however, the lieutenant asked why he had gone so far over the ice in so odd a contrivance.\\n\\nAt once his face settled into the deepest gloom, and he answered, \u201cTo find the one who fled from me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd does the fellow you were chasing go in the same way?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen I think we must have already seen him; the day before we took you up, we noticed dogs hauling a sledge, with a man aboard, crossing the ice.\u201d\\n\\nThe stranger grew alert at this, and he pressed me with a host of questions about the course the d\u00e6mon, as he called him, had taken. Soon after, when he was alone with me, he said, \u201cI dare say I\u2019ve stirred your curiosity, and that of these good people as well; but you\u2019re too kind to pry.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course; it would be both very rude and inhuman for me to trouble you with any curiosity of my own.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet you rescued me from a strange and dangerous plight, and you\u2019ve restored me to life with kindness.\u201d\\n\\nNot long after, he asked whether I thought the breaking of the ice had destroyed the other sledge. I told him I could not say for certain, since the ice did not give way until close to midnight, and the traveller might have reached a safe place before then; but beyond that I could not judge.\\n\\nFrom that point, a fresh spark of life seemed to quicken the stranger\u2019s wasting body. He showed the greatest eagerness to be on deck, watching for the sledge that had appeared before; but I persuaded him to stay in the cabin, for he was far too weak to endure the sharpness of the air. I promised that someone would keep watch for him and let him know at once if anything new came into view.\\n\\nThis is my journal of what has occurred with regard to this strange business up to the present day. The stranger has gradually grown better, though he remains very quiet, and looks uneasy whenever anyone other than myself enters his cabin. Still, his manners are so agreeable and gentle that the sailors take an interest in him, even though they have had hardly any chance to speak with him. As for me, I find myself beginning to love him like a brother; his constant, deep sorrow moves me with sympathy and pity. He must once have been a noble creature, for even now, in wreck, he is so engaging and amiable.\\n\\nIn one of my letters I told you, my dear Margaret, that I would find no friend on the wide ocean; yet I have found a man who, before his spirit was broken by misery, I would have been glad to have had as the brother of my heart.\u201d\"}"
