          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"84bb4c4d3bd9cb73d9f7fce1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Were our proud dreams meant to fade like this? Our name had been written \u201ca little lower than the angels,\u201d and, look at us\u2014we were no better than something as brief as a moment. We had called ourselves the \u201cparagon of animals,\u201d and now\u2014look\u2014we were a \u201cquint-essence of dust.\u201d We resented that the pyramids had outlasted the embalmed body of their builder. And yet, the straw shepherd\u2019s hut we passed along the road held, in its very structure, the principle of a longer life than the entire human race. How can I reconcile this painful shift with the aspirations we once had, with the powers we seemed to possess?\\n\\nSuddenly, an internal voice\u2014clear and unmistakable\u2014seemed to say:\u2014So it was decreed from eternity. The steeds that carry Time onward are bound to this hour and to this fulfillment, since the void brought forth its burden. Would you try to read backward the unchangeable laws of Necessity?\\n\\nMother of the world! Servant of the Omnipotent! eternal, unchanging Necessity! You sit with busy fingers, always weaving the indissoluble chain of events. I will not complain about your actions. If my human mind cannot accept that everything that is, is right, still, since what is must be, I will sit among the ruins and smile. Truly, we were not born to enjoy, but to submit\u2014and to hope.\\n\\nWill the reader grow tired if I describe in detail our long, drawn-out journey from Paris to Geneva? If, day by day, I were to record, in the form of a journal, the crushing miseries of our fate, could my hand write\u2014or language find words\u2014to express the variety of our suffering? Could I capture the frantic rush and the crowding of one deplorable event onto the next? Patience, oh reader\u2014whoever you are, wherever you live, whether of a spiritual race or descended from some surviving pair\u2014your nature will be human, your home the earth. Here you will read of the deeds of the extinct race, and you will wonder, as you read, whether those who endured what you find recorded were made of the same frail flesh and soft organization as you. It is true\u2014they were. So weep therefore; for surely, being alone, you will be gentle in disposition. Shed compassionate tears; but while you do, lend your attention to the tale and learn the deeds and sufferings of your predecessors.\\n\\nYet the last events that marked our progress through France were so full of strange horror and grim misery that I cannot linger too long on the narration. If I were to break down each incident, every small fragment of a second would contain a harrowing story, and the very smallest word would turn your young blood cold. It is right that I should raise, for your instruction, a monument to the race that has passed away; but it is not right that I should drag you through the wards of a hospital, nor into the secret rooms of the charnel-house.\"}"
