          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d1355e195c1f5cf68e6bb49c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cCharles Evr\u00e9monde, called Darnay,\u201d was at length brought before the court.\\n\\nHis judges sat upon the Bench in feathered hats; yet the rough red cap and tricoloured cockade were the head-dress that otherwise prevailed. When he looked toward the Jury and the uproarious crowd, he might well have thought the common order of things had been turned about, and that the guilty were judging the upright. The lowest, cruellest, and most wretched rabble of the city\u2014never short of its share of low, cruel, and vile\u2014were the ones directing the whole scene: loudly commenting, applauding, condemning, foreseeing, and driving on the outcome, with no restraint. Many of the men were armed in one way or another; among the women, some had knives, some daggers; some ate and drank while they watched, and many knitted. Of those knitters, one had a spare bit of knitting tucked under her arm as she worked. She sat in the front row, beside a man whom he had never seen since his arrival at the Barrier, though he remembered him at once as Defarge. He observed that she leaned close and whispered to him once or twice, and that she seemed to be his wife; but what struck him most about the two figures was that, though they were posted as near to himself as they could manage, they never once turned their eyes toward him. They appeared to be waiting for something with stubborn resolve, and their gaze went to the Jury, but to nothing else. Beneath the President sat Doctor Manette, in his usual quiet dress. So far as the prisoner could tell, he and Mr. Lorry were the only men there who were not tied to the Tribunal, and who wore their ordinary clothes, without putting on the coarse garb of the Carmagnole.\\n\\nCharles Evr\u00e9monde, called Darnay, was charged by the public prosecutor as an emigrant, whose life was forfeit to the Republic under the decree that banished all emigrants on pain of Death. It mattered nothing that the decree bore date since his return to France. The decree stood there, and he stood there; he had been taken in France, and his head was demanded.\\n\\n\u201cTake off his head!\u201d the audience cried. \u201cAn enemy to the Republic!\u201d\\n\\nThe President rang his bell to silence those cries, and asked the prisoner whether it was true that he had lived for many years in England.\\n\\nUndoubtedly it was.\\n\\nThen was he not an emigrant? What did he call himself?\\n\\nNot an emigrant, he hoped, in the sense and spirit of the law.\\n\\nWhy not? the President pressed.\\n\\nBecause he had willingly given up a title that was distasteful to him, and a position that was distasteful to him, and had left his country\u2014he added, before the word emigrant in the Tribunal\u2019s present meaning came into use\u2014to earn his living by his own industry in England, rather than by the industry of the overburdened people of France.\"}"
