          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"58c84be2341c11db9455dac7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I don\u2019t suddenly spring at him and run the risk of turning it into some clumsy, half-done affair; no, I slip off to one side, out yonder, and I work up on him little by little, so he never even thinks I\u2019m about to strike him. Then, little by little, all at once\u2014like a flash\u2014he\u2019s flat on his back, and he can\u2019t make out, for the life of him, how it came about. That\u2019s how I went for brother Dowley. I began talking slow and easy, as if I were only passing the time; and the oldest man in the world couldn\u2019t have taken the bearings of where I started and guessed where I meant to end up:\\n\\n\u201cBoys, there are a heap of curious things about law, and custom, and usage, and that whole sort of business, once you start looking into it; yes, and about the drift and progress of human opinion and motion, too. There are written laws\u2014they fade away; but there are also unwritten laws\u2014_they_ endure. Consider the unwritten law of wages: it says they\u2019ve got to climb, little by little, straight through the centuries. And watch how it plays out. We know what wages are now, in one place and another; we take an average and call that the wages of today. We know what wages were a hundred years back, and what they were two hundred years ago; that\u2019s as far as we can reach, but it\u2019s enough to show us the law of progress, the pace and measure of the steady increase. So, even without any paper to point the way, we can get pretty close to what wages were three, four, five hundred years ago. All right so far. Do we stop there? No\u2014we turn around and apply the same law to what\u2019s coming. My friends, I can tell you what people\u2019s wages will be at any date in the future you care to name, for hundreds upon hundreds of years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat, goodman, what!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. In seven hundred years wages will have climbed to six times what they are now, right here in your district, and farm hands will be allowed 3 cents a day, and mechanics 6.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI wouldn\u2019t I might die now and live then!\u201d Smug, the wheelwright, cut in, with a bright, greedy sort of glow in his eye.\\n\\n\u201cAnd that\u2019s not the whole of it; they\u2019ll get their board as well\u2014such as it is: it won\u2019t swell them. Two hundred and fifty years after that\u2014listen now\u2014a mechanic\u2019s wages will be\u2014mark you, this is law, not guesswork; a mechanic\u2019s wages will then be _twenty_ cents a day!\u201d\\n\\nThere was a general gasp of awed astonishment; Dickon the mason murmured, with his eyes and hands raised:\"}"
