          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dd359565b0913279d7b3a7fa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Soul and body, body and soul\u2014how mysterious they were! There was animal instinct in the soul, and the body had its moments of spirituality. The senses could sharpen, and the intellect could sink. Who could say where the impulse of the flesh ended, or where the impulse of the mind began? How shallow were the arbitrary definitions offered by ordinary psychologists! And yet how hard it was to choose between the claims of the different schools. Was the soul a shadow sitting in the house of sin? Or was the body truly in the soul, as Giordano Bruno believed? The separation of spirit from matter was a mystery, and so was the union of spirit with matter.\\n\\nHe began to wonder whether we could ever make psychology so absolute a science that each small spring of life would be laid bare to us. As it was, we always misunderstood ourselves and rarely understood others. Experience had no ethical value. It was only the name men gave to their mistakes. Moralists, as a rule, had treated it as a kind of warning; they had claimed for it a certain ethical power in shaping character; they praised it as something that teaches us what to follow and shows us what to avoid. But experience had no motive force. It was as little an active cause as conscience itself. All it really proved was that our future would be the same as our past\u2014that the sin we had once committed, with loathing, we would do again many times, and with joy.\\n\\nIt was clear to him that the experimental method was the only method by which one could reach any scientific analysis of the passions; and certainly Dorian Gray was a subject made for his purposes, one that seemed to promise rich and fruitful results. His sudden, mad love for Sibyl Vane was a psychological phenomenon of no small interest. There was no doubt that curiosity had a great deal to do with it\u2014curiosity and the desire for new experiences\u2014but it was not a simple passion; it was a very complex one. What in it had been the purely sensuous instinct of boyhood had been reshaped by the workings of the imagination, turned into something that seemed to the boy himself remote from sense, and for that very reason more dangerous. It was the passions about which we deceive ourselves regarding their origin that tyrannized us most strongly. Our weakest motives were often the ones we were aware of least. It often happened that when we thought we were experimenting on others, we were really experimenting on ourselves.\\n\\nWhile Lord Henry sat dreaming over these thoughts, there came a knock at the door, and his valet entered to remind him that it was time to dress for dinner.\"}"
