{"request_id":"0793d952ef365d4b809413bf","rewritten_text":"We spent a few miserable hours until eleven o’clock, when the trial was set to begin. Since my father and the rest of the family had to attend as witnesses, I went with them to court. Throughout this wretched performance of justice, I endured something like living torture. The outcome would determine whether the result of my curiosity and lawless experiments would bring about the death of two of my fellow human beings: one a smiling baby, full of innocence and joy; the other murdered in a far more dreadful way, with every aggravating detail of infamy that could make the crime unforgettable in horror. Justine was also a girl of real merit, with qualities that promised to make her life happy; and now all of it was going to be erased in an ignominious grave, and I was the reason. A thousand times over, I would have confessed myself guilty of the crime they accused Justine of, but I wasn’t there when it was committed. Any such statement would have been dismissed as the ravings of a madman, and it would not have cleared her, who was suffering because of me.

Justine’s appearance was calm. She was dressed in mourning, and her face—always so engaging—was made, by the solemnity of her feelings, exquisitely beautiful. Still, she seemed confident in her innocence and did not tremble, even though she was stared at and condemned by thousands. Whatever kindness her beauty might otherwise have inspired was erased in the spectators’ minds by the imagination of the enormity she was said to have committed. She remained composed, but her composure was clearly forced; and since her earlier confusion had been used as proof of her guilt, she gathered herself to present an appearance of courage. When she entered the courtroom, she looked around and quickly found where we were seated. A tear seemed to blur her eyes when she saw us, but she recovered at once, and a look of sorrowful affection appeared to testify to her complete innocence.

The trial began, and after the advocate against her had stated the charge, several witnesses were called. Several strange facts combined against her—facts that might have shaken anyone who did not have proof of her innocence, as I did. She had been out all night on the night when the murder was committed, and by morning a market-woman had seen her not far from where the body of the murdered child was later found. The woman asked her what she was doing there, but Justine looked very strangely and gave back only a confused, unintelligible answer. She returned to the house about eight o’clock, and when someone asked where she had spent the night, she said she had been looking for the child and earnestly demanded whether anything had been heard about him."}

