          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a3526a2faf40df7a120f2445\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou haven\u2019t been in Bath long enough,\u201d he said, \u201cto enjoy the evening parties here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! no. The usual kind of them has nothing for me. I\u2019m no card-player.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou weren\u2019t formerly, I know. You didn\u2019t used to like cards; but time changes a great deal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m not changed that much yet,\u201d cried Anne, stopping, afraid she hardly knew what she might be taken to mean. After waiting a few moments, he said\u2014almost as if the words came straight from the feeling itself\u2014\u201cIt is, indeed, a period! Eight and a half years is a period.\u201d\\n\\nWhether he would have gone further was left for Anne to consider in a calmer hour; for even while she still heard the sounds he had made, Henrietta startled her into other thoughts. Henrietta was eager to take advantage of the present leisure to get out, urging her companions not to waste time, lest someone else should come in.\\n\\nThey had to move. Anne talked as if she were perfectly ready, and tried to look it; but she felt that if Henrietta had known the regret and reluctance in her heart as she left that chair, and prepared to leave the room, she would have found, in all her own feelings toward her cousin, in the very security of his affection\u2014something to pity her.\\n\\nTheir preparations, however, were cut short. Alarming sounds were heard; other visitors approached, and the door was thrown open for Sir Walter and Miss Elliot, whose entrance seemed to bring a general chill. Anne felt an instant pressure, and wherever she looked she saw the same signs. The comfort, freedom, and liveliness of the room were gone, replaced by cold composure, determined silence, or dull, empty talk\u2014meant to match the heartless elegance of her father and sister. How mortifying it was to feel that it was truly so!\\n\\nHer jealous eye was satisfied on one point. Captain Wentworth was acknowledged again by everyone, by Elizabeth more graciously than before. She even spoke to him once, and looked at him more than once. In fact, Elizabeth was working through a serious plan. The rest would explain it. After wasting a few minutes on the proper little nothings, she began to extend the invitation that would cover all the remaining obligations of the Musgroves. \u201cTomorrow evening, to meet a few friends: no formal party.\u201d She said it all very gracefully, and the cards she had prepared\u2014the \u201cMiss Elliot at home\u201d\u2014were laid on the table, with a courteous, all-encompassing smile for everyone, and one smile and one card more decidedly for Captain Wentworth. The truth was that Elizabeth had been in Bath long enough to understand how important it was to have a man of such an air and appearance as his.\"}"
