          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"303989140b1bc9bab3afd994\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Soul and body, body and soul\u2014how strange and hard to understand they were. People seemed to carry animal instincts in the soul, while the body could also show moments of something almost spiritual. The senses could sharpen, and the mind could fall apart. Who could say exactly where physical urges ended and mental urges began? How thin and uncertain were the definitions made by ordinary psychologists! Still, it was hard to choose between the different schools of thought. Was the soul like a shadow living inside a house of sin? Or was the body truly inside the soul, as Giordano Bruno believed? Separating spirit from matter was a mystery, and joining spirit with matter was also a mystery.\\n\\nHe started to wonder if we could ever make psychology so exact that every small part of life would be laid bare for us. As things stood, we always misunderstood ourselves and rarely understood other people. Experience had no real moral value. It was only the name people gave to their mistakes. Moralists usually treated it as a kind of warning. They said it could shape character in a useful way, and they praised it for teaching us what to do and what to avoid. But experience did not create any real force. It was as inactive as conscience itself. What it showed, in the end, was only this: that our future would repeat our past. The sin we had once committed with disgust, we would do again and again\u2014with joy.\\n\\nHe believed it was clear that the experimental method was the only way to reach any scientific understanding of the passions. And Dorian Gray was the kind of subject that suited him, promising rich and useful results. Dorian\u2019s sudden, mad love for Sibyl Vane was a psychological event of real interest. There was no doubt that curiosity played a big part, along with the desire for new experiences. Still, it was not a simple feeling. It was a complicated passion. What was purely