          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2e9fc201ee0fb49728cbf42b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Wake up sooner or later, then look around to see what started it\u2014maybe you\u2019d catch a steamboat coughing along upstream, so far off toward the other side you couldn\u2019t make out much about her, only whether she was a stern-wheel or a side-wheel. After that, for about an hour, there\u2019d be nothing to hear and nothing to see\u2014just plain, solid lonesomeness. Then you\u2019d spot a raft sliding by, way off yonder, and maybe some fellow on it chopping, because that\u2019s what they\u2019re always doing on a raft. You\u2019d see the axe flash and come down\u2014you don\u2019t hear a thing\u2014then you watch it rise again, and by the time it\u2019s above the man\u2019s head you finally hear the _k\u2019chunk!_\u2014like it took all that time for the sound to cross the water. So we\u2019d spend the day drifting and loafing, listening to the quiet. Once there was a thick fog, and the rafts and all that traffic going by was beating tin pans, so the steamboats wouldn\u2019t run into them. A scow or a raft would pass so close we could hear the talk and the cussing and the laughing\u2014heard it plain enough\u2014but we couldn\u2019t see so much as a shape. It made you feel all creepy, like spirits carrying on out there in the air. Jim said he thought it was spirits; but I told him:\\n\\n\u201cNo; spirits wouldn\u2019t say, \u2018Dern the dern fog.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nThe minute night came, we shoved off. When we got her out to about the middle, we left her to it and let her float wherever the current wanted. Then we lit the pipes, dangled our legs in the water, and talked about every kind of thing\u2014we were always naked, day and night, whenever the mosquitoes would let us. The new clothes Buck\u2019s folks made for me were too fine to be comfortable, and besides I never did care much for clothes anyway.\\n\\nSometimes we\u2019d have the whole river to ourselves for the longest stretch. Over there were the banks and the islands, across the water; and maybe a spark\u2014like a candle in a cabin window. And sometimes on the water you could see a spark or two, on a raft or a scow, you know. Maybe you\u2019d even hear a fiddle or a song drifting over from one of those boats. It\u2019s a fine thing to live on a raft. We\u2019d stare up at the sky, all speckled with stars, and lie on our backs looking at them, talking about whether they were made or if they just happened. Jim said they were made, but I said they happened. I figured it would\u2019ve taken too long to _make_ that many.\"}"
