hey will both be more prudent. I have written to Colonel Forster to inform him of our present arrangements, and to ask that he satisfy the various creditors of Mr. Wickham in and near Brighton with assurances of speedy payment, for which I have pledged myself. And will you take the trouble of carrying similar assurances to his creditors in Meryton, of whom I shall add a list, according to his information? He has declared all his debts; I hope at least he has not deceived us. Haggerston has our instructions, and everything will be completed in a week. They will then join his regiment, unless they are first invited to Longbourn; and I understand from Mrs. Gardiner that my niece is very eager to see you all before she leaves the south.\u201d\"}"
