          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"61818b3e198e9170a3602da3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Then they passed lonely brickworks. The fog was thinner here, and he could make out the strange, bottle-shaped kilns, with their orange, fanlike flames. A dog barked as they went by, and in the distance, somewhere in the dark, a wandering sea-gull screamed. The horse caught its hoof in a rut, stumbled, then swung aside and broke into a gallop.\\n\\nAfter a while they left the clay road and rattled over rough, paved streets again. Most of the windows were dark, but now and then strange shadows showed up against a blind lit by a lamp. He watched them with interest. They moved like huge puppets, and they made gestures that looked like living people. He hated them. A dull anger filled his chest. As they turned a corner, a woman shouted something at them from an open door, and two men ran after the hansom for about a hundred yards. The driver lashed at them with his whip.\\n\\nPeople say that passion makes you think in circles. That seemed true here. With horrible repetition, the bitten lips of Dorian Gray formed and reshaped those careful words about the soul and the mind, until he felt he had found the full meaning of his mood in them. He also used his own intellectual approval to justify passions that, without that kind of justification, would still have ruled his temper. One thought crept from one part of his brain to another: the wild desire to live, the most terrible appetite a man can have. It grew stronger and pushed into every nerve and fiber. What had once disgusted him\u2014ugliness, because it made everything real\u2014became precious to him now for that same reason. Ugliness was the only real thing. The rough fighting, the filthy den, the crude violence of a life that had fallen apart, the very ugliness of thieves and outcasts\u2014all of it felt more vivid, more real in the moment, than all the graceful forms of art or the dreamy shadows of songs. It was what he needed to forget. In three days he would be free.\\n\\nSuddenly the man pulled up hard at the top of a dark lane. Above the low roofs and jagged chimney stacks, the black masts of ships rose into view. Thick white mist clung to the yards like ghostly sails.\\n\\n\u201cSomewhere about here, sir, ain\u2019t it?\u201d he asked in a hoarse voice through the trap.\\n\\nDorian started and looked around. \u201cThis will do,\u201d he said. Then, getting out quickly and giving the driver the extra money he had promised, he walked fast toward the quay. Here and there, lanterns glimmered on the sterns of big merchant ships. The light shook and broke apart in the puddles. A red glow came from a steamer that was out at sea, coaling. The slimy pavement looked like wet rubber.\"}"
