          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"388b5205d8d945d0edeb3df2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And Mrs Musgrove took the first chance to be alone with Anne. \u201cOh! Miss Anne, I can\u2019t help wishing Mrs Charles had a little of your way with those children. They are completely different with you. But, really, they are so spoiled in general. It\u2019s a pity you can\u2019t show your sister how to manage them. They are fine, healthy children, as good as you could wish for, poor little dears, and they are not unfairly treated; but Mrs Charles doesn\u2019t know how they should be handled. Bless me! They can be so troublesome sometimes. I can assure you, Miss Anne, it makes me wish I didn\u2019t have to see them at our house as often as I otherwise would. I think Mrs Charles isn\u2019t very pleased that I don\u2019t invite them more often, but you know it\u2019s very bad to have children in your home when you\u2019re always forced to stop them every moment\u2014\u2018don\u2019t do this\u2019 and \u2018don\u2019t do that\u2019\u2014or else you can only keep them in tolerable order by giving them more cake than is good for them.\u201d\\n\\nShe also had something to say on this matter from Mary. \u201cMrs Musgrove thinks all her servants are so steady that it would be treason to question it. But I\u2019m sure, without exaggerating, that her upper house-maid and her laundry-maid are not doing their work. They spend all day wandering around the village. I run into them wherever I go, and I can honestly say I never go into my nursery twice without seeing something of them. If Jemima weren\u2019t the most trustworthy, steady creature in the world, it would be enough to spoil her. She tells me they are always trying to get her to go for a walk with them.\u201d\\n\\nAnd on Mrs Musgrove\u2019s side, she said, \u201cI make it a rule never to interfere in any of my daughter-in-law\u2019s business, because I know it wouldn\u2019t do. But I\u2019ll tell you, Miss Anne, because you might be able to put things right. I don\u2019t have a very good opinion of Mrs Charles\u2019s nursery-maid. I hear strange stories about her. She\u2019s always out and about. And from what I know myself, I can say she dresses so well that she could ruin any servants she comes near. Mrs Charles speaks highly of her, I know, but I\u2019m only giving you this hint so you can keep an eye on things. If you notice anything wrong, you won\u2019t need to be afraid to mention it.\u201d\\n\\nAgain, Mary complained that Mrs Musgrove was often not willing to give her the place she deserved when they dined at the Great House with other families. Mary said she could see no reason why she should be treated as if she were so much at home that she should lose her position.\"}"
