          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8d503c4e6d25b7456a883e41\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Mr. Hiram B. Otis, the American Minister, bought Canterville Chase, everyone told him he was making a very foolish choice, because there was no doubt at all that the place was haunted. In fact, Lord Canterville himself\u2014an unusually scrupulous man\u2014had felt it was his duty to mention the matter to Mr. Otis when they met to discuss the terms.\\n\\n\u201cWe haven\u2019t cared to live here ourselves,\u201d said Lord Canterville. \u201cNot since my great-aunt, the Dowager Duchess of Bolton, was frightened into a fit\u2014one she never really recovered from\u2014when two skeleton hands were placed on her shoulders while she was dressing for dinner. And I feel I must tell you, Mr. Otis, that the ghost has been seen by several living members of my family, as well as by the rector of the parish, the Rev. Augustus Dampier, who is a Fellow of King\u2019s College, Cambridge. After the duchess\u2019s unfortunate accident, none of our younger servants would stay with us, and Lady Canterville often got very little sleep at night because of the mysterious noises coming from the corridor and the library.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy Lord,\u201d replied the Minister, \u201cI\u2019ll take the furniture and the ghost at a valuation. I come from a modern country, where we have everything that money can buy. And with all our energetic young men painting the Old World red, and stealing away your best actresses and prima donnas, I figure that if there were such a thing as a ghost in Europe, we\u2019d have it back home in no time\u2014either in one of our public museums or on the road as a show.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid the ghost does exist,\u201d said Lord Canterville, smiling, \u201cthough it may have resisted the pitches of your enterprising impresarios. It has been known for three centuries\u2014since 1584, in fact\u2014and it always shows itself before the death of any member of our family.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, so does the family doctor, for that matter, Lord Canterville. But there\u2019s no such thing as a ghost, sir, and I don\u2019t think the laws of Nature are going to be suspended for the British aristocracy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re certainly very natural in America,\u201d Lord Canterville answered, not quite understanding Mr. Otis\u2019s last remark. \u201cAnd if you don\u2019t mind a ghost in the house, then it\u2019s fine. Just remember\u2014I warned you.\u201d\\n\\nA few weeks later, the purchase was finalized, and at the end of the season the Minister and his family went down to Canterville Chase. Mrs. Otis, who\u2014back when she was Miss Lucretia R. Tappan of West 53rd Street\u2014had been a celebrated New York belle, was now a very handsome, middle-aged woman, with fine eyes and a superb profile.\"}"
