          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"de017f46094db9cb8d6a349b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The following day was fixed for the business (so that our honour should not suffer from delay), and on that day Drummle turned up with a civil little confession in Estella\u2019s hand\u2014that she had had the honour of dancing with him several times. That left me nothing to do but regret that I had been \u201cbetrayed into a warmth which,\u201d and, in the end, to reject, as impossible, the notion that I might be found anywhere. Drummle and I then sat facing one another, snorting, for an hour, while the Grove kept up indiscriminate contradiction, and at last it was announced that the promotion of good feeling had gone forward at an astonishing pace.\\n\\nI say this lightly, but it was no light matter to me. I cannot properly put into words the pain it gave me to think that Estella should show any kindness to such a contemptible, clumsy, sulky booby\u2014so far beneath the ordinary. Even to this day, I believe the only reason I could not bear the thought of her stooping to that hound was some pure fire of generosity and disinterestedness in my love for her. Of course I should have been miserable no matter who she favoured; yet a more worthy object would have caused a different sort and degree of distress.\\n\\nIt was not hard for me to learn it, and I soon did learn that Drummle had started to keep close to her, and that she let him. After a little while he was always after her, and he and I kept crossing paths every day. He persisted in his dull, stubborn way, and Estella held him there\u2014sometimes with encouragement, sometimes with discouragement; now almost flattering him, now openly despising him; now knowing him well, now scarcely remembering who he was.\\n\\nThe Spider, as Mr. Jaggers had called him, was used to lying in wait, though, and had the patience of his kind. On top of that, he had a blockhead confidence in his money and in his family greatness, which at times served him almost instead of concentration and steady purpose. So the Spider, watching Estella doggedly, outlasted many brighter insects, and would often uncoil himself and drop at just the right moment.\\n\\nAt a particular Assembly Ball in Richmond (back then there were Assembly Balls in most places), where Estella had outshone every other beauty, this blundering Drummle hovered about her so persistently, and with so much patience on her part, that I decided to speak to her about him. I seized the next opening\u2014when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home, and was sitting apart among some flowers, ready to go.\"}"
