          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aa3d724c4d58cc93ac35e28b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Darcy spoke with warm praise of his sister\u2019s skill.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m very glad to hear such a good report about her,\u201d said Lady Catherine. \u201cPlease tell her for me that she can\u2019t expect to be excellent unless she practices a great deal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you, madam,\u201d he answered, \u201cthat she doesn\u2019t need advice like that. She practices very regularly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEven better. She can never practice too much. And when I write to her next, I will tell her not to neglect it for any reason. I often tell young ladies that you can\u2019t learn to play music well without constant practice. I have told Miss Bennet several times that she will never play really well unless she practices more. And even though Mrs. Collins has no instrument, she is welcome, as I have often told her, to come to Rosings every day and play the pianoforte in Mrs. Jenkinson\u2019s room. She won\u2019t be in anyone\u2019s way there, you know.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy looked a little ashamed of his aunt\u2019s rude manner, and said nothing.\\n\\nWhen the coffee was finished, Colonel Fitzwilliam reminded Elizabeth that she had promised to play for him. She sat down at once at the instrument. He pulled a chair close to her. Lady Catherine listened to half a song, and then, as before, talked to her other nephew. The latter walked away from her, and with his usual careful slowness went toward the pianoforte. He positioned himself so that he could see Elizabeth\u2019s face clearly. Elizabeth noticed what he was doing. At the first convenient pause, she turned to him with a teasing smile and said,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou mean to scare me, Mr. Darcy, by coming in all this grand way just to hear me. But I won\u2019t be frightened, even if your sister does play so well. There is a stubbornness in me that never can stand being frightened just because someone else wants it. My courage always grows every time someone tries to intimidate me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI won\u2019t say you\u2019re wrong,\u201d he replied, \u201cbecause you couldn\u2019t really believe I would try to alarm you. I\u2019ve known you long enough to understand that you enjoy sometimes stating opinions that aren\u2019t really your own.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth laughed warmly at this picture of herself, and then said to Colonel Fitzwilliam, \u201cYour cousin will give you a very nice idea of me, and teach you not to believe a word I say. I\u2019m especially unlucky to meet someone who is so good at showing my true character, in a place where I had hoped to pass myself off with at least some credit.\u201d\"}"
