          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"91cc83f2e7d1694b222714ca\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But no\u2014there was something even flashier than that. He would be a pirate! That was the whole point! _now_ his future stood out before him, bright with an almost unbelievable magnificence. How his name would ring through the world and make people tremble! How proudly he would cut across the laughing seas, in his long, low, black-hulled racer, the Spirit of the Storm, with his grim flag flying at the bow! And at the height of his renown, how he would suddenly show up at the old village and stride straight into church\u2014brown, weather-worn, and swaggering\u2014in his black velvet doublet and trunks, his big jack-boots, his crimson sash, his belt bristling with horse-pistols, his crime-rusted cutlass at his side, and his slouch hat with its plumes tossing in the air; his black flag would be unfurled too, with the skull and crossbones on it, and he would drink in, with swelling delight, the murmured words, \u201cIt\u2019s Tom Sawyer the Pirate!\u2014the Black Avenger of the Spanish Main!\u201d\\n\\nYes, it was settled; his career was fixed. He would slip away from home and take it up. He would begin the very next morning. So now he had to get ready. He gathered what he could and went to a rotten log nearby, starting to dig under one end with his Barlow knife. Soon he struck wood that sounded hollow. He put his hand into the opening and recited the words in a showy, solemn way:\\n\\n\u201cWhat hasn\u2019t come here, come! What\u2019s here, stay here!\u201d\\n\\nThen he scraped off the dirt and uncovered a pine shingle. He lifted it and revealed a neat little hiding-place, built of shingles for its bottom and sides. Inside lay a marble. Tom\u2019s amazement knew no bounds. He scratched his head, looking thoroughly puzzled, and said:\\n\\n\u201cWell, that beats anything!\u201d\\n\\nThen he flung the marble aside with irritation and stood there thinking hard. The trouble was that one of his superstitions had failed\u2014one that he and all his mates had always treated as certain. If you buried a marble with the right incantations, left it alone for a fortnight, and then opened the spot using the same words, you\u2019d find that every marble you\u2019d ever lost had gathered itself there in the meantime, no matter how far apart they\u2019d been scattered. But this time the thing had actually, beyond question, gone wrong. Tom\u2019s whole confidence was shaken to the core. He\u2019d heard of it working, plenty of times, but never of it failing. It didn\u2019t even occur to him that he\u2019d tried it himself several times before\u2014only he\u2019d never been able to locate the hiding-places again. He worried over it for a while, and at last decided that some witch must have stepped in and snapped the charm.\"}"
