          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"75200041fcccb62077d2d4cd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I\u2019m not dining at home, so I won\u2019t need you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, sir,\u201d the man said, and left the room.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Alan, there\u2019s no moment to waste. How heavy this chest is! I\u2019ll take it for you. You bring the other things.\u201d He spoke quickly, with an authoritative edge. Campbell felt overpowered by him. They left the room together.\\n\\nWhen they reached the top landing, Dorian took out the key and turned it in the lock. Then he stopped, and a troubled look came over his face. He shuddered. \u201cI don\u2019t think I can go in, Alan,\u201d he murmured.\\n\\n\u201cIt means nothing to me. I don\u2019t need you,\u201d Campbell said coldly.\\n\\nDorian opened the door halfway. As he did, he saw his portrait\u2019s face leering in the sunlight. On the floor in front of it, the torn curtain lay in pieces. He remembered that the night before he had forgotten\u2014forgotten, for the first time in his life, to hide the fatal canvas\u2014and he was about to rush forward when he pulled back with a shudder.\\n\\nWhat was that loathsome red dew that gleamed, wet and shining, on one of the hands, as though the canvas had sweated blood? How horrible it was!\u2014horrible, it seemed to him in that moment, even more horrible than the silent thing he knew was stretched across the table, the thing whose grotesque, misshapen shadow on the spotted carpet showed him it hadn\u2019t moved, but was still there, exactly as he had left it.\\n\\nHe drew a deep breath, opened the door a little wider, and, with half-closed eyes and his head turned away, stepped quickly inside, determined he wouldn\u2019t look even once at the dead man. Then, bending down and picking up the gold-and-purple hanging, he flung it right over the picture.\\n\\nThere he stopped, afraid to turn around, and his eyes fixed on the intricate pattern in front of him. He heard Campbell bringing in the heavy chest, the irons, and the other things he needed for his dreadful work. He began to wonder whether he and Basil Hallward had ever met, and, if they had, what they would have thought of each other.\\n\\n\u201cLeave me now,\u201d said a stern voice behind him.\\n\\nHe turned and hurried out, only vaguely aware that the dead man had been pushed back into the chair and that Campbell was staring into a glistening yellow face. As he went downstairs, he heard the key turn in the lock.\\n\\nIt was long after seven when Campbell returned to the library. He was pale, but completely calm. \u201cI\u2019ve done what you asked me to do,\u201d he muttered.\"}"
