          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9b62e5d5798237fd6bf44979\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Now that he had no work to hold, he set the knuckles of his right hand into the hollow of his left, then the knuckles of his left into the hollow of his right, then dragged a hand across his bearded chin\u2014then did it all again in steady, regular changes, with not a moment\u2019s pause. The effort of bringing him back from the vagrancy he always fell into after he had spoken was like trying to rouse some very weak person from a swoon, or like trying, in the hope of some disclosure, to keep the spirit of a man who was dying fast from slipping away.\\n\\n\u201cDid you ask me for my name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course I did.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that all?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\nWith a weary sound that wasn\u2019t a sigh and wasn\u2019t a groan, he bent to work again, until the silence was broken once more.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re not a shoemaker by trade?\u201d said Mr. Lorry, staring at him.\\n\\nHis haggard eyes shifted to Defarge, as if he meant to pass the question to him; but since no help came from that direction, they returned to the man who had asked, searching for the ground.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m not a shoemaker by trade? No, I wasn\u2019t a shoemaker by trade. I\u2014I learned it here. I taught myself. I asked permission to\u2014\u201d\\n\\nHe trailed off, even for minutes, repeating those measured movements with his hands the whole time. His eyes slowly came back at last to the face they had wandered from; when they landed on it, he startled, and resumed in the way a sleeper does when he suddenly wakes, falling back on a subject from last night.\\n\\n\u201cI asked permission to teach myself, and I got it only after a long time, with a great deal of difficulty\u2014and I\u2019ve been making shoes ever since.\u201d\\n\\nAs he held out his hand for the shoe that had been taken from him, Mr. Lorry said, still staring straight into his face:\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette, do you remember nothing about me?\u201d\\n\\nThe shoe fell to the ground, and he sat staring fixedly at the questioner.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette\u201d; Mr. Lorry placed his hand on Defarge\u2019s arm; \u201cdo you remember nothing of this man? Look at him. Look at me. Is there no old banker, no old business, no old servant, no old time rising in your mind, Monsieur Manette?\u201d\\n\\nAs the captive of many years sat staring, now at Mr. Lorry and now at Defarge, some long-buried marks of an intelligence that had once been actively intent gradually pushed their way through the black mist that had settled over him. They were clouded over again; they grew fainter; they vanished\u2014but they had been there.\"}"
