          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"40553e18fe4093e958026145\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To look at a star by quick, oblique glances\u2014to catch it, as it were, in a sideward view, by directing toward it the outer parts of the _retina_ (which are more prone to faint impressions of light than the inner portions)\u2014is to see the star clearly; it is to gain the truest sense of its brilliance, a brilliance that fades in exact proportion as we bring our sight _fully_ to bear upon it. In the latter case, more rays actually strike the eye, yet in the former there is a finer, more discriminating power of perception. If one goes too far into excessive depth, one only muddles and weakens thought; and it is even possible to make Venus itself disappear from the sky by an inspection that is too steady, too concentrated, or too direct.\\n\\n\u201cAs for these murders, let\u2019s begin by examining things for ourselves, before we settle on any opinion. The inquiry will be diverting,\u201d [I found it a strange word for the matter, but I said nothing] \u201cand, besides, Le Bon has once done me a service for which I\u2019m not ungrateful. We\u2019ll go and look at the scene with our own eyes. I know G\u2014\u2014, the Prefect of Police, and I won\u2019t have trouble getting the proper permission.\u201d\\n\\nThe permission was secured, and we set out at once for the Rue Morgue. It is one of those wretched streets that run between the Rue Richelieu and the Rue St. Roch. We reached it late in the afternoon, since this part of town lay far from where we lived. The place was easy to find: across the way, many people were still staring up at the closed shutters, driven by a curiosity with no clear object. The building was a typical Parisian one, with a gateway; on one side there was a glazed watch-box, and in the window a sliding panel marked a _loge de concierge_. Before entering, we walked up the street, turned into an alley, and then, turning again, passed behind the building\u2014while Dupin, in the meantime, studied the entire neighborhood as well as the house, with a minuteness of attention I could not see any reason for.\\n\\nWe retraced our steps to the front, rang, and, after showing our credentials, were admitted by the officers on duty. We went upstairs into the chamber where Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s body had been found, where both of the dead still lay. The room\u2019s disorder had, as usual, been left to stand. I noticed nothing beyond what had already been reported in the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux.\u201d Dupin, however, examined everything\u2014nothing excluded, not even the bodies of the victims. We then moved into the other rooms and into the yard, with a _gendarme_ accompanying us the whole time.\"}"
