          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"683fc4be41d84775079658c7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Their coming kindled something like a fire in Saint Antoine\u2019s breast, spreading quickly as they moved, so that faces at nearly every door and window seemed to flare and flicker with it. Still, no one followed them, and when they entered the wine-shop not a soul spoke; yet every man\u2019s eyes turned on them.\\n\\n\u201cGood day, gentlemen!\u201d said Monsieur Defarge.\\n\\nIt may well have been the cue to loosen the general tongue. The words brought back a ready chorus of \u201cGood day!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s bad weather, gentlemen,\u201d said Defarge, shaking his head.\\n\\nAt that, each man glanced at his neighbor, then let his eyes fall and sat in silence. All except one man, who rose and went out.\\n\\n\u201cMy wife,\u201d said Defarge, speaking aloud to Madame Defarge, \u201cI\u2019ve travelled certain leagues with this good mender of roads, called Jacques. I met him\u2014by accident\u2014some day and a half\u2019s journey out of Paris. He\u2019s a good child, this mender of roads, called Jacques. Give him something to drink!\u201d\\n\\nA second man rose and went out. Madame Defarge set wine before the mender of roads called Jacques; he doffed his blue cap to the company and drank. In the breast of his blouse he carried some coarse dark bread; he ate between whiles, then sat munching and drinking near Madame Defarge\u2019s counter. A third man rose and went out.\\n\\nDefarge took a draught of wine to refresh himself, but he drank less than was offered the stranger, since it was no rarity for him. He stood waiting until the countryman had finished his breakfast. He did not look at anyone there, and no one looked at him now\u2014not even Madame Defarge, who had taken up her knitting and kept at her work.\\n\\n\u201cHave you done with your meal, friend?\u201d he asked, at the proper time.\\n\\n\u201cYes, thank you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen come! You shall see the room I told you you might take. It will suit you to a marvel.\u201d\\n\\nFrom the wine-shop into the street, from the street into a courtyard, from the courtyard up a steep stair, from the stair into a garret\u2014once the garret where a white-haired man had sat on a low bench, bending forward and very busy, making shoes.\\n\\nThat white-haired man was gone now; but the three men were there who had left the wine-shop one by one. And between those three and the white-haired man far off lay the single small link they had once glimpsed at him through the cracks in the wall.\\n\\nDefarge shut the door with care and spoke in a low voice:\\n\\n\u201cJacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three! This is the witness encountered by appointment, by me, Jacques Four.\u201d\"}"
