          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e0bf00a9bddcc93997ef6f30\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But it is of little importance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI might as well ask,\u201d she replied, \u201cwhy, when you clearly meant to offend and insult me, you chose to tell me that you liked me against your will, against your judgment, and even against your character. Was that not an excuse for being rude, if I was rude? But I have other reasons to be angry. You know I do. If my own feelings had not already turned against you\u2014if they had been indifferent, or even kind\u2014do you think anything would have persuaded me to accept the man who has been the cause of ruining, perhaps forever, the happiness of a sister I love most?\u201d\\n\\nAs she said this, Mr. Darcy blushed; but the feeling passed quickly. He listened without trying to interrupt her while she went on.\\n\\n\u201cI have every reason in the world to think badly of you. Nothing can excuse the unfair and unkind part you played there. You cannot deny that you were the main cause, if not the only one, of driving them apart. You exposed one to the world\u2019s criticism for being fickle and unstable. You exposed the other to the world\u2019s mockery for hopes that were disappointed. And you brought them both into the worst kind of misery.\u201d\\n\\nShe paused, and with real anger she noticed that he was listening in a way that showed he felt no remorse at all. He even looked at her with a smile that seemed meant to show disbelief.\\n\\n\u201cCan you deny that you did it?\u201d she asked again.\\n\\nWith an air of calm, he then answered, \u201cI have no wish to deny that I did everything I could to separate my friend from your sister, or that I am pleased with my success. Toward him I have been kinder than I have been to myself.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth pretended not to notice the polite remark, but she did not miss its meaning, and it was not likely to make her more willing to forgive him.\\n\\n\u201cBut it is not only this matter,\u201d she continued. \u201cMy dislike is based on more than what happened then. Long before it took place, my opinion of you was already formed. Your character was made clear to me in the story I heard months ago from Mr. Wickham. What can you say about that? How can you defend yourself here, in any imagined act of friendship? Or how can you mislead other people here?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou seem very eager to hear about that gentleman\u2019s concerns,\u201d Darcy said, in a less steady tone, and with a deeper blush.\\n\\n\u201cWho could avoid feeling interested in him,\u201d Elizabeth replied, \u201cwhen he has suffered so much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis misfortunes!\u201d Darcy repeated, with contempt. \u201cYes, his misfortunes have been very great indeed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd the harm you caused him,\u201d Elizabeth cried, with force. \u201cYou have brought him down to his present condition\u2014relative poverty.\u201d\"}"
