          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9cc52c6724a1053221547050\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You must learn some of my philosophy. Think only of the past, because remembering it gives you pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can\u2019t give you credit for a philosophy like that. Your habit of looking back must be so free of blame that the comfort you feel from it isn\u2019t really philosophy at all, but something better\u2014ignorance. But it isn\u2019t like that for me. Painful memories keep coming back, and they can\u2019t, and shouldn\u2019t, be pushed away. I have been selfish all my life in practice, even if not in principle. As a child, I was taught what was right, but I wasn\u2019t taught to control my temper. I was given good principles, yet I was allowed to follow them with pride and arrogance. I was an only son\u2014an only child for many years\u2014and my parents spoiled me. They were good people themselves, especially my father, who was kind and pleasant, but they let me become selfish and overbearing. They encouraged it, and in a way they even taught it: to care only about my own family, to think badly of everyone else, and to want to believe that their sense and worth were less than mine. That was who I was, from the age of eight to twenty-eight. I might still be that way, if not for you, my dearest, loveliest Elizabeth. What do I not owe you? You taught me a lesson\u2014hard at first, but very helpful. Because of you, I was truly humbled. I came to you without any confidence that you would receive me well. You showed me how little all my efforts and hopes meant when it came to pleasing a woman who is truly worth being pleased.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid you really think then that I would be persuaded?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course I did. What do you think of my vanity? I believed you were hoping for, and expecting, my proposal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy manners must have been at fault, but not on purpose, I assure you. I never meant to deceive you, but my feelings might have led me the wrong way. How could you not have hated me after that evening!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHate you! I was angry at first, perhaps, but my anger soon found the right direction.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m almost afraid to ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. You blamed me for coming?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, indeed. I felt nothing but surprise.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour surprise couldn\u2019t be greater than mine was, when you noticed me. My conscience told me I didn\u2019t deserve any special politeness, and I admit I didn\u2019t expect to receive more than what I was owed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy purpose then,\u201d Darcy replied, \u201cwas to show you, through every bit of civility I could manage, that I wasn\u2019t so small-minded as to resent what happened in the past. I hoped to win your forgiveness, and to reduce your bad opinion of me, by showing you that your criticisms had been taken to heart.\u201d}\"}"
