          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"edef25075f68ec81cb78502d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She now felt a strong urge to go to the outer door, just to see whether it was raining. Why should she suspect herself of having some other motive? Captain Wentworth had to be out of sight. She rose from her seat and decided she would go; one part of her should not always be so much wiser than the other, or always suspect the other of being worse. She would see if it rained. But before she could, Captain Wentworth himself appeared at the entrance, coming in among a group of gentlemen and ladies who were clearly his acquaintances, and who must have joined him a little below Milsom Street.\\n\\nHe seemed far more struck and confused by seeing her than she had ever noticed him to be. He looked quite red. For the first time since their renewed acquaintance, she felt that she was betraying the least sensibility of the two. She had the advantage over him in how she had prepared herself for the last few moments. The overwhelming, blinding, bewildering shock of strong surprise was already past for her. Still, she had enough to feel. It was agitation, pain, pleasure\u2014something caught between delight and misery.\\n\\nHe spoke to her, and then turned away. His manner showed embarrassment. She could not call it cold, or friendly, or anything so clearly as embarrassed.\\n\\nAfter a short interval, though, he came back toward her and spoke again. They exchanged mutual enquiries on common topics, and neither of them was probably much wiser for what they heard. Anne could still tell that he was less at ease than before. Because they had been together so much, they had learned to speak to each other with a fair show of indifference and calmness, but he could not manage it now. Time had changed him, or Louisa had changed him. There was a kind of awareness in him. He looked very well, not as if he had been suffering in health or spirits, and he talked of Uppercross, of the Musgroves, and even of Louisa. When he named her, he even gave a brief look full of his own arch meaning; yet even so, Captain Wentworth was not comfortable, not at ease, and not able to pretend that he was.\\n\\nIt did not surprise Anne, but it did grieve her, to see that Elizabeth would not recognize him. She could see that he saw Elizabeth, that Elizabeth saw him, and that there was complete recognition between them. She was sure he was ready to be acknowledged as an acquaintance, expecting it. And she had the pain of watching her sister turn away with unchanging coldness.\\n\\nLady Dalrymple\u2019s carriage, for which Miss Elliot was growing very impatient, now drew up. The servant came in to announce it.\"}"
