          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7071c487fd1dbec91b54ccfa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"FOUR days after these strange events, a funeral began at Canterville Chase at about eleven o\u2019clock at night. The hearse was pulled by eight black horses, and each one wore a large tuft of nodding ostrich feathers on its head. The coffin was covered with a rich purple cloth, embroidered in gold with the Canterville coat-of-arms. Alongside the hearse and the coaches, the servants walked with torches that were burning, and the whole procession looked truly impressive.\\n\\nLord Canterville was the main mourner. He had come all the way from Wales just to attend the funeral, and he sat in the first carriage with little Virginia. Next came the United States Minister and his wife, then Washington and the three boys. In the last carriage rode Mrs. Umney. People generally agreed that, since she had been frightened by the ghost for more than fifty years, she had every right to see the end of him.\\n\\nA deep grave had been dug in the corner of the churchyard, right under the old yew tree. The service was carried out in a very solemn way by the Rev. Augustus Dampier. When the ceremony ended, the servants, following an old custom in the Canterville family, put out their torches. As the coffin was lowered into the grave, Virginia stepped forward and placed on it a large cross made of white and pink almond blossoms. At that moment, the moon slipped out from behind a cloud and lit up the small churchyard with its quiet silver light, and from a distant grove a nightingale began to sing. She remembered the ghost\u2019s description of the Garden of Death. Her eyes filled with tears, and during the drive home she could hardly say a word.\\n\\nThe next morning, before Lord Canterville went up to town, Mr. Otis met him to talk about the jewels the ghost had given to Virginia. They were truly magnificent, especially a ruby necklace with an old Venetian setting. It was an excellent example of sixteenth-century work, and because the jewels were so valuable, Mr. Otis felt very uneasy about letting his daughter accept them.\\n\\n\u201cMy lord,\u201d he said, \u201cI know that in this country mortmain applies to small things as well as land. And it seems clear to me that these jewels are, or should be, part of your family\u2019s inheritance. I must ask you, then, to take them to London with you, and to treat them simply as property that has been returned to you under certain strange conditions. As for my daughter, she is only a child, and I\u2019m glad to say she has, as yet, very little interest in such ornaments of idle luxury.\u201d\"}"
