al months, and the variety and novelty of our pleasures gave them a lively relish.\\n\\nWe made excursions together without end. Whether it was to look upon beautiful country, or to see fine paintings, or sometimes with no particular aim beyond whatever amusement might present itself, I was always glad when I was near my father. I regretted it whenever a third person joined us; still, if I glanced at him with a troubled expression, his eyes, fixed on me and shining with tenderness, would at once bring back joy to my heart. O hours of keen delight! Though brief, they are as long to me as a whole life, when I look back upon them through the mist of grief that rose immediately afterward, as if to bar them from my sight.\"}"
