          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d003a55143d5c8b28ea94468\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The bewilderment and astonishment that seized my mind at this remarkable shift in the whole state of things was, perhaps, after all, the portion of the adventure least open to explanation. For the bouleversement itself was not only natural and unavoidable, but had long been actually foreseen as something to be expected whenever I should reach that precise stage of my voyage where the pull of the planet would give way to the pull of the satellite\u2014or, to speak more exactly, where the balloon\u2019s gravitation toward the earth should prove weaker than its gravitation toward the moon. Of course, I had risen from a sound sleep, with every sense in confusion, to observe a phenomenon so startling; and yet, though it was expected, it was not expected in the very instant. The change must, no doubt, have occurred in an easy and gradual fashion, and it is by no means certain that, even if I had been awake at the moment, I would have recognized it from any inward sign\u2014any inconvenience or disorder, either in my own person or in my apparatus.\\n\\n\u201cIt is scarcely necessary to add that, once I came to a proper understanding of my situation, and had escaped the terror that had taken hold of every faculty of my soul, my attention was first of all fixed on the moon\u2019s general physical aspect. It lay beneath me like a chart; and though I still reckoned it at a considerable distance, the markings of its surface stood out to my sight with a vividness that was both striking and wholly inexplicable. The total lack of ocean or sea\u2014and, indeed, of any lake or river, or any body of water whatever\u2014struck me, at first glance, as the most extraordinary feature in its geological condition. Still, to my surprise, I saw broad level tracts of a decidedly alluvial nature, while the greater part of the hemisphere before me was covered with countless volcanic mountains, conical in form, and looking more like artificial projections than natural elevations. The tallest of them does not rise above three and three-quarter miles in perpendicular height; but a map of the volcanic districts of the Campi Phlegraei would show your Excellencies their general surface far better than any unworthy account I might attempt. Most of these mountains were in a state of evident eruption, and they made me dreadfully aware of their rage and force through the repeated thunders of the so-called meteoric stones, which now shot upward by the balloon with an ever-increasing frequency, more and more alarming.\u201d\"}"
