          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7b4ba8f0006c5cf79502f949\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But how could I, a poor, dazed village boy, avoid the kind of contradiction that even the best and wisest men fall into every day?\\n\\n\u201cIt may be all true,\u201d I told Biddy, \u201cbut I can\u2019t help admiring her so much.\u201d\\n\\nSo when it came to that, I turned over onto my face. I grabbed the hair on both sides of my head and pulled it hard. All the while I knew my heart was acting wildly and wrongly, and I was fully aware that I deserved it. If I had lifted my face by my hair and knocked it against the pebbles, that would have been a fair punishment for being such an idiot.\\n\\nBiddy was the wisest girl, and she didn\u2019t try to argue with me anymore. She put her rough, work-worn hand on my hands, one after another, and gently pulled them out of my hair. Then she patted my shoulder softly, in a way meant to calm me. With my face pressed against my sleeve, I cried a little\u2014just like I had in the brewery yard\u2014and I felt, in a vague way, that I was being treated badly by someone, or by everyone. I couldn\u2019t tell which.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m glad of one thing,\u201d Biddy said. \u201cYou\u2019ve been able to trust me, Pip. And I\u2019m glad of another thing: you know you can always rely on me to keep your confidence, and to deserve it as much as I can. If your first teacher\u2014dear me, what a poor teacher she was, and how much she still needed to be taught herself\u2014had been your teacher right now, she would think she knew what lesson she would give you. But it would be a hard lesson to learn, and you\u2019ve already moved past her. There\u2019s no point in it now.\u201d With a quiet sigh for me, Biddy stood up from the bank. Then, with a fresh and pleasant change in her voice, she asked, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I cried, as I got up. I put my arm around her neck and kissed her. \u201cI\u2019ll always tell you everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUntil you\u2019re a gentleman,\u201d Biddy said.\\n\\n\u201cYou know I never will be,\u201d I answered, \u201cso that\u2019s settled. And it\u2019s not even like I have to tell you anything, because you already know everything I know. As I told you the other night at home.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d Biddy said, almost in a whisper, as she looked away at the ships. Then, with the same pleasant change of voice as before, she asked again, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\nI told Biddy we would walk a little farther, and we did. The summer afternoon slowly faded into summer evening, and it was beautiful.\"}"
