          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ab8012752252f4ccf3d84e5e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGit up! What you \u2019bout?\u201d\\n\\nI opened my eyes and took a look around, trying to figure out where I was. It was after sun-up, and I\u2019d been dead asleep. Pap was standing over me, looking sour and sick, same as anything. He says:\\n\\n\u201cWhat you doin\u2019 with this gun?\u201d\\n\\nI figured he didn\u2019t have a clue about what he\u2019d been up to, so I says:\\n\\n\u201cSomebody tried to get in, so I was laying for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy didn\u2019t you roust me out?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I tried to, but I couldn\u2019t; I couldn\u2019t budge you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, all right. Don\u2019t stand there palavering all day\u2014get out with you and see if there\u2019s a fish on the lines for breakfast. I\u2019ll be along in a minute.\u201d\\n\\nHe unlocked the door, and I slipped out up the river-bank. I saw bits of limbs and other stuff floating down, and a sprinkling of bark, so I knew the river had started to rise. I figured I\u2019d be having fine times now if I was over in town. The June rise used to be good luck for me\u2014once it starts here, cordwood comes floating down, and chunks of log rafts, sometimes a dozen logs together. All you had to do was catch \u2019em and sell them to the wood-yards and the sawmill.\\n\\nI went along the bank with one eye on where Pap might be and the other on what the rise might bring. Then, all at once, here comes a canoe\u2014real pretty, too\u2014about thirteen or fourteen foot long, riding high like a duck. I launched head-first off the bank like a frog, clothes and all, and paddled straight for it. I just figured there\u2019d be somebody in it, because folks often did that to make a fool of you; when a man had hauled a skiff out most of the way, they\u2019d pop up and laugh at him. But this time it wasn\u2019t like that. It was a drift-canoe for sure, and I climbed in and paddled her ashore. I thought, the old man\u2019ll be glad when he sees it\u2014she\u2019s worth ten dollars. But when I got to shore, Pap wasn\u2019t anywhere in sight. And as I was pushing her into a little creek, like a gully, all tangled over with vines and willows, I got another notion: I\u2019d hide her good. Then, instead of heading for the woods when I run off, I\u2019d go down the river about fifty mile and camp in one place for good, so I wouldn\u2019t have to keep tramping on foot.\\n\\nIt was pretty close to the shanty, and I thought I could hear the old man coming the whole time, but I got the canoe stowed. Then I stepped out and looked around through a bunch of willows, and there was the old man down the path a ways, drawing a bead on a bird with his gun.\"}"
