          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dfe6a12c9766ab2fd45b071c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"While these things were happening in the country workhouse, Mr. Fagin sat in the old den\u2014the same one from which the girl had removed Oliver\u2014brooding over a dull, smoky fire. He held a pair of bellows on his knee, apparently trying to get it going again with a bit more cheer, but he had slipped into deep thought. With his arms folded over them and his chin resting on his thumbs, he stared, absent-mindedly, at the rusty bars.\\n\\nBehind him, at a table, sat the Artful Dodger, Master Charles Bates, and Mr. Chitling, all focused on a game of whist. The Artful was playing dummy against Master Bates and Mr. Chitling. The first gentleman\u2019s face\u2014always unusually intelligent\u2014took on even more interest as he watched the game closely, studying Mr. Chitling\u2019s hand. From time to time, whenever it seemed useful, he sent him earnest looks, carefully adjusting his own play based on what he observed in his neighbor\u2019s cards. Since it was a cold night, the Dodger wore his hat, as he often did indoors. He also kept a clay pipe between his teeth, removing it only briefly when he thought it necessary to ask for a refill from the quart pot on the table, which stood ready with gin-and-water for the company.\\n\\nMaster Bates was attentive too, but because he was more excitable than his accomplished friend, it was noticeable that he went back to the gin-and-water more often, and that he also indulged in plenty of jokes and irrelevant remarks\u2014utterly inappropriate for a serious game. Indeed, the Artful, assuming their close friendship, more than once took the chance to speak gravely to him about these improprieties. Master Bates took the remonstrances in extremely good part, only asking his friend to be \u201cblowed,\u201d or to put his head in a sack, or answering with some other neatly turned bit of humor of the same kind; and the way he did it earned him considerable admiration in Mr. Chitling\u2019s mind. It was striking that Mr. Chitling and his partner always lost. Yet instead of making Master Bates angry, it seemed only to amuse him more\u2014he laughed uproariously at the end of every deal and insisted he had never seen such a jolly game in all his born days.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s two doubles and the rub,\u201d said Mr. Chitling, with a very long face, as he drew half-a-crown from his waistcoat pocket. \u201cI never see such a feller as you, Jack; you win everything.\u201d\"}"
