{"request_id":"460501683e6d33589052c056","rewritten_text":"I could remember what it was like during our great civil war in the nineteenth century. In the North, a carpenter earned three dollars a day, based on gold value. In the South, he earned fifty—paid in Confederate shinplasters that were worth a dollar a bushel. In the North, a suit of overalls cost three dollars, which was a day’s wages. In the South, it cost seventy-five—two days’ wages. Other things were in the same proportion. So wages were twice as high in the North as they were in the South, because the one wage had that much more purchasing power than the other.

Yes, I made various acquaintances in the hamlet, and one thing that really pleased me was to see our new coins in circulation—lots of milrays, lots of mills, lots of cents, plenty of nickels, and some silver; all of it among the artisans and the common people in general. Yes, and even some gold—but that was at the bank, meaning the goldsmith’s. I went in while Marco, the son of Marco, was haggling with a shopkeeper over a quarter of a pound of salt, and I asked for change for a twenty-dollar gold piece. They gave it to me—that is, after they had chewed the piece, and rung it on the counter, and tested it with acid, and asked me where I got it, and who I was, and where I was from, and where I was going, and when I expected to get there, and maybe a couple of hundred more questions. And when they finally ran out of questions, I kept right on supplying them with information on my own. I told them I owned a dog, and his name was Watch, and my first wife was a Free Will Baptist, and her grandfather was a Prohibitionist, and I used to know a man who had two thumbs on each hand and a wart on the inside of his upper lip, and died in the hope of a glorious resurrection, and so on, and so on, and so on, until even that hungry little village questioner began to look satisfied, and a shade of relief came over him too. But he still had to respect a man of my financial strength, so he didn’t give me any lip; still, I noticed he took it out of his underlings, which was a perfectly natural thing to do.

Yes, they changed my twenty, but I thought it strained the bank a little, which was to be expected. It was like walking into a small village store in the nineteenth century and suddenly asking the owner to change a two-thousand-dollar bill for you. He could probably do it, but at the same time he’d wonder how a small farmer ended up carrying that much money around in his pocket. That was probably the goldsmith’s thought too; because he followed me to the door and stood there watching me go with reverent admiration."}

