          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b6748fecdffe42c2a7779d6b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Indeed, I had no fear for her. She was the kind of woman people call notable, and she could handle things in the world without my help. To be honest, I think she always saw me as a lazy boy, a useless extra, good for nothing except building castles in the air. She was probably relieved to be rid of me.\\n\\nIt was a dark night when I said goodbye. I took with me, as aides-de-camp, the three creditors who had caused me so much trouble. We carried the balloon, along with the car and all the equipment, by a roundabout route to the station where the other items had been stored. When we got there, we found everything safe and untouched, and I went straight to work.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the first of April. As I said, the night was dark, with no stars to be seen. Rain fell in short bursts, and it made us miserable. My biggest worry was the balloon. Even though it was protected by varnish, it was starting to get heavy from the moisture, and the powder could also be damaged. So I kept the three duns busy, working hard: they pounded ice around the central cask and stirred the acid in the others. They did not stop asking me what I planned to do with all this equipment. They complained bitterly about the terrible work I was making them do. They could not understand, they said, how it could help anything to get soaked just to take part in such awful, strange rituals. I began to feel uneasy. I truly believed the idiots thought I had made a deal with the devil, and that what I was doing now was no better than it should be. I was afraid they would abandon me altogether.\\n\\nI managed, though, to calm them by promising to pay every debt in full as soon as I could finish this job. Of course, they took my words in their own way. They probably imagined that, at the very least, I would end up with a lot of ready money. If I paid them what I owed, and a little extra for their trouble, they probably did not care much what happened to my soul or my body.\\n\\n\u201cAfter about four and a half hours, I found the balloon inflated enough. I attached the car, and put all my tools inside it. I made sure not to forget the condensing apparatus, a large supply of water, and plenty of provisions, including pemmican, which has a lot of nourishment packed into a small amount.\u201d\"}"
