{"request_id":"800bd7af53e8e8e13115a3d5","rewritten_text":"Elizabeth was sad and utterly downcast. She no longer took pleasure in her ordinary tasks; every kind of joy felt to her like sacrilege against the dead. She believed that eternal misery and tears were the proper tribute she should pay to innocence, so cruelly blasted and destroyed. She was no longer the happy girl who, in earlier youth, had wandered with me along the banks of the lake and spoken with ecstasy about our future prospects. The first of those sorrows sent to pull us away from the earth had come to her, and its dulling influence had silenced her dearest smiles.

“When I reflect, my dear cousin,” she said, “on the miserable death of Justine Moritz, I can no longer see the world and its works the way I once did. Before, when I read in books or heard from others about vice and injustice, I treated it as stories from long ago or imaginary evils—at least they were distant, and more familiar to reason than to imagination. But now misery has come home, and men seem to me like monsters who thirst for each other’s blood. Yet I know I am being unfair. Everyone believed that poor girl was guilty; and if she had truly committed the crime for which she suffered, she would have been the most depraved creature among human beings. For the sake of a few jewels, to have murdered the son of her benefactor and friend—a child she had nursed from birth, and who she seemed to love as if he were her own! I could never accept the death of any human being, but I would certainly have thought such a creature unfit to remain among people. But she was innocent. I know it—I feel it. You share the same view, and that reassures me. Alas, Victor: when falsehood can look so much like the truth, who can be sure of any happiness? It feels as though I’m walking along the edge of a precipice, toward which thousands are pressing, trying to drive me into the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, and the killer gets away. He walks the world free, and perhaps even respected. But even if I were condemned to suffer on the scaffold for the same crimes, I would not trade places with such a wretch.”

I listened to her words with the most intense agony. I was not the murderer in deed, but in effect. Elizabeth saw my distress in my face, and, taking my hand with kindness, she said, “My dearest friend, you must calm yourself. These events have affected me—God knows how deeply—but I am not as miserable as you are.”"}

