          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4ac1920dc083c0f33d4713e4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His face, baked by the sun, was more than half concealed beneath a thick growth of whiskers and a _mustachio._ He carried with him a huge oaken cudgel, though he seemed otherwise to have no weapons. He bowed in a clumsy fashion and greeted us with \u201cgood evening,\u201d speaking French with accents that, though a bit tinged with Neufchatel, were still plain enough to point to Paris.\\n\\n\u201cTake a seat, my friend,\u201d said Dupin. \u201cI take it you\u2019ve come about the Ourang-Outang. Upon my word, I almost envy you for having him; a splendid creature, and no doubt a very valuable one. How old do you think he is?\u201d\\n\\nThe sailor drew in a long breath, as though freed from some unbearable weight, and then answered with confidence:\\n\\n\u201cI can\u2019t say for certain\u2014but he can\u2019t be more than four or five years old. Have you got him here?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh no; we had no arrangements for keeping him here. He\u2019s at a livery stable in the Rue Dubourg, just around the corner. You can fetch him in the morning. Of course you\u2019re ready to identify the property?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI shall be sorry to let him go,\u201d said Dupin.\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t mean you should go to all this trouble for nothing, sir,\u201d the man returned. \u201cI couldn\u2019t ask it. I\u2019m quite willing to pay a reward for finding the animal\u2014that is, anything reasonable.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell,\u201d said my friend, \u201cthat\u2019s all very fair, to be sure. Let me consider\u2014what should I ask? Ah! I\u2019ll tell you. My reward will be this: you will give me all the information you can about these murders in the Rue Morgue.\u201d\\n\\nDupin spoke the last words in a very low voice, and very quietly. In the same quiet manner he went to the door, locked it, and put the key in his pocket. Then he drew a pistol from his bosom and set it on the table, without the slightest agitation.\\n\\nThe sailor\u2019s face reddened, as if he were fighting for breath. He sprang up and seized his cudgel, but the next instant he sank back into his chair, shaking violently, with the very look of death. He said nothing. I felt for him sincerely.\\n\\n\u201cMy friend,\u201d said Dupin, in a gentle tone, \u201cyou\u2019re frightening yourself unnecessarily\u2014you really are. We mean you no harm at all. I pledge you the honor of a gentleman, and of a Frenchman, that we intend you no injury. I know perfectly well that you are innocent of the atrocities in the Rue Morgue. Still, it won\u2019t do to deny that you are, in some measure, implicated in them.\u201d\"}"
