          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f5d9ceb96fef680f7c647f00\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You see, I had my handkerchief in my helmet, along with a few other things. But it was the kind of helmet you can\u2019t take off by yourself. I hadn\u2019t thought about that when I put it there, and I didn\u2019t even know it. I just assumed it would be especially convenient right there. Now, though, the idea of it being there\u2014so close, so useful\u2014yet impossible to reach, made everything worse and harder to bear. Yes, the thing you can\u2019t get is usually the thing you want most. Everyone notices that.\\n\\nWell, it took my mind off everything else. It pulled my thoughts straight into my helmet, and mile after mile I kept imagining the handkerchief, picturing it again and again. Meanwhile the salt sweat kept running into my eyes, and I couldn\u2019t get to it. It sounds like a small problem on paper, but it wasn\u2019t small at all. It was the most real kind of misery. I wouldn\u2019t say it if it weren\u2019t true. I decided that next time I would bring a reticule, no matter what anyone thought. Of course those iron men of the Round Table would probably call it scandalous and maybe start talking about Sheol, but for me, comfort comes first, and style comes later.\\n\\nSo we jogged along. Every now and then we hit a stretch of dust, and it would rise in clouds, get into my nose, and make me sneeze and cry. And of course I said things I shouldn\u2019t have said\u2014I won\u2019t deny that. I\u2019m not better than anyone.\\n\\nWe couldn\u2019t seem to meet anyone in this lonely Britain, not even an ogre. And in the mood I was in then, it was better for the ogre\u2014an ogre with a handkerchief. Most knights would have thought only about getting his armor, but I managed to take his bandanna instead. He could keep his gear, for all of me.\\n\\nIn the meantime, it kept getting hotter and hotter inside. The sun was beating down and warming the iron more and more all the time. When you\u2019re hot like that, even small things start to irritate you. When I trotted, I rattled like a crate of dishes, and that annoyed me. I also couldn\u2019t stand the shield slatting and banging\u2014first against my breast, then around my back. If I slowed down into a walk, my joints creaked and screeched in that tiresome way a wheelbarrow does. And since we weren\u2019t making any breeze at that pace, I felt like I\u2019d get fried in a stove. Besides, the quieter I went, the more the iron seemed to settle on me, and the more and more tons it felt like I weighed every minute.\"}"
