          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0193b8fc24f269aed9a8df32\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI need to step out for a moment to observe, Sir Walter,\u201d said Mr Shepherd one morning at Kellynch Hall, as he set down the newspaper, \u201cthat the current situation is working very much in our favour. This peace will send all our rich naval officers ashore. They\u2019ll all be looking for a home. Could there be a better time, Sir Walter, to have a choice of tenants\u2014very responsible ones. Many a noble fortune has been made during the war. If a wealthy admiral were to come our way, Sir Walter\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe would be a very lucky man, Shepherd,\u201d Sir Walter replied; \u201cthat\u2019s all I have to say on it. What a prize Kellynch Hall would be to him\u2014rather the greatest prize of all, considering how many he may have taken already; eh, Shepherd?\u201d\\n\\nMr Shepherd laughed, as he knew he must, at the joke, and then added\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI would presume to observe, Sir Walter, that, in matters of business, gentlemen of the navy are well worth dealing with. I\u2019ve learned something about the way they do business, and I\u2019m free to admit they have very liberal ideas, and are as likely to make desirable tenants as any group of people one could meet. So, Sir Walter, what I would venture to suggest is this: if, as a result of any rumours getting out about your intentions\u2014which we have to consider a possible thing, because it\u2019s so hard to keep the actions and plans of one part of the world from the notice and curiosity of the other\u2014then consequence has its price. I, John Shepherd, might conceal whatever family matters I chose, because nobody would think it worth their while to watch me; but Sir Walter Elliot has eyes on him, and it may be very difficult to avoid them. Therefore, I\u2019ll say this much: it won\u2019t greatly surprise me if, despite all our caution, some rumour of the truth should get abroad. And if that happens\u2014since applications will unquestionably follow\u2014I think any from our wealthy naval commanders would be particularly worth attending to. And I beg leave to add that two hours will bring me over at any time, to save you the trouble of replying.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter only nodded. But soon afterwards, as he rose and paced the room, he remarked with a touch of sarcasm\u2014\\n\\n\u201cThere are few, I imagine, among the gentlemen of the navy who wouldn\u2019t be surprised to find themselves in a house like this.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey would look around them, no doubt, and bless their good fortune,\u201d said Mrs Clay, for Mrs Clay was there: her father had driven her over, since nothing was of more use to Mrs Clay\u2019s health than a drive to Kellynch. \u201cBut I quite agree with my father in thinking a sailor might be a very desirable tenant.\u201d\"}"
