          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"af0fa36da2ee4e8ed5da2ec4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I got up early in the morning and again tried to slip past the watchfulness of my creditors. Eagerly, I went to the bookseller\u2019s stall and laid out the little cash I had on hand to buy some books on Mechanics and Practical Astronomy. Once I got home safely, I spent every spare moment reading them. Before long, I felt I had learned enough to carry out my plan.\\n\\nDuring that time, I also worked hard to calm down the three creditors who had caused me so much trouble. I finally managed it. Partly I raised money by selling some of my household furniture to cover part of what they were owed. Partly I promised to pay the rest once I finished a small project I told them I was working on, and I asked for their help in doing it. Since they were not very knowledgeable men, I had little difficulty turning them to my purpose.\\n\\nWith everything arranged, I then planned\u2014quietly and with the help of my wife\u2014to get rid of whatever property I still had. I also borrowed small amounts of money under different excuses, and I paid no attention at all to how I would repay it later. With the money I gathered, I bought, in stages, cambric muslin, very fine cloth, cut into pieces of twelve yards each; twine; a supply of caoutchouc varnish; a large deep wicker basket made to order; and several other items needed to build and equip a balloon of extraordinary size. I told my wife to put it together as soon as possible and gave her clear instructions on exactly how to proceed.\\n\\nMeanwhile, I worked the twine into a net large enough for the job. I fitted it with a hoop and the cords it needed. I bought a quadrant, a compass, a spy-glass, and a common barometer, with some important modifications. I also acquired two astronomical instruments that were not as well known. Then, whenever I could, I carried by night to a secluded place east of Rotterdam: five iron-bound casks, each meant to hold about fifty gallons, plus one larger cask; six tinned ware tubes, three inches across and ten feet long; a quantity of a particular metallic substance, or semi-metal, which I will not name; and a dozen demijohns of a very common acid.\\n\\nThe gas made from these materials is a gas that no one else has ever produced, at least not for any similar purpose. I would not have trouble explaining the secret, except that it properly belongs to a citizen of Nantz in France, who shared it with me on the condition that I use it.\"}"
