          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c5128e2f0f6c71585234e50c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His understanding and temper, though not like hers, would still have met all her wishes. It would have been a marriage that benefited both of them. With her ease and lively spirit, his mind might have become gentler, and his manners could have improved. And from his judgment, his knowledge, and his experience of the world, she would have gained something even more important.\\n\\nBut no such happy marriage could now show the crowd what real marital happiness was. A different kind of union\u2014one that would make the other impossible\u2014was soon to be formed in their family.\\n\\nShe could not imagine how Wickham and Lydia would be supported in a tolerable independence. But she could easily guess how little lasting happiness could belong to a couple brought together only because their passions were stronger than their virtue.\\n\\nMr. Gardiner soon wrote again to his brother. He answered Mr. Bennet\u2019s acknowledgments briefly, assuring him that he was eager to promote the welfare of any of his family. He then ended by asking, very earnestly, that the subject might never be mentioned to him again. The main purpose of his letter was to tell them that Mr. Wickham had decided to leave the militia.\\n\\n\u201cIt was greatly my wish that he should do so,\u201d he added, \u201cas soon as his marriage was settled. I think you will agree with me that leaving that corps is highly advisable, both for his sake and for my niece\u2019s. Mr. Wickham intends to join the Regulars. Among his old friends, there are still some who can and will help him in the army. He has been promised an ensigncy in General----\u2019s regiment, which is now stationed in the north. It is an advantage that it is so far from this part of the kingdom. He promises fairly, and I hope that, among different people, where each of them has a character to protect, they will both be more careful. I have written to Colonel Forster to tell him about our present plans, and to ask him to satisfy the different creditors of Mr. Wickham in and near Brighton, with assurances of prompt payment. I have pledged myself for this. And will you take the trouble of giving similar assurances to his creditors in Meryton? I will attach a list, based on the information he has given. He has reported all his debts; I hope at least he has not deceived us. Haggerston has our instructions, and everything will be finished in a week. They will then join his regiment, unless they are invited to Longbourn first. And I understand from Mrs. Gardiner that my niece is very eager to see you all before she leaves the south.\u201d\"}"
