          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"088d1cf74005835ba3262934\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Had she known that her sister meant to wrench her away from prospects and realities of so particular a kind, what feelings might she have had? Only her mother could have understood them, and even she would probably have felt much the same. Lydia\u2019s going to Brighton was all that could still comfort her against the melancholy certainty that her husband never intended to go there himself.\\n\\nBut they were wholly unaware of what had occurred; and their transports went on, with scarcely any break, right up to the day Lydia left home.\\n\\nElizabeth was now to meet Mr. Wickham for the last time. Since her return she had been much in his company, so that agitation had pretty well subsided; the fluttering of former partiality was gone entirely. She had even learned to read, in the very softness that had at first charmed her, a studied sameness\u2014something meant to please, yet fitted to disgust and weary. In his conduct toward herself, too, she found a new cause of offence; for the readiness he soon showed to resume those attentions which had distinguished the beginning of their acquaintance could only, after what had since taken place, serve to irritate her. She cared no longer for him when she saw herself chosen as the mark of such empty and trifling gallantry; and though she kept her feelings down, she could not help perceiving the rebuke implied in his belief that, however long his attentions had been withheld, and for whatever reason, her vanity would still be flattered, and her preference secured, whenever he chose to renew them.\\n\\nOn the very last day that the regiment remained in Meryton, he dined, with the other officers, at Longbourn; and so little was Elizabeth disposed to part from him in ill-humour, that when he asked how her time had been spent at Hunsford, she told him that Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy had both passed three weeks at Rosings, and asked whether he knew the former.\\n\\nHe started, and looked surprised, displeased, and alarmed; but after a brief recollection, and with a smile returning, he answered that he had seen him often before. Then, having remarked that he was a very gentlemanlike man, he asked her how she had liked him. Her reply was warmly favourable. With an air of indifference, he soon added, \u201cHow long did you say that he was at Rosings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNearly three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you saw him frequently?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, almost every day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis manners are very different from his cousin\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, very different; but I think Mr. Darcy improves on acquaintance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u201d cried Wickham, with a look that did not escape her. \u201cAnd pray may I ask\u2014\u201d but stopping himself, he continued in a lighter tone, \u201cIs it in address that he improves?\u201d\"}"
