          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"70276986566be6b76c9aa7b1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They was bringing along a very fine-looking old gentleman, and a very fine-looking younger one too, with his right arm in a sling. And, my souls, how the crowd yelled and carried on\u2014laughing and keeping it up. Still, I didn\u2019t see any real joke in it, and I figured it would put the duke and the king out some to have to look at it. I thought they\u2019d go pale. But no\u2014none of them went pale. The duke never showed the least sign that he suspected anything; he just kept wandering around, cooing and acting pleased, as happy and satisfied as a jug that\u2019s spilling out buttermilk. As for the king, he just stared and stared down at the new arrivals, looking so sorrowful, like the thought of such frauds and rascals in the world gave him a stomach-ache right in his own heart. Oh, he played it admirable. A good many of the leading folks gathered close to the king, so he could see they were on his side. The old gentleman who\u2019d just come looked as if he was puzzled clear through. Pretty soon he started to talk, and I could tell at once he pronounced himself like an Englishman\u2014though it wasn\u2019t the king\u2019s way, even if the king\u2019s imitation was pretty good. I can\u2019t set down the old gent\u2019s exact words, and I can\u2019t copy his voice, but he turned to the crowd and said something pretty much like this:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a surprise to me that I wasn\u2019t expecting; and I\u2019ll admit, candid and plain, I\u2019m not in the best shape to meet it and answer it. For my brother and me have had misfortunes. He\u2019s broke his arm, and our baggage got left off at a town above here last night, by mistake. I am Peter Wilks\u2019 brother Harvey, and this is his brother William, who can\u2019t hear nor speak\u2014and can\u2019t even make signs to amount to much, seeing as he\u2019s only got one hand to work them with. We are who we say we are; and in a day or two, when I get the baggage, I can prove it. But until then I won\u2019t say anything more\u2014only go to the hotel and wait.\u201d\\n\\nSo he and the new dummy started off. The king laughed and blustered out:\\n\\n\u201cBroke his arm\u2014_very_ likely, _ain\u2019t_ it?\u2014and mighty convenient, too, for a fraud that has to make signs and hasn\u2019t learned how. Lost their baggage! That\u2019s _mighty_ fine\u2014and mighty clever\u2014under the _circumstances_!\u201d\\n\\nHe laughed again, and everybody else did too, except three or four\u2014maybe half a dozen. Among them was that doctor. Another was a sharp-looking gentleman with a carpet-bag of the old-fashioned sort, made out of carpet-stuff, who\u2019d just come off the steamboat and was talking to him in a low voice, looking toward the king now and then and nodding his head. That was Levi Bell, the lawyer that had gone up to Louisville. And there was also a big rough husky who\u2019d come along and listened to everything the old gentleman said, and was listening to the king now.\"}"
