{"request_id":"c33197bbc37233b109ca0834","rewritten_text":"The only point that really worried him was who he should get rid of; for he was not so naïve as to miss that murder, much like the Pagan world’s religions, calls for both a victim and a priest. Since he was no genius, he had no enemies to speak of, and he felt—quite firmly—that this was not the moment to indulge any petty grudge or personal dislike. The task he had taken up was one of solemn weight and seriousness. He therefore set down the names of his friends and relations on a sheet of notepaper, and after giving it careful thought, chose Lady Clementina Beauchamp—a dear old lady who lived in Curzon Street, and who, on his mother’s side, was his second cousin. He had always been fond of Lady Clem, as everyone called her, and as he himself was very well off, having come into all Lord Rugby’s property when he came of age, there was no chance of his gaining any common, vulgar profit from her death. Indeed, the more he weighed it, the more she struck him as the proper person; and, believing that any delay would be unfair to Sybil, he resolved to make his arrangements at once.

First, naturally, he had to settle with the cheiromantist. So he sat down at a small Sheraton writing-table by the window, wrote out a cheque for £105, made payable to Mr. Septimus Podgers, and, putting it in an envelope, told his valet to take it to West Moon Street. He then rang for his hansom from the stables and began dressing to go out. As he stepped out of the room, he glanced back at Sybil Merton’s photograph and swore that, whatever happened, he would never let her know what he was doing for her sake; the secret of his self-sacrifice would stay, hidden in his heart, forever.

On his way toward the Buckingham, he stopped at a florist’s and sent Sybil a handsome basket of narcissus—white petals, and pheasants’ eyes staring out. When he reached the club, he went straight to the library, rang the bell, and instructed the waiter to bring him a lemon-and-soda and a book on Toxicology. He had decided, without much wavering, that poison was the best course for this awkward business. Anything that smacked of direct violence was profoundly repugnant to him; and, besides, he was determined not to murder Lady Clementina in any manner that might draw public notice. He loathed the thought of being paraded at Lady Windermere’s, or of seeing his name printed in the paragraphs of vulgar society—newspapers."}

