          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d1d8d3537396066634670965\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Their arrival kindled a kind of fire in Saint Antoine\u2019s breast, spreading quickly as they came, stirring and flickering in flames of faces at nearly every door and window. Still, no one followed them, and when they entered the wine-shop no man spoke, though the eyes of every man there were fixed on them.\\n\\n\u201cGood day, gentlemen!\u201d said Monsieur Defarge.\\n\\nIt may have been the cue to loosen the general tongue. It brought an answering chorus of \u201cGood day!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe weather is bad, gentlemen,\u201d said Defarge, shaking his head.\\n\\nAt that, every man looked at his neighbor, then all lowered their eyes and sat in silence. Except for one man, who rose and went out.\\n\\n\u201cMy wife,\u201d said Defarge aloud, addressing Madame Defarge, \u201cI\u2019ve traveled certain leagues with this good road-mender, called Jacques. I met him\u2014by accident\u2014day and a half\u2019s journey out of Paris. He\u2019s a good child, this road-mender, called Jacques. Give him something to drink!\u201d\\n\\nA second man rose and went out. Madame Defarge set wine before the road-mender called Jacques, who tipped his blue cap to the company and drank. In the breast of his blouse he carried some coarse dark bread; he ate it in between, and sat munching and drinking near Madame Defarge\u2019s counter. A third man rose and went out.\\n\\nDefarge refreshed himself with a draught of wine, but he took less than was offered to the stranger, since he was a man for whom such things were no rarity. He stood waiting until the countryman had finished his breakfast. He did not look at anyone present, and no one looked at him now\u2014not even Madame Defarge, who had taken up her knitting and was working.\\n\\n\u201cHave you finished your meal, friend?\u201d he asked, at the right moment.\\n\\n\u201cYes, thank you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen come! You\u2019ll see the room I told you you could use. It will suit you wonderfully.\u201d\\n\\nFrom the wine-shop into the street, from the street into a courtyard, from the courtyard up a steep staircase, from the staircase into a garret\u2014once the garret where a white-haired man had sat on a low bench, stooping forward and very busy, making shoes.\\n\\nNo white-haired man was there now; but the three men were there who had gone out of the wine-shop one by one. And between them and the white-haired man far off was the one small link they had once seen him through, peering in at him through the cracks in the wall.\\n\\nDefarge closed the door carefully and spoke in a low voice:\\n\\n\u201cJacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three! This is the witness encountered by appointment, by me, Jacques Four.\u201d\"}"
