          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e7114c3a64e3fd3e79b23e07\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou won\u2019t, won\u2019t you? Oh, you precious little flat!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs it naughty, isn\u2019t it, Oliver?\u201d Charley Bates asked. \u201cHe\u2019ll end up getting scragged, won\u2019t he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know what that means,\u201d Oliver said.\\n\\n\u201cSomething like that, old fellow,\u201d Charley replied. As he spoke, Master Bates grabbed the end of his neckerchief. He held it up in the air, then dropped his head onto his shoulder and jerked his head in a strange way, making a curious sound with his teeth. It was a lively show that made it clear that scragging and hanging were the same thing.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s what it means,\u201d Charley said. \u201cLook how he stares, Jack! I\u2019ve never seen such good company as that boy. He\u2019ll be the death of me, I just know it.\u201d Master Charley Bates laughed again, then went back to his pipe, tears in his eyes.\\n\\n\u201cYou were brought up badly,\u201d the Dodger said, looking at Oliver\u2019s polished boots with satisfaction. \u201cFagin will turn you into something, but if he doesn\u2019t, you\u2019ll be the first person he ever had who turned out useless. You\u2019d better start right away, because you\u2019ll be in the trade long before you think about it. And you\u2019re wasting time, Oliver.\u201d\\n\\nMaster Bates added his own moral warnings. When those were done, he and his friend Mr. Dawkins went on to describe, in glowing terms, all the pleasures that came with the life they lived. They kept slipping in hints to Oliver that the best thing he could do was to get Fagin\u2019s favor as soon as possible, using the same methods they had used to win it.\\n\\n\u201cAnd always put this in your pipe, Nolly,\u201d the Dodger said, as the Jew was heard unlocking the door upstairs. \u201cIf you don\u2019t take fogels and tickers\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the point of talking like that?\u201d Master Bates interrupted. \u201cHe doesn\u2019t know what you mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf you don\u2019t take pocket-handkechers and watches,\u201d the Dodger said, making his words match Oliver\u2019s understanding, \u201csome other fellow will. Then the guys who lose them will be worse off, and you\u2019ll be worse off too. And nobody will be any better, except the people who get them\u2014and you\u2019ve got just as much right to them as they have.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course, of course!\u201d the Jew said. He had come in without Oliver noticing. \u201cIt all comes down to a nutshell, my dear. Take the Dodger\u2019s word for it. Ha! ha! ha! He knows the whole lesson of his trade.\u201d\\n\\nThe old man rubbed his hands together with delight as he agreed with the Dodger\u2019s reasoning, and he chuckled happily at how well his pupil understood.\\n\\nThe talk didn\u2019t go any further then, because the Jew went back home with Miss Betsy, and with a gentleman Oliver had never seen before. The Dodger called him Tom Chitling. After lingering on the stairs to exchange a few polite flirtations with the lady, he finally appeared.\"}"
