          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6298c0ac7465b28a3824aa95\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Watching him in these moods, I often found myself thinking about an old idea called the Bi-Part Soul. I would even amuse myself with the thought of a double Dupin: one part creative, the other part able to solve things.\\n\\nDon\u2019t think, though, that what I\u2019ve just said is me explaining a mystery or writing a romance. Everything I described about the Frenchman was simply the result of an excited mind, or maybe a sick one. Still, the best way to understand what his remarks were like at those moments is to give an example.\\n\\nOne night we walked together down a long, dirty street near the Palais Royal. Both of us seemed lost in thought, and for at least fifteen minutes neither of us said a word. Then Dupin suddenly spoke.\\n\\n\u201cHe is a very little fellow, that\u2019s true, and he\u2019d do better at the Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere can be no doubt of that,\u201d I answered, without thinking. At first I didn\u2019t notice anything strange about how he had suddenly joined my thoughts. I had been so absorbed in reflection. But then, right away, I caught myself, and my surprise was intense.\\n\\n\u201cDupin,\u201d I said seriously, \u201cthis is beyond me. I don\u2019t hesitate to say I\u2019m amazed. I can hardly believe what I\u2019m hearing. How could you possibly know I was thinking of\u2014?\u201d I stopped to make sure, as clearly as I could, that he really knew who I meant.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014Chantilly,\u201d he said. \u201cWhy do you pause? You were telling yourself that his small size made him unsuitable for tragedy.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly what I had been thinking about. Chantilly had once been a cobbler from the Rue St. Denis. After he became obsessed with the stage, he tried to play Xerxes in Cr\u00e9billon\u2019s tragedy. He was mocked for it, and everyone made fun of him.\\n\\n\u201cTell me, for Heaven\u2019s sake,\u201d I cried, \u201cthe method\u2014if there is any method\u2014by which you were able to read my mind in this matter.\u201d In truth, I was even more shocked than I wanted to admit.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the fruiterer,\u201d my friend replied. \u201cHe led you to the conclusion that the man who mends soles wasn\u2019t tall enough for Xerxes, and for that sort of role.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fruiterer! You surprise me. I don\u2019t know any fruiterer at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe man who bumped into you when we entered the street,\u201d Dupin said. \u201cIt might have been fifteen minutes ago.\u201d\\n\\nNow I remembered it. As we moved from the Rue C\u2014\u2014 into the street where we were standing, a fruit seller carrying a large basket of apples on his head had nearly knocked me down by accident. But I still couldn\u2019t understand what that had to do with Chantilly.\"}"
