          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"15b5dcb9ac5260df3f9579c2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was at Warwick Castle that I first ran into the odd stranger I\u2019m about to tell you of. Three things drew me to him: a plain, openhearted straightforwardness; a remarkable ease with ancient armor; and the calm comfort of his presence\u2014because he did all the talking. We drifted together, as quiet folk do, at the back of the little group being led through, and he immediately began saying things that caught my attention. As he spoke on, in a soft, pleasant, flowing way, it was as though he slipped, little by little, out of this world and its time, and into some far-off age and a country long since forgotten; and so, almost without my noticing, he spun a spell around me until I felt myself moving among the specters and shadows, the dust and mildew of a gray antiquity, conversing with a relic of it. Just as I might speak of my closest friends or foes, or the neighbors I know best, he spoke of Sir Bedivere, Sir Bors de Ganis, Sir Launcelot of the Lake, Sir Galahad, and all the other great names of the Round Table\u2014and how old, old, unspeakably old, and worn, faded, dry, and musty and timeworn he seemed to grow as he went on.\\n\\nAt last he turned to me, speaking as casually as one might mention the weather, or any other everyday matter\u2014\\n\\n\u201cDo you know about the transmigration of souls? Do you know about the transposition of epochs\u2014and bodies?\u201d\\n\\nI told him I hadn\u2019t heard of it. He showed so little interest\u2014much as people do when they talk about the weather\u2014that he didn\u2019t seem to notice whether I answered him or not. There was a brief half-moment of silence, promptly broken by the droning voice of the paid cicerone:\\n\\n\u201cAncient hauberk, date of the sixth century, time of King Arthur and the Round Table; said to have belonged to the knight Sir Sagramor le Desirous; observe the round hole through the chain-mail in the left breast; can\u2019t be accounted for; supposed to have been done with a bullet since invention of firearms\u2014perhaps maliciously by Cromwell\u2019s soldiers.\u201d\\n\\nMy acquaintance smiled\u2014not with any modern sort of smile, but one that must have fallen out of common use many, many centuries ago\u2014and muttered, as if to himself:\\n\\n\u201cWit ye well, _I saw it done_.\u201d Then, after a pause, he added: \u201cI did it myself.\u201d\\n\\nBefore I could recover from the electric shock of that remark, he was gone.\\n\\nThat whole evening I sat by the fire at the Warwick Arms, sunk in a dream of those earlier days, while rain struck the windows and the wind battered the eaves and corners. Now and then I dipped into old Sir Thomas Malory\u2019s spellbinding book, taking my fill from its rich table of marvels and adventures, breathing in the perfume of its obsolete names, and dreaming again.\"}"
