          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e5a83861ff3f13fa82140815\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was the beginning of February. Anne, after spending a month in Bath, was growing very eager for news from Uppercross and Lyme. She wanted to know much more than Mary had told her. It had been three weeks since she had heard anything at all. She only knew that Henrietta was back at home, and that Louisa, though thought to be recovering quickly, was still in Lyme. One evening, while she was thinking of them all very intensely, Mary\u2019s letter arrived\u2014thicker than usual. To make the pleasure and surprise even greater, it came with Admiral and Mrs. Croft\u2019s compliments.\\n\\nThe Crofts must be in Bath! That was something that interested her. They were people her heart naturally warmed to.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is this?\u201d Sir Walter exclaimed. \u201cThe Crofts have come to Bath? The Crofts who rent Kellynch? What have they brought you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA letter from Uppercross Cottage, Sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Those letters are convenient passports. They get you an introduction. I would have visited Admiral Croft anyway. I know what I owe my tenant.\u201d\\n\\nAnne could not listen any longer. She could not even say how the Admiral\u2019s complexion escaped notice; her own letter took over her attention. It had been written several days earlier.\\n\\n\u201cFebruary 1st.\\n\\n\u201cMY DEAR ANNE,\\n\\n\u201cI make no apology for my silence, because I know how little people care about letters in a place like Bath. You must be much too happy to think about Uppercross, which, as you know, gives you very little to write about. We have had a very dull Christmas. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove have not had a single dinner party during the holidays. I do not count the Hayters as anyone. The holidays are over at last. I believe no children ever had such long ones. I am sure I did not.\\n\\n\u201cThe house was cleared yesterday, except for the little Harvilles; but you will be surprised to hear they have never gone home. Mrs. Harville must be an odd mother to keep them so long. I do not understand it. In my opinion, the children are not at all nice; but Mrs. Musgrove seems to like them just as much, if not more, than her grandchildren.\\n\\n\u201cWhat dreadful weather we have had! It may not be felt in Bath, with your nice pavements, but in the country it matters a great deal. I have not had anyone call on me since the second week in January, except Charles Hayter, who has been calling much more often than was welcome. Between ourselves, I think it a pity Henrietta did not stay at Lyme as long as Louisa did. It would have kept her a little out of his way.\u201d\"}"
