          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"deda9706bf783ed162f6bedf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Wickham was so pleased with their conversation that he never brought it up again, and he did not bother or upset his dear sister Elizabeth by talking about it. Elizabeth was glad, because she felt she had said enough to keep him quiet.\\n\\nThe day when he and Lydia were going to leave soon arrived. Mrs. Bennet had to accept the separation, and since her husband had no interest in the plan of having all of them go to Newcastle, it was likely to last at least a year.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear Lydia,\u201d she cried, \u201cwhen shall we meet again?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Lord! I don\u2019t know. Maybe not for two or three years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWrite to me very often, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAs often as I can. But you know married women don\u2019t have much time for writing. My sisters can write to me. They will have nothing else to do.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Wickham\u2019s goodbyes were much warmer than his wife\u2019s. He smiled, looked handsome, and said many sweet things.\\n\\n\u201cHe is as fine a fellow,\u201d said Mr. Bennet as soon as they were out of the house. \u201cHe flirts and acts silly, and makes love to all of us. I\u2019m extremely proud of him. I challenge even Sir William Lucas to produce a better son-in-law.\u201d\\n\\nAfter her daughter left, Mrs. Bennet felt very dull for several days.\\n\\n\u201cI often think,\u201d she said, \u201cthat nothing is as hard as being separated from your friends. Without them, you feel so lonely.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is what happens, you see, madam, when you marry a daughter,\u201d Elizabeth replied. \u201cIt should make you feel better about the other four, since they are still single.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s not like that. Lydia isn\u2019t leaving me because she is married. She is leaving only because her husband\u2019s regiment is so far away. If it were nearer, she wouldn\u2019t have gone so soon.\u201d\\n\\nStill, the gloomy mood this caused her did not last long. Her mind soon opened again to the feeling of hope, stirred by some new news that was starting to spread. The housekeeper at Netherfield had been told to get ready for the arrival of her master. He was coming down in a day or two to stay there for several weeks. Mrs. Bennet became restless at once. She looked at Jane, smiled, and shook her head in turn.\\n\\n\u201cWell, well, so Mr. Bingley is coming down, sister,\u201d (Mrs. Philips had first told her). \u201cWell, that\u2019s good news. Not that I care much, though. He has nothing to do with us, you know, and I\u2019m sure I never want to see him again. But if he wants to come to Netherfield, he\u2019s very welcome to do so.\u201d\"}"
