          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e50ed746376b4de659049b7f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Men marry because they are tired; women, because they are curious. Either way, they end up disappointed.\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think I\u2019m likely to marry, Harry. I\u2019m too much in love. That\u2019s one of your sayings. I\u2019m following it, just like I follow everything you say.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho are you in love with?\u201d Lord Henry asked after a pause.\\n\\n\u201cWith an actress,\u201d Dorian Gray said, blushing.\\n\\nLord Henry shrugged. \u201cThat\u2019s a pretty ordinary beginning.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou wouldn\u2019t say that if you\u2019d seen her, Harry.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho is she?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHer name is Sibyl Vane.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve never heard of her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo one has. But people will know her someday. She\u2019s a genius.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear boy, no woman is a genius. Women are meant to be decorative. They never have anything important to say, but they say it in a charming way. Women show how matter wins over the mind, just as men show how the mind wins over morals.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHarry, how can you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Dorian, it\u2019s true. I\u2019m thinking about women right now, so I should know. It isn\u2019t as complicated as I first thought. In the end, there are only two kinds of women: plain ones and colored ones. Plain women are useful. If you want to build a reputation for respectability, you only need to take them to supper. The other women are very charming. But they make one mistake. They paint their faces to look younger. Our grandmothers painted to sound brilliant. Rouge and esprit used to go together. That\u2019s all changed now. As long as a woman can look ten years younger than her own daughter, she\u2019s satisfied. And when it comes to conversation, there are only five women in London worth listening to, and two of them can\u2019t be allowed into decent society. Still, tell me about your genius. How long have you known her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, Harry, your opinions frighten me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t worry about that. How long have you known her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAbout three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd where did you meet her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ll tell you, Harry, but you must promise not to be unkind about it. After all, it wouldn\u2019t have happened if I hadn\u2019t met you. You filled me with a wild desire to know everything about life. For days after I met you, something seemed to pulse in my veins. When I sat around in the park or walked down Piccadilly, I would look at everyone who passed and wonder, with a crazy curiosity, what kind of lives they lived. Some of them fascinated me. Others filled me with fear. There was an exquisite poison in the air. I had a passion for sensations....\"}"
