          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3c3b1bc635d49cc5e93d6ebb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now turn to my own story. In the early part of my life there is little to tell, and I will be brief; but I must be allowed to dwell a little on the years of my childhood, so it may be clear how, when one hope failed, all life became a blank\u2014and how, when the only affection I was allowed to cherish was taken from me, my existence was extinguished with it.\\n\\nI have said that my aunt was very unlike my father. I believe that, without the slightest hint of a bad heart, she had the coldest nature that ever filled a human breast: she was completely incapable of affection. She took me under her protection because she thought it was her duty; but she had lived too long alone, undisturbed by the noise and chatter of children, to tolerate me disturbing her peace. She had never married; and for the last five years she had lived entirely by herself on an estate that had passed to her through her mother, on the shores of Loch Lomond in Scotland. In his letters, my father had expressed a wish that she should live with me at his family mansion, which stood in a beautiful part of the country near Richmond in Yorkshire. She would not agree to this, but once she had arranged the affairs that her brother\u2019s departure had left in her care, she left England and took me with her to her Scottish estate.\\n\\nThe care of me, first as a baby and later until I reached my eighth year, fell to a servant of my mother\u2019s, who had accompanied us in our retirement for that purpose. I was placed in a remote part of the house, and I only saw my aunt at set times. These came twice a day: once around noon, when she came to my nursery, and once after her dinner, when I was taken to her. She never caressed me, and while I stayed in the room she seemed to fear that I might annoy her with some childish whim. My good nurse always prepared me with the greatest care before she would take me into the parlour\u2014and the fear my aunt\u2019s cold looks and few, tightly controlled words inspired was so strong that I seldom failed to follow her lessons, or betrayed the exemplary quiet I was taught to maintain during those brief visits.\\n\\nUnder my good nurse\u2019s care, I ran wild about our park and the neighboring fields. From my earliest years, I showed the deepest love, and with it the greatest sensitivity of temperament. I cannot say with what passion I loved everything, even the inanimate objects that surrounded me.\"}"
