          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6d4842257a4d83907864a25c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Had she known that her sister meant to tear her away from prospects and realities like these, what would her feelings have been? Only her mother could have understood them, and she might have felt nearly the same. Lydia\u2019s going to Brighton was all that consoled her for the melancholy certainty that her husband never intended to go there himself.\\n\\nBut they were completely unaware of what had happened; and their excitement continued, with hardly any break, right up to the day Lydia left home.\\n\\nElizabeth was now going to see Mr. Wickham for the last time. Since she had been in his company so often after her return, her agitation had mostly settled; the earlier agitation from partiality was entirely gone. She had even learned to recognize, in the very gentleness that had first delighted her, an affectation and a sameness that disgusted and tired her. What he did toward her now was also a fresh source of displeasure; for the inclination he soon showed to renew those attentions that had marked the beginning of their acquaintance could only, after everything that had happened since, provoke her. She lost all concern for him when she found herself chosen as the target of such idle, frivolous gallantry; and while she kept it firmly under control, she couldn\u2019t help feeling the rebuke in his belief that, no matter how long\u2014no matter for whatever reason\u2014his attentions had been withdrawn, her vanity would be pleased and her preference secured at any time by their renewal.\\n\\nOn the very last day the regiment remained in Meryton, he dined, with other officers, at Longbourn; and so little was Elizabeth inclined to part from him in anything but good humor that, when he asked how her time had passed at Hunsford, she told him that Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy had both spent three weeks at Rosings, and asked if he knew the former.\\n\\nHe looked surprised, displeased, and alarmed; but after a moment\u2019s recollection, and with a returning smile, he replied that he had seen him often before. Then, after remarking that he was a very gentlemanlike man, he asked her how she had liked him. Her answer was warmly in his favor. With an air of indifference, he soon afterward added, \u201cHow long did you say that he was at Rosings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNearly three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you saw him frequently?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, almost every day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis manners are very different from his cousin\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, very different; but I think Mr. Darcy improves with acquaintance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u201d cried Wickham, with a look that didn\u2019t escape her. \u201cAnd may I ask\u2014\u201d but stopping himself, he continued in a lighter tone, \u201cIs it in address that he improves?\u201d\"}"
