          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0e5b092fc9f64c159697b33c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I am also told by Mrs. Otis\u2014who, I should add, is no small authority on matters of Art, having once had the chance to spend a few winters in Boston\u2014that these gems are worth a great deal of money, and that if they were put up for sale they would command a handsome sum. Given that, Lord Canterville, I trust you will see how utterly impossible it would be for me to let any member of my family keep them; indeed, all such empty baubles and toys, however well they might suit the standing of the British aristocracy, would be quite out of place with people raised on the stern\u2014and, as I believe, immortal\u2014rules of republican plainness. I ought perhaps to add that Virginia is very keen for you to let her keep the box as a memento of your unfortunate, though misguided, ancestor. Since it is extremely old and therefore in a poor state of repair, you may think it best to grant her request. For my part, I admit I\u2019m rather astonished to hear one of my own children show sympathy for anything medieval, in any shape; I can only make sense of it by remembering that Virginia was born in one of your London suburbs not long after Mrs. Otis came back from a journey to Athens.\\n\\nLord Canterville listened with great seriousness to the respectable Minister\u2019s remarks, tugging at his grey moustache from time to time to conceal an involuntary smile; and when Mr. Otis finished, he took him warmly by the hand and said, \u2018My dear sir, your charming little daughter did my unlucky ancestor, Sir Simon, a very real service, and I and my household owe her a great deal for her extraordinary courage and spirit. The jewels are plainly hers; and, egad, if I were cruel enough to snatch them from her, that wicked old fellow would be out of his grave in a fortnight, giving me the devil of a time. As for calling them heirlooms, nothing deserves that name unless it is set down in a will or some legal paper, and the fact of these jewels has never been known. I can assure you I have no more right to them than your butler has; and when Miss Virginia grows up, I daresay she\u2019ll be glad to have pretty things to wear. Besides, Mr. Otis, you seem to forget that you took the furniture and the ghost at a valuation, and whatever belonged to the ghost passed straight into your hands\u2014because, however lively Sir Simon may have been in the corridor at night, in law he was truly dead, and you bought his property.\u2019\"}"
