{"request_id":"38dcab82b9c7a7538cb94c06","rewritten_text":"In the brief time he had been there, Emma had hardly had the chance to see him at all; yet it was just enough to feel that their first meeting was already done, and to form the notion that the mixture of pique and pretension had not improved him, but only spread itself more plainly over his manner. Indeed, she was beginning to question whether she had ever found him agreeable. His presence seemed so tightly bound up with feelings that were anything but pleasant, that—unless she looked at it in a moral way, as a kind of penance, a lesson, and a source of profitable mortification to her own thoughts—she would have been glad to know she would never have to see him again. She wished him well; but he caused her pain, and his comfort, twenty miles away, would give her far more satisfaction.

Still, his continued stay in Highbury must be eased by his marriage. It would stop many vain attentions, and smooth away many awkwardnesses. A Mrs. Elton would serve as a ready excuse for any change in how people met and spoke; whatever closeness had existed might fall away without anyone remarking it. It would be, in effect, the start of their life of civility all over again.

As for the lady herself, Emma thought very little of her. She was, no doubt, good enough for Mr. Elton; capable enough for Highbury—handsome enough, too—to look plain, probably, beside Harriet. As to the connection, Emma felt perfectly at ease: she was persuaded that, for all his own loud claims and his contempt for Harriet, he had done nothing. On that point, the truth seemed within reach. What she was might be uncertain; but who she was could be found out. And leaving aside the 10,000 l., it did not strike her that she was in any way superior to Harriet. She brought no name, no blood, no family alliance. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol merchant—of course he must be called so; but since the profits of his mercantile life appeared so very moderate, it was not unreasonable to suppose that the dignity of his line of trade had been moderate as well. Each winter she had been accustomed to spend part of the time in Bath; but Bristol was her home, the very heart of Bristol. Though her father and mother had died some years before, an uncle remained—in the law line—nothing more distinctly honourable being claimed for him than that he was in the law line; and with him the daughter had lived. Emma imagined him to be the drudge of some attorney, and too dull to rise. And all the consequence of the connection seemed to rest on the elder sister, who was very well married—to a gentleman in a great way, near Bristol, who kept two carriages. That was the final flourish of Miss Hawkins’s story; that was the whole glory of Miss Hawkins."}

