          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f8f005d6b99634c9f79170b2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cDo you mean\u2014Mr. Knightley?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo be sure I do. I could never have imagined anyone else\u2014and that is why I thought you understood. When we spoke of him, it was as plain as possible.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot quite,\u201d returned Emma, keeping her composure with an effort; \u201cfor all that you then said, it seemed to me to point to someone different. I could almost say that you had _named_ Mr. Frank Churchill. I am certain that what was said of the service Mr. Frank Churchill rendered you\u2014protecting you from the gipsies\u2014was what you referred to.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Miss Woodhouse, how you do forget!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Harriet, I remember perfectly well the substance of what I told you. I said I did not wonder at your attachment; that, considering the service he had done you, it was perfectly natural. You agreed to it, speaking very warmly of your sense of that service, and even mentioning the feelings you had when he came forward to your rescue. The whole impression of it is still strong in my mind.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, dear,\u201d cried Harriet, \u201cnow I remember what you mean; but I was thinking of something quite different then. It was not the gipsies\u2014it was not Mr. Frank Churchill I meant. No! (with some spirit) I was thinking of a far more precious circumstance\u2014of Mr. Knightley\u2019s coming to ask me to dance, when Mr. Elton would not rise with me, and when there was no other partner in the room. That was the kindness; that was the noble benevolence and generosity; that was the service which first made me feel how much superior he was to every other creature upon earth.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood God!\u201d cried Emma. \u201cThis is the most unfortunate\u2014most deplorable mistake!\u2014What is to be done?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou would not have encouraged me, then, if you had understood me. At least, however, I cannot be any worse off than I should have been, if the other had been the person; and now\u2014it _is_ possible\u2014\u201d\\n\\nShe stopped for a few moments. Emma could not answer.\\n\\n\u201cI do not wonder, Miss Woodhouse,\u201d she went on, \u201cthat you should feel such a difference between the two, whether in regard to me or to anyone else. You must think one five hundred million times more highly of me than the other. But I hope, Miss Woodhouse, that\u2014supposing\u2014that if\u2014strange as it may seem\u2014. You know those were your own words, and that _more_ wonderful things than this have happened: matches of far greater disparity than there is between Mr. Frank Churchill and me. So it seems as if something like this might have occurred before; and if I should be so fortunate\u2014beyond all expression\u2014as to\u2014if Mr. Knightley should really\u2014if _he_ does not mind the disparity, I hope, dear Miss Woodhouse, you will not set yourself against it, nor try to raise difficulties.\u201d\"}"
