          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7223f82ec9ad1dfc5c790a02\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI don\u2019t have the nerves for that kind of thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut could you be comfortable yourself, spending the whole evening away from the poor boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; you see his papa can, and why shouldn\u2019t I? Jemima is so careful, and she could send us word every hour about how he is. I really think Charles might as well have told his father that we would all come. I\u2019m not more alarmed about little Charles now than he is. I was dreadfully alarmed yesterday, but the situation is very different today.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, if you don\u2019t think it\u2019s too late to give notice for yourself, suppose you were to go as well as your husband. Leave little Charles to my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove can\u2019t think it wrong while I stay with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you serious?\u201d Mary cried, her eyes brightening. \u201cDear me! That\u2019s a very good idea\u2014an excellent one, truly. Of course, I might as well go as not, because I\u2019m of no use at home\u2014am I? It only wears me down. You, who don\u2019t have a mother\u2019s feelings, are a much better choice. You can get little Charles to do anything; he always listens to you at a word. It will be a great deal better than leaving him only with Jemima. Oh, I shall certainly go; I\u2019m sure I ought to, if I can, just as much as Charles, because they want me to be introduced to Captain Wentworth, and I don\u2019t mind being left alone. What a wonderful thought of yours, indeed, Anne. I\u2019ll go tell Charles and get ready right away. You can send for us at a moment\u2019s notice if anything is wrong, but I dare say there will be nothing to alarm you. I wouldn\u2019t go, you can be sure, if I didn\u2019t feel fairly at ease about my dear child.\u201d\\n\\nThe next moment she was tapping at her husband\u2019s dressing-room door, and as Anne followed her upstairs, she arrived in time to hear the whole conversation. It began with Mary saying, in a tone of great excitement\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI mean to go with you, Charles, because I\u2019m no more use at home than you are. If I were to shut myself up forever with the child, I wouldn\u2019t be able to persuade him to do anything he didn\u2019t like. Anne will stay; Anne will take care of him at home. It\u2019s Anne\u2019s own idea, so I\u2019ll go with you, which will be much better, because I haven\u2019t dined at the other house since Tuesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is very kind of Anne,\u201d her husband answered, \u201cand I should be very glad to have you go; but it seems rather hard that she should be left at home by herself, to nurse our sick child.\u201d\"}"
