          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e260fe24357dec325bb149d1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYes\u2014burglar\u2019s tools. That seems to take a great deal off your mind. But what made you change your tune? What did you suppose we\u2019d turned up?\u201d\\n\\nHuck was in a tight spot\u2014the questioning look was fixed on him\u2014he would have given anything for something that sounded like a sensible answer\u2014nothing came to him. The inquiring gaze kept boring deeper and deeper; a foolish reply slipped out\u2014there wasn\u2019t time to measure it, so, taking a chance, he said it, weakly:\\n\\n\u201cSunday-school books, maybe.\u201d\\n\\nPoor Huck was too upset to grin, but the old man burst into loud, hearty laughter, shook himself from head to foot as if the whole thing were a joke, and wound up by declaring that such laughter was money in a man\u2019s pocket, because it cut down the doctor\u2019s bill, same as anything. Then he added:\\n\\n\u201cPoor old fellow\u2014you\u2019re pale and worn out\u2014you ain\u2019t well at all; no wonder you\u2019re a little flighty and out of sorts. But you\u2019ll pull through. Rest and sleep will set you right, I hope.\u201d\\n\\nHuck was annoyed that he\u2019d been such a fool and shown such a suspicious excitement, for the moment he\u2019d heard what was being said at the widow\u2019s stile he had dropped the notion that the parcel brought from the tavern was the treasure. He had only meant that it wasn\u2019t the treasure\u2014he hadn\u2019t known it wasn\u2019t\u2014and so the idea of a captured bundle had been too much for his self-control. Still, on the whole, he felt glad the little business had happened, because now he knew for certain that that bundle was not the bundle, and his mind was at ease and comfortable. In fact, everything seemed to be drifting the right way again: the treasure must still be in No. 2, the men would be caught and locked up that day, and he and Tom could take the gold that night without any trouble or fear of being interrupted.\\n\\nWhen breakfast was finished, there came a knock at the door. Huck sprang for a hiding-place, because he had no wish to be linked, even in the slightest, with what had happened. The Welshman admitted several ladies and gentlemen, among them the Widow Douglas, and Huck saw groups of citizens climbing the hill to stare at the stile. So the word had spread. The Welshman had to tell the visitors the story of the night. The widow\u2019s gratitude for her rescue was plain enough.\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t say a word about it, madam. There\u2019s another that you\u2019re more beholden to than you are to me and my boys, perhaps\u2014but he won\u2019t have me mention his name. We wouldn\u2019t have been there but for him.\u201d\"}"
