          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"58c9f95664a231f763525443\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On my return, I found the following letter from my father:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\\n\\n\u201cYou have probably been waiting impatiently for a letter that tells you the date of your return to us. At first, I was tempted to write only a few lines, just to say the day I expected you. But that would be a cruel kindness, and I cannot do it. What would be your surprise, my son, if you were expecting a happy, joyful welcome and instead found tears and misery? And how, Victor, can I tell you about our misfortune? Being away cannot have made you cold to our joys and sorrows. How could I cause pain to my son who has been away so long? I want to prepare you for the terrible news, but I know it is impossible. Even now, your eyes are moving over the page, looking for the words that will bring you these horrible facts.\\n\\n\u201cWilliam is dead!\u2014that sweet child, whose smile warmed and delighted my heart. He is murdered, Victor\u2014so gentle, yet so full of life!\\n\\n\u201cI will not try to comfort you. I will simply explain what happened.\\n\\n\u201cLast Thursday, May 7th, I, my niece, and your two brothers went for a walk in Plainpalais. The evening was warm and calm, so we stayed out longer than usual. It was already getting dark before we thought of going back. Then we realized that William and Ernest, who had gone ahead, were not to be found. So we sat down on a bench and waited for them to return.\\n\\n\u201cSoon Ernest came back. He asked whether we had seen his brother. He said that William had been playing with him, that William had run off to hide, and that he had searched for him in vain. He waited a long time, but William did not come back.\\n\\n\u201cThis made us worry, and we kept looking for him until night fell. Then Elizabeth suggested that he might have returned home. He was not there. We went back again with torches. I could not rest, because I kept thinking that my dear boy had gotten lost and was exposed all night to the cold damp and dew. Elizabeth was in terrible distress too. About five in the morning I found my lovely boy. The night before, I had seen him healthy and active, full of life, and now he lay on the grass, pale and motionless. There was a mark on his neck, the sign of the murderer\u2019s hand.\\n\\n\u201cHe was taken home, and the anguish on my face told Elizabeth what had happened. She was very determined to see the body. At first I tried to stop her, but she would not listen. She went into the room where it lay, quickly examined the neck of the victim, and then, clasping her hands, she cried out, \u2018O God!\u2019\u201d\"}"
