          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0fa8bb0840cd362469570e49\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At the heart of that cleft I noticed a pale mark, though at first I couldn\u2019t tell what it was. I adjusted the telescope\u2019s focus, looked again, and this time recognized it as a human skull.\\n\\n\u201cOn the strength of that discovery I grew so confident that I thought the riddle solved; for the words \u2018main branch, seventh limb, east side\u2019 could only point to where the skull sat upon the tree, while \u2018shoot from the left eye of the death\u2019s head\u2019 allowed, likewise, just one meaning\u2014namely, a search for buried treasure. I saw that the plan was to drop a bullet from the skull\u2019s left eye, and that a bee-line\u2014meaning a straight line\u2014drawn from the nearest point of the trunk through \u2018the shot\u2019 (the spot where the bullet struck), and then carried out for fifty feet, would mark a definite place; and beneath that place, I thought it at least possible that something of value was hidden.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll this,\u201d I said, \u201cis remarkably plain, and though it\u2019s clever, it\u2019s still straightforward and stated without ambiguity. When you left the Bishop\u2019s Hotel, what happened next?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, after I\u2019d taken the tree\u2019s bearings with care, I made for home. The moment I stepped away from \u2018the devil\u2019s seat,\u2019 though, the circular rift disappeared; no matter how I turned afterward, I couldn\u2019t catch so much as a glimpse of it again. The chief ingenuity, as I see it, lies in the fact\u2014repeated trials have convinced me it\u2019s true\u2014that this circular opening can be seen from no other vantage than the narrow ledge on the rock\u2019s face.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn that trip to the \u2018Bishop\u2019s Hotel\u2019 I had Jupiter with me, who, no doubt, had noticed for weeks the change in my manner and took pains not to leave me by myself. But the following day I rose very early, managed to slip away from him, and went into the hills to look for the tree. After a great deal of labor I found it. When I returned home that night, my valet suggested I should be flogged. As for the rest of the affair, I take it you know it as well as I do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI take it,\u201d said I, \u201cthat you missed the spot the first time you tried digging\u2014owing to Jupiter\u2019s blunder in letting the bug fall through the right eye instead of through the left.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly. That slip altered the \u2018shot\u2019 by roughly two and a half inches\u2014that is, the position of the peg nearest the tree. And if the treasure had been beneath the \u2018shot,\u2019 the error would have mattered little; but \u2018the shot,\u2019 along with the nearest point of the tree, were only two points used to set the line of direction. Naturally, even a small beginning error grows as you extend the line, and by the time we\u2019d gone fifty feet, it had thrown us completely off the trail.\u201d\"}"
