content.text:
{"request_id":"d1fd5189f216d911eac2233f","rewritten_text":"They were her life; dearer than any place consecrated to love, dearer than everything else the earth contained. The boys listened with childish delight to our talk of leaving: Clara asked whether we were going to Athens. “It is possible,” I replied; and her face brightened with pleasure. There she would see the tomb of her parents, and the land would be filled with memories of her father’s glory. In silence, but without pause, she had been turning these scenes over in her mind. It was the recollection of them that had changed her infant cheer into seriousness, and had filled her with high, restless thoughts.

There were many dear friends we could not leave behind, humble though they were. There was the spirited, obedient horse Lord Raymond had given his daughter; there was Alfred’s dog and a pet eagle, its sight dimmed by age. But this list of favorites we were taking with us could not be made without grief at the thought of our heavy losses, and a deep sigh for all the things we would have to leave. Tears rushed into Idris’s eyes, while Alfred and Evelyn brought, now a favorite rose tree, now a marble vase beautifully carved, insisting that these must go, and lamenting the fact that we could not take the castle and the forest, the deer and the birds, and all the familiar, cherished objects along with us. “Fond and foolish ones,” I said, “we have lost forever treasures far more precious than these; and we abandon them only to preserve treasures that, compared to them, are nothing. Let us not for a moment forget our purpose and our hope; and they will form an irresistible mound to hold back the flood of our regret for trifles.”

The children were easily distracted, and once again they returned to imagining future amusements. Idris was gone. She had gone to hide her weakness; slipping out of the castle, she went down to the little park and sought solitude so she could indulge her tears there. I found her clinging to an old oak, pressing her rose-colored lips to its rough trunk as her tears fell in abundance, and her sobs and broken outcries could not be suppressed. With overwhelming grief, I saw this beloved one of my heart lost in sorrow. I drew her toward me; and when she felt my kisses on her eyelids, when she felt my arms around her, she came back to awareness of what still remained to her. “You are very kind not to reproach me,” she said. “I weep, and a bitter pang of unbearable sorrow tears at my heart. And yet I am happy; mothers grieve for their children, wives lose their husbands, while you and my children are left to me.”"}

