content.text:
{"request_id":"cc32bb6676234b926173be96","rewritten_text":"Weeks and weeks passed, and I found myself growing ever more taken with you. Then something new came to pass. I had painted you as Paris in delicate armour, and as Adonis with a huntsman’s cloak and a bright boar-spear. With heavy lotus-blossoms crowning your head, you sat on the prow of Adrian’s barge, looking out over the green, turbid Nile. At another moment you had bent over the quiet pool of some Greek woodland, and in the water’s silent silver you had beheld the marvel of your own face. It was, in every way, what art ought to be—unconscious, remote, and ideal.

One day—one of those days I sometimes call fatal—I decided to paint you as you truly are, not dressed in the costume of dead ages, but in your own clothes and in your own time. I cannot say whether it was the realism of the method, or simply the force of your own personality, that brought you to me so plainly, without any haze or veil. But I do know this: as I worked, each speck and film of colour seemed to disclose my secret. I began to fear that others might recognize my idolatry. I felt, Dorian, that I had said too much, that I had poured too much of myself into it. That was when I resolved that the picture should never be exhibited. You were a little vexed, yet you did not understand what it meant to me. Harry, when I spoke to him about it, only laughed. I did not care.

When the picture was finished and after a few days it left my studio, once I had managed to shake off the unbearable pull of its presence, it struck me that I had been foolish to think I had seen anything in it beyond the fact that you were extraordinarily handsome—and that I could paint. Even now I cannot help thinking it is a mistake to believe that the passion we feel in making is ever truly carried into what we make. Art is always more abstract than we imagine. Form and colour speak only of form and colour—that is all. It often seems to me that art hides the artist more completely than it ever reveals him. So when I received this offer from Paris, I decided to put your portrait at the centre of my exhibition. It never occurred to me that you might refuse. I understand now that you were right. The picture cannot be shown. You must not be angry with me, Dorian, for what I have told you."}

