          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"263e1e2a68aa32d96e6d4c58\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Among the other public buildings in a certain town\u2014which, for many reasons, it would be wise not to name, and which I will not give a fictitious title\u2014there is one anciently common to most towns, large or small: a workhouse. And in that workhouse he was born, on a day and date I don\u2019t need to repeat, since it can\u2019t possibly matter to the reader at this point in the story. This is the mortality whose name is placed at the start of this chapter.\\n\\nFor a long time after he was brought into this world of sorrow and trouble by the parish surgeon, it was far from certain whether the child would survive long enough to have any name at all. If he had not, it is more than likely these memoirs would never have been written; and if they had, then, being limited to a couple of pages, they would have had the priceless distinction of being the most concise and faithful example of biography ever found in the literature of any age or country.\\n\\nI\u2019m not saying that being born in a workhouse is, in itself, the most fortunate and enviable thing that can happen to a human being. But in this particular case, it was the best thing that could possibly have happened to Oliver Twist. The truth is that there was considerable difficulty in getting Oliver to do the work of breathing\u2014a troublesome practice, though custom has made it necessary for our comfortable existence. For some time he lay gasping on a small flock mattress, balanced rather unevenly between this world and the next, with the scales clearly tipping toward the latter.\\n\\nNow, if during that brief period Oliver had been surrounded by careful grandmothers, worried aunts, experienced nurses, and doctors of deep wisdom, he would most certainly and without question have been killed quickly. But there was no one there except a pauper old woman, made rather hazy by an unusual allowance of beer, and a parish surgeon who handled such matters on contract. Oliver and Nature fought it out between them. The outcome was that, after a few struggles, Oliver breathed, sneezed, and then began to announce to the workhouse inmates that a new burden had been placed on the parish\u2014by crying as loudly as could reasonably be expected from a male infant who had not yet been given that very useful appendage, and for a much longer time than three minutes and a quarter.\"}"
