          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ccf6eab1a98dec2dc08b651e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The nephew looked at him, in vain, as he passed on to the door.\\n\\n\u201cGood night!\u201d said the uncle. \u201cI look forward to seeing you again in the morning. Sleep well! Light Monsieur my nephew to his chamber there\u2014and burn Monsieur my nephew in his bed, if you please,\u201d he added to himself, before ringing his little bell again and summoning his valet to his own bedroom.\\n\\nThe valet came and went, and then Monsieur the Marquis paced his room in his loose chamber-robe, preparing himself gently for sleep on that hot, still night. Rustling around, his soft, slippered feet making no sound on the floor, he moved like a refined tiger: like some enchanted marquis of the impenitently wicked kind in stories, whose periodic change into tiger form was either just starting or just ending.\\n\\nHe crossed from one end of his voluptuous bedroom to the other, looking again at the scraps of the day\u2019s journey that kept coming unbidden into his mind\u2014the slow climb up the hill at sunset, the sun setting, the descent, the mill, the prison on the crag, the little village in the hollow, the peasants at the fountain, and the road-mender with his blue cap pointing out the chain beneath the carriage. That fountain brought to mind the Paris fountain, the small bundle lying on the step, the women bending over it, and the tall man with his arms raised, crying, \u201cDead!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m cool now,\u201d said Monsieur the Marquis. \u201cI can go to bed.\u201d\\n\\nSo, leaving only one light burning on the large hearth, he let his thin gauze curtains fall around him, and listened as the night broke its silence with a long sigh while he settled down to sleep.\\n\\nFor three heavy hours, the stone faces on the outer walls stared blindly at the black night. For three heavy hours, the horses in the stables rattled their racks, the dogs barked, and the owl made a sound that had very little resemblance to the one men-poets conventionally assign to owls. But it is the stubborn habit of creatures like that to rarely say what people have written down for them.\\n\\nFor three heavy hours, the chateau\u2019s stone faces\u2014lion and human\u2014stared blindly at the night. Dead darkness lay over the whole landscape; dead darkness added its own hush to the hush of dust on all the roads. The burial ground had reached the point where its little heaps of poor grass were no longer distinguishable from one another; the figure on the Cross might as well have come down, for all that could be seen of it. In the village, the taxers and the taxed were fast asleep. Perhaps they dreamed of banquets, as the starving usually do, and of comfort and rest, as the driven slave and the yoked ox may\u2014its lean inhabitants slept soundly, and were fed and freed.\"}"
