          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5a8135acbb6986ee1550dd8e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If, however, you want one for use at home, I can provide you with an excellent article, and I guarantee you will be satisfied with the result. Who is it intended for? If it is for the police, or for anyone connected with Scotland Yard, I\u2019m afraid I can\u2019t help you. English detectives are really our best friends. I\u2019ve always found that if we rely on their stupidity, we can do exactly what we like. I can\u2019t spare any of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you,\u201d said Lord Arthur, \u201cit has nothing to do with the police. In fact, the clock is meant for the Dean of Chichester.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood heavens! I had no idea you felt so strongly about religion, Lord Arthur. Few young men feel that way these days.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid you\u2019re overestimating me, Herr Winckelkopf,\u201d Lord Arthur said, blushing. \u201cThe truth is, I know nothing about theology.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo it\u2019s purely a private matter?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPurely private.\u201d\\n\\nHerr Winckelkopf shrugged, left the room, and returned a few minutes later with a round cake of dynamite, no bigger than a penny, and a small French clock. On top of the clock was an ormolu figure of Liberty, trampling on the hydra of Despotism.\\n\\nLord Arthur\u2019s face brightened when he saw it. \u201cThat\u2019s exactly what I want,\u201d he said. \u201cNow tell me how it works\u2014how does it go off?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, there\u2019s my secret,\u201d Herr Winckelkopf replied, looking at his invention with justified pride. \u201cTell me when you want it to explode, and I will set the machine for that moment.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, today is Tuesday. If you could send it off right away\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat isn\u2019t possible. I have a great deal of important work to do for some friends of mine in Moscow. Still, I might send it tomorrow.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, it will be plenty of time,\u201d Lord Arthur said politely. \u201cIf it\u2019s delivered tomorrow night or Thursday morning. For the explosion, set it for Friday at noon exactly. The Dean is always at home at that hour.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFriday, at noon,\u201d Herr Winckelkopf repeated. He made a note of it in a large ledger on a bureau near the fireplace.\\n\\n\u201cAnd now,\u201d said Lord Arthur, standing up, \u201cplease tell me how much I owe you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s such a small matter, Lord Arthur, that I don\u2019t even want to charge you. The dynamite costs seven shillings and sixpence, the clock is three pounds ten, and the carriage is about five shillings. I\u2019m only too happy to help any friend of Count Rouvaloff\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut what about your trouble, Herr Winckelkopf?\u201d\"}"
