4Miss Nancy got ready to go out on her errand.\\n\\n\u201cStop a minute, my dear,\u201d the Jew said, pulling out a small basket that was partly covered. \u201cCarry that in one hand. It looks more respectable.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGive her a door-key to carry in her other one, Fagin,\u201d Sikes said. \u201cIt looks real and genivine like.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, my dear, it does,\u201d the Jew agreed, and he slipped a large street-door key onto the forefinger of the young lady\u2019s right hand. \u201cThere. Very good! Very good indeed, my dear!\u201d The Jew rubbed his hands together.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my brother! My poor, dear, sweet, innocent little brother!\u201d Nancy cried, bursting into tears. She clutched the little basket and the street-door key, wringing them in distress. \u201cWhat has happened to him? Where have they taken him? Oh, please have pity\u2014tell me what\u2019s been done with the dear boy, gentlemen. Do, gentlemen, if you please, gentlemen!\u201d\"}"
