          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1c4a99daecda391c86d37b8c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In all this there was a strangeness that drew me in and enchanted me. It was as if he had just awakened from his long, visionary sleep, and he felt something like one of the seven sleepers\u2014or like Nourjahad\u2014in that sweet imitation of an eastern tale: Diana was gone; his friends were changed or dead; and now, in his awakening, I was all he had left to love on earth.\\n\\nHow dear to me were the waters, the mountains, and the woods of Loch Lomond now that I had such a beloved companion for my rambles. I visited every delightful spot with my father\u2014on the islands, or beside tree-sheltered waterfalls; every shady path, every little dingle tangled with undergrowth and fern. His conversation broadened my thoughts. I felt as if I had been remade, surrounded by the freshness and life of a new being. It was as though, since his arrival, I had been transported from a narrow patch of earth into a universe boundless to imagination and understanding. My life before had been like a pleasing country rill\u2014never meant to leave its native fields, but to do its work quietly, then be absorbed and leave no trace. Now it seemed to me like a varied river flowing through a fertile, lovely landscape\u2014always changing, always beautiful. Alas, I did not know what desert it was about to reach; what rocks would tear its waters; and what hideous scene would be reflected, in a more distorted way, in its waves. Life was then brilliant. I began to learn to hope\u2014and what brings a more bitter despair to the heart than hope destroyed?\\n\\nIs it not strange that grief should follow so quickly on such divine happiness? I drank from an enchanted cup, but there was gall at the bottom of its long, drawn sweetness. My heart was full of deep affection, yet it was calm from the very depth and fullness of it. I had no idea that misery could arise from love, and this lesson\u2014that all at last must learn\u2014was taught me in a way that few are forced to receive. I lament now, and must always lament, those few short months of blissful paradise. I disobeyed no command; I ate no apple; and yet I was ruthlessly driven out of it. Alas! my companion did, and I was thrown down in his fall. But I\u2019m straying from the point\u2014let sorrow come when it is due; at this stage of my story I can still speak of happiness.\\n\\nThree months passed in this delightful companionship when my aunt fell ill. I spent a whole month in her chamber nursing her, but her disease was mortal, and she died, leaving me for some time inconsolable. Death is so dreadful to the living. The chains of habit are so strong, even when affection does not bind them, that the heart must be agonized when they break.\"}"
