          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"90aeeabe6512dc1b7816554e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the meantime, the pull of gravity would keep getting weaker, following the rule that it drops with the square of the distance. With that, my speed would increase greatly, until I finally reached those far regions where the Earth\u2019s attraction would be weaker than the Moon\u2019s. Based on these ideas, I did not think it was worth bringing more supplies than I would need for forty days.\\n\\n\u201cThere was still, however, another problem, and it made me uneasy. People have noticed that when balloons rise to great heights, they do not only feel pain when they breathe. They also often feel a strong discomfort in the head and body, sometimes with bleeding from the nose and other frightening symptoms. These symptoms get worse the higher the balloon goes. That thought was unsettling. Would these symptoms keep increasing without limit, or at least continue until death? I finally decided that they would not. I believed the cause was the steady loss of normal air pressure on the body\u2019s surface, which makes the small blood vessels swell. It was not, as with trouble breathing, caused by the air being too thin in a chemical sense to refresh the blood in the heart. So, unless the blood could not be renewed for some other reason, I saw no why life could not continue even in a vacuum. Breathing, after all, is mainly a muscular action\u2014the expansion and contraction of the chest\u2014and it is the cause of respiration, not the result.\\n\\n\u201cIn short, I thought that as the body became used to the lack of air pressure, the pain would slowly lessen. While the pain lasted, I trusted in the toughness of my constitution.\\n\\n\u201cThus, may it please your Excellencies, I have explained some of the reasons\u2014though not all\u2014that led me to plan a voyage to the Moon. I will now tell you the outcome of an attempt that seems so daring in its design, and in any case so completely without precedent in the history of mankind.\\n\\n\u201cAfter I reached the height I mentioned\u2014three miles and three-quarters\u2014I threw a quantity of feathers out of the car. I found that I was still rising quickly enough, so there was no need to release any ballast. I was glad of that, because I wanted to keep as much weight with me as I could carry, for reasons I will explain later.\"}"
