          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"155f7b8270c92f470d762d84\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThis must have been around that same period in Mr. Elliot\u2019s life,\u201d said Anne, \u201cthe time that has always sparked my particular curiosity. It must have been about when he first became known to my father and my sister. I never met him myself; I only heard about him. Still, there was something in his behavior toward my father and sister then, and later in the circumstances of his marriage, that I could never quite bring myself to reconcile with the way things are now. It felt like it pointed to a different kind of man.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know it all, I know it all,\u201d cried Mrs. Smith. \u201cHe had already been introduced to Sir Walter and your sister before I ever met him, but I heard him talk about them constantly. I know he was invited and encouraged, and I know he chose not to go. I can probably satisfy you on points you wouldn\u2019t expect. And as for his marriage\u2014I knew everything about it at the time. I was privy to every advantage and every drawback; I was the friend he confided in about his hopes and plans. And although I didn\u2019t know his wife before, her lower position in society made that impossible, yet I knew her all her life afterward\u2014at least until the last two years of her life\u2014and I can answer any question you want to ask.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI don\u2019t have any particular questions I need to ask about her. I\u2019ve always understood they weren\u2019t a happy couple. But I would like to know why, at that stage of his life, he should have brushed off my father\u2019s acquaintance the way he did. My father was certainly disposed to give him the kind and proper attention. Why did Mr. Elliot pull back?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Elliot,\u201d replied Mrs. Smith, \u201cat that period of his life, had one goal: to make his fortune, and by a quicker route than the law. He was determined to build it through marriage. He was determined\u2014not to ruin it with an imprudent marriage. And I know it was his belief (whether he was right or not, I can\u2019t say) that your father and sister, with their politeness and invitations, were trying to arrange a match between the heir and the young lady. In his view, such a match could never fit his ideas of wealth and independence. That was why he drew back, I assure you. He told me the whole story. He kept nothing from me. It was strange that, after just leaving you behind me in Bath, my first and principal acquaintance through marriage should have been your cousin\u2014and that, through him, I should be continually hearing about your father and sister.\u201d\"}"
