content.text:
{"request_id":"556270c35b0c19d58932ae8a","rewritten_text":"We spent a few sad hours until eleven o’clock, when the trial was set to begin. My father and the rest of my family had to attend as witnesses, so I went with them to court. During the whole of this cruel show of justice, I felt like I was being tortured while still alive. The outcome would decide whether my curiosity and reckless actions would lead to the death of two people. One was a smiling baby, full of innocence and joy. The other had been murdered in a far worse way, with every added detail that could make the crime unforgettable in its horror. Justine was also a girl of good character, with qualities that should have made her life happy. Now all of it would be erased by an ignominious grave, and I was the reason. A thousand times I would have preferred to confess to the crime they said Justine had committed. But I was not there when it happened, and any such confession would have been treated as the ravings of a madman, and it would not have cleared her, even though she was suffering because of me.

Justine looked calm. She wore mourning clothes, and her face, which was always attractive, seemed even more beautiful because of the seriousness she felt. Still, she acted confident that she was innocent. She did not tremble, even though thousands stared at her and hated her. Any kindness her beauty might have inspired was erased in the minds of the spectators by what they believed she had done. She was peaceful, but it was clear her calm was forced. Since her earlier confusion had been used as proof of her guilt, she pushed herself to look brave. When she entered the courtroom, she looked around and quickly found where we were sitting. A tear seemed to blur her eyes when she saw us, but she recovered at once. Her expression of sorrowful affection made it seem as if she was completely innocent.

The trial began. After the lawyer against her stated the charge, several witnesses were called. Strange facts were brought up that pointed to her guilt, and they might have shaken anyone who did not already have proof of her innocence, as I did. She had been out all night on the night when the murder was committed. Near morning, a market woman had seen her not far from where the murdered child’s body was later found. The woman asked her what she was doing there. Justine answered in a very strange way and gave only a confused, unclear response. She returned to the house about eight o’clock. When someone asked where she had spent the night, she said she had been looking for the child and asked earnestly whether anyone had heard anything about him."}

