content.text:
{"request_id":"b1c642a22249157d046fb840","rewritten_text":"I was born in a corner of land hemmed in by the sea, under a sky made dim by clouds; and when, in my thoughts, I set it beside the globe’s surface—its ocean without shores and its continents without any marked path—it seems to me no more than a trifling speck within the vast whole. Yet, if I weigh it against the measure of mental power, it stands far above countries of greater reach and a larger population. So it is: the human mind alone fashioned everything in man that is good or great, and Nature herself was only his first attendant. England, lying far to the north in a turbid sea, now comes into my dreams in the shape of a great, well-crewed ship—one that mastered the winds and rode with pride upon the waves. In my boyhood she was the entire world to me. When I stood on my native hills and looked out until plain and mountain ran to the farthest limits of my sight, dotted with the homes of my countrymen and made fruitful by their labours, the very center of the earth seemed fixed in that place; and the rest of her round orb was to me a tale not worth remembering, the forgetting of which would have cost neither my imagination nor my understanding so much as an effort.

My fortunes, from the start, have been a living example of how mutability can govern the shifting course of a man’s life. In my case, it came almost as an inheritance. My father was one of those men on whom nature had lavished, with a kind of prodigality, the gifts of wit and imagination, and then left his bark of life to be driven by those winds, without giving reason as a rudder or judgment as the pilot for the voyage. His origin was obscure; but circumstances brought him early into public view, and his small paternal estate was soon squandered in the brilliant theatre of fashion and luxury where he played his part. In the brief span of thoughtless youth, he was adored by the well-bred triflers of the day, and not least by the youthful sovereign, who slipped away from the intrigues of party and the heavy duties of kingly business to seek in my father’s company a constant amusement and a quickening of spirit. His impulses, never truly restrained by himself, kept drawing him into troubles from which only his own ingenuity could rescue him; and the growing heap of debts of honour and of trade, which would have crushed any other man to the ground, he carried with a light heart and an untamed, laughing gaiety. His presence was so necessary at the tables and gatherings of the wealthy that his lapses were treated as pardonable, and he was welcomed with intoxicating flattery.

Such popularity, like all others, is fleeting; and the troubles of every sort he had to face multiplied in a dreadful proportion as his small means of extrication dwindled."}

