          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"18e5d60b32ea7918f74ffe27\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dark and stormy was the night. Around the throne above, not a single star flickered; yet the deep, heavy thunder kept vibrating in my ears. Meanwhile, terrifying lightning flashed in an angry mood through the clouded chambers of heaven, as if it scorned the power exerted over its terror by the great Franklin. Even the loud, unruly winds, all at once, burst from their mysterious homes and howled about, as though their help could only add to the wildness of the scene.\\n\\nAt such a time\u2014so dark, so bleak\u2014for human sympathy, my very spirit sighed; but instead of that,\\n\\n\u2018My dearest friend, my counsellor, my comforter and guide\u2014 My joy in grief, my second bliss in joy,\u2019 came to my side.\\n\\nShe moved like one of those bright beings pictured in the sunny paths of fancy\u2019s Eden by the romantic and young: a queen of beauty, unadorned except for her own transcendent loveliness. So gentle was her step that it made no sound at all; and, but for the magical thrill her warm touch gave, she would have slipped away unnoticed\u2014uninvited. A strange sadness rested on her face, like icy tears on the robe of December, as she pointed to the elements battling outside and told me to contemplate the two beings presented.\\n\\nThis nightmare filled about ten pages of manuscript and ended with a sermon so destructive of hope for non-Presbyterians that it won the first prize. The piece was considered the finest work of the evening. When the mayor of the village presented the award to its author, he gave a warm speech, saying it was by far the most \u201celoquent\u201d thing he had ever heard, and that Daniel Webster himself might well be proud of it.\\n\\nIt may be noted, in passing, that the number of compositions in which the word \u201cbeauteous\u201d was overused, and human experience was referred to as \u201clife\u2019s page,\u201d was about the usual average.\\n\\nNow the master, softened almost to the edge of geniality, pushed his chair aside, turned his back to the audience, and began drawing a map of America on the blackboard, to keep the geography class occupied. But he made a miserable job of it with his unsteady hand, and a muffled titter ran through the room. He knew what was happening and tried to correct it. He wiped out the lines and redrew them, but he only distorted them even more, and the tittering grew louder. He threw himself entirely into his work, as if determined not to be beaten down by the laughter. He felt that everyone\u2019s eyes were fixed on him; he imagined he was doing well, and yet the tittering continued\u2014it even clearly increased.\"}"
