{"request_id":"3a3626cb4b1682583265df74","rewritten_text":"When the fit of weeping had spent itself and reason, along with memory, came back to me, I began to consider what had happened with a steadier mind—how it was fitting for me to act. Only a few hours had gone by, yet a vast change had occurred in my situation: the work of many years had been settled since the morning. My father was, for me, as good as dead; and for a moment I felt as though, with his white hair, he lay in his coffin, while I—youth slipping toward age—watched and wept at his timely end. But it was not truly so. I was still young—oh, far too young—and he was not dead to others; only I, most wretched, must never again see him or speak to him. I must flee from him with a keener urgency than from my greatest enemy. In solitude or in cities, I must never more behold him. The thought left me breathless with anguish, and, pressing itself upon my imagination, it kept me for a time from following any chain of ideas. From then on, I resolved, I would live in the most dismal seclusion. I would withdraw to the Continent and become a nun—not out of devotion to religion, for I was not a Catholic, but so that I might be shut away from the world forever. There I would find solitude in which to weep, and the voices of life would never reach me.

Yet my father—my beloved, most wretched father—would he die? Would he never master the fierce passion that now ruled him without pity? Could it not be that, many years from now, when age had cooled the burning feelings he suffers at present, he might then be a father to me again? This thought smoothed my brow; and I could feel—indeed, I wept to feel it—a faint, half-melancholy smile rise to the expression of pain on my lips. I dared to entertain kinder hopes for my future life. Years might pass, and they would slip by lightly, borne on by hope; and if they passed heavily, still they would pass, and I would not have lost my father forever. Let him wander another sixteen years in desolation. Let him once more pour out his wild complaints to the wide woods and the tremendous cataracts of some other country. Let him again endure dreadful peril and hardships that break the soul. Let the hot sun of the south burn his passion-worn cheeks again, and let the cold night rains fall upon him and chill his blood.

To this life, miserable father, I devote thee!—Go!—Let thy days be spent with savages, and thy nights beneath the vault of heaven. Let thy limbs be worn and thy heart chilled, and let all youth be dead within thee!"}

