{"request_id":"8666578b20ade947ad3bfefc","rewritten_text":"Yet even now I had not drunk the bitter potion to the very end; I was not yet convinced that I had truly lost everything; I did not yet feel, in every pulse, in every nerve, in every thought, that I remained alone of my race—that I was the LAST MAN.

The day had turned cloudy, and a drizzling rain began at sunset. Even the eternal skies weep, I thought; is there any shame, then, in a mortal man spending himself in tears? I remembered the ancient fables, in which human beings are said to dissolve away through weeping into fountains that gush forever. Ah, if only it were so—then my destiny would be in some way like the watery death of Adrian and Clara. Oh, grief is fantastic; it weaves a web on which to trace the history of its suffering through every form and change around it. It takes itself into all living nature; it finds nourishment in every object. Like light, it fills everything, and, like light, it gives its own colors to all.

In my search I had wandered some distance from the spot where I had been thrown, and I came to one of those watch-towers that, at regular intervals, line the Italian shore. I was glad of shelter, glad to find a work of human hands after staring so long at nature’s bleak emptiness. So I went in and climbed the rough, winding staircase up to the guard-room. Fate was kind enough that no cruel trace remained of its former inhabitants. A few planks laid across two iron trestles, scattered with dried Indian-corn leaves, were the bed offered to me. An open chest, holding some half-molded biscuit, stirred up an appetite I perhaps had before, but until now had not realized I still had. Thirst, too—violent and burning—tormented me, the result of the sea-water I had drunk and the exhaustion of my body. Kind nature had even made these needs feel pleasurable, so that I—even I!—was refreshed and calmed as I ate this miserable food and drank a little of the sour wine that half filled a flask left in this abandoned dwelling. Then I lay down on the bed, not too good for the victim of shipwreck. The earthy smell of the dried leaves soothed my senses after the hateful odor of seaweed. I forgot my loneliness. I neither looked back nor ahead; my senses were hushed into rest. I fell asleep and dreamed of all the dear scenes inland—of hay-makers, of the shepherd’s whistle to his dog when he called for help to drive the flock to the fold—and of sights and sounds from my boyhood’s mountain life, which I had long since forgotten."}

