          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b7113a72d77c4141fc5f2d63\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The next day was set for the production (so that our honor wouldn\u2019t suffer from delay), and the next day Drummle showed up with a polite little admission in Estella\u2019s hand\u2014that she had had the honor of dancing with him several times. That left me with no real choice but to regret that I had been \u201cbetrayed into a warmth which,\u201d and, on the whole, to reject as untenable the idea that I could be found anywhere. Drummle and I then sat facing each other, snorting for an hour, while the Grove kept up indiscriminate contradiction, and at last it was declared that the promotion of good feeling had gone forward at an astonishing pace.\\n\\nI say this lightly, but it wasn\u2019t a light thing for me. I can\u2019t adequately describe the pain it caused me to think that Estella might show any favor to such a contemptible, clumsy, sulky fool\u2014so far below the average. Even now, I believe it was due to some pure fire of generosity and disinterestedness in my love for her that I couldn\u2019t bear the thought of her stooping to that hound. No doubt I would have been miserable no matter who she favored; but a worthier object would have caused a different kind and degree of distress.\\n\\nIt was easy enough for me to find out, and I soon did find out, that Drummle had begun to follow her closely, and that she let him. After a little while, he was always in pursuit of her, and he and I crossed paths every day. He kept at it in a dull, persistent way, and Estella kept him at it\u2014now with encouragement, now with discouragement, now almost flattering him, now openly despising him, now knowing him very well, now scarcely remembering who he was.\\n\\nThe Spider\u2014what Mr. Jaggers called him\u2014was used to lying in wait, though, and had the patience of his kind. On top of that, he had a thick-headed confidence in his money and in his family\u2019s greatness, which sometimes worked in his favor\u2014almost in place of concentration and determined purpose. So the Spider, doggedly watching Estella, outlasted many brighter insects, and would often uncoil himself and drop at exactly the right moment.\\n\\nAt a certain Assembly Ball in Richmond (back then, there were Assembly Balls in most places), where Estella had outshone every other beauty, this blundering Drummle hovered around her so persistently, and with so much tolerance on her part, that I decided to speak to her about him. I took the next opportunity\u2014when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home, and was sitting apart among some flowers, ready to leave.\"}"
