          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"91374c6638e0cb8e47faead2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He was short, but he stood very straight, and his voice was the sweetest I had ever heard. He began his talk by reviewing the history of chemistry and the improvements different learned men had made, speaking with great energy the names of the most famous discoverers. Then he gave a quick look at the current state of the science and explained many of its basic terms. After a few preliminary experiments, he ended with a strong praise of modern chemistry. The words were so memorable that I will never forget them:\\n\\n\u201cThe ancient teachers of this science,\u201d he said, \u201cpromised the impossible and achieved nothing. The modern masters promise very little. They know that metals cannot be turned into other substances, and that the elixir of life is only a fantasy. But these philosophers\u2014whose hands seem made only to handle dirt, and whose eyes are meant to study the microscope or the crucible\u2014have truly performed miracles. They dig into the hidden parts of nature and show how she works in her secret places. They rise into the heavens. They have learned how the blood circulates and what the air we breathe is like. They have gained new powers, almost without limit. They can command the thunder of the sky, imitate an earthquake, and even mock the invisible world with shadows of its own.\u201d\\n\\nThose were the professor\u2019s words\u2014no, the words of fate itself\u2014spoken in a way that was meant to destroy me. As he continued, I felt as if my soul were wrestling with a real enemy. One by one, the different keys that made up the mechanism of my life were turned. Note after note was struck, and soon my mind was filled with a single thought, a single idea, a single goal. \u201cSo much has been done,\u201d I heard the soul of Frankenstein exclaim; \u201cmore, far more, will I accomplish. Following the path already laid out, I will find a new way. I will explore unknown powers and reveal to the world the deepest mysteries of creation.\u201d\\n\\nThat night I did not close my eyes. Inside me, everything was in revolt and confusion. I could feel that order would come out of it, but I had no power to bring it about. Little by little, after morning dawn, sleep finally came. When I woke, the thoughts of the previous night seemed like a dream. What remained was only a firm decision: to return to my old studies and devote myself to a science for which I believed I had a natural talent. On the same day I went to see M. Waldman. In private, his manners were even gentler and more appealing than in public. There was a dignity in his face during his lectures, but in his own home that dignity was replaced by the greatest friendliness and kindness.\u201d}\"}"
