          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"52268bfd7cf64d1923b998fc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBasil, this is amazing! I have to see Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\nHallward stood up and walked back and forth in the garden. After a while, he returned. \u201cHarry,\u201d he said, \u201cto me, Dorian Gray is only a reason to make art. You might see nothing at all in him. I see everything. He is most alive in my work when there is no picture of him there. As I said, he is a sign of a new style. I see it in the shape of certain lines, in the beauty and small details of certain colors. That\u2019s all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen why don\u2019t you show his portrait?\u201d Lord Henry asked.\\n\\n\u201cBecause, without meaning to, I put into it some expression of all this strange worship of art. Of course, I\u2019ve never told him about it. He doesn\u2019t know anything. He will never know anything. But the world might guess, and I won\u2019t expose my soul to their shallow curiosity. They will never put my heart under a microscope. There\u2019s too much of me in it, Harry\u2014too much of me!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPoets aren\u2019t as careful as you are. They know how useful strong feelings are for getting published. These days, a broken heart can sell in many editions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI hate that,\u201d Hallward cried. \u201cAn artist should make beautiful things, but should put nothing of his own life into them. We live in an age where people treat art like it\u2019s supposed to be a kind of autobiography. We\u2019ve lost the pure sense of beauty. One day I\u2019ll show the world what it really is. And for that reason, the world will never see my portrait of Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you\u2019re wrong, Basil, but I won\u2019t argue. Only people who are lost in their thinking argue like that. Tell me, is Dorian Gray very fond of you?\u201d\\n\\nThe painter thought for a moment. \u201cHe likes me,\u201d he answered after a pause. \u201cI know he likes me. Of course I flatter him terribly. I get a strange pleasure from telling him things I know I\u2019ll regret later. Most of the time, he is charming to me, and we sit in the studio talking about a thousand things. But sometimes, he is horribly careless, and it seems like he truly enjoys hurting me. Then I feel, Harry, that I\u2019ve given my whole soul to someone who treats it like a flower to pin on his coat\u2014just decoration to please his vanity, an ornament for a summer day.\u201d\"}"
