th that Tsar\u2019s residence here as a ship carpenter; but it was widely believed that he was a Nihilist agent, and there was no question that the Russian Embassy viewed his presence in London with little goodwill. Lord Arthur felt Rouvaloff was precisely the sort of man he needed, and one morning he drove down to his rooms in Bloomsbury to seek his counsel and help.\\n\\n\u201cThen you\u2019re taking politics seriously?\u201d Count Rouvaloff asked, after Lord Arthur explained the purpose of his errand. But Lord Arthur, who despised any show of swagger, found himself obliged to tell him that he had not the least interest in public questions at all; he only wanted an explosive machine for a matter of family concern, in which no one else was involved but himself.\"}"
