          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ace183032d4bcf6844dc7674\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Up with the morning light, and again scheming to slip past the watchfulness of my creditors, I hurried to the bookseller\u2019s stall and spread out the small amount of ready money I had, using it to buy a few volumes on Mechanics and Practical Astronomy. Once I got home safely with them, I threw myself into their study, and before long I had made myself so far proficient in these subjects that I judged it enough to carry out my plan. During the pauses of this time, I spared no effort to soothe the three creditors who had been giving me such trouble. At last I managed it\u2014partly by selling enough of my household furniture to cover a moiety of their demand, and partly by promising to pay the remainder once I had finished a little project I told them I had in mind, and for which I asked their help. Since they were simple-minded men, I found it no great task to bring them round to my purpose.\\n\\n\u201cMatters being thus arranged, I then contrived, with the assistance of my wife, and with the utmost secrecy and care, to dispose of whatever property I still had, and to raise, in small sums, under various pretences, and with no regard at all for how I might repay, a considerable quantity of ready money. With what came in, I set about procuring, from time to time, cambric muslin\u2014very fine\u2014cut in pieces of twelve yards each; twine; a supply of caoutchouc varnish; a large, deep wicker basket made to order; and several other items needed for the construction and fitting-out of a balloon of extraordinary size. I told my wife to get it assembled as quickly as possible, and I gave her full instructions as to the exact method to follow. Meanwhile, I worked the twine into a net of the proper dimensions; fitted it with a hoop and the necessary cords; bought a quadrant, a compass, a spy-glass, and a common barometer with certain important alterations, along with two astronomical instruments not so widely known. After that, I took chances to convey by night, to a secluded spot east of Rotterdam, five iron-bound casks meant to hold about fifty gallons each, and one larger besides; six tinned ware tubes, three inches across and ten feet long; a quantity of a particular metallic substance, or semi-metal, which I will not name; and a dozen demijohns of a very ordinary acid. The gas to be produced from these materials is a gas never yet made by anyone else\u2014or, at least, never used for any like purpose. I would not hesitate to set out the secret, but it properly belongs to a citizen of Nantz in France, who communicated it to me conditionally.\u201d\"}"
