          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0277963b8c03d8044f8cb0bc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Countrymen, butchers, drovers, hawkers, boys, thieves, idlers, and vagabonds of every kind were all mixed together in one crowd. Drovers whistled, dogs barked, and oxen bellowed and surged forward. Sheep bleated, pigs grunted and squeaked, hawkers shouted, and all around there were cries, threats, and fights. Bells rang and voices roared from every public-house. People shoved and pushed and drove each other along, yelling and screaming. The noise was ugly and rough, coming from every corner of the market, and there were dirty, unwashed, unshaven figures running back and forth, pushing into the crowd and bursting out of it again and again. It was a scene so loud and confusing that it left the senses completely thrown off.\\n\\nMr. Sikes, dragging Oliver behind him, forced his way through the thickest part of the crowd. He paid almost no attention to the sights and sounds that amazed the boy. He nodded a couple of times to a friend as he passed, and he refused as many chances as he could to stop for a morning drink. Then he kept going until they were out of the worst of the chaos, and had made their way through Hosier Lane into Holborn.\\n\\n\u201cNow, young \u2019un!\u201d said Sikes, looking up at the clock on St. Andrew\u2019s Church. \u201cIt\u2019s hard on seven. You\u2019ve got to move. Don\u2019t fall behind already, Lazy-legs!\u201d\\n\\nAs he said this, Sikes jerked Oliver\u2019s wrist. Oliver hurried his pace into a fast trot, somewhere between walking and running, and tried to keep up with the quick steps of the house-breaker.\\n\\nThey kept this pace until they had passed Hyde Park Corner and were on their way to Kensington. Then Sikes slowed down. An empty cart that had been some distance behind caught up. Seeing \u201cHounslow\u201d written on it, he asked the driver, as politely as he could manage, whether he would give them a lift as far as Isleworth.\\n\\n\u201cJump up,\u201d said the man. \u201cIs that your boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; he\u2019s my boy,\u201d Sikes answered, staring hard at Oliver. At the same time, he put his hand absentmindedly into the pocket where the pistol was.\\n\\n\u201cYour father walks a bit too fast for you, doesn\u2019t he?\u201d the driver asked, noticing Oliver was out of breath.\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d Sikes cut in. \u201cHe\u2019s used to it. Here, take hold of my hand, Ned. Get in with you!\u201d\\n\\nWith that, he spoke to Oliver and helped him climb into the cart. The driver pointed to a pile of sacks and told Oliver to lie down there and rest.\\n\\nAs they passed the mile-stones, Oliver kept wondering, more and more, where Sikes planned to take him. They went by Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, and Brentford, and still they traveled on at the same steady pace, as if they had only just started their journey.\"}"
